// Auto-generated package data - do not edit manually
var packageData = [{"id": 1, "torah_ref": 1, "label": "חָכְמָה וְיַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "Wisdom and Jacob", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #1 centers on חכמה (divine intellect/sekhel) as the life-force of all things, with Jacob as the archetypal figure who attaches himself to the sekhel in every object. This cluster encompasses the firstborn/reishit concept (sekhel as primary), the sun-metaphor (sekhel illuminates all paths), the chet letter (cognate of chiyut/vitality), and the practice of hispaklut — gazing at the sekhel hidden within each thing to draw close to God.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["חכמה", "חיות", "אות חי\"ת", "בכור", "יעקב", "ראשית", "שמש", "\"תמימי דרך\"", "יוסף", "להסתכל בהשכל שיש בכל דבר", "תם", "הסתכלות", "אשרי"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה": 8, "יעקב": 6, "חיות": 3, "להסתכל בהשכל שיש בכל דבר": 2, "הסתכלות": 2, "אות חי\"ת": 2, "בכור": 2, "ראשית": 2, "שמש": 2, "\"תמימי דרך\"": 1, "יוסף": 1, "תם": 1, "אשרי": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שָׁרְשֵׁי הַנְּשָׁמוֹת וְכִסֵּא הַכָּבוֹד", "label_en": "Roots of Souls and the Throne of Glory", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of the primordial maternal source-foundation of all existence. The Throne of Glory is the universal womb from which all souls are hewn. Chava (אֵם כָּל חָי) is the Torah's name for this same reality — the mother of all living beings whose roots are lodged beneath the Throne. The concept of 'being the place of the world' (מְקוֹמוֹ שֶׁל עוֹלָם) expresses the same idea: this root-source encompasses all souls and all places. The rib/tsela is identified with Chava = Throne = soul-roots. They are not causes of each other — they are the same cosmic reality viewed from different angles.", "dominant_node": "שרשי הנשמות", "nodes": ["שרשי הנשמות", "כסא הכבוד", "אם כל חי", "חוה", "הצלע", "מקומו של עולם"], "node_degrees": {"שרשי הנשמות": 4, "כסא הכבוד": 3, "אם כל חי": 2, "חוה": 2, "הצלע": 1, "מקומו של עולם": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["שָׁרְשֵׁי הַנְּשָׁמוֹת, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת כִּסֵּא הַכָּבוֹד, אֵם כָּל חָי (section ב)", "הַצֵּלָע, שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת חַוָּה, אֵם כָּל חָי, בְּחִינַת כִּסֵּא הַכָּבוֹד, שָׁרְשֵׁי הַנְּשָׁמוֹת (section ב)", "מִי שֶׁאוֹחֵז בִּבְחִינַת כִּסֵּא הַכָּבוֹד, בִּבְחִינַת שָׁרְשֵׁי הַנְּשָׁמוֹת, הוּא גַּם כֵּן בְּחִינַת מְקוֹמוֹ שֶׁל עוֹלָם (section יד)"], "mirror": "This package has no direct evil mirror — it is the universal root that is purely of holiness.", "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הצלע", "חוה"], "reason": "The rib (הצלע) is explicitly identified as 'היא בחינת חוה' — it IS Chava, not a cause of her. A single bechina edge."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מקומו של עולם"], "reason": "Section יד explicitly states that grasping the Throne/soul-roots makes a person 'מקומו של עולם' — this is a bechina (same nature as God who is מקומו של עולם), not a consequence."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 1, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְחֵן", "label_en": "Kingship and Grace", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster orbits מלכות (divine kingship) as the nun-letter that receives illumination from the chet (חכמה/sun) and becomes the moon (לבנה) shining with reflected light. When malkhut receives from chokhmah, it generates חן (grace/favor) — the quality that causes prayers to be accepted. The ספינה metaphor (the ship whose worthiness/honor the wave tries to sink) belongs here, as does the yetzer tov identified with malkhut as 'misken v'chakham'. The tav letter (engraving/marking) and the small isolated pair 'צריך לתן כח למלכות דקדשה ↔ לעולם ירגיז' are merged here because both describe the mechanism of empowering holy malkhut over the evil kingdom.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "\"מסכן וחכם\"", "אות נו\"ן", "דל", "דלית לה מגרמה כלום", "חן", "חשיבות", "יצר טוב", "לבנה", "עני", "ספינה", "ספון", "צריך כל אחד לתן כח לבחינת מלכות דקדשה להתגבר על מלכות דסטרא אחרא", "לעולם ירגיז אדם יצר טוב על יצר הרע"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 9, "ספינה": 3, "\"מסכן וחכם\"": 2, "חן": 2, "חשיבות": 2, "יצר טוב": 2, "אות נו\"ן": 1, "דל": 1, "דלית לה מגרמה כלום": 1, "לבנה": 1, "עני": 1, "ספון": 1, "צריך כל אחד לתן כח לבחינת מלכות דקדשה להתגבר על מלכות דסטרא אחרא": 1, "לעולם ירגיז אדם יצר טוב על יצר הרע": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אות ת\"ו", "חקיקה", "רשימה"], "reason": "The tav-letter means 'engraving and marking' (chakika u-reshima). In the Torah's argument, when chen is produced (chet+nun=malkhut receiving chokhmah), it engraves a place in the heart of the petitioned person. The tav is the seal/outcome of the malkhut-chokhmah union generating chen. Thematically it is an extension of the chen-malkhut nexus."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צריך כל אחד לתן כח לבחינת מלכות דקדשה להתגבר על מלכות דסטרא אחרא", "לעולם ירגיז אדם יצר טוב על יצר הרע"], "reason": "This bechina-pair directly states the practical imperative: one must empower holy malkhut over the evil kingdom by rousing the good inclination. It is a positive counterpart to the yetzer hara cluster and belongs with the malkhut/chen package because it describes what malkhut is supposed to do."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יְרוּשָׁלַיִם וְהַיִּרְאָה שֶׁבַּלֵּב", "label_en": "Jerusalem and the Fear in the Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of 'the heart-center of awe.' Yerushalayim is explicitly called 'yirah shalem' — complete awe. The lev (heart) is the organ of awe — awe is described as 'davar hamosur lalev.' The 'shem' (divine name) is the nature of awe itself — 'l'yirah et hashem hanichbad.' They are all different expressions of the same inner spiritual reality: the seat of complete God-consciousness in the heart.", "dominant_node": "ירושלים", "nodes": ["ירושלים", "יראה שלם", "לב", "שם"], "node_degrees": {"ירושלים": 3, "יראה שלם": 2, "לב": 2, "שם": 3}, "source_bechinas": ["יְרוּשָׁלַיִם בְּחִינַת יִרְאָה שָׁלֵם, הַיְנוּ שְׁלֵמוּת הַיִּרְאָה, הַתְּלוּיָה בַּלֵּב (section ד)", "הַיִּרְאָה שֶׁבַּלֵּב הִיא גַּם כֵּן בְּחִינַת שֵׁם, בִּבְחִינַת לְיִרְאָה אֶת הַשֵּׁם הַנִּכְבָּד (section ה)", "אַנְשֵׁי שֵׁם זֶה בְּחִינַת שָׁלֹשׁ מִדּוֹת הַנַּ\"ל, שֶׁכָּל אֶחָד וְאֶחָד נִקְרָא בְּחִינַת שֵׁם (section ה)"], "mirror": "Package 3 (the three destroyers) is the evil mirror — they destroy Yerushalayim/the heart-awe.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 1, "label": "יֵצֶר הָרָע וּמַלְכוּת הָרְשָׁעָה", "label_en": "The Evil Inclination and the Wicked Kingdom", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes in Torah #1 that represent the negative pole: the yetzer hara (called 'melekh zaken u-kesil'), the wave (gal) that tries to sink the ship of Israel's honor, the evil malkhut (sitra achra's moon), Esav who despised the firstborn/sekhel, and the descriptions of sin-caused madness (ruach shtut, meshugga, baal aveira). Four small isolated pairs are merged here: 'baal aveira ↔ meshugga', 'eish levana ↔ yetzer hara disguising as mitzva', and 'shiga'on ↔ ruach shtut', all of which describe the same domain — the destructive force that Torah must overcome.", "dominant_node": "יצר הרע", "nodes": ["יצר הרע", "\"מלך זקן וכסיל\"", "גל", "מלכות הרשעה", "לבנה דסטרא אחרא", "מי שאינו מקשר עצמו אל השכל והחכמה והחיות, שיש בכל דבר", "עשו", "בעל עברה", "משוגע", "אש לבנה", "מתחלה היצר הרע מתלבש עצמו במצוות", "שגעון", "רוח שטות"], "node_degrees": {"יצר הרע": 3, "מלכות הרשעה": 3, "מי שאינו מקשר עצמו אל השכל והחכמה והחיות, שיש בכל דבר": 2, "\"מלך זקן וכסיל\"": 1, "גל": 1, "לבנה דסטרא אחרא": 1, "עשו": 1, "בעל עברה": 1, "משוגע": 1, "אש לבנה": 1, "מתחלה היצר הרע מתלבש עצמו במצוות": 1, "שגעון": 1, "רוח שטות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בעל עברה", "משוגע"], "reason": "The sinner is equated to a madman (Sota 3a — ein adam over aveira ela im ken nikhnas bo ruach shtut). This bechina-pair is the direct theological link between sin and madness that the Torah yetzer-hara-as-ruach-shtut narrative depends on. It is part of the evil domain description."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אש לבנה", "מתחלה היצר הרע מתלבש עצמו במצוות"], "reason": "White fire (eish levana) is the disguise of the yetzer hara — it appears as a mitzvah but is a damaging angel. This is a direct bechina of the yetzer hara's deceptive tactic and belongs squarely in the evil inclination cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שגעון", "רוח שטות"], "reason": "Madness (shiga'on) is the ruach shtut — the spirit of folly that the yetzer hara injects into a person. This pair describes the symptom of the yetzer hara's dominance and is part of the evil kingdom's effect on the human soul."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שָׁלֹשׁ מִדּוֹת הַמַּחֲרִיבוֹת", "label_en": "The Three Destroying Desires", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of 'the three corrupting forces rooted in the heart that destroy Yerushalayim (complete awe).' Taavas mamon, taavas achila, and taavas mishgal are not three separate things — they are three faces of the same evil reality: the three watches of the night, the darkness, the obstacles. The symbolic animals (donkey, dogs) and night-images are not causes of the desires — they ARE the desires, expressed in symbolic form. All three are called 'shem' (each desire has its own corrupting 'name-force').", "dominant_node": "תאוות ממון", "nodes": ["תאוות ממון", "תאוות אכילה", "תאוות משגל", "שלוש משמרות", "לילה", "חושך", "חמור נועֵר", "כלבים", "עזות"], "node_degrees": {"תאוות ממון": 3, "תאוות אכילה": 3, "תאוות משגל": 3, "שלוש משמרות": 2, "לילה": 2, "חושך": 1, "חמור נועֵר": 1, "כלבים": 1, "עזות": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["לַיְלָה זֶה בְּחִינַת חֹשֶׁךְ, הַיְנוּ מְנִיעוֹת, וְהֵם בְּחִינַת שָׁלֹשׁ מִשְׁמָרוֹת, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת שָׁלֹשׁ מִדּוֹת הַנַּ\"ל (section ד)", "מִשְׁמָרָה רִאשׁוֹנָה – חֲמוֹר נוֹעֵר, בְּחִינַת תַּאֲוַת מָמוֹן (section ד)", "שְׁנִיָּה – כְּלָבִים צּוֹעֲקִים. זֶה בְּחִינַת תַּאֲוַת אֲכִילָה (section ד)", "שְׁלִישִׁית – תִּינוֹק יוֹנֵק מִשְּׁדֵי אִמּוֹ; זֶה בְּחִינַת מִשְׁגָּל (section ד)", "עַזּוּת מַלְכוּתָא בְּלָא תָגָא — false leaders rise through azut (section ג)"], "mirror": "Package 2 (Yerushalayim and the heart-awe) is the good counterpart destroyed by this package.", "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עזות"], "reason": "Azut (brazenness) is described as the false-leader force that mimics true leadership — it is the face of desire-driven power, the same essential nature as the three corrupting desires. Merged here because the Rebbe explicitly connects it to 'malkhuta b'lo taga' — kingdom without proper crown, the evil expression of the name/shem domain."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 1, "label": "תּוֹרָה וָמַקֵּל", "label_en": "Torah and the Staff", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster centers on the Torah as the instrument of subduing the yetzer hara, expressed through three overlapping metaphors: (1) the vav-letter (whose shape is a staff/makel), (2) the Tablets being six-by-six handbreadths (arokhan vav, rakhban vav), and (3) the Torah as 'maklot v'shemot' — sticks and divine names used to beat back the madness of the evil inclination, as Rabbah bar bar Chanah's alwata (club) inscribed with 'Ehyeh asher Ehyeh'. Derakhei Hashem and Luchot are included as synonyms for Torah in this context.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אות וא\"ו", "מקל", "שמות", "דרכי ה'", "לוחות"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 6, "מקל": 4, "אות וא\"ו": 3, "שמות": 2, "דרכי ה'": 1, "לוחות": 1}}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דַּעַת וְשָׁלֹשׁ רְגָלִים", "label_en": "Da'at and the Three Pilgrimage Festivals", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of 'renewed divine intellect that repairs the three corrupting desires.' The three cavities of the skull (תלת חללי דגולגלתא) are not a cause of the three regalim — they ARE the three regalim, expressed at the level of the brain's structure. Da'at is the general faculty; the three brain-cavities and the three festivals are its three specific expressions. The moon (chodesh/yerach) is the vehicle of renewal that occasions the festivals, and is therefore the same nature as the renewal of consciousness that the festivals embody.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["דעת", "תלת מוחין", "שלוש רגלים", "ירח", "התחדשות המוחין"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 2, "תלת מוחין": 3, "שלוש רגלים": 3, "ירח": 1, "התחדשות המוחין": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["הַדַּעַת הוּא בְּחִינַת תְּלָת מֹחִין, תְּלָת חֲלָלֵי דְּגֻלְגַּלְתָּא, וְהֵם בְּחִינַת שָׁלֹשׁ רְגָלִים (section ה)", "בַּחֹדֶשׁ זֶה בְּחִינַת הִתְחַדְּשׁוּת הַמֹּחִין שֶׁבְּשָׁלֹשׁ רְגָלִים, בִּבְחִינַת עָשָׂה יָרֵחַ לְמוֹעֲדִים (section conclusion)"], "mirror": "Package 3 (the three desires) is the evil mirror — the three regalim each repair one of the three corrupting desires.", "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ירח", "התחדשות המוחין"], "reason": "The moon (ירח) = the renewal of consciousness (התחדשות המוחין) = the festivals. They are explicitly identified as one bechina in the final section: 'עשה ירח למועדים.' These are two names for the same reality."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 1, "label": "אוֹת תָּ\"ו וְהַחֲקִיקָה", "label_en": "The Letter Tav and the Engraving", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes (the tav-letter, engraving, and marking/reshima) all share the same essential domain: the seal or imprint that is left when prayer is accepted. The tav means 'engraving and marking' — it is the conceptual domain of impression-left-behind. This is a separate package from מלכות וחן because although grace (חן) *produces* the engraving (cause-and-effect / eitza), the engraving itself is a different essential nature from grace. Grace is receptive kingship; engraving is the mark of impact. One causes the other — that is an eitza connection between packages, not a shared nature within a package.", "dominant_node": "אות ת\"ו", "nodes": ["אות ת\"ו", "חקיקה", "רשימה"], "node_degrees": {"אות ת\"ו": 2, "חקיקה": 1, "רשימה": 1}, "correction_note": "Originally these 3 nodes were merged into Package 2 (מלכות וחן) by the algorithm. Corrected per framework rule: packages represent shared nature (bechina/parallel), not cause-and-effect chains (eitza). The tav/engraving cluster is the *result* of grace, not an aspect sharing the same nature as grace."}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּנְבוּאָה", "label_en": "Prayer and Prophecy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of 'the divine word that is the root of all vitality.' Prayer and prophecy are not cause-and-effect — they are the same faculty of divine communication, expressed from opposite directions (human speech upward vs. divine speech downward). 'Boreh niv sefatayim' equates them as two expressions of the same logos. Dvar Hashem is the root of all healing herbs, all stars, all powers — it is the supreme source. The siach hasadeh (field plants) return their powers to prayer because prayer IS their ultimate root. Smell/ruach is the soul's faculty that connects to this root.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "נבואה", "דבר ה'", "שיח השדה", "ריח", "נשמה"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 5, "נבואה": 3, "דבר ה'": 3, "שיח השדה": 2, "ריח": 2, "נשמה": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["הַתְּפִלָּה הִיא בְּחִינַת בּוֹרֵא נִיב שְׂפָתָיִם, בְּחִינַת נְבוּאָה (section ח)", "עַל־יְדֵי נְבוּאָה זוֹכִין לִתְפִלָּה, שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת דְּבַר ה' (section ט)", "עִקָּר הַתְּפִלָּה הִיא מֵהַנְּשָׁמָה... וְעִקָּר הוּא הָרֵיחַ, כִּי אֵין הַנְּשָׁמָה נֶהֱנֵית אֶלָּא מֵהָרֵיחַ (section יב)", "שִׂיחַ הַשָּׂדֶה חָזְרוּ כֹּחָם וְנָתְנוּ אוֹתָם בְּתוֹךְ תְּפִלָּתוֹ, שֶׁהִיא שָׁרְשָׁם (section יא)", "שׁוֹפָר הוּא בְּחִינַת שִׂיחַ הַשָּׂדֶה, שֶׁחוֹזְרִין כֹּחָם לְתוֹךְ הַתְּפִלָּה (section conclusion)"], "mirror": "Package 7 (the three sitra achra services) is the evil mirror — they corrupt and exile prayer.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מָשִׁיחַ וְהַחוֹטֶם", "label_en": "Mashiach and the Nose", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of 'the receptor and gatherer of all prayers.' Mashiach is explicitly called 'bechinas chotem' — the nose that receives all smells = all prayers. The 'spark of Mashiach' (hitnatztzut mashiach) is the same faculty as the nose's reception — the two are identical in their nature. The nose is not a cause of Mashiach; they are the same receptive-gathering reality. 'Utehilati echetam lach' confirms the chotem = the faculty of receiving praise/prayer.", "dominant_node": "משיח", "nodes": ["משיח", "חוטם", "התנוצצות משיח"], "node_degrees": {"משיח": 2, "חוטם": 2, "התנוצצות משיח": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["מָשִׁיחַ הוּא בְּחִינַת חֹטֶם, בִּבְחִינַת רוּחַ אַפֵּינוּ מְשִׁיחַ ה'; שֶׁמְּקַבֵּל כָּל הָרֵיחוֹת שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת הַתְּפִלּוֹת (section יב)", "וְזֶה בְּחִינַת וּתְהִלָּתִי אֶחֱטָם לָךְ; בְּחִינַת חֹטֶם (section יב)", "וְזֶה בְּחִינַת הִתְנוֹצְצוּת מָשִׁיחַ (section יב)"], "mirror": "No direct evil mirror in this Torah — Mashiach is presented as the purely positive gathering-point.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲבוֹדוֹת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "label_en": "The Three Services of the Other Side", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of 'the three spiritual corruptions that are the anti-prayer and anti-divine-service.' The Rebbe explicitly calls them 'avodot d'sitra achra.' Bizuy anashim, avoda zara, and pegam habrit are not three separate evils — they are three faces of the same servitude (avdut) to the sitra achra. Egypt = avoda zara = beit avadim confirms this: the same servitude appears in all three. They are the mirror-corruption of the three corrective services that enable proper tefillah.", "dominant_node": "עבדות", "nodes": ["עבדות", "בזוי אנשים", "עבודה זרה", "פגם הברית", "מצרים", "עזות (מנהיג שקר)"], "node_degrees": {"עבדות": 3, "בזוי אנשים": 1, "עבודה זרה": 1, "פגם הברית": 1, "מצרים": 1, "עזות (מנהיג שקר)": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["כָּל שָׁלֹשׁ מִדּוֹת אֵלּוּ הֵם בְּחִינַת עֲבוֹדוֹת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא (section י)", "בִּזּוּי אֲנָשִׁים הוּא בְּחִינַת עֲבוֹדָה... נָפְלוּ לִבְחִינַת עַבְדוּת (section י)", "עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה – כִּי אֶרֶץ מִצְרַיִם... נִקְרָאִים בֵּית עֲבָדִים, בְּחִינַת עַבְדוּת (section י)", "שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית – מִי שֶׁפּוֹגֵם בּוֹ הוּא גַּם כֵּן בִּבְחִינַת עַבְדוּת (section י)"], "mirror": "Package 5 (Prayer and Prophecy) is the good mirror — these three evils exile prayer; removing them enables tefillah.", "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מצרים"], "reason": "Egypt is explicitly called 'beit avadim' = bechinas avdut, equating it with avoda zara. It is not a historical reference here but an ontological identity: Egypt IS the house of servitude = avoda zara. One bechina edge."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עזות (מנהיג שקר)"], "reason": "False leaders who rise through brazenness are connected to this evil package because azut is called 'malkhuta b'lo taga' — the anti-Yerushalayim kingdom, and they fall before the baal tefillah because all are 'borrowers' from the divine root. Azut shares the nature of servitude/sitra achra."}]}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 1, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שׁוֹפָר", "label_en": "The Shofar", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The shofar is the structural/convergent instrument that the Torah uses at its conclusion to summarize all the teaching's domains. It is explicitly called bechina of: the heart (lev), yirah (fear), nevuah (prophecy), tefillah (prayer), and siach hasadeh (field plants). It is not a cause of these things — it IS all of them simultaneously. It is neutral in the sense that it is a physical instrument that carries all of these qualities. The shofar is the node where all packages converge, making it the Torah's unifying symbol.", "dominant_node": "שופר", "nodes": ["שופר"], "node_degrees": {"שופר": 5}, "source_bechinas": ["שׁוֹפָר זֶה בְּחִינַת הַלֵּב, שֶׁנִּזּוֹן מִשּׁוּפְרָא דְּשׁוּפְרָא", "גַּם שׁוֹפָר הוּא בְּחִינַת יִרְאָה, בִּבְחִינַת הֲיִתָּקַע שׁוֹפָר בְּעִיר וְעָם לֹא יֶחֱרָדוּ", "גַּם שׁוֹפָר הוּא בְּחִינַת נְבוּאָה, בִּבְחִינַת הָרֵם כַּשּׁוֹפָר קוֹלֶךָ", "גַּם שׁוֹפָר הוּא בְּחִינַת תְּפִלָּה, בִּבְחִינַת מִן הַמֵּצַר קָרָאתִי יָהּ", "גַּם הוּא בְּחִינַת שִׂיחַ הַשָּׂדֶה, שֶׁחוֹזְרִין כֹּחָם לְתוֹךְ הַתְּפִלָּה (section conclusion)"], "mirror": "As a neutral convergent instrument, the shofar has no evil mirror.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּמָשִׁיחַ", "label_en": "Prayer and Mashiach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of תְּפִלָּה as the supreme weapon and life-force of Mashiach. The Torah opens: 'עִקַּר כְּלֵי-זֵינוֹ שֶׁל מָשִׁיחַ הוּא הַתְּפִלָּה'. Everything here IS prayer from a different angle: חֹטֶם (the nose = organ of prayer), כְּבוֹד אֵל (the glory-name of prayer), חֶרֶב/בְּחַרְבִּי (sword = prayer per Rashi), בְּכוֹרָה (firstborn status = the service of prayer), אֱמוֹר (speech addressed to God = prayer), כְּלֵי-זֵינוֹ (weapon = prayer), מָשִׁיחַ (whose essence is prayer). The Tzaddik-Moshe cluster (צַדִּיק, מֹשֶׁה, מָשִׁיחַ, מָאוֹר הַגָּדוֹל, צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר, דָּג) is here because 'כָּל צַדִּיק שֶׁבַּדּוֹר הוּא בְּחִינַת מֹשֶׁה מָשִׁיחַ' — the Tzaddik IS the aspect of Mashiach IS the aspect of prayer. The Mishkan cluster (אֵיבָר מֵהַשְּׁכִינָה, אֶבְרֵי הַמִּשְׁכָּן) is here because each prayer IS a limb of the Shechina = a board of the Mishkan. The two-mouth cluster (פִּי שְׁנַיִם, שְׁתֵּי פִּיּוֹת, חֶרֶב פִּיפִיּוֹת) is here because prayer IS double-mouthed (praise + petition). The sun/moon cluster (מָאוֹר הַגָּדוֹל = Tzaddik/Moshe, מָאוֹר הַקָּטָן = prayer/small people) mirrors the way the Tzaddik illuminates prayer.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "איבר מהשכינה", "אברי המשכן", "חטם", "כבוד אל", "מאור הקטן", "אנשים קטנים (במעלה)", "פי שנים", "שתי פיות", "חרב פיפיות", "אמור", "בכור", "חרב", "כלי זינו של משיח", "משיח", "משה", "מאור הגדול", "אנשים גדולים (במעלה)", "צדיק", "דג", "צדיק הדור"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 10, "משיח": 2, "משה": 3, "צדיק": 1, "מאור הגדול": 2, "מאור הקטן": 2, "איבר מהשכינה": 2, "אברי המשכן": 1, "כלי זינו של משיח": 1, "חטם": 1, "כבוד אל": 1, "חרב": 1, "בכור": 1, "אמור": 1, "פי שנים": 2, "שתי פיות": 2, "חרב פיפיות": 1, "אנשים גדולים (במעלה)": 1, "אנשים קטנים (במעלה)": 1, "דג": 1, "צדיק הדור": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component M (דג / צדיק הדור) merged in: text states 'חָזִינָא לְהַאי כַּוְרָא הוּא בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר, הַנִּקְרָא דָּג, זֶה בְּחִינַת מֹשֶׁה מָשִׁיחַ' — the fish IS the Tzaddik IS Moshe-Mashiach IS prayer. Same essential nature as the rest of this package."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּמְשִׁיחַ — עֲבוֹדַת הַתְּפִלָּה", "label_en": "Prayer and Messianic Rectification", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the same essential nature: they are expressions of the תפלה (prayer) domain — its physical embodiments (אברי המשכן, חטם, כבוד אל, מאור הקטן), its weapons and power (חרב פיפיות, פי שנים, כלי זינו של משיח), its human carriers (בכור, אמור, אנשים קטנים, אנשים גדולים), and its Messianic fulfillment (משיח, משה, צדיק). Each is an aspect of the same redemptive force of prayer that builds the Shekhinah and brings Mashiach.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "איבר מהשכינה", "אברי המשכן", "חטם", "כבוד אל", "מאור הקטן", "אנשים קטנים", "פי שנים", "שתי פיות", "חרב פיפיות", "אמור", "בכור", "חרב", "כלי זינו של משיח", "משיח", "משה", "מאור הגדול", "אנשים גדולים", "צדיק", "דג", "צדיק הדור", "פניך", "רחמים"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 10, "איבר מהשכינה": 2, "אברי המשכן": 1, "חטם": 1, "כבוד אל": 1, "מאור הקטן": 2, "אנשים קטנים": 1, "פי שנים": 2, "שתי פיות": 2, "חרב פיפיות": 1, "אמור": 1, "בכור": 1, "חרב": 1, "כלי זינו של משיח": 1, "משיח": 2, "משה": 3, "מאור הגדול": 2, "אנשים גדולים": 1, "צדיק": 1, "דג": 1, "צדיק הדור": 1, "פניך": 1, "רחמים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["דג", "צדיק הדור"], "reason": "The isolated pair דג/צדיק הדור (id 2543) — the Tzaddik of the generation is called a 'fish' in the sea (כַּוְרָא). The proof text is drawn from the same narrative context as the Mishkan/prayer cluster: 'חָזִינָא לְהַאי כַּוְרָא הוּא בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר'. צדיק הדור = same entity as צדיק/משה in this Torah's framework. Shared nature: the צדיק who collects and elevates prayers is an aspect of the same messianic-prayer domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פניך", "רחמים"], "reason": "The pair פניך/רחמים (id 2735) — 'הִסְתַּרְתָּ פָנֶיךָ' = aspect of mercy. In Torah 2's context, 'turning the face' (פניך) represents divine mercy (רחמים) that responds to prayer and the Shekhinah's building. This is the same domain of divine response to prayer as the rest of Package 1. Shared nature: the responsive dimension of the prayer-Shekhinah relationship."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּנְשָׁמוֹת", "label_en": "Torah and Souls", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of Torah as the medium that carries souls (נְשָׁמוֹת). The Torah says: 'הַשָּׁמַיִם מְסַפְּרִים כְּבוֹד אֵל, הַיְנוּ הַתּוֹרָה שֶׁהוּא אֵשׁ וּמַיִם, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת הַנְּשָׁמוֹת.' Torah IS fire-and-water (אֵשׁ וּמַיִם) which IS souls. The heavens (שָׁמַיִם) = fire-and-water (the very etymology of שמים = אש + מים), and thus the heavens too share this nature. כֹּהֲנִים (priests) = Torah = souls: 'כֹּהֲנִים הֵם בְּחִינַת תּוֹרָה, בְּחִינַת נְשָׁמוֹת.' Souls are called עֲשָׂבִין (grasses/herbs), the organic dimension of their vitality. These concepts form the Torah-soul cluster that enters into prayer, illuminating it from within.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אש ומים", "שמים", "נשמות", "עשבים", "כהן"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 3, "אש ומים": 2, "שמים": 1, "נשמות": 2, "עשבים": 1, "כהן": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תּוֹרָה וּנְשָׁמוֹת", "label_en": "Torah and Souls", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of Torah as life-force: Torah is fire-and-water (אש ומים), its students are souls (נשמות) and plants (עשבים) growing in the divine garden, the Heavens (שמים) proclaim its glory, and Kohanim (כהן) guard and teach it. All are expressions of the Torah's spiritual vitality and its role in carrying souls.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אש ומים", "שמים", "נשמות", "עשבים", "כהן", "ספינה", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 3, "אש ומים": 2, "שמים": 1, "נשמות": 2, "עשבים": 1, "כהן": 1, "ספינה": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ספינה", "חסד"], "reason": "The pair ספינה/חסד (id 1210): sailors are mostly pious (חסידים). The proof text connects this to the Torah context: the ship (ספינה) is an aspect of chesed/kindness, and in Torah 2 chesed is the domain of those who support Torah scholars (חסד = הלכות per the Torah, though that concept belongs to a different cluster). Here ספינה represents those who make Torah possible through kindness/support. The essential nature shared: chesed that enables Torah. Merged into the Torah/Souls package rather than standalone."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "מִשְׁפָּט וְיַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "Judgment and Jacob — the Middle Column", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of מִשְׁפָּט as the straight, middle-column principle that allows precise spiritual aim. The Torah states: 'מִשְׁפָּט הוּא עַמּוּדָא דְּאֶמְצָעִיתָא' — judgment is the central pillar of balance. מִשְׁפָּט IS עַמּוּדָא דְּאֶמְצָעִיתָא (same nature). מִשְׁפָּט IS עֵינִין (eyes) — 'מִשְׁפָּט הוּא בְּחִינַת עֵינִין, עֵין מִשְׁפָּט.' עֵינִין IS יַעֲקֹב — 'עֵין יַעֲקֹב.' יַעֲקֹב IS מִשְׁפָּט — 'מִשְׁפָּטֵיךָ לְמֶלֶךְ תֵּן, מִשְׁפָּט וּצְדָקָה בְּיַעֲקֹב.' אַהֲרֹן IS מִשְׁפָּט — he bore the מִשְׁפַּט of Israel on his breastplate. 'אֲנִי' (the first person) IS מִשְׁפָּט — 'וַאֲנִי נָתַתִּי לְךָ... אֲנִי דַּיְקָא, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת מִשְׁפָּט.' These are all aspects of the quality of balanced, discerning judgment that calibrates prayer and charity to the exact right measure.", "dominant_node": "משפט", "nodes": ["משפט", "אהרן", "אני", "יעקב", "עינים", "עמודא דאמצעיתא"], "node_degrees": {"משפט": 5, "אהרן": 1, "אני": 1, "יעקב": 2, "עינים": 2, "עמודא דאמצעיתא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִשְׁפָּט וְיַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "Righteous Judgment and Jacob", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes are all aspects of the same righteous faculty of judgment (משפט): it is the central pillar (עמודא דאמצעיתא), the eyes of Jacob (עין יעקב), Aaron's quality of bearing Israel's judgment, and the Aleph-Nun-Yod (אני) of true self-possession. All share the essential nature of balanced, righteous discernment that keeps prayer on target.", "dominant_node": "משפט", "nodes": ["משפט", "אהרן", "אני", "יעקב", "עינים", "עמודא דאמצעיתא"], "node_degrees": {"משפט": 5, "אהרן": 1, "אני": 1, "יעקב": 2, "עינים": 2, "עמודא דאמצעיתא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית וְיוֹסֵף", "label_en": "Guarding the Covenant and Joseph", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית — the guarding of the covenant of purity as the channel through which the weapon of prayer is received. יוֹסֵף IS שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית — the paradigm of sexual purity: 'בְּחִינַת יוֹסֵף, הַיְנוּ שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית.' טָהֳרָה (purity) IS שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית — purity and covenant-keeping are the same essential quality (the Zohar: 'עִקָּרָא דְּיִצְרָא בִּישָׁא עַל עֲרַיָן'). לֹא יִטַּמָּא IS שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית — the commandment 'do not defile yourself among your people' is the aspect of covenant-guarding. חֹק IS בְּרִית — 'כִּי חֹק לְיִשְׂרָאֵל הוּא, בְּחִינַת בְּרִית, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: חֹק בִּשְׁאֵרוֹ שָׂם' — the statute placed in the flesh IS the covenant. This package represents the bodily/spiritual purification that is prerequisite to receiving the prayer-weapon.", "dominant_node": "תקון הברית", "nodes": ["תקון הברית", "לא יטמא", "טהרה", "יוסף", "חק", "ברית"], "node_degrees": {"תקון הברית": 3, "לא יטמא": 1, "טהרה": 1, "יוסף": 1, "חק": 1, "ברית": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component H (חֹק / בְּרִית) merged in: the proof 'כִּי חֹק לְיִשְׂרָאֵל הוּא, בְּחִינַת בְּרִית, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: חֹק בִּשְׁאֵרוֹ שָׂם' directly equates חֹק with בְּרִית, and the entire purity cluster IS covenant. Same essential nature."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט וּמַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת", "label_en": "Distorted Judgment and Foreign Thoughts", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes are all expressions of the same evil domain: corruption of judgment (קלקול המשפט) through bribery (שחד) causes distortion of the eyes/vision (קלקול לעינים), which manifests as foreign thoughts during prayer (מחשבות זרות שבתפלה) — described as clouds covering the eyes (עננין דמכסין על עינין). Blinding (יעור) from bribery is the mechanism. All share the nature of the evil force that prevents proper prayer by darkening inner vision.", "dominant_node": "קלקול לעינים", "nodes": ["קלקול לעינים", "מחשבות זרות שבתפלה", "עננין דמכסין על עינין", "יעור", "שחד", "קלקול המשפט"], "node_degrees": {"קלקול לעינים": 2, "מחשבות זרות שבתפלה": 2, "עננין דמכסין על עינין": 1, "יעור": 1, "שחד": 1, "קלקול המשפט": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שחד", "קלקול המשפט"], "reason": "The pair שחד/קלקול המשפט (id 2633) is the root cause of the entire evil cluster. Bribery (שחד) is the essential nature of distorted judgment (קלקול המשפט) — they are two expressions of the same evil reality. The same proof text passage shows that קלקול המשפט directly causes קלקול לעינים and מחשבות זרות. Merged into Package 4 as the causal root within the evil domain."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "הֲכָנַת הַשְּׁכִינָה לְזִוּוּג", "label_en": "Preparing the Shechina for Union", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of the Shechina's preparation for union (זִוּוּג) through the accumulation of prayers. The Torah expounds on the Agadic fish-story: 'אֲפִינָן וּבַשְׁלִינָן' (we baked and cooked it) = 'כִּי אֲפִיָּה וּבִשּׁוּל הֵם הֲכָנָה לָאֲכִילָה, לִבְחִינַת זִוּוּג.' Baking (אֲפִיָּה) IS preparation-for-zivug. Cooking (בִּשּׁוּל) IS preparation-for-zivug. Eating (אֲכִילָה) IS zivug — 'כִּי אִם הַלֶּחֶם אֲשֶׁר הוּא אוֹכֵל' refers to Yosef's eating = zivug. הֲכָנָה לָאֲכִילָה IS זִוּוּג (they describe the same reality: the Shechina ripening toward divine union). This package describes the teleological end-state toward which the accumulation of prayers drives: when enough prayers accumulate, the Shechina is fully prepared and zivug occurs, bringing redemption.", "dominant_node": "הכנה לזווג", "nodes": ["אפיה", "בשול", "הכנה לזווג", "אכילה", "זווג"], "node_degrees": {"הכנה לזווג": 2, "אפיה": 1, "בשול": 1, "אכילה": 1, "זווג": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component G (אֲכִילָה / זִוּוּג) merged in: the proof 'לָאֲכִילָה, לִבְחִינַת זִוּוּג, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: כִּי אִם הַלֶּחֶם אֲשֶׁר הוּא אוֹכֵל' equates eating with zivug. Eating IS the culmination of the baking/cooking preparation (הֲכָנָה), making it the same thematic domain: the Shechina's progresssive preparation for and arrival at divine union."]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תִּקּוּן הַבְּרִית וְיוֹסֵף", "label_en": "Covenant Rectification and Joseph", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of שמירת הברית (guarding the covenant): Joseph (יוסף) is the paradigm of covenant-keeping, purity (טהרה) is its defining quality, and 'לא יטמא' (not becoming impure) is its essential demand. All are expressions of the same domain of sexual holiness that enables Mashiach's weapon (prayer).", "dominant_node": "תקון הברית", "nodes": ["תקון הברית", "לא יטמא", "טהרה", "יוסף", "חק", "ברית"], "node_degrees": {"תקון הברית": 3, "לא יטמא": 1, "טהרה": 1, "יוסף": 1, "חק": 1, "ברית": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חק", "ברית"], "reason": "The pair חק/ברית (id 584): 'כִּי חֹק לְיִשְׂרָאֵל הוּא, בְּחִינַת בְּרִית, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב חֹק בִּשְׁאֵרוֹת שָׂם'. The חק (statute) is the essential nature of the ברית — they are two names for the same covenantal demand. This belongs squarely with the ברית/תקון הברית package. Merged here because ברית shares essential nature with תקון הברית."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט וּמַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת", "label_en": "Corruption of Judgment and Foreign Thoughts — the Evil Mirror of Mishpat", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts are the evil mirror of Package 3 (מִשְׁפָּט וְיַעֲקֹב). Where Package 3 is the good, centered, eye-opening quality of mishpat, this package represents its perversion and corruption. קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט IS שֹּׁחַד (bribery) — 'כִּי הַשֹּׁחַד יְעַוֵּר עֵינֵי חֲכָמִים' means that taking a bribe IS the corruption of judgment (same nature — the act defines the state). קִלְקוּל לָעֵינִין IS מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת שֶׁבַּתְּפִלָּה — the blinding of the eyes IS foreign thoughts in prayer (same essential nature: spiritual blindness = inability to see straight in prayer). יְעַוֵּר (blinding) IS קִלְקוּל לָעֵינִין — the act of blinding IS the state of corrupted vision. עֲנָנִין דִּמְכַסִּין עַל עֵינִין IS מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת שֶׁבַּתְּפִלָּה — the clouds covering the eyes IS foreign thoughts (same metaphysical reality described in two registers). These concepts share the nature of 'spiritual blindness induced by moral perversion,' the anti-pole to the clear vision of mishpat.", "dominant_node": "קלקול לעינים", "nodes": ["קלקול לעינים", "מחשבות זרות שבתפלה", "יעור", "עננין דמכסין על עינין", "שחד", "קלקול המשפט"], "node_degrees": {"קלקול לעינים": 3, "מחשבות זרות שבתפלה": 2, "יעור": 1, "עננין דמכסין על עינין": 1, "שחד": 1, "קלקול המשפט": 2}, "merge_notes": ["Component L (שֹּׁחַד / קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט) merged in: שֹּׁחַד IS bechina of קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט (edge 2633, reference=2, type=bechina). Both are aspects of the same evil domain: the perversion of mishpat that leads to spiritual blindness. They share the same essential nature as Components D (קִלְקוּל לָעֵינִין / מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת). The L-to-D connection appears as eitza in the dataset (edge 2635, type=eitza) but thematically שֹּׁחַד/קִלְקוּל הַמִּשְׁפָּט and קִלְקוּל לָעֵינִין/מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת are all aspects of the same evil: the corruption of spiritual vision."]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 2, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הֲכָנָה לְזִוּוּג — בִּשּׁוּל וַאֲפִיָּה", "label_en": "Preparation for Divine Union — Baking and Cooking", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These nodes describe the structural process of spiritual preparation: baking (אפיה) and cooking (בשול) are neutral physical processes that serve as metaphors for prayer-activity that prepares the Shekhinah for union (זווג). The proof text uses the kitchen metaphor for the accumulation of prayers building the Shekhinah's stature. The eating-union (אכילה/זווג) represents the fulfillment of that preparation. All share the nature of preparatory process toward spiritual union.", "dominant_node": "הכנה לזווג", "nodes": ["הכנה לזווג", "אפיה", "בשול", "אכילה", "זווג", "פנה ערף", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"הכנה לזווג": 2, "אפיה": 1, "בשול": 1, "אכילה": 1, "זווג": 1, "פנה ערף": 1, "דין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אכילה", "זווג"], "reason": "The pair אכילה/זווג (id 1053): 'לָאֲכִילָה, לִבְחִינַת זִוּוּג' — eating is an aspect of union. The proof text for אפיה/בשול also references this same verse (Genesis 39: 'כִּי אִם הַלֶּחֶם אֲשֶׁר הוּא אוֹכֵל'). These are two names for the same experiential reality of the Shekhinah receiving prayer and achieving union. Merged here as the fulfillment dimension of the preparation cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פנה ערף", "דין"], "reason": "The pair פנה ערף/דין (id 740): 'פָנָה עֹרֶף שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת דִּין' — turning the back of the neck is an aspect of strict judgment/divine absence. This represents the inverse state to the union (זווג) — when prayers fail to reach their destination and God 'turns away' (פנה ערף), strict judgment (דין) reigns. Thematically this could be placed with the evil cluster (Package 4), but structurally it is the anti-union counterpart to Package 6. The shared essential nature is: the structural conditions of the divine-human relational axis. Placed here as the negative pole of the union/preparation domain."}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 2, "label": "חֶסֶד וְרַחֲמִים בְּגָלוּת", "label_en": "Chesed and Mercy in Exile — the Divine Face and the Ship", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of the divine attribute-structure in exile: the two faces of God's engagement with Israel in galut, and the grace (chesed) that preserves them. פָּנֶיךָ IS רַחֲמִים — 'הִסְתַּרְתָּ פָנֶיךָ, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת רַחֲמִים' — God's face is the aspect of mercy. פָּנָה עֹרֶף IS דִּין — 'פָּנָה עֹרֶף שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת דִּין' — God's turning away is the aspect of strict judgment. These two pairs (panim/rachamin and oref/din) are explicitly mirror aspects of the same divine reality in exile: one is God facing us (rachamin), the other is God's back to us (din). The ship/chesed pair (סְפִינְתָּא IS חֶסֶד, 'הַסַּפָּנִין רֻבָּן חֲסִידִים') anchors this package: the 'ship' that saves us in the stormy exile IS divine chesed without which we would drown. This package is marked neutral because these divine attributes (chesed, din, rachamin) are structural features of the divine-cosmic order — they appear in both redemptive and punitive registers depending on context.", "dominant_node": "רחמים", "nodes": ["פניך", "רחמים", "פנה ערף", "דין", "ספינה", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"פניך": 1, "רחמים": 1, "פנה ערף": 1, "דין": 1, "ספינה": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Components I (פָּנָה עֹרֶף / דִּין), J (סְפִינְתָּא / חֶסֶד), and K (פָּנֶיךָ / רַחֲמִים) merged into one package. All three are aspects of the same thematic domain: God's relationship with Israel in galut expressed through attribute-language (face/back = mercy/judgment) and the metaphor of salvation (the ship = chesed). פָּנֶיךָ/רַחֲמִים and פָּנָה עֹרֶף/דִּין are explicitly presented as the two sides of the same exilic reality in the same paragraph of the Torah source. The ship/chesed pair belongs here because it resolves the din/oref situation — chesed is what saves from the stern judgment of galut."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 3, "label": "נְגִינָה וּמַלְכוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה", "label_en": "Holy Music and the Kingdom of Holiness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central thematic cluster of Torah #3 is the identity of holy music (נגינה) with the building (בנין) of malkhut deKedushah. The two holy birds (שתי צפרים חיות טהורות) ARE the building of holy malkhut; the root of holy music IS the mochin/da'at of malkhut deKedushah; the cherubim ARE the two birds; the musician/cantor (חזן, מנגן) IS prophecy because music draws from the same source as prophecy; the frog (צפרדע) IS the bird of knowledge (צפור דעה), the allegorical singer of the whole Talmudic narrative. All these nodes share the essential nature of 'holy music as spiritual royalty.' They are all different expressions of the same metaphysical reality: the dimension of malkhut where the word of prophecy and song unite.", "dominant_node": "נבואה", "nodes": ["נגינה", "בנין המלכות", "שתי צפרים חיות טהורות", "בנין המלכות דקדשה", "שרש הנגינה שבקדשה", "מחין ודעת של מלכות דקדשה", "חזן", "מנגן", "נבואה", "צפרדע", "צפור דעה", "כרובים", "שתי צפרים"], "node_degrees": {"נבואה": 2, "נגינה": 1, "בנין המלכות": 1, "שתי צפרים חיות טהורות": 1, "בנין המלכות דקדשה": 1, "שרש הנגינה שבקדשה": 1, "מחין ודעת של מלכות דקדשה": 1, "חזן": 1, "מנגן": 1, "צפרדע": 1, "צפור דעה": 1, "כרובים": 1, "שתי צפרים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC3 {שתי צפרים חיות טהורות, בנין המלכות דקדשה} merged here: the holy birds ARE the building of malkhut deKedushah — same essential reality as CC2's נגינה=בנין המלכות.", "CC6 {שרש הנגינה שבקדשה, מחין ודעת של מלכות דקדשה} merged here: the root of holy music is explicitly identified as the mochin/da'at of holy malkhut — core of this package.", "CC8 {חזן, מנגן, נבואה} merged here: the cantor/musician is called חזן from the word חזון (prophecy) because music draws from the prophetic source — same holy-music-as-kingdom domain.", "CC9 {צפרדע, צפור דעה} merged here: the frog in the aggadic narrative is interpreted as צפור דעה (bird of knowledge), the symbolic singer whose throat-call (אקרא דהגרוניא) frames the entire Torah — it is the allegorical embodiment of holy neginah.", "CC11 {כרובים, שתי צפרים} merged here: prophecy draws from the cherubim who ARE the two holy birds — explicit bechina in the text: 'הנבואה נמשכת מן הכרובים שהם בחינת שתי צפרים'."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 3, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פִּדְיוֹן וְהַמְתָּקַת הַדִּין", "label_en": "Redemption and the Sweetening of Judgment", "polarity": "good", "reason": "פִּדְיוֹן (redemption) and הַמְתָּקָה (sweetening) are not two sequential steps — they are two names for the same spiritual act. The Torah uses them interchangeably throughout: 'לַעֲשׂוֹת פִּדְיוֹן לְהַמְתִּיק הַדִּין', 'עַל-יְדֵי פִּדְיוֹן וְהַהַמְתָּקָה'. Neither produces the other; each IS the other from a different angle. The cholam vowel (חוֹלָם) is drawn into this package because the Torah states explicitly 'חוֹלָם הוּא בְּחִינַת הַמְתָּקָה' — the vowel IS sweetening, not a symbol of it or a cause of it. The three Havayot (שָׁלֹשׁ הֲוָיוֹת) are part of this same cluster because the gematria of cholam equals three Havayot, establishing that cholam and the three Havayot share the same essential nature (they are the kabbalistic mechanism by which the sweetening operates). The word וְרַפֹּא with cholam also belongs here: 'וְרַפֹּא בְּחוֹלָם... בְּחִינַת הַמְתָּקָה'. All of these — פדיון, המתקה, חולם, שלש הויות, ורפא-בחולם — are different facets of the same reality: the act of nullifying and sweetening the harsh decree.", "dominant_node": "פדיון", "nodes": ["פדיון", "המתקה", "המתקת הדין", "חולם", "שלש הויות", "ורפא בחולם"], "node_degrees": {"פדיון": 3, "המתקה": 4, "המתקת הדין": 2, "חולם": 3, "שלש הויות": 2, "ורפא בחולם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ורפא בחולם", "חולם"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly says 'וְרַפֹּא בְּחוֹלָם, בְּחִינַת הַמְתָּקָה'. The word with the cholam vowel IS the aspect of sweetening. 'ורפא-בחולם' and 'חולם' are two formulations of the same node — the cholam vowel as the medium of sweetening. Merged into Package 1."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 3, "label": "לַיְלָה וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל פֶּה", "label_en": "Night and Oral Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of 'night as the dimension of Oral Torah.' Gemara IS night (from the Midrash about Moses who identified night by learning Oral Torah). The raven/crow (עורב) IS night (מלשון ערבית — from the word 'evening'). Torah she-be'al peh IS night — these are all three bechinas of the same single reality. The pair שמים/קול is merged here because in the text the verse 'ki gadol me'al shamayim chasdecha' — shamayim = kol (voice), and the voice is what is repaired through night-learning; it belongs to the same domain of 'night transforms voice' rather than the music/malkhut domain.", "dominant_node": "לילה", "nodes": ["גמרא", "לילה", "עורב", "תורה שבעל פה", "שמים", "קול"], "node_degrees": {"לילה": 3, "גמרא": 1, "עורב": 1, "תורה שבעל פה": 1, "שמים": 1, "קול": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC10 {שמים, קול} merged here: the verse 'כי גדול מעל שמים חסדך' — shamayim = kol (voice) in the Torah's reasoning; the kol that is repaired by night-learning. Shamayim-as-kol belongs to the night/oral-torah dimension rather than the holy-malkhut/neginah dimension, since the verse is cited to explain how the chesed drawn through night learning (=laylah) repairs the voice."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 3, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רְפוּאָה וּרְשׁוּת הָרוֹפֵא", "label_en": "Healing and the Physician's Permission", "polarity": "good", "reason": "רְפוּאָה (healing/medicine), יְרַפֵּא-בְּצֵירֵי (the word with tzeirei vowel), and רְשׁוּת לְרוֹפֵא (the permission granted to the physician) all share the same essential nature: they are expressions of the domain of actual healing that becomes possible AFTER the sweetening. The Torah states 'יְרַפֵּא בְּצֵירֵי, בְּחִינַת... רְפוּאוֹת' — the tzeirei form of the word IS the aspect of medicine/healing. The physician's permission ('רְשׁוּת לְרוֹפֵא לְרַפְּאוֹת') is not merely a consequence of the sweetening — it IS the state of open healing space, the domain in which the illness no longer has a fixed decree governing it. All three are different expressions of the same reality: the opened, unlocked space of healing.", "dominant_node": "רפואה", "nodes": ["רפואה", "ירפא בצירי", "רשות לרופא לרפאות"], "node_degrees": {"רפואה": 3, "ירפא בצירי": 1, "רשות לרופא לרפאות": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 3, "label": "צִפֳּרִים שֶׁבַּקְּלִפָּה וּנְגִינָה רְשָׁעָה", "label_en": "Klipah Birds and Evil Music", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. Just as holy music draws from the two holy birds who feed at the place where prophets nurse, evil music draws from the birds of the klipah who suck from 'the breasts of malkhut [of impurity].' The birds of the klipah ARE the mochin of malkhut diklipah — this is the exact structural mirror of the holy cluster (שתי צפרים חיות=בנין המלכות דקדשה, and צפרים שבקלפה=מחין שבמלכות דקלפה). The evil music that comes 'from behind holiness' (מאחר עלות, מאחורי הקדשה) belongs in this evil cluster: it IS the quality of being situated at the back of holiness rather than at the source.", "dominant_node": "צפרים שבקלפה", "nodes": ["צפרים שבקלפה", "מחין שבמלכות דקלפה", "נגינה שאינו בקדשה", "מאחר עלות מאחורי הקדשה"], "node_degrees": {"צפרים שבקלפה": 1, "מחין שבמלכות דקלפה": 1, "נגינה שאינו בקדשה": 1, "מאחר עלות מאחורי הקדשה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC5 {נגינה שאינו בקדשה, מאחר עלות מאחורי הקדשה} merged here: the evil/non-holy music IS the quality of 'being at the back of holiness' (מאחר עלות = מאחורי הקדשה), making it the same domain as the klipah-birds cluster. This is the evil neginah that mirrors Package 1's holy neginah."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 3, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִדַּת הַדִּין וּמֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת", "label_en": "The Attribute of Strict Judgment and Constricted Consciousness", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "מִדַּת הַדִּין (the attribute of strict judgment), גְּזֵרָה (the decree), and מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת (constricted consciousness) all share the same essential nature: they are expressions of the domain of divine constriction and harsh judgment. The Torah states 'מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת שֶׁיּוֹרְדִין בַּגָּרוֹן' — the three Elokim names at the throat ARE the mochin d'katnut. Mochin d'katnut is not the cause of the harsh decree; it IS the inner nature of the constricted state that gives rise to harsh judgment. The גְּזֵרָה (divine decree that illness continues until a specific day, medicine, and physician converge) IS middat hadin in action. The three Elokim names (שְׁלֹשָׁה אֱלֹקִים שֶׁבַּגָּרוֹן) are the kabbalistic embodiment of this constricted domain, seated in the throat, needing sweetening via the three Havayot. This package is the evil mirror of Package 1 (פדיון/המתקה): Package 1 sweetens and dissolves Package 3.", "dominant_node": "מדת הדין", "nodes": ["מדת הדין", "גזרה", "מחין דקטנות", "שלשה אלקים שבגרון"], "node_degrees": {"מדת הדין": 3, "גזרה": 2, "מחין דקטנות": 2, "שלשה אלקים שבגרון": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מחין דקטנות", "שלשה אלקים שבגרון"], "reason": "The Torah states explicitly that the three Elokim names in the throat ARE the mochin d'katnut ('שְׁלֹשָׁה אֱלֹקִים שֶׁבַּגָּרוֹן, שֶׁהֵם מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת'). These two nodes share the same essential nature — one is the functional state (constricted consciousness), the other is the kabbalistic name-configuration that embodies it. They are merged into Package 3."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 3, "label": "אַבְרָהָם, חֶסֶד וְהָאִילָן", "label_en": "Abraham, Chesed, and the Tree", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The tree (אילן) in Rabbah bar bar Chanah's story is interpreted by the Maharsha as Abraham ('vayita eshel' — he planted a tree — = chesed). Therefore: אילן IS Abraham IS chesed. These three are different expressions of a single archetype: the inexhaustible protective power of chesed. This is the 'tree' whose strength Rabbah marvels at — 'ta chazei kamah nafish cheileh de'ilana' — because the thread of chesed drawn from Abraham's quality protects even one who learns shelo lishmah. This is a small but self-contained package representing the chesed-protection axis of the Torah.", "dominant_node": "אברהם", "nodes": ["אילן", "אברהם", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"אברהם": 2, "אילן": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְהַתִּקּוּן — Kingship and Its Repair", "label_en": "Malkhut and Its Restoration", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This vast cluster orbits מלכות (divine kingship / speech-dimension) as the central concept of Torah #4. Malkhut is the aspect of divinity that can fall into exile (among the nations, into impure letter-combinations, into sin) and must be restored. The mechanism of restoration is ודוי דברים (verbal confession) before a תלמיד חכם, who is a bechina of משה, who is a bechina of אין (nothingness). Malkhut is equated with אותיות (letters of speech), דבר/דברים (words/leadership), ישות (existence), רצונות (divine wills/forms), תמונות (letter-shapes), and bears names like אלהים (divine name of kingship/judgment), יהודה (who confessed), מואב (from whom David came). The תלמיד חכם cluster (פרשת דרכים, אבוקה של אור, שארית, תורה, חלב) forms the positive mechanism of repair. The אש / תורה bridge is critical: Torah is fire from which malkhut originates, while חמימות היצר is the evil fire that pulled malkhut into impurity — both share the nature of אש. Merged into this package: צדיק, גדר, 'ברית מלח עולם' (the tzaddik who protects like a fence and a covenant of salt); צדיק הדור/אם (the tzaddik nurses Israel like a mother with Torah-milk); ישראל/דג (Israel as the fish in the parable of the whale, struggling with impure prayer); מצוה/התחברות (mitzvah = connection/bonding, which reconnects broken bones); 'והולכתי עורים'/הצדיק הורה (the tzaddik guides the blind on the right path); עמוד השחר/צדקה (charity as the dawn that delivers from evil — the second of three approaches to the tzaddik).", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "אותיות", "אלהים", "דבר אחד לדור", "ודוי דברים", "חשיבות", "יהודה", "ישות", "מואב", "רצונות", "שמו", "תמונות", "דבר", "דברים", "רעוין", "שם", "דין", "מושל", "מנהיג", "יום המיתה", "מיתה", "פרשת דרכים", "תלמיד חכם", "אבוקה של אור", "שארית", "תלמידי חכמים", "משה", "אין", "תלת מאה", "גיא", "תורה", "אש", "חלב", "חמימות היצר", "צדיק", "גדר", "\"ברית מלח עולם\"", "צדיק הדור", "אם", "ישראל", "דג", "מצוה", "התחברות", "\"והולכתי עורים בדרך לא ידעו\"", "הצדיק הורה לו דרך ישר", "עמוד השחר", "צדקה"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 15, "תלמיד חכם": 5, "ודוי דברים": 4, "אלהים": 3, "דבר": 3, "תורה": 3, "משה": 3, "אש": 2, "פרשת דרכים": 2, "אין": 2, "יום המיתה": 2, "מיתה": 2, "שמו": 2, "אותיות": 1, "דבר אחד לדור": 1, "חשיבות": 1, "יהודה": 1, "ישות": 1, "מואב": 1, "רצונות": 1, "תמונות": 1, "דברים": 1, "רעוין": 1, "שם": 1, "דין": 1, "מושל": 1, "מנהיג": 1, "אבוקה של אור": 1, "שארית": 1, "תלמידי חכמים": 1, "תלת מאה": 1, "גיא": 1, "חלב": 1, "חמימות היצר": 1, "צדיק": 2, "גדר": 1, "\"ברית מלח עולם\"": 1, "צדיק הדור": 1, "אם": 1, "ישראל": 1, "דג": 1, "מצוה": 1, "התחברות": 1, "\"והולכתי עורים בדרך לא ידעו\"": 1, "הצדיק הורה לו דרך ישר": 1, "עמוד השחר": 1, "צדקה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 9 (צדיק, גדר, 'ברית מלח עולם'): The tzaddik is described as a fence (גדר) protecting Israel's breaches and as an eternal salt-covenant (ברית מלח עולם) preserving against corruption. This is structurally identical to the תלמיד חכם / משה cluster — all three terms describe the same righteous guide who repairs malkhut. Merged by shared nature: the righteous teacher who heals.", "Component 10 (צדיק הדור, אם): The tzaddik of the generation is called 'mother' because he nurses Israel with the light of his Torah (Torah = milk). This connects directly to the Torah/תלמיד חכם axis and the pastoral-nursing metaphor of spiritual guidance. Merged by shared nature: the tzaddik as spiritual nurturer.", "Component 11 (ישראל, דג): In the Rabba bar bar Chana parable, Israel is compared to fish (דגים) — a neutral metaphor describing the Jewish people as those who elevate malkhut. Since the parable is entirely framed around the malkhut-restoration theme of this Torah, the fish-Israel identity belongs here. Merged by shared narrative context.", "Component 16 (מצוה, התחברות): 'Mitzvah' derives from 'tzavta' (connection/bonding) — when one does bundles of mitzvot, the broken bones rejoin. This is a positive repair mechanism parallel to the ודוי/תלמיד חכם pathway. Merged by shared nature: mechanisms of spiritual reconnection and repair.", "Component 17 ('והולכתי עורים', הצדיק הורה לו דרך ישר): 'I will lead the blind on unknown paths' = the tzaddik guiding to the right path = explicitly equated with פרשת דרכים (the crossroads / the talmid chakham) in the same proof text. Already adjacent to this package's תלמיד חכם cluster. Merged by shared nature.", "Component 18 (עמוד השחר, צדקה): Dawn (עמוד השחר) = tzedakah, which delivers from the 'evil beast and bandits' (evil elements of Chai and Medaber). Tzedakah is the second of the three approaches to the tzaddik described in this Torah. Since the three approaches (seeing tzaddik, giving tzedakah, verbal confession) all function as mechanisms for repairing malkhut, tzedakah belongs in the malkhut-repair cluster. Merged by shared nature: practical mechanisms of malkhut restoration."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 4, "volume": "lm2", "label": "צְדָקָה וְהָעוֹרֵב — Tzedaka and the Raven", "label_en": "Charity: Breaking Cruelty and Opening Beginnings", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all concepts that share the nature of צְדָקָה (charity) — the act of breaking one's innate cruelty (אַכְזָרִיּוּת) and transforming it into compassion (רַחֲמָנוּת). The raven (עוֹרֵב) is the key emblem: cruel by nature, yet commanded to sustain Eliyahu — exactly the bechina of tzedaka. The 'Poshkantza' bird (crow) in the Aggadah that kills the serpent is likewise called a bechina of tzedaka. The mincha (grain offering) is equated with tzedaka via the verse 'magishei mincha bitzedaka'. The 'mincha chadasha' = beginning of tzedaka = opening of the 'petach' (doorway). In the healing metaphor tzedaka is a bechina of 'ptichat pi hamaka' (opening the wound). All these are different expressions of the same essential reality: charity as the initiating act that opens every spiritual doorway.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "הָעֹרְבִים", "עוֹרֵב", "פּוּשְׁקַנְצָא", "מִנְחָה", "מִנְחָה חֲדָשָׁה", "הַתְחָלַת הַצְּדָקָה", "פְּתִיחַת פִּי הַמַּכָּה", "אַכְזָרִיּוּת", "רַחֲמָנוּת"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 5, "עוֹרֵב": 2, "הָעֹרְבִים": 1, "פּוּשְׁקַנְצָא": 1, "מִנְחָה": 2, "מִנְחָה חֲדָשָׁה": 1, "הַתְחָלַת הַצְּדָקָה": 1, "פְּתִיחַת פִּי הַמַּכָּה": 1, "אַכְזָרִיּוּת": 1, "רַחֲמָנוּת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עוֹרֵב", "פּוּשְׁקַנְצָא"], "reason": "The Aramaic 'Poshkantza' (the bird in the Bava Batra aggadah) is explicitly identified in the Torah text as 'עוֹרֵב' (raven) = tzedaka. Both are the same symbolic figure."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מִנְחָה חֲדָשָׁה", "הַתְחָלַת הַצְּדָקָה"], "reason": "The verse 'uveYom haBikurim beHakrivkhem mincha chadasha' is explicitly decoded as 'this is the bechina of beginning tzedaka, starting fresh to give tzedaka.' Mincha chadasha = beginning of tzedaka — two expressions of one concept."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פְּתִיחַת פִּי הַמַּכָּה", "צדקה"], "reason": "In the healing metaphor (section יב), tzedaka is explicitly stated as 'bechina of ptichat pi hamaka' (opening the wound). Same essential function: tzedaka opens what is closed."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אַכְזָרִיּוּת", "רַחֲמָנוּת"], "reason": "The Torah's core teaching about tzedaka defines it as the work of converting cruelty (achzariut) to compassion (rachamnut). These are the two poles of the tzedaka process — both belong to this package as the substance of what tzedaka transforms."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "דַּעַת וְאַחְדוּת — Da'at and Divine Unity", "label_en": "Unified Consciousness: Knowing Chesed and Din Are One", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster centers on דַּעַת (unified knowledge/consciousness) as the cognitive attainment that results from restoring malkhut. Da'at is defined as knowing that chesed and din are one (אחדות של חסד וגבורה) — not separating Hashem's mercy from His judgment. The intellectual oil (גרבי דמשחא = barrels of anointing oil = שכל/wisdom) is what the tzaddik draws down after cleaving to Ein Sof in the state of 'Ratzo veShov' (running and returning). The cluster includes: 'שמן משחת קדש' (holy anointing oil = sekhel), גרבי דמשחא (oil barrels = da'at), אחדות של חסד וגבורה (unity of mercy and judgment = da'at), שלא יחלק בין חסד לדין (not distinguishing between din and chesed = da'at). Merged into this package: יהוה/רחמים (Hashem's name = mercy — because knowing Hashem is the unifier of rachamim and din is the essence of da'at in this Torah); אחד/אהבה ('Echad' gematria = Ahavah / love — because God's oneness means that even din is love, the very definition of da'at in section 4 of the Torah).", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["דעת", "גרבי דמשחא", "חכמה", "\"שמן משחת קדש\"", "אחדות של חסד וגבורה", "שלא יחלק בין חסד לדין", "יהוה", "רחמים", "אחד", "אהבה"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 3, "גרבי דמשחא": 2, "חכמה": 2, "\"שמן משחת קדש\"": 1, "אחדות של חסד וגבורה": 1, "שלא יחלק בין חסד לדין": 1, "יהוה": 1, "רחמים": 1, "אחד": 1, "אהבה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 14 (יהוה, רחמים): The proof text states 'Hashem = rachamim' and links this to the core teaching that 'Hashem is One and His Name is One' — meaning both din and rachamim are one unity. This is the very definition of da'at as taught in section 4 of Torah #4: 'the essence of da'at is the unity of chesed and gevurot.' Merged by shared nature: divine attributes as unified da'at.", "Component 19 (אחד, אהבה): 'Echad' gematria = Ahavah. The teaching is: 'Hashem is One and His Name is One' because He loves you — even din is love. This is the experiential content of da'at (knowing that all events are love). This concept bridges Packages 2 and 4, but its essential nature (the identity of oneness and love as the content of divine consciousness) places it more precisely in the da'at cluster. Merged by shared nature: the identity of oneness and love as cognitive-unifying da'at."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 4, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִרְאָה וְרֶגֶל — Yirah and Regel", "label_en": "Fear as the Vessel: The Channel That Receives Chesed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups concepts whose essential nature is יִרְאָה (fear of God) as the structural vessel (כְּלִי וְצִנּוֹר) that enables reception of chesed. The Torah explicitly equates: (1) יִרְאָה = רֶגֶל (foot/base — because Yirah is the 'end' dimension, as in 'sof davar hakol nishma et haElohim yera'); (2) יָמִים טוֹבִים = רֶגֶל ('the Yamim Tovim are called Regel, which is bechina of Yirah'); (3) רֶגֶל = סִבּוֹת ('Regel is bechina of causes/reasons,' per the verse 'vaYevarekh Hashem otkha leragli = besubati'); (4) יִרְאָה = חֲקִיקָה וְצִנּוֹר (through yirah is made an engraving and channel to receive chesed); (5) רֶגֶל = הֲדָחַת וְכִבּוּס הַדָּמִים (in the healing metaphor, regel/yom tov corresponds to washing the blood). All these are different faces of the same reality: the dimension of awe/base that structures the flow of divine blessing.", "dominant_node": "יִרְאָה", "nodes": ["יִרְאָה", "רֶגֶל", "יָמִים טוֹבִים", "צִנּוֹר", "חֲקִיקָה", "סִבּוֹת", "הֲדָחַת וְכִבּוּס הַדָּמִים", "שָׁבוּעוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"יִרְאָה": 4, "רֶגֶל": 5, "יָמִים טוֹבִים": 2, "צִנּוֹר": 2, "חֲקִיקָה": 2, "סִבּוֹת": 1, "הֲדָחַת וְכִבּוּס הַדָּמִים": 1, "שָׁבוּעוֹת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יָמִים טוֹבִים", "רֶגֶל"], "reason": "Text: 'Therefore Yamim Tovim are called Regel, which is bechina of Yirah.' Yamim Tovim and Regel share the same essential nature — the aspect that creates Yirah through revelation of Ratzon."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שָׁבוּעוֹת", "יָמִים טוֹבִים"], "reason": "Shavuot is one of the three Yamim Tovim (Regalim), and is specifically named in the closing exegesis: 'beShavuoteikhem — this is bechina of Zaken.' However, Shavuot as a Regel belongs to the Yirah/Regel package. The connection to 'Zaken' reflects an eitza relationship (tzedaka repairs the Zaken so the Regel of Shavuot reveals Ratzon), not a bechina equivalence between Shavuot and Zaken."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צִנּוֹר", "חֲקִיקָה"], "reason": "These two terms always appear together in the Torah as the functional form of Yirah: 'al yedei Yirah na'aseh chakika veTzinnor.' Both describe the same vessel-function of Yirah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הֲדָחַת וְכִבּוּס הַדָּמִים", "רֶגֶל"], "reason": "In the healing metaphor, Regel/Yom Tov is the bechina of washing/purifying the blood — 'ein Rogel = ein koves' per Rashi. Same structural role."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "אֵין סוֹף וְרְצוֹן — The Infinite and Its Will", "label_en": "Ein Sof: The Infinite Will Behind All Wills", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This small but conceptually pivotal cluster describes the highest divine level in Torah #4: אֵין סוֹף (the Infinite), whose essential nature is רְצוֹן (will/desire) — specifically the 'will of all wills' (רַעֲוָא דְּרַעֲוִין). The teaching holds that all individual divine wills (the shapes of letters = malkhut) receive their life-force from this single infinite simple will in which there is no change of will. The three nodes form a tight chain: אין סוף ↔ רעוא דרעוין ↔ רצון אין סוף. This is the divine 'pole' to which malkhut must be returned, and from which the tzaddik (Moshe, the bechina of Ayin) draws blessing when he cleaves in hitpashetut hagashmiyut (dissolution of corporeality). This cluster is the apex of the Torah's positive aspiration — the final destination of the malkhut-restoration process.", "dominant_node": "רעוא דרעוין", "nodes": ["אין סוף", "רעוא דרעוין", "רצון אין סוף"], "node_degrees": {"רעוא דרעוין": 2, "אין סוף": 1, "רצון אין סוף": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 4, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הִתְגַּלּוּת הָרָצוֹן — Revelation of the Will", "label_en": "Ratzon: The Divine Will That Defeats Naturalism", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package centers on רָצוֹן (Will/Ratzon) — specifically the divine free Will that is not bound by necessity of nature. The Torah's metaphysical axis is: God created and sustains the world through pure Will (not mechanical necessity), and when this is revealed, Yirah is born. The bechina equalities: (1) מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן ↔ הֶחָכָם שֶׁבִּקְדֻשָּׁה (the holy sage is a bechina of Metzach HaRatzon — he is where Moshe's departure was, at the root of Will); (2) נְשִׂיאַת פָּנִים ↔ רָצוֹן ('lifting of face is bechina of Ratzon' — from Elisha's gracious acceptance of Yehoshafat); (3) אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה ↔ רָצוֹן ('even shelema reitzono' — the perfect stone is bechina of Ratzon, the stone David used to slay Goliath/Metzach HaNachash); (4) מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ ↔ הִתְגַּלּוּת הָרָצוֹן (the holy convocation = the revelation of Will that Yom Tov proclaims); (5) זָקֵן (the proper elder) = channel for Ratzon revelation (per 'Zaken venasui fanim' — the proper elder = metzach haratzon). These all share the essential nature of divine Will as the counterforce to necessity.", "dominant_node": "רָצוֹן", "nodes": ["רָצוֹן", "הִתְגַּלּוּת הָרָצוֹן", "מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן", "שֹׁרֶשׁ הָרָצוֹן", "הֶחָכָם שֶׁבִּקְדֻשָּׁה", "נְשִׂיאַת פָּנִים", "אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה", "מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ", "זָקֵן"], "node_degrees": {"רָצוֹן": 4, "הִתְגַּלּוּת הָרָצוֹן": 3, "מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן": 2, "שֹׁרֶשׁ הָרָצוֹן": 2, "הֶחָכָם שֶׁבִּקְדֻשָּׁה": 1, "נְשִׂיאַת פָּנִים": 1, "אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה": 1, "מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ": 1, "זָקֵן": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן", "הֶחָכָם שֶׁבִּקְדֻשָּׁה"], "reason": "Text section ז: 'He is the bechina of Metzach HaRatzon' — the great holy sage who ties all wills to the root of Will is identified as a bechina of Metzach HaRatzon. Same essential domain: the channel through which divine Will overcomes natural philosophy."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אֶבֶן שְׁלֵמָה", "רָצוֹן"], "reason": "Text section יא: 'vatitba ha'even bemitzcho — that is Metzach HaNachash, which he defeated through the bechina of even shelema reitzono, bechina Ratzon.' The perfect stone = Ratzon — same bechina."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מִקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ", "הִתְגַּלּוּת הָרָצוֹן"], "reason": "Text section פ (closing exegesis): 'Mikra Kodesh — that is kol hakriya shel Yom Tov shehu hitgalut haRatzon.' Mikra Kodesh and revelation of Ratzon are equated as bechina."}, {"merged_nodes": ["זָקֵן", "רָצוֹן"], "reason": "Text section ח: 'Zaken unasui fanim' — when elders are as they should be (each day adding holiness = growing in Ratzon consciousness), this strengthens hitgalut haRatzon. The zaken is the conduit for Ratzon. His name 'nasui fanim' = Ratzon (as proven from Elisha). Merged into the Ratzon cluster as an expression of properly aged wisdom that channels divine Will."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "יְדִיעָה שְׁלֵמָה — Complete Knowledge", "label_en": "The Attainment of Knowing All Events Are for Good", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster captures the primary spiritual attainment taught at the opening of Torah #4: כְּשֶׁאָדָם יוֹדֵעַ שֶׁכָּל מְאֹרְעוֹתָיו הֵם לְטוֹבָתוֹ (when a person knows that all his experiences are for his good). This knowing is called 'יְדִיעָה שְׁלֵמָה' (complete/perfect knowledge) and is equated with 'מֵעֵין עוֹלָם הַבָּא' (a foretaste of the World to Come). The connection: today we bless good and bad with different blessings (hatov vehameitiv / dayan ha'emet), but in the future all will be hatov vehameitiv — meaning in the future state of consciousness, even apparent evil is recognized as good. This knowledge is the beginning and end of the Torah's teaching arc. It is distinct from the da'at package (Package 2) in that Package 2 describes the cognitive unity of chesed/din as divine attributes, while this package describes the personal experiential attainment of that knowledge in one's own life.", "dominant_node": "כשאדם יודע שכל מארעותיו הם לטובתו", "nodes": ["כשאדם יודע שכל מארעותיו הם לטובתו", "ידיעה שלמה", "מעין עולם הבא"], "node_degrees": {"כשאדם יודע שכל מארעותיו הם לטובתו": 2, "ידיעה שלמה": 1, "מעין עולם הבא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 4, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חֶסֶד וְכֹהֵן — Chesed and the Priestly State", "label_en": "Chesed: The World Sustained by Grace Alone", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups concepts whose essential nature is חֶסֶד (loving-kindness/grace) — the divine sustenance that flows when the vessel of Yirah receives it. The Torah teaches that in the ideal state ('olam chesed yibaneh') the world is sustained by chesed alone with no need for labor. The bechina equalities: (1) חֶסֶד ↔ כֹּהֵן (section יב: 'bechina of Kohen, Chesed — through it the wound is closed'); (2) מַעֲשֵׂה בְרֵאשִׁית ↔ עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה (section יב: 'maase bereshit — that is olam chesed yibaneh'); (3) כֹּהֲנֵי ה' ↔ the state of pure chesed (sections ג, יב: 'atem kohanei Hashem tikareu' = they need not labor, the world is sustained by chesed). (4) In the healing metaphor, closing the wound (segirat pi hamaka) = chesed = kohen. All these are expressions of the same essential quality: divine grace freely flowing.", "dominant_node": "חֶסֶד", "nodes": ["חֶסֶד", "כֹּהֵן", "כֹּהֲנֵי ה'", "מַעֲשֵׂה בְרֵאשִׁית", "עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה", "סְגִירַת פִּי הַמַּכָּה", "אִתְעָרוּתָא דִּלְתַתָּא"], "node_degrees": {"חֶסֶד": 4, "כֹּהֵן": 2, "כֹּהֲנֵי ה'": 2, "מַעֲשֵׂה בְרֵאשִׁית": 2, "עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה": 1, "סְגִירַת פִּי הַמַּכָּה": 1, "אִתְעָרוּתָא דִּלְתַתָּא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מַעֲשֵׂה בְרֵאשִׁית", "עוֹלָם חֶסֶד יִבָּנֶה"], "reason": "Text section יב: 'maase bereshit — dehaynu bechina: olam chesed yibaneh.' The work of creation is a bechina of 'the world is built by chesed.' Same essential domain: the cosmic dimension of chesed."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כֹּהֵן", "כֹּהֲנֵי ה'"], "reason": "Both are expressions of the priestly domain = chesed. The individual 'kohen' in the healing metaphor and 'kohanei Hashem' in the redemptive vision share the essential nature of chesed-service."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אִתְעָרוּתָא דִּלְתַתָּא", "חֶסֶד"], "reason": "Text section ג: ordinary labor is the itaruta diletata (arousal from below) needed when chesed is withheld. The absence of itaruta is the state of pure chesed. They share the essential domain of the chesed-flow vs. labor dynamic. Itaruta belongs here as the structural mirror: when chesed flows, no itaruta is needed."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "חַד גַּלְגַּלָא דְּעֵינָא — The Single Eye of Mercy", "label_en": "The Future Single Eye: Pure Goodness and Mercy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This compact cluster describes the Zoharic image of the messianic/future divine vision: 'חַד גַּלְגַּלָא דְּעֵינָא' (a single eye-ball of mercy), equated with 'כֻּלּוֹ הַטּוֹב וְהַמֵּטִיב' (entirely the Good-and-Beneficent, the blessing of pure goodness) and 'עֵינָא חַד דְּרַחֲמֵי' (a single eye of mercy). In Torah #4, this is the messianic state that the tzaddik partially achieves when he cleaves to Ein Sof in hitpashetut: after returning (the 'shoov' of ratzo vaShov), he draws down this unified merciful gaze to illuminate his own da'at. This cluster is the good/evil mirror complement of the blindness and darkness of night-walking described in the parable (section 8). Where the parable-traveler fears thorns and pits in darkness, the attainment of the single eye of mercy sees only light.", "dominant_node": "חד גלגלא דעינא", "nodes": ["חד גלגלא דעינא", "כלו הטוב והמטיב", "עינא חד דרחמי"], "node_degrees": {"חד גלגלא דעינא": 2, "כלו הטוב והמטיב": 1, "עינא חד דרחמי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 4, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ וְחַכְמֵי הַטֶּבַע — The Serpent's Forehead and the Natural Philosophers", "label_en": "Metzach HaNachash: The Evil Root of Natural Necessity", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil package — the counterforce to Package 3 (Ratzon). The Torah explicitly identifies Metzach HaNachash (forehead of the serpent) as the root (שֹׁרֶשׁ) of chochmat hateva (wisdom of nature/naturalism). The natural philosophers (חַכְמֵי הַטֶּבַע) are called a bechina of evil beasts (חַיּוֹת רָעוֹת) that prowl and tear. Goliath (גָּלְיַת) sucked from Metzach HaNachash via 'umitzachat nechoshet al raglav' — he denied divine Will and attributed everything to natural necessity. The serpent (תַּנִּינָא) in the Aggadah is Metzach HaNachash. The bechina equalities: (1) מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ ↔ שֹׁרֶשׁ חָכְמַת הַטֶּבַע (explicit in text section ז); (2) חַכְמֵי הַטֶּבַע ↔ חַיּוֹת רָעוֹת (section ו); (3) תַּנִּינָא ↔ מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ (Aggadah interpretation); (4) גָּלְיַת ↔ מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ (section ז, יא). This is the good/evil mirror of Package 3: where Package 3 is Metzach HaRatzon (the forehead of divine Will), Package 5 is Metzach HaNachash (the forehead of the serpent/necessity).", "dominant_node": "מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ", "nodes": ["מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ", "שֹׁרֶשׁ חָכְמַת הַטֶּבַע", "חַכְמֵי הַטֶּבַע", "חַיּוֹת רָעוֹת", "תַּנִּינָא", "גָּלְיַת", "חִיּוּב הַטֶּבַע", "כְּפִירָה בָּרָצוֹן"], "node_degrees": {"מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ": 5, "שֹׁרֶשׁ חָכְמַת הַטֶּבַע": 2, "חַכְמֵי הַטֶּבַע": 2, "חַיּוֹת רָעוֹת": 2, "תַּנִּינָא": 2, "גָּלְיַת": 2, "חִיּוּב הַטֶּבַע": 1, "כְּפִירָה בָּרָצוֹן": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תַּנִּינָא", "מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ"], "reason": "In the Aggadah interpretation, the serpent (tannina) who tries to swallow the ship (root of Ratzon) is identified as Metzach HaNachash. The Aramaic 'tannina' = the serpent whose forehead is the root of naturalism."}, {"merged_nodes": ["גָּלְיַת", "מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ"], "reason": "Text sections ז, יא: Goliath sucked his strength from Metzach HaNachash ('umitzachat nechoshet al raglav' = metzach hanachash), denied divine Will, and claimed everything runs by natural necessity. He is a bechina of Metzach HaNachash."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חִיּוּב הַטֶּבַע", "שֹׁרֶשׁ חָכְמַת הַטֶּבַע"], "reason": "The 'necessity of nature' (chiyuv hateva) is the conceptual content of natural philosophy — it is what Metzach HaNachash asserts. Two names for the same evil claim."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כְּפִירָה בָּרָצוֹן", "מֵצַח הַנָּחָשׁ"], "reason": "Denial of Will (kefirah baratzon) is the functional definition of Metzach HaNachash — it is what the serpent's forehead does. These are one bechina: the force that denies divine freedom."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 4, "label": "מִדּוֹת רָעוֹת מֵאַרְבַּע יְסוֹדוֹת — Evil Middot from the Four Elements", "label_en": "The Evil Inclinations Rooted in the Four Physical Elements", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the good/evil mirror of Package 1 (malkhut and its repair). The Torah teaches that all evil middot (character flaws) derive from the four physical elements (יסודות) — earth/domem (producing sadness), plant/tzomea'ch (producing evil desires), animal/chai (producing idle speech), speaking-being/medaber (producing pride). These evil middot appear in the night-walking parable as the obstacles the traveler fears: קוצים (thorns) = צומח; פחתים (pits) = דומם; חיה רעה (evil beast) = חי; לסטים (bandits) = מדבר. The evil desires (גאוה, דברים בטלים) are the direct manifestations. The moon (ירח) governs all four sub-lunar elements, which are therefore called 'עבדים' (slaves) — the Egyptian slavery ('מבית עבדים') is a metaphor for bondage to these evil middot. The שרץ/טמאה pair (vermin/impurity) represents the spiritual impurity that mixes into prayer and disrupts divine service. This entire evil domain is what the three approaches to the tzaddik (seeing him, giving tzedakah, verbal confession) are designed to neutralize. Merged small components: Component 6a (דברים בטלים, חיה רעה, חי), Component 6b (גאוה, לסטים, מדבר), Component 7 (ירח, עבד), Component 8 (מדות רעות של ארבע יסודות, עבדים), Component 12 (פחתים, דומם), Component 13 (קוצים, צומח), Component 15 (שרץ, טמאה).", "dominant_node": "מדות רעות של ארבע יסודות", "nodes": ["מדות רעות של ארבע יסודות", "עבדים", "דברים בטלים", "חיה רעה", "חי", "גאוה", "לסטים", "מדבר", "ירח", "עבד", "פחתים", "דומם", "קוצים", "צומח", "שרץ", "טמאה"], "node_degrees": {"מדות רעות של ארבע יסודות": 1, "עבדים": 1, "חיה רעה": 2, "דברים בטלים": 1, "חי": 1, "לסטים": 2, "גאוה": 1, "מדבר": 1, "ירח": 1, "עבד": 1, "פחתים": 1, "דומם": 1, "קוצים": 1, "צומח": 1, "שרץ": 1, "טמאה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 6a (דברים בטלים, חיה רעה, חי): The element 'חי' produces the evil middot of idle speech (דברים בטלים) — these are the 'evil beast' (חיה רעה) in the parable. Kept as evil-elements cluster.", "Component 6b (גאוה, לסטים, מדבר): The element 'מדבר' (speech capacity) produces pride (גאוה) — these are the 'bandits' (לסטים) in the parable. Same domain as Component 6a (evil middot from elements); merged into this evil package.", "Component 7 (ירח, עבד): The moon (ירח) governs all four sub-lunar elements, and the Zohar calls it 'עבד' (slave). All four elements/middot are therefore 'עבדים' (slaves) — the Egyptian 'מבית עבדים' is a bechina of these evil middot. Merged into evil-elements package.", "Component 8 (מדות רעות של ארבע יסודות, עבדים): Explicit statement that the four elements' evil middot are called 'עבדים'. Same proof text as Component 7. Merged into evil-elements package.", "Component 12 (פחתים, דומם): 'Pits' = domem element = earth = source of sadness/depression. This is two of the four parable-obstacles directly equated with evil-element domains. Merged into evil-elements package.", "Component 13 (קוצים, צומח): 'Thorns' = tzomea'ch element = plant = source of evil desires. Same structure as Component 12. Merged into evil-elements package.", "Component 15 (שרץ, טמאה): The vermin (שרץ) in the whale's nostril = spiritual impurity (טמאה) that mixes into prayer and service. This is the evil domain that the three approaches to the tzaddik are designed to expel. While not directly from the four elements, it represents the evil force that infiltrates holy service — the enemy of Package 1's repair mechanism. Merged into evil-elements/impurity package."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "אַסְפַּקְלַרְיָא הַמְּאִירָה — The Illuminating Mirror", "label_en": "The Clear Prophetic Vision: Doing Mitzvot for Their Own Sake", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This 2-node package represents the higher level of prophetic consciousness described at the opening of Torah #5. Moses prophesied in the mode of 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' (This is the word) — the illuminating mirror (אַסְפַּקְלַרְיָא הַמְּאִירָה). This corresponds to a person who performs mitzvot out of pure love for the commandment itself, wanting no reward except another mitzvah. The two nodes are bechina-identical: 'זֶה הַדָּבָר' IS the illuminating mirror — one sees the divine command up close, clearly, and immediately. This package is the spiritual apex of Torah #5 — the ultimate good. Its evil mirror is Package 2 (כֹּה / non-illuminating mirror). The distinction between the two prophetic modes (זה הדבר vs כה) is the Torah's opening premise and structural spine.", "dominant_node": "זה הדבר", "nodes": ["זה הדבר", "אספקלריא המאירה"], "node_degrees": {"זה הדבר": 1, "אספקלריא המאירה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גְּבוּרוֹת וּרְעָמִים", "label_en": "Gevurot and Thunder", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the same essential nature: the divine powers of severity (gevurot) that, when combined with love and directed through the throat (kaneh), produce thunder — the powerful voice of prayer that strikes the skull and illumines the mochin. שם אג\"לא is the divine name of gevurot; גילדנא shares the same letter-root; כח וגבורה שאדם מוציא הקול בכח and קול שאדם מוציא בכח בתפלתו are the experiential and functional equivalents of re'amim; הרעים is an alternative verbal form of רעמים. The kaneh (throat-pipe) is the physical conduit through which the powerful voice (re'amim) exits.", "dominant_node": "גבורות", "nodes": ["גבורות", "רעמים", "שם אג\"לא", "גילדנא", "כח וגבורה, שאדם מוציא את הקול בכח גדול", "קול שאדם מוציא בכח בתפלתו", "הרעים", "קנה", "קול היוצא מהקנה"], "node_degrees": {"גבורות": 3, "רעמים": 3, "שם אג\"לא": 2, "גילדנא": 1, "כח וגבורה, שאדם מוציא את הקול בכח גדול": 1, "קול שאדם מוציא בכח בתפלתו": 1, "הרעים": 1, "קנה": 1, "קול היוצא מהקנה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קנה", "קול היוצא מהקנה"], "reason": "The kaneh (throat-reed) is defined as the bechina of the voice issuing from it — a structural/instrumental definition that places it in the same domain as re'amim and gevurot. The Torah teaches that the powerful voice of prayer passes through the kaneh and becomes thunder; קנה and its voice are therefore two faces of the same reality as gevurot/re'amim."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "כֹּה וְאַסְפַּקְלַרְיָא שֶׁאֵינָהּ מְאִירָה — The Non-Illuminating Mirror", "label_en": "The Distant Prophetic Vision: Doing Mitzvot for Future Reward", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This 3-node package represents the lower level of prophetic vision in Torah #5. Other prophets (besides Moses) used the formula 'כֹּה אָמַר ה'' (Thus says God) — the non-illuminating mirror. This corresponds to a person who performs mitzvot only for the reward of the World to Come, not experiencing joy in the mitzvah itself. Such a person 'sees from a distance' — his reward is far off, after death. The node 'אָדָם הָעוֹשֶׂה הַמִּצְוָה בִּשְׂכַר עוֹלָם הַבָּא...' is explicitly equated with 'כֹּה' (bechina). This is not evil — it is a lower but valid rung of divine service. Its polarity is neutral/lower because the Rebbe presents it as legitimate but incomplete, the starting point one should aspire to transcend rather than the enemy to defeat.", "dominant_node": "כה", "nodes": ["כה", "אספקלריא שאינה מאירה", "אדם העושה המצוה בשכר עולם הבא, שאינו נהנה מהמצוה בעצמה, אלו לא היו נותנין לו עולם הבא בשכרה, לא היה עושה אותה"], "node_degrees": {"כה": 2, "אספקלריא שאינה מאירה": 1, "אדם העושה המצוה בשכר עולם הבא, שאינו נהנה מהמצוה בעצמה, אלו לא היו נותנין לו עולם הבא בשכרה, לא היה עושה אותה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִרְאָה וַחֲשִׁיבוּת", "label_en": "Fear and Dignity", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of holy fear (yirat Hashem), which is the same as genuine dignity/importance (chashivut). Yirah is described explicitly as gevurah and chashivut — fear of Heaven is the treasury of true honor. אשה (the feminine) is the bechina of yirah (the receptive, inward quality). הרים (mountains) are the bechina of tzaddikim — the ones who embody genuine yirat Hashem and thus carry real chashivut. All express the inward dimension of holy awe.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "חשיבות", "גבורה", "אשה", "הרים", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 2, "חשיבות": 2, "גבורה": 1, "אשה": 1, "הרים": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הרים", "צדיק"], "reason": "The proof text 'הרים בחינת צדיקים' establishes that mountains = tzaddikim. In the context of Torah #5 (LM2), the tzaddikim are the 'maginnei eretz' (shields of the earth) whose genuine yirat Hashem gives them true chashivut. Mountains carry the same qualities of stability, elevation, and genuine greatness that characterize yirah and chashivut — they are two faces of the same lofty, grounded quality. Merging with Package 2 (yirah/chashivut) rather than treating as a stand-alone pair."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "יִרְאָה וַחֲשִׁיבוּת — Fear and Importance", "label_en": "True Fear of Heaven: The Weighty Inner Seriousness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package is built around the conceptual cluster of true, inner, healthy fear of God (יִרְאָה) as the fundamental spiritual quality that enables the voice of prayer to become thunder. The core chain: חֲשִׁיבוּת (importance / weight) is a bechina of גְּבוּרָה (inner strength), and חֲשִׁיבוּת is also a bechina of יִרְאָה (fear), and יִרְאָה is a bechina of אִשָּׁה (the feminine principle — the receiving, devoted, faithful partner). These four nodes form a 4-node connected component. Merged into this package: the 2-node pair אַהֲבָה (love) ↔ אִישׁ (man) — because the text explicitly makes love/אהבה the masculine principle (אִישׁ) that is paired with fear/יראה (אִשָּׁה), teaching that love seeks fear the way a husband seeks a lost wife. The אהבה/איש pair shares the exact same structural nature (gendered cosmic pair) as the יראה/אשה pair in this Torah. Together, the 6-node package represents the full אהבה-יראה cosmic pair that must be united for the voice of prayer to ascend properly. This is the good/evil mirror of Package 7 (external fear).", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "חשיבות", "גבורה", "אשה", "אהבה", "איש"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 2, "חשיבות": 2, "גבורה": 1, "אשה": 1, "אהבה": 1, "איש": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (אהבה, איש): Merged into Package 3 because in Torah #5, love (אהבה = איש/masculine) and fear (יראה = אשה/feminine) are explicitly described as a cosmic married pair. The teaching states: 'כִּי אַהֲבָה הוּא בְּחִינַת אִישׁ וְיִרְאָה הִיא בְּחִינַת אִשָּׁה' — love pursues fear the way a husband pursues a lost wife (בַּעַל אֲבֵדָה מַחֲזִיר עַל אֲבֵדָתוֹ). The two pairs share the same essential nature: the gendered polarity of the cosmic love-fear relationship. Merged by shared nature: two faces of the divine אהבה-יראה union."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִרְאָה חִיצוֹנִית וְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא דִּגְבוּרָה", "label_en": "External Fear and the Evil Side of Gevurah", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes all share the essential nature of the external, corrupt dimension of gevurot. The Torah explicitly calls the chain of gevurot that descend without being sweetened with love 'yirah chitzonit' (external fear), and the Aramaic name for this is 'suspita d'dahava' (golden chip). They represent the evil counterpart of Package 1 (gevurot/re'amim) — when gevurot are not mixed with chasadim they become a klipa that blocks the voice from being heard.", "dominant_node": "השתלשלות הגבורות", "nodes": ["השתלשלות הגבורות", "יראה חיצונית", "סוספיתא דדהבא"], "node_degrees": {"השתלשלות הגבורות": 2, "יראה חיצונית": 1, "סוספיתא דדהבא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "גְּבוּרוֹת וּרְעָמִים — Divine Powers and Thunder", "label_en": "The Holy Gevurot: Voice, Thunder, and the Name of Power", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the largest and most central package of Torah #5, describing the mechanism by which the forceful voice of prayer (קוֹל שֶׁאָדָם מוֹצִיא בְּכֹחַ בִּתְפִלָּתוֹ) hits the clouds of the intellect (עָבֵי מִטְרָא / מֹחִין) and produces thunder (רְעָמִים). Thunder straightens the crookedness of the heart and brings joy and prayer. The dominant node is גְּבוּרוֹת (divine powers) with 4 bechina connections. The cluster includes: שֵׁם אגל\"א (the divine name AGLA = 'Atah Gibor Le'olam Adonai' = gevurot) and גִּילְדְּנָא (the Talmudic 'gildana de-yama' = the sea-creature of the aggadah = identified as AGLA). The cluster also includes: הָרְעִים (the divine thundering) as bechina of רְעָמִים, and רְעָמִים as bechina of קוֹל שֶׁאָדָם מוֹצִיא בְּכֹחַ. The קָנֶה (windpipe/reed) is the bechina of the voice itself. Merged small components: (1) the יִּרְעַם / הָרִים pair — the mountains (הָרִים = צַדִּיקִים) who receive and amplify the thunder into prayer-song; (2) the קָנֶה / קוֹל הַיּוֹצֵא מֵהַקָּנֶה pair — the holy reed/windpipe from which the voice emerges. The good/evil mirror of the holy reed (Package 6) is the קָנֶה דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא (evil reed of foreign thought).", "dominant_node": "גבורות", "nodes": ["גבורות", "שם אג\"לא", "גילדנא", "רעמים", "הרעים", "קול שאדם מוציא בכח בתפלתו", "כח וגבורה, שאדם מוציא את הקול בכח גדול", "הרים", "צדיק", "קנה", "קול היוצא מהקנה"], "node_degrees": {"גבורות": 4, "שם אג\"לא": 2, "רעמים": 3, "הרעים": 1, "קול שאדם מוציא בכח בתפלתו": 1, "כח וגבורה, שאדם מוציא את הקול בכח גדול": 1, "גילדנא": 1, "הרים": 1, "צדיק": 1, "קנה": 1, "קול היוצא מהקנה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (הרים, צדיק): Merged into Package 4 because the text states 'הָרִים בְּחִינַת צַדִּיקִים' in the context of thunder-prayer: 'יַחַד הָרִים יְרַנֵּנוּ' — the tzaddikim (mountains) rejoice together in prayer after the thunder of the gevurot straightens the heart. The mountains/tzaddikim receive the thunder-sound and transform it into tefillah/rinah. Merged by shared nature: the tzaddik as the mountain-resonator of the holy voice.", "Component (קנה, קול היוצא מהקנה): Merged into Package 4 because the holy reed (קָנֶה) is the physical source of the voice/sound — the throat-windpipe from which the holy voice emerges. The text explains the Zoharic proof: 'כַּד קָלָא נָפִיק וְאַעָרָא בְּעָבֵי מִטְרָא' — the voice going forth from the קָנֶה and striking the brain-clouds (מחין) is the mechanism of the gevurot. The קנה is structurally identical to the גבורות/voice theme. Merged by shared nature: the windpipe as the physical vessel of the holy voice."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מֹחִין וּמַצָּה", "label_en": "The Intellect and Matzah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of pure, open intellect (mochin). Matzah = mochin (pure dough, unyeasted = unblocked mind). 'Be'avei mitra' (thick rain clouds) = mochin because the mochin drip down drop by drop like rain from above. 'Mei merivah' (waters of dispute) = matzah = mochin (via the Psalms verse about hidden thunder and Mei Merivah). Sheshet yemei hamaaseh = maaseh hamitzvot, because the practical mitzvot (physical action) are the vessel for constructing the mochin from below. Shabbat = joy of the world to come = the state of perfected mochin (olam haba is the eternal shabbat of illumined consciousness). All are expressions of the same pure, rectified intellect that the Torah instructs us to build.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["מחין", "מצה", "בעבי מטרא", "מי מריבה", "ששת ימי המעשה", "מעשה המצוות", "שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא", "שבת"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 2, "מצה": 2, "בעבי מטרא": 1, "מי מריבה": 1, "ששת ימי המעשה": 1, "מעשה המצוות": 1, "שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא": 1, "שבת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ששת ימי המעשה", "מעשה המצוות"], "reason": "The proof text 'שישת ימי המעשה שהן בחינת מעשה המצוות שבהם נבראו עולם שנה נפש' equates the six working days with the practical mitzvot. In the Torah's argument, practical action (maaseh) is what draws down the mochin from above into the vessels below. The pair belongs with mochin/matzah since both represent the practical, physical dimension through which the pure intellect is constructed and maintained."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא", "שבת"], "reason": "The proof text 'שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא שהוא בחינת שבת' makes these two directly equivalent. In the Torah's argument, simcha (joy) strengthens the malach (angel), which in turn protects the mochin/tefillin complex. Shabbat is the culminating state of perfect mochin — the complete rectification of intellect that the entire Torah is building toward. It belongs in the mochin cluster as the perfected state."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "מֹחִין וּמַצָּה — Intellect and Matzah", "label_en": "The Open Brain: Pure Consciousness Free of Foreign Thought", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package describes the second major structural element of Torah #5: the intellect/brain (מֹחִין) that must remain open and unpolluted so that the voice-thunder can resonate within it. The core: מֹחִין = עָבֵי מִטְרָא (rain-clouds = the dense brain-cloud), and מַצָּה = מֹחִין (matzah = intellect; the letters of מַצָּה vs חָמֵץ differ by the gap between ה and ח — an open vs a closed brain). Also: מֵי מְרִיבָה (waters of Meribah) is a bechina of מַצָּה in this Torah (both are 'strife for the sake of heaven' = an open brain engaged in proper spiritual combat). The good/evil mirror of this package is Package 6 (חָמֵץ / sitra de-mota). Merged into this package: (1) שֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַמַּעֲשֶׂה / מַעֲשֵׂה הַמִּצְווֹת — the six weekdays are bechinot of the mitzvot (the text: 'שֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַמַּעֲשֶׂה שֶׁהֵן בְּחִינַת מַעֲשֵׂה הַמִּצְווֹת') — the mitzvot are the practical dimension of keeping the brain open; (2) שִׂמְחַת וְתַעֲנוּג עוֹלָם הַבָּא / שַׁבָּת — the joy of the World to Come is a bechina of Shabbat (the text: 'שִׂמְחַת וְתַעֲנוּג עוֹלָם הַבָּא שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת'), the culmination and reward of the mitzvot-joy; (3) פַּרְשָׁא / חֶסֶד — the 'rider' (פַּרְשָׁא) is a bechina of חֶסֶד (love/mercy) that fires an arrow (גִּירָא = the reaching-out of chesed) which precedes the thunder, softening the brain for reception. Chesed/parsha belongs with the open-intellect theme since it is the masculine activating force that opens the receiving brain.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["מחין", "מצה", "בעבי מטרא", "מי מריבה", "ששת ימי המעשה", "מעשה המצוות", "שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא", "שבת", "פרשא", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 2, "מצה": 2, "בעבי מטרא": 1, "מי מריבה": 1, "ששת ימי המעשה": 1, "מעשה המצוות": 1, "שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא": 1, "שבת": 1, "פרשא": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (ששת ימי המעשה, מעשה המצוות): The text explicitly states 'שֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַמַּעֲשֶׂה שֶׁהֵן בְּחִינַת מַעֲשֵׂה הַמִּצְווֹת' — the six weekdays ARE the domain of mitzvah-action, and the mitzvot vivify the three dimensions of world-year-soul (עוֹלָם שָׁנָה נֶפֶשׁ). The essential nature: weekdays are the time of practical mitzvot which keep the brain open and alive. Merged into Package 5 by shared nature: the mitzvot as the maintenance mechanism for open (matzah) consciousness.", "Component (שמחת ותענוג עולם הבא, שבת): The Shabbat / World-to-Come pair is the reward and culmination of the mitzvot-joy theme: 'שִׂמְחַת וְתַעֲנוּג עוֹלָם הַבָּא, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת, יַרְגִּישׁ בְּשֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַמַּעֲשֶׂה.' The instruction is to FEEL Shabbat-joy (World-to-Come) already during the weekday mitzvot — i.e., to access the illuminating mirror even now. This pair is structurally continuous with the mitzvot pair above and with the intellect-joy theme of Package 5. Merged by shared nature: the experience of World-to-Come joy as the living reality inside matzah consciousness.", "Component (פרשא, חסד): In the Rabbah bar bar Chana parable, 'וְשָׁדִי פַּרְשָׁא גִּירָא' — the 'rider' (פַּרְשָׁא) fires an arrow: text states 'פַּרְשָׁא דָּא בְּחִינַת חֶסֶד' (chesed). Chesed is the activating power that 'spreads out hidden things' (מְפָרֵשׁ אוֹתָם). Structurally, chesed is the positive force that softens and opens the intellect for reception of the gevurot-thunder. It belongs with the open-brain/matzah package as the activating principle of the receptive intellect. Merged by shared nature: chesed as the force that opens and purifies the mind for holy reception."]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "סִטְרָא דְּמוֹתָא וְחָמֵץ", "label_en": "The Side of Death and Chametz", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Chametz (leavened bread, swollen dough) and sitra de-muta (the side of death) share the same essential nature: the impure, blocked intellect — the foreign wisdoms and evil thoughts that clog the brain like fermenting dough. Evil thoughts (hirhurim ra'im) are explicitly the 'kaneh destra achra' — the evil pipe/throat through which impurity flows into the mochin. This is the evil mirror of Package 4 (mochin/matzah).", "dominant_node": "סטרא דמותא", "nodes": ["סטרא דמותא", "חמץ", "הרהורים רעים", "קנה דסטרא אחרא"], "node_degrees": {"סטרא דמותא": 1, "חמץ": 1, "הרהורים רעים": 1, "קנה דסטרא אחרא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הרהורים רעים", "קנה דסטרא אחרא"], "reason": "The proof text 'שלא תהרהר בהרהורים רעים שהם בחינת קנה דסטרא אחרא' defines evil thoughts as the evil throat-pipe. This is the direct functional counterpart of the holy kaneh in Package 1. In the sitra achra, evil thoughts flow through the kaneh destra achra into the mochin, blocking them with chametz (impure, foreign thoughts). Merged into Package 5 because hirhurim ra'im and kaneh destra achra are two faces of the same reality as sitra de-muta/chametz: the corruption and blocking of the intellect."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "סִטְרָא דְּמוֹתָא וְחָמֵץ — The Side of Death and Chametz", "label_en": "The Closed, Fermenting Brain: Foreign Thought and Death", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 5 (מֹחִין וּמַצָּה). Where matzah = the open, pure intellect, chametz = the closed, fermenting, blocked intellect. The Zohar teaches: 'מַחְמֶצֶת, תַּמָּן סִטְרָא דְּמוֹתָא' — fermentation = the side of death. When the brain (מֹחִין) is blocked by chametz (foreign thoughts, impure wisdom), the voice cannot resonate within it and produce thunder. The evil reed (קָנֶה דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא) = evil thoughts (הִרְהוּרִים רָעִים) that penetrate the sea of wisdom and block the voice — the exact mirror of the holy reed (Package 4) which opens the voice. The text explicitly: 'שֶׁלֹּא תְּהַרְהֵר בְּהִרְהוּרִים רָעִים, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת קָנֶה דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא.' Merged into this package: the 2-node pair (הִרְהוּרִים רָעִים, קָנֶה דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא) — the evil reed of foreign thought is the chametz that clogs the brain, the mechanism of the sitra de-mota. Evil reed and evil brain/chametz share the same essential nature: corruption of the intellectual vessel.", "dominant_node": "חמץ", "nodes": ["חמץ", "סטרא דמותא", "הרהורים רעים", "קנה דסטרא אחרא"], "node_degrees": {"חמץ": 1, "סטרא דמותא": 1, "הרהורים רעים": 1, "קנה דסטרא אחרא": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (הרהורים רעים, קנה דסטרא אחרא): The evil thoughts (הִרְהוּרִים רָעִים) are the bechina of the evil reed (קָנֶה דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא) — the impure 'reed' that Gabriel drove into the sea of wisdom (as in the Talmudic story 'יָרַד גַּבְרִיאֵל וְנָעַץ קָנֶה'). The essential nature of both is: foreign/evil thought penetrating and blocking the intellect. This is the same evil domain as chametz/sitra de-mota — all four nodes describe the clogged, chametz-brain in which the holy voice cannot resonate. Merged by shared nature: evil intellectual contamination."]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אַסְפַּקְלַרְיָא הַמְּאִירָה", "label_en": "The Luminous Mirror / Clear Prophecy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes share the same essential nature: the highest level of prophetic clarity. 'Zeh hadavar' (this is the thing) is the language Moses uses — direct, unmediated, the clear mirror of prophecy. The Torah uses this as a positive touchstone for the level of direct apprehension of divine will. Both nodes express the same reality of unobstructed prophetic sight.", "dominant_node": "זה הדבר", "nodes": ["זה הדבר", "אספקלריא המאירה"], "node_degrees": {"זה הדבר": 1, "אספקלריא המאירה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 5, "label": "יִרְאָה חִיצוֹנִית — External Fear", "label_en": "External/Fallen Fear: The Descended Powers That Block the Voice", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This 3-node package is the evil mirror of Package 3 (יִרְאָה וַחֲשִׁיבוּת — true inner fear). External fear (יִרְאָה חִיצוֹנִית) is described in Torah #5 as the danger that blocks the voice of prayer from producing thunder. The text says: 'שֶׁלֹּא יִהְיֶה לוֹ יִרְאָה חִיצוֹנִיּוּת' — one must not have external fear. This external fear = the downward flow/descent of the gevurot (הִשְׁתַּלְשְׁלוּת הַגְּבוּרוֹת) — when the divine powers cascade downward without proper inner foundation, they produce a superficial fear that is the sitra achra's imitation of true fear. The image in the text: 'יָרַד גַּבְרִיאֵל וְנָעַץ קָנֶה' — Gabriel descended and drove a reed into the sea: this descent of Gabriel = the הִשְׁתַּלְשְׁלוּת הַגְּבוּרוֹת = יִרְאָה חִיצוֹנִית = סוּסְפִיתָּא דְּדַהֲבָא (the 'golden lance/boat' = external/ornamented fear, impressive on the surface but hollow within). All three nodes are bechina-identical: descended gevurot = external fear = the golden lance/boat.", "dominant_node": "השתלשלות הגבורות", "nodes": ["השתלשלות הגבורות", "יראה חיצונית", "סוספיתא דדהבא"], "node_degrees": {"השתלשלות הגבורות": 2, "יראה חיצונית": 1, "סוספיתא דדהבא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "כֹּה וְאַסְפַּקְלַרְיָא שֶׁאֵינָהּ מְאִירָה", "label_en": "Koh and the Dim Mirror / Indirect Prophetic Sight", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of indirect, incomplete perception of God. 'Koh' (thus/so) is the word used by other prophets who see from afar, through the dim mirror. The person who does mitzvot for their reward sees God only from a distance — they experience 'koh' rather than 'zeh.' This is a structural category (prophetic level) that can be good or deficient depending on context. Package 7 is the dim mirror that corresponds to Package 6 (the luminous mirror) — together they form the good/incomplete prophetic duality that runs through the Torah.", "dominant_node": "כה", "nodes": ["כה", "אספקלריא שאינה מאירה", "אדם העושה המצוה בשכר עולם הבא, שאינו נהנה מהמצוה בעצמה, אלו לא היו נותנין לו עולם הבא בשכרה, לא היה עושה אותה"], "node_degrees": {"כה": 2, "אספקלריא שאינה מאירה": 1, "אדם העושה המצוה בשכר עולם הבא, שאינו נהנה מהמצוה בעצמה, אלו לא היו נותנין לו עולם הבא בשכרה, לא היה עושה אותה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 5, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אַהֲבָה וָחֶסֶד", "label_en": "Love and Chesed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Ahavah (love) is bechina of 'ish' (the active male principle — the outpouring, giving dimension). Chesed is the quality of giving and revealing hidden things — the parsha (archer) who draws out and brings to light. All three (ahavah, ish, chesed/parsha) are two-faces of the same reality: the outpouring, revealing force of holy love. The Torah teaches that love must be combined with yirah (Package 2) to produce thunder (Package 1). Chesed (ahavah) is the counterpart to gevurot — they must be blended.", "dominant_node": "אהבה", "nodes": ["אהבה", "איש", "פרשא", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"אהבה": 1, "איש": 1, "פרשא": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פרשא", "חסד"], "reason": "The proof text 'פרשא דא בחינת חסד שהוא מוציא לאור תעלומות' establishes that the force of the archer/shooter is chesed because chesed reveals what is hidden. In the context of Torah #5 (LM2), chesed is the counterpart to gevurot — the Rebbe explicitly teaches that the gevurot (re'amim/thunder) must be accompanied by ahavah/chesed to reach the kavod (wisdom). Chesed/parsha shares the essential nature of ahavah/ish — all are the outpouring, revealing, active force of love."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "כֶּתֶר וּתְשׁוּבָה — Keter and Repentance", "label_en": "Keter: The Crown of Silence, Repentance, and Divine Honor", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the central conceptual cluster of Torah #6, orbiting כֶּתֶר (crown/Keter) as the dominant node with 7 direct bechina connections. The teaching's core argument: the person who flees honor, accepts shame in silence, and makes teshuvah achieves כֶּתֶר — the highest sefirah, which is the same in nature as אֶהֱיֶה (the divine name of future-being/potential), תְּשׁוּבָה (repentance), שְׁתִיקָה (silence), כָּבוֹד אֱלֹהִים (divine honor), the upper point of the Aleph, the Throne above the firmament, and מֹשֶׁה (who is the bechina of דַּעַת). Keter = the state of 'אֲנָא זָמִין לְמֵהֱוֵי' — I am about to come into being — the threshold moment of teshuvah, before one has full existence in the world. The 'כָּ\"ף' of כָּבוֹד = כֶּתֶר = הַמְתָּנָה (waiting/patience) = the divine helping hand that meets the person who purifies himself. The cluster includes: the prophetic-Ezekiel vision terminology ('וּמִמַּעַל לָרָקִיעַ דְּמוּת כִּסֵּא וְנַעֲשֶׂה אָדָם לָשֶׁבֶת עַל הַכִּסֵּא' = 'בַּמְּכֻסֶּה מִמְּךָ אַל תַּחְקֹר'), confirming that keter/the throne cannot be investigated — it is the concealed divine honor. Moshe as the upper point = daat = the Temple (כְּאִלּוּ נִבְנֶה בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ). Merged into this package: (a) כָּבוֹד אֱלֹהִים ↔ שֶׁמֶן — divine honor = anointing oil (same essential nature as keter-anointing); (b) חֲכָמִים ↔ אַוָּזֵי — the wise are called 'geese' in a dream (the geese-wisdom connection from Rabba bar bar Chana's vision, whose silence made him worthy of this revelation, same nature as keter/silence cluster); (c) יִשְׂרָאֵל ↔ אָדָם — Israel = Adam, the human who sits on the throne (the אָדָם עַל הַכִּסֵּא is the central image of the throne-vision = keter); (d) בָּקִי ↔ שֵׁם יב\"ק — 'expert' = the divine name Yabak (connecting the expertise in ratzo-vshuv to the overall teshuvah path); (e) דֶּרֶךְ ↔ שְׁתֵּי פְּעָמִים הַשֵּׁם יב\"ק — the Way of teshuvah = twice the name Yabak gematria (the outcome of possessing both bechina-expertise = the road of teshuvah itself); (f) כְּבוֹד עוֹלָם הַבָּא ↔ תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁנִיָּה and תְּשׁוּבָה עַל תְּשׁוּבָתוֹ — the second teshuvah (repenting on one's first teshuvah upon reaching higher perception) = World-to-Come honor = this is the upper dimension of teshuvah = keter nature.", "dominant_node": "כתר", "nodes": ["כתר", "אהיה", "תשובה", "\"כבוד אלקים הסתר דבר\"", "\"במכסה ממך אל תחקר\"", "\"וממעל לרקיע דמות כסא וכו, ונעשה אדם לשבת על הכסא\"", "\"כמראה אדם עליו מלמעלה\"", "\"וממעל לרקיע אשר על ראשם אבן ספיר דמות כסא\"", "נקדה העליונה שעל האלף", "משה", "דעת", "לבנות להם בית הבחירה", "כסא", "חמה", "סגו\"ל", "סיג", "שתיקה", "כבוד", "\"ולבי חלל בקרבי\"", "דם לה", "קדם התשובה", "אהיה בהסתרת פנים ממנו", "כל הזובח את יצרו", "כנף", "כבוד אלהים", "שמן", "חכמים", "אוזי", "ישראל", "אדם", "בקי", "שם יב\"ק", "דרך", "שתי פעמים השם יב\"ק", "כבוד עולם הבא", "תשובה שניה", "תשובה על תשובתו"], "node_degrees": {"כתר": 7, "אהיה": 5, "תשובה": 5, "נקדה העליונה שעל האלף": 5, "משה": 4, "כסא": 4, "\"כבוד אלקים הסתר דבר\"": 3, "\"במכסה ממך אל תחקר\"": 2, "\"וממעל לרקיע דמות כסא וכו, ונעשה אדם לשבת על הכסא\"": 2, "דם לה": 2, "כבוד עולם הבא": 2, "\"ולבי חלל בקרבי\"": 2, "\"וממעל לרקיע אשר על ראשם אבן ספיר דמות כסא\"": 1, "\"כמראה אדם עליו מלמעלה\"": 1, "דעת": 1, "לבנות להם בית הבחירה": 1, "חמה": 1, "סגו\"ל": 1, "סיג": 1, "שתיקה": 1, "כבוד": 1, "קדם התשובה": 1, "אהיה בהסתרת פנים ממנו": 1, "כל הזובח את יצרו": 1, "כנף": 1, "כבוד אלהים": 1, "שמן": 1, "חכמים": 1, "אוזי": 1, "ישראל": 1, "אדם": 1, "בקי": 1, "שם יב\"ק": 1, "דרך": 1, "שתי פעמים השם יב\"ק": 1, "תשובה שניה": 1, "תשובה על תשובתו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (כבוד אלהים, שמן): Divine honor equated to anointing oil (שמן) — through silence one merits divine honor which is in its essential nature the anointing of the king (Psalm 45: 'you loved righteousness, therefore God anointed you'). This is the same nature as כתר (crown/anointing). Merged by shared nature: divine-honor-as-anointing = keter.", "Component (חכמים, אוזי): The wise men (חכמים) that Rabba bar bar Chana saw are equated with geese (אוזי) — from the Talmud (Berakhot 57b): one who sees a goose in a dream should expect wisdom. This is part of the Rabba bar bar Chana parable about humility (treading like a desert), which is the same virtue cluster as silence/keter. The goose-wisdom identity is a bechina of the silent humility that produces wisdom. Merged by shared nature: wisdom from humility = keter-wisdom cluster.", "Component (ישראל, אדם): Israel = Adam (the human being). In the context of Torah 6, this is the 'אדם על הכסא' (human sitting on the throne) — the central image of the Ezekiel throne-vision. Israel being the אדם connects directly to the Keter/throne/upper-point cluster, since the throne-vision is the primary metaphor for keter in this Torah. Merged by shared nature: Israel as the throne-occupant = keter cluster.", "Component (בקי, שם יב\"ק): 'Expert' (בקי) = the divine name Yabak (יב\"ק) — because the letters of Yabak spell Baki. Yabak is the combined divine name used in teshuvah prayer; achieving baki in both ratzo and shuv produces the Way (דרך) of teshuvah. This is the synthesis-achievement that belongs to the teshuvah/keter package as the crowning attainment. Merged by shared nature: expert-in-teshuvah = the way of keter.", "Component (דרך, שתי פעמים השם יב\"ק): The 'Way' (דרך, numerically = 2 × Yabak) is the way of teshuvah itself — the outcome of having both bechina-expertises (ratzo and shuv). This is thematically the culmination of the whole teaching, landing in the 'Way of teshuvah' which = keter = the crown achievement. Merged by shared nature: the Way of teshuvah = keter.", "Component (כבוד עולם הבא, תשובה שניה, תשובה על תשובתו): The second teshuvah is teshuvah done on the first teshuvah — when one reaches greater divine perception, the previous level seems insufficient and one repents on it. This second/higher teshuvah is in its essential nature 'כבוד עולם הבא' (World-to-Come honor), equated in the text with 'כבוד אלקים הסתר דבר' — the concealed divine honor = keter. Three-node component merged into Package 1 by shared nature: upper-teshuvah = keter."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 6, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שִׂמְחָה וְזֵעָה טוֹבָה", "label_en": "Joy and Holy Sweat", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: the domain of holy vitality, purity, and divine joy. שמחה is the inner state; זיעה טובה is the physical vehicle that produces it; שמחת יום טוב names the category of that joy; דמים זכים are the purified blood that carries the joy; and 'זֶה הַיּוֹם עָשָׂה ה'' is the scriptural expression of that same joy (encoded in the very letters of the word זיעה). They are all faces of the same spiritual-physical reality: holy overflow producing rejoicing. The fact that the Rebbe spells out the acronym זיעה = 'זֶה הַיּוֹם עָשָׂה ה'' confirms that sweat and Yom Tov joy share the same essential nature, not merely a causal link.", "dominant_node": "שמחה", "nodes": ["שמחה", "זיעה טובה", "שמחת יום טוב", "יום טוב", "דמים זכים", "זה היום עשה ה'"], "node_degrees": {"שמחה": 3, "זיעה טובה": 2, "שמחת יום טוב": 2, "יום טוב": 1, "דמים זכים": 1, "זה היום עשה ה'": 1}, "mirror_package_id": 2, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שמחת יום טוב", "יום טוב"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly equates these: 'כָּל יוֹם שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת טוֹב, נִקְרָא יוֹם-טוֹב' — any day that is a bechina of 'good' is called Yom Tov. These are two expressions of the same concept, both bechinas of joy."}, {"merged_nodes": ["זה היום עשה ה'", "זיעה טובה"], "reason": "The Rebbe reveals that the letters of זֵיעָה are an acronym for 'זֶה הַיּוֹם עָשָׂה יְיָ', meaning these two expressions share the same essential nature — both are expressions of holy joy/Yom Tov. This is explicit bechina language in the text."}, {"merged_nodes": ["דמים זכים", "שמחה"], "reason": "Pure blood (דמים זכים) is the physiological substrate of joy (שמחה). The Rebbe states 'עִקָּר הָעַצְבוּת — עַל-יְדֵי הַטְּחוֹל' and by contrast, pure blood produces joy. This is the physiological face of the same spiritual reality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "נְקֻדָּה תַּחְתּוֹנָה — The Lower Point: Yehoshua, Moon, and Earth", "label_en": "The Lower Dot of the Aleph: Yehoshua, the Moon, and the Earthly Domain", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster is the lower structural pole of the Aleph (אָלֶ\"ף), which is the Torah's master metaphor for the triune structure of reality (upper point / vav / lower point = heaven / firmament / earth = Moshe / ohel / Yehoshua). The lower dot of the Aleph = יְהוֹשֻׁעַ = לְבָנָה (the moon) = 'וְהָאָרֶץ הֲדֹם רַגְלָי' (the earth as God's footstool) = נְקֻדַּת חִירִיק (the vowel-point for the lowest level). Yehoshua is the lower aspect because 'the destruction of Amalek's seed depends on Yehoshua' — he is the warrior who fights at ground level (נְקֻדָּה תַּחְתּוֹנָה = the aspect that subdues the nations beneath). The moon/levana is this point because it receives light from above (Moshe/sun = upper point) and rules the night-domain. Merged into this package: (a) דְּמִימָה ↔ 'הֲדֹם' ↔ הַנְּקֻדָּה הַתַּחְתּוֹנָה שֶׁל תְּמוּנַת א — silence/stillness is the lower point of the aleph shape; the Hebrew word 'הֲדֹם' (footstool) = דְּמִימָה (silence/stillness) because both describe the receptive-passive lower dimension; (b) תְּשׁוּבָה הָרִאשׁוֹנָה ↔ כְּבוֹד עוֹלָם הַזֶּה — the first (lower/initial) teshuvah is by nature the honor of this world — the earthly-level teshuvah corresponds to the lower point; (c) יְכַלּוּ זַרְעוֹ שֶׁל עֲמָלֵק ↔ 'יַדְבֵּר עַמִּים תַּחְתֵּינוּ' — Amalek's defeat = 'He subdues peoples beneath us' (Psalm 47) = the lower-point subduing the earthly enemies = same nature as Yehoshua/lower-point.", "dominant_node": "נקדה התחתונה", "nodes": ["נקדה התחתונה", "חיריק", "יהושע", "לבנה", "\"ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת\"", "\"ותחת רגליו\"", "\"והארץ הדם רגלי\"", "אטמא", "להכרית זרעו של עמלק", "\"הֲדֹם\"", "דמימה", "הנקדה התחתונה של תמונת א", "תשובה הראשונה", "כבוד עולם הזה", "יכלו זרעו של עמלק", "\"ידבר עמים תחתינו\""], "node_degrees": {"נקדה התחתונה": 3, "יהושע": 3, "חיריק": 2, "לבנה": 2, "\"והארץ הדם רגלי\"": 2, "דמימה": 2, "\"ותחת רגליו כמעשה לבנת\"": 1, "\"ותחת רגליו\"": 1, "אטמא": 1, "להכרית זרעו של עמלק": 1, "\"הֲדֹם\"": 1, "הנקדה התחתונה של תמונת א": 1, "תשובה הראשונה": 1, "כבוד עולם הזה": 1, "יכלו זרעו של עמלק": 1, "\"ידבר עמים תחתינו\"": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component ('הדם', דמימה, הנקדה התחתונה של תמונת א): The word 'הֲדֹם' (footstool) = דְּמִימָה (silence/stillness/arrest). Both are the essential nature of the lower point of the Aleph shape. דְּמִימָה literally means becoming still — the earth as God's footstool is the image of total stillness/receptivity beneath the divine. The lower point of the aleph-shape is this very stillness-point. Merged by shared nature: the footstool-stillness = lower point.", "Component (תשובה הראשונה, כבוד עולם הזה): The first (lower-level) teshuvah is in its essential nature the honor of this world. The Torah teaches a two-tier teshuvah: first is earthly-level teshuvah (honoring the Earthly domain), second is the concealed/divine teshuvah (= keter, Package 1). The first tier's essential nature aligns with the lower point (earthly, 'הֲדֹם') domain. Merged by shared nature: first/earthly teshuvah = lower-point domain.", "Component (יכלו זרעו של עמלק, 'ידבר עמים תחתינו'): The annihilation of Amalek's seed is equated with 'He subdues peoples beneath us' (Psalm 47:4) = the lower point (תַּחְתֵּינוּ = beneath us). Amalek's defeat is the lower-point's defining action — Yehoshua fights Amalek at the ground level. While node_id 'יכלו זרעו של עמלק' is distinct from 'להכרית זרעו של עמלק', both refer to the same theme and the former's bechina-partner 'ידבר עמים תחתינו' explicitly describes the lower-point domain. Merged by shared nature: lower-point subduing = lower-point cluster."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 6, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עַצְבוּת וְטֻמְאַת הַדָּם", "label_en": "Sadness and Impure Blood", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: the domain of the negative force, physical impurity, and spiritual depression. עצבות is the inner state of sadness; טחול is the organ that embodies it; עכירות הדמים is the physiological reality of turbid/impure blood that the spleen holds; and ארס is the toxin/venom within that impure blood that causes illness. The Rebbe explicitly equates: 'הָעַצְבוּת — מִן הַטְּחוֹל' and 'טְחוֹל הִיא עֲכִירוּת הַדָּמִים'. These three are bechinas of each other — different names for the same negative state at different levels of reality (spiritual/psychological, physiological, toxic).", "dominant_node": "עצבות", "nodes": ["עצבות", "טחול", "עכירות הדמים", "ארס"], "node_degrees": {"עצבות": 2, "טחול": 2, "עכירות הדמים": 2, "ארס": 1}, "mirror_package_id": 1, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עצבות", "טחול", "עכירות הדמים"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly collapses these three into one: 'הָעַצְבוּת — עַל-יְדֵי הַטְּחוֹל' and 'טְחוֹל הִיא עֲכִירוּת הַדָּמִים'. The spleen IS the turbid blood, and that turbid blood IS the sadness. Three names for the same negative reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ארס", "עכירות הדמים"], "reason": "The ארס (venom/toxin) is described as what is contained within the עכירות הדמים (turbid blood). It is the active harmful agent that is the same domain as the impure blood — the negative substance that causes illness and ultimately leaves via sweat."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "הַוָּא\"ו שֶׁבְּתוֹךְ הָאָלֶ\"ף — The Vav: Firmament, Shame, and Gaon Yaakov", "label_en": "The Middle Vav of the Aleph: Firmament, the Blush of Shame, and Royal Gaon", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The middle element of the Aleph (the vav/וָּא\"ו connecting upper and lower dots) = the רָקִיעַ (firmament) = the mediating space of fire and water = the phenomenon of בּוּשָׁה (blushing shame), because the firmament changes colors just as a face that blushes changes from red to white. The vav is also the אֹהֶל (tent = Moshe's tent of meeting = the space where upper and lower meet). 'לְמַנּוֹת לָהֶם מֶלֶךְ' = appointing a king = the nature of the firmament/vav, because the king is the mediating royal presence between heaven and earth. 'גְּאוֹן יַעֲקֹב' = the pride/greatness of Jacob = the nature of the appointed king = 'דָּרַךְ כּוֹכָב מִיַּעֲקֹב' (a star shall stride from Jacob) = the kingly comet/star that traverses from Yaakov = מֶלֶךְ. This cluster captures the mediating royal-shame dimension: shame is the emotion of standing between two poles (red/white, honored/shamed, heaven/earth) — the exact position of the vav/firmament.", "dominant_node": "וא\"ו שבתוך האלף", "nodes": ["וא\"ו שבתוך האלף", "רקיע", "אהל", "בושה", "נשתנה פניו לכמה גונין", "למנות מלך", "\"גאון יעקב\"", "\"דרך כוכב מיעקב\"", "מלך"], "node_degrees": {"וא\"ו שבתוך האלף": 4, "רקיע": 3, "למנות מלך": 2, "\"גאון יעקב\"": 2, "\"דרך כוכב מיעקב\"": 2, "בושה": 1, "נשתנה פניו לכמה גונין": 1, "אהל": 1, "מלך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "בָּקִי בְּרָצוֹא — Expert in Ascending", "label_en": "Bakhi b'Ratzo: The Upward Motion — Never Standing Still on a High Level", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster captures the first of two spiritual expertise-dimensions taught in Torah #6. 'בָּקִי בְּרָצוֹא' (expert in the outward rush) = the person who reaches a high spiritual level and does NOT rest there but continues to ascend higher — 'אַף עַל פִּי כֵן אֵינוֹ עוֹמֵד שָׁם, וְהוּא מִתְחַזֵּק וּמִתְגַּבֵּר לַעֲלוֹת יוֹתֵר וְיוֹתֵר' (even so he does not stay there, but strengthens himself to go higher and higher). This is equated with: 'עַיִל' (the entering/ascending motion), 'אֲנִי לְדוֹדִי' (I am for my beloved — the worshipper moving toward God), 'אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה' (if I ascend to heaven, You are there), and 'סֶגּוֹל דְּקס\"א' (the vowel-segol of the divine name Ehyeh=161, because 'אֶסַּק' has the same letters as 'קס\"א'). This is the ascending pole of the Ratzo-veShov (running and returning) dynamic from Ezekiel's vision — the 'חַיּוֹת רָצוֹא וָשׁוֹב' that the Torah uses as its structural metaphor for spiritual persistence. Together with Package 5 (Bakhi b'Shuv), this forms the two-dimensional expertise of the person on the path of teshuvah.", "dominant_node": "בקי ברצוא", "nodes": ["בקי ברצוא", "עיל", "הינו בין שיזכה לאיזו עליה, לאיזו מדרגה גדולה, אף על פי כן אל יעמד שם, ולא יסתפק עצמו בזה, רק צריך שיהיה בקי בזה מאד, לידע ולהאמין שהוא צריך ללכת יותר ויותר וכו", "סגול דקס\"א", "\"אם אסק שמים שם אתה\"", "אני לדודי"], "node_degrees": {"בקי ברצוא": 4, "עיל": 3, "הינו בין שיזכה לאיזו עליה, לאיזו מדרגה גדולה, אף על פי כן אל יעמד שם, ולא יסתפק עצמו בזה, רק צריך שיהיה בקי בזה מאד, לידע ולהאמין שהוא צריך ללכת יותר ויותר וכו": 1, "סגול דקס\"א": 1, "\"אם אסק שמים שם אתה\"": 1, "אני לדודי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "בָּקִי בְּשׁוֹב — Expert in Descending", "label_en": "Bakhi b'Shuv: The Downward Motion — Never Despairing Even in the Lowest Place", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster captures the second of two spiritual expertise-dimensions: 'בָּקִי בְּשׁוֹב' (expert in the inward/downward return) = the person who falls to a very low spiritual level and does NOT despair — 'שֶׁאֲפִלּוּ אִם יִפֹּל לִמְקוֹם שֶׁיִּפֹּל אֲפִלּוּ בִּשְׁאוֹל תַּחְתִּיּוֹת, גַּם כֵּן אַל יִתְיָאֵשׁ עַצְמוֹ לְעוֹלָם' (even if he falls to the lowest pit of the underworld, he must never despair). This is equated with: 'נָפִיק' (the exiting/descending motion), 'וְדוֹדִי לִי' (and my beloved is for me — God reaching down even to the fallen), 'וְאַצִּיעָה שְׁאוֹל הִנֶּךָּ' (if I make my bed in the underworld, You are there — Psalm 139), and 'חִירִיק דְס\"ג' (the vowel-chirik of the divine name Havayah=63, associated with the lowest level where God's presence still lives). This corresponds to the 'shoov' (returning) half of the Ratzo-veShov dynamic. The teaching is radical: expertise in falling without despairing is as important as expertise in rising. Together with Package 4, these two form the complete two-bechina teshuvah skill that produces the Way (דֶּרֶךְ = Package 1 merged).", "dominant_node": "בקי בשוב", "nodes": ["בקי בשוב", "נפיק", "שאפלו אם יפל, חס ושלום, למקום שיפל אפלו בשאול תחתיות, גם כן אל יתיאש עצמו לעולם, ותמיד יחפש ויבקש את השם יתברך, ויחזק עצמו בכל מקום שהוא, בכל מה שיוכל, כי גם בשאול תחתיות נמצא השם יתברך, וגם שם יכולין לדבק את עצמו אליו יתברך", "\"ואציעה שאול הנך\"", "ודודי לי", "חיריק דס\"ג"], "node_degrees": {"בקי בשוב": 3, "נפיק": 3, "שאפלו אם יפל, חס ושלום, למקום שיפל אפלו בשאול תחתיות, גם כן אל יתיאש עצמו לעולם, ותמיד יחפש ויבקש את השם יתברך, ויחזק עצמו בכל מקום שהוא, בכל מה שיוכל, כי גם בשאול תחתיות נמצא השם יתברך, וגם שם יכולין לדבק את עצמו אליו יתברך": 1, "\"ואציעה שאול הנך\"": 1, "ודודי לי": 1, "חיריק דס\"ג": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 6, "label": "בִּזְיוֹנוֹת וּמַחֲלֹקֶת — Shame, Strife, and the Evil Inclination", "label_en": "The Evil Mirror: Seeking Honor Produces Strife and the Withdrawal of God's Face", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil counterpart of the positive keter-teshuvah teaching. Torah #6 opens with the antithesis: the person who PURSUES honor (in contrast to the one who flees it) loses divine honor and gains instead 'כְּבוֹד מְלָכִים' — human/royal honor that is constantly challenged and scrutinized ('כְּבֹד מְלָכִים חֲקֹר דָּבָר'). The evil side of the teaching is expressed through two bechina-pairs: (1) נוֹצוֹת (feathers from the goose/wisdom bird) = מַחֲלֹקֶת וּבִזְיוֹנוֹת (strife and shaming) — the feathers plucked from the wisdom-goose represent the honor-seeker who plucks honor from wisdom without achieving it, producing only conflict; (2) בִּזְיוֹנוֹת וּשְׁפִיכוּת דָּמִים (shaming and bloodshed) = הַסְתָּרַת וְהַחְזָרַת פְּנֵי אקי\"ה (the concealing and revealing of the divine face of Ehyeh). The evil person who has abundant blood in the left chamber of the heart (the dwelling of the evil inclination) brings upon himself shaming and bloodshed — which is the divine face turning away and back. This is the structural opposite of Package 1: instead of silence-humility-teshuvah producing keter (divine honor concealed), honor-seeking produces public shaming and divine withdrawal. The two evil-domain components are merged by shared nature: both describe the consequences of ego-driven honor-pursuit = the antithesis of keter.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת ובזיונות", "nodes": ["נוצות", "מחלקת ובזיונות", "בזיונות ושפיכות דמים", "הסתרת והחזרת פני אקי\"ה"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת ובזיונות": 1, "נוצות": 1, "בזיונות ושפיכות דמים": 1, "הסתרת והחזרת פני אקי\"ה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component (נוצות, מחלקת ובזיונות): Goose-feathers = strife and shaming. The goose in Torah 6 is the wisdom-bird (חֲכָמִים = אַוָּזֵי, in Package 1). The feathers being plucked = the wisdom being stripped from its source by honor-seekers, leaving only strife. Evil mirror of the חכמים/אוזי node in Package 1. Merged into this evil package by shared nature: the evil consequence of honor-pursuit.", "Component (בזיונות ושפיכות דמים, הסתרת והחזרת פני אקי\"ה): The shaming and bloodshed experienced by the person with excess left-chamber blood (= evil inclination still strong) is in its essential nature the divine face (אקי\"ה = Ehyeh = keter-name) turning away and returning. This is the evil mirror of the keter-teshuvah teaching: instead of the keter-light descending, it hides. Merged into this evil package by shared nature: withdrawal of the divine keter-presence as consequence of evil inclination."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 7, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּתְפִלָּה", "label_en": "Emunah and Tefilla", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: the domain of faith, prayer, and miracles that transcend nature. The Torah opens by equating emunah, tefilla, and nisim as a single reality. Circles (egulim) express emunah's encompassing quality; isha is the receptive emunah; ish/emet are its masculine counterpart; Yaakov u-vanav are the 12 nuschaot of prayer; tehomot upper and lower are the two registers of miracles; prita sifvateih is the angel of prayer. All are faces of the same transcendent, supra-natural dimension.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "תפלה", "נסים", "עגול", "אשה", "למעלה מהטבע", "פריטא שפותה", "נסים תתאין", "תהומא תתאה", "נסין עלאין", "תהום עלאה", "יעקב ובניו", "שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה", "איש", "אמת", "ישמעאל"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 5, "תפלה": 5, "נסים": 3, "עגול": 1, "אשה": 1, "למעלה מהטבע": 2, "פריטא שפותה": 1, "נסים תתאין": 1, "תהומא תתאה": 1, "נסין עלאין": 1, "תהום עלאה": 1, "יעקב ובניו": 1, "שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה": 1, "איש": 1, "אמת": 1, "ישמעאל": 1}, "merge_notes": ["יעקב ובניו / שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה: merged in because יעקב ובניו is explicitly בחינת תפלה in the text (s.1) — 2-node component absorbed into the tefilla cluster.", "איש / אמת: merged in because the text explicitly equates אשה=אמונה and איש=אמת as one pair ('הִתְחַבְּרוּת אֱמֶת וֶאֱמוּנָה'); אשה is already in this package.", "ישמעאל: merged in via edge 3184 (ישמעאל — תפלה, reference 7, bechina). Yishmael's name encodes 'God heard my prayer' and the Targum renders this explicitly as tefilla.", "This package is the Torah's primary positive teaching about the supra-natural dimension of faith, prayer, and redemption."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּתְפִלָּה וְנִסִּים", "label_en": "Faith, Prayer, and Miracles", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of being above nature (לְמַעְלָה מֵהַטֶּבַע). Emunah is the prerequisite and essence of tefilla; tefilla is emunah in action; miracles are what is above nature; circles (egulim) are the form of emunah because faith surrounds and encompasses like a circle; woman/isha is the bechina of emunah in its receptive posture; Yishmael and Perita Sifvateh are both expressions of the mouth of prayer. The Torah explicitly equates all these as different facets of the same transcendent reality that breaks through the natural order.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "תפלה", "עגול", "אשה", "למעלה מהטבע", "נסים", "פריטא שפותה", "ישמעאל"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 5, "תפלה": 5, "עגול": 1, "אשה": 1, "למעלה מהטבע": 2, "נסים": 2, "פריטא שפותה": 1, "ישמעאל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 7, "label": "יוֹסֵף וְצִיצִית", "label_en": "Yosef and Tzitzit", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the nature of shmirat ha-brit — the guarding of the covenant of holiness. Yosef is the archetype of shmirat ha-brit; tzitzit is its practical expression (covering/protecting against sexual impurity); shekhem is the body location of tzitzit (shoulders); 'v'eleh' (the additive word) is its exegetical marker. The group forms the domain of holy guarding that enables receiving emet from tzaddikim.", "dominant_node": "יוסף", "nodes": ["יוסף", "תקון הברית", "ציצית", "שכם", "\"ואלה\" כל מקום שנאמר ואלה מוסיף"], "node_degrees": {"יוסף": 3, "תקון הברית": 3, "ציצית": 2, "שכם": 1, "\"ואלה\" כל מקום שנאמר ואלה מוסיף": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Note: edges 1290 and 3222 are both יוסף—תקון הברית (duplicate bechina stating the same equivalence); counted as 1 unique connection for degree purposes.", "Tzitzit is the practical guardian against the serpent's counsel — it enables passage from this package to the eitza package (counsel from tzaddikim)."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֵצָה וְנִשּׂוּאִין — צַד הַקְּדֻשָּׁה", "label_en": "Counsel and Marriage — Holy Side", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The core bechina of this Torah is that receiving advice from a person is structurally equivalent to receiving his seed — it is a form of marriage (zivug). Receiving advice from the tzaddik (counsel = holy marriage) is the bechina of yosef/tzitzit/shemirat habrit on the receiving side. Mount Sinai is where 613 counsels (etzot) were received, hence it is the bechina of etzot. The concept of 'ha-etzot she-mekabel me-hem' (the counsel one receives from tzaddikim) equals zivug/nissu'in in holiness. Note: the node 'etzot ha-nachash → nissu'in' appears here because it connects through the shared hub node 'nissu'in' — however the evil advice is structurally opposite in polarity; this is the good cluster anchored by etzah/nissu'in in holiness.", "dominant_node": "נשואין", "nodes": ["עצה", "נשואין", "העצות שמקבל מהם", "זווג", "הר סיני", "עצות הנחש"], "node_degrees": {"עצה": 2, "נשואין": 3, "העצות שמקבל מהם": 2, "זווג": 1, "הר סיני": 1, "עצות הנחש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עצות הנחש", "נשואין"], "reason": "The serpent's advice to Chava is explicitly called a bechina of nissu'in (marriage) in the proof text: 'ha-nachash hishi'ani — lashon nissu'in'. Although 'etzot ha-nachash' is evil, it connects through the hub node 'nissu'in' which is the structural concept unifying both sides. The evil polarity of this sub-node does not override the structural placement; it flags the dark-side dimension of the etzah-nissu'in bechina. The good/evil distinction within this component is captured in Package 3."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 7, "label": "עֵצָה וְנִשּׂוּאִין", "label_en": "Counsel and Marriage — the Holy Side", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the nature of holy counsel: the metaphysical structure whereby receiving advice from a tzaddik is like receiving seed (zera emet). Har Sinai is the prototype of holy counsel (613 eitin); the shureq vowel = 3 drops = 3 brains, embodying how the tzaddik's sekhel flows into the receiver through counsel. All are expressions of the same receptive, intellectual-generative structure of holy advice.", "dominant_node": "נשואין", "nodes": ["הר סיני", "עצה", "נשואין", "העצות שמקבל מהם", "זווג", "שורק", "תלת טפין", "תלת מחין"], "node_degrees": {"הר סיני": 1, "עצה": 2, "נשואין": 4, "העצות שמקבל מהם": 2, "זווג": 1, "שורק": 1, "תלת טפין": 2, "תלת מחין": 1}, "merge_notes": ["שורק / תלת טפין / תלת מחין: 3-node component merged into the counsel package because the shureq appears explicitly in the context of receiving the tzaddik's intellectual drops (tipei ha-sekhel) through counsel — same essential nature as the advice-as-seed metaphor.", "נשואין serves as the pivot node between the good and evil counsel packages (Package 4). The node 'נשואין' is shared in nature — advice has a marriage-structure regardless of whether it is holy or impure."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֵצוֹת רְשָׁעִים וְנִשּׂוּאֵי הַקְּלִפָּה", "label_en": "Evil Counsel and Marriage of the Klipa", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "When one receives advice (etzot) from wicked people, it is the bechina of marriage with the klipa (nissu'in ba-klipa). This is the direct evil mirror of Package 2: just as receiving the tzaddik's counsel = holy marriage/zivug, receiving the wicked person's counsel = defiled marriage/zivug of the sitra achra. The node 'nissu'in ba-klipa' is structurally separate from 'nissu'in' because the klipa marriage is not merely a corrupted version but an independent entity in the evil domain.", "dominant_node": "נשואין בקלפה", "nodes": ["כשמקבלין עצות מרשעים", "נשואין בקלפה"], "node_degrees": {"כשמקבלין עצות מרשעים": 1, "נשואין בקלפה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 7, "label": "עֲצַת הַנָּחָשׁ", "label_en": "The Serpent's Counsel — Evil Marriage", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the nature of impure counsel: receiving advice from the wicked is structurally a marriage with the klipa — it implants spiritual impurity (zuhama). The serpent's counsel to Eve is the prototype; 'nisuin ba-klipa' and niuf are its downstream expressions. This is the direct evil mirror of Package 3.", "dominant_node": "עצות הנחש", "nodes": ["עצות הנחש", "כשמקבלין עצות מרשעים", "נשואין בקלפה", "מנשואין של רשע", "נאוף"], "node_degrees": {"עצות הנחש": 1, "כשמקבלין עצות מרשעים": 1, "נשואין בקלפה": 1, "מנשואין של רשע": 1, "נאוף": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is the evil mirror of Package 3 (Counsel and Marriage). The node נשואין appears in Package 3; נשואין בקלפה is its impure counterpart — a separate node representing the same structural reality oriented toward evil.", "מנשואין של רשע connects to נאוף — the direct manifestation of evil counsel in sexual misconduct."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יוֹסֵף וְשְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית וְצִיצִית", "label_en": "Yosef, Covenant-Keeping, and Tzitzit", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the same essential nature of sexual holiness and the protection of the sacred covenant. Yosef is the archetypal figure who preserved the brit (covenant of chastity); shemirat ha-brit IS the bechina of Yosef; tzitzit is the bechina of shemirat ha-brit because the fringes of the garment guard against the eyes and against the serpent's advice (sexual temptation); Shechem in the verse 'your brothers are shepherding in Shechem' is identified as a bechina of tzitzit (both are worn on the shoulders). The text uses the acronym 'va-eleh mosif' (ve-eleh adds) to show Yosef is the aspect of addition/increase (brit = the capacity to add holy offspring).", "dominant_node": "תקון הברית", "nodes": ["יוסף", "תקון הברית", "ציצית", "שכם", "\"ואלה\" כל מקום שנאמר ואלה מוסיף"], "node_degrees": {"יוסף": 3, "תקון הברית": 3, "ציצית": 2, "שכם": 1, "\"ואלה\" כל מקום שנאמר ואלה מוסיף": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 7, "label": "שִׁכְחָה", "label_en": "Forgetting — the Anti-Emunah Force", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "מס (the letter-pair mem-samekh) and שכחה (forgetting) share the same essential nature: they are both expressions of the force that covers and negates the renewal that emunah embodies. The appendix explicitly says: 'מס הוא בחינת השכחה' and that nes (miracle) is the opposite of shikcha — because miracles embody emunah's perception of constant renewal, while forgetting embodies the naturalistic/astrological mindset that is the opposite of emunah. This 2-node component is preserved as its own package because it forms the explicit evil mirror of Package 1 (Emunah).", "dominant_node": "שכחה", "nodes": ["מס", "שכחה"], "node_degrees": {"מס": 1, "שכחה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["2-node component retained as a separate evil package rather than merged into Package 1 because it represents a distinct evil domain (the anti-emunah force of forgetting/naturalism) that mirrors Package 1's positive content.", "Would merge into Package 1 only if a polarity-neutral approach were taken, but the teaching's structure makes this a standalone evil counterpart."]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "נִאוּף וְנִשּׂוּאֵי הָרָשָׁע", "label_en": "Adultery and Marriage of the Wicked", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Marriage with a wicked person (nissu'in shel rasha) is structurally identical to adultery (ni'uf) — they are both expressions of the same spiritual defilement. This is the direct evil mirror of the Yosef/shemirat-ha-brit package: just as tzitzit protects from the serpent's counsel and wicked marriage, the evil side is defined by wicked marriage equaling ni'uf. The proof text states this explicitly: 'me-etzat ha-nachash, mi-nissu'in shel rasha, mi-bechina't ni'uf' — listing all three as equivalent evil states.", "dominant_node": "נאוף", "nodes": ["מנשואין של רשע", "נאוף"], "node_degrees": {"מנשואין של רשע": 1, "נאוף": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמֶת וְאִישׁ", "label_en": "Truth and the Male Principle", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Ish (man) is the bechina of emet (truth), just as isha (woman) is the bechina of emunah. Together, ish-isha = emet-emunah (truth-faith). The male principle (ish) embodies emet because emet is the giving, active, seeding quality — like the male who gives seed. The pair emet-ish is the good counterpart to the isha-emunah pair in Package 1. This Torah teaches that the union of ish and isha is the bechina of the union of emet and emunah, and on this the redemption depends.", "dominant_node": "אמת", "nodes": ["איש", "אמת"], "node_degrees": {"איש": 1, "אמת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["איש", "אמת"], "reason": "This pair belongs thematically to a broader truth/faith axis. Emet and emunah are closely related — emet is the active/male side and emunah the receptive/female side. The pair ish/emet should ideally merge with Package 1 (אמונה/תפלה) but the bechina connection between אמת and אמונה in this Torah is recorded as EITZA (edge 335: 'al yedei emet efchar lavo le-emunah'), not bechina. Per the rules, we cannot merge across eitza boundaries. Hence ish-emet remains its own package with a note that it is structurally related to Package 1."}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "נִסִּין עִלָּאִין וְתְּהוֹם עִלָּאָה", "label_en": "Upper Miracles and the Upper Deep", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The upper miracles (nisin ila'in) are the bechina of the upper deep (tehom ila'ah). This is the supernal/upper dimension of the miracle-faith axis. The Torah describes two levels of miracles: nisin ila'in (upper miracles, corresponding to the supernal tehom) and nisin tata'in (lower miracles, corresponding to tehoma tata'ah). The upper level is associated with the higher unity (yichuda ila'ah) and deeper faith.", "dominant_node": "נסין עלאין", "nodes": ["נסין עלאין", "תהום עלאה"], "node_degrees": {"נסין עלאין": 1, "תהום עלאה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נסין עלאין", "תהום עלאה"], "reason": "This pair is thematically part of Package 1 (faith/prayer/miracles) — it describes the upper dimension of 'nisim'. However, there is no direct bechina edge connecting this pair to the main nisim/emunah cluster in the dataset. It is therefore kept as a distinct package but flagged as belonging to the same thematic domain as Package 1. A gap-analysis addition is proposed."}]}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "נִסִּים תַּתָּאִין וּתְּהוֹמָא תַתָּאָה", "label_en": "Lower Miracles and the Lower Deep", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The lower miracles (nisin tata'in) are the bechina of the lower deep (tehoma tata'ah). This is the earthly/lower dimension of the miracle-faith axis, the expression of divine intervention within the lower worlds. Together with Package 7, they form the two dimensions (upper and lower) of the miracle-reality.", "dominant_node": "נסים תתאין", "nodes": ["תהומא תתאה", "נסים תתאין"], "node_degrees": {"תהומא תתאה": 1, "נסים תתאין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תהומא תתאה", "נסים תתאין"], "reason": "Like Package 7, this pair is thematically part of the miracle/faith axis. The lower and upper tehom/nisin pairs (Packages 7 and 8) mirror the two-level structure of faith that this Torah teaches: emunah operates both above and below nature. These two packages could be merged with Package 1, but there is no direct bechina edge connecting tehoma/nisin tata'in to the emunah/tefilla cluster nodes. Kept separate pending gap-analysis verification."}]}, {"id": 9, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יַעֲקֹב וּבָנָיו וּשְׁנֵים-עָשָׂר נֻסְחָאוֹת הַתְּפִלָּה", "label_en": "Jacob and His Sons and the Twelve Prayer Formulas", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Jacob and his sons are the bechina of the twelve gates/formulas of prayer. The Torah explains that precisely Jacob and his sons descended to Egypt — because they are the bechina of prayer (twelve gates of prayer), and thus prayer itself went into exile. This pairs with the emunah/tefilla cluster but is a distinct structural node (twelve = the twelve tribes = twelve prayer gates).", "dominant_node": "יעקב ובניו", "nodes": ["יעקב ובניו", "שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב ובניו": 1, "שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יעקב ובניו", "שנים עשר נסחאות התפלה"], "reason": "This pair is thematically part of the emunah/tefilla package (Package 1). The twelve prayer formulas connect directly to tefilla. However, the bechina edge does not connect these nodes directly to the main tefilla cluster — so it remains a separate component. Proposed for merge into Package 1 via gap-analysis addition."}]}, {"id": 10, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שׁוּרֻק וְטִפֵּי הַשֵּׂכֶל", "label_en": "The Shoruq Vowel, Three Drops, and Three Brains", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The shoruq vowel (three dots forming the u-sound) is the bechina of three drops (talt tipin), because the shoruq shape consists of three dots. Those three drops are in turn the bechina of the three brains (talt mochin = chochma, bina, da'at), because the drop of seed comes from the brain/intellect and reaches the kidney-vessels that produce seed. This is the Kabbalistic concept that intellectual/spiritual seed (the tzaddik's advice = drops of his intellect, as in 'hu mekabel tipei hasechel shel ha-tzaddik') flows like a drop from brain to reproductive organ. This cluster is NEUTRAL because it describes a structural/anatomical correspondence (vowel sign, drops, brains) that operates in both holy and profane contexts.", "dominant_node": "תלת טפין", "nodes": ["שורק", "תלת טפין", "תלת מחין"], "node_degrees": {"שורק": 1, "תלת טפין": 2, "תלת מחין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 11, "torah_ref": 7, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַס וְשִׁכְחָה", "label_en": "The Letter Mem-Samech and Forgetting", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The letters mem and samech (which together spell 'mas' = taxation/burden) stood miraculously in the tablets; they are the bechina of forgetting (shikha). The connection: the Talmud says 'mem and samech in the tablets stood by miracle' (Shabbat 104a) — implying these letters were suspended without support, relating to the concept of forgetting (things that lose their support/foundation and disappear from consciousness). This is a neutral/structural bechina — a letter-concept pair that describes how forgetting operates in the spiritual-cognitive domain. Its thematic connection to the Torah is through the tzitzit/brit/memory axis (tzitzit helps against forgetting).", "dominant_node": "שכחה", "nodes": ["מס", "שכחה"], "node_degrees": {"מס": 1, "שכחה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מס", "שכחה"], "reason": "This isolated pair connects thematically to the broader Torah through the emunah/forgetting axis — the Torah explicitly links emunah and its opposite (shikha/forgetting). However, the only bechina edge for this pair in this Torah is within the pair itself. Kept as its own package pending the gap-analysis check for whether additional bechina edges should connect it to Package 1 (emunah/tefilla), which is the domain of memory vs. forgetting in this Torah."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 8, "label": "רוּחַ חַיִּים", "label_en": "Breath of Life", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of ruach (breath/spirit) as the life-force that animates every thing. Includes: the sigh (אנחה) as the drawing of that spirit; ארך אפים (long-breathing) as the nature of the sigh; שלמות החסרון / מלאפום as what the ruach fills; the body organs (חטם, פניו) through which ruach passes; צפון-wind and David's harp as expressions of the same holy spirit; the letter vav and the encompassing (מקיף) as further faces of ruach-drawing. The tzaddik's 13 attributes of mercy are also the holy ruach. Dominant node is רוח חיים with the highest degree (6 bechina edges).", "dominant_node": "רוח חיים", "nodes": ["רוח חיים", "ארך אפים", "אנחה הוא אריכת הנשימה", "רוח, שהוא מאריך רוחה", "אנחה", "ארבע ציצית", "רוח הצפון בלבו של אדם", "רוח צפון", "מקיף", "נגון של כנור של דוד", "שלש עשרה מדות", "שלמות החסרון", "\"ויתן לך משאלת לבך\".", "\"הרחב פיך ואמלאהו\"", "מלאפום", "נתמלא החסרון", "טלית", "רוח מארבע רוחות", "חטם", "רוח", "פניו", "רוח צפונית", "רוח החיים", "אות וא\"ו", "המשכת הרוח", "חיות של אותו הדבר", "רוח חיים של אותו הדבר"], "node_degrees": {"רוח חיים": 6, "ארך אפים": 3, "מלאפום": 3, "רוח, שהוא מאריך רוחה": 2, "רוח הצפון בלבו של אדם": 2, "שלמות החסרון": 2, "רוח": 2, "אנחה הוא אריכת הנשימה": 1, "אנחה": 1, "ארבע ציצית": 1, "רוח צפון": 1, "מקיף": 1, "נגון של כנור של דוד": 1, "שלש עשרה מדות": 1, "\"ויתן לך משאלת לבך\".": 1, "\"הרחב פיך ואמלאהו\"": 1, "נתמלא החסרון": 1, "טלית": 1, "רוח מארבע רוחות": 1, "חטם": 1, "פניו": 1, "רוח צפונית": 1, "רוח החיים": 1, "אות וא\"ו": 1, "המשכת הרוח": 1, "חיות של אותו הדבר": 1, "רוח חיים של אותו הדבר": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged raw component (שלמות החסרון, מלאפום, נתמלא החסרון, הרחב פיך ואמלאהו, ויתן לך משאלת לבך): these 5 nodes share the nature of 'filling of the void', which is what the ruach-chaim accomplishes — same essential nature, not a downstream effect.", "Merged raw component (טלית, רוח מארבע רוחות): the holy tallit draws spirit from four winds — same nature as ruach-chaim drawing.", "Merged raw component (חטם, רוח, פניו): body organs that are bechina of ruach — same essential nature as life-breath.", "Merged raw component (רוח צפונית, רוח החיים): direct bechina of holy ruach-chaim.", "Merged raw component (אות וא״ו, המשכת הרוח): vav is the drawing of the spirit — same essential drawing-of-breath nature.", "Merged raw component (חיות של אותו הדבר, רוח חיים של אותו הדבר): the vitality of any thing IS its ruach-chaim — identical nature stated explicitly."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קוֹל וְרֵיחַ", "label_en": "Voice and Scent", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The kol (voice) is the central mechanism that 'waters the garden' and causes all scents to grow. The scent IS the yirah (fear of God), which IS the soul's nourishment. The future song is a bechina of this same kol. Yaakov's kol is its embodiment. The river from Eden is its cosmic form. The chut shel chesed (thread of kindness) is the string on which this song plays. All these nodes share the same essential nature: they are expressions of the positive, life-giving, spirit-sustaining resonance that comes from holy speech.", "dominant_node": "קוֹל", "nodes": ["קוֹל", "רֵיחַ", "יִרְאָה", "מְזוֹנָא דְנִשְׁמָתָא", "שִׁיר חָדָשׁ", "שִׁיר פָּשׁוּט כָּפוּל מְשֻׁלָּשׁ מְרֻבָּע", "קוֹל יַעֲקֹב", "נָהָר יוֹצֵא מֵעֵדֶן", "חוּט שֶׁל חֶסֶד", "נִגּוּן", "עֲנָוָה"], "node_degrees": {"קוֹל": 7, "רֵיחַ": 4, "יִרְאָה": 3, "מְזוֹנָא דְנִשְׁמָתָא": 2, "שִׁיר חָדָשׁ": 3, "שִׁיר פָּשׁוּט כָּפוּל מְשֻׁלָּשׁ מְרֻבָּע": 2, "קוֹל יַעֲקֹב": 2, "נָהָר יוֹצֵא מֵעֵדֶן": 2, "חוּט שֶׁל חֶסֶד": 2, "נִגּוּן": 2, "עֲנָוָה": 1}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["קול הוא בחינת נהר יוצא מעדן (§1: ונהר יצא מעדן להשקות את הגן — נשאו נהרות קולם)", "קול הוא בחינת קול יעקב (§1: הקל קול יעקב)", "ריח is בחינת יראה: והריחו ביראת ה' (§1, citing Isaiah 11:3)", "ריח is בחינת מזונא דנשמתא (§1: עיקר יניקת הנשמה הוא מהריח)", "יראה is בחינת עקב (§1, citing Proverbs 22:4: עקב ענוה יראת ה')", "שיר פשוט כפול משולש מרובע = כשופר (§1: כשופר ר\"ת פשוט כפול שלוש רבוע)", "חוט שֶׁל חֶסֶד = בחינת הנימין (strings) עליהם יתנגן הניגון (§1)", "שיר חדש is בחינת הקול המשקה את הגן (§10)"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עֲנָוָה", "יִרְאָה"], "reason": "Proverbs 22:4: 'עקב ענוה יראת ה' — anava (humility) and yirah (fear) are stated as one compound, both being bechina of the heel/scent domain. The text equates the holy ekev (heel) with yirat Hashem which is the scent. Anava is what that yirah rests upon — same domain of the scent-soul-nourishment cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נִגּוּן", "שִׁיר חָדָשׁ"], "reason": "The niggun and the shir chadash are explicitly identified as the same entity throughout the Torah (§10, §12). The niggun of the future is the shir chadash which is the shir pashut kaful. They are not cause-and-effect but different names for the same musical-spiritual reality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 8, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּבְרִיאָה בְּכֹחַ", "label_en": "Prayer and Potential Creation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of 'creation in potential' (בריאה בכח) — the latent, hidden dimension above nature. Prayer is bechina of חדוש העולם (renewal of the world), of עדן (the hidden Garden), of עדן עין לא ראתה (the future hidden light), and of בריאה בכח. חכמה (wisdom) is a spring (מעין) — the potential wellspring activated by prayer. The התעוררות החכמה that comes through prayer is also בריאה בכח. These nodes all inhabit the domain of the hidden/potential before it is actualized into Torah-study.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "בריאה בכח", "התעוררות החכמה שהוא בתפלה", "חדוש העולם", "עדן", "עדן עין לא ראתה", "אור הגנוז לעתיד", "חכמה", "מעין"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 4, "בריאה בכח": 2, "עדן עין לא ראתה": 2, "חכמה": 2, "מעין": 2, "התעוררות החכמה שהוא בתפלה": 1, "חדוש העולם": 1, "עדן": 1, "אור הגנוז לעתיד": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged raw component (חכמה, מעין): חכמה is the spring/מעין that flows from prayer. Its essential nature — the latent wellspring of wisdom — is the same dimension as 'potential creation' (בריאה בכח). Connected to תפלה via its bechina relationship to התעוררות החכמה שהוא בתפלה.", "Note: עדן עין לא ראתה initially formed its own 2-node component with אור הגנוז לעתיד, but was then connected to תפלה via edge 39, making it part of this component in the primary pass."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְרַחֲמָנוּת", "label_en": "Prayer and Compassion", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Prayer is essentially mercy and supplication (§2: 'עיקר התפלה הוא רחמים ותחנונים'). Mercy depends on da'at — and da'at is what sustains it. The three brains produce three types of mercy which are the three daily prayers. The mateh oz (staff of strength) is the bechina of the din-prayer of the baal koach — it is still a form of prayer, not cause-and-effect. These all share the essential nature of being the positive upward movement of the soul toward God.", "dominant_node": "תְּפִלָּה", "nodes": ["תְּפִלָּה", "רַחֲמָנוּת", "דַּעַת", "שְׁלֹשָׁה מֹחִין", "שָׁלֹשׁ תְּפִלּוֹת", "שַׁדַּי", "מַטֵּה עֹז", "תְּפִלָּה בִּבְחִינַת דִּין", "פִּינְחָס", "בַּעַל כֹּחַ"], "node_degrees": {"תְּפִלָּה": 6, "רַחֲמָנוּת": 4, "דַּעַת": 4, "שְׁלֹשָׁה מֹחִין": 3, "שָׁלֹשׁ תְּפִלּוֹת": 2, "שַׁדַּי": 2, "מַטֵּה עֹז": 3, "תְּפִלָּה בִּבְחִינַת דִּין": 2, "פִּינְחָס": 2, "בַּעַל כֹּחַ": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["תפלה is בחינת רחמים ותחנונים (§2, citing Avot 2)", "שדי is בחינת רחמנות דקדושה (§2, citing Genesis 43:14: ואל שדי יתן לכם רחמים — זה בחינת תפלה)", "שלשה מוחין → שלשה מיני רחמנות → שלש תפלות: 'כל תפלה ותפלה הוא בחינת מוח מיוחד' (§2)", "מטה עז הוא בחינת תפלת הבעל כח בבחינת דין (§3: ויעמד פינחס ויפלל = תפלה בבחינת דין)", "תפלה בבחינת דין: בצוארו ילין עז (§3, Job 41:14)", "פינחס is בחינת הבעל כח המתפלל תפלה בבחינת דין (§3)"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תְּפִלָּה בִּבְחִינַת דִּין", "תְּפִלָּה"], "reason": "The 'prayer of din' is still prayer — it is the same essential act of human soul reaching toward God, but in the key of judgment rather than mercy. The Rebbe presents it as a special mode of prayer, not a different domain. The mateh oz IS a prayer; the whole discussion of Pinchas is about a form of tfilah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פִּינְחָס", "בַּעַל כֹּחַ"], "reason": "Pinchas IS the baal koach in this Torah. He is the embodiment of the one with the holy power to pray with din. These are the same node described from different angles."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 8, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּבְרִיאָה בְּפֹעַל", "label_en": "Torah and Actual Creation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of 'creation in act' (בריאה בפועל) — Torah is the craftsman/אמן through which the world was actually created. The כנור of David (5 strings = 5 Books of Moses) is bechina of Torah. The spirit of Torah (רוח מ״ם = 40 days of Sinai, also ורוח אלקים מרחפת על פני המים) and the lance/רומח are all expressions of Torah as actualized creative force. The souls of Israel who study Torah are the garden-plants that grow in the Torah-garden.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "בריאה בפעל", "כנור של חמש נימין", "רוח מ\"ם", "\"ורוח אלקים מרחפת על פני המים\"", "רומח", "נשמות ישראל המעינים ומבינים בהתורה", "עשבין ודשאין דאתרביאו בגן"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 2, "רוח מ\"ם": 2, "בריאה בפעל": 1, "כנור של חמש נימין": 1, "\"ורוח אלקים מרחפת על פני המים\"": 1, "רומח": 1, "נשמות ישראל המעינים ומבינים בהתורה": 1, "עשבין ודשאין דאתרביאו בגן": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged raw component (רוח מ״ם, ורוח אלקים מרחפת, רומח): the spirit/ruach of Torah given at 40 days, which is also the divine spirit hovering over the waters (Torah). The lance (רומח) is the same bechina — instrument that draws/projects the spirit of Torah.", "Merged raw component (נשמות ישראל, עשבין ודשאין): souls studying Torah are garden-plants — their essential nature is as fruits/students of the Torah-garden, which is itself the Torah."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "סִטְרָא אָחֳרָא וְנָחָשׁ", "label_en": "The Sitra Achra and the Serpent", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The sitra achra and the nachash are the evil mirror of Package 2. Where prayer/mercy/da'at is the positive domain, the sitra achra sucks from mercy, corrupts it into cruelty, and sucks from da'at. The nachash = 'evil da'at'. Amalek = the nachash that was ערום (cunning from fallen da'at). The clouds/kelipot block the prayer from ascending. Cruelty (akzariyut) is what the sitra achra produces when it sucks from rachmanus. Lust (taavat niuf) is what comes when da'at is damaged. All these share the essential nature of being the corruption of the holiness domain of Package 2.", "dominant_node": "סִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "nodes": ["סִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "נָחָשׁ", "עָקֵב דְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "אַכְזָרִיּוּת", "עֲמָלֵק", "קְלִפּוֹת", "עָנָן", "תַּאֲוַת נִיאוּף", "דַּעַת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "רַחֲמָנוּת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת"], "node_degrees": {"סִטְרָא אָחֳרָא": 6, "נָחָשׁ": 4, "עָקֵב דְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא": 2, "אַכְזָרִיּוּת": 2, "עֲמָלֵק": 2, "קְלִפּוֹת": 2, "עָנָן": 2, "תַּאֲוַת נִיאוּף": 2, "דַּעַת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא": 2, "רַחֲמָנוּת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא": 2, "מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["רחמנות דסטרא אחרא = שד משדי (§2, citing Isaiah 13:6: כשד משדי)", "גם תנינים חלצו שד = בחינת רחמנות דסטרא אחרא (§2, citing Lamentations 4:3)", "והנחש היה ערום = דעת דסטרא אחרא (§2: שיונק על ידי נפילת הדעת דקדושה)", "עמלק is בחינת: ראשית גוים עמלק = והנחש היה ערום (§3: עמלק יונק מבחינת הדעת)", "קלפות/ענן = סכות בענן לך מעבור תפלה (§4, citing Lamentations 3:44: הקלפות המעכבים את התפלה)", "עקב דסטרא אחרא is בחינת גדלות through eating (§1: אוכל לחמי הגדיל עלי עקב)"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עָנָן", "קְלִפּוֹת"], "reason": "The text explicitly equates them: 'ענני דשפכי מיא — זה בחינת הסטרא אחרא, שהיא בחינת סכות בענן לך מעבור תפלה, הינו הקלפות המעכבים את התפלה' (§4). Cloud and kelipot are identical in this Torah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת", "דַּעַת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא"], "reason": "Mochin d'katnut is what the sitra achra produces when it damages da'at (§2: 'נקטן הדעת ונעשה בבחינת מוחין דקטנות, ואזי הם יונקים מפגם הדעת'). Both are expressions of the same deficient-consciousness state that enables the evil side to draw sustenance."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 8, "label": "עֵשָׂו וְרוּחַ הַטֻּמְאָה", "label_en": "Esav and the Impure Wind", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the evil counterpart to the holy ruach-chaim: the storm-wind (רוח סערה) of Esav, who draws his vitality from the evil Rav (רב דקלפה). אלופי עשו / אלפי are the commanders of Esav's camp (the klipa-rav's officers). כשתויי יין (the wine-drunk) is bechina of Esav the red/Edomite. The evil tallit (טלית דקלפה = טלית אדום, the red garment = Esav's hairy garment) corresponds to the holy tallit. רוח הטמאה is bechina of רוח סערה, the violent storm-wind that Esav draws — great in its moment but with no permanence. This is the evil mirror of Package 1 (רוח חיים).", "dominant_node": "עשו", "nodes": ["עשו", "אלופי עשו", "רב דקלפה", "אלפי", "כשתויי יין", "טלית דקלפה", "טלית אדום", "רוח הטמאה", "רוח סערה"], "node_degrees": {"עשו": 2, "אלופי עשו": 2, "רב דקלפה": 2, "אלפי": 1, "כשתויי יין": 1, "טלית דקלפה": 1, "טלית אדום": 1, "רוח הטמאה": 1, "רוח סערה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged raw component (טלית דקלפה, טלית אדום): the evil/red tallit is Esav's 'hairy garment' — it is explicitly the evil counterpart of the holy four-winged tallit. אדום = Edom = Esav. Same essential nature as the Esav cluster.", "Merged raw component (רוח הטמאה, רוח סערה): the impure wind is bechina of the storm-wind, and the Torah text explicitly links this to 'הן עשו אחי איש שעיר' — Esav the hairy one. Same essential nature as the Esav cluster."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "כָּבוֹד וּנְבוּאָה", "label_en": "Glory and Prophecy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Kavod and nevuah are identified as sharing the same root — the root of the souls of Israel. The kavod is what the soul-roots are (§5: 'שורש הנפשות הוא בחינת כבוד'). Nevuah flows from kavod precisely because they share the same nature. Gerim (converts) are a bechina of thunder, which is a bechina of kavod-revelation. The teki'ah (shofar blast) is explicitly called a bechina of kavod-revelation. All these are expressions of the divine glory made manifest through the Jewish people's spiritual root.", "dominant_node": "כָּבוֹד", "nodes": ["כָּבוֹד", "נְבוּאָה", "שׁוֹרֶשׁ הַנְּפָשׁוֹת", "גֵּרִים", "רְעָמִים", "שִׁבְעִים נֶפֶשׁ", "תְּקִיעָה", "תְּרוּעָה", "שְׁכִינָה", "רוּחַ נְבוּאָה", "מְנוּחָה"], "node_degrees": {"כָּבוֹד": 7, "נְבוּאָה": 6, "שׁוֹרֶשׁ הַנְּפָשׁוֹת": 2, "גֵּרִים": 3, "רְעָמִים": 2, "שִׁבְעִים נֶפֶשׁ": 2, "תְּקִיעָה": 2, "תְּרוּעָה": 2, "שְׁכִינָה": 2, "רוּחַ נְבוּאָה": 3, "מְנוּחָה": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["שורש הנפשות is בחינת כבוד: 'בסודם אל תבא נפשי ובקהלם אל תחד כבודי' (§5)", "גרים = רעמים (§4: 'צרוף גרים הוא ראשי תיבות: רעם גבורתו מי יתבונן')", "רעמים = בחינת כבוד (§4: 'אל הכבוד הרעים, ה' על מים רבים')", "תקיעה = התגלות הכבוד (§11: 'תקיעה זה בחינת התגלות הכבוד — ותקעתיו יתד במקום נאמן והיה לכסא כבוד')", "תרועה = רוח נבואה (§11: 'תרועה זה בחינת רוח נבואה — ה' אלהיו עמו ותרועת מלך בו, ותרגומו: ושכינת מלכיהון בהון')", "מנוחה = נבואה (§7/11: 'מנוחה זה בחינת נבואה — ומנוחה לא מצאתי, זו נבואה')", "שבעים נפש = שורש נבואה (§5: 'בשבעים נפש ירדו אבותיך — ראשי תיבות נביא')"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שְׁכִינָה", "תְּרוּעָה"], "reason": "The Targum on 'ותרועת מלך בו' is 'ושכינת מלכיהון בהון' — the teru'ah IS the Shekhinah's presence. They are explicitly equated, not cause-and-effect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["רוּחַ נְבוּאָה", "נְבוּאָה"], "reason": "Ruach nevuah and nevuah are the same domain — the Rebbe uses them interchangeably throughout (§7, §8). 'רוח נבואה' is simply the name for nevuah as an experiential reality (a ruach that settles on the prophet)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מְנוּחָה", "נְבוּאָה"], "reason": "From the Midrash cited in §7: 'ומנוחה לא מצאתי — זו נבואה'. The word menuchah in the verse about Baruch ben Neriyah is identified as prophecy. This is a direct bechina equation."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמוּנָה וְכֹחַ הַמְדַמֶּה", "label_en": "Faith and the Imagination-Power", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Emunah and the koach hamadame operate in exactly the same space — where intellect ends, imagination takes over, and that is precisely where faith is needed and exercised (§7: 'עיקר האמונה היא בבחינת המדמה'). The renewal of the world depends on emunah, which means it also depends on the purified madame. Eretz Yisrael is a bechina of chiddush ha'olam, which is a bechina of hashgacha. The shvarim (broken shofar blasts) are a bechina of shattering false beliefs. The chag (holiday) is a bechina of madame (from 'ובמחוגה יתארהו'). All these are expressions of the domain of believing/imagining the divine reality beyond intellect.", "dominant_node": "אֱמוּנָה", "nodes": ["אֱמוּנָה", "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה", "חִדּוּשׁ הָעוֹלָם", "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "הַשְׁגָּחָה", "שְׁבָרִים", "חַג", "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת", "חֶסֶד"], "node_degrees": {"אֱמוּנָה": 7, "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה": 4, "חִדּוּשׁ הָעוֹלָם": 4, "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל": 3, "הַשְׁגָּחָה": 3, "שְׁבָרִים": 2, "חַג": 2, "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת": 2, "חֶסֶד": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["אמונה is בחינת כח המדמה: 'עיקר האמונה היא בבחינת המדמה' (§7)", "כח המדמה is בחינת לילה/חלום: 'אמונתך בלילות — זה בחינת האמונה שתלויה בכח המדמה שהוא בחינת לילה, בחינת חלום לילה' (§9)", "חידוש העולם תלוי באמונה: 'עיקר חידוש העולם הוא רק על ידי אמונה' (§9)", "חידוש העולם is בחינת ארץ ישראל (§10: 'לעתיד יחדש ה' את כל העולם בבחינת ארץ ישראל')", "ארץ ישראל = בחינת השגחה (§10: 'עיקר קדושת ארץ ישראל הוא ששם הוא השגחתו יתברך תמיד')", "שברים = בטול אמונות כזביות (§11, citing Exodus 23:24: שבר תשבר מצבותיהם)", "חג = כח המדמה (§11: 'לעתיד לום חגנו — זה בחינת אמונה שנתתקן על ידי תיקון כח המדמה שהוא בחינת חג, בחינת ובמחוגה יתארהו')", "עשרה מאמרות = כלי מלאכה של מעשה בראשית = אמונה בחידוש העולם (§Og: 'נרגא בת עשר אמין... על ידי נבואה זוכין לאמונה שהיא בחינת עשרה מאמרות')"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חֶסֶד", "חִדּוּשׁ הָעוֹלָם"], "reason": "§9 explicitly states: 'עולם חסד יבנה — זה בחינת חידוש העולם לעתיד'. The chesed and the chiddush ha'olam are the same event/domain — the world is built/renewed through chesed. They share the same essential nature."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמוּנוֹת כָּזְבִּיּוֹת וּמַנְהִיג שֶׁל שֶׁקֶר", "label_en": "False Beliefs and the False Leader", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 5. Where true emunah and purified imagination belong to the good side, false beliefs and the corrupted imagination belong to the evil side. The false prophet/leader corrupts the madame, which produces false beliefs. The serpent's filth (zuhamat hanachash) IS false beliefs. This whole cluster is about the perversion of the imagination-faith domain.", "dominant_node": "אֱמוּנוֹת כָּזְבִּיּוֹת", "nodes": ["אֱמוּנוֹת כָּזְבִּיּוֹת", "נְבִיא הַשֶּׁקֶר", "מַנְהִיג שֶׁל שֶׁקֶר", "רוּחַ שֶׁקֶר", "זֻהֲמַת הַנָּחָשׁ", "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה הַמְקֻלְקָל"], "node_degrees": {"אֱמוּנוֹת כָּזְבִּיּוֹת": 5, "נְבִיא הַשֶּׁקֶר": 3, "מַנְהִיג שֶׁל שֶׁקֶר": 2, "רוּחַ שֶׁקֶר": 2, "זֻהֲמַת הַנָּחָשׁ": 2, "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה הַמְקֻלְקָל": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["מנהיג של שקר = נביא השקר = רוח שקר (§8, citing I Kings 22: 'מנהיג שקר הוא בחינת נביא השקר, בחינת רוח שקר')", "זוהמת הנחש = אמונות כזביות (§8: 'ערוב ובלבול המדמה שמערב ומבלבל באמונות כזביות, זה בחינת זוהמת הנחש')", "כח המדמה המקולקל → אמונות כזביות (§8: 'בלא בחינת נבואה אין המדמה ברור ומתוקן, ואזי המדמה מערב ומבלבל את האדם באמונות כזביות')", "פסקה זוהמתן = שזכו לנבואה ונתברר המדמה ועל ידי זה זכו לאמונה שלמה (§8: 'ישראל שעמדו על הר סיני פסקה זוהמתן')"], "mirror_of_package": 5, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נְבִיא הַשֶּׁקֶר", "מַנְהִיג שֶׁל שֶׁקֶר"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly equates them: 'מנהיג של שקר הוא בחינת נביא השקר'. They are two names for the same corrupting force in the domain of leadership/guidance."}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 8, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הָרֵאָה", "label_en": "The Lung", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The lung is the structural metaphor that contains all the positive themes of this Torah within the human body. The Rebbe explicitly opens §12 with: 'זה בחינת הריאה. כשמזג הריאה בשלמות, נמצאין בה כל הבחינות הנ\"ל' — 'this is the aspect of the lung; when the lung is in balance, all the above aspects are found within it.' The lung is neutral because it is a physical organ used as a metaphor; it is not itself aligned with good or evil but serves as the structural locus of all the spiritual realities discussed.", "dominant_node": "רֵאָה", "nodes": ["רֵאָה", "גָּרוֹן", "כַּנְפֵי הָרֵאָה", "נְשִׁימָה", "צִינָּה וּקְרִירוּת"], "node_degrees": {"רֵאָה": 8, "גָּרוֹן": 3, "כַּנְפֵי הָרֵאָה": 3, "נְשִׁימָה": 2, "צִינָּה וּקְרִירוּת": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["ריאה = ציר נאמן לשולחיו (§12: הבעל כח הוא בחינת ציר נאמן — כצנת שלג ביום קציר כן ציר נאמן)", "ריאה = קרירות (§12: 'ריאה מקררת חמימות הגוף')", "גרון = ריאה (§12: 'כבד את ה' מהונך — אל תקרי מהונך אלא מגרונך, והגרון יוצא מהריאה')", "כנפי ריאה = רוח נבואה (§12: 'הרוח של כנפי ריאה שהוא בחינת רוח נבואה')", "ריאה = כח המדמה: 'שינה ודמיון הוא תלוי בריאה' (§12)", "ריאה = חידוש העולם: 'תשלח רוחך יבראון ותחדש פני אדמה, בחינת הרוח של כנפי ריאה' (§12)", "ריאה = ארץ ישראל: 'למה נקרא שמה ריאה שמאירת את העינים — זה בחינת תמיד עיני ה' אלהיך בה' (§12)", "גרון/ריאה = ניגון (§12: 'קרא בגרון אל תחשך, כשופר הרם קולך. והגרון הוא הריאה')"], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְסִימָנֶיהָ", "label_en": "Prayer and Its Symbols", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central cluster of the teaching. תפלה is directly equated with: אמונה (faith), עמידה (standing), חרב (sword), עפר (dust), חטם (nose/smell), סוחר ישמעאל (the Ishmaelite trader), רגלים (feet), ארץ ישראל (Land of Israel), and נסים (miracles). All these are different symbolic faces of the same spiritual reality: sincere prayer directed toward God. The chain is tight — עמידה↔תפלה, חרב↔תפלה, עפר↔חרב (via תפלה), עפר↔חרב directly, ארץ ישראל↔תפלה (×2), חטם↔תפלה, רגלים↔תפלה, סוחר ישמעאל↔תפלה — all connect into one large component through the תפלה hub. אמונה connects via תפלה↔אמונה and סביב↔אמונה. נסים connects via תפלה↔נסים, ארץ ישראל↔נסים, תהומות↔נסים. The Rebbe equates all of these as expressions of the same transcendent reality that breaks nature.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "אמונה", "עמידה", "חרב", "עפר", "חטם", "סוחר ישמעאל", "רגלים", "ארץ ישראל", "נסים", "תהומות", "סביב", "\"שפכי לבך כמים נכח פני ה'\"", "מים"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 9, "אמונה": 3, "ארץ ישראל": 3, "נסים": 3, "עמידה": 2, "חרב": 2, "עפר": 2, "סוחר ישמעאל": 2, "מים": 1, "\"שפכי לבך כמים נכח פני ה'\"": 1, "סביב": 1, "חטם": 1, "רגלים": 1, "תהומות": 1}, "merge_notes": ["מים and 'שפכי לבך כמים' (2-node component) merged here: both are aspects of prayer reaching God, directly paralleling תפלה and the water/pouring imagery. The bechina states that distance from 'water' = distance from the prayer of 'pour out your heart like water before God' — this is a sub-aspect of תפלה's nature.", "סביב↔אמונה merged here: in the teaching, סביב (surrounding/circling) equals אמונה because 'ואמונתך סביבותיך' — סביב is thus a face of אמונה which is itself a face of תפלה.", "תהומות merged here: תהומות = נסים, and נסים = תפלה — same cluster.", "מצולת and מצרים kept in Package 2 — they are the evil mirror."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 9, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִשְׂרָאֵל וְהַלֵּב", "label_en": "Israel and the Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "ישראל, לבא, and לבא דכל עלמא share a single essential nature: Israel collectively IS the heart of the world. The Rebbe cites the Zohar (Pinchas 221b): 'ישראל הם לבא דכל עלמא.' The two nodes לבא and לבא דכל עלמא are two expressions of the same identity — the first names the organ, the second names its cosmic scope. ישראל is the node that carries both. All three form a single bechina cluster: they are not three different things connected causally, but one reality described from three angles. The heart-nature of Israel means they burn (or fail to burn) for God, they are the living fire of the world, and their capacity to burn depends on the leaders removing the עפרוריות from them.", "dominant_node": "ישראל", "nodes": ["ישראל", "לבא", "לבא דכל עלמא"], "node_degrees": {"ישראל": 2, "לבא": 1, "לבא דכל עלמא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "מִצְרַיִם וְהַגָּלוּת", "label_en": "Egypt and Exile — the Anti-Prayer", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "מצולת (the depths/abyss) is equated with מצרים (Egypt) — this is the evil counterpart to the prayer/miracle cluster. Egypt is explicitly described as the opposite of Eretz Israel: 'זה לעומת זה', the place where prayer cannot ascend, where miracles are suppressed, where nature reigns over transcendence. The Rebbe says those who deny miracles and cover them with 'natural explanations' are in the bechina of Egypt/exile. This forms the evil mirror to Package 1.", "dominant_node": "מצרים", "nodes": ["מצולת", "מצרים"], "node_degrees": {"מצולת": 1, "מצרים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component is retained as an independent evil package rather than merged into Package 1, because it represents the structural evil mirror (זה לעומת זה) of the prayer/miracle cluster. Merging it into Package 1 would collapse the polarity distinction that is fundamental to the teaching's structure.", "The text explicitly frames מצרים as the inversion of ארץ ישראל — this is exactly the Good/Evil mirror pattern from framework §4."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 9, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַנְהִיגֵי הַדּוֹר וְהָרוּחַ", "label_en": "Leaders of the Generation and the Wind", "polarity": "good", "reason": "מנהיגי הדור and רוח share the essential nature of the life-giving breath/spirit. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'מנהיגי הדור הם בחינת רוח, כמו שכתוב: איש אשר רוח בו — שיודע לילך נגד רוחו של כל אחד.' The leader's defining quality is that he IS wind — he knows how to move according to each person's spirit, to blow across the heart and reignite its fire. This is not cause-and-effect (the leader does not produce wind); rather, the leader IS a bechina of wind, they share the same essential nature: movement, breath, the animating invisible force that goes against and with each person's inner direction. This package is the good mirror to Package 3 (the destructive storm-wind), and it connects by eitza to Package 1 (the leaders blow on Israel's heart).", "dominant_node": "מנהיגי הדור", "nodes": ["מנהיגי הדור", "רוח"], "node_degrees": {"מנהיגי הדור": 1, "רוח": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר וּמָשִׁיחַ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik of the Generation and Mashiach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Three nodes connect in a chain: סוחר ישמעאל ↔ צדיק הדור ↔ משיח. The Ishmaelite trader is a bechina of the tzaddik of the generation (section ד of the teaching), and the tzaddik is a bechina of Mashiach — because the tzaddik contains all the prayers just as Mashiach does. This is conceptually distinct from Package 1: these nodes describe the *bearer* of the prayers (the tzaddik/Mashiach), not the prayer itself. The tzaddik knows how to route each prayer to its proper gate — this is his essential nature as the comprehensive vessel. סוחר ישמעאל belongs here (not Package 1) as its primary bechina in this Torah is צדיק הדור, not תפלה.", "dominant_node": "צדיק הדור", "nodes": ["סוחר ישמעאל", "צדיק הדור", "משיח"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק הדור": 2, "סוחר ישמעאל": 2, "משיח": 1}, "merge_notes": ["סוחר ישמעאל appears in both Package 1 (תפלה↔סוחר ישמעאל) and Package 3 (סוחר ישמעאל↔צדיק הדור). Per the framework, every node belongs to exactly one package per Torah. The stronger conceptual home is Package 3: in section ד, סוחר ישמעאל is introduced as an allegory for the tzaddik of the generation, not for prayer itself. The bechina to תפלה (Package 1) is secondary — prayers are what the trader/tzaddik *handles*, but his nature is tzaddik/Mashiach. Assign to Package 3; the connection to Package 1 flows as an eitza-adjacent link.", "פרוטה↔קול התור is a 2-node component (see Package 4) — not merged here because it belongs thematically to the miracle/nature domain."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 9, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עַפְרוּרִיּוּת וּמָרָה שְׁחוֹרָה", "label_en": "Dust-Particles and Melancholy", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "עפרוריות and מרה שחורה share the essential nature of spiritual suffocation — the substance that falls on the heart and separates the fire-parts so the person can no longer burn for God. The proof text explicitly equates them: 'לנפח מהם העפרוריות, דהינו מרה שחורה שנופל עליהם' — עפרוריות IS מרה שחורה, the 'that is' (דהינו) signals direct bechina identity. Physiologically, the parallel is to dust falling on a candle-flame, causing its fire-particles to separate and the flame to go out. Spiritually, melancholy is precisely that dust: it separates the inner fire of Israel's soul. Both are expressions of the same reality: the particulate-matter that smothers inner fire. This is the primary evil force the Torah addresses. The wicked (ערב רב) who fall upon Israel are also a bechina of עפרוריות (see additions file).", "dominant_node": "עפרוריות", "nodes": ["עפרוריות", "מרה שחורה"], "node_degrees": {"עפרוריות": 1, "מרה שחורה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "פְּרוּטָה וְקוֹל הַתּוֹר", "label_en": "The Coin and the Voice of the Turtledove", "polarity": "good", "reason": "פרוטה (the coin, the small thing) is a bechina of קול התור (voice of the turtledove). The Talmud's image of 'the coin in the pocket' stands between two depths (תהומות), representing the hidden, compressed miracle that stands ready to be revealed. This is the image of latent miracles: things that appear small and natural but contain concentrated supernatural potential. In section ה the Rebbe uses the Talmudic passage about rudya (the mill-wheel) whose lips resemble a dove, standing between two abysses — the two תהומות = the collective of miracles. This 2-node component belongs thematically with the miracle cluster (Package 1) but represents a specific symbolic register — the *compressed hidden miracle* — that deserves its own label.", "dominant_node": "קול התור", "nodes": ["פרוטה", "קול התור"], "node_degrees": {"פרוטה": 1, "קול התור": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Candidate for merger into Package 1 (נסים cluster): both פרוטה and קול התור are aspects of miracles. Merged into Package 1 per Step 6 rule — shared NATURE (both are bechina of נסים / hidden miracle), not cause-and-effect. See additions file for final recommendation."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 9, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רוּחַ סְעָרָה וּבְעִירָה חוּץ מִן הַמִּדָּה", "label_en": "Storm-Wind and Burning Beyond Measure", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "רוח סערה and כשרואה אדם שבוער ביותר חוץ מהמדה share the essential nature of destructive excess — the force that inflames beyond the proper measure. The proof text: 'כשמנשב רוח סערה, אזי הוא מבעיר האש מאד חוץ מן המדה. כמוכן לפעמים יש שמנשב בהאדם בחינת רוח סערה, ומבעירו ביותר חוץ מן המדה.' The sign that a person has been struck by רוח סערה is precisely that they burn beyond measure — so the burning-beyond-measure IS the bechina of רוח סערה in the human domain. This is not the extinguishing force (Package 3) but its opposite: destructive over-ignition, the storm that shatters instead of nourishing. The Rebbe frames this as הריסה (breaking/destruction), citing 'פן יהרסו לעלות אל ה'' — the forbidden attempt to ascend beyond one's level. Elijah could ride and subdue these fiery horses. This package is the evil mirror to Package 2 (good measured wind vs. destructive unmeasured storm).", "dominant_node": "רוח סערה", "nodes": ["רוח סערה", "כשרואה אדם שבוער ביותר חוץ מהמדה"], "node_degrees": {"רוח סערה": 1, "כשרואה אדם שבוער ביותר חוץ מהמדה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "מַטֶּה — זִוּוּג וּפַרְנָסָה", "label_en": "The Staff/Tribe — Union and Livelihood", "polarity": "good", "reason": "מטה (staff/tribe) is simultaneously a bechina of זווג (marital union) AND a bechina of ממון (livelihood/sustenance). These two edges share the same node מטה. The Rebbe explicitly brings both equations: 'מטה לשון זווג' (the staff is an idiom for union) and 'מטה הוא בחינת פרנסה, כמו שכתוב בשברי לכם מטה לחם'. The twelve tribes (שנים עשר מטות) produce both union (זווג) and sustenance (פרנסה) through prayer. זווג and ממון are thus two faces of the same reality mediated by מטה.", "dominant_node": "מטה", "nodes": ["מטה", "זווג", "ממון"], "node_degrees": {"מטה": 2, "זווג": 1, "ממון": 1}, "merge_notes": ["זווג and ממון are structurally the earthly outputs of prayer (Package 1) — they appear in the teaching as the rewards/parallels of prayer (קשין מזונותיו כקריעת ים סוף / קשה זיווגו). They are connected to Package 1 via eitza, not bechina. The מטה package represents the *earthly domain* that mirrors the spiritual prayer structure."]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "וְקָם — עֲמִידָה בִּתְפִלָּה", "label_en": "Rising / Standing in Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "וקם (and he rose) ↔ עמידה (standing). This bechina pair is directly connected to the prayer cluster via עמידה↔תפלה (Package 1). The word 'וקם' from the verse 'דרך כוכב מיעקב וקם שבט מישראל' is interpreted as the act of standing to pray. Since עמידה is already in Package 1, וקם merges into Package 1 as well.", "dominant_node": "עמידה", "nodes": ["וקם", "עמידה"], "node_degrees": {"וקם": 1, "עמידה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["וקם is a single-alias bechina for עמידה, which itself is already in Package 1 (עמידה↔תפלה). This 2-node component merges entirely into Package 1. The וקם node is best understood as a textual alias of עמידה in Torah #9."]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 9, "label": "יַעֲקֹב וּבָנָיו — שְׁנֵים עָשָׂר שְׁבָטִים", "label_en": "Jacob and His Sons — Twelve Tribes as Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "יעקב ובניו ↔ תפלה (Jacob and his sons = prayer) AND יעקב ובניו ↔ שנים עשר שערי תפלה (= the twelve gates of prayer). These are two separate bechina edges both pointing from the same node. Jacob and his sons are the embodiment of the twelve prayer registers — each tribe has its unique prayer gate. This is the primary identification in Torah #9: Jacob encompasses all twelve tribes/prayers, and his 'gathering of his feet onto the bed' is interpreted as gathering all the prayers to their root.", "dominant_node": "יעקב ובניו", "nodes": ["יעקב ובניו", "שנים עשר שערי תפלה"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב ובניו": 2, "שנים עשר שערי תפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["יעקב ובניו is already connected to Package 1 via יעקב ובניו↔תפלה. Per framework rule: every node belongs to exactly one package. The richer conceptual home is Package 1 (תפלה is the dominant hub). Both יעקב ובניו and שנים עשר שערי תפלה merge into Package 1.", "שנים עשר שערי תפלה is a sub-aspect of the overall תפלה cluster — it specifies the structural architecture (twelve gates) of what תפלה is."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 10, "label": "יָדַיִן וְרַגְלַיִן", "label_en": "Hands and Feet", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of ידים/רגלים — the revelation of divine light through the body's extremities. The teaching equates: the explicit Torah (nigleh) = hands; the esoteric Torah (nistar) = feet; Mordechai = hands (dror/freedom = engraving on tablets); Esther = feet (shokayin/hidden); clapping (hamchat kaf) = hands; dancing (rikudin) = feet; kimu = feet; kiblu = hands; the omer = Mordechai; the barley = Esther; the Shechina = isha = Esther; KBH = ish = Mordechai. All these are bechina-equivalent expressions of the same limb-structure of holiness.", "dominant_node": "רגלים", "nodes": ["רגלים", "ידים", "ידים ורגלים", "המחאת כף", "רקודים", "הארת הידים", "הארת הרגלים", "התגלות הארת הידים", "נשא לבו את רגליו", "מרדכי", "אסתר", "אסתר ברוח הקדש", "שעורים", "עמר שעורים", "עמר", "שוקים", "אשה", "שכינה", "איש", "קדשא בריך הוא", "נגלה", "נסתר", "קבלו", "קימו", "ערבי נחל", "ערבין", "חרות על הלחת", "תורה שבנגלה", "\"עין בעין\"", "\"דודי שלח ידו מן החור\"", "תורה", "\"רגלי עמדה במישור\"", "אמונה"], "node_degrees": {"רגלים": 6, "ידים": 4, "ידים ורגלים": 2, "המחאת כף": 3, "רקודים": 3, "הארת הידים": 2, "הארת הרגלים": 1, "התגלות הארת הידים": 1, "נשא לבו את רגליו": 1, "מרדכי": 4, "אסתר": 3, "אסתר ברוח הקדש": 2, "שעורים": 1, "עמר שעורים": 1, "עמר": 1, "שוקים": 1, "אשה": 2, "שכינה": 1, "איש": 2, "קדשא בריך הוא": 1, "נגלה": 1, "נסתר": 1, "קבלו": 1, "קימו": 1, "ערבי נחל": 2, "ערבין": 1, "חרות על הלחת": 2, "תורה שבנגלה": 2, "\"עין בעין\"": 1, "\"דודי שלח ידו מן החור\"": 1, "תורה": 1, "\"רגלי עמדה במישור\"": 1, "אמונה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw component already contains 31 nodes.", "Merged in 2-node isolated component {\"רגלי עמדה במישור\", אמונה}: 'רגלי עמדה במישור' is the positive state of the feet (Tehilim 26:12), equated with emunah — the direct output of the hands/feet system being rectified. Shared nature: emunah = the stable feet = the rectified foot-dimension of holiness."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 10, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שִׂמְחָה וְעוֹלָם הַחֵרוּת", "label_en": "Joy and the World of Freedom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "שמחה (Joy) and עולם החרות (the world of freedom) share the same essential nature: they are two expressions of the same inner reality of liberation. The Rebbe states explicitly 'שִׂמְחָה הוּא עוֹלָם הַחֵרוּת' — joy IS the world of freedom. They are not two different things where one produces the other; joy itself is the domain of freedom, and freedom itself is what joy expresses. Both describe the state of the mind and spirit when unchained from depression, exile, and constraint. This is the positive pole of the Torah: the holy domain of simcha as the path back to God.", "dominant_node": "שמחה", "nodes": ["שמחה", "עולם החרות"], "node_degrees": {"שמחה": 1, "עולם החרות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 10, "label": "צַדִּיק וְרוּחַ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Ruach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of the tzaddik as the spiritual wind/spirit that animates and rectifies. The teaching equates: tzaddik = ruach (Bamidbar 27); ruach shbalev = the tzaddik; Yosef = the tzaddik (the one through whom hands and feet are raised); 'dofeik' (knocking) = ruach (Tikkunim 25:68); mishpatim = ruach (Yeshayahu 28:6); Yaakov = sekhel/wisdom (Onkelos on Bereshit 27); the tzaddik = son of wisdom (av = wisdom). Tikkun hame'uvat = Yosef's domain. Shimon (the rebuker) and 'ki shama H' ki senuah anochi' share the nature of the one who hears and rebukes — the tzaddik's corrective function.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "רוח", "רוח שבלב", "יוסף", "דופק", "משפטים", "תקון המעות", "יעקב", "חכמה", "שמעון", "\"כי שמע ה כי שנואה אנכי\""], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 3, "רוח": 3, "רוח שבלב": 1, "יוסף": 2, "דופק": 1, "משפטים": 1, "תקון המעות": 1, "יעקב": 1, "חכמה": 1, "שמעון": 1, "\"כי שמע ה כי שנואה אנכי\"": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: {צדיק, רוח, רוח שבלב, יוסף, דופק, משפטים, תקון המעות} — 7 nodes.", "Merged {יעקב, חכמה}: Yaakov = sekhel is the tzaddik's cognitive dimension; the text explicitly states 'Yaakov hu bechina hasechel' (section ד). Nature: wisdom/sekhel = tzaddik's inner identity = the rebuking authority.", "Merged {שמעון, כי שמע ה}: Shimon represents the tzaddik's rebuke capacity — 'the one who rebukes is hated' (Ketubot 105b). Shared nature: corrective/rebuking function of tzaddik-wisdom."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 10, "label": "גַּאֲוָה וַעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה", "label_en": "Pride and Idolatry", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of the evil side — pride as idolatry, and its associated manifestations. The teaching equates: המן = avodah zarah (the Sages say he made himself an idol); גאוה = avodah zarah (Mishlei 16: tovat H kol gevah lev); תוספות/ribui = gaavah (Devarim 7: lo meruvchem; also 've'eleh mosif al harishonim' = Haman-Amalek); גאות = המן's defining quality. The minim (heretics) and 'natavu raglai' share the evil nature — heresy = the fallen/deflected feet. Sitra demota = akum = the evil dimension opposed to God's glorification. The body/sack (guf) without correction is the vessel of persistent desires — not inherently evil but in this Torah appears in the context of ba'alei gaavah whose fasting fails.", "dominant_node": "גאוה", "nodes": ["גאוה", "עבודה זרה", "המן", "גאות", "תוספות", "מינות", "נטיו רגלי", "סטרא דמותא", "עכו\"ם", "שק", "גוף"], "node_degrees": {"גאוה": 2, "עבודה זרה": 2, "המן": 2, "גאות": 1, "תוספות": 1, "מינות": 1, "נטיו רגלי": 1, "סטרא דמותא": 1, "עכו\"ם": 1, "שק": 1, "גוף": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: {גאוה, עבודה זרה, המן, גאות, תוספות} — 5 nodes.", "Merged {מינות, נטיו רגלי}: מינות (heresy/atheism) is the evil counterpart to אמונה; 'natavu raglai' is the fallen feet — the evil version of the feet dimension. Shared nature: the deflected/corrupted foot-dimension of evil = minut.", "Merged {סטרא דמותא, עכו\"ם}: Both express the domain of death/evil from which idolatry emanates; akum = sitra demota in this Torah. Shared nature: the realm of spiritual death that opposes divine revelation.", "Merged {שק, גוף}: The body-as-sack metaphor appears in the context of ba'alei gaavah — their fasting empties the sack but leaves the holes (desires). The nature is: body = vessel of uncorrected desires = the physical substrate of gaavah."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 10, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּבַיִת", "label_en": "Prayer and the House", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Both pairs share the nature of prayer as the elevated, accessible form of divine service — the 'bayit' level where even non-Jews can grasp God's greatness. 'Eishet' = roshei teivot 'Adonai Sefatai Tiftach' = tefila. 'Bayit' = ir Elokeinu = the place of prayer accessible to all peoples (Yeshayahu 56:7). These two pairs are thematically identical: the essence of prayer as the bayit-house, the settled/inhabited place of the divine. They form the stated goal of the Torah's first sections (gimel-dalet).", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "אשת", "בית", "עיר אלקינו"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "אשת": 1, "בית": 1, "עיר אלקינו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Two isolated 2-node components merged: {אשת, תפלה} + {בית, עיר אלקינו}.", "Justification: Both express the nature of prayer as the 'bayit' form — the accessible, inhabited manifestation of divine service. 'Eishet' = tefila (roshei teivot Adonai Sefatai Tiftach). 'Bayit' = ir Elokeinu = the place of tefila for all peoples. The Torah explicitly links these: 'ki veiti beit tefila yikarei lekhol ha'amim' (Yeshayahu 56:7). Shared nature: tefila at the bayit/ir level."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "יִחוּדָא עִלָּאָה וְקַבָּלָה", "label_en": "Upper Unity and Kabbalah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the nature of the upper, Shabbat-level divine unity — the higher covenant (brit ila'ah), kabbalah / esoteric wisdom, the שדי-name in its Shabbat dimension, and the posuk 'Hishtachavu' whose initials spell Kabbalah. שדי also equates to 'there is sufficiency in My divinity for all creatures,' the theological ground of the upper covenant. שמש (sun) = ברית עלאה connects via shared nature of upper/solar brit. All 10 nodes belong to the same essential domain: the supernal unity expressed through the Shabbat-time covenant observance and esoteric wisdom.", "dominant_node": "יחודא עלאה", "nodes": ["יחודא עלאה", "\"השתחוו לה בהדרת קדש\"", "קבלה", "רזין דרזין", "מי שהוא שומר הברית, שזווגו משבת לשבת", "שדי", "שדי של שבת", "שיש די באלקותי לכל בריה", "שמש", "ברית עלאה"], "node_degrees": {"יחודא עלאה": 4, "\"השתחוו לה בהדרת קדש\"": 2, "קבלה": 2, "רזין דרזין": 1, "מי שהוא שומר הברית, שזווגו משבת לשבת": 1, "שדי": 2, "שדי של שבת": 1, "שיש די באלקותי לכל בריה": 1, "שמש": 1, "ברית עלאה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 9 ({שמש, ברית עלאה}) merged in: שמש = ברית עלאה shares the nature of the upper/solar covenant, directly parallel to שדי של שבת = יחודא עלאה (same Shabbat-level, upper-unity domain).", "שיש די באלקותי לכל בריה merged in via shared node שדי (edge 2875), extending the שדי cluster within this package."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 11, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִחוּדָא עִלָּאָה וְיִחוּדָא תַּתָּאָה", "label_en": "Upper and Lower Unification — The Two Modes of Covenant", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package contains all concepts that are expressions of the two modes of divine unity (yichuda ila'a = Shabbat union; yichuda tata'a = weekday union) and their structural equivalents. They share the essential nature of brit/covenant: the two forms of conjugal holiness (Shabbat-to-Shabbat and weekday-within-Torah-law), the halakha/kabbalah pair (lower and upper mystery), Metatron who mediates the weekday, the grasses (small people) and trees (great people), and the divine name Shaday. All are expressions of the same underlying reality: the boundary (tzimtzum) between domains that constitutes holy union.", "dominant_node": "יחודא תתאה", "nodes": ["יחודא תתאה", "יחודא עלאה", "יחודא עלאה ויחודא תתאה", "שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות", "\"השתחוו לה' בהדרת קדש\"", "מי שהוא שומר הברית, שזווגו משבת לשבת", "שדי", "שדי של שבת", "שיש די באלקותי לכל בריה", "קבלה", "רזין דרזין", "\"הריעו לה כל הארץ\"", "הלכה", "רזין", "התלבשות הקבלה בהלכה", "הנהגת הקדוש ברוך הוא בששת ימי החל", "דשאים", "זיז", "מי שזווגו בששת ימי החל, ואף על פי כן הוא שומר את בריתו על פי התורה, שאינו יוצא מדיני התורה", "מט\"ט", "שדי של חל", "מכנף הארץ", "ששה סדרי משנה", "שמש", "ברית עלאה", "משכן בבנינה", "ל\"ט אורות", "צבי לצדיק", "קדשת הזווג"], "node_degrees": {"יחודא תתאה": 7, "יחודא עלאה": 5, "יחודא עלאה ויחודא תתאה": 3, "הלכה": 3, "מט\"ט": 4, "\"הריעו לה כל הארץ\"": 2, "שדי": 2, "\"השתחוו לה' בהדרת קדש\"": 2, "קבלה": 2, "הנהגת הקדוש ברוך הוא בששת ימי החל": 2, "התלבשות הקבלה בהלכה": 2, "דשאים": 1, "זיז": 1, "מי שזווגו בששת ימי החל, ואף על פי כן הוא שומר את בריתו על פי התורה, שאינו יוצא מדיני התורה": 1, "שדי של שבת": 1, "שדי של חל": 1, "מכנף הארץ": 1, "ששה סדרי משנה": 1, "שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות": 1, "רזין": 1, "רזין דרזין": 1, "מי שהוא שומר הברית, שזווגו משבת לשבת": 1, "שיש די באלקותי לכל בריה": 1, "שמש": 1, "ברית עלאה": 1, "משכן בבנינה": 1, "ל\"ט אורות": 1, "צבי לצדיק": 1, "קדשת הזווג": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שמש", "ברית עלאה"], "reason": "The upper covenant (brit ila'a) IS the sun (proof: 'brit ila'a she-hu bechinas hashemesh'). This pair is the Shabbat-level brit, a direct expression of yichuda ila'a."}, {"merged_nodes": ["משכן בבנינה", "ל\"ט אורות"], "reason": "The Tabernacle in its building-state IS the 39 lights. This is the positive pole of the brit: one who guards the covenant, his 39 labors are like the Tabernacle's construction — full of holy light. This is the reward-side of yichuda/brit."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צבי לצדיק", "קדשת הזווג"], "reason": "From the source text (§7): 'tzvi latzaddik' means the beauty of holy conjugation. It contains both razin (halacha = yichuda tata'a) and razin d'razin (kabbalah = yichuda ila'a). The essential nature is the same as the whole package: holy conjugal union."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "יִחוּדָא תַּתָּאָה וּמְטַ\"ט וַהֲלָכָה", "label_en": "Lower Unity, Metatron, and Halacha", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the nature of the lower, weekday-level divine governance — Metatron (מט\"ט) rules the six weekdays, the halacha / revealed wisdom in its weekday dimension, the posuk 'Harie'u' whose initials spell Halacha, the deshe (grass/small people with frequent conjugal union), and the Shabbat-Shekhinah garbed in Metatron during the six days. הנהגת הקדוש ברוך הוא בששת ימי החל = יחודא תתאה = the core nature of this package. זיז שדי = Metatron (the great bird of the field) = lower unity. ששה סדרי משנה and מכנף הארץ are further aspects of Metatron's domain.", "dominant_node": "יחודא תתאה", "nodes": ["יחודא תתאה", "\"הריעו לה כל הארץ\"", "הלכה", "רזין", "דשאים", "הנהגת הקדוש ברוך הוא בששת ימי החל", "זיז", "מי שזווגו בששת ימי החל, ואף על פי כן הוא שומר את בריתו על פי התורה, שאינו יוצא מדיני התורה", "מט\"ט", "שדי של חל", "מכנף הארץ", "ששה סדרי משנה", "התלבשות הקבלה בהלכה"], "node_degrees": {"יחודא תתאה": 6, "\"הריעו לה כל הארץ\"": 2, "הלכה": 2, "רזין": 1, "דשאים": 1, "הנהגת הקדוש ברוך הוא בששת ימי החל": 2, "זיז": 1, "מי שזווגו בששת ימי החל, ואף על פי כן הוא שומר את בריתו על פי התורה, שאינו יוצא מדיני התורה": 1, "מט\"ט": 4, "שדי של חל": 1, "מכנף הארץ": 1, "ששה סדרי משנה": 1, "התלבשות הקבלה בהלכה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 11, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פְּגַם הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Covenant Violation — The Broken Union", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package contains all expressions of covenant violation and its consequences. They share the essential nature of 'tosafot' — the forbidden addition/overflow that violates the boundary of holiness. The verse 'arbaim yakenu velo yosif' (40 lashes, not one more) IS the brit violation: going beyond what is permitted. The destroyed Tabernacle and the 39 lashes are two faces of the same damaged reality. This is the exact evil mirror of Package 1.", "dominant_node": "פגם הברית", "nodes": ["פגם הברית", "\"ארבעים יכנו ולא יוסיף\"", "ל\"ט מלקות", "תוספות", "משכן בחרבנה", "עצבות", "ל\"ט מלאכות"], "node_degrees": {"\"ארבעים יכנו ולא יוסיף\"": 2, "פגם הברית": 2, "ל\"ט מלקות": 2, "תוספות": 1, "משכן בחרבנה": 1, "עצבות": 1, "ל\"ט מלאכות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עצבות", "ל\"ט מלאכות"], "reason": "From the source text (§4 context): one who violates the covenant draws upon himself the yoke of livelihood — the 39 labors (melacha) become a burden, and this burden produces sadness (atzvut). The source text explicitly states the 39 melachot are what the covenant-violator draws upon himself instead of the 39 lights. Depression (atzvut) is a state that characterizes this domain — it IS the experience of the 39 melachot as burden."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "דִּבּוּר הַמֵּאִיר וּתְבוּנוֹת הַתּוֹרָה", "label_en": "Illuminating Speech and Torah Depth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the nature of the illuminating word / Torah depth dimension. The bird (צפור / צפרתא) is the bechina of illuminating speech, which mediates between מים (= the human being at the lower level) and שמים (= the depths of Torah). תבן = תבונה, שמים = תבונות התורה — the upper destination of illuminating speech. מים = אדם — the lower starting point. The צפור stands ankle-deep in מים (=אדם) with its head in the heaven (=תבונות התורה), embodying the mediating role of illuminating speech.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["צפור", "דבור", "דבור המאיר", "תבן", "תבונה", "שמים", "תבונות התורה", "מים", "אדם"], "node_degrees": {"צפור": 2, "דבור": 1, "דבור המאיר": 1, "תבן": 1, "תבונה": 1, "שמים": 1, "תבונות התורה": 1, "מים": 1, "אדם": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 7 ({תבן, תבונה, שמים, תבונות התורה}) merged in: these share the nature of Torah depth, which is the upper pole of the bird-metaphor (the bird's head reaches the heavens = תבונות התורה). Same conceptual domain as illuminating speech.", "Component 8 ({מים, אדם}) merged in: מים = אדם is the lower pole of the bird-metaphor (the bird stands ankle-deep in water = the human being at a low level). Same structural image as the speech package."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 11, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דִּבּוּר הַמֵּאִיר", "label_en": "Illuminated Speech — The Bird Between Water and Heaven", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package contains all concepts that share the essential nature of illuminated speech (dibbur ha-meir) and its fruits. The tzipparta (bird) IS speech; it stands ankle-deep in the water (man, who is made of male and female waters) and its head reaches heaven (the deep understanding of Torah). Speech illuminates the places where one needs to do teshuvah. Straw (teven) IS understanding (tevuna); heaven (shamayim) IS the deep insights of Torah; ירקא IS teshuvah; ירקא שקל IS teshuvat ha-mishkal. All of these are two faces of the same reality: the transformative power of verbalized Torah — speech that raises man from his lowly state toward Torah depth.", "dominant_node": "צפור", "nodes": ["צפור", "דבור", "דבור המאיר", "מים", "אדם", "תבן", "תבונה", "שמים", "תבונות התורה", "ירקא", "תשובה", "ירקא שקל", "תשובת המשקל"], "node_degrees": {"צפור": 2, "דבור": 1, "דבור המאיר": 1, "מים": 1, "אדם": 1, "תבן": 1, "תבונה": 1, "שמים": 1, "תבונות התורה": 1, "ירקא": 1, "תשובה": 1, "ירקא שקל": 1, "תשובת המשקל": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים", "אדם"], "reason": "Man (adam) IS water (mayim) — he is formed from male and female waters (mayim duchrin and nukvin). This pair identifies the 'lower realm' in which the bird of speech stands: the water = mankind in its lowly state. Bechina: they are two names for the same reality (man's material constitution)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תבן", "תבונה"], "reason": "In the Moshe–Yochani exchange: 'teven ata machniss la'afrayim' — straw (teven) is a bechina of tevuna (deep understanding, Binah). This is an explicit bechina in the source text (§1, line 5). It belongs in this package because straw/tevuna is what illuminated speech delivers to the lowly (afar = dust)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שמים", "תבונות התורה"], "reason": "Heaven (shamayim) IS the deep insights of Torah — the bird's head reaching heaven means speech bridges man up to Torah depth. The proof in the source text cites the metzora narrative: 'yavo pattataya vikhaper al pattataya.' This is the height that illuminated speech reaches."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ירקא", "תשובה"], "reason": "Yraka (green herbs / the word וַיָּרֶק — he armed/stirred) IS teshuvah. From Midrash Bereishit Rabbah 43: Avraham 'armed' his disciples in the portion of Shofetim, which is about teshuvah. This is the destination that illuminated speech leads to: the places that need teshuvah are lit up by speech."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ירקא שקל", "תשובת המשקל"], "reason": "Yraka shekul (measured/weighted herbs) IS teshuvat ha-mishkal (precise, weighed repentance). This is a deeper or more complete form of the same repentance concept as ירקא/תשובה — two levels of the same teshuvah domain."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "פְּגַם הַבְּרִית וּמִשְׁכַּן בְּחֻרְבָּנֵהּ", "label_en": "Covenant Violation and the Destroyed Tabernacle", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes share the nature of brit violation and its structural consequences. The 'forty lashes minus one' = the bechina of פגם הברית (covenant blemish) = תוספות (excess/transgression of boundaries). משכן בחרבנה (Tabernacle in its destruction) = ל\"ט מלקות. The good mirror nodes (משכן בבנינה, ל\"ט אורות) are included from the 2-node isolated component because they share the SAME structural domain (Mishkan/39-count), being the identical metaphor applied to the positive pole. Dominant polarity is evil (brit violation, 39 lashes, destruction) with an embedded good mirror.", "dominant_node": "פגם הברית", "nodes": ["ל\"ט מלקות", "\"ארבעים יכנו ולא יוסיף\"", "פגם הברית", "תוספות", "משכן בחרבנה", "משכן בבנינה", "ל\"ט אורות"], "node_degrees": {"ל\"ט מלקות": 2, "\"ארבעים יכנו ולא יוסיף\"": 2, "פגם הברית": 2, "תוספות": 1, "משכן בחרבנה": 1, "משכן בבנינה": 1, "ל\"ט אורות": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 5 ({משכן בבנינה, ל\"ט אורות}) merged in: these 2 nodes share the same Mishkan/39-count structural nature as the larger Component 4, being the positive pole of the identical metaphor (Mishkan in construction = 39 lights vs. Mishkan in destruction = 39 lashes). Merged on shared NATURE (the Mishkan/39 structural framework), not cause-effect."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 11, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גֵּאוּת וַעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה", "label_en": "Pride and Idolatry — The Evil Mirror of Covenant", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Pride (gaavah/gadlut) and idolatry share the same essential nature: both claim that God is insufficient ('ein dai be'elokuto'), both represent the antithesis of the Shaday consciousness (she-yesh dai be'elokuto). The Torah explicitly equates gaavah with avodah zarah in §2–3. This is the evil mirror of Package 1: if Package 1 is the domain of Shaday (divine sufficiency, covenant, boundary), Package 4 is the domain of the one who rejects divine sufficiency and goes beyond the boundary.", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה", "nodes": ["גאות", "גדלות", "עבודה זרה"], "node_degrees": {"עבודה זרה": 2, "גאות": 1, "גדלות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "גֵּאוּת וַעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה", "label_en": "Arrogance and Idolatry", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "גאות (arrogance) and גדלות (self-aggrandizement) are explicitly stated to be the bechina of עבודה זרה (idolatry/alien worship). The Rebbe teaches that pride is structurally identical to idolatry — both are expressions of the same evil orientation: claiming autonomy from God. This 3-node cluster is the evil mirror of the כבוד package (humility/honor of Heaven), but forms its own distinct evil cluster as it is internally bechina-connected.", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה", "nodes": ["גאות", "גדלות", "עבודה זרה"], "node_degrees": {"גאות": 1, "גדלות": 1, "עבודה זרה": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית בִּשְׁתֵּי בְּחִינוֹת וְקְדֻשַּׁת הַזִּוּוּג", "label_en": "Two-Level Covenant Observance and Holiness of Union", "polarity": "good", "reason": "יחודא עלאה ויחודא תתאה (compound node) = שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות. This compound node is distinct from the individual יחודא עלאה and יחודא תתאה nodes — it represents the SYNTHESIS of both levels as the brit's two-mode observance. צבי לצדיק = קדשת הזווג — the beauty/glory of the tzaddik is the bechina of the holiness of marital union. These share the nature of integrated/holy brit observance at its most refined level. The 2-node component (צבי לצדיק, קדשת הזווג) merged here on shared nature: kedushat hazivug is the same domain as brit shmirah.", "dominant_node": "שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות", "nodes": ["יחודא עלאה ויחודא תתאה", "שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות", "צבי לצדיק", "קדשת הזווג"], "node_degrees": {"יחודא עלאה ויחודא תתאה": 1, "שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות": 1, "צבי לצדיק": 1, "קדשת הזווג": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component 13 ({צבי לצדיק, קדשת הזווג}) merged in: kedushat hazivug is the positive bechina of covenant holiness, same domain as שמירת הברית בשתי בחינות. Merged on shared NATURE (the sanctity of marital union as an expression of brit observance), not cause-effect."]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 11, "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וְיַרְקָא", "label_en": "Repentance and the Vegetable (Illuminated Place)", "polarity": "good", "reason": "ירקא = תשובה (from the midrash on 'vayarék et chanichav' — he animated them with the portion of Shoftim about teshuva). ירקא שקל = תשובת המשקל (weighed/measured repentance). This 4-node cluster captures the repentance domain: the specific quality of illuminated speech that shows a person the places where they need to do teshuva, and the weighed/calibrated form of that teshuva. These are not large enough to stand alone as a 2-node cluster and naturally belong here. Kept as a distinct package (4 nodes, not 1-2) rather than merging into the speech package, because teshuva is the GOAL that illuminating speech reaches, not structurally identical TO the speech itself.", "dominant_node": "תשובה", "nodes": ["ירקא", "תשובה", "ירקא שקל", "תשובת המשקל"], "node_degrees": {"ירקא": 1, "תשובה": 1, "ירקא שקל": 1, "תשובת המשקל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 12, "label": "נְשִׁיקִין", "label_en": "The Spiritual Kiss", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three nodes share the essential nature of the holy kiss — the merging of two spirits through the mouth of Torah. The teaching equates: נשיקין = התדבקות רוחא ברוחא (union of spirit with spirit); 'שפתותיו שושנים' (Song of Songs 5:13) = the lips that kiss = נשיקין; 'שפתותיו שושנים' = התדבקות רוחא ברוחא. This is a perfect triangle: the kiss IS the union of spirits, and the Song of Songs verse IS the kiss. All three are faces of the same spiritual-union reality that occurs when a righteous learner studies the Torah of a Tanna.", "dominant_node": "נשיקין", "nodes": ["נשיקין", "\"שפתותיו שושנים\"", "התדבקות רוחא ברוחא"], "node_degrees": {"נשיקין": 2, "\"שפתותיו שושנים\"": 2, "התדבקות רוחא ברוחא": 2}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: triangle of 3 nodes with 2 edges each. No merges required."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 12, "volume": "lm2", "label": "כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן וּמַאֲמָר סָתוּם", "label_en": "Supreme Hidden Glory and the Sealed Utterance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the same essential nature: the utterly concealed, transcendent dimension of God's glory that is the root of all creation, yet cannot be grasped by intellect. The Rebbe explicitly states: 'אַיֵּה מְקוֹם כְּבוֹדוֹ – זֶה בְּחִינַת הַכָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן שֶׁל הַמַּאֲמָר הָעֶלְיוֹן, דְּהַיְנוּ הַמַּאֲמָר סָתוּם בְּרֵאשִׁית' (line 12) and 'כִּי אַיֵּה הוּא בְּחִינוֹת מַאֲמָר סָתוּם' (line 23). These three — 'אַיֵּה', 'כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן', 'מַאֲמָר סָתוּם בְּרֵאשִׁית' — are explicitly identified as the same bechina from multiple directions. Additionally, שֶׂה לְעֹלָה (the Akeidah sacrifice) is called 'בְּחִינוֹת שֶׂה לְעוֹלָה' that equals 'בְּחִינַת אַיֵּה' (line 16), and the חָלָל הַפָּנוּי (the apparently void space) is also identified as sustained by this same maamar satum (line 27). The binding principle across all nodes is: the absolute hiddenness that transcends the revealed utterances — their shared nature is transcendent concealment as the root of life.", "dominant_node": "כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן", "nodes": ["כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן", "מַאֲמָר סָתוּם", "\"אַיֵּה\"", "שֶׂה לְעֹלָה", "חָלָל הַפָּנוּי"], "node_degrees": {"כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן": 3, "מַאֲמָר סָתוּם": 3, "\"אַיֵּה\"": 3, "שֶׂה לְעֹלָה": 1, "חָלָל הַפָּנוּי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן", "מַאֲמָר סָתוּם", "\"אַיֵּה\""], "reason": "Core triangle: all three are explicitly identified as the same bechina by the Rebbe in lines 12 and 23. 'אַיֵּה מְקוֹם כְּבוֹדוֹ' = the upper kavod = the maamar satum. This is the densest bechina cluster in the Torah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שֶׂה לְעֹלָה", "כָּבוֹד עֶלְיוֹן"], "reason": "Line 16: 'שֶׁבְּחִינַת אַיֵּה הִיא בְּחִינוֹת שֶׂה לְעוֹלָה'. The Akeidah sacrifice IS the same bechina as 'אַיֵּה', which is the same bechina as the supreme kavod. Shared nature: the ascent-through-concealment that redeems."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חָלָל הַפָּנוּי", "מַאֲמָר סָתוּם"], "reason": "Line 27: the chalal hapanui (apparently vacant space, seemingly devoid of divinity) receives life specifically from the maamar satum. The chalal's essential nature IS that it is sustained by the same hidden source. Both are expressions of the same hiddenness-that-gives-life."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 12, "label": "תּוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה", "label_en": "The Oral Torah / Shekhinah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of תורה שבעל פה — the Oral Torah as the Shekhinah, the mouth-dimension of holiness, the repository of halakhot that give life. Core connections: דוד = תורה שבעל פה (the Psalm 'Tehillah leDavid' speaks of the fallen/mixed Oral Torah); יין = תורה שבעל פה (wine = malkhut rav = the Oral Torah in its royal dimension); רחל = תורה שבעל פה (Rachel stands before her shearers = the Oral Torah from which all halakhot are sheared); יין = בינה לבא (the two faces of wine — Oral Torah and the heart's understanding); יין = שמחת הלב (wine = heart's joy = the same inner-dimension). The שמאלה node and its pair (the Tanna's involuntary slight fall) are also merged here: 'שמאלה — עושר וכבוד' is explicitly the LEFT face of Torah (Mishlei 3:16), the dimension through which material sustenance flows — it is a structural feature of תורה שבעל פה itself, not a separate or evil domain. The green-beginning cluster (אני חבצלת, אסתר ירקרקת) is also merged here: 'אני חבצלת' = the Shekhinah in its initial green state before ascent; 'אסתר ירקרקת' = the same green quality. The Shekhinah IS תורה שבעל פה, and its initial state of being fallen among the Lamdanim is the teaching's core drama.", "dominant_node": "תורה שבעל פה", "nodes": ["תורה שבעל פה", "יין", "בינה לבא", "שמחת הלב", "דוד", "רחל", "אני חבצלת", "אסתר ירקרקת", "נפל התנא בשעת אמירת תורתו לאיזה שגיאה דקה כחוט השערה", "שמאלה"], "node_degrees": {"תורה שבעל פה": 3, "יין": 3, "בינה לבא": 1, "שמחת הלב": 1, "דוד": 1, "רחל": 1, "אני חבצלת": 1, "אסתר ירקרקת": 1, "נפל התנא בשעת אמירת תורתו לאיזה שגיאה דקה כחוט השערה": 1, "שמאלה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component {תורה שבעל פה, יין, בינה לבא, שמחת הלב, דוד, רחל}: 6 nodes, 5 edges (יין is the hub with degree 3 in raw component).", "Merged isolated pair {אני חבצלת, אסתר ירקרקת}: 'אני חבצלת' = the Shekhinah in its initial green/fallen state (Song of Songs 2:1); 'ירקרקת' = green = the same quality. The source text explicitly identifies this green state as the Shekhinah (= תורה שבעל פה) before it ascends through חיבוק. Nature: the Shekhinah-Torah in its beginning/fallen state.", "Merged isolated pair {נפל התנא..., שמאלה}: 'שמאלה' = the left hand of Torah = 'עושר וכבוד' (Mishlei 3:16). The Tanna's involuntary slip to שמאלה is how the Torah draws material sustenance for the world. This is a structural feature of תורה שבעל פה — its left/material-sustaining face. Shared nature: the left dimension of Oral Torah that provides for the world."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 12, "volume": "lm2", "label": "כָּבוֹד גָּלוּי וַעֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת", "label_en": "Revealed Glory and the Ten Utterances", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the revealed, bounded dimension of divine glory — the glory that is manifest in the world through the ten utterances of creation. The Rebbe states (line 5): 'בְּכָל מַאֲמָר וּמַאֲמָר יֵשׁ בּוֹ בְּחִינַת כָּבוֹד מְיֻחָד' — each utterance contains a particular bechina of kavod as its root. The verse 'וּבְהֵיכָלוֹ כֻּלּוֹ אֹמֵר כָּבוֹד' (Psalms 29:9) is called the bechina of this structure (line 6): 'שֶׁבְּכָל מַאֲמָר מְלֻבָּשׁ בּוֹ כְּבוֹדוֹ'. The שֹׁרֶשׁ כָּל הַבְּרִיאָה (root of all creation) is explicitly identified as כָּבוֹד in line 4. These nodes share the nature of bounded/revealed divine glory — kavod as it flows into and sustains the structure of the world.", "dominant_node": "כָּבוֹד", "nodes": ["כָּבוֹד", "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת", "שֹׁרֶשׁ הַבְּרִיאָה", "תְּמִימוּת וּפְשִׁיטוּת"], "node_degrees": {"כָּבוֹד": 3, "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת": 2, "שֹׁרֶשׁ הַבְּרִיאָה": 1, "תְּמִימוּת וּפְשִׁיטוּת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כָּבוֹד", "שֹׁרֶשׁ הַבְּרִיאָה"], "reason": "Line 4: 'כִּי שֹׁרֶשׁ כָּל הַבְּרִיאָה הוּא הַכָּבוֹד' — kavod IS the root of all creation. These are two names for the same essential reality. Shared nature: the cosmic ground of all existence."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כָּבוֹד", "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת"], "reason": "Lines 5-6: each of the ten utterances contains a specific bechina of kavod as its root. The ten utterances are the revealed structure through which kavod is expressed in the world. Shared nature: revealed divine glory subdivided into the world's structure."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תְּמִימוּת וּפְשִׁיטוּת", "כָּבוֹד"], "reason": "Line 2: the entire teaching opens with the prescription of temimut/peshitut as the antidote to relying on one's own sekhel, and this is immediately connected to acting only for 'כְּבוֹד הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ'. The walk of simplicity and the orientation toward God's kavod are two expressions of the same essential alignment — temimut IS the direct connection to kavod. One who goes in peshitut automatically acts for kevod Hashem rather than his own kavod. Shared nature: the correct human orientation toward divine glory."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 12, "label": "חִבּוּק וִימִין", "label_en": "Embrace and the Right", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of חיבוק — the loving embrace of the right hand. Core connections: ימין = חבוק (the right hand IS the embrace); אהבה שהצדיק מקבל את החרפה באהבה = חבוק (the tzaddik's loving acceptance of humiliation IS the embrace); אברהם = ימין (Abraham embodies the right side, the quality of chesed). The isolated pairs חניכיו/חסדים and וירק/קו הירק are merged here by shared nature: חניכיו = חסדים (the 318 trained men = Abraham's chesed-disciples, per Midrash Bereshit Rabbah 43 — חניכה = name = Abraham = chesed); וירק = קו הירק (the green line drawn from Binah that encircles the world = the external expression of the chesed/embrace-energy). In the source text, 'וירק' and 'חניכיו' appear in the same verse (Bereshit 14:14) about Abraham — together they are the mechanism of חיבוק: Abraham (right/chesed) pours the green line of chesed through his disciples. Shared nature across all eight nodes: right-side / chesed / loving-embrace.", "dominant_node": "חבוק", "nodes": ["חבוק", "ימין", "אהבה, שהצדיק מקבל את החרפה באהבה", "אברהם", "חניכיו", "חסדים", "וירק", "קו הירק"], "node_degrees": {"חבוק": 2, "ימין": 2, "אהבה, שהצדיק מקבל את החרפה באהבה": 1, "אברהם": 1, "חניכיו": 1, "חסדים": 1, "וירק": 1, "קו הירק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component {חבוק, ימין, אהבה שהצדיק..., אברהם}: 4 nodes. ימין and חבוק are the hub-pair (degree 2 each).", "Merged isolated pair {חניכיו, חסדים}: 'חניכיו' = the 318 trained men of Abraham = חסדים (Bereshit Rabbah 43: 'leshon chanikato sheshman Avraham kishmo'). חניכיו and חסדים share the nature of Abraham's chesed — they are the concrete embodiment of ימין/חבוק. Nature: Abraham's chesed-emanation = the right-arm embrace in action.", "Merged isolated pair {וירק, קו הירק}: 'וירק' (Bereshit 14:14) = קו הירק (the green line drawn from Binah that encircles the world). This green line is the same quality as 'אני חבצלת / אסתר ירקרקת' (green). In the source text this is explicitly the mechanism of חיבוק — the flow from Binah through the right arm that embraces. Nature: the green-chesed emanation that IS the embrace's energy. Note: although the green quality appears in Package 2 as well (חבצלת/ירקרקת), the node 'קו הירק' specifically refers to Abraham's action-moment ('וַיָּרֶק אֶת חֲנִיכָיו') and flows into this right/chesed cluster."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 12, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ וּסְפֵקוֹת", "label_en": "Klipat Nogah and Doubts", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the obstructing/confusing force that twists the heart and generates doubt — the domain of the kelipah that sits at the boundary between holiness and impurity. The Rebbe makes two explicit bechina statements: (1) line 14: 'לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר ... וְהִיא בְּחִינַת קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ ... וְנֹגַהּ לוֹ סָבִיב' — the circular/twisting heart IS klipat nogah; (2) line 19: 'וְאֵלּוּ הַסְּפֵקוֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת עֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת נֹגַהּ' — the doubts and confusions ARE eitz hada'at tov vera, which IS nogah. Thus: סְפֵקוֹת = עֵץ הַדַּעַת = קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ = לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר. All are faces of the same mixed/churning reality at the boundary of holiness. The מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים (polluted places, places of sin and confusion) are explicitly identified as the domain where one falls when dominated by this force.", "dominant_node": "קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ", "nodes": ["קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ", "עֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע", "סְפֵקוֹת וְהִרְהוּרִים", "לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר", "מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים"], "node_degrees": {"קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ": 4, "עֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע": 2, "סְפֵקוֹת וְהִרְהוּרִים": 2, "לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר": 1, "מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר", "קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ"], "reason": "Line 14: explicitly stated as bechina. 'לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר' (my heart goes around/twists) = klipat nogah = 'וְנֹגַהּ לוֹ סָבִיב' (Ezekiel 1:4). The circular spinning of the confused heart IS the essential nature of klipat nogah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["סְפֵקוֹת וְהִרְהוּרִים", "עֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע", "קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ"], "reason": "Line 19: 'אֵלּוּ הַסְּפֵקוֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת עֵץ הַדַּעַת טוֹב וָרָע, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת נֹגַהּ'. Double bechina chain: doubts = eitz hada'at = nogah. All three are the same force of mixture and confusion."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים", "קְלִפַּת נֹגַהּ"], "reason": "Lines 11 and 15: falling to 'מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים' is synonymous with falling into doubts and confusions. The polluted places are the existential location of klipat nogah's domain — they are two expressions of the same fallen state. Shared nature: the domain of mixed/polluted spiritual reality."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 12, "label": "שְׁנֵי צַדִּיקִים", "label_en": "The Two Tzaddikim", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes share the essential nature of the two-Tzaddikim structure between whom the Shekhinah dwells. The teaching quotes Zohar: 'tzaddikim terei mashma — tzaddikim yirshu aretz' = two tzaddikim. The bechina equations: 'יעלו שמים' (Psalms 107) = צדיק עליון (the upper/originating Tzaddik, the Tanna who innovated the Torah); 'ירדו תהומות' = צדיק תחתון (the lower/receiving Tzaddik, the one who currently studies). The isolated pair {'ירדו תהומות', צדיק תחתון} merged with {'צדיק עליון', 'יעלו שמים'} because they share the SAME NATURE — both pairs are faces of the identical two-tzaddikim structure. 'יעלו שמים' and 'ירדו תהומות' are the two poles of the same cosmic verse that maps onto the upper and lower tzaddikim. They are inseparable by definition — neither can exist without the other.", "dominant_node": "צדיק עליון", "nodes": ["צדיק עליון", "\"יעלו שמים\"", "\"ירדו תהומות\"", "צדיק תחתון"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק עליון": 1, "\"יעלו שמים\"": 1, "\"ירדו תהומות\"": 1, "צדיק תחתון": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Two isolated 2-node components merged: {צדיק עליון, 'יעלו שמים'} + {'ירדו תהומות', צדיק תחתון}.", "Justification: These two pairs are explicitly parallel faces of the same structure — the verse 'יעלו שמים ירדו תהומות' (Psalms 107:26) is decoded in the source text (section ד, lines 51-52) as the two tzaddikim between whom the Shekhinah sits. 'יעלו שמים' = the upper tzaddik (who lifts); 'ירדו תהומות' = the lower tzaddik (who descends to receive). Their shared nature is: the two-pole structure of tzaddik-Shekhinah-tzaddik = the vessel for the Shekhinah's dwelling. They could not meaningfully be placed in any other package — their essential identity IS this duality, and both pairs must be in the same package."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 12, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וְעֵץ הַחַיִּים", "label_en": "Return and the Tree of Life", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the soul's return/ascent from the fallen state — the process of reconnection with divine glory. The Rebbe calls the act of seeking 'אַיֵּה מְקוֹם כְּבוֹדוֹ' by the name teshuvah (line 18): 'וְזֶה בְּחִינוֹת תְּשׁוּבָה'. He also calls it the bechina of the olah sacrifice (line 13): 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת קָרְבָּן עוֹלָה, שֶׁמְּכַפֵּר עַל הִרְהוּר הַלֵּב'. And he states that arriving at the Torah itself (after passing through the doubts) is the bechina of eitz hachayyim (line 19): 'וּכְשֶׁמַּגִּיעַ אֶל הַתּוֹרָה בְּעַצְמָהּ, זֶה בְּחִינַת עֵץ הַחַיִּים'. The shared nature across all these nodes is: the active return-to-holiness through seeking God's glory — the rectification process that is the polar opposite of the doubts/kelipah cluster.", "dominant_node": "תְּשׁוּבָה", "nodes": ["תְּשׁוּבָה", "קָרְבָּן עוֹלָה", "עֵץ הַחַיִּים", "יְרִידָה תַּכְלִית הָעֲלִיָּה"], "node_degrees": {"תְּשׁוּבָה": 2, "קָרְבָּן עוֹלָה": 2, "עֵץ הַחַיִּים": 1, "יְרִידָה תַּכְלִית הָעֲלִיָּה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תְּשׁוּבָה", "קָרְבָּן עוֹלָה"], "reason": "Lines 13 and 18: the olah sacrifice atones for hirhurim (contemplations), which is the same as teshuvah — both are the rectification of the heart's fall into doubts. Shared nature: the mechanism of ascent/atonement from the fallen state."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עֵץ הַחַיִּים", "תְּשׁוּבָה"], "reason": "Line 19: arriving at the Torah itself (after passing through the doubts) = bechina of eitz hachayyim. This arrival IS the teshuvah — returning through the Torah to the Tree of Life is the completion of the ascent. Shared nature: the destination of the return — pure Torah/holiness without mixture."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יְרִידָה תַּכְלִית הָעֲלִיָּה", "תְּשׁוּבָה"], "reason": "Lines 3 and 11: the descent is explicitly called 'תַּכְלִית הָעֲלִיָּה' (the ultimate ascent). The fall into doubts that results in seeking God's glory IS the teshuvah. 'יְרִידָה תַּכְלִית הָעֲלִיָּה' and תְּשׁוּבָה share the same essential structure: the inverted dynamic where the fall generates the highest rise. Shared nature: the paradoxical ascent-through-descent that characterizes genuine return."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 13, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ וּמֶרְכָּבָה", "label_en": "Soul and Merkavah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four living-creatures (chayot) and throne-structure that the Torah assigns to the domain of nefesh: Shor-shebaNefesh = polished light of soul-gathering; Nesher-shebaNefesh = renewal of soul in ibbur; Adam-shebaNefesh = nefesh chayah / collected souls as rich and poor; Kise-shebaNefesh = the Tzaddik's hidden soul that clothes all other souls; plus the core equation nefesh = ratzon, the nefeshot as 'yehalkhun', 'kol basar yachdav' = lekitat hanefashot, and the pinnacle 'ha-Adam hayoshev al haKise' = daat haChakham. All share the same essential nature: the soul-dimension of the Merkavah structure.", "dominant_node": "נפש", "nodes": ["נפש", "רצון", "שור שבנפש", "האור המצחצח שנתוסף בנפש מחמת הקבוץ, שנתקבץ פזורי הנפש, ואז מאיר הנפש ביותר", "נשר שבבחינת נפש", "חדוש שנתחדש הנפש בעליתה בבחינת עבור", "אדם שבנפש", "\"ויהי האדם לנפש חיה\"", "כסא שבבחינת נפש", "נפש החכם שנתכסה", "\"כל בשר יחדו\"", "לקיטת הנפשות כלם", "יהלכון", "נפשות", "אדם היושב על הכסא", "דעתו של החכם"], "node_degrees": {"נפש": 1, "רצון": 1, "שור שבנפש": 1, "האור המצחצח שנתוסף בנפש מחמת הקבוץ, שנתקבץ פזורי הנפש, ואז מאיר הנפש ביותר": 1, "נשר שבבחינת נפש": 1, "חדוש שנתחדש הנפש בעליתה בבחינת עבור": 1, "אדם שבנפש": 1, "\"ויהי האדם לנפש חיה\"": 1, "כסא שבבחינת נפש": 1, "נפש החכם שנתכסה": 1, "\"כל בשר יחדו\"": 1, "לקיטת הנפשות כלם": 1, "יהלכון": 1, "נפשות": 1, "אדם היושב על הכסא": 1, "דעתו של החכם": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components merged: C4 (אדם שבנפש), C10 (שור שבנפש), C11 (נשר שבנפש), C17 (כסא שבנפש), C22 (נפש-רצון), C15 (כל בשר יחדו), C18 (יהלכון-נפשות), C9 (אדם היושב על הכסא). All share the same essential nature: elements of the soul-dimension of the divine chariot (Merkavah baNefesh) as described in section ה of the Torah. C9 (דעתו של החכם) merged here rather than into Daas package because 'האדם היושב על הכסא' is explicitly positioned as the crown of the nefesh-Merkavah structure."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 13, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רְדִיפָה וְקֵרוּב לַה'", "label_en": "Persecution and Drawing Close to Hashem", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the paradoxical dynamic the Rebbe describes: opposition and pursuit are in truth the mechanism of drawing near to Hashem. They are all expressions of the same inverted reality — what appears as an act of driving away is in fact an act of bringing close. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'וְכָל מַה שֶּׁחוֹלְקִין עָלָיו יוֹתֵר, מְקָרְבִין אוֹתוֹ יוֹתֵר לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ'. He then gives the bechina name for this entire dynamic: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת: וּפַרְעֹה הִקְרִיב' — Pharaoh's pursuit IS the archetypal bechina of this dynamic. The bechina chain is: חֲלוֹקָה (opposition) = רְדִיפָה (pursuit) = in truth קֵרוּב (drawing close). These are not sequential cause-and-effect but three faces of the same inverted reality: the act of opposition IS the act of drawing close, as mediated by the person's flight. The dominant node is בְּרִיחָה לַה' (fleeing to Hashem) because the Rebbe places it at the center of the dynamic — it is the pivotal act that transforms persecution into closeness.", "dominant_node": "בְּרִיחָה לַה'", "nodes": ["חֲלוֹקָה", "רְדִיפָה", "בְּרִיחָה לַה'", "קֵרוּב לַה'", "פַּרְעֹה הִקְרִיב"], "node_degrees": {"חֲלוֹקָה": 2, "רְדִיפָה": 2, "בְּרִיחָה לַה'": 3, "קֵרוּב לַה'": 2, "פַּרְעֹה הִקְרִיב": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חֲלוֹקָה", "רְדִיפָה"], "reason": "The Rebbe opens by equating these two: 'כְּשֶׁחוֹלְקִין עַל הָאָדָם, נִמְצָא שֶׁרוֹדְפִין אוֹתוֹ'. Being opposed (machloket/cholek) = being pursued (rodef). They are two expressions of the same hostile action from the outside. Shared nature: the external force that drives a person away."}, {"merged_nodes": ["בְּרִיחָה לַה'", "קֵרוּב לַה'"], "reason": "The Rebbe equates these directly: fleeing to Hashem in every place IS being drawn closer to Hashem. 'נִמְצָא שֶׁבְּכָל מָקוֹם בּוֹרֵחַ לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ' = 'מְקָרְבִין אוֹתוֹ יוֹתֵר לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ'. The flight and the closeness are two descriptions of the same act — fleeing to Hashem IS drawing close to Hashem. Shared nature: the person's turning toward God under pressure."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פַּרְעֹה הִקְרִיב", "רְדִיפָה"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly states: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת: וּפַרְעֹה הִקְרִיב — שֶׁהִקְרִיב אֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל לַאֲבִיהֶם שֶׁבַּשָּׁמַיִם, שֶׁעַל־יְדֵי רְדִיפָתוֹ אוֹתָם נִתְקָרְבוּ יוֹתֵר לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ'. Pharaoh's act of pursuit (his רְדִיפָה) IS the bechina of ופרעה הקריב — 'the one who brings near'. The persecutor and the bringer-near are the same person expressing the same bechina from two directions. Shared nature: the external hostile force that is in truth the agent of drawing near."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 13, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּמֶרְכָּבָה", "label_en": "Torah and Merkavah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four living-creatures and throne-structure that the Torah assigns to the domain of Torah itself: Shor-shebaTorah = rule of princes; Nesher-shebaTorah = chidushei Torah; Adam-shebaTorah = 'zot haTorah adam' with lenient and stringent laws; Opanim-shebaTorah = the actual halakhot; Kise-shebaTorah = hidden things covered by Ancient of Days; Yoshev al haKise = Atik Yomin. Also Torah = ra'iyah (vision/sight), and Ibbur = 72/216 (the mystical process through which Torah is born). All share the same essential nature: the Torah-dimension of the Merkavah structure.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "ראיה", "שור שבתורה", "\"בי שרים ישרו\"", "אדם שבתורה", "\"זאת התורה אדם\"", "אופנים שבתורה", "גופי הלכות", "כסא שבבחינת תורה", "דברים שכסה עתיק יומין", "נשר שבתורה", "חדושי תורה", "יושב על הכסא", "עתיק יומין", "עבור", "ע\"ב רי\"ו"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "ראיה": 1, "שור שבתורה": 1, "\"בי שרים ישרו\"": 1, "אדם שבתורה": 1, "\"זאת התורה אדם\"": 1, "אופנים שבתורה": 1, "גופי הלכות": 1, "כסא שבבחינת תורה": 1, "דברים שכסה עתיק יומין": 1, "נשר שבתורה": 1, "חדושי תורה": 1, "יושב על הכסא": 1, "עתיק יומין": 1, "עבור": 1, "ע\"ב רי\"ו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components merged: C3 (שור שבתורה), C5 (אדם שבתורה), C7 (אופנים שבתורה), C8 (כסא שבבחינת תורה), C12 (נשר שבתורה), C20 (יושב על הכסא-עתיק יומין), C21 (תורה-ראיה), C19 (עבור-ע\"ב רי\"ו). All share the same essential nature: elements of the Torah-dimension of the divine chariot. C19 (עבור) merged here: ibbur is the process through which the Tzaddik draws down Torah (section ו of the source text: 'עבור זה בחינת ע\"ב רי\"ו' — a mystical Torah-birth concept)."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 13, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַשֵּׁם בְּכָל מָקוֹם", "label_en": "Hashem is in Every Place", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of divine omnipresence — the principle that there is no place devoid of Hashem, whether the heights of heaven or the depths of the grave. This is the theological foundation that makes the entire dynamic of Package 1 possible: the person can always flee to Hashem in every place precisely because Hashem is in every place. The Rebbe states this explicitly as the bechina of Psalm 139:8: 'הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם, בִּבְחִינַת (תהילים קל״ט:ח׳): אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה, וְאַצִּיעָה שְׁאוֹל הִנֶּךָּ'. The concept of omnipresence and the Psalm verse are two expressions of the same reality: the inescapable divine presence that fills all space. The שָׁמַיִם (heaven) and שְׁאוֹל (the grave/depths) serve as extreme poles to express the totality of this presence — they are bechina instances of the full span of divine presence.", "dominant_node": "הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם", "nodes": ["הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם", "אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה", "וְאַצִּיעָה שְׁאוֹל הִנֶּךָּ"], "node_degrees": {"הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם": 2, "אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה": 1, "וְאַצִּיעָה שְׁאוֹל הִנֶּךָּ": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם", "אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה", "וְאַצִּיעָה שְׁאוֹל הִנֶּךָּ"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly calls the Psalm verse the bechina of divine omnipresence: 'הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּכָל מָקוֹם, בִּבְחִינַת... אִם אֶסַּק שָׁמַיִם שָׁם אָתָּה'. The two halves of the verse (heaven / grave) are not separate concepts but expressions of the single idea of total divine presence — from the highest extreme to the lowest. All three share the same essential nature: the totality of Hashem's presence that leaves no space unfilled."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 13, "label": "דַּעַת וְשֵׂכֶל", "label_en": "Daas and Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Daas is equated with Bayit (the Temple/house), and the verse 'in the light of your face' is equated with sekhel (wisdom). Both are aspects of the same intellectual/knowledge faculty — the divine intellect that, when present in a person, is as if the Temple were built. The Tzaddik's wisdom (Moshe-Mashiach aspect) is the faculty through which Torah is received and souls are gathered. These are two faces of the same essential quality: divine knowledge/wisdom.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["דעת", "בית", "באור פניך", "חכמה"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 1, "בית": 1, "באור פניך": 1, "חכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components merged: C6 (דעת-בית) and C13 (באור פניך-חכמה). Both pairs express the same essential reality: the divine intellect/wisdom faculty. Note: node2_id for edge 1154 is 'חכמה' (the node_id field) though the display text is 'השכל' — treated as חכמה. C9 (אדם היושב על הכסא — דעתו של החכם) was placed in Package 1 (Nefesh-Merkavah) rather than here because 'האדם היושב על הכסא' is an explicit Merkavah element; its equation with the Chakham's daas is secondary to its structural position in the chariot."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 13, "label": "חֶסֶד וַהֲוָיוֹת", "label_en": "Chesed and the Divine Name", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Divine Name (Havayot) is equated with Chasadim; the deep place (Atra Amiki) is equated with Chesed. These three concepts — Hashem's name, flows of chesed, and the 'deep place' — are all aspects of the same divine mercy-dimension. In Torah #13's argument, chesed is what flows into the world once money-lust is broken, enabling the building of the Temple (daas). These are the chesed-face of the divine overflow.", "dominant_node": "חסד", "nodes": ["ה'", "הויות", "חסדים", "אתרא עמיקי", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"ה'": 1, "הויות": 2, "חסדים": 1, "אתרא עמיקי": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": ["C1 (ה'-הויות-חסדים) was already a 3-node raw component via shared node 'הויות'. C14 (אתרא עמיקי-חסד) merged in: 'אתרא עמיקי' = chesed shares the same essential nature as the other chesed-concepts in C1. The source text (section ו) explicitly links them: 'שכך תאות ממון זכה לאתרא עמיקי שהוא בחינת חסד.'"]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 13, "label": "צְדָקָה וּשְׁבִירַת תַּאֲוַת מָמוֹן", "label_en": "Tzedakah and Breaking the Lust for Money", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Yaakov is a bechina of tzedakah; Ruach (the wind/spirit) is also a bechina of tzedakah (the generous spirit that cools money-lust); the Levite song (Nigun d'Leva'ei) is a bechina of honest commerce — being satisfied with one's portion without rushing to get rich. All three pairs share the same essential nature: the domain of tzedakah as the antidote to money-lust (ta'avat mamon). This is the opening move of the Torah's argument: tzedakah breaks money-lust and enables hashgachah.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["יעקב", "צדקה", "רוח", "נגונא דליואי", "משא ומתן באמונה"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב": 1, "צדקה": 2, "רוח": 1, "נגונא דליואי": 1, "משא ומתן באמונה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["C2 (יעקב-צדקה-רוח) was already a 3-node raw component via shared node 'צדקה'. C16 (נגונא דליואי-משא ומתן באמונה) merged in: honest commerce / contentment with one's portion is described in the source text as the practical expression of not lusting after money — the same domain as tzedakah-as-spirit-of-generosity. The Zohar passage (section א) explicitly links them: 'ניגונא דלוואי זה בחינת משא ומתן באמונה שששמח בחלקו ואינו אץ להעשיר.'"]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 14, "label": "כָּבוֹד וְהֶאָרָתוֹ", "label_en": "Glory and Its Illumination", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of divine Kavod (Glory): light, illumination, the Hanukkah candle that manifests it, the act of returning it to its root, and the mechanism (bringing people to teshuvah / Torah soul-sparking) by which that elevation is achieved. The Rebbe explicitly equates אור = כבוד (Yechezkel 43:2), יום = אור (light = day), הדלקת הנר = הארת הכבוד, and מכבד יראי השם = החזרת הכבוד לשרשו. Bringing people to teshuvah is equated with 'אם תוציא יקר מזולל' which IS the definition of Kavod's elevation. Soul-sparking through Torah recitation is equated with זיווג — the reproductive mechanism that creates the illuminated souls needed for Kavod's rise.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["כבוד", "אור", "יום", "שלום בית", "הדלקת הנר", "הארת הכבוד", "מכבד יראי השם", "החזרת הכבוד לשרשו", "להחזיר בני אדם בתשובה", "אם תוציא יקר מזולל", "נתעוררים הנשמות על ידי אותיות התורה שהוציא מפיו ומתנוצצים זה לזה", "זווג"], "node_degrees": {"כבוד": 2, "אור": 2, "יום": 2, "שלום בית": 1, "הדלקת הנר": 1, "הארת הכבוד": 1, "מכבד יראי השם": 1, "החזרת הכבוד לשרשו": 1, "להחזיר בני אדם בתשובה": 1, "אם תוציא יקר מזולל": 1, "נתעוררים הנשמות על ידי אותיות התורה שהוציא מפיו ומתנוצצים זה לזה": 1, "זווג": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components B {יום, אור, כבוד, שלום בית} and E {הדלקת הנר, הארת הכבוד} merged: הארת הכבוד shares essential nature with כבוד (both are expressions of Kavod's luminosity).", "Component F {מכבד יראי השם, החזרת הכבוד לשרשו} merged: honoring G-d-fearers IS defined as returning Kavod to its root — same bechina.", "Component C {להחזיר בני אדם בתשובה, אם תוציא יקר מזולל} merged: bringing to teshuvah is explicitly equated with 'extracting the precious from the vile' which is how Kavod rises — same bechina.", "Component G {נתעוררים הנשמות, זווג} merged: the sparking of souls through Torah = zivug is the mechanism of drawing souls into holiness — same kavod-elevation domain."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 14, "label": "יִרְאָה", "label_en": "Fear of God — Root of Glory", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes share the essential nature of Yirah (awe/fear of God). The Rebbe equates: כסא = יראה (the throne-word alludes to what is hidden in the heart — fear of God, 'דבר המסור ללב'); גבורה = יראה ('וֶאֱלֹקִים עָשָׂה שֶׁיִּירְאוּ מִלְּפָנָיו' are the thunders, and 'רַעַם גְּבוּרוֹתָיו'). Yirah is the שרש (root) to which Kavod must be returned — it is a distinct domain from Kavod itself, representing the inner, hidden quality that anchors everything.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "בכסא", "גבורה"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 2, "בכסא": 1, "גבורה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["No merges needed — this is a coherent 3-node component already. Kept separate from Package 1 because Yirah is the root/שרש to which Kavod returns — a structurally distinct level in the teaching's architecture."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 14, "label": "שָׁלוֹם — גּוּף וּנְשָׁמָה", "label_en": "Peace — Body and Soul", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of Shalom (peace/wholeness), expressed through the lens of body-soul alignment and universal peace. The Rebbe equates: ארץ = גוף (earth = body), עשיה = גוף (action-world = body), מעשיך = עשיה (Psalm 145 'your deeds' = physical world), רקיע = נשמה (firmament = soul), חסידיך = נפש (Psalm 145 'thy pious' = the soul-aspect), דנשקי ארעא ורקיעא = שלום בעצמיו (earth and sky kissing = inner peace of body-soul alignment), and שתכלה רגל מן השוק = שלום (cessation of the strife-walkers = Peace). All these are different expressions of the same underlying reality: harmony/completeness/peace.", "dominant_node": "שלום בעצמיו", "nodes": ["שלום בעצמיו", "דנשקי ארעא ורקיעא", "גוף", "ארץ", "עשיה", "מעשיך", "נשמה", "רקיע", "נפש", "חסידיך", "שלום", "שתכלה רגל מן השוק"], "node_degrees": {"שלום בעצמיו": 1, "דנשקי ארעא ורקיעא": 1, "גוף": 2, "ארץ": 1, "עשיה": 2, "מעשיך": 1, "נשמה": 1, "רקיע": 1, "נפש": 1, "חסידיך": 1, "שלום": 1, "שתכלה רגל מן השוק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components D {ארץ, גוף, עשיה, מעשיך} (4 nodes), K {רקיע, נשמה} (2 nodes), J {חסידיך, נפש} (2 nodes), O {דנשקי ארעא ורקיעא, שלום בעצמיו} (2 nodes), and N {שתכלה רגל מן השוק, שלום} (2 nodes) all merged here.", "D merges as body-metaphors (גוף, ארץ, עשיה = physical plane); K and J as soul-metaphors (נשמה, רקיע, נפש, חסידיך = spiritual plane); O directly equates earth-sky conjunction with inner peace; N equates cessation of strife-walkers with shalom — all expressing the same peace/wholeness domain.", "Note: מעשיך connects to עשיה which connects to גוף; this internal bechina chain was already present in raw Component D before the merge."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 14, "label": "תְּפִלָּה", "label_en": "Prayer and Divine Service", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of Tefillah (prayer / divine service). The Rebbe equates: 'טוב ה' לכל' (the verse expressing complete faith in God's goodness) = תפלה (because the one who believes God is good to all directs all his effort toward God — prayer — rather than worldly stratagems); שמירת המועד = עבודה תמה (observing festivals is equated with complete/pure divine service, and 'there is no service except prayer' — אין עבודה אלא תפלה). These all express the same essence: perfect orientation of the person toward God in prayer.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "\"טוב ה לכל\"", "שמירת המועד", "עבודה תמה"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "\"טוב ה לכל\"": 1, "שמירת המועד": 1, "עבודה תמה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw component P {\"טוב ה לכל\", תפלה} and component M {שמירת המועד, עבודה תמה} merged: both pairs are about the same essential nature — perfect, wholehearted divine service as prayer. The Rebbe explicitly links: עבודה תמה = תפלה (via the Talmudic ruling אין עבודה אלא תפלה); belief in 'Hashem is good to all' IS prayer (directing all effort toward God rather than human stratagems)."]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 14, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וּלְשׁוֹן הָרָע", "label_en": "Discord and Evil Speech", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of Machlokes (strife, discord) and its manifestations. The Rebbe equates: רגל = 'נרגן מפריד אלוף' (the 'walker/foot' of evil speech = the slanderer who separates alliances); רגל = בעלי לשון הרע ומחלקת (the foot = masters of evil speech and strife); כל החולאת = מחלקת (all illnesses = strife — because disease is the elements within the body warring against each other, no shalom within); כנעני = סוחר (the Canaanite = the merchant, because commerce itself arises from the absence of peace between buyers and sellers, i.e., machlokes of wills). This cluster is the evil mirror of the good Package 3 (Shalom): where Shalom is the alignment of opposites, Machlokes is their warfare.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "כל החולאת", "רגל", "\"נרגן מפריד אלוף\"", "בעלי לשון הרע ומחלקת", "כנעני", "סוחר"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 2, "כל החולאת": 1, "רגל": 2, "\"נרגן מפריד אלוף\"": 1, "בעלי לשון הרע ומחלקת": 1, "כנעני": 1, "סוחר": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw components A {רגל, נרגן מפריד אלוף, בעלי לשון הרע ומחלקת} (3 nodes), I {כל החולאת, מחלקת} (2 nodes), and L {כנעני, סוחר} (2 nodes) merged.", "A and I share essential nature: both are expressions of discord/strife — slander creates social machlokes, illness is physical machlokes (elements at war). The Rebbe states explicitly: כל חולאת = בחינת מחלקת.", "L merged: כנעני = סוחר is the economic manifestation of machlokes (commerce exists because people's wills are in conflict). The Rebbe cites Bereishit 13:7 — 'ויהי ריב... והכנעני אז בארץ' — strife causes merchants to exist. Same essential nature as discord."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דִּבּוּר הַקָּדוֹשׁ וְהַצַּדִּיק הַגָּדוֹל", "label_en": "Holy Speech and the Great Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five nodes are aspects of the same positive domain: the great tzaddik whose holy mouth utters greatness with dual meaning. The rose (שושנה) and the lips (שפתותיו) are the same as holy speech (דבור הקדוש) — three metaphors for one reality. The ox image (תורי = בכור שורו הדר לו) is the Rebbe's explicit bechina for the great tzaddik (צדיקים הגדולים), and the great tzaddik is the very source of holy speech. Thus all five nodes express the single essential identity: the great tzaddik as the bearer of dual-meaning holy utterance.", "dominant_node": "דבור הקדוש", "nodes": ["דבור הקדוש", "שפתותיו", "שושנה", "תורי", "צדיקים הגדולים"], "node_degrees": {"דבור הקדוש": 2, "שפתותיו": 1, "שושנה": 1, "תורי": 1, "צדיקים הגדולים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תורי", "צדיקים הגדולים"], "reason": "Component G (תורי — צדיקים הגדולים) merged into Package 1 because 'תורי' is the Rebbe's image for the great tzaddik ('זֶהוּ בְּחִינַת הַצַּדִּיקִים הַגְּדוֹלִים, בְּחִינַת: בְּכוֹר שׁוֹרוֹ הָדָר לוֹ'). The great tzaddik and his holy speech are two faces of the same reality in this Torah — the great tzaddik IS the one with holy speech of dual meaning. Merging on shared nature (bechina), not cause-effect."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "צְדָקָה וּמַיִם", "label_en": "Charity and Water", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All six nodes are different faces of the same essential reality: the flowing, softened, generous heart that gives charity. The Torah explicitly states: צדקה = מים (charity is water), נדיבי לב = בעלי צדקה (generous-hearted = people of charity), and אגם מים = לב רך (pool of water = soft heart ready to give). These are three separate bechina pairs in the dataset but all express the single quality of open-hearted charitable generosity — the softened heart that pours itself out like water.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "מים", "נדיבי לב", "בעלי צדקה", "אגם מים", "לב רך"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 2, "מים": 2, "נדיבי לב": 1, "בעלי צדקה": 1, "אגם מים": 1, "לב רך": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נדיבי לב", "בעלי צדקה"], "reason": "Component E merged into Package 2 because 'נדיבי לב' and 'בעלי צדקה' are the Torah's explicit bechina: 'שֶׁהַצַּדִּיק מְקַבֵּל דִּבּוּר פִּיו מִנְּדִיבֵי לֵב, דְּהַיְנוּ בַּעֲלֵי צְדָקָה'. These generous-hearted donors share the essential nature of צדקה/מים — they are the human embodiment of the charity-water quality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אגם מים", "לב רך"], "reason": "Component C merged into Package 2 because 'אגם מים' (pool of water, from 'הַהֹפְכִי הַצּוּר אֲגַם מָיִם') is explicitly equated with 'לב רך' — the soft heart that volunteers for charity ('הַיְנוּ לֵב רַךְ לְהִתְנַדֵּב לִצְדָקָה'). Both are the same quality as מים and צדקה: the flowing, open, giving heart."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "לֵב אֶבֶן", "label_en": "The Stone Heart", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All four nodes are aspects of the same evil domain: the hardened heart that is closed to charity. 'לב אבן' (stone heart) = 'צור לבבי' (rock of my heart) = 'הצור' (the rock in the verse) = 'אבירי לב הרחוקים מצדקה' (the strong-hearted who are far from charity). These are all the same condition of spiritual hardness, the opposite of the soft, water-flowing heart of Package 2. The Rebbe identifies them explicitly through the verse 'הַהֹפְכִי הַצּוּר אֲגַם מָיִם' — the rock (evil Package 3) needs to be transformed into a pool of water (good Package 2).", "dominant_node": "\"צור לבבי\"", "nodes": ["\"צור לבבי\"", "\"לב אבן\"", "\"אבירי לב הרחוקים מצדקה\"", "הצור"], "node_degrees": {"\"צור לבבי\"": 3, "\"לב אבן\"": 1, "\"אבירי לב הרחוקים מצדקה\"": 1, "הצור": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַצַּדִּיק הַפָּשׁוּט", "label_en": "The Simple Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "גמלא and צדיק הפשוט are two names for the same positive figure: the humble tzaddik who walks in simplicity, does not speak of greatness, and whose entire service is in the domain of silence. The Rebbe derives this from the camel (גמל) metaphor: 'וְלֹא הִלַּכְתִּי בִּגְדֹלוֹת וְנִפְלָאוֹת כְּגָמֻל עֲלֵי אִמּוֹ'. This is a distinct positive figure from the great tzaddik (Package 1): the simple tzaddik is the one who has no parasitic false prophets clinging to him.", "dominant_node": "גמלא", "nodes": ["גמלא", "צדיק הפשוט"], "node_degrees": {"גמלא": 1, "צדיק הפשוט": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "נָבִיא וּמוֹרֶה שֶׁקֶר", "label_en": "The False Prophet", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "נביא ומורה שקר and זנב are two expressions of the same evil domain: the hypocritical deceiver who mimics the true tzaddik. The Rebbe uses the tail (זנב) as the metaphor for the false prophet — the tail is attached to the ox but is not the ox; it is the parasite that imitates. This is the evil mirror of the great tzaddik (Package 1): Package 1 is the true bearer of holy speech; Package 5 is the one who steals and corrupts that speech.", "dominant_node": "נביא ומורה שקר", "nodes": ["נביא ומורה שקר", "זנב"], "node_degrees": {"נביא ומורה שקר": 1, "זנב": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 15, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יַבֹּ\"ק וְזִוּוּג", "label_en": "YBK and Holy Union", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes are aspects of the same spiritual reality: יב\"ק (the acronym for יחוד ברכה קדושה — unity, blessing, holiness). 'בקי' is the Aramaic word in the Talmudic dialogue that the Rebbe decodes as יב\"ק; 'יבק בקדשה' is the holy dimension of this concept; and 'זווג' (holy union/coupling) is what יבק in holiness actually means — 'בְּחִינַת הַיַּבֹּק בִּקְדֻשָּׁה הוּא בְּחִינַת זִוּוּגִין דִּקְדֻשָּׁה'. All four are the same essential quality of holy union and blessing that flows from proper charity.", "dominant_node": "יב\"ק", "nodes": ["יב\"ק", "בקי", "יבק בקדשה", "זווג"], "node_degrees": {"יב\"ק": 1, "בקי": 1, "יבק בקדשה": 1, "זווג": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בקי", "יב\"ק"], "reason": "Component F (בקי — יב\"ק) merged with Component J (יבק בקדשה — זווג) because both pairs refer to the same underlying concept: יב\"ק. 'בקי' is the Talmudic word that the Rebbe decodes as an acronym for יב\"ק (יחוד ברכה קדושה). 'יבק בקדשה = זווג' is the holy meaning of that same יב\"ק. Two components, one reality."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 16, "label": "סוּרָא וְהָעַכּוּ\"ם", "label_en": "Sura and the Nations — The Side of Idolatry", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These two nodes share the essential nature of the forces of deviation from God — the nations (עכו\"ם) in their aspect of idolatry and separation. The Rebbe explicitly states: 'סוּרָא זֶה בְּחִינַת עַכּוּ\"ם, עַל שֵׁם עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: סָרוּ מַהֵר מִן הַדֶּרֶךְ' — the place-name Sura IS the nations, named from the root סור (to deviate). Both express the same essential quality: the reality of idolatry as radical departure from the path. The context in Torah #16 makes this evil identification structurally important: the two rivers of Sura represent the two Messianic streams that will eventually draw the nations back — indicating that Sura/the nations are the very audience that must be redeemed. This is the only bechina edge present in the dataset for this Torah.", "dominant_node": "עכו\"ם", "nodes": ["סורא", "עכו\"ם"], "node_degrees": {"סורא": 1, "עכו\"ם": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw component: {סורא, עכו\"ם} — 2 nodes, 1 edge (dataset id 2388).", "No merges possible: this is the only bechina-connected component in the Torah #16 dataset. The teaching contains a rich structure of additional bechinas not yet in the dataset (see torah_16_additions.json and torah_16_new_packages.json). Until those are added, this 2-node evil package stands alone.", "This package would naturally expand to include: עֵשָׂו וְיִשְׁמָעֵאל (the two nations explicitly listed as comprising the עכו\"ם of Sura), and תְּרֵין עֲנָנִין (the two clouds = Esav and Yishmael covering the Tzadik's eyes). Those additions are proposed in torah_16_additions.json."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 16, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פַּרְנָסָה וּמַעֲדַנֵּי מֶלֶךְ", "label_en": "Livelihood and Kingly Delicacies", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Parnasa (livelihood) and Maadanei Melekh (kingly delicacies) share the same essential identity: the divine sustenance channeled through the King is not merely food but a royal feast — it IS kingly delicacies in its essential nature. The Rebbe states this explicitly: 'הַפַּרְנָסָה שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת מַעֲדַנֵּי מֶלֶךְ, כִּי מְקַבְּלִין אוֹתָהּ עַל יְדֵי הַמֶּלֶךְ'. Parnasa received through the King is essentially royal, not mundane provision — both expressions point to the same divine bounty flowing through royalty.", "dominant_node": "פרנסה", "nodes": ["פרנסה", "מעדני מלך"], "node_degrees": {"פרנסה": 1, "מעדני מלך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 16, "volume": "lm2", "label": "בֵּרוּרִים וּקְטֹרֶת", "label_en": "Clarifications and Incense", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Birurim (spiritual clarifications/refinements) and Ketoret (incense) share the same essential nature: both are the process and result of extracting the holy from the shell. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'וְאֵלּוּ הַבֵּרוּרִים הֵם בְּחִינַת קְטֹרֶת, כַּיָּדוּעַ, שֶׁקְּטֹרֶת הִיא בְּחִינַת בֵּרוּרִים'. The incense offering was known to BE clarification — the burning that releases pure fragrance from physical matter is the same essential act as the spiritual clarification that extracts holy sparks from their material coating.", "dominant_node": "ברורים", "nodes": ["ברורים", "קטרת"], "node_degrees": {"ברורים": 1, "קטרת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 16, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הִתְחַבְּרוּת אִמְרֵי שֶׁפֶר עִם הַמֶּלֶךְ וְחֲתֻנָּה", "label_en": "The Union of Beautiful Words with the King and Wedding", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The joining of Imrei Shefer (beautiful words — blessings and prayers) with the King shares the same essential nature as Chatuna (wedding/sacred union). The Rebbe states: 'כִּי הִתְחַבְּרוּת שֶׁמִּתְחַבְּרִין הָאִמְרֵי שֶׁפֶר עִם הַמֶּלֶךְ, זֶה בְּחִינַת חֲתֻנָּה'. The joining of holy speech with royalty IS a wedding in its essential nature — the intimate bonding of two distinct domains into one is exactly what a wedding is. The verse 'חֵן שְׂפָתָיו רֵעֵהוּ מֶלֶךְ' confirms this: the grace of lips (Imrei Shefer) joins with the King as a companion/friend (reut), the intimacy of marriage.", "dominant_node": "חתנה", "nodes": ["התחברות שמתחברין האמרי שפר עם המלך", "חתנה"], "node_degrees": {"התחברות שמתחברין האמרי שפר עם המלך": 1, "חתנה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וְגִלּוּי אוֹרוֹ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Revelation of His Light", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant thread of Torah #17 is the tzaddik who seeks out and reveals the glory (tiferet) of God hidden in every Jew. This cluster groups the concrete names/images the Rebbe assigns to the tzaddik's function: the biblical Mordechai (who 'searches' = dibchisha), Tzror ha-kessef (the bundle of silver = the tzaddik's concentrated holiness), and the act of denying idol worship (כפירה בעבודה זרה) which is what makes someone a Jew/tzaddik. These four nodes plus the tzaddik node itself all share the same essential nature: they are faces of the tzaddik's being and activity — one who concentrates and seeks and refuses to bow. This is the largest single connected component in the Torah (5 nodes, with צדיק having 3 connections).", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "מרדכי", "דבחישא", "צרור כספו", "כפירה בעבודה זרה"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 3, "מרדכי": 2, "דבחישא": 1, "צרור כספו": 1, "כפירה בעבודה זרה": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 17, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִרְאָה וְיָרֵא שַׁבָּת", "label_en": "Fear of God and Shabbat Awe", "polarity": "good", "reason": "ירא שבת is explicitly called a bechina of יראה (complete fear of God). They share the same essential nature: awe of the Divine. ירא שבת is not a different quality from yirah — it is yirah in its complete form, when da'at is whole and kesilut has been removed by Shabbat freedom. One is the general principle, the other is its full expression on Shabbat.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "ירא שבת"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 1, "ירא שבת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "הִתְפָּאֲרוּת יִשְׂרָאֵל וְנִשְׁמוֹת הַגְּנוּזוֹת", "label_en": "The Glory of Israel and the Hidden Soul-Sparks", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #17's central theological claim is that God's glory (hitpaarut) is expressed through Israel — this is the very reason for creation. This package clusters all bechina-equivalents of that glory and its structural consequences: the hitpaarut of God with Israel = the birthday of the king (yom huledeth hamelekh), = God's tefillin. Equally part of this domain: the hitpaarut of Israel itself = Israel's tefillin. And 'all goodness' = souls of Israel, plus the hidden good (sparks of Israel's souls captured among the nations). The revelation of the glory = revelation of yirah (edge 993) belongs here because the two are stated as the same thing (the moment the tiferet is uncovered IS the moment yirah emerges — they share one essential nature). Seven isolated smaller components are merged into this package: Component I (3), Component T (2), Component U (2), Component H (2), Component E (2) = total 11 nodes. All share the nature: divine glory, Israel's essential holiness, and the sacred equivalence between hitpaarut and yirah.", "dominant_node": "התפארות של השם יתברך שמתפאר בישראל", "nodes": ["התפארות של השם יתברך שמתפאר בישראל", "יום הלדת המלך", "תפלין של השם יתברך", "התפארות של ישראל", "תפלין", "כל הטוב", "נשמות ישראל", "טוב הכבוש תחת ידם", "חלקי נשמות ישראל הכבוש אצלם", "התגלות ההתפארות", "התגלות היראה"], "node_degrees": {"התפארות של השם יתברך שמתפאר בישראל": 2, "התגלות ההתפארות": 1, "התגלות היראה": 1, "יום הלדת המלך": 1, "תפלין של השם יתברך": 1, "התפארות של ישראל": 1, "תפלין": 1, "כל הטוב": 1, "נשמות ישראל": 1, "טוב הכבוש תחת ידם": 1, "חלקי נשמות ישראל הכבוש אצלם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["התגלות ההתפארות", "התגלות היראה"], "reason": "Edge 993 (ref=17, bechina): 'hitgalut hahitpaarut, zeh bechina hitgalut hayirah'. The revelation of God's glory and the revelation of fear are stated as bechina-equivalents — two names for the same moment. This is structurally a face of the hitpaarut cluster: when glory is uncovered, yirah emerges as its twin. Merged into Package 2 because the shared nature is 'hitpaarut' — the revelation that flows from God glorifying Himself with Israel."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יום הלדת המלך", "תפלין של השם יתברך"], "reason": "Both are explicitly stated as bechinot of 'hitpaarut of God with Israel' (edges 1284 and 3208). Yom huledeth hamelekh = the moment kingship is born = when the tzaddik reveals the glory; God's tefillin = the parchment inside which IS the statement 'who is like your people Israel' = the hitpaarut encoded in leather and parchment. Same nature as the hitpaarut package."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התפארות של ישראל", "תפלין"], "reason": "Edge 3207 (ref=17, bechina): 'hitpaarut of Israel = tefillin, which are called pe'er (glory)'. Israel's glory is the nature of tefillin — same domain as the God-Israel hitpaarut. Merged into Package 2."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כל הטוב", "נשמות ישראל"], "reason": "Edge 2230 (ref=17, bechina): 'kol hatov hu rak bechina nishmat Yisrael'. All goodness = Israel's souls. This is the metaphysical ground of the hitpaarut doctrine: Israel's holiness (= all goodness in the world) is the content of God's glorification. Merged into Package 2."}, {"merged_nodes": ["טוב הכבוש תחת ידם", "חלקי נשמות ישראל הכבוש אצלם"], "reason": "Edge 1184 (ref=17, bechina): the good hidden among the nations = the captive Israel-soul-sparks. This is the narrative of how the hitpaarut-content (Israel's holiness) gets captured by the klipot and must be redeemed — the very problem the tzaddik's work of revealing glory is meant to solve. Same bechina domain: the stuff of Israel's essential holiness. Merged into Package 2."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "יִרְאָה וְאַהֲבָה וְאֲוִיר הַנָּח", "label_en": "Fear, Love, and the Calm Air", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Yirah and ahavah are the Torah's opening and closing theme — the two inner spiritual states that the tzaddik reveals. This package groups all concepts that are essentially the same as yirah and ahavah: 'shamayim' = fire and water = yirah and ahavah (edges 467, 1413); 'oz' = yirah (edges 1403, 1404); 'ezah' = the goat of the sea (the tzaddik's searching creature whose strength is yirah); 'ahavah' = avir hanach (love is cleaving spirit to spirit = calm transparent air, edge 147); 'ratzon' = ahavah (edge 133). The three sub-components all share the essential nature of yirah-ahavah as a unified spiritual force that can be present (calm air) or absent (stormy wind). Component A (3 nodes: אהבה, רצון, אויר הנח), Component C (3 nodes: שמים, אש ומים, יראה ואהבה), Component J (3 nodes: עז, יראה, עזא) = 9 nodes total.", "dominant_node": "יראה ואהבה", "nodes": ["יראה ואהבה", "שמים", "אש ומים", "אהבה", "רצון", "אויר הנח", "יראה", "עז", "עזא"], "node_degrees": {"שמים": 2, "אהבה": 2, "עז": 3, "יראה ואהבה": 1, "אש ומים": 1, "רצון": 1, "אויר הנח": 1, "יראה": 1, "עזא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אהבה", "רצון", "אויר הנח"], "reason": "Edge 133: ratzon = ahavah. Edge 147: ahavah = avir hanach. These three form a bechina triangle: God's will/desire is love, and love is the calm transparent air through which the tzaddik's voice travels. All three are expressions of the same quality: the spiritual adhesion (itdabkut rucha be-rucha) that enables transmission. Merged into Package 3."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שמים", "אש ומים", "יראה ואהבה"], "reason": "Edges 467 and 1413: shamayim = esh u-mayim = yirah ve-ahavah. The word 'shamayim' (heaven) is explicitly unpacked twice as the union of fire (yirah) and water (ahavah). This is the same bechina nature as the main yirah-ahavah cluster. Merged into Package 3."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יראה", "עז", "עזא"], "reason": "Edges 1403, 1404, and the null-id edge: oz = yirah (stated twice for emphasis), and iza (the sea-goat creature) = oz. So iza = oz = yirah — all three are faces of the same spiritual quality: the 'strength of fear' that the tzaddik reveals (ki tiferet uzamo atah). Merged into Package 3."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "מִזְבֵּחַ וַאֲכִילָה וְשֵׂכֶל", "label_en": "The Altar, Holy Eating, and Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #17 teaches that the Jew's dining table atones like the altar (shulchano shel adam mechaper ke-mizbeach). This cluster encompasses: the mizbeach = eating (edge 243), the table = mizbeach (edge 2937), the 'wolf in Benjamin's portion / Torah's territory' = completeness of the altar (edges 1730, 2942), and proper eating = 'teref natan lire'av' = the portion of the altar in the wolf's territory. Through proper eating (not gluttony), the sekhel is purified and the klipot/kessilut are subdued. The companion isolated components F (אבא, חכמה) and G (אות יו\"ד, חכמה שמתפשט בכל העולמות) also belong here: chokhmah = the intellect that proper eating supports; 'av be-chokhmah' = the sekhel that is called father (the text at line 93 explicitly links the mezuman's father — avihems — to sekhel called 'av be-chokhmah'). So the mizbeach cluster and the chokhmah cluster share the same nature in this Torah: they are both about the sekhel's clarity that is damaged by gluttony and protected by holy eating. Total 7 (B+L components) + 4 (F+G) = 11 nodes.", "dominant_node": "מזבח", "nodes": ["מזבח", "אכילה", "\"טרף נתן ליראיו\"", "שלחנו של אדם", "שלמות המזבח", "מזבח בחלקו של טורף", "יאכל טרף", "אבא", "חכמה", "אות יו\"ד", "חכמה שמתפשט בכל העולמות"], "node_degrees": {"מזבח": 2, "שלמות המזבח": 2, "אכילה": 2, "\"טרף נתן ליראיו\"": 1, "שלחנו של אדם": 1, "מזבח בחלקו של טורף": 1, "יאכל טרף": 1, "אבא": 1, "חכמה": 1, "אות יו\"ד": 1, "חכמה שמתפשט בכל העולמות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שלמות המזבח", "מזבח בחלקו של טורף", "יאכל טרף"], "reason": "Edges 1730 and 2942: 'shleimut hamizbeach = mizbeach be-chelko shel toref' (the altar's territory being in Benjamin's tribal land = the altar's wholeness), and 'yokhal teref = shleimut hamizbeach'. These three are the same concept viewed from different scriptural angles: the altar is complete precisely when it occupies the territory of the 'wolf' (binyamin/toref) — the paradox of holiness sustained within wildness. Same nature as the mizbeach/eating cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אבא", "חכמה"], "reason": "Edge 1153: 'avihema hu ha-sekhel she-nikra av, bechina av be-chokhmah'. In this Torah the 'father' = sekhel (intellect/chokhmah). The text (line 93) explicitly connects the father-figure to the sekhel that must be pure for the tzaddik's light to be visible. Chokhmah/sekhel is what proper eating at the mizbeach supports and gluttony damages. Merged into Package 4."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אות יו\"ד", "חכמה שמתפשט בכל העולמות"], "reason": "Edge 1171: yud = chokhmah that spreads through all the worlds (kulam be-chokhmah asita). This appears in the 'yam' (sea) exposition (line 77): the yod of 'yam' = chokhmah spreading through all worlds. In this Torah, the yod-chokhmah is the spiritual intellect behind creation, which is exactly what the sekhel of the av/father represents — the same quality. Same domain as the chokhmah/mizbeach package."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "גֵּרִים וּצְדָקָה וּשְׁלֵמוּת הַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Converts, Charity, and the Completion of Knowledge", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The middle movement of Torah #17 (sections ד-ז) is devoted to the tzaddik's voice spreading through the world via tzedakah (charity creates love, love creates pure air, pure air carries the voice). The result: gerim (converts) — people who find the opposite of their old faith in their own books and abandon idolatry. The 'horn' (karna) = tzedakah = raising the horn of Israel. The gerim are identified with 'the evil that separates from the nations along with the good it was holding' (רע שנתלש). The final result of the gerim-process: 'yakiru ve-yeid'u kol yoshvei tevel' — universal recognition = shleimut hada'at. All these concepts share the same bechina nature: they are all the outcome of the tzaddik's voice spreading via the pure-air of love/tzedakah, drawing the lost sparks back. Components D (3), P (2), S (2) = 7 nodes.", "dominant_node": "גרים", "nodes": ["גרים", "\"להפנות אליך כל רשעי ארץ\"", "רע שנתלש מהם עם הטוב, על - ידי שחזר הטוב למקומו", "קרן", "צדקה", "\"יכירו וידעו כל יושבי תבל\"", "שלמות הדעת"], "node_degrees": {"גרים": 2, "\"להפנות אליך כל רשעי ארץ\"": 1, "רע שנתלש מהם עם הטוב, על - ידי שחזר הטוב למקומו": 1, "קרן": 1, "צדקה": 1, "\"יכירו וידעו כל יושבי תבל\"": 1, "שלמות הדעת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קרן", "צדקה"], "reason": "Edge 2555: 'karna = tzedakah, she-hu bechina hon yosif reim rabim'. The horn (karna) of the sea-goat creature is tzedakah — because tzedakah is the principal (keren kayemet) and what builds friendships. These two are bechinically identical in the context of this Torah: the raised horn = the tzedakah that multiplies friends = the avir hanach that carries the voice. Tzedakah is the instrument of the gerim process (the voice spreading), so it belongs in the gerim package."}, {"merged_nodes": ["\"יכירו וידעו כל יושבי תבל\"", "שלמות הדעת"], "reason": "Edge 2940: 'yakiru ve-yeidu kol yoshvei tevel = shleimut hada'at'. This verse from the Aleinu prayer describes the ultimate goal of the gerim process: when all nations recognize God = complete knowledge. It is the telos of the tzedakah/gerim/voice chain. Merged into Package 5 as the culminating bechina of what gerim achieve."}, {"merged_nodes": ["גרים", "\"להפנות אליך כל רשעי ארץ\"", "רע שנתלש מהם עם הטוב, על - ידי שחזר הטוב למקומו"], "reason": "Edges 632 and 2749: 'lehafnot elekha kol rish'ei aretz = gerim' and 'gerim = the evil that tears loose from them along with the good, when the good returns to its root'. These three describe the same phenomenon — the wicked of the earth being turned to God = the convert phenomenon = what happens when the stolen sparks of Israel's souls return home dragging the evil shell with them. All three are faces of the gerim bechina."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וְנֶפֶשׁ הַיּוֹרֶדֶת", "label_en": "Repentance and the Descending Soul", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Section ח of the Torah (the Leviathan midrash) is devoted to the soul's metaphysical journey: it descends through the four worlds (mem = 4 worlds, yud = chokhmah spreading through them), remembers its divine origin via the shin-machshava (thought), is reminded of divine counsel via the parsah-regel-etzah (the hooves = foot = advice), faces extinction (puma de-livyatan = the leviathan's mouth), and repents, becoming ba'alei teshuvah and gerim. The concepts in this package — thought/shin, foot/advice/parsah, teshuvah/Shaddai — all share the nature of the soul's interior process of descent and return. Components M (2), N (3), and R (3) = 8 nodes. The three components connect thematically through the text of the 'sea-creature' midrash: the animal's hoof (parsah) = foot = advice that the soul needs; the shin = the three-headed thought that splits into intellect/emotion/action; teshuvah = Shaddai = the cosmic reconciliation of 'there is enough in My divinity for every being' (ein tzarich le-shum avoda acheret).", "dominant_node": "תשובה", "nodes": ["תשובה", "שדי", "\"לתקן עולם במלכות שדי וכל בני בשר יקראו בשמך\"", "אות שין", "מחשבה", "רגל", "עצה", "פרסה"], "node_degrees": {"תשובה": 2, "רגל": 2, "שדי": 1, "\"לתקן עולם במלכות שדי וכל בני בשר יקראו בשמך\"": 1, "אות שין": 1, "מחשבה": 1, "עצה": 1, "פרסה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תשובה", "שדי", "\"לתקן עולם במלכות שדי וכל בני בשר יקראו בשמך\""], "reason": "Edges 2842 and 3248: teshuvah = Shaddai (because Shaddai = 'there is enough in My divinity for every creature', meaning one needs no other avodah — this is the inner logic of abandoning idolatry = teshuvah), and 'letaken olam be-malkhut Shaddai = teshuvah, when the good returns to its place'. These three share the bechina of teshuvah: the return of the stolen good to its root. Shaddai is the divine name that encodes this sufficiency."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אות שין", "מחשבה"], "reason": "Null-id edge (ref=17, bechina): 'shin = machshava she-necheleket le-shalosh rashim: machshavot sikhli'ot, machshavot midoti'ot, machshavot ma'asiyot'. The three-pronged letter shin is explicitly an aspect of thought — the threefold subdivision of the soul's interior life. In the midrash context this is the soul remembering its origin in divine thought. Merged into Package 6 as the mental/interior dimension of the soul's descent."}, {"merged_nodes": ["רגל", "עצה", "פרסה"], "reason": "Edges 2429 and 2662: regel = etzah, parsah = regel. So parsah (hoof) = regel (foot) = etzah (advice/counsel). In the midrash, the sea-creature's hoof reminds the descending soul of the divine counsel (etzah) at the beginning of creation — that the soul was 'consulted' in the creation of the world. This is the soul's reminder of its purpose. All three are bechina-equivalents describing the same concept: the foot/hoof as the organ of guidance-following. Merged into Package 6."}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "שִׂנְאָה וְרוּחַ רָעָה — פְּגַם הָאַהֲבָה", "label_en": "Hatred and the Evil Wind — the Defect in Love", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #17 teaches that love is the calm air (avir hanach) through which the tzaddik's voice travels. Its evil mirror is hatred (sinah), which is explicitly equated to a 'ruach ra'ah' (evil wind) that scatters the air-particles and makes it impossible to hear even a voice, let alone speech. Edge 2717: sinah = ruach ra'ah. This is the exact negative of the love/avir-hanach cluster (Package 3) — same structural quality, opposite orientation. A separate evil package is warranted because this is an independent bechina (the evil wind), not merely absence of good. It is the active force opposing the tzaddik's voice-transmission.", "dominant_node": "שנאה", "nodes": ["שנאה", "רוח רעה"], "node_degrees": {"שנאה": 1, "רוח רעה": 1}}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 17, "label": "אֲכִילַת זוֹלֵל וְסוֹבֵא — פְּגַם הַמִּזְבֵּחַ", "label_en": "Gluttonous Eating — Defiling the Altar", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 4 (holy eating = purifying the altar/sekhel): when one eats like a glutton and drunkard (zolel ve-soveh), this is a bechina of 'pgam hamizbeach' — desecrating the altar. Edge 2474: 'ke-she-okhel ke-zolel ve-soveh, zeh bechina pgam hamizbeach'. The consequence is that the klipot suck from the altar more than their due, and the kessilut (foolishness/klipah) overpowers the sekhel. This is structurally the evil counterpart of Package 4's holy eating theme. The two nodes (gluttony and desecration of the altar) form an isolated bechina pair and constitute their own evil domain — the klipah of the eating-table.", "dominant_node": "פגם המזבח", "nodes": ["אוכל כזולל וסובא", "פגם המזבח"], "node_degrees": {"אוכל כזולל וסובא": 1, "פגם המזבח": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 18, "label": "שְׁלֵמוּת הָאוֹתִיּוֹת וְהַתַּכְלִית", "label_en": "Perfection of Letters and Ultimate Purpose", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster centers on the teaching's grand unifying insight: the perfection of the letters of speech IS the ultimate purpose of all creation, and that purpose IS the letter yud, which IS the World to Come. All nodes in this package share the same essential nature — the domain of divine speech-letters at their point of completion. 'אשת' (wife) is an acronym-bechina of the letters aleph-shin-tav ('אבנים שלמות תבנה'); 'אותיות' are the building-blocks of creation; 'שפה ברורה' and 'שלמות הדבור' are the realized form of perfected speech; 'תכלת' is an explicit bechina of 'תכלית'; 'שלמות האותיות של דבורי אמונה' and 'אשת רבי חנינא בן דוסא' are the same reality from two angles (the letters-of-faith quality that belongs to the tzaddik's household). The dominant node 'שלמות האותיות' has the highest degree (2) and appears in two key edges: connecting to 'אות יו\"ד' (the completion-point of every letter) and to 'תכלית של כל הנבראים' (the telos of all existence).", "dominant_node": "שלמות האותיות", "nodes": ["שלמות האותיות", "אות יו\"ד", "עולם הבא", "תכלית של כל הנבראים", "תכלת", "תכלית", "אשת", "אותיות", "שלמות האותיות של דבורי אמונה", "אשת רבי חנינא בן דוסא", "שפה ברורה", "שלמות הדבור"], "node_degrees": {"שלמות האותיות": 2, "אות יו\"ד": 2, "עולם הבא": 1, "תכלית של כל הנבראים": 1, "תכלת": 1, "תכלית": 1, "אשת": 1, "אותיות": 1, "שלמות האותיות של דבורי אמונה": 1, "אשת רבי חנינא בן דוסא": 1, "שפה ברורה": 1, "שלמות הדבור": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אשת", "אותיות"], "reason": "The text states explicitly: 'אֵשֶׁת זֶה בְּחִינוֹת הָאוֹתִיּוֹת, שֶׁשְּׁלֵמוּתָם עַל-יְדֵי אֱמוּנָה, בִּבְחִינַת רָאשֵׁי תֵּבוֹת: אֲבָנִים שְׁלֵמוֹת תִּבְנֶה'. The word 'אשת' (wife) IS the letters — they share the same essential nature as the rest of the letters-cluster. Not merged into emunah because the shared nature here is the letter-domain itself, not the faith-quality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שלמות האותיות של דבורי אמונה", "אשת רבי חנינא בן דוסא"], "reason": "Edge 481 bechina: perfection of letters of faith-speech = wife of R. Chanina ben Dosa. This pair belongs with the letters-package because its essential nature is the completed letters of speech (דבורי אמונה), not the emunah-quality itself. The 'אשת' (wife) dimension is the letters, as established above."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שפה ברורה", "שלמות הדבור"], "reason": "Edge 2939 bechina: pure speech = perfect speech. 'שפה ברורה' (clear language) IS 'שלמות הדבור' (perfection of speech). Both are aspects of the same domain as 'שלמות האותיות' — the completion of divine language. Merged into the letters-package."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תכלת", "תכלית"], "reason": "Edge 3125 bechina: the blue dye (תכלת) IS the ultimate purpose (תכלית). The text says this explicitly. Since 'תכלית' is essentially synonymous with 'תכלית של כל הנבראים' (both appear in the same teaching passage describing the same theological referent — the purpose that letters point toward), this pair merges into the letters-completion cluster which already contains 'תכלית של כל הנבראים'."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 18, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וְרַבִּי חֲנִינָא", "label_en": "Faith and Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster unites all nodes that share the essential quality of simple, steadfast faith (אמונה). Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa is a bechina of faith because his defining quality is 'a qav of carobs' — minimal material sustenance sustained entirely by divine trust. The carobs (חרובין) ARE the beroshim-tree (ברושים) ARE the biyarsha-fish (בירשא) — all of these are bechina expressions of the same quality: the minimal sustenance that characterizes pure faith. The scriptural formula 'לִקְרֹא כֻלָּם בְּשֵׁם ה'' is an explicit bechina of emunah in the text. The dominant node 'אמונה' has the highest degree in this cluster (2 edges: to 'לקרא כלם בשם ה'' and to 'רבי חנינא בן דוסא'), making it the natural cluster-head.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "\"לקרא כלם בשם ה'\"", "רבי חנינא בן דוסא", "ברושים", "בירשא", "חרובין"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 2, "\"לקרא כלם בשם ה'\"": 1, "רבי חנינא בן דוסא": 1, "ברושים": 2, "בירשא": 1, "חרובין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ברושים", "בירשא", "חרובין"], "reason": "This raw component (edges 539+573: חרובין↔ברושים↔בירשא) describes three plant/fish metaphors that are bechina-equivalents of each other. The source text makes explicit that חרובין = ברושים = בירשא = the expression of R. Chanina's faith-quality ('קב חרובין – מדת אמונה; חרובין – בחינת ברושים, בחינת בירשא'). All three are aspects of the same domain as אמונה — the sparse, ascetic vehicle through which faith is expressed. Merged into the emunah cluster on grounds of shared essential nature."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 18, "label": "רַחֲמָנוּת וְעֲטָרָה", "label_en": "Mercy and the Crown", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes sharing the essential nature of rachamim (divine mercy) as the quality that sweetens anger and crowns the humble tzaddik. 'עטרה' (crown) IS 'רחמנות' (mercy) — the crown IS the quality of mercy, and this is an explicit bechina in the text. 'יַמְתִּיק הַכַּעַס בְּרַחֲמָנוּת' and its scriptural bechina 'בְּרֹגֶז רַחֵם תִּזְכּוֹר' both express the same essential act — the transformation of anger into mercy that defines righteous leadership. The dominant node is 'רחמנות' — it connects the crown-concept (עטרה) with the practice of sweetening (ימתיק), making it the conceptual hub of this package.", "dominant_node": "רחמנות", "nodes": ["רחמנות", "עטרה", "ימתיק הכעס ברחמנות", "\"ברגז רחם תזכור\""], "node_degrees": {"רחמנות": 1, "עטרה": 1, "ימתיק הכעס ברחמנות": 1, "\"ברגז רחם תזכור\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ימתיק הכעס ברחמנות", "\"ברגז רחם תזכור\""], "reason": "This raw 2-node component (edge 20) describes the essential practice of sweetening anger through mercy, with the verse 'בְּרֹגֶז רַחֵם תִּזְכּוֹר' as its scriptural expression. Its core concept is רחמנות — shared nature with the עטרה↔רחמנות pair (edge 2740). Merged here because both pairs describe the same essential quality: mercy that transforms anger into a crown. This is NOT merged into Package 4 (Malkhut) even though rachamim leads to leadership — that causal flow is eitza, not bechina."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 18, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְחֲכָמִים", "label_en": "Kingship and Sages", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster unites the domain of holy kingship and its proper foundation in wisdom. 'מלכות' IS 'הנהגה' (leadership/governance) — they share the same essential nature: sovereign ordering of a community. 'משפט' (justice/law) IS 'חכמים' (sages) — the text derives this from Leviticus 26: 'if you despise my mishpatim' = 'one who hates the sages.' Therefore mishpat and chachamim are bechina-equivalents, both describing the wisdom-dimension that makes kingship sustainable. The text says explicitly: 'עיקר המלכות שרשה בחכמה... על ידי ראשי עם, שהם המוחין והחכמה, על ידם תיכון בחינת המלכות.' Wisdom/sages IS the foundation of malkhut — they share the same structural domain.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "הנהגה", "משפט", "חכמים"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 1, "הנהגה": 1, "משפט": 1, "חכמים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["משפט", "חכמים"], "reason": "Edge 1177 bechina: mishpat = chachamim, derived from 'ואם את משפטי תגעל נפשכם — זה השונא את החכמים.' Both nodes describe the same domain: the wisdom-principle that upholds kingship. Merged with the מלכות↔הנהגה pair because all four nodes share the essential nature of holy, wisdom-grounded governance. This is NOT a causal merge — the teaching is that the essence of malkhut IS the chachamim/mishpat quality, not merely that sages cause good governance."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 18, "label": "הַסְתָּרַת פְּנֵי הַשֵּׁם וְהַכַּעַס", "label_en": "Divine Hiddenness and Anger", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 3 (Rachamim/Crown). Where Package 3 describes anger SWEETENED into mercy (the good pole), Package 5 describes anger NOT sweetened — the result being divine hiddenness (hester panim). The text is explicit: 'הַהַסְתָּרָה הַזֹּאת הִיא בְּחִינוֹת כַּעְסוֹ וַחֲרוֹן-אַפּוֹ' — the hiding of God's face IS His anger. This bechina-pair (edge 1518) represents the negative pole of the Torah's central dynamic. Unlike the good cluster where the tzaddik sweetens anger with rachamim, here the anger remains un-sweetened, producing divine withdrawal and preventing proper leadership of the world. This package stands alone as a 2-node evil component — it is kept separate from Package 3 even though the teaching discusses them together, because they are opposite polarities of the same coin (not the same essential nature).", "dominant_node": "הסתרת פני השם", "nodes": ["הסתרת פני השם", "כעס"], "node_degrees": {"הסתרת פני השם": 1, "כעס": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 19, "label": "לְשׁוֹן הַקֹּדֶשׁ וּבְחִינַת הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Holy Speech and the Covenant", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All 19 nodes in this package share the essential nature of the holy speech dimension — the divine, creative, covenantal dimension of language as Rebbe Nachman elaborates it. חוה IS the speech of לשון הקדש (the text states: 'וזה בחינת חוה, בחינת ולילה ללילה יחוה, הינו בחינת הדבור של לשון הקדש'). אשה and אשה חכמה are bechinas of לשון הקדש because חוה-אשה IS the holy speech. שנים מקרא IS לשון הקדש (reading Scripture twice in the holy tongue). The verse-fragment 'ולילה ללילה יחוה' IS the speech of לשון הקדש. 'אעשה לו עזר' IS חוה IS לשון הקדש. 'עזרך' IS 'אעשה לו עזר'. בשר (flesh) IS חוה because 'נחש שלטנותיה על בשרא — הינו בחינת חוה הנ\"ל, שהיא בשר מבשרו' — the bodily dimension of the same speech-soul reality. רוח הקדש IS לשון הקדש ('מלשון הקדש, מרוח הקדש'). ברית IS רוח הקדש (id=582: the holy covenant IS the holy spirit). תקון הברית IS רוח הקדש (id=2698). מילה IS תקון הברית (id=3217 — circumcision IS the repair of the covenant). תקון המילה IS תקון הברית (id=3220). חשמ\"ל IS מילה (id=1241 — 'חש-מל: בחינת מילה'). מתקלא IS שלמות לשון הקדש (id=2948 — the cosmic scale/balance IS the perfection of holy speech). פנים מאירות IS פנים בפנים (id=2497 — radiant face IS the face-to-face state; the Torah opens with 'עד לא הוי מתקלא לא הוי משגיחין אפין באפין', placing this state at the very center of the teaching). All nodes belong to the single essential domain of holy speech-as-covenant, spanning its spiritual (רוח הקדש, ברית), bodily (מילה, חשמ\"ל, בשר), personal (חוה, אשה), and cosmic (מתקלא, שלמות לשון הקדש, פנים בפנים) dimensions.", "dominant_node": "לשון הקדש", "nodes": ["לשון הקדש", "חוה", "אשה", "אשה חכמה", "שנים מקרא", "\"ולילה ללילה יחוה\"", "\"אעשה לו עזר\"", "\"עזרך\"", "בשר", "רוח הקדש", "ברית", "תקון הברית", "מילה", "תקון המילה", "חשמ\"ל", "מתקלא", "שלמות לשון הקדש", "פנים מאירות", "פנים בפנים"], "node_degrees": {"לשון הקדש": 6, "חוה": 4, "אשה": 2, "אשה חכמה": 1, "שנים מקרא": 1, "\"ולילה ללילה יחוה\"": 1, "\"אעשה לו עזר\"": 2, "\"עזרך\"": 1, "בשר": 1, "רוח הקדש": 3, "ברית": 1, "תקון הברית": 3, "מילה": 2, "תקון המילה": 1, "חשמ\"ל": 1, "מתקלא": 1, "שלמות לשון הקדש": 1, "פנים מאירות": 1, "פנים בפנים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מתקלא", "שלמות לשון הקדש"], "reason": "Raw component CC6: edge 2948 says 'מתקלא, שהוא בחינת שלמות לשון הקדש.' The source text at line 61 says 'עד לא הוי מתקלא, הינו בחינת לשון הקדש, שעיקר שלמותו על ידי תרגום.' The cosmic balance-scale (מתקלא) IS the holy speech in its state of completion. This 2-node component shares essential nature with the Holy Speech package (CC1) — both מתקלא and שלמות לשון הקדש describe the same dimension as לשון הקדש, namely the divine speech-reality at its point of fullness. Not merged into Package 3 (Targum) even though the text mentions that תרגום completes it — that relationship is eitza (cause-effect), not bechina (shared nature)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פנים מאירות", "פנים בפנים"], "reason": "Raw component CC7: edge 2497 says 'פנים מאירות, שהם בחינת פנים בפנים.' The Torah opens with 'עד לא הוי מתקלא, לא הוי משגיחין אפין באפין' (Sifra deTzniuta) — the face-to-face state is the defining image of what מתקלא (= שלמות לשון הקדש = Package 1) achieves. The radiant face and the face-to-face encounter are aspects of the same dimension as the Holy Speech package, specifically the 'פנים מזכות' (the tzaddik's illuminated face that enables others to see themselves, as the Torah describes in section ב). Merged into Package 1 — NOT into Package 3 — because what IS the radiant face is the holy speech, not the targum's mediation of it."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 19, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲמָלֵק, פִילוֹסוֹפִים וְאֶבֶן נֶגֶף", "label_en": "Amalek, Philosophers, and the Stumbling Block", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Amalek, the philosophers-and-heretics, and the stumbling block embedded in each of the seven wisdoms are all different expressions of the same evil reality: philosophical inquiry that operates by natural reason alone, denies God, and thereby causes spiritual downfall. The Rebbe makes this identity explicit in both directions: 'עֲמָלֵק, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַפִילוֹסוֹפִים וְהַכּוֹפְרִים' and 'יֵשׁ אֶבֶן נֶגֶף בְּכָל חָכְמָה וְחָכְמָה, שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת עֲמָלֵק'. The stumbling block (אֶבֶן נֶגֶף) is not a different entity from Amalek — it IS Amalek manifesting within each discipline. The philosophers and heretics are not a different entity from Amalek — they ARE Amalek in its contemporary expression. All three share the same essential nature: the anti-faith intellectual force that denies fear of God and practical commandments.", "dominant_node": "עמלק", "nodes": ["עמלק", "פילוסופים והכופרים", "אבן נגף בכל חכמה וחכמה"], "node_degrees": {"עמלק": 2, "פילוסופים והכופרים": 1, "אבן נגף בכל חכמה וחכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 19, "label": "פְּגַם הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Damage of the Covenant", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster is the direct evil mirror of Package 1's ברית dimension. פגם הברית IS רע הכולל (id=2746: 'פגם הברית, שהוא רע הכולל') — the damage of the covenant IS the comprehensive evil, containing within it all seventy nations' evils. רע הכולל IS לשונות העמים (id=1669: the comprehensive evil IS the nations' tongues — the opposite of לשון הקדש). פגם הברית IS משוך בערלתו (id=1970: covenantal damage IS the state of uncircumcision — 'אדם הראשון משוך בערלתו היה; אדם הראשון בלשון ארמית ספר', source text line 77). All four nodes describe the same essential evil domain: the negation and damage of the covenantal bond whose positive pole is Package 1. The source text makes the structural symmetry explicit: holy speech (Package 1) IS tikkun habrit; damage of the brit (Package 2) IS comprehensive evil IS the nations' tongues. Where Package 1 defines holy speech-as-covenant, Package 2 defines the evil inverse — broken covenant as the root of all evil and foreign speech.", "dominant_node": "פגם הברית", "nodes": ["פגם הברית", "משוך בערלתו", "רע הכולל", "לשונות העמים"], "node_degrees": {"פגם הברית": 2, "משוך בערלתו": 1, "רע הכולל": 2, "לשונות העמים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 19, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תַּכְלִית וְסוֹף דָּבָר", "label_en": "Ultimate Purpose and the Final Word", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly states: 'עִקָּר הַשָּׂגַת הַתַּכְלִית, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת סוֹף דָּבָר'. The verse from Kohelet — 'סוֹף דָּבָר הַכֹּל נִשְׁמָע' — is not merely an illustration of the concept of telos; the Rebbe identifies it as the same thing. Both concepts point to the identical reality: the ultimate endpoint and summation of existence, which is achieved through simplicity and fear of God. תַּכְלִית (telos/ultimate purpose) and סוֹף דָּבָר (the final word/conclusion of all things) are two names for the same essential nature — the completion and culmination of all human effort and spiritual aspiration.", "dominant_node": "תכלית", "nodes": ["תכלית", "סוף דבר"], "node_degrees": {"תכלית": 1, "סוף דבר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 19, "label": "תַּרְגּוּם וְנֹגַהּ", "label_en": "Aramaic Translation and the Mixed Twilight Domain", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes that share the essential nature of the mixed, twilight domain between holiness and evil — the domain of Aramaic translation (תרגום) as the liminal zone of the עץ הדעת טוב ורע. The governing bechina is id=2418: 'תרגום הוא בחינת עץ הדעת טוב ורע, שמעורב טוב ורע.' The שוגג (inadvertent sinner) IS תרגום (id=3236) because both have 'טוב ורע — the act is evil, the intention is good.' שגיונות IS תרגום (id=3235 — the verse title of the Torah, שגיונות = unintentional errors = targum-domain). 'אשה משכלת' IS תרגום (id=9 — Targum is a bechina of 'the wise woman' from Proverbs 19, who can be a חכמה or a מְשַׁכֵּל depending on orientation). תרדמה IS לשון תרגום (id=1668 — deep sleep is the targum-state; the targum ascends through sleep to suckle from holy speech). נגה IS תרגום (id=3234 — נגה, the kabbalistic sephirot of 'twilight-brightness', is the mixed-good world that IS the Aramaic translation domain). נסיון IS נגה (id=2098 — the trial/test IS the נגה state). בחירה IS נגה (id=2036 — free will/choice IS the נגה state: 'עיקר המתקלא והנסיון והבחירה הוא בבחינת נגה'). All nodes in this package share the essential character of the intermediate zone: neither pure holiness nor pure evil, but the domain where discernment, trial, choice, and the elevation of good from mixed sources occurs. Polarity is neutral because the targum-domain by its own nature contains both.", "dominant_node": "תרגום", "nodes": ["תרגום", "\"אשה משכלת\"", "עץ הדעת טוב ורע", "תרדמה", "לשון תרגום", "שגיונות", "שוגג", "נגה", "נסיון", "בחירה"], "node_degrees": {"תרגום": 5, "\"אשה משכלת\"": 1, "עץ הדעת טוב ורע": 1, "תרדמה": 1, "לשון תרגום": 1, "שגיונות": 1, "שוגג": 1, "נגה": 3, "נסיון": 1, "בחירה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תרדמה", "לשון תרגום"], "reason": "Edge 1668: תרדמה IS לשון תרגום ('תרדמה, שהוא בחינת לשון תרגום'). The node 'לשון תרגום' is the same concept as 'תרגום' by consonantal root and essential referent — both designate the Aramaic translation. In the dataset they appear as distinct node_ids, so they are treated as distinct nodes in this package; both belong here as aspects of the deep-sleep / translation state that is the targum's essential nature."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 19, "label": "מְדוּרָה שֶׁל שִׁבְעִין כּוֹכְבִין", "label_en": "The Fiery Furnace of the Seventy Stars", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nine nodes share the essential nature of the dominant evil force in Torah #19: the collective fire of the seventy nations' spirit-energy, which is the root of sexual lust and the domination of foreign speech. מדורה של שבעין כוכבין IS תאות נאוף (id=3069: 'המדורה של שבעין כוכבין הנ\"ל, שהוא בחינת תאות נאוף'). שאר לשונות העמים IS מדורה (id=1708: the nations' other languages ARE the fire of the seventy stars). מ\"ל (from חשמ\"ל) IS מדורה (id=1706: the 'מל' part of חשמ\"ל = gematria 70, IS the fire of seventy stars). מל IS מדורה (id=1707). שאר לשונות העמים IS רע גמור (id=2745: the other nations' languages ARE pure evil — in contrast to תרגום which is mixed). רוח שטות IS שבעים לשון (id=2825: the spirit of folly IS the seventy languages). רוח סערה IS רוח שטות (id=2720: the storm-wind IS the spirit of folly). The package represents the undifferentiated evil pole of the language-domain: the fire of sexual lust, the spirit of folly, and the nations' languages all share the same essential nature as expressions of the seventy-star fire that opposes the holy covenant. This is the evil counterpart of Package 1's ברית-dimension: where מילה cuts and subdues this fire (via חשמ\"ל), the מ\"ל that gets cut away IS this fiery domain.", "dominant_node": "מדורה של שבעין כוכבין", "nodes": ["מדורה של שבעין כוכבין", "תאות נאוף", "מ\"ל מ\"חשמ\"ל\"", "מל", "שאר לשונות העמים", "רע גמור", "רוח שטות", "שבעים לשון", "רוח סערה"], "node_degrees": {"מדורה של שבעין כוכבין": 4, "תאות נאוף": 1, "מ\"ל מ\"חשמ\"ל\"": 1, "מל": 1, "שאר לשונות העמים": 2, "רע גמור": 1, "רוח שטות": 2, "שבעים לשון": 1, "רוח סערה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 19, "label": "ל\"ב אֱלֹקִים וְאוֹתִיּוֹת הַבְּרִיאָה", "label_en": "The Thirty-Two Divine Names and the Letters of Creation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The five nodes in this package share the essential nature of the cosmic creative letters — the divine letters (ל\"ב אלקים) that were used to create the world and that are present, albeit concealed, in every material thing. ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית IS אותיות שיש בכל דבר (id=203: 'ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית, שהם בחינת האותיות שיש בכל דבר, שכולם נמשכין מבחינת ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית'). שופרא דשופרא IS ל\"ב אלקים (null id: 'ונזון משופרא דשופרא, מל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית' — the beauty-of-beauties, the nourishing light of creation, IS the thirty-two divine names). ברכות המזון IS אכילתו ושתיתו מהתנוצצות האותיות (id=261: blessing over food IS eating and drinking from the flashing letters that exist within the food and drink — the Grace after Meals IS the act of consuming the letter-sparks). The five nodes describe the same domain: the divine creative letters that animate physical reality, accessible through blessing over food, which activates the same creative names that formed the world. This package is the cosmic substrate that makes the Holy Speech package (Package 1) possible — the letters of לשון הקדש derive their power from the same ל\"ב אלקים, though the causal relationship is eitza, not bechina.", "dominant_node": "ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית", "nodes": ["ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית", "אותיות שיש בכל דבר", "שופרא דשופרא", "ברכות המזון", "אכילתו ושתיתו מהתנוצצות האותיות שיש בהאכילה ושתיה"], "node_degrees": {"ל\"ב אלקים של מעשה בראשית": 2, "אותיות שיש בכל דבר": 1, "שופרא דשופרא": 1, "ברכות המזון": 1, "אכילתו ושתיתו מהתנוצצות האותיות שיש בהאכילה ושתיה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ברכות המזון", "אכילתו ושתיתו מהתנוצצות האותיות שיש בהאכילה ושתיה"], "reason": "Edge 261: ברכות המזון IS the act of eating from the letter-sparks in food. These two nodes are bechinas of each other within the same domain as ל\"ב אלקים (the letters in food ARE the same letters that ל\"ב אלקים planted in all created things). The raw CC5 component (ל\"ב אלקים / אותיות / שופרא דשופרא / ברכות המזון / אכילתו) is unified by the text's statement that one nourishes oneself from שופרא דשופרא = ל\"ב אלקים of creation = the letters in all things = the letter-sparks in food = activated by blessing (ברכות המזון). All five nodes share the cosmic-letters-of-creation essential nature and are kept together as Package 5."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "חֹטֶם, יִרְאָה וּתְפִלָּה", "label_en": "The Nose, Awe and Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The nose (חוטם) is the organ of spiritual smell — it 'smells' the awe of God ('והריחו ביראת ה''). In Torah #20 the nose is equated with: (1) the verse 'ודברתם אל הסלע' — the humble supplication before the rock/heart, (2) the phrase 'יראת ה' היא תתהלל' — fear of God as the quality of prayer. Independently, יראה = תפלה (bechina edge 3161). All these nodes share the same essential nature: the quality of humble awe before God, expressed through the nose as the organ of 'smelling holiness' and through prayer as the verbal manifestation of that awe. The חוטם is also the Sefira of the nose in the beard-tikunim commentary that structures the second half of the Torah. CC1 and CC13 merged here because יראה=תפלה and חטם='יראת ה' היא תתהלל' are both expressions of the same essential spiritual quality of humble awe.", "dominant_node": "חטם", "nodes": ["חטם", "\"והריחו ביראת ה\"", "ודברתם אל הסלע", "\"יראת ה היא תתהלל\"", "יראה", "תפלה"], "node_degrees": {"חטם": 3, "\"והריחו ביראת ה\"": 1, "ודברתם אל הסלע": 1, "\"יראת ה היא תתהלל\"": 1, "יראה": 1, "תפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יראה", "תפלה"], "reason": "CC13 (יראה=תפלה) merged into CC1 (חטם cluster) because the nose = יראת ה' = תהלה/תפלה. All three concepts share the same essential spiritual quality: humble awe before God expressed as prayer. This is shared nature, not cause-effect — חטם IS the organ of יראה, and יראה IS תפלה."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 20, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דֶּרֶךְ חָכְמָה וְהֵרָיוֹן", "label_en": "The Path of Wisdom and Spiritual Gestation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three pairs all describe the same essential reality: a spiritual path (derech) in its gestation phase is identical in nature to a pregnant woman. (E2) הדרך חכמה בעצמו = אשה הרה — the path of wisdom itself IS the pregnant woman; both are vessels of unrevealed potential awaiting the right moment of birth. (E5) כשמתחיל לכנס לאיזה דרך = הריון — the moment of entering a new path IS pregnancy in its essential nature; the Rebbe states explicitly 'כשמתחיל לכנס לאיזה דרך הוא בחינת הריון'. (E1) נפסד גם הדרך חכמה בעצמו באפן שאפלו איש אחר אינו יכול לכנס לשם = מיתת האשה הרה — the total ruin of the path IS the death of the pregnant woman; both describe the catastrophic termination not merely of its fruit but of its very generative capacity. All three pairs are expressions of the same domain: the holy path that must gestate in hiddenness before being revealed to the world.", "dominant_node": "הדרך חכמה בעצמו", "nodes": ["הדרך חכמה בעצמו", "אשה הרה", "כשמתחיל לכנס לאיזה דרך", "הריון", "נפסד גם הדרך חכמה בעצמו, באפן שאפלו איש אחר אינו יכול לכנס לשם בבחינת הריון, לגלות ולהוציא זה הדרך לעולם", "מיתת האשה הרה"], "node_degrees": {"הדרך חכמה בעצמו": 1, "אשה הרה": 1, "כשמתחיל לכנס לאיזה דרך": 1, "הריון": 1, "נפסד גם הדרך חכמה בעצמו, באפן שאפלו איש אחר אינו יכול לכנס לשם בבחינת הריון, לגלות ולהוציא זה הדרך לעולם": 1, "מיתת האשה הרה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הדרך חכמה בעצמו", "אשה הרה", "כשמתחיל לכנס לאיזה דרך", "הריון", "נפסד גם הדרך חכמה בעצמו, באפן שאפלו איש אחר אינו יכול לכנס לשם בבחינת הריון, לגלות ולהוציא זה הדרך לעולם", "מיתת האשה הרה"], "reason": "Three raw connected components (E1, E2, E5) are merged into one package because all three share the same essential nature: the spiritual derech and its gestation phase. (E2) הדרך חכמה = אשה הרה: the path IS the pregnant woman. (E5) כניסה לדרך = הריון: entering a path IS pregnancy. (E1) הרס הדרך = מיתת האשה הרה: destruction of the path IS the woman's death. These are not cause-and-effect — each pair states a bechina identity (A IS B), and all three pairs illuminate the same thematic family: the path of wisdom as a living, gestating entity that can be born or killed. The E1 long node is a descriptive formulation of הדרך חכמה בעצמו in its destroyed state, which is functionally the same domain node as E2's הדרך חכמה בעצמו, making E1 a further specification within the same pregnancy metaphor domain."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "לֵב הָעֶלְיוֹן וְהַסֶּלַע", "label_en": "The Upper Heart and the Rock", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant cluster of Torah #20 identifies four synonyms for the Divine Source from which Torah interpretation flows: לב העליון (the upper heart), סלע (rock), צור (rock/cliff), and לב (heart). These are four names for the same metaphysical reality — the upper divine heart from which hot, fiery speech and Torah interpretation emanate when prayer opens it. ירושלים = לב because 'דברו על לב ירושלים' (Isaiah 40). 'צור לבבי' (Psalms 73) equates the rock with the heart. 'פתח צור ויזובו מים' (Psalms 105) equates the rock with the source of speech-water. The תפוחים (rosy apples of the beard, tikun 5) are the opening-keys of this heart — when the heart opens with divine compassion, fiery speech/Torah flows. All nodes in this package share one essential nature: the divine source of Torah interpretation, the holy heart that must be opened through prayer.", "dominant_node": "לב העליון", "nodes": ["לב העליון", "\"צור לבבי\"", "סלע", "ירושלים", "לב", "צור", "תפוחים", "מפתחי הלב שנפתחים"], "node_degrees": {"לב העליון": 2, "סלע": 2, "צור": 2, "לב": 2, "\"צור לבבי\"": 1, "ירושלים": 1, "תפוחים": 1, "מפתחי הלב שנפתחים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תפוחים", "מפתחי הלב שנפתחים"], "reason": "CC10 (תפוחים=מפתחי הלב שנפתחים) merged into CC3 (לב העליון cluster) because תפוחים are the keys that open the upper heart. The tappuchin (tikun 5 of the beard) share the essential nature of 'the mechanism by which the upper heart opens.' They ARE the opening of the heart, not a cause of it — shared nature with לב העליון/סלע."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 20, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מְפֻרְסָמִים קֹדֶם זְמַנָּם וּנְפָלִים", "label_en": "Those Made Famous Before Their Time and Stillborn Fetuses", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Two pairs describing the same essential nature from slightly different angles. (E3) המפרסמים שנעשין קדם זמנם = נפלים — those who are made famous before their time ARE stillborn fetuses (plural); the Rebbe states 'הם בבחינות נפלים כנ\"ל'. (E4) יוצא קדם זמנו = נפל — one who goes out before his time IS a stillborn (singular). Both pairs are bechina statements equating premature emergence with stillbirth. They belong in the same package because they are two formulations of the identical equivalence (one plural, one singular; one referring to the group, one to the individual). The polarity is neutral: these persons are not the evil agents (machloket-makers are the cause) but rather the metaphorical victims — the premature births. They are structural metaphor-objects describing the effect of controversy on spiritual emergence, capable of existing in any spiritual context.", "dominant_node": "נפלים", "nodes": ["המפרסמים שנעשין קדם זמנם", "נפלים", "יוצא קדם זמנו", "נפל"], "node_degrees": {"המפרסמים שנעשין קדם זמנם": 1, "נפלים": 1, "יוצא קדם זמנו": 1, "נפל": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["המפרסמים שנעשין קדם זמנם", "נפלים", "יוצא קדם זמנו", "נפל"], "reason": "E3 and E4 are merged into one package because both encode the same bechina: premature revelation = spiritual stillbirth. E3 states it in the plural (those who become famous = nfalim) and E4 in the singular (one who goes out before time = nefal). The shared nature is identical across both edges — premature emergence is stillbirth. This is pure bechina (shared nature), not eitza: the prematurely-revealed person does not produce the stillborn, they ARE the stillborn."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "קְלִפּוֹת סְבִיב הַלֵּב — מִרְאַת יְרוּשָׁלַיִם בַּגּוֹיִם", "label_en": "Klipot Surrounding the Heart — Jerusalem Among the Nations", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 2. While Package 2 is the upper heart (lev ha-elyon) as the holy source of Torah, Package 3 is the klipot — the impure husks — that surround and besiege that same heart. The verse 'זאת ירושלים שמתיה בתוך הגוים' (Ezekiel 5:5) represents the heart/Jerusalem surrounded by hostile nations = the klipot. These two nodes share the essential nature: the encircling evil forces that prevent Torah interpretation from flowing by besieging the upper heart. Note that 'ירושלים' appears in Package 2 as the positive identity (Jerusalem = heart of holiness) and here 'ירושלים שמתיה בתוך הגוים' appears as the symbol of that holy heart under siege — the same concept with opposite orientation, exactly as the framework predicts.", "dominant_node": "קלפות השוכנים סביב לב העליון", "nodes": ["קלפות השוכנים סביב לב העליון", "\"זאת ירושלים שמתיה בתוך הגוים\""], "node_degrees": {"קלפות השוכנים סביב לב העליון": 1, "\"זאת ירושלים שמתיה בתוך הגוים\"": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "מַטֵּה הַצַּדִּיק, שַׁעֲרֵי צִיּוֹן וְהַמִּשְׁפָּט", "label_en": "The Tzaddik's Staff, Gates of Zion, and Judgment", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Three originally separate components share the same essential nature: the power of the tzaddik's holy service as a force of conquest and judgment in the world. (1) שערות/שערי ציון/נימין — the hairs of the beard in the Zoharic tikunim equal 'שערי ציון' = the gates of Zion, from which מטה עוז goes forth ('מטה עזך ישלח ה' מציון'). The hairs ARE the gates of power. (2) מטה/ממשלת וכח הצדיק — the staff equals the dominion and power the tzaddik builds through his divine service. The staff IS that power, not a tool of it. (3) מימיך/משפט — 'your waters' = mishpat/judgment ('ויגל כמים משפט', Amos 5:24). Judgment flows like water from Edom's territory. All three clusters share the essential nature: the tzaddik's authority to subdue wickedness — whether expressed as the hairs/gates of Zion, the staff of his service, or the judgment-water that flows from Edom. They form one domain: holy power subduing evil.", "dominant_node": "שערות", "nodes": ["שערות", "\"שערי ציון\"", "נימין", "מטה עזך ישלח מציון", "מטה", "ממשלת וכח הצדיק שנעשה על ידי עבודתו", "מימיך", "משפט"], "node_degrees": {"שערות": 2, "\"שערי ציון\"": 2, "נימין": 1, "מטה עזך ישלח מציון": 1, "מטה": 1, "ממשלת וכח הצדיק שנעשה על ידי עבודתו": 1, "מימיך": 1, "משפט": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מטה", "ממשלת וכח הצדיק שנעשה על ידי עבודתו"], "reason": "CC9 (מטה=ממשלת וכח הצדיק) merged with CC4 (שערות=שערי ציון=מטה עוז) because the 'matte' in CC9 IS the same 'מטה עוז' in CC4 — the staff that goes forth from Zion. Shared nature: the tzaddik's power/staff/authority. Not cause-effect — the staff IS the power."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מימיך", "משפט"], "reason": "CC11 (מימיך=משפט) merged here because the teaching uses judgment (mishpat from Edom's waters) as one expression of the tzaddik's subduing power, alongside the staff and the gates of Zion. 'Waters' = judgment = the tzaddik's authority flowing into the world. Shared essential nature: holy power that subdues the wicked — same domain as the matte and sha'arei Zion."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "בֵּאוּרֵי הַתּוֹרָה, הַפָּנִים וְהַבְּאֵר", "label_en": "Torah Interpretation, the Face, and the Well", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all represent the same metaphysical entity: the substance of Torah interpretation as it manifests in the world. (1) באר = Torah interpretation — 'הבאר, הינו בחינת באורי התורה' (the well = Torah explanations, established from the Midrashic tradition that the Beer of Miriam = Torah). (2) באורי התורה = פנים (face) — 'באורי התורה, שהם בחינת פנים' — face/presence = Torah interpretation. (3) פני ה' = באורי ודרושי התורה — 'פני ה' זה בחינת באורי ודרושי התורה, כמו 'והדרת פני זקן'; זקן זה הדרת פנים, והם שלש-עשרה תיקוני דיקנא, והם שלש-עשרה מידות שהתורה נדרשת בהן' — the face of God = the Torah's 13 hermeneutical principles as embodied in the 13 beard-tikunim. All five nodes share one essential nature: Torah interpretation in its various expressions (well/face/divine presence/hermeneutic rules).", "dominant_node": "באורי התורה", "nodes": ["באר", "באורי התורה", "פנים", "פני ה'", "באורי ודרושי התורה"], "node_degrees": {"באורי התורה": 2, "באר": 1, "פנים": 1, "פני ה'": 1, "באורי ודרושי התורה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פני ה'", "באורי ודרושי התורה"], "reason": "CC6 (פני ה'=באורי ודרושי התורה) merged into CC5 (באר=באורי התורה=פנים) because 'פני ה'' and 'באורי ודרושי התורה' are semantically identical to 'פנים' and 'באורי התורה' in CC5. The text uses both formulations for the same concept: Torah interpretation = Face/Presence. Shared nature: Torah's expository content."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "מִדְבַּר צִן — הַדִּבּוּר הַמְּצֻנָּן", "label_en": "Midbar Tzin — Frozen Speech (Evil Mirror of Torah)", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 5 (Torah interpretation/the well). 'מדבר צן' (Numbers 20:1) = 'דבור מצנן' — frozen/cold speech. When the soul that draws Torah interpretations falls from the level of 'הלא כה דברי כאש' (fiery divine speech), her speech becomes cold (מצנן). In Midbar Tzin, Miriam died — and the well (Torah interpretation, Package 5) departed. These two nodes share the essential nature: the failure-state of speech, the anti-Torah that results when divine fire is absent. They are structurally opposite to Package 5's 'well of Torah.' The place-name מדבר צן IS the bechina of frozen speech — same essential negative nature.", "dominant_node": "מדבר צן", "nodes": ["מדבר צן", "דבור מצנן"], "node_degrees": {"מדבר צן": 1, "דבור מצנן": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 20, "label": "משֶׁה, דַּעַת וּפְגַם הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Moses, Da'at, and the Blemish of the Covenant", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The node 'דעת' (da'at/divine knowledge) bridges two bechina edges pulling in opposite moral directions: (1) משה = דעת — Moses embodies da'at in its positive, rectified form (he is the angel sent to draw Israel out of Egypt by revealing da'at, per section י); (2) פגם הברית = פגם הדעת — the sin of the covenant IS the blemish of da'at, because sexual knowledge ('וידע אדם') is da'at corrupted. The same concept 'דעת' is both the rectification (Moses) and the wound (פגם הברית). This package is marked neutral because da'at itself is the battleground — it can be holy (Moses) or corrupted (blemish of covenant). The package captures the dual nature of da'at as described in this Torah. The evil dimension (פגם הברית/פגם הדעת) is the mirror of the positive dimension (משה/דעת), with דעת as the bridge node.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["משה", "דעת", "פגם הברית", "פגם הדעת"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 2, "פגם הדעת": 2, "משה": 1, "פגם הברית": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פגם הברית", "פגם הדעת"], "reason": "CC12 (פגם הברית=פגם הדעת) merged with CC8 (משה=דעת) via the shared node 'דעת.' Both components center on da'at: Moses IS da'at (good pole), and the blemish of the covenant IS the blemish of da'at (evil pole). Shared essential nature: da'at as the conceptual center — both the positive (Moses-da'at) and its negation (pgam ha-brit = pgam ha-da'at) are faces of the same da'at-domain."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 21, "label": "שֶׁפַע אֱלֹקִי וּמַקִּיפִין — הָאוֹר הָאֱלֹקִי הַסּוֹבֵב", "label_en": "Divine Influx and Surrounding Lights — The Encircling Divine Light", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are names or vessels for the same divine reality — the transcendent divine influx (שפע אלקי) that surrounds and exceeds human comprehension. שפע אלקי, רוח הקדש, מקיפין, קדש, חפה, קדושין, סכה — all are synonymous expressions of this surrounding holy light. The 7 candles/clouds/feast-days and their equivalents (שבעת הנרות, שבעה עננים/עננים הנ\"ל, שמן, שבעת ימי המשתה, פה-חטמא-עינין-אדנין) are the channels through which this influx reaches the human brain. ישראל = התגלות המחין = המשכת אור הפנים = שפע אלקי forms a single identity chain. עתיקא, קדם/פנים (the 'front' = the divine face-presence), לבוש/חשמל (the garment-as-protective-layer of the internalized מקיפין) all share this nature — they are aspects of the transcendent divine surrounding-light as it approaches the human. This is the dominant positive cluster of the entire Torah.", "dominant_node": "שפע אלקי", "nodes": ["שפע אלקי", "מקיפים", "סכה", "קדש", "המשכת אור הפנים", "פני המנורה", "רוח הקדש", "חפה", "עתיקא", "למעלה משכל אנושי", "קדושין", "עננים הנ\"ל", "אד", "שבעת הנרות", "פה ותרין נקבא חטמא ותרין עינין ותרין אדנין", "שבעת ימי המשתה שאחר החפה", "שמן", "התגלות המחין", "ישראל", "לבוש", "חשמ\"ל", "קדם", "פנים"], "node_degrees": {"שפע אלקי": 6, "מקיפים": 4, "שבעת הנרות": 4, "חפה": 3, "קדש": 3, "עננים הנ\"ל": 3, "סכה": 2, "רוח הקדש": 2, "המשכת אור הפנים": 2, "התגלות המחין": 2, "עתיקא": 1, "למעלה משכל אנושי": 1, "קדושין": 1, "אד": 1, "פני המנורה": 1, "ישראל": 1, "פה ותרין נקבא חטמא ותרין עינין ותרין אדנין": 1, "שבעת ימי המשתה שאחר החפה": 1, "שמן": 1, "לבוש": 1, "חשמ\"ל": 1, "קדם": 1, "פנים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["לבוש", "חשמ\"ל"], "reason": "CC7 (לבוש=חשמל) merged into Package A because the garment/חשמל is explicitly the protective form of the internalized מקיפין. 'מלבוש' in gematria = 'חשמל' and the חשמל repels all destroyers. The garment IS the מקיף made into an external shell — it shares the essential nature of surrounding/protecting light, not a cause-effect relationship. Belongs with the שפע-אלקי/מקיפין cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קדם", "פנים"], "reason": "CC9 (קדם=פנים) merged into Package A because קדם (= 'before' = front) is an explicit synonym for פנים (face/presence of God) throughout Torah #21. The text equates: 'שכל שבא בלא הקדמות הוא קדם, בשם פנים' = שפע אלקי. קדם/פנים share the essential nature of the divine face-light = שפע אלקי. Not a cause, but the same reality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 21, "label": "הַמֹּחַ וּמְנוֹרַת אֵבְרֵי הָרֹאשׁ", "label_en": "The Brain and the Menorah of the Head's Organs", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are the brain/intellect (מח/מחין) and its organs — the seven lights of the head-menorah through which divine influx enters. The מנורה IS the head/brain (רישא); the four pairs of organs (פה-חטם-עינין-אדנין) each have their own dimension of brain-light (מח הנאמר אצל X). The scripture verses paired with each organ ('ראש דברך אמת' = מח אצל פה; 'ראשית חכמה יראת ה' = מח אצל חטם; 'הוכח לחכם ויאהבך' = מח אצל אדנין; 'ותפקחנה עיני שניהם' = מח אצל עינין) are the bechina of each organ's brain-dimension. יראת שמים = חטם because 'והריחו ביראת ה'' — they share the essential nature of the organ of awe. כונה = מחין because 'הקול מעורר הכוונה, הינו המחין' — intention IS the brain. זעיר אנפין = all human-attainable wisdom — these wisdoms are the inner face of the brain as vessel. All are aspects of the same domain: the human intellect as the seven-branched menorah of the head.", "dominant_node": "מח", "nodes": ["מנורה", "מח", "רישא", "מח הנאמר אצל אדנין", "\"הוכח לחכם ויאהבך\"", "מח הנאמר אצל חטם", "\"ראשית חכמה יראת ה\"", "מח הנאמר אצל עינין", "\"ותפקחנה עיני שניהם\"", "מח הנאמר אצל פה", "\"ראש דברך אמת\"", "כונה", "מחין", "יראת שמים", "חטם", "כל החכמות שיכול מח אנושי להשיג אותם הם", "זעירא דאנפין"], "node_degrees": {"מנורה": 2, "מח": 1, "רישא": 1, "מח הנאמר אצל אדנין": 1, "\"הוכח לחכם ויאהבך\"": 1, "מח הנאמר אצל חטם": 1, "\"ראשית חכמה יראת ה\"": 1, "מח הנאמר אצל עינין": 1, "\"ותפקחנה עיני שניהם\"": 1, "מח הנאמר אצל פה": 1, "\"ראש דברך אמת\"": 1, "כונה": 1, "מחין": 1, "יראת שמים": 1, "חטם": 1, "כל החכמות שיכול מח אנושי להשיג אותם הם": 1, "זעירא דאנפין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כונה", "מחין"], "reason": "CC4 (כונה=מחין) merged into Package B because the equation 'הקול מעורר הכוונה, הינו המחין' makes כוונה/intention a direct bechina of מחין/brain. Intention shares the essential nature of the brain — it IS the brain in its active state. Belongs with the מח/מנורה cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יראת שמים", "חטם"], "reason": "CC5 (יראת-שמים=חטם) merged into Package B because the nose is the organ of awe ('והריחו ביראת ה'') — the nose IS fear-of-heaven in its sensory form. Furthermore, Package B already contains 'מח הנאמר אצל חטם' and '\"ראשית חכמה יראת ה\"' — the חטם-dimension of the brain. יראת-שמים-חטם belongs with the brain-organ cluster, sharing the essential nature of the nose as the organ of divine awe."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כל החכמות שיכול מח אנושי להשיג אותם הם", "זעירא דאנפין"], "reason": "CC6 (human-wisdom=זעיר-אנפין) merged into Package B because זעיר אנפין IS the inner face of human-attainable wisdom — the realm of what the human brain can grasp. This is contrasted with עתיקא (merged into Package A as transcendent). זעיר אנפין shares the essential nature of the human brain as vessel of wisdom."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 21, "label": "עִבּוּר, הֶעְלֵם וְלֵדַת הַמֹּחִין", "label_en": "Gestation, Concealment and the Birth of the Intellect", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are states, forms, and actors of the concealment-then-revelation cycle of the intellect. העלם = עבור (concealment = gestation) — these are two names for the same state. לא אתגליא = העלם (not-yet-revealed = concealment). יעקב = עבור — Jacob in the womb grasping his brother is the bechina of the intellect in its pre-birth, latent state. 'כשאדם לומד תורה ואין מבין חדוש' = עבור — Torah without new insight IS the gestation state. גלוי = לדה — revelation = birth, the moment the latent becomes manifest. The birth-cry of the woman in labor = the prayer-cry during intellectual gestation; both share the essential nature of the voice that labors toward birth. This package is neutral — it describes a universal structural condition (intellect oscillating between hidden and revealed states) that applies to both holy and mundane contexts.", "dominant_node": "עבור", "nodes": ["לא אתגליא", "העלם", "עבור", "יעקב", "כשאדם לומד תורה ואין מבין בה שום חדוש", "גלוי", "לדה", "צעקה שאדם צועק בתפלתו ובתורתו, כשנסתלקין המחין בבחינת עבור", "צעקת היולדת"], "node_degrees": {"עבור": 3, "העלם": 2, "לא אתגליא": 1, "יעקב": 1, "כשאדם לומד תורה ואין מבין בה שום חדוש": 1, "גלוי": 1, "לדה": 1, "צעקה שאדם צועק בתפלתו ובתורתו, כשנסתלקין המחין בבחינת עבור": 1, "צעקת היולדת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גלוי", "לדה"], "reason": "CC8 (גלוי=לדה) merged into Package C because revelation = birth shares the essential nature of the עבור/העלם cluster. גלוי is the opposite pole of העלם — they are the two states of the same cycle. לדה is the moment the עבור resolves. These belong with the concealment-revelation cluster as expressions of the same structural reality (the intellect oscillating between hidden and manifest)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צעקה שאדם צועק בתפלתו ובתורתו, כשנסתלקין המחין בבחינת עבור", "צעקת היולדת"], "reason": "CC10 (prayer-cry-during-gestation = birth-cry) merged into Package C because this cry IS the עבור state expressing itself. The prayer-cry during intellectual gestation shares the essential nature of concealment-straining-toward-birth. It is not a cause (it causes the birth, per the eitza edges) but here as a bechina it IS the gestational state in its active voice-form."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "נִשְׁמָע, תְּפִלָּה וּמַאֲמַר הַשָּׁלֵם — קֹדֶם הַבְּרִיאָה", "label_en": "Nishma, Prayer and the Complete Utterance — Before Creation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are names and aspects of the hidden, transcendent dimension — the 'complete utterance' (מאמר השלם) that precedes creation, which is identical to prayer (תפלה) and to Nishma (the hidden/aspirational dimension of divine service). The teaching's core equation is: בראשית = מאמר השלם = נשמע = תפלה = יראה = קדם הבריאה = אין סוף = עדן עלאה = חכמה עלאה = אבא. These are all parallel names for the same transcendent reality: the dimension that cannot yet be fully grasped, that exists before creation, that is oriented toward God in pure devotion without intellectual apprehension. The joy of נעשה ונשמע (merged from isolate M) IS this dimension's experiential form — the moment Israel said 'we will do and we will hear' they entered the domain of this package. The 'nishma'-dimension (hidden Torah, תורת ה', דבורי-התורה-שסביבות-המצוה) is the mystery that surrounds every commandment. בשת, עדן, and the witnesses (הנסתרת-לה-אלהינו, בימי-רבן-גמליאל) all express the same transcendent-hidden quality. ענוה/אין represents the self-nullification that is the essence of this domain.", "dominant_node": "נשמע", "nodes": ["נשמע", "קדם הבריאה", "תפלה", "מאמר השלם", "עדן עלאה", "אין סוף", "בראשית", "תורת ה'", "יראה", "תחלה", "אבא", "חכמה עלאה", "דבורי התורה שסביבות המצוה", "עדן", "אין", "נסתרות", "נעשה ונשמע", "שמחה", "ענוה", "תפלת ה'", "דבקות", "עמידה", "נסתר", "דברי תורה", "בשת", "בימי רבן גמליאל היה הדור", "הנסתרת לה אלהינו"], "node_degrees": {"נשמע": 9, "קדם הבריאה": 6, "עדן עלאה": 4, "תפלה": 5, "מאמר השלם": 4, "תחלה": 4, "אין סוף": 3, "בראשית": 3, "דבורי התורה שסביבות המצוה": 3, "חכמה עלאה": 2, "יראה": 2, "אבא": 2, "תורת ה'": 2, "נסתרות": 2, "אין": 2, "עדן": 2, "נעשה ונשמע": 1, "שמחה": 1, "ענוה": 1, "תפלת ה'": 1, "דבקות": 1, "עמידה": 1, "נסתר": 1, "דברי תורה": 1, "בשת": 1, "בימי רבן גמליאל היה הדור": 1, "הנסתרת לה אלהינו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נעשה ונשמע", "שמחה"], "reason": "Isolate M (נעשה ונשמע = שמחה) merged into Package 1 because the compound 'נעשה ונשמע' IS the joyful moment of entering the hidden dimension — 'בשעה שאמרו ישראל נעשה ונשמע, ירדו ששים ריבוא מלאכים... ועתיד הקב\"ה להחזירם... ושמחת עולם על ראשם'. The joy of this moment IS the שמחה of entering the transcendent nishma-domain. This is the same reality as נשמע/תפלה — not a cause (the eitza says שמחה → עזות → shepherds), but here as a bechina the compound IS the nature of this package's domain. Both nodes share the essential nature of 'the aspirational, hidden dimension of divine service as experienced in joy.'"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְנַעֲשֶׂה — הַמַּאֲמַר הַחֲצִי וְהַנִּגְלוֹת", "label_en": "Torah and Na'ase — The Half-Utterance and the Revealed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are names and aspects of the manifest, visible, actionable dimension — the 'half utterance' (חצי מאמר) of creation, identified with Torah (the revealed commandments), with Na'ase (we will do — what can be enacted), and with the created world itself. The teaching equates: ברא = חצי מאמר = כלליות הבריאה = חכמה תתאה = עדן תתאה = בן. And: נעשה = נגלות = תורה = תורתו = נגלה. These are parallel names for the same reality: the dimension of divine service that is within reach, graspable, actionable — the world as it can be experienced and commandments as they can be performed. 'נעשה הוא בחינת נגלות, הינו המצוות שאפשר לכל אחד לקיים לפי מדרגתו'. The pair בראשית/ברא maps to the full/half utterance, which maps to father/son (אב ובן), which maps to upper/lower Eden, which maps to hidden/revealed Torah. This package represents the lower, manifest pole of the Torah's central dialectic.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "נעשה", "ברא", "כלליות הבריאה", "חצי מאמר", "תורתו", "חכמה תתאה", "נגלות", "בן", "עדן תתאה", "נגלה", "נגלת", "והנגלת לנו ולבנינו"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 4, "נעשה": 4, "ברא": 3, "כלליות הבריאה": 2, "חצי מאמר": 2, "תורתו": 2, "חכמה תתאה": 2, "נגלות": 2, "בן": 2, "עדן תתאה": 2, "נגלה": 1, "נגלת": 1, "והנגלת לנו ולבנינו": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "עַזּוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה וְקוֹלוֹת — הַשּׁוֹפָר וְהַתְּרוּעָה", "label_en": "Holy Boldness and Voices — The Shofar and the Teruah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are all expressions of holy azut (boldness/strength) through voice and sound. The teaching equates: עזות דקדשה = כל הקולות דקדשה = קולות דקדשה = קול שופר = קול נגינה = קול אנחה = תשרי (the month of boldness). And: עזות = קולות (generic holy sounds). And the shofar sounds: קול התקיעות = 'מקול אנחתי', and תרועה = שופר = קול הנגון. 'כל הקולות — הן של צעקה, הן של אנחה, הן קול שופר, הן קול זמרה — כולם הם בחינת עזות'. These are all one domain: the voice-sounds through which holy boldness is expressed and evil-body-boldness is broken. The shofar, groan, song, and trumpet-blast are all aspects of the same holy force. תשרי is the month defined by this quality — 'ירח האיתנים' (month of strength). The disparate sound-types are unified by their shared function: they all ARE holy boldness.", "dominant_node": "עזות דקדשה", "nodes": ["עזות דקדשה", "קול שופר", "קולות דקדשה", "עזות", "קולות", "תרועה", "קול הנגון", "שופר", "קול אנחה", "קול נגינה", "קול המוכיח", "קול התקיעות", "\"מקול אנחתי\"", "כל הקולות דקדשה", "תשרי"], "node_degrees": {"עזות דקדשה": 4, "קול שופר": 3, "קולות דקדשה": 2, "תרועה": 2, "עזות": 1, "קולות": 1, "קול הנגון": 1, "שופר": 1, "קול אנחה": 1, "קול נגינה": 1, "קול המוכיח": 1, "קול התקיעות": 1, "\"מקול אנחתי\"": 1, "כל הקולות דקדשה": 1, "תשרי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עזות", "קולות"], "reason": "Isolate D (עזות = קולות, 2 nodes) merged into Package 3 because generic 'עזות' IS 'קולות' in the same sense as 'עזות דקדשה' = 'כל הקולות דקדשה'. The parallel is exact — the generic forms of these concepts share the same essential nature as the more specific forms already in the cluster. 'כל הקולות... כולם הם בחינת עזות'."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קול התקיעות", "\"מקול אנחתי\""], "reason": "Isolate F (קול-התקיעות = 'מקול-אנחתי', 2 nodes) merged into Package 3 because the shofar-blasts ARE the groaning voice of holy azut. 'ובחינת קול התקיעות הוא בחינת מקול אנחתי' — the trumpet sounds ARE the sighing-voice. This is the same domain of holy voice-sounds as the rest of Package 3."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תרועה", "שופר", "קול הנגון"], "reason": "Isolate G (תרועה = שופר = קול-הנגון, 3 nodes) merged into Package 3 because תרועה, שופר and the melody-voice are the same type of holy voice-sounds. 'תרועה — בחינת ואתה תרעה את עמי' = the sound that brings one to the holy shepherds. קול הנגון IS תרועה. All are aspects of the same holy voice/boldness domain."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "עַזּוּת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא — עַזּוּת הַגּוּף", "label_en": "Evil Boldness — The Boldness of the Body", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These two nodes share a single essential nature: they are the evil mirror of holy boldness (Package 3). The teaching explicitly distinguishes two types of azut: the holy azut of voices and prayer, and the evil azut of the body's appetites and the sitra achra's false leaders. 'כי עזות — מלכותא בלא תגא' (shameless kingship). 'הכלבים עזי נפש המה רועים' — the evil shepherds rule through this bodily-azut. This is the structural evil-counterpart of Package 3: same concept (boldness), opposite orientation (sitra achra vs. holiness). They are the obstacle that holy voices/azut must overcome.", "dominant_node": "עזות דסטרא אחרא", "nodes": ["עזות דסטרא אחרא", "עזות הגוף"], "node_degrees": {"עזות דסטרא אחרא": 1, "עזות הגוף": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "רַגְלִין, מוֹכִיחִים וְשִׁבְעָה רוֹעִים — הֶחָכָם וְהָעָם", "label_en": "Legs, Rebukers and Seven Shepherds — The Wise Man and the People", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are the tzaddikim/shepherds/leaders who stand between God and Israel, and the structural relationship between the wise man (soul/bone) and the people (flesh/body). Specifically: רגלים = המוכיחים = מוכיחי הדור (the feet/legs of the divine structure = the rebukers who go as God's emissaries). And: צדיק = כלליות שבעה רועים (the tzaddik who embodies all seven shepherds). And: חכם האמת = נשמה להעם = עצם (the true wise man IS the soul to the people IS the bone). And: עם = בשר (the people are the flesh/body). These are all aspects of the same structural reality: the tzaddik as the living connection between the divine (soul/bone) and the people (flesh), who rebuke and lead by going as God's messengers. The rebukers ARE the legs because they walk on God's behalf. The seven shepherds ARE the collective tzaddik. This package embodies the outer, walking, leading dimension of holiness — distinct from the inner seal (faith/Package 6) that they help fill.", "dominant_node": "רגלים", "nodes": ["רגלים", "חכם האמת", "צדיק", "כלליות שבעה רועים", "המוכיחים", "מוכיחי הדור", "עקב", "נשמה להעם", "עצם", "עם", "בשר"], "node_degrees": {"רגלים": 3, "חכם האמת": 2, "צדיק": 1, "כלליות שבעה רועים": 1, "המוכיחים": 1, "מוכיחי הדור": 1, "עקב": 1, "נשמה להעם": 1, "עצם": 1, "עם": 1, "בשר": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["צדיק", "כלליות שבעה רועים"], "reason": "Isolate J (צדיק = כלליות-שבעה-רועים) merged into Package 5 because the tzaddik IS the embodiment of the seven shepherds — same essential nature as the Rebukers/Legs cluster. The seven shepherds are the concrete form of what it means to be a רגל (leg/emissary) of God. 'כי ראה לפניו כלליות שבעה רועים' — the tzaddik who represents all the shepherds IS the source from which the rebukers draw."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חכם האמת", "נשמה להעם", "עצם"], "reason": "Isolate K (חכם-האמת = נשמה-להעם = עצם) merged into Package 5 because the true wise man IS the soul to the people IS the bone in the soul-body relationship — which is the tzaddik-people relationship structurally. 'החכם האמת, שהוא בחינת הנשמה להעם... הוא בחינת עצם'. This is the same domain as the shepherds/rebukers — the tzaddik as the animating force (soul/bone) of the people (flesh). They share the essential nature of holy leadership."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עם", "בשר"], "reason": "Isolate L (עם = בשר, people = flesh) merged into Package 5 because the people ARE the flesh in the tzaddik-people relationship, which is the central structural metaphor of this package. 'והעם הם נגדו בחינת בשר'. The people's nature (as flesh to the wise man's bone) belongs in the same package as the wise man/soul/bone cluster — they form the two poles of a single structural relationship."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 22, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וִירוּשָׁלַיִם — חוֹתָם הַיָּדַיִם", "label_en": "Faith and Jerusalem — The Seal of the Hands", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share a single essential nature: they are names and aspects of faith (אמונה) — the inner seal of holiness that is the hands' quality (חותם הידין). The teaching equates: אמונה = חותם הידין (faith = the inner seal — 'הידים זה בחינת אמונה'). And: אמונה = ירושלים = קריה נאמנה (faith = Jerusalem the faithful city). And: אמונה שלמה = ירושלים. Faith is what the seven shepherds tend ('ורעה אמונה'). Faith-Jerusalem is the culminating state of holiness toward which the entire Torah's argument flows. It is the 'inner seal' that must be protected — the שמירה יתרה of the teaching. This package is the goal/telos of the Torah's argument: after rebukers seal judgments → hands receive in faith → shepherds fill the hands → voices/boldness bring one to the shepherds → faith/Jerusalem is achieved.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "ירושלים", "חותם הידין", "אמונה שלמה"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 2, "ירושלים": 2, "חותם הידין": 1, "אמונה שלמה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "אֱמֶת וְאוֹר הַפָּנִים", "label_en": "Truth and the Light of the Countenance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of holy illumination emanating from truth-in-action. אמת, אור הפנים, אנפין נהירין, and פנים דקדשה are all expressions of the same luminous divine presence revealed through honest conduct. שבירת תאות ממון is equated with אמת (one is a bechina of the other), and both connect to אור הפנים. יעקב embodies אמת ('תתן אמת ליעקב') and is himself an aspect of אור הפנים. שמחה, חיים, and תקון המטבע are all facets of this same luminous-truth domain. חוט השני and זריחת אור הפנים share the nature of divine light breaking through darkness — the red thread of Rachav being the dawn of the face's light.", "dominant_node": "אמת", "nodes": ["אמת", "יעקב", "שבירת תאות ממון", "חיים", "אור הפנים", "\"שמח זבולון בצאתך\"", "תקון המטבע", "אנפין נהירין", "\"ויחן פני העיר\"", "אמונת אלקות", "פנים דקדשה", "ועליך יזרח ה", "שמחה", "חוט השני", "זריחת אור הפנים"], "node_degrees": {"אמת": 3, "יעקב": 2, "שבירת תאות ממון": 3, "חיים": 2, "אור הפנים": 3, "\"שמח זבולון בצאתך\"": 1, "תקון המטבע": 2, "אנפין נהירין": 2, "\"ויחן פני העיר\"": 2, "אמונת אלקות": 2, "פנים דקדשה": 2, "ועליך יזרח ה": 1, "שמחה": 1, "חוט השני": 1, "זריחת אור הפנים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חוט השני", "זריחת אור הפנים"], "reason": "Raw component of 2 nodes. חוט השני (the scarlet thread of Rachav) is equated with זריחת אור הפנים (the sunrise of divine light — 'ויקרא שמו זרח'). Both express the essential nature of holy light breaking through darkness, identical to the אור הפנים domain that anchors Package 1."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "עַצְבוּת וְאַנְפִּין חֲשׁוּכִין", "label_en": "Sadness and the Dark Countenance", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the dark side of the face — the spiritual-psychological state of money-lust, sadness, idolatry and death. עצבות is the hub: it IS the same thing as אנפין חשוכין, חשך, תאות ממון, דאגת הפרנסה, and the evil of the spleen (טחול). All are aspects of the same existential condition: the sitra achra of פנים — the face turned dark. שקר, עבודה זרה, and אלהים אחרים are identified as the same thing as the dark faces. פרנסה שהוא בעצבון (livelihood in sadness) is 'bitter as death' — it belongs to the same death-domain as מיתה and חשך. פרדה and עכו\"ם express the same rootlessness/separation that defines the evil side.", "dominant_node": "עצבות", "nodes": ["עצבות", "אנפין חשוכין", "דאגת ותאות הפרנסה", "טחול", "ממון של המשקעים בממון", "חשך", "תאות ממון", "עבודה זרה", "צער כפלים כיולדה", "אמא דערב רב", "אלהים אחרים", "מיתה", "שקר", "שחוק הכסיל", "פנים דסטרא אחרא", "אל אחר", "פרדה", "עכו\"ם", "פרנסה שהוא בעצבון", "מוצא אני מר ממות את האשה", "מרירותא דעלמא שצריך להמתיקה על ידי מלח"], "node_degrees": {"עצבות": 6, "אנפין חשוכין": 2, "דאגת ותאות הפרנסה": 2, "טחול": 2, "ממון של המשקעים בממון": 1, "חשך": 3, "תאות ממון": 1, "עבודה זרה": 2, "צער כפלים כיולדה": 1, "אמא דערב רב": 2, "אלהים אחרים": 2, "מיתה": 1, "שקר": 1, "שחוק הכסיל": 2, "פנים דסטרא אחרא": 1, "אל אחר": 1, "פרדה": 1, "עכו\"ם": 1, "פרנסה שהוא בעצבון": 2, "מוצא אני מר ממות את האשה": 1, "מרירותא דעלמא שצריך להמתיקה על ידי מלח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פרדה", "עכו\"ם"], "reason": "Raw component of 2 nodes. פרדה (mule) = עכו\"ם in the Talmudic riddle of the wise men of Athens: the mule that cannot give birth represents the gentile/idolater who has no connection to the source of blessing. This is the same essential nature as the sterility and separation that defines the evil cluster — שאריbחיבורא וסיים בפרודא (begins in connection, ends in separation)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פרנסה שהוא בעצבון", "מוצא אני מר ממות את האשה", "מרירותא דעלמא שצריך להמתיקה על ידי מלח"], "reason": "Raw component of 3 nodes. Livelihood-in-sadness is 'bitter as death' — its essential nature is the same as מיתה, חשך, עצבות. The מרירותא דעלמא (world-bitterness) that needs salt to sweeten it is identical to the existential condition of the dark-face cluster. These nodes share the essential nature of bitter, death-tinged existence."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "בְּרִית וּמֶלַח", "label_en": "Covenant and Salt", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the covenant as the sweetener/preserver of existence. ברית, מלח, and שדי are three faces of the same metaphysical reality: the covenant (ברית) is the salt (מלח) that sweetens the bitterness of livelihood — 'ברית מלח עולם הוא'. שדי is identified as ברית ('אני אל שדי פרה ורבה'). The second sub-cluster — ברית מלח, צדיק, טוב — shares the same nature: צדיק is identified as the keeper of the covenant ('ברית מלח'), and טוב is a name for the צדיק ('אמרו צדיק כי טוב'). Both sub-clusters are expressions of the covenant-dimension of holiness. Merged because both orbit the covenant-salt domain with identical polarity and essential nature, even though not directly connected by a bechina edge (ברית vs. ברית מלח are distinct nodes).", "dominant_node": "ברית", "nodes": ["ברית", "שדי", "מלח", "ברית מלח", "צדיק", "טוב"], "node_degrees": {"ברית": 2, "שדי": 1, "מלח": 1, "ברית מלח": 1, "צדיק": 2, "טוב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ברית", "שדי", "מלח", "ברית מלח", "צדיק", "טוב"], "reason": "Two raw components of 3 nodes each, merged into one. (A) ברית/שדי/מלח: all are bechina aspects of the covenant-as-preserver. (B) ברית מלח/צדיק/טוב: all are bechina aspects of the righteous one who embodies the eternal covenant. Both clusters share the essential nature of the ברית dimension — the covenant's power to sweeten bitterness and preserve holiness. Merged because shared nature (not cause-effect) is identical, and keeping them separate would fragment the single covenant-domain of the Torah."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "דַּלְתֵי בִטְנִי וְאוֹת מֵ\"ם", "label_en": "The Gates of the Womb and the Letter Mem", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the closed/sealed vessel that contains potential abundance before birth. דלתי בטני (the two gates of the womb) forms the shape of מ\"ם סתומה (the closed letter mem) — they are structurally identical. שליא (placenta/afterbirth) is the same thing as דלתי בטני. מ\"ם יום של יצירת הולד (the forty days of fetal formation) derives from the same mem-shape. All four are aspects of the same cosmological structure: the sealed container from which blessing (שפע) is born. Neutral because these are structural/anatomical concepts that serve as vessels for either holy or evil dimensions of livelihood.", "dominant_node": "דלתי בטני", "nodes": ["דלתי בטני", "אות מ\"ם סתומה", "שליא", "מ\"ם יום של יצירת הולד"], "node_degrees": {"דלתי בטני": 3, "אות מ\"ם סתומה": 1, "שליא": 1, "מ\"ם יום של יצירת הולד": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "אֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Faith and Faithfulness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of אמונה as the foundation of all divine attributes — the base-state of loyalty to God that expresses itself especially through honest commerce. אמונה IS אחרית הימים (the end of days, the ultimate reality that faith establishes). תקוה (hope) is a bechina of אמונה ('ויש תקוה לאחריתך'). The person who loves a mitzvah so much they feel no financial loss is called a person of אמונה. צדק (justice) is the incomplete version — doing mitzvot without cost — merged here because it shares the same essential nature as אמונה: the orientation of a person's mitzvot practice relative to money. Together these nodes define the continuum of faith-in-practice from incomplete (צדק) to complete (אמונה = אחרית הימים).", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "אחרית הימים", "כשחביב כל כך המצוה, עד שאין מרגיש בהפסד ממון", "תקוה", "כל המצוות שאדם עושה אותם בלא ממון, הינו שאין רוצה להפסיד ממון בשביל המצוה", "צדק"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 3, "אחרית הימים": 1, "כשחביב כל כך המצוה, עד שאין מרגיש בהפסד ממון": 1, "תקוה": 1, "כל המצוות שאדם עושה אותם בלא ממון, הינו שאין רוצה להפסיד ממון בשביל המצוה": 1, "צדק": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כל המצוות שאדם עושה אותם בלא ממון, הינו שאין רוצה להפסיד ממון בשביל המצוה", "צדק"], "reason": "Raw component of 2 nodes. Doing mitzvot without willingness to spend money = צדק. The Torah explicitly frames this as the lower level of אמונה — the same domain of faith-vs-money. Shared nature with אמונה package: both are about the quality of one's religious practice relative to financial sacrifice. Not cause-effect (צדק does not produce אמונה in this bechina context — they are different degrees of the same essential orientation)."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 23, "label": "מָמוֹן וְאִשָּׁה", "label_en": "Money and Woman / Livelihood as Partner", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of ממון (money/livelihood) as a structural/relational entity — neither purely holy nor purely evil, but the domain of human economic existence as a metaphysical fact. ממון IS אשה (woman/partner) in the Talmudic saying 'היורד לפרנסת חברו כאילו בא על אשתו'. ממון IS עמידה (standing/sustenance — 'ממון שמעמידו על רגליו'). עפר (earth/dust) is a bechina of ממון ('ועפרות זהב לו'). פנים (faces/presence) is a bechina of ממון ('הממון והעשירות הוא בחינת פנים'). לדה (birth/relief) and רוח (relief/space) are both names for the moment parnassah arrives — the birthing of livelihood. These are the structural 'face' of money as a reality in human life, before it is oriented toward holiness or evil.", "dominant_node": "ממון", "nodes": ["ממון", "אשה", "עמידה", "עפר", "פנים", "לדה", "רוח"], "node_degrees": {"ממון": 4, "אשה": 1, "עמידה": 1, "עפר": 1, "פנים": 1, "לדה": 1, "רוח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["לדה", "רוח"], "reason": "Raw component of 2 nodes. לדה (birth) and רוח (relief/space) are both names for the same moment — when parnassah arrives, it is called a 'birth' (וילדה זה בחינת רוח המכונה בשם לדה). Both express the same essential nature as ממון: the moment of livelihood's arrival as a structural event. Merged into Package 6 because the root metaphor is ממון-as-birth/emergence."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 24, "label": "כֶּתֶר, הַמֹּחִין וּבִרְכַּת הַיָּדַיִם", "label_en": "Crown, Intellect, and the Hands of Blessing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share a single essential nature: they are aspects of the divine Crown (כתר) as the stabilizing, mediating boundary of the intellect, and of the mochin/hands through which divine blessing flows. The Torah in section ח explicitly defines כתר as 'the power within the intellect that settles and orders the mind so it does not break through its boundary' — two dataset nodes (המסדר והמישב את השכל and הכח שיש בהשכל...) are both bechina-equated with כתר and are therefore the same essential thing. ממקומו (from Ezekiel 3:12 'ברוך כבוד ה' ממקומו') is the bechina of כתר — it is the place of the Crown. The very same verse supplies the other two pairs: ברוך=ידים (the hands that distribute blessing downward) and כבוד ה'=אמונה (divine glory = faith). These three pairs are one structural triad in this Torah — the hands carry the blessing of faith to the place of the Crown. קדש=מחין ('קֹדֶשׁ זֶה בְּחִינַת מֹחִין') equates holiness with the intellect/brain — the mochin that race toward Ein Sof and strike Keter to create the heikhalot. All of these are facets of the same upper-intellect/Crown domain: the boundary, the blessing-channels, the faith that fills them, and the holiness that IS the intellect.", "dominant_node": "כתר", "nodes": ["כתר", "המסדר והמישב את השכל", "הכח שיש בהשכל של אדם לישב ולסדר את המח והדעת לבל יהרס לצאת חוץ מן הגבול", "ממקומו", "אמונה", "כבוד ה'", "ברוך", "ידים", "קדש", "מחין"], "node_degrees": {"כתר": 3, "המסדר והמישב את השכל": 1, "הכח שיש בהשכל של אדם לישב ולסדר את המח והדעת לבל יהרס לצאת חוץ מן הגבול": 1, "ממקומו": 1, "אמונה": 1, "כבוד ה'": 1, "ברוך": 1, "ידים": 1, "קדש": 1, "מחין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כבוד ה'", "אמונה"], "reason": "CC-A (כבוד ה'=אמונה) merged into Package 1 because the Torah in section ז parses Ezekiel 3:12 as a three-part identity: 'ברוך זה בחינת ידים; כבוד ה' זה בחינת אמונה; ממקומו זה בחינת כתר'. Since ממקומו=כתר is the dominant node of Component C, and the verse treats all three pairs as a single structural statement, כבוד ה' and אמונה share the essential nature of the כתר-domain in this Torah. Emunah is also explicitly described as 'אמון מופלא' (hidden faithfulness) within Keter in section ז — faith IS the hidden quality of the Crown."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ברוך", "ידים"], "reason": "CC-B (ברוך=ידים) merged into Package 1 because the same Ezekiel verse ('ברוך זה בחינת ידים') places ברוך/ידים as the first element of the three-part triad culminating in כתר. The hands in this Torah are the vessels through which the sekhel-blessing flows downward (section ה: 'עיקר הברכה שנשפעין מהידים הם שכל'). The hands belong to the intellectual/crown domain — they are the expression of the mochin's blessing in the world. Shared nature: the hands = the downward-flowing intellect-blessing = the same domain as mochin/keter."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קדש", "מחין"], "reason": "CC-F (קדש=מחין) merged into Package 1 because the Torah in section ו states 'קֹדֶשׁ זֶה בְּחִינַת מֹחִין' — holiness IS the intellect/brain. The mochin in this Torah are the central agents: they rush toward Ein Sof, strike Keter (the partition), and create the nine heikhalot. Mochin and holiness are the same essential reality — the intellect in its holy active state — and belong with the כתר cluster that the mochin crash against."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 24, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שִׂמְחָה וְרִקּוּדִין וְתִקּוּן הַחוֹלֶה", "label_en": "Joy, Dancing, and the Healing of Illness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes in this package share the same essential reality: that joy and dancing ARE healing — they are not the instrument of healing but healing itself. The Rebbe states explicitly (1) 'הַשִּׂמְחָה הִיא בְּחִינַת תִּקּוּן הַחוֹלֶה' (simcha IS tikkun hacholeh) and (2) 'וּבִשְׁבִיל זֶה נִקְרָאִין הַשִּׂמְחָה וְהָרִקּוּדִין חוֹלָה' (simcha and dancing ARE CALLED choleh). The word 'choleh' is deliberately polysemous: it means both 'sick person' and 'circle dance' (mahol / rosh holeh). Joy is the healing of illness (tikkun hacholeh); dancing is itself the circle (choleh); and all of these are facets of one essential domain: simcha as the curative reality. The two raw components {שמחה והרקודין, חולה} and {שמחה, תקון החולה} are merged because 'שמחה' and 'שמחה והרקודין' share the same essential nature (dancing is the embodied expression of simcha, not its effect), and 'חולה' and 'תקון החולה' are the same domain — the pivot word 'choleh' that means both sickness and dance. The Rebbe's wordplay makes these four expressions of one unified truth.", "dominant_node": "שמחה", "nodes": ["שמחה", "תקון החולה", "שמחה והרקודין", "חולה"], "node_degrees": {"שמחה": 1, "תקון החולה": 1, "שמחה והרקודין": 1, "חולה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שמחה והרקודין", "חולה"], "reason": "Raw component A was merged into the main package rather than kept separate. 'שמחה והרקודין' is not a distinct domain from 'שמחה' — dancing is the embodied form of simcha, sharing the same essential nature (bechina), not a causal product of it. Likewise 'חולה' (the sick person / circle dance) belongs to the same domain as 'תקון החולה' (healing of illness) because the Rebbe's point is precisely that these are the same word — choleh = sick / choleh = circle dance. The merge is grounded in shared nature, not cause-and-effect."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 24, "label": "שְׁכִינָה, לֵב וְשִׂמְחַת יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "Shekhinah, Heart, and the Joy of Israel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three nodes share a single essential nature: they are the same divine presence experienced from Israel's side. The Torah in section ב states 'גָּלוּת הַשְּׁכִינָה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת לֵב שֶׁהוּא שִׂמְחָתָן שֶׁל יִשְׂרָאֵל' — a single clause that equates Shekhinah, heart, and Israel's joy. The Shekhinah IS the heart of the world; the heart IS where Israel's joy resides; and Israel's joy IS the Shekhinah's presence. These three are not cause and effect — they are three names for the same experiential-theological reality: the immanent divine presence that dwells within Israel as the joy of the heart. When sadness (the klipot, Package 3) overpowers the heart/Shekhinah, Israel goes into exile; when joy returns, the Shekhinah is liberated.", "dominant_node": "שכינה", "nodes": ["שכינה", "לב", "שמחתן של ישראל"], "node_degrees": {"שכינה": 2, "לב": 1, "שמחתן של ישראל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 24, "label": "קְלִפּוֹת, מוֹתָרוֹת, עַצְבוּת וְתָקְפָּא דְּדִינָא", "label_en": "Klipot, Excess-Waste, Sadness, and the Power of Harsh Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All four nodes share a single essential nature: they are aspects and names of the impure husks (קלipot) in their essential character as excess, sadness, and harsh judgment. The Torah in section ב states this in a single chain: 'הַקְּלִפּוֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת מוֹתָרוֹת, וְהֵם בְּחִינַת עַצְבוּת... וְהֵם תָּקְפָּא דְּדִינָא'. Klipot = excess-waste (the material that was not absorbed, the dregs); excess-waste = sadness (for wherever there is sorrow there is excess — Proverbs 14:23); sadness = the power of harsh judgment (the 'וַיִּתְעַצֵּב אֶל לִבּוֹ' of Genesis 6 being the prototype of divine grief/judgment). These four are not cause-and-effect — they are four simultaneous identities of the same dark reality. They form the evil counterpart to Package 2: where the Shekhinah is Israel's joy, the klipot are the force of sadness that exiles the Shekhinah from the heart.", "dominant_node": "מותרות", "nodes": ["קלפות", "מותרות", "עצבות", "תקפא דדינא"], "node_degrees": {"מותרות": 2, "קלפות": 1, "עצבות": 2, "תקפא דדינא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 25, "label": "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה וּשְׁרִירוּת הַלֵּב", "label_en": "The Imagination Faculty and Stubbornness of Heart", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All four nodes are expressions of the same essential evil faculty: the imagination (כח המדמה) that operates in the animal-soul (בהמה), the state of being drawn after it (נמשך אחר המדמה), and its manifestation as the stubbornness of the heart (שרירות לב). These are not cause-and-effect but different names and faces of the same inner evil force that opposes the intellect.", "dominant_node": "כח המדמה", "nodes": ["כח המדמה", "בהמה", "נמשך אחר המדמה", "שרירות לב"], "node_degrees": {"כח המדמה": 1, "בהמה": 1, "נמשך אחר המדמה": 1, "שרירות לב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בהמה", "כח המדמה"], "reason": "Raw component 3 (edge ID 1483): the animal (בהמה) is an explicit bechina of the imagination faculty (כח המדמה) — both are expressions of the same animalistic imaginative nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נמשך אחר המדמה", "שרירות לב"], "reason": "Raw component 7 (edge ID 3045): being drawn after the imagination is a bechina of stubbornness of heart — same essential state of the person captured by imagination."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כח המדמה / בהמה cluster", "נמשך אחר המדמה / שרירות לב cluster"], "reason": "The two 2-node components share the same essential evil domain: both are about the imagination faculty (כח המדמה) — one names the faculty and its animal-parallel, the other names the human experiential state of captivity to it. Merging by shared nature (not cause-and-effect)."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 25, "label": "שָׁמִיר וּשְׁבִירַת לֵב הָאֶבֶן", "label_en": "The Shamir and the Breaking of the Stone Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes form the bechina of the positive act of leaving the imagination domain: 'when one exits from stubbornness of the heart and breaks one's stone heart — this is a bechina of the shamir.' The shamir (שמיר) is the essential nature of the act of heart-breaking that defeats the stone-heart. This is a distinct good domain — the capacity and act of self-liberation from imagination — and does not share essential nature with the larger neutral (inner/outer) or good (speech/colors) packages.", "dominant_node": "שמיר", "nodes": ["כשיוצא משרירות הלב, ומשבר לבו האבן", "שמיר"], "node_degrees": {"כשיוצא משרירות הלב, ומשבר לבו האבן": 1, "שמיר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 25, "label": "פְּנִימִיּוּת וְחִיצוֹנִיּוּת — כִּסֵּא וְכָבוֹד", "label_en": "Inner and Outer Dimensions — Throne and Glory", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All five nodes are expressions of the inner/outer (pnimiyut/chitzoniyut) structural distinction that permeates every spiritual level. Kise (throne/כסא) is a bechina of pnimiyut (inner); kavod (glory/כבוד) is a bechina of both chitzoniyut (outer) and levush (garment). These are not polar opposites but two structural dimensions of the same spiritual reality — a neutral ontological framework that applies across both good and evil contexts. The merge of the kise/pnimiyut pair and the kavod/chitzoniyut/levush cluster is justified by the shared domain of the inner-outer structural axis.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["כסא", "פנימיות", "כבוד", "חיצוניות", "לבוש"], "node_degrees": {"כסא": 1, "פנימיות": 1, "כבוד": 2, "חיצוניות": 1, "לבוש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כסא", "פנימיות"], "reason": "Raw component 5 (edge ID 2502): כסא is a bechina of פנימיות (from the Zohar: 'itkas'ya' — hidden/inner). This pair belongs to the inner dimension of the spiritual structure."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כבוד", "חיצוניות", "לבוש"], "reason": "Raw component 2 (edges ID 1138 and 1558): כבוד is a bechina of both חיצוניות (outer) and לבוש (garment). This cluster is the outer/garment dimension of spiritual structure."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כסא/פנימיות cluster", "כבוד/חיצוניות/לבוש cluster"], "reason": "Both 2-node and 3-node components express the same structural axis of pnimiyut/chitzoniyut that is the Torah's central framework in sections ג–ד. Kise and kavod are the two poles of the same inner/outer reality discussed in the single verse 'כסא כבוד מרום מראשון.' Their shared nature is the structural topology of every spiritual level."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 25, "label": "דִּבּוּר וּמוֹצִיא מִכֹּחַ אֶל הַפֹּעַל", "label_en": "Speech and Bringing from Potential to Actuality", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three nodes share the essential nature of actualization through speech: שפתותיך (the lips) is a bechina of מוציא מכח אל הפועל (bringing from potential to actuality), which is itself a bechina of דבור (speech). All three are faces of the same phenomenon — the actualization of inner intellect into expressed form via speech. This is aligned with holiness: it is the Nefet Tzufim dimension of Torah #25, the bringing forth of the sweetness of wisdom.", "dominant_node": "מוציא מכח אל הפעל", "nodes": ["מוציא מכח אל הפעל", "דבור", "שפתותיך"], "node_degrees": {"מוציא מכח אל הפעל": 2, "דבור": 1, "שפתותיך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 25, "label": "גְּוָנִין עִלָּאִין וְכֶסֶף וָזָהָב", "label_en": "Supernal Colors and Silver and Gold", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes are a direct bechina pair: the supernal Divine colors (גונין עלאין) are a bechina of silver and gold (כסף וזהב), since the supernal colors are embodied in physical precious metals. This is the Domain of Divine beauty/glory/splendor (פאר וחן) that is revealed when money is in the hands of Israel and given as charity. This domain is distinct from both the structural inner/outer package (Package 3) and the speech/actualization package (Package 4).", "dominant_node": "גונין עלאין", "nodes": ["גונין עלאין", "כסף וזהב"], "node_degrees": {"גונין עלאין": 1, "כסף וזהב": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 26, "label": "אֶפְרוֹחַ וְהַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "The Fledgling and the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The two nodes share a single essential nature: the chick (אפרוח) and the tzaddik are two names for the same structural reality. The teaching opens by decoding the Aramaic riddle: 'רְצִיצָא, זֶה אֶפְרוֹחַ. זֶה בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק, בְּחִינַת (תהילים ע\"ב:ז): יִפְרַח בְּיָמָיו צַדִּיק.' The chick is the thing that is pressed/enclosed within the egg yet emerges through its spirit — and the tzaddik is precisely the one who is pressed and enclosed (in prayer, in the confrontation with alien thoughts) yet whose spirit bursts forth. The verse 'יִפְרַח בְּיָמָיו צַדִּיק' uses the root פרח (blossom/emerge/fly) — the same word as אפרוח (the one who flies/springs). The tzaddik does not merely resemble the chick; the chick IS the tzaddik: both are beings whose life-force (spirit/soul) departs from and must be surrendered at the exact point where it was originally enclosed (the egg / the moment of alien thought's entry). This is a good-polarity package: the tzaddik and his essential nature as one who blooms through self-sacrifice is a wholly positive domain.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["אפרוח", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"אפרוח": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 26, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דַּעַת וַחֲסָדִים — מֹשֶׁה וְאַבְרָהָם", "label_en": "Da'at and Chassadim — Moshe and Avraham", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three bechina pairs in this Torah describe a single unified reality from three angles: (1) Moshe IS Da'at — the leader-personality and the sefira are the same essential thing; (2) Avraham IS the 248 limbs — Avraham (gematria 248) ish haChesed IS the 248 limbs of the body, i.e., the structure of Chesed embodied in physical form; (3) the clothing of Moshe in the limbs IS the very same event as the clothing of Da'at in Chassadim — the Rebbe states 'זֶהוּ בְּעַצְמוֹ' (this itself is). Together, these three pairs define one thematic family: the interpenetration of Da'at/consciousness and Chesed/compassion as the animating principle of the holy human being. Moshe/Da'at is clothed in Avraham/248 limbs/Chassadim — three name-pairs for one structural reality.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["משה", "דעת", "אברהם", "רמ\"ח איברים", "התלבשות משה באיברים", "התלבשות הדעת בחסדים"], "node_degrees": {"משה": 1, "דעת": 1, "אברהם": 1, "רמ\"ח איברים": 1, "התלבשות משה באיברים": 1, "התלבשות הדעת בחסדים": 1}, "mirror_package_id": null, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אברהם", "רמ\"ח איברים", "משה", "דעת", "התלבשות משה באיברים", "התלבשות הדעת בחסדים"], "reason": "The three raw connected components are distinct pairs, but the Rebbe explicitly bridges them in the proof text: 'כִּי מֹשֶׁה הוּא הַדַּעַת, וְהָאֵיבָרִים הֵם רְמַ\"ח, בְּחִינַת אַבְרָהָם אִישׁ הַחֶסֶד'. This sentence links all three pairs into one chain: Moshe=Da'at (Edge 2) → the limbs=248=Avraham/Chesed (Edge 1) → and the clothing of Moshe in the limbs = the clothing of Da'at in Chassadim (Edge 3). All three pairs are expressions of the same essential reality: Da'at clothed in Chassadim. Since they share the same essential nature and are all oriented toward holiness (good domain), they form one package. No eitza relationship separates them — they are parallel bechina descriptions of one structure."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 27, "label": "הַדְרַת פָּנִים וְהַקּוֹל", "label_en": "Radiant Countenance and Voice", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five nodes are aspects of the same underlying spiritual reality: the outward radiance of refined wisdom. The elder/sage (זקן = קנה חכמה) is the human archetype of this quality; הדרת פנים is its visual expression (luminous countenance); דרושי התורה is its intellectual expression (Torah expounded via the 13 hermeneutical principles drawn from the 13 tikunei dikna); קול is its acoustic expression (the refined voice); and הון (vocal wealth/throat quality) is the vehicle of קול. The Song of Songs verse 'הראיני את מראיך — השמיעיני את קולך' explicitly equates הדרת פנים and קול as two simultaneous manifestations of the same spiritual presence, confirming their shared essential nature. The text states: 'זקן — זה קנה חכמה, הקנה מוציא קול' — all three (זקן, קנה-as-wisdom, קול) are the same reality expressed through different registers (person, image, sound).", "dominant_node": "הדרת פנים", "nodes": ["הדרת פנים", "דרושי התורה", "זקן", "קול", "הון"], "node_degrees": {"הדרת פנים": 2, "דרושי התורה": 1, "זקן": 1, "קול": 1, "הון": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הון", "קול"], "reason": "Component {הון, קול} has only 2 nodes and was merged into the dominant component {הדרת פנים, דרושי התורה, זקן}. The merge is justified by SHARED NATURE, not eitza: all five nodes are expressions of the same quality of refined, wisdom-permeated spiritual presence. Specifically, קול is the acoustic dimension of the same outward radiance that הדרת פנים is the visual dimension of — confirmed by Song of Songs 2:14 where both are invoked as two faces of one divine encounter ('הראיני את מראיך / השמיעיני את קולך'). The text further equates: 'לפי ההזדכות חכמתו... כן הזדכות קול רנתו' — the refinement of wisdom-face and the refinement of voice are parallel (bechina) phenomena, not one producing the other. הון connects to קול by explicit bechina ('הון זה בחינת קול'), and קול connects to the הדרת פנים cluster via זקן's dual role as wisdom-holder and voice-producer."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 28, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וְחֶסֶד וְתוֹלַעַת יַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "Faith, Lovingkindness, and the Worm of Jacob", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All six nodes share one essential nature: they are all expressions of the foundational holy force that defeats heresy and shame. The teaching explicitly states that תולע (the crimson worm) IS אמונה (Lamentations 4:5 — 'הָאֱמֻנִים עֲלֵי תּוֹלָע'); that אמונה IS אברהם ('וְהֶאֱמִין בַּה''); that אברהם IS חסד; and that חסד IS אמונה. These four form one cluster (CC-A). The two additional nodes — עולת תמיד and תולעת יעקב — are the ritual/prophetic names for the same reality: the post-text commentary states explicitly 'תּוֹלָע דִּקְדֻשָּׁה, בְּחִינַת עוֹלַת תָּמִד, הוּא בְּחִינַת חֶסֶד, בְּחִינַת אַבְרָהָם.' The worm of Jacob (Isaiah 41:14 'אַל תִּירְאִי תּוֹלַעַת יַעֲקֹב') is the same worm as the תולע of CC-A. The daily burnt offering IS this holy worm. All six nodes are thus two faces of one reality: the holy crimson-faith-lovingkindness cluster, expressed through person (Abraham), attribute (chesed), spiritual quality (emunah), symbol (worm/crimson), prophetic image (Worm of Jacob), and ritual act (daily burnt offering).", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "אברהם", "חסד", "תולע", "עולת תמיד", "תולעת יעקב"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 3, "אברהם": 2, "חסד": 2, "תולע": 1, "עולת תמיד": 1, "תולעת יעקב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עולת תמיד", "תולעת יעקב"], "reason": "CC-D ({עולת תמיד, תולעת יעקב}) merges into CC-A because these nodes share essential nature with the core cluster. The post-text commentary of Torah #28 (lines 29-35) states explicitly: 'תּוֹלָע דִּקְדֻשָּׁה, בְּחִינַת עוֹלַת תָּמִד, הוּא בְּחִינַת חֶסֶד, בְּחִינַת אַבְרָהָם.' The עולת תמיד IS the holy worm (תולע דקדושה), which IS the תולע of CC-A (already established as אמונה), which IS אברהם and חסד. The dataset has the edge 'עולת תמיד — תולעת יעקב' (bechina, ref 28) and the main text has 'תולע — אמונה' (bechina, ref 28). The reason for the gap is that the dataset did not encode the cross-component bechina (e.g., 'תולע — תולעת יעקב' or 'עולת תמיד — תולע') explicitly, though both are stated in the source text commentary. This is a SHARED NATURE merge, not cause-effect: the daily offering does not produce faith — it IS an expression of the same holy worm-nature."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 28, "label": "לֵאָה וְהָאַלְפִין בִּקְדֻשָּׁה", "label_en": "Leah and the Holy Alephs", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes share the essential nature of the supernal feminine vessel (Leah as a Kabbalistic partzuf) and its associated holy content. 'אַלְפִין בִּקְדֻשָּׁה הֵם בְּחִינַת לֵאָה' — the holy alephs ARE Leah; they are the thousand-fold wisdom that descended from Leah's domain in holiness, made holy through Solomon's verse ('וַיְדַבֵּר שְׁלֹמֹה שְׁלֹשֶׁת אֲלָפִים מָשָׁל'). 'כָּל קָרְבָּן עוֹלָה, הִיא בִּבְחִינַת לֵאָה' — every burnt offering IS Leah. The עולה (ascending offering) is structurally identified with Leah because it rises (עולה = ascends) as Leah's harsh judgments must be sweetened and elevated. All three nodes are aspects of the same structural/Kabbalistic domain: the Leah-vessel that contains and generates the holy alephs and whose rectification is the burnt offering.", "dominant_node": "לאה", "nodes": ["לאה", "אלפין בקדשה", "קרבן עולה"], "node_degrees": {"לאה": 2, "אלפין בקדשה": 1, "קרבן עולה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 28, "label": "כְּפִירוּת וַעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה", "label_en": "Heresy and Idolatry", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The two nodes share the essential nature of spiritual falsehood and the sitra achra's claim on human consciousness. 'כְּפִירוּת בְּחִינַת עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה' — heresy IS idolatry. They are not cause-and-effect (heresy does not produce idolatry as a sequence); rather, they are two names for the same spiritual reality: the rejection of God's presence and the substitution of alien authority. The teaching explicitly equates them as two aspects of the same thing (the word 'בְּחִינַת' used). This is the evil mirror of Package 1's faith cluster: where Package 1 is faith/chesed/Abraham (holy ground), Package 3 is heresy/idolatry (sitra achra ground).", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה", "nodes": ["כפירות", "עבודה זרה"], "node_degrees": {"כפירות": 1, "עבודה זרה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "תִּקּוּן הַכְּלָלִי וְהַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "The General Rectification and the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of the redemptive/rectifying principle: the Tzaddik (Rashbi), the rainbow of the covenant, the eagle that awakens the mochin, the whitening radiance — all are faces of the single rectifying force that heals the sinews and purifies the blood. They are not linked by cause-and-effect but are all expressions of the same transcendent corrective reality.", "dominant_node": "רוח", "nodes": ["תקון הברית", "\"דרך הנשר בשמים\"", "שמים", "\"זהר הרקיע\"", "\"לשון של זהורית\"", "\"יושב אהלים\"", "תקון הכללי", "צדקה", "כלליות הגידין", "רוח", "צדיק", "קשת הברית", "נשר"], "node_degrees": {"תקון הברית": 2, "\"דרך הנשר בשמים\"": 3, "שמים": 2, "\"זהר הרקיע\"": 2, "\"לשון של זהורית\"": 1, "\"יושב אהלים\"": 1, "תקון הכללי": 2, "צדקה": 2, "כלליות הגידין": 2, "רוח": 3, "צדיק": 2, "קשת הברית": 1, "נשר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 29, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִחוּדִים וְזִוּוּגִים וּבִטּוּל אִסּוּר", "label_en": "Divine Unions, Matchings, and the Nullification of Prohibition", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes one foundational bechina in this Torah: all yichudim (divine unifications of upper and lower worlds) and all zivugim (marital matchings) are in the nature of biṭṭul isur — the transformation of the forbidden into the permitted. These are not cause and effect; they are two faces of the same spiritual event. A yichud/zivug IS biṭṭul isur — the separation that made something forbidden is dissolved when the two become one. The Rebbe repeats this equation three times (paragraphs 1, 2, 4), confirming it as the central structural claim. Both concepts belong to the same positive, holy domain: the restoration of wholeness, marriage, and unity.", "dominant_node": "יחודים והזווגים", "nodes": ["יחודים והזווגים", "בטול אסור"], "node_degrees": {"יחודים והזווגים": 1, "בטול אסור": 1}, "mirror_package_id": null, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "הַמֹּחִין וְכֵלֵיהֶם", "label_en": "The Brain and Its Vessels", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of the mochin (the higher mind/brain-consciousness) and its structural extensions: the head, the seven locks of hair (which are the overflow of the mochin), the seven layers of the eye (which are the same as the seven locks), vision, the seven emotional attributes that pass through the mochin, and the diminished state of the mochin (katnut/sleep). They are all facets of the same psycho-spiritual structure — the cognitive-spiritual center of the person — that can be awakened or diminished.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["מחין", "ראש", "\"שבע מחלפות ראשו\"", "שבע מדות", "\"שבע גלדי עינא\"", "ראיה", "מחין דקטנות", "שנה", "עינים", "שערות"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 2, "ראש": 1, "\"שבע מחלפות ראשו\"": 3, "שבע מדות": 1, "\"שבע גלדי עינא\"": 2, "ראיה": 1, "מחין דקטנות": 1, "שנה": 1, "עינים": 1, "שערות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מחין דקטנות", "שנה"], "reason": "Mochin dkatnut and sleep are the diminished state of the mochin — sharing the same essential nature as the mochin cluster. The text explicitly says the eagle/tzaddik awakens the mochin 'from their dormancy, from the aspect of mochin dkatnut, from the aspect of sleep.' They are the mochin in their lowered state, not a separate domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עינים", "שערות"], "reason": "The text explicitly equates eyes and hair via the mochin: 'the seven layers of the eye are the aspect of the seven locks of his head, and they are the aspect of the mochin, for the hairs are the overflow of the mochin.' Eyes and hair are structural expressions of the same mochin-reality. Their 2-node component shares essential nature with the mochin cluster."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "מַלְכוּת פֶּה — הַבְּגָדִים וְהַדִּבּוּר", "label_en": "Malkhut of the Mouth — Garments and Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of malkhut-as-expression: the garments are malkhut (as Rabbi Yochanan called them 'his honors'), kavod is malkhut (Melekh HaKavod), yirat Elohim is malkhut ('if not for the fear of the kingdom'), white garments = tikun of speech = malkhut of the mouth, Torah words (pegs and nails) are the instrument of that holy speech. They all inhabit the domain of the holy Shekhinah/malkhut as she expresses herself through honor, dress, and holy utterance.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["כבוד", "בגדים", "מלכות", "יראת אלקים", "בגדים לבנים", "דבור", "יתדות ומסמרות", "דברי תורה"], "node_degrees": {"כבוד": 1, "בגדים": 2, "מלכות": 2, "יראת אלקים": 1, "בגדים לבנים": 1, "דבור": 1, "יתדות ומסמרות": 1, "דברי תורה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בגדים לבנים", "דבור"], "reason": "The text states explicitly: 'white garments = tikun of speech, which is the aspect of malkhut of the mouth.' White garments and tikun of speech are bechinas of each other and both are bechinas of malkhut. Their 2-node component merges into the malkhut cluster by shared essential nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יתדות ומסמרות", "דברי תורה"], "reason": "Torah words (as pegs and nails) are the instrument of holy speech. The passage interprets 'he drove it [the peg] downward' as Torah speech without tikun haklali being unable to penetrate, and upward as holy Torah-speech that can. Their essential nature is Torah-as-speech, belonging to the malkhut-speech domain."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "דַּם נִדָּה וּפְגַם הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "The Blood of Impurity and the Flaw of the Covenant", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of spiritual corruption/redness — the stain that separates the Shekhinah from the Holy One. Dam nidda, redness, the stain on the garments, the city of bloodshed, the flaw of the covenant, the flaw of the sinews, and the flaw of the garments are all expressions of the same corrupting force: transgression that activates blood-impurity in the Shekhinah and causes separation (firda). They are not causes of each other — they are all faces of the same polluting reality.", "dominant_node": "דם נדה", "nodes": ["דם נדה", "אדמימות", "הכתם והרבב שנמצא על בגדו", "עיר הדמים", "\"מדוע אדם ללבושך\"", "\"נמצא רבב על בגדיו\"", "פגם הברית", "פגם כלליות הגידין", "פגם הבגדים"], "node_degrees": {"דם נדה": 4, "אדמימות": 1, "הכתם והרבב שנמצא על בגדו": 1, "עיר הדמים": 2, "\"מדוע אדם ללבושך\"": 3, "\"נמצא רבב על בגדיו\"": 2, "פגם הברית": 2, "פגם כלליות הגידין": 2, "פגם הבגדים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "שִׁפְחָה בִּישָׁא — הַעֵת רָעָה", "label_en": "The Evil Maidservant — The Evil Time", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three concepts share the essential nature of the evil counter-force that seizes dominion when the Shekhinah is in her impure state: the evil maidservant (sitra achra's malkhut) who inherits her mistress, the evil woman (opposite of the Woman of Valor), and the evil time (of the 28 times, those times that belong to the evil side). All three are the same malkhut-deSitra-Achra — the evil Shekhinah — viewed from different angles.", "dominant_node": "עת רעה", "nodes": ["אשה רעה", "שפחה בישא", "עת רעה"], "node_degrees": {"אשה רעה": 1, "שפחה בישא": 1, "עת רעה": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 29, "label": "מָן — פַּרְנָסָה מִן הַשָּׁמַיִם", "label_en": "Manna — Heavenly Sustenance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts share the essential nature of divinely-given, effortless sustenance: the manna that fell from heaven, 'bread from heaven,' and parnassa without toil. They are all expressions of the same reality — divine nourishment that flows without human effort, as an expression of tikun (the whiteness of the mochin descending as sustenance). They are not a chain of cause-and-effect; they are three faces of the same concept.", "dominant_node": "מן", "nodes": ["ממון", "מן", "\"לחם מן השמים\""], "node_degrees": {"ממון": 2, "מן": 2, "\"לחם מן השמים\"": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 30, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְשֵׂכֶל תַּחְתּוֹן", "label_en": "Malkhut and the Lower Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of tzimtzum (contraction/limitation): the lower intellect concentrates the vast upper light into a graspable form, just as the dark pupil of the eye (bat ayin) focuses a mountain into its scope, or the sha'ara be'igula (circular preambles) leads step-by-step to the target point. Malkhut = dalet = chokhmah tata'ah = shekhinah = the governing wisdom of each world that channels the higher intellect downward. The festival-related nodes (four cups, four species, four Mishna repetitions) are all bechina of the letter dalet (malkhut = dalet) because they each represent the four-faced malkhut in a specific holiday context. Shacharut = the dark of the eye = sekhel tachton = the quality of Abraham's shacharit-prayer. The sekhel elyon (banim/sons with settled da'at) is the mirror-counterpart and is grouped here because the Torah's entire argument about lower/upper intellect is a single conceptual domain of cognitive structure.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "אות דל\"ת", "בסכות היא ארבעה מינים", "בפסח הם ארבע כוסות", "בשבועות הוא סדר המשנה, שהוא ארבע פעמים ביד כל אחד", "חכמה תתאה", "חכמה המנהגת את העולם", "מישרא דסכינא", "שכל התחתון", "שחרות", "שחר שבעין", "שחרית", "הקדמות", "שערא אכמא", "\"חרב נוקמת\"", "בת", "בת עין", "שערא בעגולא", "הקדמות המסבבין עד שבאים אל המכון", "בנים", "שכל העליון"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 4, "שכל התחתון": 4, "שחרות": 3, "חכמה תתאה": 3, "אות דל\"ת": 4, "בסכות היא ארבעה מינים": 1, "בפסח הם ארבע כוסות": 1, "בשבועות הוא סדר המשנה, שהוא ארבע פעמים ביד כל אחד": 1, "חכמה המנהגת את העולם": 1, "מישרא דסכינא": 1, "\"חרב נוקמת\"": 1, "שחר שבעין": 1, "שחרית": 1, "הקדמות": 1, "שערא אכמא": 1, "בת": 1, "בת עין": 1, "שערא בעגולא": 1, "הקדמות המסבבין עד שבאים אל המכון": 1, "בנים": 1, "שכל העליון": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בת", "בת עין"], "reason": "Bat/bat ayin (pupil of the eye) shares the essential nature of tzimtzum: the dark of the eye concentrates enormous objects (like a mountain) into a small visible impression. This is identical to sekhel tachton = malkhut's function of limiting/concentrating the upper sekhel so it can be grasped. Direct source support: Torah #30 lines 20-21."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שערא בעגולא", "הקדמות המסבבין עד שבאים אל המכון"], "reason": "Sha'ara be'igula (circular gate) = the roundabout preambles that circle toward the intended point. This is the teaching function of sekhel tachton: the teacher provides smaller preliminary ideas (hekdamot) that circle around the great sekhel elyon to make it accessible. Shares nature with hekdamot = sekhel tachton already in CC-1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["בנים", "שכל העליון"], "reason": "Banim (sons with settled da'at) = sekhel elyon. The Torah defines sekhel elyon and sekhel tachton as a unified domain — one is the concentrating lens, the other the light being concentrated. They form a single cognitive structure pair. Grouped with the lower intellect package because Torah #30's argument is precisely about this pair: how lower intellect (malkhut) enables access to upper intellect."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 30, "label": "הָרְגָלִים וּבִינָה לָעִתִּים", "label_en": "The Pilgrimage Festivals and Knowing the Times", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes here share the essential nature of binah knowing/discerning the right times: the three pilgrimage festivals (regalim) ARE binah la'itim — the concentrated moments of the year where bina touches time. Chamra (donkey) = Yissachar = yodei bina la'itim — the same bina-knowing-times nature. 'Teshuvat hashanah' = shalosh regalim — the turning-point of the year through which the festivals are defined as days of judgment/teshuvah. All express the domain of divine time-consciousness that governs the lifting of malkhut to or-hapanim.", "dominant_node": "רגלים", "nodes": ["רגלים", "בינה לעתים", "חמרא", "\"יששכר חמור גרם\"", "\"תשובת השנה\"", "שלש רגלים"], "node_degrees": {"רגלים": 1, "בינה לעתים": 1, "חמרא": 1, "\"יששכר חמור גרם\"": 1, "\"תשובת השנה\"": 1, "שלש רגלים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חמרא", "\"יששכר חמור גרם\""], "reason": "Chamra = Yissachar (Yissachar chamor garem) = yodei bina la'itim. The donkey-quality of Yissachar IS the quality of knowing the times = binah la'itim = the regalim. The karna-de-chamra (horn of the donkey) is the vehicle for rebuke that lifts malkhut to the regalim. Chamra shares the essential nature of bina la'itim."}, {"merged_nodes": ["\"תשובת השנה\"", "שלש רגלים"], "reason": "Teshuvat hashanah = shalosh regalim because the festivals are the turning-point of the year (teshuvat = returning/turning), which are days of judgment and teshuvah. They are structurally identical in nature to the regalim cluster."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 30, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק הַמּוֹכִיחַ וְהַתְּפִלּוֹת", "label_en": "The Rebuking Tzaddik, the Nose, and the Prayers", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the tzaddik's discernment through the sense of smell: Mashiach = the nose that smells/senses the prayers, discerning who is worthy and who is not. Tzaddikim = Mashiach (they carry the same discerning function in each generation). The prayers (tefillot) = yirat Hashem = the spiritual 'scent' that Mashiach perceives. Karna = the rebuker's voice (qol hamokhi'ach) = qeren hashofar — the horn that amplifies the tzaddik's rebuke. Torah haqedoshah = 'pesel lekha' (solid, heavy Torah that sinks to the bottom) = the real Torah that the tzaddik teaches, as opposed to the light dross. All these are manifestations of the tzaddik's role: discerning, rebuking, and elevating.", "dominant_node": "משיח", "nodes": ["משיח", "חטם", "צדיק", "תפלות", "\"יראת ה היא תתהלל\"", "קרנא", "קול המוכיח", "קרן השופר", "תורה הקדושה", "\"פסל לך\""], "node_degrees": {"משיח": 2, "קול המוכיח": 2, "חטם": 1, "צדיק": 1, "תפלות": 1, "\"יראת ה היא תתהלל\"": 1, "קרנא": 1, "קרן השופר": 1, "תורה הקדושה": 1, "\"פסל לך\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תפלות", "\"יראת ה היא תתהלל\""], "reason": "Prayers = 'Yirat Hashem hi tit'halal' (Proverbs 31:30). The tefillot ARE yirat Hashem — prayer is the external manifestation of divine fear. This dyad is grouped with the Mashiach/nose cluster because the Mashiach discerns the prayers precisely through their quality of yirat Hashem — he smells/knows whether genuine fear is present."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קרנא", "קול המוכיח", "קרן השופר"], "reason": "Karna (horn) = qol hamokhi'ach (the rebuker's voice) = qeren hashofar. These three are the same essential reality: the amplified, penetrating voice of the tzaddik who rebukes. The karna in the Talmudic riddle IS qol hamokhi'ach. Grouped with Mashiach/tzaddik because the rebuking voice IS the tzaddik's primary mode of spiritual action in this Torah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תורה הקדושה", "\"פסל לך\""], "reason": "Holy Torah = 'Pesel lekha' (Exodus 34:1) — the solid, weighty Torah that sinks to the bottom (as the Talmud notes: 'or that is precious in this world will be heavy/sunken in the next'). This contrasts with kafui vekal/pessolet. The holy Torah's essential nature is its solid, dense quality — what the tzaddik teaches and what distinguishes real from false Torah."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 30, "label": "אַרְבָּעָה בָנִים וְאַרְבַּע מַלְכֻיּוֹת", "label_en": "The Four Sons and the Four Kingdoms of Holiness", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All nodes share the essential structure of the four-type typology: wise, wicked, simple, and one who doesn't know how to ask. This quaternary structure is the same essential nature expressed in the patriarchal sons (Yitzchak/Yishmael/Yaakov/Esav), the four Haggadah sons, and the four kingdoms of holiness. The polarity is neutral because this package contains both good archetypes (Yitzchak=wise, Yaakov=simple) and evil/neutral ones (Esav=wicked, Yishmael=doesn't-know), making it a structural taxonomy rather than a purely good or evil domain.", "dominant_node": "כנגד ארבעה בנים דברה תורה, אחד חכם ואחד רשע ואחד תם ואחד שאינו יודע לשאל", "nodes": ["כנגד ארבעה בנים דברה תורה, אחד חכם ואחד רשע ואחד תם ואחד שאינו יודע לשאל", "יצחק וישמעאל יעקב ועשו", "כנגד ארבע מלכיות שבקדשה", "יצחק", "בן חכם", "עשו", "בן רשע", "ישמעאל", "בן שאינו יודע לשאל", "יעקב", "בן תם"], "node_degrees": {"כנגד ארבעה בנים דברה תורה, אחד חכם ואחד רשע ואחד תם ואחד שאינו יודע לשאל": 2, "יצחק וישמעאל יעקב ועשו": 1, "כנגד ארבע מלכיות שבקדשה": 1, "יצחק": 1, "בן חכם": 1, "עשו": 1, "בן רשע": 1, "ישמעאל": 1, "בן שאינו יודע לשאל": 1, "יעקב": 1, "בן תם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יצחק", "בן חכם"], "reason": "Yitzchak = ben chakham (the wise son). The name Yitzchak relates to tzachok/simcha, hence 'ben chakham yesmach av' (Proverbs 10:1). Yitzchak IS the wise son archetype. Merged into four-sons package as one of the four."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עשו", "בן רשע"], "reason": "Esav = the wicked son. Explicitly stated in the Torah text. Merged as one of the four archetypes."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ישמעאל", "בן שאינו יודע לשאל"], "reason": "Yishmael = the son who doesn't know to ask. The proof text explains that Yishmael did teshuvah, and his teshuvah category is that of one who doesn't know how to ask for forgiveness for unknown sins. Merged as one of the four archetypes."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יעקב", "בן תם"], "reason": "Yaakov = ben tam (wholesome/simple son), as written: 'veYaakov ish tam' (Genesis 25:27). Merged as one of the four archetypes."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 30, "label": "תּוֹרוֹת אֲחֵרוֹת וּפְסִילִים", "label_en": "Foreign Toroth, Idols, and Dross", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of spiritual falseness/dross: foreign toroth (the teachings of the sitra achra) ARE pesilim (idols), because they claim to be Torah but are hollow simulacra. Kafui vekal (floated/light matter) = pessolet (dross/waste) — this is the physical description of what false Torah is made of: it is light, it floats to the surface, it has no substance. This contrasts with the holy Torah which is heavy/solid (kafui = precious = heavy in future world). The evil audacity (azut de-sitra achra) from which foreign toroth emerge is their shared root.", "dominant_node": "תורות אחרות", "nodes": ["תורות אחרות", "פסילים", "קפוי וקל", "פסלת"], "node_degrees": {"תורות אחרות": 1, "פסילים": 1, "קפוי וקל": 1, "פסלת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קפוי וקל", "פסלת"], "reason": "Kafui vekal (light/floated) = pessolet (dross/waste). The Talmudic principle is that 'or that is precious in this world will be sunken/heavy in the next; or that is light (kafui) in this world will float to the top in the next.' The pessolet of Torah = the kafui vekal = the false/alien toroth. Merged into the evil package because the dross IS the foreign toroth' essential substance."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "צְדָקָה וְגַלְגַּלִּים", "label_en": "Charity and the Celestial Spheres", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the cosmic circulation of blessing: tzedaka IS the galgalim because both describe the same revolving, cyclical mechanism of divine providence — the phrase 'ki bigalal hadavar hazeh' (Shabbat 151b) equates tzedaka directly with the galal/circle that turns in the world. Nesher = rachamim = tzedaka (Zohar, Bava Batra): the eagle's upward soaring through the heavens IS the quality of compassion/tzedaka. Nehori de'a (Shmuel's 'the paths of the firmament are as clear to me as the streets of my city') = tzedaka-rachamim: the illuminated pathways of the sky are the same reality as compassionate giving. The cardinal directions east/west ARE the giver/receiver of tzedaka: east = the one who gives (zore'a = sows seeds of tzedaka going outward), west = the one who collects/the poor person (mekabetz = gathering inward, as 'umima'arav akabetzekha'). Galgalei harakia = mo'ed: the celestial spheres govern time and the appointed times (the verse 'darchei tzion avelot mibloi ba'ei mo'ed' equates mo'ed with the celestial spheres). All these are different faces of the same revolving, compassion-driven cosmic structure.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "גלגלים", "נהורי דעה", "רחמי", "נשר", "גלגלי הרקיע", "מועד", "מערב", "המקבץ הצדקה", "מזרח", "הנותן צדקה"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 3, "גלגלים": 1, "נהורי דעה": 1, "רחמי": 2, "נשר": 1, "גלגלי הרקיע": 1, "מועד": 1, "מערב": 1, "המקבץ הצדקה": 1, "מזרח": 1, "הנותן צדקה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גלגלי הרקיע", "מועד"], "reason": "Galgalei harakia = mo'ed: the celestial spheres ARE the appointed times because it is the spheres that govern and calculate the calendar. The verse 'darchei tzion avelot mibloi ba'ei mo'ed' — mo'ed is a bechina of the galgalei harakia, as the Rebbe states explicitly (source line 16). Since galgalim is already in CC8, this pair (galgalei harakia/mo'ed) shares the identical essential nature — they are the same domain of celestial-temporal structure — and merges into Package 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מזרח", "הנותן צדקה", "מערב", "המקבץ הצדקה"], "reason": "East = the giver of tzedaka; west = the collector of tzedaka (the poor person). Source line 11: 'mizrach, zeh bechina hanotein tzedaka... umarav, zeh hamekabetz hatzedaka.' The directional quality of east and west IS the tzedaka relationship — the outward-flowing giving energy (mizrach = zore'a latzedaka) and the inward-gathering receiving energy (marav = ukibatzekha). These two dyads are bechina of the tzedaka/galgalim domain: the galgal moves east to west and west to east, and tzedaka flows from giver (east) to receiver (west) and back. Merged into Package 1 as expressions of the same cosmic circulation."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "שַׁבָּת וֶאֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Shabbat and Faith", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Shabbat IS emunah because Shabbat testifies to two fundamental articles of faith: the renewal of the world (chidush ha'olam) and the unity of God (yichudo). These are not two separate things — Shabbat observance IS the act of emunah; they are the same essential reality expressed through time and action. The Torah (source line 8) states this with the bechina formula explicitly: 'veShabbat hi emunah, shema'amin bechidush ha'olam uveyichudo.' The emunah/Shabbat pair is the completion that tzedaka needs: without emunah (=Shabbat), the galgalim/tzedaka are missing their perfection. 'Shemesh beShabbat tzedaka la'aniyim' — the Sun of shabbat-emunah is what gives the galgalim their full illumination.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["שבת", "אמונה"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 1, "אמונה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "בְּרִית וְשֶׁמֶשׁ", "label_en": "Covenant and the Sun", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Shemesh (the sun) IS brit because the full radiance of the sun is only possible through the covenant: 'shemesh beShabbat tzedaka la'aniyim' — the sun shining fully is dependent on shmirat habrit, and conversely, a blemished brit means only the shadow/reflection of the sun appears (mareh chamah amukah min hatzel). Shaddai IS brit because God's name Shaddai is associated with 'she'amar le'olamo dai' and with 'shim na yadkha tachat yerechi' — Shaddai is the name of the brit-covenant that sustains the world. Source line 50: 'veshada lahu, zeh bechina brit, bechina Shaddai kanaal.' The three form a single cluster of the covenant's cosmic dimension: Shaddai (the divine name of covenant-sustaining) = brit (the covenant quality) = shemesh (the solar illumination that the covenant enables).", "dominant_node": "ברית", "nodes": ["שמש", "ברית", "שדי"], "node_degrees": {"ברית": 2, "שמש": 1, "שדי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "פַּרְנָסָה וְלֶחֶם הַפָּנִים", "label_en": "Sustenance and the Showbread", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the Abraham-level of sustenance, where earthly food is elevated to the quality of the Showbread (lechem hapanim). Bira/bar umzon = the well in the wilderness = food that is outside holiness, 'behind the face' (vehistarti panai); once brought inside through proper service it becomes lechem hapanim. Mamon = bar umzon: money IS food/sustenance — both are the same reality of material provision. Se'uda = mamon: the meal IS money (both are the material sustenance domain, one abstract, one concrete). Chamah = 'kecham hayom' = 'lechem cham': the heat of Abraham's day (kecham hayom) IS the warm bread (lechem cham) which IS the Showbread — these three are the same concept seen through three different lenses (solar, temporal, gastronomic). Madregat Avraham = ba'al nefesh: the level of Abraham IS the ba'al nefesh — Abraham is the paradigm of the one who makes souls ('nefesh asher asu'), and his meals were always lechem hapanim in quality. Hithalekh = regalim (feet) = parnasa: the verse 'hithalekh lefanai' uses the root of walking/feet, and the Rebbe identifies this with 'regalim' = parnasa ('hayekum asher beragleihem — ze mamono shel adam sheama'amido al raglave'). The feet/walking of Abraham = his parnasa, which became parnasa on the level of the Showbread.", "dominant_node": "בר ומזון", "nodes": ["בר ומזון", "בירא", "ממון", "סעדה", "חמה", "\"כחם היום\"", "\"לחם חם\"", "מדרגת אברהם", "בעל נפש", "התהלך", "רגלים"], "node_degrees": {"בר ומזון": 2, "ממון": 2, "בירא": 1, "סעדה": 1, "חמה": 1, "\"כחם היום\"": 2, "\"לחם חם\"": 1, "מדרגת אברהם": 1, "בעל נפש": 1, "התהלך": 1, "רגלים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חמה", "\"כחם היום\"", "\"לחם חם\""], "reason": "These three form a single bechina chain (CC1): chamah ↔ kecham hayom ↔ lechem cham. The Rebbe identifies them explicitly in source lines 38-39: 'lechem cham beyom hilakcho — lechem hapanim, hem lechem cham, dargin de'Avraham, she'hu bechina kecham hayom. Umara'e chamah amukah min hatzel, chamah, bechina: kecham hayom, bechina lechem cham.' All three are expressions of Abraham's solar-level of warmth/illumination that manifests in his food. Merged into Package 4 because they are specifically about the parnasa/Abraham domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מדרגת אברהם", "בעל נפש"], "reason": "Madregat Avraham = ba'al nefesh: Abraham's level IS being a ba'al nefesh — a master of souls, one who makes nefashot. Source line 37: 'umi she'hu bemadregat Avraham, haynu bechina ba'al nefesh, azai beva'dai kol akhilotav use'udotav bechina lechem hapanim.' Their bechina relationship is direct: the Abraham-level = ba'al nefesh quality. Merged into Package 4 as specifying the type of person whose sustenance is naturally lechem hapanim."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התהלך", "רגלים"], "reason": "Hithalekh = regalim (feet/walking) = parnasa. Source line 37: 'hithalekh, zeh bechina raglin, bechina parnasato, bechina: veet hayekum asher beragleihem (ve'dorshim raboteinu zachronam livrakha: ze mamono shel adam sheama'amido al raglave).' The hithalekh command to Abraham uses the root of feet/walking (raglin), and this IS bechina of his parnasa. Merged into Package 4 (parnasa domain) rather than Package 6 (spiritual/tzaddik domain) because this bechina explicitly grounds tefila's 'regel' quality in the material parnasa context of Abraham's walking before God."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "כִּסּוּפִין וּנְפָשׁוֹת", "label_en": "Holy Yearning and the Souls", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the kisufin (holy yearning/longing) that creates nefashot (souls): the nekudot ARE nefesh because just as the soul is the life-force that animates the body, the vowel-points are the life-force that animates the letters — the same essential reality of 'animated form.' Ratzon = kisufin veha'hishtokekut: the will/desire IS the yearning — they are two names for the same interior striving. Ha'ala'at mayyin nukbin = kisufin: the mystical 'raising of feminine waters' is identical in nature to the yearning/longing — both describe the upward movement of desire toward the divine. Zahav = zivug: gold IS pairing/union, because 'mitzafon zahav ye'eteh' and the Zohar teaches 'it'aruta dizzivuga misitra dismala hu tzafon' — gold comes from the north which is the awakening of zivug; gold and zivug are the same essential energy. The three zivug-pairs (zivug hagufot via letters, zivugei haneshamot via nekudot, nezdavgu venitztarfu ha'otiyot = zivug hagufni) are all aspects of the same pairing-energy that originates in the kisufin. Teavutah dedkhar = mah shehadavar chozer veniksef elav (the object of male desire); teavutah denukba = mah shehe'niksef (the desiring subject's own longing) — these ARE the two poles of kisufin that generate two types of nefesh (male/female soul). All flow from the same root: yearning creates soul, soul creates union, union creates life.", "dominant_node": "כסופין וההשתוקקות", "nodes": ["כסופין וההשתוקקות", "העלאת מיין נוקבין", "רצון", "נקדות", "נפש", "זהב", "זווג", "נזדוגו ונצטרפו האותיות", "זווג הגופני", "זווגן והצטרפותן של הנקדות", "זווגי הנפשות", "מה שהדבר חוזר ונכסף אליו", "תאבותה דדכר", "מה שהוא נכסף", "תאבותה דנוקב"], "node_degrees": {"כסופין וההשתוקקות": 2, "העלאת מיין נוקבין": 1, "רצון": 1, "נקדות": 1, "נפש": 1, "זהב": 1, "זווג": 1, "נזדוגו ונצטרפו האותיות": 1, "זווג הגופני": 1, "זווגן והצטרפותן של הנקדות": 1, "זווגי הנפשות": 1, "מה שהדבר חוזר ונכסף אליו": 1, "תאבותה דדכר": 1, "מה שהוא נכסף": 1, "תאבותה דנוקב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נקדות", "נפש"], "reason": "Nekudot = nefesh: the vowel-points ARE the soul of the letters. Source line 55: 'al ken hanekudot hem bechina nefesh, ki kemo shehanefesh hu chiut ha'adam... ken hanekudot hem hachiut vehanefesh shel ha'otiyot.' This is a pure bechina: two names for the same animating-life-force quality, one in the domain of language, one in the domain of persons. Merged into Package 5 as a core member of the kisufin-soul cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["זהב", "זווג"], "reason": "Zahav = zivug: gold IS pairing/union. Source line 77: 'zahav hu bechina zivug, kemo shekatuv: mitzafon zahav ye'eteh, veit'aruta dizzivuga misitra dishmala shehu tzafon.' The north-direction, gold, and the awakening of zivug are the same essential energy. Merged into Package 5 as expressing the zivug-dimension that emerges from the kisufin."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נזדוגו ונצטרפו האותיות", "זווג הגופני"], "reason": "Nezdavgu venitztarfu ha'otiyot = zivug hagufni: the pairing/combining of letters IS the bodily union. Source line 86: 've'al yedei hanekudot nezdavgu venitztarfu ha'otiyot, zeh bechina zivugei hagufot.' The combining of letters through nekudot IS the pairing of bodies. Merged into Package 5 as a sub-dimension of the kisufin-zivug chain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["זווגן והצטרפותן של הנקדות", "זווגי הנפשות"], "reason": "Zivugam vehistarfutam shel hanekudot = zivugei haneshamot: the pairing of nekudot IS soul-unions. Source line 86: 'vezeh bechina zivugam vehistarfutam shel hanekudot, shehem bechina zivugei haneshamot.' Direct bechina statement. Merged into Package 5 as complementing the bodily-zivug pair — together they show the two levels of zivug (soul-level and body-level) that emerge from kisufin."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מה שהדבר חוזר ונכסף אליו", "תאבותה דדכר"], "reason": "Mah shehadavar chozer veniksef elav = teavutah dedkhar (male desire/wanting): what the object returns and yearns back toward is the male soul-yearning. Source line 79: 'umah shehadavar chozer veniksef elav, hu bechina teavutah deddkhar, ve'aved nefesh dedar.' Direct bechina. Merged into Package 5 as one pole of the kisufin dyad."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מה שהוא נכסף", "תאבותה דנוקב"], "reason": "Mah shehu niksef = teavutah denukba (female desire/wanting): the subject's own yearning IS the feminine soul-desire. Source line 79: 'ki mah shehu niksef hu bechina teavutah denukba, vehu aved nefesh nukba.' Direct bechina. Merged into Package 5 as the other pole of the kisufin dyad. Together CC23+CC24 express the dual-nature of yearning: the outgoing desire and the return-desire, which together produce a male soul and a female soul."}, {"merged_nodes": ["רצון", "העלאת מיין נוקבין"], "reason": "Ratzon = kisufin (CC13 already encodes this); ha'alat mayyin nukbin = kisufin. Source line 86: 'zeh bechina ha'alat mayyin nukbin kayadu'a.' The Zohar concept of ha'alat mayyin nukbin IS the kisufin — both describe the upward movement of feminine longing/arousal toward the divine. All three (ratzon, kisufin, ha'alat mayyin nukbin) are synonyms for the same essential reality. Merged into Package 5 as core kisufin-related nodes."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "לַמְדָן וְחָסִיד — הַצַּדִּיק וְהָרַב", "label_en": "Scholar and Devout — the Tzaddik and the Rebbe", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the complete tzaddik/rav who must embody both Torah-scholarship (lamdan) and piety (chasid). Malakh = lamdan = Metatron (Mitat): the angel Metatron IS the lamdan quality because Metatron is the heavenly scribe who separates pure from impure, permitted from forbidden — exactly the function of the lamdan in halacha. Mitat = mishnah: Metatron IS Mishna because the Tikkunim equate the verse 'yehi rakia' (which is Metatron in the Tikkunim) with the Mishna that distinguishes between the upper and lower waters = between tahor and tameh, muttar and asur. Hashem Tzvaot = chasid: the divine name Tzvaot, interpreted as 'ot hu ba'tzava dileh' (it is a sign in its army), IS the chasid — the one who goes beyond the letter of the law in divine service. Ot hu betzava dilah = ot brit: the 'sign in its army' IS the sign of the covenant (the brit) — both describe the tzaddik's character as an embodied sign of divine presence. Tzaddik = ot brit = Rabbi Akiva: the tzaddik IS the covenant-sign, and Rabbi Akiva IS the tzaddik (he entered and departed in peace). Mi shenikhnas veniskarev latzaddik = tzaddik: the one who approaches the tzaddik properly and receives a straight path IS the bechina of tzaddik — he becomes a tzaddik by his proper approach. Arba'ah shenikhnesu laPardes = tzaddik verasha oved Elokim velo avado: the four who entered Pardes ARE the four types — this is the taxonomy frame that contains all four types. Tefila = regel: prayer IS the foot/regalim — prayer is called a bechina of regel because just as regel = regalim = the festivals where one appears before God, prayer is the daily standing-before-God that corresponds to the regel. This belongs in the spiritual-tzaddik package (not parnasa) because the context is the tzaddik's prayer that flows from his mouth ('torah yevakshu mipihu').", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["מלאך", "למדן", "מט\"ט", "משנה", "חסיד", "ה' צבאות", "אות הוא בצבא דילה", "אות ברית", "צבאות", "צדיק", "מי שהוא נכנס ונתקרב להצדיק והוא מקבל ממנו דרך ישרה לעבודת ה לפי מדרגתו וערכו", "רבי עקיבא", "ארבעה שנכנסו לפרדס", "צדיק ורשע עובד אלקים ולא עבדו", "תפלה", "רגל"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 3, "למדן": 2, "מט\"ט": 2, "מלאך": 1, "משנה": 1, "חסיד": 1, "ה' צבאות": 1, "אות הוא בצבא דילה": 2, "אות ברית": 2, "צבאות": 1, "מי שהוא נכנס ונתקרב להצדיק והוא מקבל ממנו דרך ישרה לעבודת ה לפי מדרגתו וערכו": 1, "רבי עקיבא": 1, "ארבעה שנכנסו לפרדס": 1, "צדיק ורשע עובד אלקים ולא עבדו": 1, "תפלה": 1, "רגל": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מלאך", "למדן", "מט\"ט", "משנה"], "reason": "Malakh = lamdan = Mitat = Mishna — these four form a bechina chain (CC25). Source lines 22-23, 73: 'malakh, zeh bechina lamdan, bechina Mitat kanaal; tzvaot, ot hu batzava dileh, bechina: tzaddik ot brit.' And source line 73: 'mah shehu lamdan batorah, hu bechina malakh shehu Mitat... shehu bechina Mishna, hamavdil umafrish bein mayim dakin ubein mayim mesa'avim.' These four are the lamdan-dimension of the complete tzaddik: Torah-knowledge (mishna/halacha) = Metatron's separating function = angelic quality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חסיד", "ה' צבאות"], "reason": "Chasid = Hashem Tzvaot: the pious/devout quality IS the Hashem Tzvaot dimension. Source line 73: 'vetzarich leidamot leqono lihyot chasid bema'asim tovim, vezeh bechina Hashem Tzvaot.' The chasid embodies the Hashem Tzvaot quality — serving God with love and going beyond the letter of the law. This is the complement to the lamdan/Mitat dimension."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אות הוא בצבא דילה", "אות ברית", "צדיק", "צבאות", "רבי עקיבא", "מי שהוא נכנס ונתקרב להצדיק והוא מקבל ממנו דרך ישרה לעבודת ה לפי מדרגתו וערכו"], "reason": "These form CC2, already connected via bechina edges. Ot hu betzava dilah = ot brit (source line 23 and 70): 'tzvaot, ot hu batzava dileh, bechina: tzaddik ot brit.' The covenant-sign = the tzaddik. Tzaddik = Rabbi Akiva (he is the archetype of the tzaddik who enters and departs in peace). Mi shenikhnas... = tzaddik (source line 67): the one who properly approaches the tzaddik IS the tzaddik-type. All merged into Package 6 as facets of the tzaddik's spiritual identity."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ארבעה שנכנסו לפרדס", "צדיק ורשע עובד אלקים ולא עבדו"], "reason": "The four who entered Pardes = the four types (tzaddik, rasha, oved Elokim, lo avado). Source line 66: 'veeleh ha'arba bechinot hem bechinot tzaddik verasha oved Elokim velo avado.' This is a direct bechina — the Pardes story IS the four-type taxonomy. Merged into Package 6 as the structural frame for the tzaddik discussion. Note: the rasha (Aher) is present here as a type within the four-type taxonomy; Aher as an independent concept belongs in Package 8 (evil)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תפלה", "רגל"], "reason": "Tefila = regel: prayer IS a bechina of regel/foot. Source line 106 (final line of source text): 'hatefilah nikret bechina regel.' While 'regalim' in the parnasa context (Package 4) refers to feet = material sustenance (hithalekh = regalim = parnasa), the concept 'regel' here is explicitly named as the bechina of prayer itself — a distinct spiritual concept. Prayer's standing-before-God corresponds structurally to the regel-festivals as standing-before-God. Merged into Package 6 (spiritual/tzaddik domain) because the source context is the tzaddik's mouth bringing out kisufin through prayer ('torah yevakshu mipihu... ve'et shav'atam yishma veyoshi'em')."}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "הֵצִיץ וָמֵת וְנִפְגַּע", "label_en": "Gazed and Died, or Gazed and Was Harmed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the sincere but imperfect encounter with the tzaddik's light — the person who approaches the tzaddik with a true heart but receives more light than their level can bear. This is a good polarity: the Rebbe explicitly states (source line 68) 'afillu heititz vamet heititz venifga, shnehem hem bechina tzaddik, va'aleihem gam ken ne'emar: tzaddikim yelkhu vam.' They are tzaddikim — their error is one of level, not of intent. Oved Elokim = heititz vamet: the 'server of God' type who encounters the rav's light and his heart blazes so intensely that he can die from it — this is bechina oved Elokim (a genuine divine-server but beyond his level). Lo avado = heititz venifga: the one who 'did not serve' (lo avado) = the one who goes out of his mind because he rises above his level — this is bechina heititz venifga. Mi sheyotze mida'ato = heititz venifga: one who loses his mind from rising above his level IS the heititz venifga — same essential reality.", "dominant_node": "הציץ ומת", "nodes": ["עובד אלקים", "שנתלהב לבו מאד ועל ידי זה יוכל למות", "הציץ ומת", "מי שיוצא מדעתו מחמת שעולה למעלה ממדרגתו", "הציץ ונפגע", "לא עבדו"], "node_degrees": {"הציץ ומת": 2, "הציץ ונפגע": 2, "עובד אלקים": 1, "שנתלהב לבו מאד ועל ידי זה יוכל למות": 1, "מי שיוצא מדעתו מחמת שעולה למעלה ממדרגתו": 1, "לא עבדו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עובד אלקים", "שנתלהב לבו מאד ועל ידי זה יוכל למות", "הציץ ומת"], "reason": "These three form CC14. Oved Elokim = heititz vamet: the oved Elokim type IS the one who gazes and dies. Source line 68: 'veyesh mi shehu bechina heititz vamet... bezeh bechina oved Elokim.' Shenitlahav libo = heititz vamet: the one whose heart blazes from the rav's light = heititz vamet. Same essential reality — the overwhelming encounter with light beyond one's level that can cause death. Merged together as the first sub-cluster of the imperfect encounter."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מי שיוצא מדעתו מחמת שעולה למעלה ממדרגתו", "הציץ ונפגע", "לא עבדו"], "reason": "These form CC15. Lo avado = heititz venifga: source line 68: 'veyesh mi sheyotze mida'ato mechamat she'oleh lema'alah mimadregato, vehu bechina heititz venifga, bechina lo avado.' The one who goes out of his mind from rising above his level IS heititz venifga IS lo avado (the one who 'did not serve' = Ben Azzai). Same essential nature: spiritual overwhelm causing mental disruption. Merged as the second sub-cluster. Both CC14 and CC15 are merged into Package 7 because they share the essential nature of the good-but-overwhelmed spiritual encounter (polarity: good), as distinct from Package 8 (Aher, who is evil/rasha)."}]}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 31, "label": "אַחֵר וּמְקַצֵּץ בִּנְטִיעוֹת", "label_en": "Aher and the One Who Cuts the Shoots", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the rasha who approaches the tzaddik with evil intent and uses the encounter to sin more deeply. Aher = mi sheto'eh vesover shebechinot lamdan levad hu ha'ikar: Aher IS the one who erroneously believes that Torah-scholarship alone is the essence — Aher saw Metatron (the lamdan-angel) and concluded it had independent authority, exactly the error of believing Torah without chasidut/piety is sufficient (source line 74: 'umi sheto'eh vesover... she'lamdan levad hu ha'ikar, hu bechina Aher shekiletz benitiyot'). Aher = mi shemitkarev latzaddik umekaletz benitiyot vekofer bakol: Aher IS the one who approaches the tzaddik and cuts the shoots and denies everything — this is the rasha type (source line 69). Both describe the same essential reality: the person who comes to the tzaddik with evil in his heart (tina hayeta belibo) and inevitably finds a way to sin through the encounter.", "dominant_node": "אחר", "nodes": ["אחר", "מי שטועה וסובר שבחינות למדן לבד הוא העקר", "מי שמתקרב להצדיק ומקצץ בנטיעות וכופר בכל"], "node_degrees": {"אחר": 2, "מי שטועה וסובר שבחינות למדן לבד הוא העקר": 1, "מי שמתקרב להצדיק ומקצץ בנטיעות וכופר בכל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 32, "label": "עָלְמָא דְּאִתְכַּסְיָא — לֵאָה, הַלֵּב, וְרִקּוּדֵי הַחֲתֻנָּה", "label_en": "The Hidden World — Leah, the Heart, and the Wedding Dance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five concepts share the essential nature of Binah / Imma — the concealed upper world whose hidden light (the aleph of Ehyeh) flows downward to rectify the Shekhinah. עלמא דאתכסיא is explicitly the hidden world (Zohar Vayeitzei 154). לב is an aspect of עלמא דאתכסיא because the heart is where Torah is hidden ('in my heart I have hidden Your word'). לאה IS עלמא דאתכסיא (same Zohar source). 'אדני שפתי תפתח' IS the wedding dance (declared equivalent in the opening line). רקודין של חתנה is the moment when Binah's hidden light (drawn from Leah's hidden world through the heart) opens and sweetens the Shekhinah. All five are faces of the same concealed-becoming-revealed reality: Binah's hidden world expressing itself as the opening of the lips, the dance, and the zivug.", "dominant_node": "עלמא דאתכסיא", "nodes": ["עלמא דאתכסיא", "לב", "לאה", "\"אדני שפתי תפתח\"", "רקודין של חתנה"], "node_degrees": {"עלמא דאתכסיא": 2, "לב": 1, "לאה": 1, "\"אדני שפתי תפתח\"": 1, "רקודין של חתנה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"אדני שפתי תפתח\"", "רקודין של חתנה"], "reason": "CC-C was a 2-node isolated component. 'אדני שפתי תפתח' and רקודין של חתנה are declared equivalent ('IS the wedding dance') and represent the opening/revelation of Binah's hidden light — which is the essential nature of עלמא דאתכסיא. The source text makes explicit that the wedding dance draws the aleph from the heart (Leah/עלמא דאתכסיא) downward: 'לְהַמְשִׁיךְ מִלֵּאָה הָאַלְפִין'. The wedding dance and its verse are not a cause-effect appendage — they ARE the manifestation of Leah's hidden world becoming revealed. Merged by shared essential nature (Binah-revelation), not by eitza logic."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 32, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רוּחַ מָשִׁיחַ וְחֹטֶם", "label_en": "Spirit of Messiah and the Nose", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package are expressions of the same essential Mashiach-force. Mashiach is identified with the nose (חֹטֶם) as the seat of breath/spirit ('רוּחַ אַפֵּינוּ מְשִׁיחַ ה''); his spirit (רוּחוֹ שֶׁל מָשִׁיחַ) is identified with the divine wind that hovered over creation (רוּחַ אֱלֹקִים), and operationally that same spirit becomes the ruach-kina'ah — the spirit of zealous guarding — when it encounters impurity. These are not cause-and-effect steps; they are three faces of the same Mashiach dimension: its physical locus (nose), its divine origin (ruach Elohim at creation), and its active manifestation in the world (ruach kina'ah). Together they form one bechina-cluster: the Spirit of Mashiach.", "dominant_node": "רוחו של משיח", "nodes": ["רוחו של משיח", "רוח קנאה", "רוח אלקים", "משיח", "חטם"], "node_degrees": {"רוחו של משיח": 2, "רוח קנאה": 1, "רוח אלקים": 1, "משיח": 1, "חטם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["משיח", "חטם"], "reason": "This isolated pair (raw component C3) represents the physical-name dimension of Mashiach: 'מָשִׁיחַ הוּא בְּחִינוֹת חֹטֶם' — the nose is the physical locus of the Mashiach-spirit. The nose is where breath/ruach enters and exits. C3 and C1 (רוחו של משיח, רוח קנאה, רוח אלקים) share the same essential nature — the Mashiach force. C3 is the body; C1 is the spirit of that same body. This is bechina (same essence), not eitza (cause→effect). Merged into Package 1."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 32, "label": "שַׁ\"ךְ הַדִּינִים — נַעַ\"ר וַחֲמֵשׁ פְּעָמִים דִּין", "label_en": "The 330 Judgments — The Unmarried Girl and the Five-Times-Din", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All three concepts share the essential nature of the harsh judgments (dinnim) that afflict the Shekhinah in her uncorrected, un-betrothed state. ש\"ך (330) is gematria of נע\"ר (the young girl without the ה) — they are the same numerical reality named two ways. חמש פעמים דין (five times the word דין = 330) is the third face of the same reality. These are not causes of one another — they are three ways of expressing the same domain of harsh judgment that keeps the Kallah in the state of נַעַר (without the ה of Binah). They are the evil counter-force to Package 1: while Package 1 is the hidden light of Binah, Package 2 is the harsh judgment that blocks its flow.", "dominant_node": "ש\"ך", "nodes": ["ש\"ך", "נע\"ר", "חמש פעמים דין"], "node_degrees": {"ש\"ך": 2, "נע\"ר": 1, "חמש פעמים דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 32, "volume": "lm2", "label": "סֵפֶר וְשֵׁם ה' וְתוֹרָה", "label_en": "The Holy Book, the Name of God, and Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package are expressions of the same sacred written/revealed domain. The ספר (holy book) is a bechina of שם ה' (Name of God), because 'ספר במספר שם' (Zohar), and it is specifically 'שמי שנכתב בקדשה ונתפשט בעולם ועושה שם' — the written Name that spreads through the world. Meanwhile, Torah itself is identified with מים (water): 'מַיִם הוּא הַתּוֹרָה'. The holy book is the face/revelation (פני האורייתא) of Torah; Torah/water is the deeper substrate. The ספר is the sanctified outer form through which Torah (as divine Name) enters the world. All five nodes — ספר, שם ה, שמי שנכתב בקדשה, מים, תורה — are aspects of the same revelatory-sacred dimension: the written Word of God.", "dominant_node": "ספר", "nodes": ["ספר", "שם ה", "שמי שנכתב בקדשה, ונתפשט בעולם ועושה שם", "מים", "תורה"], "node_degrees": {"ספר": 2, "שם ה": 1, "שמי שנכתב בקדשה, ונתפשט בעולם ועושה שם": 1, "מים": 1, "תורה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים", "תורה"], "reason": "This isolated pair (raw component C2) establishes that Torah is water: 'מַיִם הוּא הַתּוֹרָה, כַּמּוּבָא'. The proof text in which this appears is the same passage explaining that Mashiach's spirit hovers over the books 'כְּרוּחַ אֱלֹקִים מְרַחֶפֶת עַל פְּנֵי הַמָּיִם'. The ספר is the 'face' (פני) of Torah/water — it is the revealed surface on which Mashiach's spirit rests. מים/תורה are the underlying sacred substance; ספר/שם ה are its outward, named form. This is bechina (same essence seen from different angles), not eitza. Merged into Package 2."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 33, "label": "דַּעַת וְאַהֲבָה — חַיִּים, כֹּהֵן, וְחֶסֶד", "label_en": "Da'at and Love — Life, the Kohen, and Chesed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four concepts share the essential nature of the inner holy knowing (da'at) as love and life-force. חיים and דעת are declared identical: 'חַיִּים הוּא בְּחִינַת דַּעַת' — life IS an aspect of da'at, for 'מְקוֹר חַיִּים שֵׂכֶל בְּעָלָיו' (Proverbs 16:22). כהן and חסד are declared identical: 'כֹּהֵן הוּא בְּחִינוֹת חֶסֶד.' The חסד/אהבה identity is explicit throughout the text ('הַחֶסֶד הַיְנוּ הָאַהֲבָה'). The link between the two sub-clusters (חיים-דעת and כהן-חסד) is bechina, not eitza: da'at is the inner cognitive face of love ('אַהֲבָה שֶׁבְּדַעַת'), and chesed is the relational face of the same reality. Both are expressions of the same essential quality — the holy ahavah that God has for Israel, which is simultaneously the life-force of da'at and the generous outpouring of chesed. The kohen 'guards da'at' (יִשְׁמְרוּ דַּעַת) not as a cause-effect chain but because the kohen IS chesed IS love — and love IS da'at IS life. The entire Torah is built on this single good cluster: the ahavah-she-b'da'at that underlies all of existence.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["חיים", "דעת", "כהן", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"חיים": 1, "דעת": 1, "כהן": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חיים", "דעת", "כהן", "חסד"], "reason": "The bechina subgraph for Torah #33 contains exactly 2 edges forming 2 isolated 2-node components: CC-A (חיים ↔ דעת, edge 763) and CC-B (כהן ↔ חסד, edge 1201). Both components are of equal size (2 nodes each); neither is 'larger.' The merge rule applies to small components (1-2 nodes), which both are. Merge criterion: SHARED NATURE, not eitza. The two sub-clusters share the essential nature of the holy ahavah-she-b'da'at that is the Torah's central concept. The source text explicitly equates chesed with ahavah ('הַחֶסֶד הַיְנוּ הָאַהֲבָה') and da'at with life ('חַיִּים הוּא בְּחִינַת דַּעַת'). The teaching's climax at section ז (lines 43-45) puts all four nodes into one conceptual frame: 'מִי הָאִישׁ הֶחָפֵץ חַיִּים' — חפץ (desire) = לב (heart); חַיִּים = דַּעַת. The kohen who has chesed guards da'at (שמירת דעת). The essential nature of da'at-as-love and chesed-as-da'at makes these two 2-node clusters faces of the same good reality. Merging is by shared nature, not cause-effect."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 34, "label": "נְקֻדָּה וְיוּד — הַצַּדִּיק, הַחָכְמָה, וְהַפֶּה", "label_en": "The Point and the Yud — The Tzaddik, Wisdom, and the Mouth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this component share the essential nature of the concentrated, seminal point of divine wisdom — the yud, the spring, the source. חכמה IS a bechina of נקדה ('חָכְמָה הִיא בְּחִינַת נְקֻדָּה'). נקדה IS a bechina of יוד ('נְקֻדָּה, הַיְנוּ יוּד'). מעין IS a bechina of יוד ('חָכְמָה הִיא בְּחִינַת נְקֻדָּה, הַיְנוּ יוּד, מַעְיָן'). צדיק IS a bechina of יוד ('צַדִּיק הוּא יוּד, כִּי הַצַּדִּיקִים הֵם נִקְרָאִים חַכְמֵי הָעֵדָה'). תורה IS a bechina of יוד ('הַתּוֹרָה הִיא יוּד, שֶׁנִּקְרֵאת רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה'). עשרת הדברות IS a bechina of יוד (the Ten Commandments with the Tablets = מלאפום, the Ten Commandments themselves = the yud). פה IS a bechina of יוד ('יוּד, עַל-שֵׁם הַפֶּה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: פִּי יְדַבֵּר חָכְמוֹת'). 'פי ידבר חכמות' IS a bechina of נקדה (the point illuminating from the mouth-wisdom). לולב IS a bechina of צדיק ('הַלּוּלָב, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק כַּיָּדוּעַ, בְּחִינַת: צַדִּיק כַּתָּמָר יִפְרָח'). The essential shared nature: all are expressions of the seminal, concentrated divine point — the source of wisdom, speech, and leadership. The tzaddik is this point for all of Israel; Torah is this point in the domain of divine will; the mouth is this point in the human body; the yud is this point in the alphabet.", "dominant_node": "אות יו\"ד", "nodes": ["נקדה", "אות יו\"ד", "מעין", "עשרת הדברות", "צדיק", "תורה", "חכמה", "פה", "\"פי ידבר חכמות\"", "לולב"], "node_degrees": {"נקדה": 3, "אות יו\"ד": 6, "מעין": 1, "עשרת הדברות": 1, "צדיק": 2, "תורה": 1, "חכמה": 1, "פה": 1, "\"פי ידבר חכמות\"": 1, "לולב": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 34, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַשֹּׁרֶשׁ וְשִׂמְחַת כָּל הַטּוֹבוֹת", "label_en": "The Root and the Joy of All Goods", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the same essential nature: they are different facets of the spiritual principle that at the ROOT all goods are unified, and true joy consists in perceiving that unity. The Root (שרש) IS the state where all goods are one (כל הטובות ביחד); looking upward (הסתכלות למעלה) IS the Root (the text says 'דהיינו על השרש'); joy from all goods together (שמחה מכל הטובות ביחד) IS perfect/complete joy (שלמות וגדלות השמחה); and Jethro (יתרו) is the exemplar of this state — 'וַיִּחַדְּ יִתְרוֹ עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה' proves he was one who looked upward at the root. All are expressions of the same spiritual reality: the vantage point of the divine root from which all goods flow as one.", "dominant_node": "שרש", "nodes": ["שרש", "הסתכלות למעלה על כל הטובה", "שמחה מכל הטובות ביחד", "שלמות וגדלות השמחה", "יתרו"], "node_degrees": {"שרש": 3, "הסתכלות למעלה על כל הטובה": 2, "שמחה מכל הטובות ביחד": 2, "שלמות וגדלות השמחה": 1, "יתרו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שלמות וגדלות השמחה", "שמחה מכל הטובות ביחד"], "reason": "The text equates these directly: 'שְׁלֵמוּת וְגַדְלוּת הַשִּׂמְחָה הִוא, מִי שֶׁזּוֹכֶה לִשְׂמֹחַ מִכָּל הַטּוֹבוֹת בְּיַחַד.' Perfect joy IS joy from all goods together — two names for the same state, not a cause-effect sequence."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יתרו", "הסתכלות למעלה על כל הטובה"], "reason": "Jethro is the embodied example of the principle: 'וַיִּחַדְּ יִתְרוֹ עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה... כִּי הָיָה מִסְתַּכֵּל לְמַעְלָה עַל כָּל הַטּוֹבָה, הַיְנוּ עַל הַשֹּׁרֶשׁ.' Jethro IS the one who looks upward. He is not the cause of that looking, nor the effect — he is the person-dimension of the same essential reality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 34, "label": "וָאו וְלֵב — יִשְׂרָאֵל, הַבִּינָה, וְהַלּוּחוֹת", "label_en": "The Vav and the Heart — Israel, Binah, and the Tablets", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this component share the essential nature of the extended, receptive vessel — the vav, the stream, the heart. בינה IS a bechina of וא\"ו ('וּבִינָה הוּא נַחַל הַנִּמְשָׁךְ מִן הַמַּעְיָן. וְעַל-שֵׁם הַמַּשְׁכוּת, הוּא וָאו'). הלוחות IS a bechina of וא\"ו ('הַלּוּחוֹת אָרְכָּן שִׁשָּׁה וְרָחְבָּן שִׁשָּׁה' — they are the vav). העולם IS a bechina of וא\"ו ('הָעוֹלָם נִבְרָא בְּשֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַמַּעֲשֶׂה' — six days = vav). ווי העמודים IS a bechina of וא\"ו (they are literally named after the letter). ישראל IS a bechina of וא\"ו ('יִשְׂרָאֵל הֵם בְּחִינַת וָאו, שֶׁהֵם תָּמְכֵי אוֹרַיְתָא'). תמכי אוריתא IS a bechina of וא\"ו (same equating proof). לב IS a bechina of וא\"ו ('וָאו, עַל שֵׁם: וְהָגוּת לִבִּי תְבוּנוֹת' — the heart receives and meditates, like the stream/vav). 'והגות לבי תבונות' IS a bechina of וא\"ו (directly stated). נחל הנמשך מן המעין IS a bechina of בינה ('וּבִינָה הוּא נַחַל הַנִּמְשָׁךְ מִן הַמַּעְיָן'). The essential shared nature: the extended stream of divine overflow, the receptive letter/vessel that receives illumination from the point (נקדה/יוד). The lev, Israel, Binah, the Tablets, the world — all are the 'vav,' the elongated flowing channel that receives from the spring.", "dominant_node": "אות וא\"ו", "nodes": ["אות וא\"ו", "בינה", "הלוחות", "העולם", "ווי העמודים", "ישראל", "תמכי אוריתא", "\"והגות לבי תבונות\"", "לב", "נחל הנמשך מן המעין"], "node_degrees": {"אות וא\"ו": 8, "בינה": 2, "הלוחות": 1, "העולם": 1, "ווי העמודים": 1, "ישראל": 1, "תמכי אוריתא": 1, "\"והגות לבי תבונות\"": 1, "לב": 1, "נחל הנמשך מן המעין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 34, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַכְּלָלִיּוּת וְהַהִתְנוֹצְצוּת", "label_en": "Inclusion and Mutual Sparkling", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of the mechanism by which unified joy produces amplified light. Inclusion (כלליות) IS the state of joy being included within its fellow (שמחה בחברתה) — the text defines them as identical. The sparkling (התנוצצות) from joy to joy IS the increase of light (נגדל ונתוסף האור) — they are two descriptions of the same phenomenon: what inclusion looks like in its active, luminous dimension. The multiplication of joys included together (ריבוי שמחות שנכללין ביחד) is the structural expression of this same reality. All these nodes articulate the same essential truth: when goods/joys are unified at their root, they illuminate each other and the light multiplies.", "dominant_node": "כלליות", "nodes": ["כלליות", "שמחה בחברתה", "התנוצצות", "אור השמחה", "ריבוי שמחות שנכללין ביחד"], "node_degrees": {"כלליות": 3, "שמחה בחברתה": 2, "התנוצצות": 2, "אור השמחה": 2, "ריבוי שמחות שנכללין ביחד": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כלליות", "שמחה בחברתה"], "reason": "The text defines them as identical: 'עַל־יְדֵי הַכְּלָלִיּוּת שֶׁנִּכְלָל שִׂמְחָה בַּחֲבֶרְתָּהּ' — inclusiveness IS joy being included within its fellow joy. One is the abstract principle (כלליות), the other is its concrete instantiation (שמחה בחברתה); they are parallel expressions of the same essential reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התנוצצות", "אור השמחה"], "reason": "Sparkling (התנוצצות) and the light of joy (אור השמחה) are the two faces of the same luminous reality: 'נִגְדָּל וְנִתּוֹסֵף הָאוֹר מְאֹד, עַל־יְדֵי הִתְנוֹצְצוּת שֶׁמִּתְנוֹצֵץ מִשִּׂמְחָה לַחֲבֶרְתָּהּ.' The sparkling IS the light increasing. These are not cause-effect; sparkling is the name for the light's mode of amplification, and the amplified light is the name for what sparkling produces — at the level of essential nature, they are the same reality described from two angles."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 34, "label": "מְלָאפוּם — פֶּה מָלֵא, יוֹסֵף הַשַּׁלִּיט, נְקֻדָּה עִם וָאו", "label_en": "Mela'fum — The Full Mouth, Joseph the Ruler, Point-with-Vav", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the complete union of yud and vav — the full vessel of divine abundance. מלאפום IS a bechina of נקדה עם וא\"ו ('מְלָאפוּם הוּא בְּחִינַת נְקֻדָּה עִם וָאו'). 'ויוסף הוא השליט' IS a bechina of מלאפום ('וְיוֹסֵף הוּא הַשַּׁלִּיט, הוּא בְּחִינַת מְלָאפוּם' — Joseph the ruler embodies the full-mouth state). 'הרחב פיך ואמלאהו' IS a bechina of מלאפום ('\"הַרְחֶב פִּיךָ וַאֲמַלְאֵהוּ\", זֶה בְּחִינַת מְלָאפוּם'). עשרת הדברות עם הלוחות IS a bechina of מלאפום ('עֲשֶׂרֶת הַדִּבְּרוֹת עִם הַלּוּחוֹת, הוּא בְּחִינַת מְלָאפוּם' — the Ten Commandments+Tablets together = yud+vav = the full-mouth/abundance). 'צדיק מושל' IS a bechina of מלא פום ('\"צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל\" שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת מְלֹא פוּם'). מלא פום and מלאפום are the same concept: מלאפום literally means 'מלא פום' (full mouth) — the vowel-sign mela'fum = full mouth of God's abundance filling the tzaddik's speech. The essential shared nature of this package: the combined state of yud and vav in fullness — the ruler whose mouth is completely filled with divine overflow, enabling him to draw abundance to Israel. This is distinct from Package 1 (just the yud/point) and Package 2 (just the vav/stream) — Package 3 is the integrated state of both together.", "dominant_node": "מלאפום", "nodes": ["מלאפום", "נקדה עם וא\"ו", "\"הרחב פיך ואמלאהו\"", "\"ויוסף הוא השליט\"", "עשרת הדברות עם הלוחות", "מלא פום", "\"צדיק מושל\""], "node_degrees": {"מלאפום": 4, "נקדה עם וא\"ו": 1, "\"הרחב פיך ואמלאהו\"": 1, "\"ויוסף הוא השליט\"": 1, "עשרת הדברות עם הלוחות": 1, "מלא פום": 1, "\"צדיק מושל\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מלא פום", "מלאפום"], "reason": "מלאפום and מלא פום appear as distinct node_ids in the dataset but name the same concept: מלאפום (the vowel-sign holam-vav) literally means מלא פום (full mouth). The Torah text makes this identity explicit: 'הַמְּלָאפוּם הוּא הָאוֹתִיּוֹת מְלֹא פוּם' (section ג). The edge connecting 'צדיק מושל' to מלא פום (id 1809) and the cluster of edges connecting to מלאפום (ids 1811-1813, 2194) are two faces of the same dataset entry. Both node_ids are merged into this package under the dominant node מלאפום. Merge criterion: SHARED NATURE — they are the same concept under two different spellings/presentations in the dataset."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 34, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פִּירוּד הַשְּׂמָחוֹת — חִלּוּקִים", "label_en": "Fragmented Joy — Separations", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of spiritual fragmentation — the state in which a person's experience of good is divided rather than unified. Divisions (חלוקים) IS fragmentation (פירוד), which in this Torah functions as the evil/deficient counterforce to the unified joy of Package 1. Jealousy (קנאה) and anguish (צער) are the consequence that emerges when a person has no joy at all — even becoming resentful over a match-making (מקנא על השידוך). This is the anti-Jethro, the person stuck in the fragment who cannot rise to the root. The fragmented state is the evil mirror of the unified state of Package 1: where Package 1 shows all goods as one at the root, Package 3 shows the person trapped in the multiplicity who cannot access the root.", "dominant_node": "חלוקים", "nodes": ["חלוקים", "פירוד השמחות", "קנאה וצער"], "node_degrees": {"חלוקים": 2, "פירוד השמחות": 1, "קנאה וצער": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חלוקים", "פירוד השמחות"], "reason": "Divisions (חלוקים) and fragmentation of joys (פירוד השמחות) are the same essential reality from two angles. The text opens: 'כִּי יֵשׁ חִלּוּקִים רַבִּים בְּעִנְיַן הַשִּׂמְחָה' — the many divisions ARE the fragmented state. One is the structural description (many divisions), the other is the experiential result (joys fragmented). They share essential nature: the absence of unity."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קנאה וצער", "חלוקים"], "reason": "Jealousy and anguish (קנאה וצער) represent the furthest extreme of the fragmented state — not merely unable to rejoice from all goods, but unable to rejoice at all, and actively suffering from envy. They belong in the same evil package as חלוקים because they are the natural fruit of the divided state: 'יֵשׁ לוֹ קִנְאָה וָצַעַר, שֶׁמְּקַנֵּא עַל הַשִּׁדּוּךְ.' This is still the essential nature of division/separation, not a new domain."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 34, "label": "קְדֻשַּׁת הַחֶסֶד — יְסוֹד, אַהֲבָה שֶׁבְּדַעַת, חֲסָדִים שֶׁבַּגּוּף", "label_en": "Holy Chesed — Yesod, Love-within-Da'at, Chesed-in-the-Body", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this merged package share the essential nature of the holy chesed — the divine love in its pure, unbroken form. יסוד דאצילות IS a bechina of 'צדיק יסוד עולם' ('בִּיסוֹד דַּאֲצִילוּת, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת: צַדִּיק יְסוֹד עוֹלָם'). The Torah explicitly states that after the breaking of the vessels, 'הָאוֹר הַחֶסֶד נִשְׁאָר בִּיסוֹד דַּאֲצִילוּת' — the light of chesed remained in yesod. שרש החסדים שבדעת IS a bechina of אהבה שבדעת ('שֹּׁרֶשׁ הַחֲסָדִים שֶׁבְּדַעַת, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת אַהֲבָה שֶׁבְּדַעַת'). חסדים שבגוף IS a bechina of כלליות המדות ('הַחֲסָדִים שֶׁבְּגוּף, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת כְּלָלִיּוּת הַמִּדּוֹת, שֶׁבְּכֻלָּם מְלֻבָּשִׁים חֲסָדִים, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת אַהֲבָה'). The three sub-clusters (CC-E: yesod/chesed-preserved; CC-G: root-of-chesed-in-da'at/love-in-da'at; CC-H: chesed-embodied/midot) are all expressions of the same essential reality: holy chesed/love in its pure, preserved, holy form — as opposed to the fallen/broken chesed of Package 5. All three are the domain of 'אוֹר הַחֶסֶד' — the light of chesed that did not fall.", "dominant_node": "יסוד דאצילות", "nodes": ["יסוד דאצילות", "\"צדיק יסוד עולם\"", "שרש החסדים שבדעת", "אהבה שבדעת", "חסדים שבגוף", "כלליות המדות"], "node_degrees": {"יסוד דאצילות": 1, "\"צדיק יסוד עולם\"": 1, "שרש החסדים שבדעת": 1, "אהבה שבדעת": 1, "חסדים שבגוף": 1, "כלליות המדות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יסוד דאצילות", "\"צדיק יסוד עולם\"", "שרש החסדים שבדעת", "אהבה שבדעת", "חסדים שבגוף", "כלליות המדות"], "reason": "Three raw connected components are merged here: CC-E (יסוד דאצילות + 'צדיק יסוד עולם'), CC-G (שרש החסדים שבדעת + אהבה שבדעת), and CC-H (חסדים שבגוף + כלליות המדות). All three are 2-node components (small, per the merge rule). Merge criterion: SHARED NATURE, not eitza. CC-E's essential nature: the yesod/tzaddik is the repository of the light of chesed that remained after the breaking of the vessels ('אוֹר הַחֶסֶד נִשְׁאָר בִּיסוֹד דַּאֲצִילוּת'). CC-G's essential nature: the root of chesed within da'at IS ahavah-within-da'at — both are the inner quality of holy love at the level of knowing. CC-H's essential nature: the chesed embedded throughout the body IS the totality of the midot — both describe the all-pervasive holy love. All three sub-clusters describe the same domain: holy chesed/ahavah in its pure, preserved form — the positive counterpart to the fallen-chesed of Package 5 (חרפה/ערלה/אהבה נפולה). The merge is justified by shared essential nature: all are expressions of 'אוֹר הַחֶסֶד' — the holy light of divine love that was not broken or fallen."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 34, "label": "חֶרְפָּה וְעָרְלַת לֵב — הָאַהֲבָה הַנְּפוּלָה", "label_en": "Shame and Foreskin of the Heart — Fallen Love", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes in this component share the essential nature of the fallen, broken chesed — love that has collapsed from its holy source into its negative, obstructing form. חרפה IS a bechina of אהבה נפולה ('וְהוּא בְּחִינַת אַהֲבָה נְפוּלָה וּשְׁבוּרָה, כִּי יָדוּעַ שֶׁהַיֵּצֶר הָרָע וְהַקְּלִפּוֹת נִתְהַוִּים מִן שְׁבִירַת כֵּלִים'). חרפה IS a bechina of ערלה ('וְחֶרְפָּה הוּא בְּחִינַת עָרְלָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: כִּי חֶרְפָּה הִוא לָנוּ'). The essential shared nature: these three nodes are the single evil domain of this Torah — the 'ערלת לב' (foreskin of the heart), which is the clogging/blocking of the heart by fallen love. When the vessels of chesed broke, the light of love fell into the lower realms and became inverted — producing shame/disgrace (חרפה), blockage (ערלה), and disordered love (אהבה נפולה). These are the three faces of the same negative reality: the holy chesed of Package 4 when it falls and inverts. This is the evil mirror of Package 4: just as Package 4 represents holy chesed preserved in yesod, Package 5 represents the same chesed when it has fallen into the klipot.", "dominant_node": "חרפה", "nodes": ["חרפה", "אהבה נפולה", "ערלה"], "node_degrees": {"חרפה": 2, "אהבה נפולה": 1, "ערלה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 35, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּמַלְכוּת", "label_en": "Faith and Kingship", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #35 centers on אמונה (faith) as the essential nature of מלכות (divine kingship), Jerusalem, the moon (מאור הקטן), and the night. All nodes in this cluster are different expressions of the same underlying reality: the receptive vessel of the divine feminine that receives wisdom from the light of the Face. שנה (sleep), פשטא אוריתא (plain Torah study), משא ומתן באמונה (faithful commerce), and ראש השנה are all aspects of the same liminal state in which the soul rests within faith and is renewed. The moon (מאור הקטן) is identical with faith, which is identical with Jerusalem, which is identical with sleep/night — all are the same receptive vessel illuminated from above.", "dominant_node": "פשטא אוריתא", "nodes": ["אמונה", "ירושלים", "מלכות", "בן ננס", "העמדת המלך", "לילה", "מאור הקטן", "ינוקא", "פשטא אוריתא", "מט\"ט", "עוסק בדיני ממונות", "משא ומתן באמונה", "למוד פשטא אוריתא", "שנה", "ראש השנה"], "node_degrees": {"פשטא אוריתא": 5, "אמונה": 4, "שנה": 4, "לילה": 3, "מאור הקטן": 3, "ירושלים": 2, "משא ומתן באמונה": 2, "למוד פשטא אוריתא": 2, "מלכות": 1, "בן ננס": 1, "העמדת המלך": 1, "ינוקא": 1, "מט\"ט": 1, "עוסק בדיני ממונות": 1, "ראש השנה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 35, "label": "אוֹר הַפָּנִים וְחִדּוּשׁ הַחָכְמָה", "label_en": "Light of the Face and Renewal of Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups concepts that all express the same essential reality: the divine light that flows from above to renew the sekhel/soul. אור הפנים (the light of the Face) is a bechina of חדוש המחין (renewal of the intellect), which is itself a bechina of חדוש הנשמה (renewal of the soul). בנין בית המקדש (building the Temple) is an aspect of this same light — the Talmud states 'whoever has da'at, it is as if the Temple was built in his days.' Merged into this package: חלק אלוה ממעל and חכמה (Component F), because the divine spark within every Jew IS chokhmah, and this chokhmah is precisely what gets renewed by the אור הפנים. These six nodes all inhabit the same essential domain: the illuminating, renewing, upper dimension of divine intellect that descends from above.", "dominant_node": "אור הפנים", "nodes": ["אור הפנים", "בנין בית המקדש", "חדוש המחין", "חדוש הנשמה", "חלק אלוה ממעל", "חכמה"], "node_degrees": {"אור הפנים": 2, "חדוש המחין": 2, "בנין בית המקדש": 1, "חדוש הנשמה": 1, "חלק אלוה ממעל": 1, "חכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חלק אלוה ממעל", "חכמה"], "reason": "This isolated pair (original Component F) shares essential nature with the אור הפנים cluster: חכמה is the divine substance that the Face's light renews, and חלק אלוה ממעל is simply חכמה seen from below. They are the same divine intellect described in two registers — cosmic and personal. Thematically, they belong to the 'illuminating/renewing source' domain, not the 'receptive vessel' domain of Package 1."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 35, "label": "חַשְׁמַ\"ל וּרְצוּעוֹת", "label_en": "Chasmal and the Straps — the Protective Garment", "polarity": "good", "reason": "חשמ\"ל (the luminous protective fire-speech of Ezekiel's chariot) is a bechina of 'דובר אמת בלבבו' (speaking truth in one's heart, the practice of Rav Safra) and simultaneously a bechina of רצועה (the leather strap of the tefillin). All three concepts share the same essential nature: they are the garment/enveloping of the faith. Just as the strap encircles and protects, and just as the chasmal is described as a luminous casing around the divine Chariot, so 'speaking truth in one's heart' is the behavioral act that generates this protective enveloping around the malkhut/faith. These three are three names for the same reality: the act and substance of holy protection that prevents the external forces from drawing sustenance from the inner sanctity of faith.", "dominant_node": "חשמ\"ל", "nodes": ["חשמ\"ל", "\"דובר אמת בלבבו\"", "רצועה"], "node_degrees": {"חשמ\"ל": 2, "\"דובר אמת בלבבו\"": 1, "רצועה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 35, "label": "הַקְטָרַת הַקְּטֹרֶת וְעֲלִיַּת הַנִּיצוֹצוֹת", "label_en": "The Ketoret Incense and the Elevation of Sparks", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes that express the same essential reality: the sacred act of elevating holy sparks from the domain of the nations/klipot back to holiness. 'כשאדם עוסק במשא ומתן באמונה' (when one engages in faithful commerce) is a bechina of 'הקרבת הקרבן תמיד והקטרת הקטרת' (the daily Tamid and Ketoret offerings). 'כריתות זרעו של עמלק' (cutting off Amalek's seed) is a bechina of 'אחד עשר סממני הקטרת' (the eleven spice-ingredients of the Ketoret). These four nodes share a single essential nature: they are all expressions of the mystical act of sorting and elevating the ניצוצות (holy sparks) embedded in the world of the nations, bringing them back to their holy source through acts of faithful service.", "dominant_node": "הקרבת הקרבן תמיד, והקטרת הקטרת", "nodes": ["כשאדם עוסק במשא ומתן באמונה", "הקרבת הקרבן תמיד, והקטרת הקטרת", "כריתות זרעו של עמלק", "אחד עשר סממני הקטרת"], "node_degrees": {"כשאדם עוסק במשא ומתן באמונה": 1, "הקרבת הקרבן תמיד, והקטרת הקטרת": 1, "כריתות זרעו של עמלק": 1, "אחד עשר סממני הקטרת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כריתות זרעו של עמלק", "אחד עשר סממני הקטרת"], "reason": "The original 2-node Component D (כריתות זרעו של עמלק ↔ אחד עשר סממני הקטרת) shares its essential nature with the 2-node Component E (faithful commerce = Korban Tamid). Both pairs are about the same cosmic act: the elevation of holy sparks from the klipot/nations via sacred Temple-equivalent service. The eleven spices of the Ketoret correspond to the eleven types of impure forces from which sparks must be extracted — identical in nature to the annihilation of Amalek's seed. These four nodes all inhabit the domain of 'sacred sorting and elevation of embedded holiness from the domain of the nations.'"}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 35, "label": "חָכְמוֹת חִיצוֹנִיּוֹת וְקָנֶה", "label_en": "External Wisdoms and the Reed — the Evil Counterfeit", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror package in Torah #35. External wisdoms (חכמות חיצוניות) are a bechina of the klipa-reed (קנה שבקליפות) — just as holy wisdom is called 'kaneh' ('קנה חכמה' — acquire wisdom), the counterfeit external wisdoms are also called kaneh, but the impure reed that is thrust into the sea of holy wisdom and displaces it. These two concepts share a single essential nature: they are the alien intellectual substance that invades and occupies the space of holy chokhmah, mirroring the holy kaneh but oriented toward nullification (the name Pharaoh = bitul/nullification). This is the direct evil counterpart of Package 2's divine wisdom.", "dominant_node": "חכמות חיצוניות", "nodes": ["חכמות חיצוניות", "קנה"], "node_degrees": {"חכמות חיצוניות": 1, "קנה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 36, "label": "מַלְכוּתָא דִּשְׁמַיָּא", "label_en": "Holy Kingdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of holy sovereignty — the divine feminine presence (Shekhinah) that receives and contains the holy. The 'woman who fears God' (Mishlei 31), the Tribes of Yah, the Mistress/Gevirah, and the Sea of Solomon are all expressions of the same receptive, holy, royal quality. They are contrasted in the Torah with the evil maidservant (Shifha Bisha).", "dominant_node": "מלכותא דשמיא", "nodes": ["מלכותא דשמיא", "\"אשה יראת ה\"", "שבטי יה", "גבירתא", "ים של שלמה"], "node_degrees": {"מלכותא דשמיא": 2, "\"אשה יראת ה\"": 2, "שבטי יה": 1, "גבירתא": 2, "ים של שלמה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 36, "label": "מֹחִין וְחָכְמָה", "label_en": "Divine Intellect and Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the upper sefirot / mochin — the intellect, light, and wisdom that flow from above. Abba/Chokhmah and Imma/Binah are the partzufim of the upper intellect; Yah is their divine name; Mochin is the direct term. Hitgalut/Leidah share this nature because in this Torah revelation of Torah IS the birth of the mochin — they are structurally identical. Tzeirei (the vowel of formed light) and the Kamatz/Or HaPashut (concealed light) are the vessel-and-light dynamic that governs how mochin are received — both are bechina expressions of the same light-transmission nature. The compound nodes Abba-ve-Imma and Chokhmah-u-Vinah bridge the B1/B2/B3 raw sub-components, which all belong to this single conceptual domain.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["אמא", "בינה", "אבא", "חכמה", "אבא ואמא", "חכמה ובינה", "י\"ה", "מחין", "שם יה", "התגלות", "לדה", "צירי", "נצטיר האור לפי בחינת הכלי", "אור הפשוט הבא מלעיל", "קמיץ וסתים", "קמץ"], "node_degrees": {"אמא": 1, "בינה": 1, "אבא": 1, "חכמה": 1, "אבא ואמא": 1, "חכמה ובינה": 2, "י\"ה": 2, "מחין": 2, "שם יה": 1, "התגלות": 1, "לדה": 1, "צירי": 1, "נצטיר האור לפי בחינת הכלי": 1, "אור הפשוט הבא מלעיל": 1, "קמיץ וסתים": 2, "קמץ": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אמא", "בינה"], "reason": "Raw component B1 (2 nodes) — Imma IS Binah; both are bechina of the same sefira. Merged into the mochin package because Binah/Imma are the second of the two upper sefirot explicitly called 'mochin' in this Torah (the Zohar phrase 'siluka de-Yesoda ad Abba ve-Imma' anchors both Abba/Chokhmah and Imma/Binah as mochin). Shared nature: the upper divine intellect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אבא", "חכמה"], "reason": "Raw component B2 (2 nodes) — Abba IS Chokhmah. Merged into the mochin package for the same reason as B1. The Torah explicitly states 'Abba ve-Imma, she-hem bechina Chokhmah u-Vinah.' Shared nature: upper divine intellect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התגלות", "לדה"], "reason": "Raw component C (2 nodes) — 'Hitgalut hi bechina Leidah' is an explicit bechina in this Torah. This dyad belongs with the mochin package because in Torah 36, revelation of Torah = birth of the mochin (the soul receives new mochin from the Tzaddik's Torah). Shared nature: the actualization of divine intellect descending into the world."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צירי", "נצטיר האור לפי בחינת הכלי"], "reason": "Raw component E (2 nodes) — Tzeirei = 'the light taking form according to the vessel.' This is the mechanism by which mochin/Or Pashut actualizes into a particular form. Belongs to the mochin package because the Torah uses this vowel-symbolism specifically to explain how divine light (chokhmah) is received through the kli. Shared nature: actualization and formation of divine light/intellect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אור הפשוט הבא מלעיל", "קמיץ וסתים", "קמץ"], "reason": "Raw component F (3 nodes) — The simple/undifferentiated light from above = Kamatz/concealed. This is the pre-formed state of divine light before it takes shape in a vessel. Belongs to the mochin package because the Torah's whole discussion of Kamatz-Tzeirei-blessing/curse is about how the Chokhmah-level light descends and is formed. Shared nature: the divine light of the upper intellect in its concealed, primordial state. Note: 'קמיץ וסתים' (E14) and 'קמץ וסתים' (E15) are variant spellings of the same concept; treated as one node."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 36, "label": "מַלְכוּת הָרְשָׁעָה", "label_en": "Evil Kingdom", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Both nodes share the essential nature of the evil feminine sovereignty — the dark side of malkhut, rooted in the spleen (an organ associated with black bile/melancholy in kabbalistic physiology) and manifesting as the kingdom of impurity that opposes the holy Malkhuta di-Shemaya. This is the exact evil mirror of Package 1.", "dominant_node": "מלכות הרשעה", "nodes": ["טחול", "מלכות הרשעה"], "node_degrees": {"טחול": 1, "מלכות הרשעה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 37, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ וְתוֹרָה", "label_en": "Soul and Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the holy divine soul (neshamah/nefesh) and its expressions in the world. נפש, the soul, is identified as a bechina of: אדם (the human being), תורה (Torah), אור (light), זכרון (memory), חיים (life), חכמה (wisdom), חכמות התורה (Torah wisdom), צורה (form/form of the human). These are not cause-and-effect relationships — they are all different names for the same essential quality: the divine, intellectual, luminous, life-giving principle in creation. The cluster extends naturally to: (a) צדקה דארץ ישראל — which the Torah explicitly equates with נפש ('tzedakat Eretz-Yisrael, bechina nefesh'); (b) חרב לה' — which is a bechina of הבל שאין בו חטא, which is itself a bechina of נפש; (c) the entire sub-cluster of הבל שאין בו חטא / אוירא דארץ ישראל / הבל פיהם של תינוקות / תינוקות / כרובים — all expressions of the pure, sinless breath of holiness, which is equated with soul-light. The merged Component 2 (צדקה / תענית / עולם הבא) is joined here because: the Torah explicitly states 'צדקה ותענית הם בחינה אחת' and 'צדקה רמז לאור' — sharing the essential nature of the light/soul domain. They are the same spiritual coin: the suppression of the body that allows the soul's light to prevail.", "dominant_node": "נפש", "nodes": ["נפש", "אדם", "תורה", "אור", "זכרון", "חיים", "חכמה", "חכמות התורה", "צדקה דארץ ישראל", "צורה", "חרב לה'", "הבל שאין בו חטא", "אוירא דארץ ישראל", "הבל פיהם של תינוקות", "תינוקות", "כרובים", "צדקה", "תענית", "עולם הבא"], "node_degrees": {"נפש": 9, "אדם": 2, "תורה": 2, "אור": 1, "זכרון": 1, "חיים": 1, "חכמה": 1, "חכמות התורה": 1, "צדקה דארץ ישראל": 2, "צורה": 1, "חרב לה'": 2, "הבל שאין בו חטא": 4, "אוירא דארץ ישראל": 1, "הבל פיהם של תינוקות": 1, "תינוקות": 2, "כרובים": 1, "צדקה": 1, "תענית": 2, "עולם הבא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["צדקה", "תענית", "עולם הבא"], "reason": "Raw Component 2 (3 nodes) — merged into Package 1 (Soul/Torah) on the basis of shared nature, not cause-effect. The Torah explicitly equates צדקה and תענית as a single bechina: 'צדקה ותענית הם בחינה אחת, כי אגרא דתעניתא צדקתא.' Furthermore, the Torah states 'צדקה רמז לאור' — charity is a symbol/bechina of אור (light), which is already in Package 1 via נפש—אור. תענית = עולם הבא (via the manna-metaphor: the closed mem = the world to come where there is no eating). All three share the essential nature of the holy, light-giving, soul-elevating domain. They do NOT belong in Package 2 (body/evil) even though fasting suppresses the body, because their intrinsic nature is positive and holy — the suppression is not their essence, their alignment with light is."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 37, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִלּוּי בֶּטֶן וְהַבְּחִירָה הָעַצְמִית", "label_en": "Belly-Filling and Self-Seeking Choice", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah's central structural category: two seemingly opposite orientations — seeking reward in this world and seeking reward in the next world — are revealed to share the same essential nature: self-oriented appetite. Both are called 'filling the belly' (מִלּוּי בֶּטֶן). The concept of 'portion in life' (חֶלְקָם בַּחַיִּים) and 'Your hidden store' (צְפוּנְךָ, i.e., world to come) are both declared to be aspects of belly-filling. This package represents any service of God that is motivated by personal gain rather than fulfilling God's will.", "dominant_node": "מילוי בטן", "nodes": ["מילוי בטן", "חלקם בחיים", "צפונך", "תאוות עולם הזה", "עולם הבא (כבחירה עצמית)"], "node_degrees": {"מילוי בטן": 4, "חלקם בחיים": 2, "צפונך": 2, "תאוות עולם הזה": 1, "עולם הבא (כבחירה עצמית)": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חלקם בחיים", "צפונך"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly states that both 'חֶלְקָם בַּחַיִּים' (this-world seekers) and 'צְפוּנְךָ' (next-world seekers) are two faces of the same reality — מִלּוּי בֶּטֶן. They are not cause-and-effect but two manifestations of the same self-oriented nature, and therefore belong in the same package."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 37, "label": "גּוּף וְחֹשֶׁךְ", "label_en": "Body and Darkness", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the material body, physicality, and the opposing force against the divine soul. גוף (the body) is the hub: it is a bechina of בהמה (the animal), חמר (matter/clay), חשך (darkness), מיתה (death), סכלות (folly), שכחה (forgetting), and חכמות חיצוניות (external/foreign wisdoms). These are not different names for different things — they are all the same essential quality: the material-physical dimension of existence that opposes light, wisdom, memory, and life. The Torah states this explicitly in section ב: 'גוף ונפש הם בחינת אדם ובהמה, חמר וצורה, חכמה וסכלות, בחינת אור וחשך... בחינות שכחה וזכרון.' Each member of this cluster is a face of the same anti-divine, death-oriented, forgetting quality.", "dominant_node": "גוף", "nodes": ["גוף", "בהמה", "חכמות חיצוניות", "חמר", "חשך", "מיתה", "סכלות", "שכחה"], "node_degrees": {"גוף": 7, "בהמה": 1, "חכמות חיצוניות": 1, "חמר": 1, "חשך": 1, "מיתה": 1, "סכלות": 1, "שכחה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 37, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מוֹתָרוֹת וְטִנּוּף", "label_en": "Surplus, Waste, and Filth", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Money accumulated through worldly desire is called מוֹתָרוֹת (surplus/excess) — what is left over after all appetites are satisfied. Children born outside of holiness are also called מוֹתָרוֹת, because they come from the overflow of the brain (שכבת זרע) that exits as a rotten drop (טִפָּה סְרוּחָה). The word עוֹלְלִים (infants/children) is exegetically linked to טִינוּף (filth) via Rashi on Psalm 8. Money and unholy children share the same essential nature: both are the surplus remainder of appetite-driven existence.", "dominant_node": "מותרות", "nodes": ["מותרות", "מנון (ממון תאווה)", "בנים שאינם נולדים בקדושה", "טיפה סרוחה", "עוללים", "טינוף"], "node_degrees": {"מותרות": 4, "מנון (ממון תאווה)": 2, "בנים שאינם נולדים בקדושה": 2, "טיפה סרוחה": 1, "עוללים": 2, "טינוף": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מותרות", "טיפה סרוחה"], "reason": "The text explicitly states that unholy children are מוֹתָרוֹת in the literal sense — they arise from the brain-overflow (סִרְחוֹן וַעֲכִירַת הַמֹּחַ) as a rotten drop (טִפָּה סְרוּחָה), making טִפָּה סְרוּחָה and מוֹתָרוֹת two expressions of the same surplus/waste reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עוללים", "טינוף"], "reason": "The text cites Rashi: 'עוֹלֵל פֵּרֵשׁ רַשִׁ\"י: טִינוּף וְלִכְלוּךְ' — the word עוֹלְלִים is defined as an aspect (בְּחִינַת) of filth, making these two the same essential concept."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 37, "label": "מַנְצְפַּ\"ךְ וְרִי\"ו דִּין", "label_en": "The Five Final Letters and the Din-Number", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The three nodes share the essential nature of the five strict judgments (dinim) as embodied in the blood-letters. מנצפך (the five final letters of the Hebrew alphabet) = ורידים (veins/blood vessels) = רי\"ו דין (the numerical value 216, associated with strict judgment/gevurot). All three are bechina expressions of the same underlying reality: the five blood-types in the body correspond to the five final letters, which numerically encode the force of strict din. The Torah text states: 'מנצפך הוא בחינת ורידים, בחינת ריו דין.' This is a dimension of strict judgment that — in the teaching's positive direction — is sweetened by the holy shochet to become Adonai (speech/Shekhinah). The polarity is marked evil because ריו דין represents unmitigated strict judgment; however, this component is the raw material that can be sweetened — it is the evil/judgment side that the teaching seeks to transform.", "dominant_node": "ורידים", "nodes": ["מנצפך", "ורידים", "רי\"ו דין"], "node_degrees": {"מנצפך": 1, "ורידים": 2, "רי\"ו דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 37, "volume": "lm2", "label": "צֶלֶם דְּמוּת תַּבְנִיתוֹ", "label_en": "God's Image and Holy Children", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Holy children (בָּנִים דִּקְדֻשָּׁה) are explicitly described as a bechina of 'צֶלֶם דְּמוּת תַּבְנִיתוֹ' — God's image and form. When a child is born in holiness, a divine image enters the world. This is the positive mirror of Package 2: where unholy children are surplus and filth, holy children are the completion and manifestation of God's image. The term 'תְּמוּנָתֶךָ' (Your image/form) appearing in the verse is identified as the holy dimension of children.", "dominant_node": "צלם דמות תבניתו", "nodes": ["צלם דמות תבניתו", "בנים דקדושה", "תמונתך"], "node_degrees": {"צלם דמות תבניתו": 3, "בנים דקדושה": 2, "תמונתך": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 37, "label": "דִּבּוּר וּשְׁכִינָה", "label_en": "Holy Speech and the Shekhinah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes share the essential nature of the divine receptive speech — the Shekhinah as the mouth of creation. דבור (speech) is a bechina of שכינה (the divine Presence) because 'מלכות — פה' (the Shekhinah is the mouth of divinity). דבור is also a bechina of חרב פיפיות (the double-edged sword) because speech is the instrument of divine word-as-sword: 'ורוממות אל בגרונם וחרב פיפיות.' All three — holy speech, the Shekhinah, and the sword-of-the-mouth — are the same essential reality: the divine receptive channel through which souls ascend as mayin nukvin and through which sustenance flows to Israel. This package is the good mirror of Package 3: Package 3 (מנצפך/ורידים/ריו דין) is the raw din-blood that needs to be sweetened, and Package 4 (דבור/שכינה) is what it becomes when sweetened — 'כי מריו נעשה צורת א, ונעשה אדני' — the din is transformed into Adonai = speech = Shekhinah.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["דבור", "חרב פיפיות", "שכינה"], "node_degrees": {"דבור": 2, "חרב פיפיות": 1, "שכינה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 37, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חֲזוֹת פְּנֵי ה' וְהַכָּרָה", "label_en": "Beholding God's Face and Divine Knowledge", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package represents the highest goal of human service — not reward in this world or the next, but to know and recognize the Creator. David's declaration 'אֲנִי בְּצֶדֶק אֶחֱזֶה פָנֶיךָ' frames the entire Torah. The concepts of 'seeing God's face' (חֲזוֹת פְּנֵי ה') and 'knowing/recognizing God' (הַכָּרַת הַבּוֹרֵא / דַּעַת ה') share the same essential nature — they are different expressions of the direct apprehension of God. Righteousness/justice (צֶדֶק) is described as the path-quality through which one attains this vision, making it part of the same domain.", "dominant_node": "חזות פני ה'", "nodes": ["חזות פני ה'", "הכרת הבורא", "דעת ה'", "צדק"], "node_degrees": {"חזות פני ה'": 3, "הכרת הבורא": 2, "דעת ה'": 2, "צדק": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 37, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִמְתִים מֵחֶלֶד — מוֹת טִבְעִי", "label_en": "Natural Death and Children of This World", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The text distinguishes two modes of death: 'מִמְתִים יָדְךָ ה'' (those whose death is decreed by God before their time, as punishment) and 'מִמְתִים מֵחֶלֶד' (those who live out their natural lifespan by natural moisture/vitality until it expires). The latter are declared to be 'בְּנֵי עוֹלָם הַזֶּה' — children of this world who have no share in the world to come. 'לַחוּת שָׁרְשִׁיִּית' (root/natural moisture) is the physiological bechina of natural lifespan. These concepts share the nature of the purely natural-temporal existence that runs its course and ends.", "dominant_node": "מיתה טבעית", "nodes": ["מיתה טבעית", "מיתה מחלד", "בני עולם הזה", "לחות שרשית"], "node_degrees": {"מיתה טבעית": 3, "מיתה מחלד": 2, "בני עולם הזה": 2, "לחות שרשית": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "תְּפִלִּין וּבוּשָׁה", "label_en": "Tefillin and Shame", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: they are different expressions of the luminous quality that appears on the forehead — the revealed 'or ha-panim' that is simultaneously the radiance of tefillin, the blushing of shame before God, the Divine Name Adoshem radiating outward, and the quality of awe/yir'ah. The Torah equates them explicitly: 'this shame is a bechina of the light of tefillin'; 'yir'at Hashem — this is tefillin'; 'bushah — bechina of tefillin'; 'teshuvah brings tefillin which is keron or ha-panim'. They all share the single quality of the face that reveals inward connection to God.", "dominant_node": "תפלין", "nodes": ["תפלין", "בושה", "\"יראת ה\"", "מתביש ביותר, ונתגלה הבושה על פניו", "אמא על ברא", "עץ החיים", "קרון עור הפנים", "שבת"], "node_degrees": {"תפלין": 5, "בושה": 2, "\"יראת ה\"": 1, "מתביש ביותר, ונתגלה הבושה על פניו": 1, "אמא על ברא": 1, "עץ החיים": 1, "קרון עור הפנים": 1, "שבת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"יראת ה\""], "reason": "Edge id=3202 equates yir'at Hashem directly with tefillin via the Gemara quote in Berakhot 6. Yir'ah IS bushah (Nedarim 20), and both are tefillin. Single node joins the large component."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מתביש ביותר, ונתגלה הבושה על פניו"], "reason": "Edge id=167 equates this state directly with or ha-tefillin. This is the activated form of bushah — they share the same essential nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אמא על ברא"], "reason": "Edge id=287 equates tefillin with imma al bera. In the Torah's kabbalistic framework, tefillin ARE the expression of binah-imma shining on the son. Same essential nature as tefillin."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עץ החיים"], "reason": "Edge id=2419 equates tefillin with the Tree of Life (Yeshayah 38:16). Tefillin = life = etz ha-chayyim. Joins the tefillin component."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קרון עור הפנים"], "reason": "Edge id=2646 equates tefillin directly with the radiance of the skin of the face. Same concept: tefillin as the shining forehead-light."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שבת"], "reason": "Edge id=508 equates Shabbat with bushah. Since bushah = tefillin in this Torah, Shabbat shares this essential nature. Shabbat is the cessation from the 39 melakhot (zuhmat ha-nachash) which is precisely the quality of holy speech and tefillin that the Torah develops."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 38, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מֹחִין דְּגַדְלוּת וְהַמְתָּקָה", "label_en": "Expanded Consciousness and Sweetening", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes share the same essential nature: the state of expanded, luminous consciousness (מֹחִין דְּגַדְלוּת) IS the sweetening (הַמְתָּקָה). When the great Tzaddik illuminates the small one and elevates him from smallness (מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת) to greatness (מֹחִין דְּגַדְלוּת), that very arrival at expanded consciousness is itself the sweetening — they are two names for the same transformative reality. The Rebbe uses the explicit formula 'שֶׁזֶּהוּ בְּחִינַת הַמְתָּקָה' to make the identity.", "dominant_node": "שיבוא למחין דגדלות", "nodes": ["שיבוא למחין דגדלות", "המתקה"], "node_degrees": {"שיבוא למחין דגדלות": 1, "המתקה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "דִּבּוּר, יָם וּמַלְכוּת פֶּה", "label_en": "Speech, Sea, and Malkhut-Mouth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature as the holy vessel of speech — malkhut, the mouth, the sea that receives all rivers, the Name Adoshem (which is the mouth of speech). Rua'h piv shel ha-Kadosh Barukh Hu, Yam, Malkhut-Peh, and Adoshem are all different faces of the same Divine-human speech dimension. The Torah states them as direct bechinot of each other.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["דבור", "ים", "מלכות פה", "אדני", "רוח פיו של הקדוש ברוך הוא"], "node_degrees": {"דבור": 4, "ים": 2, "מלכות פה": 1, "אדני": 1, "רוח פיו של הקדוש ברוך הוא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ים", "מלכות פה"], "reason": "Edge id=1356 equates malkhut-peh with yam. Edge id=659 equates yam with dibur. These three form a tight triangle: malkhut-peh = yam = dibur."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אדני"], "reason": "Edge id=130 equates dibur with Adoshem (the Name associated with speech and the mouth). All share the same domain: the holy vessel of divine speech."}, {"merged_nodes": ["רוח פיו של הקדוש ברוך הוא"], "reason": "Edge id=660 equates rua'h piv shel HaKadosh Barukh Hu with dibur. They are the same reality from different perspectives."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "קֵץ כָּל בָּשָׂר וְרוּחַ סְעָרָה", "label_en": "The End of All Flesh and the Storm Wind", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature as the evil accusing force — the serpent's pollution, the storm wind, the accuser that sucks from speech when it finds an opening. The Torah equates them explicitly and repeatedly: ketz kol basar IS rua'h se'arah, IS achar ha-devarim, IS zuhmat ha-nachash. They are all different names for the same demonic force that opposes holy speech.", "dominant_node": "קץ כל בשר", "nodes": ["קץ כל בשר", "רוח סערה", "אחר הדברים", "זהמת הנחש", "סוף"], "node_degrees": {"קץ כל בשר": 5, "רוח סערה": 3, "אחר הדברים": 1, "זהמת הנחש": 1, "סוף": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["רוח סערה"], "reason": "Edges id=2642 and id=2713 establish bidirectional bechina: ketz kol basar = rua'h se'arah. These are explicitly equated in the Torah text."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אחר הדברים"], "reason": "Edge id=211 equates ketz kol basar with achar ha-devarim — the accuser after speech. Same essential nature as the evil sucking force."}, {"merged_nodes": ["זהמת הנחש"], "reason": "Edge id=2640 equates zuhmat ha-nachash with ketz kol basar. The serpent's pollution IS the end-of-all-flesh force. Same component."}, {"merged_nodes": ["סוף"], "reason": "Edge id=2641 equates suf (end/sea of reeds) with ketz kol basar. The 'end' hidden in yam suf = the same evil force. Same essential nature."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "גְּבוּרוֹת וּמוֹצָאוֹת הַפֶּה", "label_en": "Gevurot and the Mouth's Articulations", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The five gevurot, the five fingers of the left hand (zerov'a), and the five articulations of the mouth share the same essential nature: they are all the sefiratic dimension of gevurah as it manifests in speech. The Torah identifies them as bechinot of each other — the arm, the fingers, and the mouth-sounds are all the same gevurah-force expressing itself.", "dominant_node": "חמשה גבורות", "nodes": ["חמשה גבורות", "חמשת מוצאות הפה", "חמש אצבעין שביד שמאל"], "node_degrees": {"חמשה גבורות": 2, "חמשת מוצאות הפה": 1, "חמש אצבעין שביד שמאל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "גְּוָנִין וְאֵשׁ וּמַיִם", "label_en": "Colors, Fire, and Water", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Ash u-mayim (fire and water = shamayim) and gvanim (colors) share the same essential nature in this Torah. The blushing of shame produces multiple colors on the face; shamayim = esh u-mayim = the spectrum of gvanim. They are the visual-material expression of the glory (tiferet/pe'er) that is tefillin. The Torah in section 5 (pe'er) explicitly links them.", "dominant_node": "גונין", "nodes": ["גונין", "אש ומים"], "node_degrees": {"גונין": 1, "אש ומים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "לַיְלָה, עֲנִיּוּת וְצַר", "label_en": "Night, Poverty, and Distress", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Layla, aniyyut u-dachakut, and tzar share the same essential nature in this Torah: they all denote the state of constriction and darkness that is the time when ketz kol basar has dominion. The Torah calls study in distress 'bechina of layla.' These are not evil per se — they are the neutral condition of darkness/night that is the testing ground where the evil force rules, and within which holy study (Torah she-bi-leil) operates.", "dominant_node": "לילה", "nodes": ["לילה", "עניות ובדחקות", "צר"], "node_degrees": {"לילה": 2, "עניות ובדחקות": 1, "צר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 38, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְעַמּוּדִים", "label_en": "Prayer and Pillars", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Tefilah and amudim share the same essential nature in this Torah: the pillars upon which the earth stands (from Chagigah 12b) are called tefilah in the Torah's exposition of the cosmic stacking structure (the earth on pillars, pillars on water, water on mountains, mountains on ruach, ruach on se'arah, se'arah on the arm of God). The prayer-pillar is the holy counterforce to the storm-wind.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "עמודים"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "עמודים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 39, "label": "עָרְלָה וּמַחֲלֹקֶת", "label_en": "The Foreskin-Husk and the Conflict", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All four nodes — ערלה (the impurity husk encircling the covenant), חרפה (shame/disgrace), רעב (hunger/famine), and מחלקת (strife/conflict) — are declared by the Rebbe to be bechinas of one another in a single chain: 'וְרָעָב הַזֶּה, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת מַחֲלֹקֶת, בְּחִינַת חֶרְפָּה, הוּא בְּחִינַת עָרְלָה' (source line 6). They are four faces of the same essential reality: the anti-shalom force that blocks the ברית שלום (covenant of peace) and produces spiritual and physical lack. The two raw bechina edges from the dataset (ערלה–חרפה and רעב–מחלקת) form 2 separate components of 2 nodes each only because the cross-linking edges (e.g. מחלקת–חרפה and רעב–ערלה, also explicitly stated in the same proof text) have not yet been entered into the dataset. The text equates all four in one continuous bechina statement, making this a single coherent domain: the multi-form evil that surrounds and suppresses the covenant.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "רעב", "חרפה", "ערלה"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 1, "רעב": 1, "חרפה": 1, "ערלה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["רעב", "מחלקת"], "reason": "Raw CC2 from dataset edge B2 (id=1765, reference=39, bechina). These 2 nodes form their own connected component in the dataset graph. Merged into main package because the source text proof for B1 (id=1232) explicitly equates ALL four nodes — ערלה, חרפה, רעב, מחלקת — in one bechina chain: 'וְרָעָב הַזֶּה, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת מַחֲלֹקֶת, בְּחִינַת חֶרְפָּה, הוּא בְּחִינַת עָרְלָה'. The missing cross-linking edges (מחלקת–חרפה, רעב–ערלה) exist in the source text but are not yet in the dataset. Merge basis: SHARED NATURE (bechina), not cause-effect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ערלה", "חרפה"], "reason": "Raw CC1 from dataset edge B1 (id=1232, reference=39, bechina). These 2 nodes form their own connected component in the dataset graph. The same bechina chain that equates them also equates them with מחלקת and רעב, justifying merger. In this Torah, ערלה is identified as 'שָׁלֹשׁ קְלִפּוֹת הַמְסַבְּבִים הַבְּרִית שָׁלוֹם' — the three husks encircling the covenant of peace. חרפה is its social manifestation (shame delivered by antagonists). Both are expressions of the same evil domain."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 39, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תַּכְלִית וְעוֹלַם הַנְּשָׁמוֹת", "label_en": "Ultimate Purpose and the World of Souls", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts are all different names/faces of the same metaphysical reality: the ultimate destination for which all of creation was made. The Rebbe explicitly equates תכלית (ultimate purpose), שמות (the acronym for תכלית מעשה שמים וארץ, read as 'שמות' = souls), עולם הנשמות (world of souls), and שבת (the seventh day / eternal rest). They are not cause-and-effect — each is a bechina of the same thing: the endpoint, the goal, the culmination of all existence. Shabbat is the tachlit of creation; the world of souls is what Shabbat embodies; 'שמות' is how the tachlit is encoded in the verse; they are all one shared essential nature.", "dominant_node": "תכלית", "nodes": ["תכלית", "שמות"], "node_degrees": {"תכלית": 1, "שמות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עולם הנשמות", "שבת"], "reason": "The source text explicitly states 'שַׁבָּת הוּא בְּחִינַת עוֹלָם הַנְּשָׁמוֹת' and 'שַׁבָּת...שֶׁהוּא הַתַּכְלִית'. Both concepts are bechina-equated with תכלית throughout the Torah. They belong to the same domain: the ultimate destination. These nodes are not in the dataset but appear in the source text — flagged in additions for entry. This merge note documents the thematic unity that should inform future data entry."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 39, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רַגְלִין וּבְחִינַת הַשָּׁפָל", "label_en": "Feet / The Lower Aspect", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Rebbe uses 'רגלין' (feet/legs) as a structural metaphor for those who are far from the מוח (intellect/brain) — ordinary people at the lowest rung of spiritual attainment. 'לכו' in the verse is explicitly called 'בחינת הרגלין' because feet are the instruments of walking (כלי ההליכה). This cluster is neutral: the רגלין are neither inherently holy nor evil — they are a structural/anatomical metaphor for distance from the source of intellect. The Torah's entire teaching is that even these distant ones can attain the tachlit through the great leader.", "dominant_node": "רגלין", "nodes": ["רגלין"], "node_degrees": {"רגלין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כלי ההליכה", "לכו"], "reason": "The source text says 'לְכוּ דַּיְקָא, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת הָרַגְלִין, שֶׁהֵם כְּלֵי הַהֲלִיכָה'. These three expressions are all bechinas of the same thing: the aspect of being at the lowest/most distant point from the intellect. 'לכו' (the imperative 'go!') in the verse, 'רגלין' (feet), and 'כלי ההליכה' (instruments of walking) are treated as identical. These nodes are not currently in the dataset — flagged in additions."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 39, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַנְהִיג וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "The True Leader / Moses", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes a cluster of concepts that all share one essential nature: the true leader (מנהיג) who has the power to illuminate even the lowest (רגלין) with the highest da'at (knowledge/intellect). Moses is the paradigmatic example; the 'faithful shepherd' (רועה נאמן) is the same concept. The 'מוח' (intellect/brain) that the leader draws downward is the defining power of this cluster. These are not cause-and-effect — the minhag, Moshe, and the ro'eh ne'eman are all bechinas of the same spiritual type: the one whose spiritual level is so great that he can extend da'at to the furthest reaches.", "dominant_node": "מנהיג", "nodes": ["מנהיג"], "node_degrees": {"מנהיג": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["רועה נאמן", "משה רבנו"], "reason": "The Rebbe uses all three interchangeably: 'מַנְהִיג הַדּוֹר, רוֹעֶה נֶאֱמָן' and then points to Moshe as the exemplar. They share the same essential nature — the towering leader whose greatness illuminates the lowest. These are bechina-equivalences (same type, not cause and effect). Flagged in additions for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 40, "label": "נְסִיעָה וְעֲבוֹדָה זָרָה", "label_en": "Travel and Idolatry — Corruption of Faith", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All four nodes share the same essential nature: the domain of spiritual displacement rooted in loss of faith. In Torah #40, the Rebbe teaches that all travel (נסיעה וטלטול) IS corruption of faith (קלקול האמונה), and corruption of faith IS a bechina of idolatry (עבודת אלילים/עבודה זרה). The word 'צא' (go out/travel) in Isaiah 30:22 is explicitly cited in the context of idolatry and IS a bechina of travel and displacement. Thus: צא = נסיעה וטלטול = קלקול האמונה = עבודה זרה. These are not four different things linked by cause-and-effect — they are four facets of the same corrupted spiritual state: the state of a person who does not believe that God can provide everything he needs, and therefore leaves his place to seek his needs elsewhere. This is, in essence, what idolatry is: attributing one's provision to something other than God.", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה", "nodes": ["צא", "נסיעה וטלטול", "קלקול האמונה", "עבודה זרה"], "node_degrees": {"צא": 1, "נסיעה וטלטול": 1, "קלקול האמונה": 1, "עבודה זרה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["צא", "נסיעה וטלטול"], "reason": "Raw Component A — these two nodes are explicitly linked by bechina edge 2106: 'צא זה בחינת נסיעה וטלטול'. Both are expressions of the concept of displacement/going-out."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קלקול האמונה", "עבודה זרה"], "reason": "Raw Component B — linked by bechina edge 2328: 'קלקול האמונה, היינו בחינת עבודה זרה'. Both express the same corrupted spiritual state."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צא / נסיעה וטלטול", "קלקול האמונה / עבודה זרה"], "reason": "Cross-component merge: The source text of Torah #40 explicitly unifies both components through a bechina chain — 'כל הנסיעות של אדם, הוא בשביל קלקול האמונה, היינו בחינת עבודת אלילים'. Travel (Component A) IS corruption of faith IS idolatry (Component B). Furthermore, the word 'צא' is cited from a verse explicitly about idolatry (Isaiah 30:22), making 'צא' a direct linguistic token of the idolatry domain. All four nodes are different expressions of the same underlying reality: the state of displaced-from-God spiritual existence. Shared nature, not cause-and-effect, justifies this merge."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 40, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חָכְמָה וְעֵינַיִם וּתְפִלִּין", "label_en": "Wisdom, Eyes, and Tefillin", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the same essential nature: the quality of divine wisdom (Chokhmah) and its structural vehicles. 'Eyes' are named for Chokhmah because the unfolding of wisdom is called 'eyes' (Rashi on Gen. 3:7). 'Merachkim' (distant things) is another name for Chokhmah ('amarti echkama vehi rechoka', Kohelet 7:23). Tefillin are called 'peer' (glory) and the Torah states they are the bechina of mochin (intellect/mind). Mochin is itself a synonym for Chokhmah in the kabbalistic framework. All these concepts — the unfolding of wisdom, the eyes through which it manifests, and the tefillin/mochin that are its structural expression — form one thematic family: the domain of holy wisdom as divine oversight and intellectual illumination.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["חכמה", "מרחקים", "התפתחות החכמה", "עינים", "תפלין", "מחין"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה": 1, "מרחקים": 1, "התפתחות החכמה": 1, "עינים": 1, "תפלין": 1, "מחין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חכמה", "מרחקים"], "reason": "Raw component A: Chokhmah and Merachkim are two names for the same thing — wisdom is explicitly called 'distant' (rechoka) in Kohelet 7:23. They share the same essence."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התפתחות החכמה", "עינים"], "reason": "Raw component B: The unfolding/blossoming of Chokhmah is explicitly called 'eyes' by Rashi on 'vatipakachna einei shnehem' (Gen. 3:7). Eyes are the bechina of the opening of wisdom. Same essential nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תפלין", "מחין"], "reason": "Raw component C: Tefillin are the bechina of mochin (mind/intellect), as stated directly in the Torah text. Mochin is the kabbalistic term for Chokhmah-Binah-Daat — the same domain as wisdom. Both components A and B describe the nature of Chokhmah from different angles (its name, its structural expression), and component C (mochin = the intellectual vessels) belongs to the same family. Merged here because mochin shares the essential nature of divine wisdom, not because tefillin produce mochin (which would be eitza)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חכמה", "התפתחות החכמה", "עינים", "מרחקים", "תפלין", "מחין"], "reason": "All three raw components (Chokhmah/Merachkim; Hitpatchut HaChokhmah/Einayim; Tefillin/Mochin) are merged into one package because they all describe aspects of the same essential quality: divine wisdom and its structural embodiments. The Torah's central thesis is that wisdom = eyes = tefillin/mochin. These are three facets of one reality, not three separate packages."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 40, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מֶלֶךְ וְצַדִּיק", "label_en": "King and Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The king and the tzaddik are two names for the same spiritual figure in this Torah. The verse 'melech beyafyo techezena einecha' (Isaiah 33:17) is interpreted as referring to the true tzaddik in his beauty and glory. Both 'melech' (king) and 'tzaddik' describe the holy leader — the one whose role is to bring Israel close to their Father in Heaven, and whose beauty at the gathering (kibbutz) constitutes the divine Hitpaarut. They share the same essential nature: the holy leader who is himself the Hitpaarut of God among His people.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["מלך", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"מלך": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 41, "label": "בִּינָה וְיַיִן הַמְשַׂמֵּחַ", "label_en": "Binah and the Joyful Wine — The Holy Root of Sweetening", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five nodes share the essential nature of the holy upper source that sweetens all judgments. Binah (the upper mother, source of all gevurot) IS the root of firstborn blessing (שרש הבכורה), which IS the wine of joy (יין המשמח), which IS the root of gevurot (שרש הגבורות), and wine (יין) IS Binah — these are five different names for the same cosmic reality: the holy source of joyful power that, when drawn down into the lower limbs, expels the external forces. The Torah establishes this entire cluster as a single domain: the holy intoxication (התלהבות שבקדושה, which is יין המשמח) that is the antidote to judgment. Binah is the dominant node because it is explicitly named as the definition of this entire cluster: 'יין, זה בחינת בינה, שרש הבכורה, יין המשמח' (line 16), making Binah the hub to which יין, שרש הבכורה, and יין המשמח all directly attach, plus שרש הגבורות chains through יין המשמח.", "dominant_node": "בינה", "nodes": ["יין", "בינה", "שרש הבכורה", "יין המשמח", "שרש הגבורות"], "node_degrees": {"יין": 1, "בינה": 2, "שרש הבכורה": 2, "יין המשמח": 2, "שרש הגבורות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 41, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מוֹפְתִים וּמִשְׁפְּטֵי פֶּה", "label_en": "Miracles and Mouth-Rulings of the Posek", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah turns on a single bechina: the miracles performed through halachic decisors (ba'alei posekim) and the divine 'judgments of His mouth' (מִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ) are two expressions of the same thing. The Rebbe quotes I Chronicles 16:12 — 'מֹפְתָיו וּמִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ' — and uses 'בִּבְחִינַת' to equate the posek's rulings with divine wonders. It is not that rulings cause miracles (eitza) — rather, the posek's ruling IS the miracle, because his accepted authority makes them one and the same act. 'מוֹפְתִים' and 'מִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ' share the same essential nature: the divine will expressed through the authoritative mouth. Similarly, the miracles of the Geonim are a bechina of the same phenomenon — 'וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינַת הַמּוֹפְתִים שֶׁמְּסַפְּרִין מֵהַגְּאוֹנִים'. All three — מוֹפְתִים, מִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ, and מוֹפְתֵי הַגְּאוֹנִים — are different expressions of the one domain: divine power manifesting through the authoritative halachic voice.", "dominant_node": "מוֹפְתִים", "nodes": [], "node_degrees": {}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מוֹפְתִים", "מִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ", "מוֹפְתֵי הַגְּאוֹנִים", "בַּעֲלֵי הַפּוֹסְקִים"], "reason": "None of these nodes are currently in the dataset (torahData.js has no entries for reference 1041). The Rebbe explicitly uses 'בִּבְחִינַת' twice: first equating the posek's miracle-working with 'מֹפְתָיו וּמִשְׁפְּטֵי פִיהוּ', and then calling the Geonim's miracles 'בְּחִינַת' of this same phenomenon. These four concepts are bechina-equivalent faces of one domain: divine miracle as halachic ruling. They should all be entered as nodes with bechina edges between them. Flagged in additions.json for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 41, "label": "רַגְלִין וְעַמּוּדִים", "label_en": "Legs and Pillars — The Lower Domain of Judgment and Scholars", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All six nodes share the essential nature of 'the lower limbs' — the domain of Netzach and Hod in kabbalistic anatomy, which the Torah identifies as the site of cosmic conflict: the place where judgment takes hold and from which it must be sweetened by drawing Binah down into it. עמודים (pillars = mammon = legs per Deuteronomy 11:6), רגלים (legs = the lower sefirot), עקבים (heels = gematria of 2x Elohim = judgment), דין (judgment itself), תלמידי חכמים (called 'legs and pillars' in Midrash — 'שוקיו עמודי שש'), and נצח הוד (the kabbalistic sefirot that ARE the knees/legs per Etz Chaim) — all are different expressions of the same cosmic locus. The package is classified as neutral (not purely good or evil) because it is the contested arena: the heels carry judgment and give the external forces a foothold, yet the Torah scholars who fill this domain are holy pillars. This is the lower terrain that needs to be sanctified — not inherently evil but the battleground where the sweetening must occur. Package 2 connects to Package 1 via eitza: drawing Binah's wine DOWN into the legs (Package 2's domain) is the practical act (dancing, pidyon) that resolves the problem.", "dominant_node": "נצח הוד", "nodes": ["עמודים", "רגלים", "עקבים", "דין", "תלמידי חכמים", "נצח הוד"], "node_degrees": {"עמודים": 1, "רגלים": 2, "עקבים": 2, "דין": 1, "תלמידי חכמים": 1, "נצח הוד": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תלמידי חכמים", "נצח הוד"], "reason": "Raw Component C ({תלמידי חכמים, נצח הוד}, edge ID 2185) is a 2-node component that must merge by shared nature into the larger Component B. The source text of Torah #41 explicitly states: 'תלמידי חכמים נקראים נצח הוד, רגלין' (line 21) — Torah scholars ARE Netzach-Hod, ARE the legs. This is direct bechina language ('נקראים') equating נצח הוד with רגלין — precisely the node that anchors Component B's chain (עמודים — רגלים — עקבים — דין). The merge is by shared nature: נצח הוד and רגלים are the same cosmic reality (the lower sefirot/limbs domain), not two things causally related. This is confirmed by the Etz Chaim citation that opens the Torah itself: 'הברכים, הינו הרגלין, הם נצח הוד' — a direct bechina statement. Component C belongs in Package 2, not Package 1, because תלמידי חכמים / נצח הוד share the LOCATION (legs/lower domain) with Component B — they are part of the contested terrain — not the upper holy source (Package 1)."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 42, "label": "קֶשֶׁת וְצַדִּיק — The Bow and the Tzaddik", "label_en": "The Rainbow, the Tzaddik, the Shekhinah, and the Patriarchs as Garments of Light", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina edge in the dataset (צדיק ↔ קשת) captures the axial identity of Torah #42: the tzaddik IS the bow (rainbow). This identity is sourced from the Talmud (Ketubot 77b: Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai said to Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi, 'Has a rainbow appeared in your days?' — meaning: a tzaddik of your level). The Zohar (Pinchas 215) then unfolds the bow-nature: the bow IS the Shekhinah, and the three colors of the bow ARE the Patriarchs, who ARE the luminous garments of the Shekhinah. Since the tzaddik = the bow = the Shekhinah = the three colors = the Patriarchs, all these identities share a single essential nature in this Torah: they are all different facets of the divine feminine presence clothed in the light of the righteous. Furthermore: the letters of prayer ARE the Shekhinah (Psalms 51: 'Adonai sefatai tiftach' — speech is the name Adonai = the Shekhinah), and the bow IS called prayer (Rashi, Gen. 48: 'be-charbi u-ve-kashti' = prayer and supplication). This makes the complete bechina ring: צדיק = קשת = שכינה = אותיות התפלה. The three colors of the bow = האבות = קול הנגינה (the voice of melody contains fire/water/wind = the three Patriarchs). The name שדי = the Patriarchs (three branches of the letter shin = three divine names of the Shema). Teki'ah-shevarim-teru'ah = the Patriarchs = keshet (the shofar-blasts' mnemonic is the word 'keshet'). All of these are good-domain concepts: the holy bow/tzaddik, the Shekhinah, the Patriarchs, prayer, melody. There is no evil counterpart package in Torah #42 — the teaching describes a positive mechanism (sweetening the judgments) without naming an evil bechina cluster. The dominant node is קשת, which connects to צדיק (dataset), שכינה, אותיות התפלה, תלת גוונין, and האבות (all from source text).", "dominant_node": "קשת", "nodes": ["צדיק", "קשת"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 1, "קשת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["צדיק", "קשת"], "reason": "Raw Component 1 — these two nodes are directly linked by bechina edge 2651: 'הצדיק הוא בחינת קשת' (the tzaddik IS a bechina of the bow). This is the only dataset bechina edge for Torah #42. The component is the nucleus of Package 1 and is not merged with any other component (there are no other components)."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 43, "label": "דַּעַת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "Holy Da'at and Moshe", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central good cluster of Torah #43 is da'at of holiness, embodied by Moshe Rabbeinu. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'מֹשֶׁה הוּא דַּעַת' (Moshe IS da'at), and that his generation was therefore called 'דּוֹר דֵּעָה' (Generation of Knowledge). Da'at of holiness is the source of holy unions (zivug dikdushah) and of complete, unperturbed consciousness — 'when da'at is whole, there is no anger'. The polar mirror to Bilam/da'at-of-klipah is Moshe/da'at-of-holiness; the Sifri makes this comparison explicit ('lO kam keMoshe' — in Israel there arose none like Moshe, but among the nations arose Bilam). Da'at dikdushah also generates holy union (hitkashot litzaddikim vela-Torah vela-Shem Yitbarakh), which is the positive zivug — so the concept of zivug dikdushah belongs here as a bechina-expression of what holy da'at produces/is. The 'Dor De'ah' generation label is a bechina-alias for the collective human expression of Moshe's da'at.", "dominant_node": "דעת דקדשה", "nodes": ["דעת דקדשה", "משה", "זיווג דקדשה"], "node_degrees": {"דעת דקדשה": 3, "משה": 1, "זיווג דקדשה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["זיווג דקדשה"], "reason": "זיווג דקדשה is a 1-node gap concept (not yet in dataset). The source text (line 2) defines it as: הִתְקַשְּׁרוּת לְצַדִּיקִים וְאֶל הַתּוֹרָה וְאֶל הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ, drawn from דַּעַת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה. Its essential nature IS holy da'at — it is what holy da'at IS (not what it causes). Merging into Package 1 because its shared nature with דעת דקדשה is the holiness-domain orientation of both concepts."}, {"merged_nodes": ["משה"], "reason": "The Rebbe says: 'מֹשֶׁה הוּא דַּעַת' — Moshe IS da'at. This is a direct 'הוּא' equivalence, the strongest bechina form. Moshe is the human embodiment and personification of da'at dikdushah. He belongs in this package as the archetype/face of the concept."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 43, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גְּבוּרָה וְחֹזֶק הַלֵּב", "label_en": "Bravery and Strength of Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes that גְּבוּרָה (bravery/heroism) and חֹזֶק הַלֵּב (strength of the heart) share the same essential nature — they are two faces of the same reality. He states explicitly: 'עִקָּר הַגְּבוּרָה הוּא בַּלֵּב' — the essence of bravery IS in the heart. This is a bechina statement equating the two. The גִּבּוֹר (the hero/warrior) is defined by this same quality — he is not a separate cause of bravery but is the bechina of the one who possesses חֹזֶק לֵב. The concepts of אֹמֶץ לֵב (courage of heart) and חֹזֶק לֵב are used interchangeably in the same clause ('חֹזֶק וְאֹמֶץ לִבּוֹ'), making them bechina-identical expressions of the same inner quality. These concepts all describe the same positive spiritual-psychological reality: the domain of inner heroism rooted in the heart.", "dominant_node": "גבורה", "nodes": [], "node_degrees": {}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גבורה", "חוזק לב", "גיבור", "אומץ לב"], "reason": "None of these nodes currently exist in torahData.js with reference 1043 — the dataset has zero bechina edges for LM2 Torah 43. However, the source text contains explicit bechina language equating these concepts: 'עִקָּר הַגְּבוּרָה הוּא בַּלֵּב' (the essence of bravery IS in the heart), and 'מִתְגַּבֵּר עַל־יְדֵי חֹזֶק וְאֹמֶץ לִבּוֹ' treats חֹזֶק and אֹמֶץ לֵב as a unified quality. The גִּבּוֹר is the human archetype who embodies this quality — he is not defined by a cause-effect chain but by the essential nature of his heart. All four belong to the same good package. Flagged in additions for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 43, "label": "דַּעַת דִּקְלִפָּה וּבִלְעָם", "label_en": "Da'at of the Klipah and Bilam", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 1. Bilam is explicitly called a bechina of da'at diklifah (Edge 773 in the dataset — the only pre-existing bechina edge for Torah #43). The Rebbe argues from Sifri/Zohar that just as Moshe IS holy da'at among Israel, Bilam IS klipah-da'at among the nations — this is the structural 'zeh l'umat zeh' (Kohelet 7:14: one opposite the other God made). Da'at diklifah is further defined in line 8 as מוֹתָרוֹת שֶׁל דַּעַת עֶלְיוֹן — the residue/excesses of supernal da'at. Bilam's power was specifically 'kocho ba-peh' (his power was in his mouth), which is the hitgalut of his klipah-da'at. Zivug shel aveirah (sinful union) is drawn from da'at diklifah and is the evil mirror of zivug dikdushah; it belongs here as a bechina-expression of what klipah-da'at IS. The air/niuf (immorality) generated by Bilam's speech is the manifest form of klipah-da'at's harvest.", "dominant_node": "דעת דקלפה", "nodes": ["דעת דקלפה", "בלעם", "זיווגים של עברה", "מותרות של דעת עליון"], "node_degrees": {"דעת דקלפה": 4, "בלעם": 1, "זיווגים של עברה": 1, "מותרות של דעת עליון": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["זיווגים של עברה"], "reason": "זיווגִים שֶׁל עֲבֵרָה is a gap concept (not yet in dataset for Torah 43). Line 2 states: 'וְזִוּוּגִים שֶׁל עֲבֵרָה, נִמְשָׁכִים מִדַּעַת דִּקְלִפָּה'. Like the way זיווג דקדשה shares its nature with דעת דקדשה (good, Package 1), so too זיווגים של עברה shares its nature with דעת דקלפה — it IS the expression and embodiment of klipah-da'at in the domain of human relationships. Merged into Package 2 because its shared essential nature is klipah/evil da'at."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מותרות של דעת עליון"], "reason": "Line 8 defines דעת דקלפה as 'מוֹתָרוֹת שֶׁל דַּעַת עֶלְיוֹן' — the excesses/overflow of supernal da'at. This is a bechina-definition of da'at diklifah (using 'שֶׁהוּא' equivalence). It is not a third entity but rather the theological explanation of what da'at diklifah IS: the residue that falls from the supernal da'at. It belongs in Package 2 as a descriptive bechina-alias of the dominant node."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 43, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חֲלִישׁוּת הַלֵּב וְהַיִּרְאָה", "label_en": "Weakness of Heart and Fear", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes the evil mirror of Package 1: חֲלִישׁוּת הַלֵּב (weakness of the heart) is the essential nature of the domain of fear. Rashi's gloss — cited explicitly by the Rebbe — equates 'הַיָּרֵא' (the fearful one) with 'רַךְ הַלֵּבָב' (soft-heartedness), making these bechina-identical: to be fearful IS to have a soft heart; to have a soft heart IS to be fearful. The Rebbe confirms this equation: 'כִּי עַל־יְדֵי רַךְ־לֵבָב... הוּא מִתְיָרֵא, כַּנַּ\"ל'. The 'כַּנַּ\"ל' (as above) is a classic bechina marker — the Rebbe is saying rakh-levav IS the same as the previous description of fear. פַּחַד (fear/terror) and יִרְאָה (timidity/fear) are two names for the same experienced reality that flows from this weak-hearted nature. This is the evil/negative polarity counterpart to Package 1: where Package 1 has חֹזֶק לֵב, Package 2 has חֲלִישׁוּת הַלֵּב; where Package 1 has the גִּבּוֹר, Package 2 has the יָרֵא וְרַךְ הַלֵּבָב.", "dominant_node": "חלישות הלב", "nodes": [], "node_degrees": {}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חלישות הלב", "רך לבב", "הירא", "פחד"], "reason": "None of these nodes exist in torahData.js for reference 1043. However, the source text gives explicit bechina equations: Rashi (cited by the Rebbe) equates 'הַיָּרֵא' with 'רַךְ הַלֵּבָב' — they are the same condition described from two angles. The Rebbe confirms this with 'כַּנַּ\"ל', the bechina marker meaning 'as stated above' (i.e., identical). חֲלִישׁוּת הַלֵּב is the root-nature that generates פְּחָדִים — though the causal chain (chalishut → pachad) is eitza (cause-effect between packages), the concepts of רַךְ לֵבָב, חֲלִישׁוּת לֵב, and הַיָּרֵא are bechina-identical: all expressions of the same weak-hearted nature. Flagged in additions for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 43, "label": "דִּבּוּר וּפֶה — הִתְגַּלּוּת הַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Speech and Mouth — Revelation of Da'at", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Rebbe introduces a structural axiom that cuts across both good and evil: the mouth/speech IS the revelation of da'at — whether holy or klipah. Line 3: 'הַדִּבּוּר הוּא הִתְגַּלּוּת הַדַּעַת'. Line 5: 'הַפֶּה הוּא הִתְגַּלּוּת הַדַּעַת'. These are explicit 'הוּא' bechina declarations establishing that dibur/peh = hitgalut da'at. The proof text used is Tehillim 19:3: 'וְלַיְלָה לְלַיְלָה יְחַוֶּה דַּעַת' — 'יְחַוֶּה is language of speech, for speech speaks what is in the da'at'. This package is NEUTRAL because the mouth/speech serves as the instrument of both domains: 'כֹּחוֹ בַּפֶּה' applies to Moshe (holy Torah speech) and to Bilam (toxic klipah speech) alike. The structural function — that speech is how da'at becomes manifest in the world — is domain-neutral. The peh is the gateway/interface that exists in both holy and klipah realms, carrying either life or poison depending on whose da'at it reveals.", "dominant_node": "דיבור", "nodes": ["דיבור", "פה", "התגלות הדעת"], "node_degrees": {"דיבור": 2, "פה": 2, "התגלות הדעת": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פה", "התגלות הדעת"], "reason": "Lines 3 and 5 each contain the same bechina declaration from two angles: 'הַדִּבּוּר הוּא הִתְגַּלּוּת הַדַּעַת' and 'הַפֶּה הוּא הִתְגַּלּוּת הַדַּעַת'. Peh (mouth), dibur (speech), and hitgalut ha-da'at (revelation of da'at) are three names for the same reality — the interface between inner da'at and its outward expression. All three form one bechina-cluster. The 'yechaaveh' verb from Tehillim 19:3 is explicitly glossed as 'lashon dibur', confirming dibur's identity as da'at-revelation."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 44, "label": "תְּפִלָּה, אֱמוּנָה וּבְרִית עוֹלָם", "label_en": "Prayer, Faith, and the Eternal Covenant", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this component share a single essential nature: they are all different expressions of the dimension of covenant-relationship between Israel and God, manifested through standing in prayer. 'Prayer IS an aspect of faith' (תפלה היא בחינת אמונה) — the verse cited is Moses' hands being 'faith' (אמונה) while spread in prayer; the Targum renders this 'prishon betzlo' (spread in prayer). The bechina is anchored in the physical posture of the hands: spreading the hands in prayer = faith. Then: 'ברית עולם = בחינות תפלה, חי ברכאן דצלותא, צדיק חי עלמין' — the eternal covenant IS prayer (the 18 blessings of the amidah = chai = life = the covenant of life). Thus: תפלה = אמונה = ברית עולם. All three are different names for the same spiritual reality: the living, continuous relationship of standing before God in covenantal faith. The dominant node is תפלה (degree 2 — hub connecting both אמונה and ברית עולם). This package is the 'internal/spiritual faculty' side of the Torah's teaching: the what-prayer-essentially-IS, before asking how to achieve it or where to pray.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "אמונה", "ברית עולם"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 2, "אמונה": 1, "ברית עולם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 44, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּפֶה וּתְמִימוּת", "label_en": "Faith, Mouth, and Wholesomeness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah opens with a single foundational bechina: 'הָאֱמוּנָה תּוֹלָה בְּפֶה שֶׁל אָדָם, בִּבְחִינַת: אוֹדִיעַ אֱמוּנָתְךָ בְּפִי' — Faith is tethered to the mouth, in the aspect of 'I will declare Your faithfulness with my mouth' (Ps. 89:2). The Rebbe then makes the identity explicit: 'זֶהוּ בְּעַצְמוֹ אֱמוּנָה' — speaking faith IS itself faith. This is a full bechina: אמונה and דיבור בפה (speech with the mouth) are two faces of the same thing, not cause and effect. The Torah then establishes תמימות (wholesomeness, simplicity) as the essential quality of faith — it is the name for what faith IS in the domain of divine service: 'לַעֲבֹד אוֹתוֹ יִתְבָּרַךְ בִּתְמִימוּת'. Similarly, פשיטות (plain simplicity) and תמימות are used interchangeably as the positive state of one who serves God without cleverness: 'לַעֲבֹד אֶת ה' בִּתְמִימוּת בִּפְשִׁיטוּת גָּמוּר'. The Rebbe's ultimate conclusion is 'רַחֲמָנָא לִבָּא בָּעֵי' — God wants the heart (לב), which is the final name for what תמימות and אמונה are: heartfelt sincerity before God. All four — אמונה, דיבור בפה, תמימות/פשיטות, and לב — belong to the same thematic family: the domain of genuine, simple, wholehearted faith expressed through the mouth and lived through sincerity of heart.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": [], "node_degrees": {}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אמונה", "דיבור בפה", "תמימות", "פשיטות", "לב"], "reason": "None of these nodes are currently in the dataset as bechina-connected nodes for reference 1044 (torahData.js has zero bechina edges for this Torah). The Rebbe explicitly uses 'בִּבְחִינַת' (line 1) to equate אמונה with speech of the mouth. He further treats תמימות and פשיטות as identical concepts (line 6: 'בִּתְמִימוּת בִּפְשִׁיטוּת גָּמוּר'), and concludes with 'רַחֲמָנָא לִבָּא בָּעֵי', establishing לב (heart) as what all of the above actually is. These five nodes share the same essential nature: the quality of simple, sincere faith that is the foundation of divine service. They should be entered as nodes with bechina edges between them. Flagged in additions.json for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 44, "label": "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל, תָּמִיד וְלִפְנֵי ה'", "label_en": "Eretz Yisrael, the Tamid, and Standing Before God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this component share a single essential nature: they are all different expressions of the sacred-space dimension of divine presence — the domain of 'before God' as a continuous, unconditional state. 'Tamid IS Eretz Yisrael' (תמיד = ארץ ישראל) because of Devarim 11: 'תמיד עיני ה׳ אלקיך בה' — the perpetual attention of God's eyes IS the definition of Eretz Yisrael's special quality. The tamid offering, which is brought every single day without interruption, physically enacts this quality of tamid = continual presence. Then: '\"לפני ה׳ אלקיכם\" זה בחינות ארץ ישראל' — 'before Hashem your God' = Eretz Yisrael, because 'whoever dwells in Eretz Yisrael is as if he has a God' (Ketubot 110b). The phrase 'לפני ה' (before God) and Eretz Yisrael are the same essential reality: the state of being in the presence of God. The dominant node is ארץ ישראל (degree 2 — hub connecting both תמיד and \"לפני ה אלקיכם\"). This package is the 'sacred-space / place of prayer' side of the Torah's teaching: the dimension of where and how prayer must be situated to be real prayer — in the air of Eretz Yisrael, under the perpetual gaze of God.", "dominant_node": "ארץ ישראל", "nodes": ["ארץ ישראל", "תמיד", "\"לפני ה אלקיכם\""], "node_degrees": {"ארץ ישראל": 2, "תמיד": 1, "\"לפני ה אלקיכם\"": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 44, "volume": "lm2", "label": "כְּפִירָה וְאֶפִּיקוֹרְסוּת וְחַקִּירָה", "label_en": "Heresy, Apostasy, and Philosophical Inquiry", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 1. Just as the good package centers on faith expressed through the mouth and lived in simplicity, the evil package centers on the opposite: speech of denial and the intellectual inquiry that damages faith. The Rebbe establishes the bechina explicitly by the mirror structure: 'דִּבּוּרִים שֶׁל כְּפִירָה' (speech of heresy) is the precise counterpart to 'מְדַבְּרִין הָאֱמוּנָה בַּפֶּה' (speaking faith). Since speaking faith IS faith (bechina in Package 1), speaking heresy IS heresy — the same structural identity applies in the evil domain. Kefira (denial), apikorset (heresy/apostasy), and chakira (philosophical inquiry) are used throughout the Torah as interchangeable names for the same corrupting force: 'הֵם מַזִּיקִים מְאֹד לָאֱמוּנָה' (they damage faith very much). Philosophy books (סִפְרֵי הַמְחַקְרִים הַפִילוֹסוֹפְיָא) and inquiry books (סְפָרִים שֶׁל חַקִירָה) are the same thing. 'דִּמְיוֹנוֹת וּשְׁטוּתִים וּבִלְבּוּלִים' (fantasies, foolishness, confusions) are the true nature of these wisdoms. All these concepts — kefira, apikorset, chakira, philosophy, and dimyonot — share the same essential nature: the counterfeit of faith, the domain of false intellectual wisdom that displaces the simple certainty of received tradition.", "dominant_node": "כפירה", "nodes": [], "node_degrees": {}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כפירה", "אפיקורסות", "חקירה", "פילוסופיה", "דימיונות ובלבולים"], "reason": "None of these nodes are in the dataset for reference 1044. They are the evil counterparts of Package 1's concepts. The Rebbe treats them as structural synonyms: philosophy books and inquiry books are both described identically as 'מַזִּיקִים מְאֹד לָאֱמוּנָה'. The kefira of the apikoros and the pseudo-wisdoms of divine service are both classified as dimyonot (fantasies). Their shared essential nature is: the intellectual counterfeit of faith that corrodes simple belief. Flagged in additions.json for dataset entry."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 45, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שַׁבָּת וְהַנְּקֻדָּה הַפְּנִימִית וְהַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "Shabbat, the Inner Point, and the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts share one essential nature: the holy inner core from which all sustenance flows. The Rebbe explicitly uses bechina language twice: 'שַׁבָּת הִיא נְקֻדָּה הַפְּנִימִית' (Shabbat IS the inner point) and 'הַצַּדִּיק הוּא בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת, בְּחִינַת הַנְּקֻדָּה פְּנִימִית' (the Tzaddik IS a bechina of Shabbat, IS a bechina of the inner point). These are not cause-and-effect: the Tzaddik does not produce Shabbat, nor does Shabbat produce the inner point. Rather, all three are different names for the same cosmic reality — the singular holy source from which all the surrounding circles (the six days, all dimensions of created reality) receive their vitality. The Rebbe cites the Zohar (Vayakhel 204a) as support. The inner point is also the goal of the spiritual seeker who journeys to the Tzaddik: reaching the Tzaddik = reaching Shabbat = reaching the inner point.", "dominant_node": "הנקדה פנימית", "nodes": ["שבת", "הנקדה פנימית", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 2, "הנקדה פנימית": 2, "צדיק": 2}, "source_bechinas": ["שַׁבָּת הִיא נְקֻדָּה הַפְּנִימִית, וּמִמֶּנָּהּ יוֹנְקִים כָּל הַשִּׁשָּׁה יָמִים", "הַצַּדִּיק הוּא בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת, בְּחִינַת הַנְּקֻדָּה פְּנִימִית, שֶׁמִּמֶּנּוּ יוֹנְקִים הַכֹּל"], "mirror": "The evil mirror is the domain of the klipot and the wicked who circle the inner point but cannot enter it ('סָבִיב רְשָׁעִים יִתְהַלָּכוּן'). However, the Rebbe does not build a parallel evil bechina cluster — the evil side is described functionally (they circle, they obstruct) rather than by bechina equations. There is no explicit 'X is a bechina of Y' for the evil domain in this Torah.", "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שבת", "הנקדה פנימית", "צדיק"], "reason": "The dataset contains one bechina edge: שבת ↔ הנקדה פנימית (reference 1045). The source text then adds a second explicit bechina statement identifying the Tzaddik as 'בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת, בְּחִינַת הַנְּקֻדָּה פְּנִימִית.' Since צדיק is bechina of שבת, and שבת is bechina of הנקדה פנימית, all three form one connected bechina component. The Tzaddik node does not yet appear in the dataset and must be added."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 45, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַחֹל וְהָעִגּוּלִים", "label_en": "The Six Weekdays and the Circles", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The six weekdays (ששת הימים) and the circles (עיגולים) are explicitly equated: 'הַשִּׁשָּׁה יָמִים, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת הָעִגּוּלִים סְבִיב הַנְּקֻדָּה.' They share the same essential nature: both are the outer ring that surrounds and depends on the inner point. The six days are not a cause of the circles — they ARE the circles. This is a structural/neutral domain: the circles/weekdays serve as the spatial and temporal vessel through which all of creation draws from the inner core. They can be inhabited by souls who are moving closer to or further from the inner point — making them a 'battlefield' domain, neutral in polarity. The klipot manipulate people within this circle-domain; the righteous journey through it toward the inner point.", "dominant_node": "העיגולים", "nodes": ["ששת הימים", "העיגולים"], "node_degrees": {"ששת הימים": 1, "העיגולים": 1}, "source_bechinas": ["הַשִּׁשָּׁה יָמִים, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת הָעִגּוּלִים סְבִיב הַנְּקֻדָּה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב בַּזֹּהַר"], "mirror": "The circles are not evil in themselves — they are the space through which the wicked walk (סביב) without penetrating. Package 2 is therefore the arena in which the good (approaching the inner point) and evil (being kept outside) play out. It is neutral rather than good or evil.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "תְּפִלָּה, נֶפֶשׁ, דִּבּוּר, זִוּוּג וּמִטָּה", "label_en": "Prayer, Soul, Speech, Union, and the Bed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Five nodes linked by bechina: (1) נפש ↔ דבור (id 655): 'this is the aspect of speech, as it says: my soul went out in His speaking' — soul IS speech; (2) נפש ↔ תפלה (id 3152): 'soul = speech = prayer, as it says: he who tears his soul in his anger' — the same soul that IS speech IS prayer; (3) זווג ↔ תפלה (id 3151): 'זווג = the aspect of prayer (Tikun 10, see Tikun 58)' — divine union IS prayer; (4) מטה ↔ זווג (id 1048): 'the bed, that is the aspect of זווג' — the bed IS divine union. The chain: מטה = זווג = תפלה = נפש = דבור. All five are different expressions of the same underlying spiritual reality: prayer as a living act of divine union — soul meeting God through speech. The dominant node is תפלה (degree 2: connects to נפש and זווג, and through them to the full chain). This is the WHAT-IS-PRAYER cluster: what prayer essentially IS at its root — the soul's speech, the divine union, the state of the bed (intimate presence) oriented to God. This is the central positive domain of Torah #46, which is entirely about prayer (clapping the hands during prayer). It is supported by the opening of the Torah: 'clapping in prayer — this is the aspect of placing the bed between north and south, for the bed is the aspect of זווג, the aspect of תפלה.' All five nodes belong to this domain of prayer-as-sacred-union.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "זווג", "מטה", "נפש", "דבור"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 2, "זווג": 2, "מטה": 1, "נפש": 2, "דבור": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 46, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ", "label_en": "Self-Sacrifice / Devotion", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: they are all faces of מסירת נפש — the act of surrendering oneself entirely to the Holy One. The Rebbe states explicitly that giving charity IS mesirut nefesh (because money = the soul, as one risks one's life to earn it), and that prayer IS a bechina of mesirut nefesh (per the Midrash HaNe'elam: 'for your sake we are killed all day'). מסירת נפש is not a result of prayer or charity — it IS them. They are three expressions of the same essential act of self-surrender. A strong heart (לב חזק) is also the very embodiment of this quality — the Rebbe says that even heroism of soldiers is only from strength of heart, making the courageous heart the structural essence of mesirut nefesh.", "dominant_node": "מסירת נפש", "nodes": ["מסירת נפש", "תפלה", "ממון / צדקה", "לב חזק ואמיץ"], "node_degrees": {"מסירת נפש": 3, "תפלה": 1, "ממון / צדקה": 1, "לב חזק ואמיץ": 1}, "mirror_package_id": 2, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תפלה", "מסירת נפש"], "reason": "The Rebbe states explicitly: 'וְכֵן בִּתְפִלָּה אִיתָא בַּמִּדְרָשׁ הַנֶּעְלָם שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת כִּי עָלֶיךָ הֹרַגְנוּ כָּל הַיּוֹם — הַיְנוּ מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ'. Prayer requires enormous effort and battle against distracting thoughts — it IS mesirut nefesh, not a product of it."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ממון / צדקה", "מסירת נפש"], "reason": "The Rebbe opens the Torah with: 'שֶׁנּוֹתֵן מָמוֹנוֹ לִצְדָקָה, וְהַמָּמוֹן הוּא הַנֶּפֶשׁ, כִּי אֵלָיו הוּא נוֹשֵׂא אֶת נַפְשׁוֹ — נִמְצָא מוֹסֵר נַפְשׁוֹ'. Money is the soul (one risks life to earn it); giving it away is therefore surrendering one's soul. Charity and self-sacrifice share the same essential nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["לב חזק ואמיץ", "מסירת נפש"], "reason": "The Rebbe concludes: 'גְבוּרָה שֶׁל הַגִּבּוֹרִים, הוּא רַק מֵחֹזֶק וְאֹמֶץ הַלֵּב'. The strong and courageous heart is the root of all heroism and self-sacrifice. It is not an effect of mesirut nefesh — it is its structural essence, the same thing at the level of inner quality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "מֶחָאַת כַּפַּיִם, תֵּבֵל, חֵשֶׁק הַלֵּב וּנְתִינַת הַמִּטָּה", "label_en": "Clapping the Hands, the World (Tevel), Desire of the Heart, and Orienting the Bed", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Four nodes linked by bechina (with alias resolution): (1) שנתגלה החשק שבלב בידים ↔ מחאת כפים (id 1736): 'that the desire/longing of the heart is revealed through the hands — this is the aspect of clapping'; (2) תבל ↔ מחאת כפים (id 1737): 'תבל is the aspect of clapping the hands — for right is 72 and left is 216, and when they are included in each other, two times 216 = גימטריא תבל'; (3) מחאת כפים בתפלה ↔ נתינת המטה בין צפון לדרום (id 2274): 'clapping in prayer = the aspect of placing the bed between north and south, for the bed is the aspect of זווג/prayer, and north and south are the aspect of hands.' The alias 'מחאת כפים בתפלה' = 'מחאת כפים' (same concept, same Torah). The essential nature of all four nodes: the PHYSICAL ACT of clapping as it IS cosmic integration. 'Tevel' (which derives from the integration of right+left numerics into a unified worldly sum) IS the act of clapping. 'Desire revealed in the hands' IS the act of clapping. 'Orienting the bed between north and south' IS clapping (north/south = hands, bed = prayer/זווג). So all four are different ways of saying the same thing: the act of uniting the hands in prayer IS the manifestation of the soul's desire AND the integration of the world AND the proper orientation of prayer. The dominant node is מחאת כפים (degree 3: hub connecting שנתגלה החשק, תבל, and נתינת המטה). This is the PHYSICAL-ACT-AS-COSMIC-REALITY cluster: what the physical clapping essentially IS — desire, cosmos, directed prayer.", "dominant_node": "מחאת כפים", "nodes": ["מחאת כפים", "תבל", "שנתגלה החשק שבלב, בידים", "נתינת המטה בין צפון לדרום"], "node_degrees": {"מחאת כפים": 3, "תבל": 1, "שנתגלה החשק שבלב, בידים": 1, "נתינת המטה בין צפון לדרום": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Alias resolution: 'מחאת כפים בתפלה' (node1_id in edge 2274) treated as same node as 'מחאת כפים' (node2_id in edges 1736, 1737). Torah #46 is entirely about clapping in prayer; the qualifier 'בתפלה' is implicit throughout the Torah. Conceptually and contextually identical."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 46, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מְנִיעוֹת וְחוֹמוֹת — הַסְּטָרָא אַחְרָא", "label_en": "Obstacles and Illusory Walls — the Opposing Force", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: they are all expressions of the opposing force that blocks a person from divine service and from the Tzaddik. The dataset records the explicit bechina: obstacles-seductions-temptations ARE a bechina of walls (חוֹמוֹת) — the illusory walls surrounding the treasury of fear of Heaven. The Ba'al Shem Tov's parable reveals that these walls have no real existence (they are achisat einayim — illusions). The inner dimension of these obstacles is the twisted/spinning heart (לב סחרחר), which is explicitly called a bechina of the bitter servitude in Egypt and the Tikunim's 'be-kushya' — meaning kushyot and kfirut (doubts and heresy) in the heart. The greatest obstacle of all is מניעת המוח — the division of mind and heart from God or the Tzaddik. All these are the same essential force: the klipa that circles the inner point and prevents approach.", "dominant_node": "המניעות וההסתות ופתויים", "nodes": ["המניעות וההסתות ופתויים", "חומות", "לב סחרחר", "קושיות וכפירות", "מניעת המוח"], "node_degrees": {"המניעות וההסתות ופתויים": 2, "חומות": 2, "לב סחרחר": 1, "קושיות וכפירות": 1, "מניעת המוח": 1}, "mirror_package_id": 1, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["המניעות וההסתות ופתויים", "חומות"], "reason": "Explicitly in the dataset (reference 1046, bechina): 'הַמְּנִיעוֹת וְהַהֲסָתוֹת וּפִתּוּיִים שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת חוֹמוֹת'. The obstacles and seductions ARE a bechina of walls. This is the direct dataset edge."}, {"merged_nodes": ["לב סחרחר", "קושיות וכפירות"], "reason": "The Rebbe states: 'הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת לִבִּי סְחַרְחַר — הַיְנוּ שֶׁלִּבּוֹ מְסֻבָּב וּמֻקָּף וּמְעֻקָּם בְּעַקְמוּמִיּוּת וְקֻשְׁיוֹת וּכְפִירוֹת'. The spinning/circling heart IS the state of being surrounded by questions and heresy. They are the same spiritual ailment named from different angles."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מניעת המוח", "המניעות וההסתות ופתויים"], "reason": "The Rebbe identifies מניעת המוח (division of mind from God/Tzaddik) as 'הַמְּנִיעָה הַגְּדוֹלָה שֶׁבְּכָל הַמְּנִיעוֹת'. It is the supreme form of all the obstacles — the inner, cognitive dimension of the same blocking force. The external obstacles (walls) have their ultimate expression in this inner obstacle. They share the same essential nature: prevention from approaching the holy inner point."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "יָד, הֲוָיָה, יָד רָמָה, צָפוֹן וְדָרוֹם", "label_en": "The Hand, the Divine Name Havaya, the Raised Hand, North and South", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Six nodes from three merged CCs, all sharing the same essential nature: the HAND(S) AS DIVINE METAPHYSICAL STRUCTURES. CC2 {יד, הויה}: 'the hand is the aspect of Havaya — yud letters and dalet letters [= numerical value of Havaya]' (id 821) — the hand IS the divine Name. CC5 {בשעת הכאה שנתחברים יחד, יד רמה}: 'at the moment of striking/joining together, this is the aspect of the Raised Hand' (id 1246) — the joined hands IS the Raised Hand (third of the three divine Hands corresponding to three Havayahs). CC6 {צפון ודרום, ידים}: 'north and south, this is the aspect of the hands' (id 1247) — the cardinal directions ARE the hands. Merge of CC2+CC5+CC6 is by SHARED NATURE: יד = הויה (the hand's metaphysical identity); the joined hands = יד רמה (one of three Hands = three Havayahs); north/south = ידים (spatial expression of the hands). All three sub-groups are saying: the hand(s) ARE divine Names and cosmic structures. None of these merges involves a causal relationship — they are all parallel aspects of the same reality. The dominant node is ידים/יד (appears in both CC2 and CC6; treating יד and ידים as the same canonical node by consonantal root י-ד, degree 3 across the merged package: יד↔הויה, בשעת הכאה↔יד רמה, צפון ודרום↔ידים). Note: 'יד' and 'ידים' are singular/plural of the same root — they refer to the same conceptual entity (hand/hands). The hands in Torah #46 are the hands clapped in prayer; their metaphysical identity is what this package captures. The connection to Package 2 (clapping) is via eitza: the hands' divine identity (Package 3) is WHAT MAKES clapping (Package 2) effective. This is the WHY behind the act.", "dominant_node": "ידים", "nodes": ["יד", "הויה", "בשעת הכאה שנתחברים יחד", "יד רמה", "צפון ודרום", "ידים"], "node_degrees": {"יד": 1, "הויה": 1, "בשעת הכאה שנתחברים יחד": 1, "יד רמה": 1, "צפון ודרום": 1, "ידים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged CC2 {יד, הויה} + CC5 {בשעת הכאה שנתחברים יחד, יד רמה} + CC6 {צפון ודרום, ידים}: all share the essential nature of 'the hands as divine/cosmic structures.' Merge is by SHARED NATURE (not eitza): all three express what the hand(s) essentially ARE — a divine Name, a cosmic direction, an exalted divine force. The causal relationship (hand's divinity → clapping → sweetening) operates between packages, not within them.", "Treating 'יד' (CC2) and 'ידים' (CC6) as expressions of the same canonical concept by consonantal root י-ד. Both refer to the hands as the physical-metaphysical instrument of prayer."]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "אַהֲרֹן, יְמִינָא, קֹרַח וּשְׂמָאלָא", "label_en": "Aaron, the Right, Korach, and the Left", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Four nodes from two merged CCs: CC7 {אהרן, ימינא}: 'Korach against Aaron — they are the aspect of left and right' (id 1362) — Aaron IS the right side. CC9 {קרח, שמאלא}: 'Korach against Aaron — they are the aspect of left and right' (id 2964) — Korach IS the left side. These share the essential nature of 'the two archetypal poles of the right-left split.' The text explicitly presents them as a pair: 'כל המחלקת נמשכים מבחינת קרח על אהרן, שהם בחינת שמאלא וימינא' — ALL conflict descends from the archetype of Korach-Aaron = left-right. Aaron and Korach are not simply good-and-evil individually; they ARE the two-sided structure of the polarity itself. Together they constitute one unified domain: the right-left split as the root of all conflict (and its resolution through clapping). Polarity is NEUTRAL because the package explicitly contains both poles as a paired structure — neither side alone defines the package; the package IS the pair. Aaron (good: right side, ימינא) and Korach (evil: left side, שמאלא) are both named in the same sentence by the Rebbe as the two aspects of the same archetypal split. The dominant node cannot be either Aaron or Korach alone; the package is named for both. Structurally this package is the 'root of conflict' domain — it explains WHY clapping (Package 2) resolves all conflict: by including right in left and left in right (נכללים שמאל בימין וימין בשמאל ונעשים אחדות), it repairs the primal Aaron-Korach split.", "dominant_node": "אהרן", "nodes": ["אהרן", "ימינא", "קרח", "שמאלא"], "node_degrees": {"אהרן": 1, "ימינא": 1, "קרח": 1, "שמאלא": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Merged CC7 {אהרן, ימינא} + CC9 {קרח, שמאלא}: shared nature is 'the two poles of the right-left archetypal split.' The text presents them as one paired concept in a single sentence: 'קרח על אהרן, שהם בחינת שמאלא וימינא.' Aaron=ימינא and Korach=שמאלא together IS the concept of ימינא ושמאלא. Merge is by SHARED NATURE (they are two halves of the same structural reality — the primal split), not by eitza.", "Polarity is neutral because the package inherently contains both a good node (אהרן/ימינא) and an evil node (קרח/שמאלא) as constitutive parts of the same domain."]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת וֶאֱלֹקִים", "label_en": "Small Consciousness and the Divine Name Elokim (Judgment Domain)", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Two nodes: מחין דקטנות ↔ אלהים (id 268): 'small-mind consciousness = the aspect of Elokim.' The essential nature: the state of contracted, judgment-dominated consciousness. In Rebbe Nachman's framework, Elokim is the divine Name associated with divine justice/severity (מידת הדין) — contraction, limitation, judgment. מחין דקטנות (small mind / small consciousness) IS that same contracted quality at the level of the human soul. Both are different expressions of the same reality: the dimension of divine judgment/severity as it manifests in the soul's consciousness. Polarity is 'evil' in the specific sense used by the framework: this is the negative pole of the teaching — not evil as in sitra achra, but the problematic state that the Torah's teaching aims to remedy. The text says: 'the main forgetting comes from מחין דקטנות, from the aspect of Elokim; and when one sweetens Elokim through one's hands, thereby one no longer forgets Torah.' The מחין דקטנות/אלהים package IS the negative state — the condition that the remedy (clapping = המתקת הדינים) is designed to fix. It is NOT merged with Package 6 (המתקת הדינים/ואפלטהו) because those two packages have different essential natures: Package 5 is the STATE of judgment-consciousness, Package 6 is the DIVINE RESCUE from that state. The causal link between them (sweetening Elokim removes מחין דקטנות) is eitza, not shared nature.", "dominant_node": "מחין דקטנות", "nodes": ["מחין דקטנות", "אלהים"], "node_degrees": {"מחין דקטנות": 1, "אלהים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC3 kept separate. Not merged with Package 6 (המתקת הדינים / ואפלטהו): the causal relationship between them (sweetening Elokim removes small-mind consciousness) is eitza, not shared nature. Package 5 describes the PROBLEM (contracted judgment-state); Package 6 describes the RESCUE (divine deliverance = sweetening). Different essential natures.", "Polarity assigned 'evil' as the problematic state — the negative pole that the Torah's teaching addresses. Not sitra achra evil, but the dimension of Elokim/din/judgment-consciousness that must be sweetened."]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 46, "label": "\"וַאֲפַלְּטֵהוּ\" וְהַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים", "label_en": "\"I Will Rescue Him\" and the Sweetening of Judgments", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Two nodes: 'ואפלטהו' ↔ המתקת הדינים (id 894): '\"ואפלטהו\" = the aspect of the sweetening of judgments.' The essential nature: divine rescue = sweetening of judgments. The verse from Psalms 91:14 — 'כי בי חשק ואפלטהו' — 'because he has desire for me, I will rescue him' — is interpreted in the Torah as: the revealed desire (חשק) of the heart in the hands (clapping) leads to divine rescue, which IS the sweetening of judgments. So 'ואפלטהו' (I will rescue him) and 'המתקת הדינים' (sweetening of judgments) name the same divine action from two perspectives: one is the divine promise of rescue (ואפלטהו), the other is the metaphysical description of what that rescue consists of (sweetening the severity/judgments). Both ARE the same reality — divine deliverance = the transformation of strict judgment into mercy. Polarity: good (this is the positive outcome, the divine mercy dimension). This package is the RESULT domain: what God does in response to the clapping/desire. It is connected to Package 5 (מחין דקטנות/אלהים) via eitza (God sweetens the Elokim → removes small-mind consciousness), and to Package 2 (clapping) via eitza (clapping → ואפלטהו/המתקת הדינים). The two-node structure is small but legitimate: the bechina statement is explicit and the package represents the Torah's promised outcome.", "dominant_node": "המתקת הדינים", "nodes": ["\"ואפלטהו\"", "המתקת הדינים"], "node_degrees": {"\"ואפלטהו\"": 1, "המתקת הדינים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC4 kept separate. Not merged with Package 5 (מחין דקטנות/אלהים): the causal link is eitza (sweetening removes the contraction). Their natures differ: Package 5 IS the state of judgment-consciousness (problem); Package 6 IS the divine rescue = sweetening (solution). Different domains.", "Not merged with Package 2 (clapping): clapping CAUSES the sweetening (eitza); they are not the same essential reality."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 47, "label": "אֱמֶת, יַעֲקֹב וּתְפִלִּין — כְּלָלוּת הַגְּוָנִין", "label_en": "Truth, Jacob, and Tefillin — the Fullness of Colors", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All 16 nodes in this package are different expressions of the single meta-concept that Torah #47 calls 'אמת' (Truth in its cosmic dimension). The Torah explicitly constructs the following identity chain through direct bechina statements: אמת = יעקב ('תתן אמת ליעקב') = מדת תפארת ('יעקב הוא תפארת') = כלליות הגונין ('תפארת הוא כלליות הגונין') = שמים ('כלליות הגונין הוא שמים, אש ומים') = ארץ ישראל ('ארץ ישראל מקבל שפעה ממדת יעקב') = אילה שלוחה ('ארץ ישראל היא אילה שלוחה' which IS אמת) = תפלין ('יעקב הוא תפארת, כלליות הגונין, בחינת תפלין הנקראים פאר') = פאר = שפר ('שפר זה בחינת פאר בחינת תפלין') = נשיאות פנים ('נשיאות פנים הוא בחינות אמת') = הארות ונשיאות פנים ('הארות ונשיאות פנים שהוא בחינות המתקת ובטול הדינים') = המתקת הדינים. The two peripheral bechina pairs — דבורי אמת = יעקב (truth-speech IS Jacob) and בושה = כלליות הגונין (shame IS the fallen expression of the totality of colors, the 'מדות הנפולין' inversion of the same quality) — anchor the package in the Torah's concluding movement: when a person speaks words of truth (nourished by Eretz Yisrael's shefa flowing through Jacob's quality) they become a partner with God in creating new heavens and earth; and their faces will not be ashamed, because shame (the fallen colors) is the exact inversion of the glorious tiferet-colors of truth. This is one unified conceptual cluster — the positive ontological axis of Torah #47.", "dominant_node": "יעקב", "nodes": ["אמת", "יעקב", "מדת תפארת", "ארץ ישראל", "\"אילה שלוחה\"", "נשיאות פנים", "כללות הגונין", "שמים", "אש ומים", "תפלין", "פאר", "שפר", "דבורי אמת", "בושה", "הארות ונשיאות פנים", "המתקת הדינים"], "node_degrees": {"אמת": 3, "יעקב": 5, "מדת תפארת": 3, "ארץ ישראל": 2, "\"אילה שלוחה\"": 2, "נשיאות פנים": 1, "כללות הגונין": 2, "שמים": 2, "אש ומים": 1, "תפלין": 2, "פאר": 2, "שפר": 1, "דבורי אמת": 1, "בושה": 1, "הארות ונשיאות פנים": 1, "המתקת הדינים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC3 ({הארות ונשיאות פנים, המתקת הדינים}) merged into Package 1. Justification: 'הארות ונשיאות פנים' shares essential nature with 'נשיאות פנים' (already in CC1 via נשיאות פנים = אמת). Both are the same spiritual quality — the illumination of the divine face — expressed with slightly different emphasis ('הארות ונשיאות' = illuminations-and-lifting, 'נשיאות פנים' = lifting of the face). The bechina 'הארות ונשיאות פנים הוא בחינות המתקת ובטול הדינים' confirms that face-illumination is the positive pole of the dinin-dimension, belonging to the same truth-and-face-light domain as CC1. The merge is warranted by shared conceptual nature (both = divine face illumination = aspect of אמת), NOT by eitza reasoning."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 47, "label": "הַסְתָּרַת פָּנִים וְדִינִים", "label_en": "Concealment of the Face and Harsh Judgments", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This 2-node package is the evil mirror of Package 1's dominant axis. The bechina 'הסתרת פנים = דינים' (concealment of the divine face IS harsh judgments) is the exact negative counterpart to 'נשיאות פנים = אמת' (illumination of the face IS truth). Torah #47 explicitly constructs the opposition: when Israel wallows in the desire for food, 'והסתרתי פני' (I will hide my face) — and this concealment IS judgment/strictness (דינים). The Torah connects this to 'כי הם משוקעים בתאות אכילה, אזי הסתרת פנים, בחינות דינים.' The package stands as the structural evil counter to Package 1: where Package 1 has face-illumination = truth = abundance, Package 2 has face-concealment = judgment = poverty/shame. The dominant node is הסתרת פנים (degree 1, but it is the more theologically charged and specific concept — 'הסתרת פנים' is the primary term the Torah uses for God's withdrawal; 'דינים' is its essential nature/consequence at the cosmic level). This small package is not merged into CC1 because its polarity is opposite — it represents the evil dimension, which must remain separate per the framework's good/evil mirror structure.", "dominant_node": "הסתרת פנים", "nodes": ["הסתרת פנים", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"הסתרת פנים": 1, "דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": "torah_48_pkg_1", "torah_ref": 48, "label": "סֻכָּה וּבִינָה וְתַפְלָה", "label_en": "Sukka, Binah, and Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the same essential nature: the divine covering / protective enclosure that is simultaneously the womb of formation (binah), the force of prayer spoken with full bodily strength, the embrace of God's right hand (chesed), and the surrounding divine light (makifim). Tzeirei, the vowel marking formation, is the graphic sign of this entire cluster — it points to vayyitzar (formation), to binah (the supernal mother-womb that shelters), to the sukka (which shelters like a womb), and to the power of holy speech that IS God's own words. Chesed, the Waters of Chesed, the utterances of creation, the shadow of God's hand, and the embrace are all different angles on the same divine protective/generative force. Inner Chesed (chavdim penimiyim) is absorbed here as it shares the essential chesed/sukka nature, representing the inner aspect of the same domain.", "dominant_node": "סכה", "nodes": ["סכה", "צירי", "בינה", "דין ורחמים", "וייצר", "חסד", "חבוק", "חבוק יד ימין", "צל יד", "מי החסדים", "המאמרות שבהם נברא העולם", "כח התפלה מה שמתפללין בכח", "מקיפים", "\"והצנע לכת\"", "\"ואהבת חסד\"", "חסדים הפנימיים"], "node_degrees": {"סכה": 8, "צירי": 4, "חסד": 3, "מקיפים": 2, "כח התפלה מה שמתפללין בכח": 2, "צל יד": 2, "בינה": 1, "דין ורחמים": 1, "וייצר": 1, "המאמרות שבהם נברא העולם": 1, "חבוק": 1, "חבוק יד ימין": 1, "מי החסדים": 1, "\"והצנע לכת\"": 1, "\"ואהבת חסד\"": 1, "חסדים הפנימיים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Component originally split from raw graph: (a) the 14-node component connected through סכה and צירי; (b) the 2-node component {'ואהבת חסד', חסדים הפנימיים}. Component (b) was merged into the main package by shared nature: חסדים הפנימיים is an inner aspect of חסד, the same essential domain. The teaching (Micah 6:8 section) distinguishes inner from outer chesed but both remain expressions of the chesed/sukka domain. Never merged by eitza. Edge 1487 and 2315 both represent the same סכה—כח התפלה pair (one edge each direction in the source data); degree counted as 2 incidences for סכה.", "The node 'חבוק יד ימין' and 'צל יד' are distinct node_ids from 'חבוק' per the dataset, and are kept separate in the node list accordingly. Their relationship: צל יד = חבוק יד ימין (edge 1094), both = aspects of סכה (edge 2569 for צל יד directly).", "Node '\"והצנע לכת\"' (the full phrase, edge 1934) is distinct from 'והצנע' (edge 1365) and 'לכת' (edge 2184). The full phrase belongs to Package 1 (connected to מקיפים); the broken-out components 'והצנע' and 'לכת' belong to Package 2 (connected to יסוד and נצח והוד respectively)."]}, {"id": "torah_48_pkg_2", "torah_ref": 48, "label": "דַּפְנוֹת הַסֻּכָּה — נֶצַח, הוֹד, יְסוֹד", "label_en": "The Walls of the Sukka — Netzach, Hod, Yesod", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of the structural sefirot that constitute the physical walls of the sukka. The teaching (Micah 6:8: 'להצנע לכת') provides three components: 'עשות משפט' (din), 'ואהבת חסד' (inner chesed), 'הצנע לכת' (the hidden walk). The latter breaks into 'הצנע' = יסוד (one handbreadth revealed, one covered) and 'לכת' = נצח והוד (the two full walls). The wall of יסוד is the partial third wall (טפח, one handbreadth only, per Sukka 6a). These concepts share the nature of 'foundational pillars that frame the sukka's enclosure' — the structural sefirot. They are distinguished from the main Package 1 nodes which represent the FUNCTION and ESSENCE of the sukka (shelter, light, prayer, chesed); this package represents the STRUCTURE.", "dominant_node": "יסוד", "nodes": ["יסוד", "והצנע", "טפח", "נצח והוד", "לכת"], "node_degrees": {"יסוד": 2, "והצנע": 1, "טפח": 1, "נצח והוד": 1, "לכת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Two raw connected components merged by shared nature: Component A = {יסוד, והצנע, טפח} (edges 1365, 1366); Component B = {נצח והוד, לכת} (edge 2184). Shared nature: both components represent structural sefirot forming the dafnot (walls) of the sukka as derived from Micah 6:8 ('הצנע לכת'). The Rebbe explicitly teaches: the walls are נצח and הוד (two full walls) and יסוד (one partial wall = one טפח). 'הצנע' = יסוד; 'לכת' = נצח והוד. Both components belong to the same structural teaching about the sukka's walls.", "Note on יסוד as dominant: יסוד has degree 2 (connected to both והצנע and טפח), while נצח והוד has degree 1. יסוד is named dominant for degree, but conceptually the cluster represents all three sefirot equally."]}, {"id": "torah_49_pkg_1", "torah_ref": 49, "label": "מַלְכוּת וּתְפִלָּה", "label_en": "Malkhut and Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of Malkhut — divine kingship — in its various expressions across Torah 49. The Torah's central thesis is that prayer is the manifestation of Malkhut within the chalal panui of the heart (§ב: 'תפלה בחינת מלכות'). Malkhut David is the epitome of holy kingship-as-song ('ואני תפלה'; 'נעים זמירות ישראל'). Shemen (anointing oil) IS Malkhut David — the oil of anointment that enacts kingship. The small/diminished Malkhut ('מלכות בשפלות ובקטנות') — expressed as 'אחות לנו קטנה', 'את המאור הקטן', and 'אתתך גוצא' — is the same Malkhut in its unrectified exile state (the small moon, the humbled wife). Malkhut Peh (mouth-Malkhut) = Kevod Elokim (divine glory), because when Malkhut ascends to the 'aifah' (where-is-the-place-of-His-glory), that IS Malkhut-as-speech. 'Isha yirat H'' IS the Eitza — the humble Malkhut in its counsel-state is the same small-Malkhut-as-good-advice domain. All nodes are aspects of the same ontological concept: Malkhut as holy kingship/prayer/song in its multiple states.", "dominant_node": "מלכות דוד", "nodes": ["מלכות דוד", "תפלה", "מלכות", "שמן", "נעים זמירות ישראל", "מלכות, בשפלות ובקטנות", "\"אחות לנו קטנה\"", "\"את המאור הקטן\"", "אתתך גוצא", "מלכות פה", "כבוד אלהים", "\"אשה יראת ה'\"", "עצה"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות דוד": 3, "מלכות, בשפלות ובקטנות": 3, "תפלה": 2, "מלכות": 1, "שמן": 1, "נעים זמירות ישראל": 1, "\"אחות לנו קטנה\"": 1, "\"את המאור הקטן\"": 1, "אתתך גוצא": 1, "מלכות פה": 1, "כבוד אלהים": 1, "\"אשה יראת ה'\"": 1, "עצה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Four raw connected components were merged into this package by shared nature (never by eitza):", "Component A (5 nodes): {תפלה, מלכות, מלכות דוד, שמן, נעים זמירות ישראל} — the core Malkhut-as-prayer-and-song cluster, connected via edges 1836, 1864, 1865, 2111.", "Component B (4 nodes): {מלכות, בשפלות ובקטנות; 'אחות לנו קטנה'; 'את המאור הקטן'; אתתך גוצא} — Malkhut in its small/diminished state, connected via edges 2, 13, 489. Merged by shared nature: this IS the same Malkhut as Component A but in its unrectified state. Torah 49 §ה explicitly teaches that the Dalet (small-Malkhut) and the He (rectified-Malkhut) are the same letter-concept at different stages. The small moon, the humbled wife, and the small sister are all faces of the same Malkhut.", "Component C (2 nodes): {מלכות פה, כבוד אלהים} — Malkhut Peh = Kevod Elokim, from §ז (edge 1449). Merged by shared nature: Malkhut Peh is explicitly an aspect of Malkhut (the 'mouth' of divine sovereignty); Kevod Elokim is what Malkhut IS when elevated ('כד איהי סליקית'). This 2-node component shares the essential Malkhut nature.", "Component D (2 nodes): {'אשה יראת ה'', עצה} — the God-fearing woman IS the Eitza (edge 2428). Merged by shared nature: Torah 49 §ה directly equates 'אשה יראת ה'' with 'בחינת מלכות בשפלות — אתתך גוצא', and the proof-text for edge 489 explicitly contains 'אתתך גוצא' as a synonym. The God-fearing woman-as-advice IS the small-Malkhut in its role as the humble counselor. Same essential nature as Component B.", "Tie-breaking for dominant node: both מלכות דוד and מלכות, בשפלות ובקטנות have degree 3. מלכות דוד is chosen as dominant because it is the aspirational realization of Malkhut in this Torah — the explicit telos of the teaching (§ה: 'מלכות דוד = שיר'). The small-Malkhut is the starting state; Malkhut David is the goal state."]}, {"id": "torah_49_pkg_2", "torah_ref": 49, "label": "תְּכֵלֶת וְכַּלָּה וּמַקִּיפִים", "label_en": "Tekhelet, the Kallah, and the Encompassing Lights", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of tekhelet — the divine blue-force of encompassing light that simultaneously destroys the klipa-nations and sustains Israel. Torah 49 §ז teaches that tzitzit IS tekhelet IS makifim — the surrounding encompassing light. The tekhelet 'consumes all and destroys all' (din/gevarot) yet Israel who clings to it remain alive. Kallah IS tekhelet (she who destroys all; 'כלה' and 'כלה בחמה' share the same root). Nefesh David IS tekhelet (the driving consuming passion of David's soul). Ruach Tzafon = din/gevarot = tekhelet — the north wind that awakened David's lyre came from the same din-force that is tekhelet. Mincha = din (Yitzhak established Mincha-prayer which IS din). All nodes are expressions of the same consuming, encompassing divine-force-of-din that is tekhelet/makifim — the holy outer light that overwhelms and destroys evil.", "dominant_node": "תכלת", "nodes": ["תכלת", "מקיפים", "דין", "כלה", "נפש דוד", "ציצית", "מנחה", "רוח צפון"], "node_degrees": {"תכלת": 5, "דין": 3, "מקיפים": 1, "כלה": 1, "נפש דוד": 1, "ציצית": 1, "מנחה": 1, "רוח צפון": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package is a single raw connected component (no merges needed). All 8 nodes connect through tekhelet (degree 5) as the hub: tzitzit-tekhelet (3131), kallah-tekhelet (3129), nefesh-David-tekhelet (3130), din-tekhelet (3128), tekhelet-makifim (1933). The din sub-cluster connects through edges 731 (mincha-din) and 732 (ruach-tzafon-din), which links into the tekhelet cluster via edge 3128 (din-tekhelet).", "Note on polarity: din and gevarot are often neutral or evil, but in Torah 49 they appear exclusively in the holy context of tekhelet's protective-destructive force. The Rebbe explicitly states that this tekhelet 'consumes all' but 'Israel who clings to it live' (§ז). The din here is the holy din of Malkhut deKedusha that annihilates Malkhut deSitra Achra. Polarity is therefore 'good'."]}, {"id": "torah_49_pkg_3", "torah_ref": 49, "label": "שִׁיר וְאוֹת דָּלֶ\"ת", "label_en": "Song and the Letter Dalet", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of the future divine Song (שיר שיתעורר לעתיד) in its structural dimension, as taught in Torah 49 §ה. The Dalet letter IS the vessel of the fourfold song (shir pashut, kaful, meshulash, meruva) — the Dalet's four sides/corners are the structural container for the four levels of song. Dalet kenafot (the four wings/corners of the earth = mikkenaf ha'aretz) IS the Dalet letter's cosmic expression. Sarah (the matriarch whose name means 'ruling') IS Song — 'שרה = שיר' because the singing of holy rule is how Malkhut is revealed ('שרה על כל העולם = שיר'). The core shir (song, simple) is a separate node from 'shir pashut kaful' etc., but both are manifestations of the same essential category: the song that accompanies and reveals Malkhut. The Dalet nodes cluster around the letter's structural role as the vessel of song and its cosmic extension (four corners of earth).", "dominant_node": "אות דל\"ת", "nodes": ["אות דל\"ת", "דלת כנפות", "מכנף הארץ", "שיר פשוט כפול משלש מרבע", "שרה", "שיר"], "node_degrees": {"אות דל\"ת": 2, "דלת כנפות": 2, "שרה": 1, "שיר": 1, "מכנף הארץ": 1, "שיר פשוט כפול משלש מרבע": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Two raw connected components merged by shared nature:", "Component A (4 nodes): {אות דל\"ת, דלת כנפות, מכנף הארץ, שיר פשוט כפול משלש מרבע} — connected via edges 749 (dalet→dalet-kenafot), 1807 (dalet-kenafot→mikkenaf-ha'aretz), 2874 (dalet→shir-pashut-etc.).", "Component B (2 nodes): {שרה, שיר} — connected via edge 2873 (sarah→shir). This is a small (2-node) component eligible for merging.", "Merge basis (shared nature, not eitza): Both components belong to the same domain of 'the future Song as revealed through/by Malkhut.' Torah 49 §ה explicitly teaches these in the same passage: שרה = שיר = וְשָׁרִים כְּחוֹלְלִים (the singing of divine rule), and Dalet = the structural container of shir pashut/kaful/meshulash/meruva. Sarah-as-song and Dalet-as-song are two faces of the same song-domain — one via the feminine-ruling name (שרה → שיר as verbal parallel), the other via the letter structure. Both belong to the song-of-Malkhut-revelation domain. Note: the שיר in edge 2873 (node2) and the שיר פשוט כפול משלש מרבע in edge 2874 (node2) are distinct node_ids and are kept separate in the node list."]}, {"id": "torah_49_pkg_4", "torah_ref": 49, "label": "יוּ\"ד וּקְדֻשָּׁה וַעֲשָׂרָה נִגּוּנִים", "label_en": "The Letter Yud, Holiness, and the Ten Types of Melody", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of the Yud letter as the vessel of kedusha and the ten types of niggun. Torah 49 §ג and §ה teach that the Yud drawn into the Dalet transforms it into He. The Yud = kedusha (because 'there is no kedusha except with ten' — Megillah 23a; Yud = ten = minyan = kedusha). The Yud = ten types of melody (עשרה מיני נגונא) — the Yud's numerical value of 10 maps directly to the ten song-types. Together, the Yud embodies the concentrated, point-force of holiness (kedusha as the essential point, as the ten-fold generative power) that generates the fullness of song when combined with the Dalet vessel. These three nodes are all faces of the same single concept: the Yud in its role as the point of concentrated kedusha/niggun.", "dominant_node": "אות יו\"ד", "nodes": ["אות יו\"ד", "עשרה מיני נגונא", "קדשה"], "node_degrees": {"אות יו\"ד": 2, "עשרה מיני נגונא": 1, "קדשה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Single raw connected component — no merges needed. Edges: 2461 (yud→esarah-mini-niguna), 2602 (yud→kedusha).", "This package stands independently (3 nodes, above the merge threshold). The Yud cluster is conceptually related to Package 3 (Song/Dalet) because Torah 49 §ה explicitly states 'He = Dalet + Yud' and the full song = Dalet + Yud combined. However, since Package 4 has 3 nodes (not a 'small' 1-2 node component) and both packages represent distinct sub-aspects of the letter He (the Dalet dimension vs. the Yud dimension), they are kept separate to preserve structural granularity. Their relationship is eitza-like (the two combine to form He), not bechina of shared nature."]}, {"id": "torah_49_pkg_5", "torah_ref": 49, "label": "שַׁבָּת וּתְשׁוּבָה", "label_en": "Shabbat and Teshuva", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes share the essential nature of teshuva and the future Shabbat-rest as the vessel for the revealed Torah de-Atika. Torah 49 §ו teaches that Shabbat IS teshuva ('וְשַׁבְתָּ עַד ה' = שַׁבָּת = תְּשׁוּבָה'). 'Mashivat nefesh' (the restoring/returning of the soul, from the verse about Torah) IS Shabbat — because the restoration of the soul through Torah IS the Shabbat rest ('כי בו שָׁבַת וַיִּנָּפַשׁ'). Great teshuva that 'reaches the Throne of Glory' is the same as the revelation of Oraiyta de-Atika — the future Torah of the Ancient One — which is the essence of Shabbat. All three nodes are expressions of the same reality: the return/rest that reaches beyond the present world into the depth of Ein Sof, which is what Shabbat and Teshuva both ultimately point to.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "תשובה", "משיבת נפש"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 2, "תשובה": 1, "משיבת נפש": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Single raw connected component — no merges needed. Edges: 3247 (shabbat→teshuva), null-id edge (mashivat-nefesh→shabbat).", "Note: The null-id edge for 'משיבת נפש — שבת' was included in the dataset without an assigned ID. It is a valid bechina edge with explicit proof-text ('מְשִׁיבַת נָפֶשׁ, זֶה בְּחִינַת שַׁבָּת, כִּי בוֹ שָׁבַת וַיִּנָּפַשׁ') from §ו/Torah text line 81."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 50, "label": "פְּגַם הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Violation of the Covenant", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All seven nodes in this cluster share the same essential nature: they are different faces of the single spiritual reality of defilement produced by covenant-transgression. The hub concept מי שפגם בבריתו (the covenant-violator) is directly equated via bechina edges to: מיין מרירין (bitter waters), מרה (bitterness), חרב פיות (the double-edged sword), and גיהנם (Gehinnom). Radiating outward from מיין מרירין are two further bechina-equivalents: מיין מסאבין (defiled waters) and זרע טמא (impure seed). Every node in this component is an aspect of the same reality — the spiritual pollution of the person who has violated the covenant. The Rebbe's proof-text structure makes this explicit: the covenant-violator IS bitter waters (line 6), and bitter waters IS the dog (line 7), IS Mara (line 7), IS the double-edged sword (line 7), IS Gehinnom (line 7). The dominant node is מי שפגם בבריתו with 4 direct bechina connections.", "dominant_node": "מי שפגם בבריתו", "nodes": ["מי שפגם בבריתו", "מיין מרירין", "מיין מסאבין", "זרע טמא", "מרה", "\"חרב פיות\"", "גיהנם"], "node_degrees": {"מי שפגם בבריתו": 4, "מיין מרירין": 2, "מיין מסאבין": 2, "זרע טמא": 1, "מרה": 1, "\"חרב פיות\"": 1, "גיהנם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 50, "label": "שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Guarding the Covenant", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all nodes in Torah #50 belonging to the positive/holy domain. Component B (4 nodes) forms the core: מיין מתיקין (sweet waters) = מיין דדכין (pure/clean waters) = זרע קדש (holy seed) = תקון הברית (guarding the covenant). These four are parallel names for the same spiritual reality — the purity that flows from guarding the covenant. The bechina equations are explicit in line 3 of the source text: 'מַיִּין מְתִיקִין זֶה בְּחִינַת מַיִּין דְּדַכְיָן, זֶרַע קֹדֶשׁ, שְׁמִירַת הַבְּרִית.' The dominant nodes are מיין דדכין and זרע קדש, each with 2 bechina connections, sharing the hub role. Component C (2 nodes: עצם, אריה) is merged into this package by shared essential nature: the Rebbe teaches that when a person has pure waters (the covenant-keeper's essence), his words enter his bones and the bones feel their sweetness — bone IS a bechina of lion (line 5: 'עֶצֶם זֶה בְּחִינַת אַרְיֵה'). The lion (not the dog) accepts the covenant-keeper's sacrifice as salvation. Bones and lion are not cause-and-effect extensions of the pure-waters cluster — they are parallel aspects of the same holy domain: the spiritual dimension of covenant-keeping as it manifests in speech, body, and divine reception. The merge is by shared essential nature, not by eitza flow.", "dominant_node": "מיין דדכין", "nodes": ["מיין מתיקין", "מיין דדכין", "זרע קדש", "תקון הברית", "עצם", "אריה"], "node_degrees": {"מיין דדכין": 2, "זרע קדש": 2, "מיין מתיקין": 1, "תקון הברית": 1, "עצם": 1, "אריה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עצם", "אריה"], "source_component": "Component C (isolated 2-node pair)", "reason": "The bone-lion pair (Component C) was isolated from Component B in the raw graph — no direct bechina edge connects עצם or אריה to any node in Component B. The framework requires merging 2-node isolated components into the thematically nearest larger package by shared nature. The nature of עצם-אריה is unambiguously the positive/holy domain: line 5 of the source text states 'עֶצֶם זֶה בְּחִינַת אַרְיֵה' in the context of the covenant-keeper's prayer. When the covenant-keeper has pure/sweet waters, his words enter his bones and the lion (not the dog) accepts his sacrifice as salvation. The lion is explicitly the positive counterpart of the dog: where the dog (Package 1, evil) devours the prayer of the covenant-violator, the lion (holy) receives the sacrifice of the covenant-keeper. The bone-lion pair shares its essential spiritual nature with the pure-waters cluster — both are aspects of holiness and covenant-keeping. This is a merge by shared essential nature (bechina domain), not by causal flow (eitza)."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 51, "label": "שֶׁקֶר וְרִבּוּי", "label_en": "Falsehood and Multiplicity", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #51 centers on שקר (falsehood) as the defining evil — the antithesis of divine oneness (אחד). The Rebbe explicitly equates sheker with multiplicity ('בקשו חשבנות רבים' — they sought many reckonings), because truth is singular (a silver vessel can only be called a silver vessel, one description) while falsehood is plural (it can be called gold, copper, or anything else). Sheker is also a bechina of דמעות (tears, salty water) because: sheker clouds the blood → melancho ly → tears flow → eyes dim → they 'lie' about what they see (showing big as small, one as two). The מלוח ('salty/bitter herb') of Iyov 30:4 is a bechina of tears — salty water that come through speech (שיחה = both 'speech' and 'bush'). Finally, מים מים (the cry 'water! water!' that R. Akiva warns against) is a bechina of sheker — salty water that does not satisfy thirst but multiplies it, just as falsehood does not quench spiritual need but amplifies it. The dominant node is שקר with degree 3.", "dominant_node": "שקר", "nodes": ["שקר", "\"בקשו חשבנות רבים\"", "דמעות", "\"מלוח\"", "מים מים"], "node_degrees": {"שקר": 3, "דמעות": 2, "\"בקשו חשבנות רבים\"": 1, "\"מלוח\"": 1, "מים מים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 51, "label": "יְשׁוּת וְשַׁיִשׁ טָהוֹר", "label_en": "Created Existence and Pure Marble", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This 3-node component describes the cosmological domain of post-creation existence — the state of the world after God brought creation from potentiality (כח) into actuality (פעל). שיש טהור (pure marble) is the symbol from R. Akiva's warning: when you arrive at stones of pure marble, don't say 'water water.' The Rebbe explains that pure marble is a bechina of אחר הפעלה — the post-creation world, the realm of yesh (existence), where the name 'pure' (טהור) becomes applicable for the first time (before creation there was no impurity, so the category of purity was irrelevant). אחר הפעלה is in turn a bechina of ישות וטהרה — 'existence and purity' — the compound defining quality of the post-creation world. These three nodes all describe the same metaphysical state: the world as it is after God created it. This is neutral (neither good nor evil) — it is the structural context within which falsehood and truth both operate. The dominant node is אחר הפעלה (degree 2) as the hub connecting שיש טהור and ישות וטהרה.", "dominant_node": "אחר הפעלה", "nodes": ["שיש טהור", "אחר הפעלה", "ישות וטהרה"], "node_degrees": {"אחר הפעלה": 2, "שיש טהור": 1, "ישות וטהרה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 51, "label": "טָהֳרָה וּבְחִירָה", "label_en": "Purity and Free Will", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This component groups the metaphysical structure of purity/free choice — the intermediary zone between holiness and impurity. טהרה (purity) is explicitly a bechina of בחירה (free will), because both are 'intermediaries between two things': purity stands between holiness (קדש) and impurity (טומאה) and through it impurity can be rectified; free will stands between the pre-creation absolute goodness and the post-creation possibility of evil. Purity is also a bechina of שמאל (the left side), because according to the Zohar the primary hold of impurity is from the left, and Levi — the tribe of purification ('וְטִהַרְתָּ אֶת בְּנֵי לֵוִי') — is the בחינת שמאל. שמאל and לוי are therefore both bechinot of purity. ממצע בין הקדש והטמאה (the intermediary between the holy and the impure, through which impurity is rectified) is the explicit definitional bechina of טהרה stated in the text (line 15). This package is neutral: it describes a structural position in the cosmic order — the middle ground — rather than alignment with either good or evil.", "dominant_node": "טהרה", "nodes": ["טהרה", "בחירה", "ממצע בין הקדש והטמאה, שעל ידו נתתקן הטמאה", "שמאל", "לוי"], "node_degrees": {"טהרה": 3, "שמאל": 2, "בחירה": 1, "ממצע בין הקדש והטמאה, שעל ידו נתתקן הטמאה": 1, "לוי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 51, "label": "אֱמֶת וְהַשְׁגָּחָה", "label_en": "Truth and Divine Providence", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Two small 2-node components were merged here by shared nature. Component E (אמת ↔ כלו אחד) describes truth as the domain of absolute unity — אמת is a bechina of כלו אחד (all is one), because truth is singular by nature (only one true statement can be made about any given thing, while falsehood is infinitely plural). Component D (עינים ↔ השגחה) describes the eyes as the instrument of divine providence — the eyes are a bechina of השגחה, as in 'עֵינַי בְּנֶאֶמְנֵי אָרֶץ' (My eyes are on the faithful of the land). The merge is grounded in the source text itself: the Rebbe explicitly chains these two pairs — 'through אמת, the השגחה of God rests on a person; through שקר, one removes the השגחה from oneself' (line 20, 31). The eyes (עינים = השגחה) watch over the truthful (אמת = כלו אחד). Both pairs share the same essential domain: the positive side of divine unity and watchfulness, as opposed to the multiplicity/falsehood of Package 1. The dominant node is אמת (degree 1 in bechina graph, but the conceptual center of the teaching's positive side).", "dominant_node": "אמת", "nodes": ["אמת", "כלו אחד", "עינים", "השגחה"], "node_degrees": {"אמת": 1, "כלו אחד": 1, "עינים": 1, "השגחה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_components": [["עינים", "השגחה"], ["אמת", "כלו אחד"]], "reason": "Both are 2-node isolated pairs. The merge is justified by shared nature (not eitza): all four nodes belong to the domain of divine truth/unity and its positive expression in the world. The source text chains them directly: אמת draws השגחה; eyes (עינים) = השגחה; God's eyes watch the אמת. They are four faces of the same reality — that truth, unity, divine watchfulness, and the instrument of watchfulness (eyes) are all bechinot of the one divine reality. Polarity is good for all four."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 52, "label": "מְחֻיַּב הַמְּצִיאוּת וְאַחְדוּת הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "Necessary Existence and Divine Unity", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central metaphysical cluster of Torah #52. The Rebbe's entire teaching revolves around the distinction between necessary existence (מחויב המציאות) — which belongs to God alone — and contingent existence (אפשרי המציאות) — which describes all created worlds. God's unity (אחדות השם) is explicitly equated with His necessary existence as one's 'shoresh' (root/source). Nishmat Yisrael's soul-root is in this Divine unity, and the purpose of human life is to return and be subsumed (להיכלל) within it. The concept of שרשו (his root) is used as a synonym for אחדות ה׳ = מחויב המציאות in lines 10 and 16, making it a bechina-equivalent. This package captures the ontological peak — the 'destination' that the entire teaching points toward.", "dominant_node": "מחויב המציאות", "nodes": ["מחויב המציאות", "אחדות השם יתברך", "שרשו", "השם יתברך לבד", "נשמות ישראל"], "node_degrees": {"מחויב המציאות": 8, "אחדות השם יתברך": 4, "שרשו": 3, "השם יתברך לבד": 2, "נשמות ישראל": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שרשו", "אחדות השם יתברך"], "reason": "Line 10 equates them explicitly: 'לִזְכּוֹת לָזֶה לְהִכָּלֵל בְּשָׁרְשׁוֹ, דְּהַיְנוּ לַחֲזֹר וּלְהִכָּלֵל בְּאַחְדוּת הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ, שֶׁהוּא מְחֻיַּב הַמְּצִיאוּת.' 'His root' is defined as divine unity which is necessary existence — these are three bechina labels for the same ultimate reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נשמות ישראל"], "reason": "Nishmat Yisrael are the souls whose shoresh IS the Divine unity. Lines 4-5 establish that the world's quasi-mechuyav status derives from Israel's souls having been emanated (נֶאֶצְלוּ). Israel's function is to return and merge into the Divine root — they are the human side of the mechuyav-metziut structure. Their destiny is in this package."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 52, "label": "בִּטּוּל וָאַיִן", "label_en": "Self-Annihilation and Nothingness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Bitul (self-annihilation) is the pivotal practical concept of the Torah — it is the state a person must achieve in order to be subsumed into divine unity. The Rebbe explicitly identifies bitul with the concept of 'mah' (nothingness/הַמָּה) in line 15: 'שֶׁיִּהְיֶה בִּבְחִינַת \"מָה\" בֶּאֱמֶת'. Line 24 defines bitul as 'מְבַטֵּל יֵשׁוּתוֹ וְנַעֲשֶׂה אַיִן' — to nullify one's yeshut and become ayin (nothing/ein). The phrase 'מְפַנֶּה לִבּוֹ לְבַטָּלָה' from the Mishna (line 20) is explicitly glossed as 'כְּדֵי לִזְכּוֹת לִבְחִינַת בִּטּוּל' — clearing the heart IS the bechina of bitul. These three — bitul, 'mah', and ayin — form one cluster: different names for the same state of radical self-nullification before God. Yeshut (egotistical selfhood) is what bitul overcomes, making it the natural conceptual antipode within this cluster.", "dominant_node": "בטול", "nodes": ["בטול", "\"מה\"", "אין", "מפנה לבו לבטלה", "יֵשׁוּת"], "node_degrees": {"בטול": 6, "\"מה\"": 2, "אין": 2, "מפנה לבו לבטלה": 2, "יֵשׁוּת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"מה\"", "אין"], "reason": "Line 15 identifies bitul with 'מָה': 'שֶׁיִּהְיֶה בִּבְחִינַת \"מָה\" בֶּאֱמֶת, עַד שֶׁיִּזְכֶּה לִבְחִינַת בִּטּוּל בֶּאֱמֶת'. Line 24 states that bitul means 'מְבַטֵּל יֵשׁוּתוֹ וְנַעֲשֶׂה אַיִן'. Both 'mah' and 'ayin' are bechina-names for the same state of complete self-nullification before God — they are parallel aspects of the same essential nature as bitul."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מפנה לבו לבטלה"], "reason": "The Mishna phrase 'מְפַנֶּה לִבּוֹ לְבַטָּלָה' is reread in line 20 as the bechina of bitul itself ('כְּדֵי לִזְכּוֹת לִבְחִינַת בִּטּוּל'). The clearing of heart is the human behavioral expression that IS bitul — it shares the same essential nature, not a cause-and-effect relationship to it."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יֵשׁוּת"], "reason": "Yeshut (egotistical selfhood, physicality) is the explicit antonym that bitul overcomes. Line 24-25: 'בִּטּוּל כָּל הַיֵּשׁוּת'. Yeshut belongs in this cluster as the structural counter-pole that defines the domain — it is what bitul IS in opposition to. It is not a separate evil package because it is not autonomous evil; rather it is the precondition of bitul that the practice dissolves."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 52, "label": "הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת, לַיְלָה וְדֶרֶךְ יְחִידִי", "label_en": "Hitbodedut: Solitary Practice, Night, and the Solitary Path", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups the three conditions for effective hitbodedut that R. Nachman derives from the Mishna. The Mishna's three elements are reread as bechina-equivalences of hitbodedut's requirements: (1) 'הַנֵּעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה' (the one who is awake at night) = the TIME of hitbodedut, because at night the world is free of worldly distraction (line 25: 'הַיְנוּ: הַנֵּעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה'); (2) 'וְהַמְהַלֵּךְ בַּדֶּרֶךְ יְחִידִי' (who walks the solitary path) = the PLACE of hitbodedut — a place where no people walk (line 25: 'הַיְנוּ: וְהַמְהַלֵּךְ בַּדֶּרֶךְ יְחִידִי'); and hitbodedut itself = speaking one's inner prayer (שִׂיחָה) between oneself and one's Maker (line 18: 'הַנֵּעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה – כִּפְשׁוּטוֹ, דְּהַיְנוּ שֶׁהוּא נֵעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה, וּמִתְבּוֹדֵד וּמְפָרֵשׁ שִׂיחָתוֹ'). These are all aspects of the same practice-domain — not cause-and-effect but different faces of the same spiritual act.", "dominant_node": "התבודדות", "nodes": ["התבודדות", "הנעור בלילה", "והמהלך בדרך יחידי", "מקום מיחד", "זמן מיחד", "שיחה בינו לבין קונו", "לילה"], "node_degrees": {"התבודדות": 7, "הנעור בלילה": 4, "והמהלך בדרך יחידי": 4, "שיחה בינו לבין קונו": 3, "לילה": 3, "מקום מיחד": 2, "זמן מיחד": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הנעור בלילה", "לילה", "זמן מיחד"], "reason": "Line 25 equates these explicitly: 'וְהַזְּמַן הוּא בַּלַּילָה, הַיְנוּ: הַנֵּעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה, שֶׁאָז הַכֹּל יְשֵׁנִים.' The time (זמן מיחד) IS the night (לילה) which IS the state of the one who is awake at night (הנעור בלילה) — three bechina-names for the same temporal dimension of hitbodedut."}, {"merged_nodes": ["והמהלך בדרך יחידי", "מקום מיחד"], "reason": "Line 25 equates them: 'וְהַמָּקוֹם הוּא בְּדֶרֶךְ יְחִידִי ... הַיְנוּ: וְהַמְהַלֵּךְ בַּדֶּרֶךְ יְחִידִי.' The place (מקום מיחד) IS the solitary path — these are bechina-equivalents for the spatial dimension of hitbodedut."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שיחה בינו לבין קונו"], "reason": "Line 18 uses 'הַיְנוּ' to equate the night-watcher with being awake at night AND doing hitbodedut AND speaking one's prayer: 'דְּהַיְנוּ שֶׁהוּא נֵעוֹר בַּלַּיְלָה, וּמִתְבּוֹדֵד וּמְפָרֵשׁ שִׂיחָתוֹ בֵּינוֹ לְבֵין קוֹנוֹ.' The sicha (personal prayer-speech) is the essence of hitbodedut — same nature, not cause-and-effect."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 52, "label": "אֶפְשָׁרִי הַמְּצִיאוּת וְטָעוּת הָאֶפִּיקוֹרְסִים", "label_en": "Contingent Existence and the Epikoros Error", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The necessary counter-pole to Package 1. Torah #52 opens by refuting epikorsim (heretics/deniers) who claim the world has necessary existence. The error (טעות) and those who hold it (אפיקורסים) share an essential nature: they represent the false attribution of divine necessity to created existence. Afshari ha-metziut (contingent existence) is the correct ontological status of the world — the mirror-concept to Package 1's mechuyav ha-metziut. The Rebbe's theological correction is that everything other than God is afshari (contingent). The epikoros worldview is evil not because it is malicious but because it denies God's absolute singularity as the only necessary being. This is the conceptual anti-pole to Package 1 in the Torah's good/evil mirror structure.", "dominant_node": "אפשרי המציאות", "nodes": ["אפשרי המציאות", "אפיקורסים", "טעות האפיקורסים", "כל העולמות"], "node_degrees": {"אפשרי המציאות": 5, "אפיקורסים": 3, "טעות האפיקורסים": 2, "כל העולמות": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כל העולמות"], "reason": "Lines 3 and 6 establish 'כָּל הָעוֹלָמוֹת עִם כָּל אֲשֶׁר בָּהֶם הֵם אֶפְשָׁרִי הַמְּצִיאוּת.' All the worlds in their true status ARE afshari ha-metziut — this is a bechina equation (shared essential nature: the worlds and contingent existence are parallel aspects of the same reality)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["טעות האפיקורסים"], "reason": "Line 2 identifies the epikorsim and their error as a single conceptual unit. Line 6: 'וּמִזֶּה נִשְׁתַּלְשֵׁל וְנִמְשָׁךְ הַטָּעוּת שֶׁל הָאֶפִּיקוֹרְסִים.' The error (טעות) and the errorists (אפיקורסים) share the same essential domain — they are the evil/false pole of the mechuyav/afshari distinction."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 53, "label": "דַּעַת הַשֵּׁם וְאוֹת הֵ\"א", "label_en": "Divine Da'at and the Letter Heh", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single connected bechina component in Torah #53 centers on the letter ה\"א as the shared nature of two concepts: divine da'at (דַּעַת הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ) and perfect/complete human da'at (שְׁלֵמוּת הַדַּעַת). Both are explicitly called a 'בְּחִינַת הֵ\"א' in the source text. The letter ה\"א is dominant (degree 2) because it is the hub through which both concepts are equated. The teaching's core claim is that when a person achieves shleimut ha-da'at — bringing their intellect from potential (כֹּחַ) to actuality (פֹּעַל) and narrowing the gap to only the five irreducible differences — their da'at nurses directly from divine da'at, which is the ה\"א. The ה\"א here refers to the divine name-letter added to Avraham's name (אַבְרָם → אַבְרָהָם), embodying the descent of divine knowledge into the human frame. This is the positive pole and the destination of the teaching's argument.", "dominant_node": "אות ה\"א", "nodes": ["אות ה\"א", "דעת השם יתברך", "שלמות הדעת"], "node_degrees": {"אות ה\"א": 2, "דעת השם יתברך": 1, "שלמות הדעת": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 53, "label": "כְּלֵי הַדַּעַת — אִשָּׁה, דִּירָה וְכֵלִים", "label_en": "The Three Vessels of Da'at — Woman, Dwelling, and Vessels", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package is sourced entirely from the gap analysis (source text lines 9–12), as the three הַיְנוּ equations are not yet represented as bechina edges in the dataset. The Talmudic teaching from Berakhot 57b — 'three things broaden a person's da'at: a beautiful woman, a beautiful dwelling, and beautiful vessels' — is decoded by the Rebbe via three explicit הַיְנוּ bechina equations. Each Talmudic symbol is shown to be a bechina of a spiritual/structural prerequisite for reaching shleimut ha-da'at: (1) אִשָּׁה נָאָה, הַיְנוּ הַחֹמֶר — the body/matter (חֹמֶר) called 'woman', which must be properly balanced (מְמֻזָּג בַּמֶּזֶג הַשָּׁוֶה) so as to be fit to receive sekhel; (2) דִּירָה נָאָה, הַיְנוּ הַיִּרְאָה — the beautiful dwelling is the fear-of-sin (יִרְאַת חֵטְאוֹ), because one's awe must precede one's wisdom; (3) כֵּלִים, הַיְנוּ תַּלְמִידִים הֲגוּנִים — the vessels are worthy students who receive from the teacher. These three are not sequential causes but three parallel aspects (different dimensions) of the same essential domain: the required conditions/vessels (הֲכָנוֹת) that together constitute the structural readiness for shleimut ha-da'at. Polarity is good because these are all positive prerequisites on the path toward divine knowledge.", "dominant_node": "תלמידים הגונים", "nodes": ["אשה נאה", "חומר ממוזג", "דירה נאה", "יראה", "כלים", "תלמידים הגונים"], "node_degrees": {"תלמידים הגונים": 2, "אשה נאה": 1, "חומר ממוזג": 1, "דירה נאה": 1, "יראה": 1, "כלים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אשה נאה", "חומר ממוזג"], "reason": "Line 10: 'אִשָּׁה, הַיְנוּ הַחֹמֶר מְכֻנֶּה בְּשֵׁם אִשָּׁה, שֶׁיְּהֵא לוֹ חֹמֶר מְמֻזָּג בַּמֶּזֶג הַשָּׁוֶה, שֶׁיְּהֵא רָאוּי לְקַבֵּל שֵׂכֶל.' The beautiful woman IS the properly balanced matter/body — an explicit הַיְנוּ equation making them bechina-equivalents."}, {"merged_nodes": ["דירה נאה", "יראה"], "reason": "Line 11: 'וְדִירָה נָאָה, הַיְנוּ הַיִּרְאָה, כַּמַּאֲמָר (שַׁבָּת ל\"א:): חֲבָל עַל מָאן דְּלֵית לֵהּ דַּרְתָא, כִּי צָרִיךְ שֶׁיְּהֵא יִרְאַת חֶטְאוֹ קוֹדֶמֶת לְחָכְמָתוֹ.' The beautiful dwelling IS the fear/awe — explicit הַיְנוּ bechina equation. Without a 'home' (yirat chet), the wisdom has nowhere to dwell."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כלים", "תלמידים הגונים"], "reason": "Line 12: 'וְכֵלִים, הַיְנוּ תַּלְמִידִים הֲגוּנִים שֶׁיְּקַבְּלוּ מִמֶּנּוּ.' The vessels ARE worthy students — explicit הַיְנוּ bechina equation. The students are the containers (keilim) that receive the teacher's da'at and by receiving they sharpen and complete it ('וּמִתַּלְמִידַי יוֹתֵר מִכֻּלָּן')."}], "source_status": "gap_sourced", "gap_source_note": "None of the six nodes in this package appear as bechina edges in torahData.js for reference===53. All three הַיְנוּ equations are from the source text (torah_53.txt lines 9–12) and await entry as bechina edges in the dataset."}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 53, "label": "הוֹלָדָה וּבָנִים", "label_en": "Procreation and Children", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the spiritual procreation domain — the ultimate fruit of shleimut ha-da'at in Torah #53. The teaching opens with the declaration that procreation (הוֹלָדָה) depends on the ה\"א, and the Rebbe demonstrates this through Abraham and Sarah. The key bechina equation here is: תַּלְמִידִים נִקְרָאִים בָּנִים (students are CALLED children — explicit נִקְרָא language, line 4). This makes spiritual students the bechina-equivalent of physical children, establishing that the Torah is about both biological procreation and spiritual 'giving birth' to new followers of divine service. Abraham's bringing converts (גֵּרִים) and Sarah's bringing female converts were their path to completing da'at — students/converts ARE their children in the full bechina sense. Polarity is good: this is the positive outcome-domain that the entire teaching aims toward.", "dominant_node": "הולדה", "nodes": ["הולדה", "בנים", "תלמידים", "גרים"], "node_degrees": {"הולדה": 3, "בנים": 2, "תלמידים": 2, "גרים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תלמידים", "בנים"], "reason": "Line 4: 'תַּלְמִידִים נִקְרָאִים בָּנִים (סִפְרִי פָּרָשַׁת וָאֶתְחַנַּן עַל-פָּסוּק וְשִׁנַּנְתָּם לְבָנֶיךָ), כִּי עַל יָדָם בָּא הַהוֹלָדָה.' Students ARE CALLED children — explicit נִקְרָא bechina language. They share the same essential nature as one's children: they are the fruit of the teacher's spiritual procreation. This is the central bechina that grounds the Torah's claim that engaging with students is the path to true spiritual birth."}, {"merged_nodes": ["גרים", "תלמידים"], "reason": "Line 5: 'וּבִשְׁבִיל זֶה אַבְרָהָם וְשָׂרָה טָרְחוּ אֶת עַצְמָן לְגַיֵּר גֵּרִים, כִּי עַל-יְדֵי-זֶה הִשְׁלִימוּ דַּעְתָּם, בִּבְחִינַת: וּמִתַּלְמִידַי יוֹתֵר מִכֻּלָּם.' The converts serve exactly the same function as students — they are the human raw material whose questions and challenges sharpen the teacher's da'at. The Rebbe explicitly equates גֵּרִים with תַּלְמִידִים via the same proof-text (ומתלמידי יותר מכולם), making them bechina-equivalents in this Torah."}], "source_status": "gap_sourced", "gap_source_note": "The nodes הולדה and בנים appear in torahData.js for reference===53 only as nodes in eitza edges (id 829: דעת→הולדה as eitza; id 1567: דעת השלם→לבנים as eitza). The bechina equation תלמידים=בנים (נקראים language) is not recorded as a bechina edge for reference===53. These await entry as bechina edges."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "זִכָּרוֹן וְעָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי", "label_en": "Memory and the World to Come", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest good-side component in Torah 54 centers on זִכָּרוֹן (memory/remembrance) as the spiritual act of attaching one's thought to the World to Come. The Rebbe identifies memory as the prophetic spirit — the רוּחַ טוֹבָה within a person (the good points/נְקֻדּוֹת טוֹבוֹת) — and equates memory with the possibility of receiving רוּחַ נְבוּאָה. A male child (בֵּן זָכָר) is a bechina of זִכָּרוֹן, because the very word זָכָר means memory. The practice of attaching one's thought to the World to Come in general terms (לְאַדְבָּקָא מַחֲשַׁבְתָּא בְּעָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי) is the definition of זִכָּרוֹן itself. The sub-cluster of sleep (שֵׁנָה) is merged here: during sleep the soul ascends to the World to Come — this IS the general, collective form of the same attachment, called לְאַדְבָּקָא מַחֲשַׁבְתָּא בְּעָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי בִּכְלָלִיּוּת, which in turn is the bechina of the Divine name ה' אֱלֹקַי (the complete Name = World to Come, as in Saba de-Mishpatim). All these nodes share the single essential nature: the soul's orientation toward its ultimate source.", "dominant_node": "זכרון", "nodes": ["זכרון", "בן זכר", "רוח נבואה", "לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי", "רוח טובה", "נקדות טובות", "שנה", "לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי בכלליות", "ה אלקי"], "node_degrees": {"זכרון": 3, "רוח נבואה": 2, "רוח טובה": 2, "לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי בכלליות": 2, "בן זכר": 1, "לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי": 1, "נקדות טובות": 1, "שנה": 1, "ה אלקי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שנה", "לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי בכלליות", "ה אלקי"], "reason": "The 3-node component {שנה, לאדבקא מחשבתא בעלמא דאתי בכלליות, ה' אלקי} describes sleep as the collective/general form of attaching one's thought to the World to Come. The Rebbe states: 'הַשֵּׁנָה הִיא בְּחִינַת לְאַדְבָּקָא מַחֲשַׁבְתָּא בְּעָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי בִּכְלָלִיּוּת'. This is structurally the same domain as the main זכרון component — the soul-orientation toward the World to Come — differing only in that the main component describes the detailed/particular (פְּרָטִיּוּת) form and this describes the general (כְּלָלִיּוּת) form. They are the two faces of the same essential nature and belong in one package."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה", "label_en": "The Power of Imagination (Evil)", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the central evil cluster of Torah 54. The koach ha-medame (imaginative faculty) is identified as the force that corrupts memory, produces slander (lashon ha-ra), and destroys the divine/prophetic orientation. All its names — הַמְדַמֶּה, כֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה (three variant node_ids for the same entity) — form one conceptual hub. The evil-spirit chain shows its full identity: it IS עַצְבוּת רוּחַ (dejection of spirit) = רוּחַ נְכֵאָה = רוּחַ רָעָה = רוּחַ שְׁטוּת (spirit of folly). The kelipot (קְּלִפּוֹת) are a bechina of this force; the dream (חֲלוֹם) is its mode of operation (it is the dream-power); and Pharaoh (פַּרְעֹה) is its archetypal embodiment — the one who stands over the Pishon river (= mouth of the one who recites halachot). The 2-node component {פִּישׁוֹן, פִּי שׁוֹנֵה הֲלָכוֹת} is merged here because in the text Pharaoh (= imagination) specifically stands over the Pishon (= the שׁוֹנֶה הֲלָכוֹת), preying on the person who recites halachot to seek a body to inhabit. The Pishon-river/halachot-reciter pair is the target and habitat of the evil imagination — its bechina nature is inseparable from the imagination cluster.", "dominant_node": "כח המדמה", "nodes": ["כח המדמה", "קלפות", "חלום", "פרעה", "המדמה", "מדמה", "עצבות", "רוח נכאה", "רוח רעה", "רוח שטות", "פישון", "פי שונה הלכות"], "node_degrees": {"כח המדמה": 3, "רוח רעה": 3, "המדמה": 2, "עצבות": 2, "רוח נכאה": 2, "קלפות": 1, "חלום": 1, "פרעה": 1, "מדמה": 1, "רוח שטות": 1, "פישון": 1, "פי שונה הלכות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["פישון", "פי שונה הלכות"], "reason": "The 2-node component {פישון — פי שונה הלכות} belongs in the evil imagination package because the Rebbe's text explicitly states: 'הַיְאֹר זֶה פִּישׁוֹן, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת פִּי שׁוֹנֵה הֲלָכוֹת, שֶׁמִּתְגַּבֵּר עֲלֵיהֶם הַכֹּחַ הַמְדַמֶּה'. Pharaoh (= the evil imagination) stands over this river. The pishon/shone-halachot node IS the habitat of the imagination — described as the target it always circles. The shared bechina nature: both the imagination and the pishon are the context/meeting-point where good Torah knowledge is corrupted by the imagination mixing into it."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "רַע עַיִן וּמִיתַת הַלֵּב", "label_en": "Evil Eye and Death of the Heart", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package captures the second evil cluster in Torah 54: the evil eye (רַע עַיִן) which is identical in essence to death of the heart (מִיתַת הַלֵּב), which is itself the breaking of the tablets (שִׁבְרֵי לוּחוֹת) — the source of forgetting (שִׁכְחָה). The 3-node component {מַיִם, לֵב, לוּחוֹת} is merged here because the Rebbe states: 'לֵב הוּא בְּחִינַת לוּחוֹת' — the heart IS the tablets. And in the same passage: 'מִיתַת הַלֵּב הִיא בְּחִינַת שִׁבְרֵי לוּחוֹת'. Death of the heart = broken tablets; the heart = whole tablets. These are two halves of the same conceptual structure. The water (מַיִם) = heart equation (from the verse 'שִׁפְכִי כַמַּיִם לִבֵּךְ') belongs here as the positive counterpart of the broken-heart image — water can flow freely when the heart is alive, and is poured out when the heart dies. Forgetting (שִׁכְחָה) is the direct outcome of this evil eye/heart-death, and is also present in the same bechina chain.", "dominant_node": "מיתת הלב", "nodes": ["שכחה", "מיתת הלב", "שברי לוחות", "רע עין", "מים", "לב", "לוחות"], "node_degrees": {"מיתת הלב": 3, "לב": 2, "שכחה": 1, "שברי לוחות": 1, "רע עין": 1, "מים": 1, "לוחות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים", "לב", "לוחות"], "reason": "The 3-node component {מים — לב — לוחות} (water=heart, heart=tablets) shares the same essential structural domain as the main evil-eye/death-of-heart component. The argument's logic is: the heart is the tablets; when evil eye strikes, the heart dies = tablets break = forgetting. The whole-tablet/living-heart side (לב=לוחות, מים=לב) and the broken-tablet/dead-heart side (מיתת הלב=שברי לוחות) are the two poles of the same concept — the heart as the seat of memory. They belong together because they describe the same structural entity (heart=tablets) in its healthy and broken states."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "הִסְתַּפְּקוּת וְצַדִּיק", "label_en": "Contentment, Light, and the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups the positive pole of the Torah's second axis: the Tzaddik's light and the flow of blessing downward. The core component connects Torah (תּוֹרָה) to contentment (הִסְתַּפְּקוּת) — Torah is like the Tree of Life, providing sufficient sustenance to each person. Contentment in turn IS 'wrapping oneself in light like a garment' (עוֹטֶה אוֹר כַּשַּׂלְמָה), which equals full-light (אוֹר מָלֵא). The Tzaddik-commerce cluster {מַשָּׂא וּמַתָּן, נוֹטֶה שָׁמַיִם כַּיְרִיעָה, יִחוּד צַדִּיק וּכְנֶסֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל} is merged here: the Torah says commerce itself (in its essential nature) is the union of Tzaddik and Knesset Israel — the Tzaddik spreads the tent-cloth (yeri'ah) of heaven over the Ark of the Covenant below. This 'spreading' of the yeri'ah IS the same light/blessing that contentment embodies. Three further small components merge in: {אֲרוֹן הַבְּרִית — עָנִי} because the Aron is the poor/receiving-vessel that sits beneath the yeri'ah = Knesset Israel in the commerce metaphor; {שְׁבִירָה — אוֹתִיּוֹת שֶׁנִּשְׁבְּרוּ} because commerce (mashsa u-matan) IS the mechanism for elevating the broken letters/fallen sparks that need to return to their root — the shared nature is the tikun/restoration that commerce/Torah provides; {בְּרָכוֹת — אֱמֶת} because the good-eyed person (טוֹב עַיִן) is blessed — blessings ARE truth, and the Tzaddik/full-light person is the source of blessing.", "dominant_node": "הסתפקות", "nodes": ["תורה", "הסתפקות", "עוטה אור כשלמה", "אור מלא", "משא ומתן", "\"נוטה שמים כיריעה\"", "יחוד צדיק וכנסת ישראל", "ארון הברית", "עני", "שבירה", "אותיות שנשברו ונפלו לכל דבר ודבר מזה העולם", "ברכות", "אמת"], "node_degrees": {"הסתפקות": 2, "עוטה אור כשלמה": 2, "\"נוטה שמים כיריעה\"": 2, "תורה": 1, "אור מלא": 1, "משא ומתן": 1, "יחוד צדיק וכנסת ישראל": 1, "ארון הברית": 1, "עני": 1, "שבירה": 1, "אותיות שנשברו ונפלו לכל דבר ודבר מזה העולם": 1, "ברכות": 1, "אמת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["משא ומתן", "\"נוטה שמים כיריעה\"", "יחוד צדיק וכנסת ישראל"], "reason": "Commerce (mashsa u-matan) is a bechina of 'נוֹטֶה שָׁמַיִם כַּיְרִיעָה' which is a bechina of 'יִחוּד צַדִּיק וּכְנֶסֶת יִשְׂרָאֵל'. The Rebbe says commerce IS the union of Tzaddik and Knesset Israel. The Tzaddik spreads the yeri'ah (tent-cloth of heaven) over the Aron below. This IS the light-giving / blessing-flowing reality described by the main contentment cluster. The shared bechina nature: the Tzaddik giving downward = contentment = light as a garment."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ארון הברית", "עני"], "reason": "The Aron ha-Brit IS the 'עָנִי' (poor/receiving vessel) — explicitly stated: 'אֲרוֹן הַבְּרִית שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת עָנִי, הִיא תַּחַת הַיְרִיעָה'. The Aron is Knesset Israel below, receiving from the Tzaddik above. It belongs in the Tzaddik/light/commerce cluster as the receiving pole of the relationship, the counterpart to the yeri'ah/Tzaddik that gives."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שבירה", "אותיות שנשברו ונפלו לכל דבר ודבר מזה העולם"], "reason": "The Rebbe explains that commerce (mashsa u-matan) functions precisely because broken holy letters/sparks (שְׁבִירָה = fallen letters) need to reach the person whose soul-root can elevate them. Commerce is the vessel for this elevation — it IS the mechanism of gathering scattered light back into the Tzaddik. The shared bechina nature of שבירה/broken-letters with the commerce/light cluster: both describe the flow and restoration of scattered divine sparks, which is what the Tzaddik's commerce/contentment model facilitates."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ברכות", "אמת"], "reason": "Blessings ARE truth ('הַמִּתְבָּרֵךְ בָּאָרֶץ יִתְבָּרֵךְ בֵּאלֹקֵי אָמֶן'). In the Abba Shaul story, his dvekut/truth caused a cave to open — and through truth, blessings flow. This is contextually part of the good-eye/light domain: 'טוֹב עַיִן יְבֹרָךְ'. The Tzaddik (Package 4 = light/contentment/full-measure) is the one whose eye is good and from whom blessings flow. Blessings=truth connects to the Tzaddik-as-light cluster."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "תְּפִלִּין וְשִׂמְחָה", "label_en": "Tefillin and Joy — The Daily Mitzvot that Guard Memory", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all the physical practices and instruments through which ordinary people (הֲמוֹן עַם who cannot intellectually grasp the hidden divine hints) nonetheless achieve the same spiritual connection. Tefillin (תְּפִלִּין) are the sekhel/intellect (בְּחִינַת מֹחִין) — the daily amplification of understanding (הַגְדָּלַת הַשֵּׂכֶל); they are also life (חַיִּים). The knot of tefillin (קֶשֶׁר שֶׁל תְּפִלִּין) is identified as 'acharai' (the back/behind), the seat of Mashiach (מֶלֶךְ אָסוּר בָּרְהָטִים). Tzitzit (צִיצִית) are the tzimtzum/contraction (צִמְצוּם) — the left-side/din that precedes the right-side/wisdom. Joy (שִׂמְחָה) is playing music with the hand (מְנַגֵּן בַּיָּד) — the hand (יָד) being the instrument by which the good spirit is gathered and the evil imagination is subdued. Prayer (תְּפִלָּה) is 'הוֹד וְהָדָר' = the feet of holiness clothed in this world. And the verse 'הָאָרֶץ הֲדֹם רַגְלָי' equals the feet of holiness clothed in this world. All these nodes share the single essential nature: they are the instruments/vehicles through which holiness descends into the ordinary world and memory of the World to Come is maintained in daily life. Two small components are merged in: the tefillin-knot/acharai pair (same tefillin-cluster), and the prayer/hod-vehadar pair (same feet-of-holiness domain described in the opening of the Torah).", "dominant_node": "תפלין", "nodes": ["תפלין", "הגדלת השכל", "חיים", "קשר של תפלין", "אחורי", "ציצית", "צמצום", "יד", "שמחה", "מנגן ביד", "תפלה", "\"הוד והדר\"", "\"והארץ הדם רגלי\"", "רגלי הקדשה המלבשין בזה העולם"], "node_degrees": {"תפלין": 2, "שמחה": 2, "קשר של תפלין": 1, "אחורי": 1, "ציצית": 1, "צמצום": 1, "יד": 1, "מנגן ביד": 1, "הגדלת השכל": 1, "חיים": 1, "תפלה": 1, "\"הוד והדר\"": 1, "\"והארץ הדם רגלי\"": 1, "רגלי הקדשה המלבשין בזה העולם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קשר של תפלין", "אחורי"], "reason": "The knot of tefillin (קֶשֶׁר שֶׁל תְּפִלִּין) is a bechina of 'אֲחוֹרַי' (Berakhot 7: 'וְרָאִיתָ אֶת אֲחוֹרָי = זֶה קֶשֶׁר שֶׁל תְּפִלִּין'). The knot of tefillin belongs structurally with the tefillin cluster (same object, different aspect of it), and is directly connected to the Mashiach concept (Package 6) via the אחורי = place where Mashiach is bound. In this Torah, the tefillin-knot/acharai is the mechanism through which Mashiach subdues the evil eye. It shares the bechina nature of the tefillin domain: the physical mitzvah garment that connects to the highest spiritual power."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ציצית", "צמצום"], "reason": "Tzitzit = tzimtzum/contraction (the blue thread = throne of judgment = left side = restriction). The Rebbe explicitly pairs tzitzit (left/restriction) with tefillin (right/expansion of mind): 'צִיצִית קוֹדֶם לִתְפִלִּין — שְׂמֹאלוֹ תַּחַת לְרֹאשִׁי, וְאַחַר כָּךְ: וִימִינוֹ תְּחַבְּקֵנִי'. They are the two complementary mitzvot of the morning, the left-and-right pair of the daily practice. Their shared bechina nature: both are tools for proper relationship with the world-this-world represents (regalim/feet of holiness)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תפלה", "\"הוד והדר\""], "reason": "Prayer (תְּפִלָּה) is 'חַיֵּי שָׁעָה' — the life of the hour, meaning the life that is clothed in this world. It is a bechina of 'הָאָרֶץ הֲדֹם רַגְלָי' — the feet of holiness in this world. 'הוֹד וְהָדָר' from the Psalm verse is explicitly called the feet of holiness (רַגְלִין) clothed in this world. Prayer/hod-vehadar belongs in the daily-mitzvot cluster that forms Package 5 because it is the same domain as tefillin and tzitzit — the ordinary person's tools for maintaining the spiritual connection."}, {"merged_nodes": ["\"והארץ הדם רגלי\"", "רגלי הקדשה המלבשין בזה העולם"], "reason": "The verse 'הָאָרֶץ הֲדֹם רַגְלָי' IS the bechina of the feet of holiness clothed in this world. This 2-node pair describes the structural concept underlying this entire package: this world is the footstool of holiness, the lowest level of the divine that is directly accessible. It belongs in Package 5 as the foundational metaphor for the entire set of daily mitzvot described there."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 54, "label": "כֹּהֵן וְאַהֲבָה", "label_en": "The Kohen and Sacred Love — Good Eye and Mashiach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the positive instrument of tikkun in Torah 54: the quality of sacred love (אַהֲבָה דִּקְדֻשָּׁה) which is the kohen (priest), which is Avraham who loved God, which is the posture of 'kamatz ha-kohen' (gathering/grasping inward). The kohen is the one with the good eye (טוֹב עַיִן) as opposed to the evil eye (רַע עַיִן). To fix the evil eye, one must go to the root of kingship (שֹׁרֶשׁ הַמַּלְכוּת) which is Mashiach (מָשִׁיחַ) — because Mashiach is called 'טוֹב עַיִן' (David: יְפֵה עֵינַיִם וְטוֹב רֹאִי). The 2-node component {שֹׁרֶשׁ הַמַּלְכוּת — מָשִׁיחַ} is merged here because the Rebbe's logic is: the tikkun for evil eye is the humbling at the root of malkhut = Mashiach = the good-eyed one. This is the same domain as the kohen/love cluster: both describe the quality of tzaddik-with-good-eye that opposes the evil imagination. The 2-node component {מְעָרָה — דְּבֵקוּת} (cave = dvekut) is merged here because the story of Abba Shaul opening the cave through his truth (אֱמֶת) is the narrative illustration of the kohen/good-eye quality: one who has dvekut and truth has the cave of closeness to God opened to them. The cave/dvekut IS the experiential manifestation of the kohen/love state.", "dominant_node": "כהן", "nodes": ["כהן", "\"אברהם אוהבי\"", "\"אתה כהן\"", "\"וקמץ הכהן\"", "אהבה", "שרש המלכות", "משיח", "מערה", "דבקות"], "node_degrees": {"כהן": 2, "\"אברהם אוהבי\"": 2, "\"אתה כהן\"": 2, "\"וקמץ הכהן\"": 2, "אהבה": 2, "שרש המלכות": 1, "משיח": 1, "מערה": 1, "דבקות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שרש המלכות", "משיח"], "reason": "The 2-node component {שֹׁרֶשׁ הַמַּלְכוּת — מָשִׁיחַ} connects directly to the kohen/love domain because the Rebbe teaches: when a person falls into evil eye of pride (envy of another's elevation), the tikkun is to humble oneself before the root of malkhut = Mashiach. And Mashiach is 'יְפֵה עֵינַיִם וְטוֹב רֹאִי' — the one with the good eye. Mashiach and kohen (= Abraham = love) are both expressions of the same essential quality: loving freely, seeing with the good eye, the capacity to receive and transmit divine light without the distortion of the imagination."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מערה", "דבקות"], "reason": "The 2-node component {מְעָרָה — דְּבֵקוּת} belongs in the kohen/love cluster because the Rebbe narrates Abba Shaul's story as an illustration of how a person who engages in tikkun of evil eye can achieve dvekut. The cave (מְעָרָה) that opens beneath the person of truth is bechina of dvekut (from 'כְּמַעַר אִישׁ וְלֹיוֹת'). Dvekut = the state of the kohen (avodah from love = clinging to God). The shared bechina nature: the kohen/love/good-eye state IS dvekut — clinging upward rather than gazing sideways with envy."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 55, "label": "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל וּקְדֻשָּׁתָהּ", "label_en": "The Land of Israel and Its Holiness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #55 centers on ארץ ישראל as the dominant node (3 direct bechina connections). The Torah teaches that seeing the downfall of the wicked is only possible through the bechina of Eretz Yisrael — the right side (ימין), the permanent divine gaze (תמיד), and Binyamin (who was born in Eretz Yisrael, being the son-of-the-right-hand). The concept of חלה merges here because the Torah explicitly equates חלה with ירשת ארץ ישראל — the chalah offering is the concrete mitzvah of inheriting the Land, and where the Avot are, the Shekhinah is. Together ימין, תמיד, בנימין, חלה, and ירשת ארץ ישראל are all expressions of the same essential domain: the holiness of Eretz Yisrael present even in exile. The word גם (from 'גם זאת' of Vayikra 26:41) is the halachic/scriptural expression for this presence of Eretz Yisrael holiness in exile and belongs in this package.", "dominant_node": "ארץ ישראל", "nodes": ["ארץ ישראל", "ימין", "תמיד", "בנימין", "חלה", "ירשת ארץ ישראל", "גם"], "node_degrees": {"ארץ ישראל": 3, "חלה": 2, "ימין": 1, "תמיד": 1, "בנימין": 1, "ירשת ארץ ישראל": 1, "גם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גם", "חלה", "ירשת ארץ ישראל"], "reason": "These three nodes form a chain: גם ↔ חלה ↔ ירשת ארץ ישראל. The Torah explicitly states that the Shekhinah (= בחינת חלה) is where the Avot are, and that חלה = ירשת ארץ ישראל. The word גם (from 'וְאַף גַּם זֹאת בְּאֶרֶץ אֹיְבֵיהֶם') is defined as the ability to reveal ארץ ישראל holiness even in exile — it is a bechina of the chalah measure (שיעור מ\"ג ביצים = גם numerically). All three are expressions of the same domain as ארץ ישראל and are properly merged into this package rather than standing as a separate 3-node package, because their shared nature IS the Land's holiness."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 55, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וֶאֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Prayer and Faith", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package centers on תפלה as the dominant concept, built from four separate small bechina pairs merged by shared nature. The פרה (red cow) is a bechina of prayer (explicitly: 'פָּרָה, זֶהוּ בְחִינַת תְּפִלָּה'). ישרת לב is a bechina of אמונה, and אמונה is the essential foundation of prayer — the Torah states 'עִקַּר הַתְּפִלָּה הִיא עַל יְדֵי אֱמוּנָה'. The צדיקים = עפר bechina belongs here because in section ה, the עפר of prayer is defined as connecting with the souls of the righteous (שוכני עפר) — the tzaddikim ARE the dust that prayer incorporates. The אזוב = קטני קומה bechina belongs here because אזוב is one of the three ritual items of the פרה אדומה that the prayer must repair (the three voices/kolot), and the פרה = תפלה is this package's dominant bechina. All these nodes share the same essential nature: aspects of the structure, components, and conditions of holy prayer.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "פרה", "אמונה", "ישרת לב", "צדיק", "עפר", "אזוב", "קטני קומה"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "פרה": 1, "אמונה": 1, "ישרת לב": 1, "צדיק": 1, "עפר": 1, "אזוב": 1, "קטני קומה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אמונה", "ישרת לב"], "reason": "Direct bechina pair: 'יִשְׁרַת לֵב, שֶׁהִיא בְחִינַת אֱמוּנָה'. The Torah argues that straightened heart (ישרת לב) IS emunah, and emunah is the essential basis of prayer. These belong with the תפלה package because both describe the inner quality that enables prayer."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צדיק", "עפר"], "reason": "Section ה explicitly says: 'צַדִּיקִים, זֶהוּ בְחִינַת עָפָר' — the tzaddikim of the dead (שוכני עפר) are the עפר dimension of prayer. The entire section on עפר ואפר is about prayer's components. The צדיקים/עפר pair is an aspect of prayer: prayer incorporates the dust-souls of righteous predecessors. Belongs in the prayer package."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אזוב", "קטני קומה"], "reason": "Section ו-ז: אזוב is one of the three items of the פרה אדומה used in prayer-rectification; it represents the short-statured philosophers (קטני קומה) whose misaligned intellect the prayer of the tzaddik must repair. Since פרה = תפלה is already in this package, the components of the פרה ritual that bear explicit bechina language also belong here. The אזוב/קטני קומה pair is a bechina-defined sub-component of the פרה-as-prayer complex."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 55, "label": "רְשָׁעִים וְאֵפֶר", "label_en": "The Wicked and Ash", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This two-node package is the evil mirror of Package 2 (prayer). The Torah states explicitly: 'רְשָׁעִים, זֶהוּ בְחִינַת אֵפֶר' — the wicked are the ash-dimension of prayer. The 'tzibur' (ציבור) acronym — Tzaddikim, Beinonim, Resha'im — defines the three components of communal prayer. Package 2 contains the tzaddikim (= עפר/good). This package contains the resha'im (= אפר/evil). The ash (אפר) is produced by burning — it is the transformed state of evil once the prayer has subdued it: 'וַעֲסוֹתֶם רְשָׁעִים כִּי יִהְיוּ אֵפֶר תַּחַת כַּפּוֹת רַגְלֵיכֶם'. The wicked are not absent from prayer — the tzaddik who prays for the community must incorporate and subdue the prayers of the resha'im, converting their 'evil' into a 'throne for holiness'. This is the negative pole of the prayer structure. The רשעים ↔ אפר bechina stands as its own small but structurally essential package precisely because it is the evil counterpart to the good prayer domain of Package 2.", "dominant_node": "רשעים", "nodes": ["רשעים", "אפר"], "node_degrees": {"רשעים": 1, "אפר": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "דַּעַת וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "Knowledge and Moses", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #56 centers on דעת (knowledge/awareness) as the dominant concept with 4 direct bechina connections. This package encodes the teaching's central positive principle: that supernal daat, in its many forms, is the source of all blessing — it is the manna from heaven, Moses (whose name is an acronym of machloket Shamai Hillel = holy controversy = daat), the mikveh of the 50th gate, Shavuot, peace, and the concealment that can be transformed into Torah. All nodes share the essential nature of daat: they are different faces of the same divine knowing/consciousness. The holy controversy (מחלקת לשם שמים) is not opposed to daat but IS daat at its highest, because genuine dispute for heaven's sake flows from the deepest place of divine knowledge. Matzah = machloket = daat = man = the 50th gate of understanding = Shavuot. The transformation of hidden-within-hidden (אסתר = הסתרה שבתוך הסתרה) into Torah is the central act of daat described in sections ד-ה of the teaching. The number forty belongs here because the manna was eaten forty years and forty = binah vedaat.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["דעת", "מן", "משה", "דעת גדול", "מקוה של שער החמשים", "מקוה של שבועות", "שכל ודעת עליון", "מחלקת לשם שמים", "מחלוקת שמאי הלל", "שלום", "ארבעים", "בינה ודעת", "הסתרה שבתוך הסתרה", "אסתר", "שבועות", "שכל גדול וגבוה מאד", "מצה", "מחלקת"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 4, "מקוה של שער החמשים": 4, "מן": 3, "מחלקת לשם שמים": 2, "דעת גדול": 2, "מקוה של שבועות": 1, "שכל ודעת עליון": 1, "משה": 1, "מחלוקת שמאי הלל": 1, "שלום": 1, "ארבעים": 1, "בינה ודעת": 1, "הסתרה שבתוך הסתרה": 1, "אסתר": 1, "שבועות": 1, "שכל גדול וגבוה מאד": 1, "מצה": 1, "מחלקת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ארבעים", "בינה ודעת"], "reason": "Edge 2: ארבעים ↔ בינה ודעת. Source text line 59: 'כִּי בֶּן אַרְבָּעִים לַבִּינָה' — forty is the age of binah, and the Israelites ate manna forty years because manna = daat = binah vedaat. Forty IS binah vedaat; both belong in the daat cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הסתרה שבתוך הסתרה", "אסתר"], "reason": "Edge 406: הסתרה שבתוך הסתרה ↔ אסתר. Source text line 39: 'שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת אֶסְתֵּר, בְּחִינַת הַסְתֵּר אַסְתִּיר.' The entire teaching is about transforming the deepest concealment (Esther = הסתר אסתיר) into daat. The daat-process converts this concealment into Torah. These concepts belong in the daat package as the object/domain upon which daat operates."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שבועות", "שכל גדול וגבוה מאד"], "reason": "Edge 2894: שבועות ↔ שכל גדול וגבוה מאד. Source text line 52: 'שָׁבוּעוֹת הוּא בְּחִינַת שֵׂכֶל גָּדוֹל וְגָּבוֹהַּ מְאֹד.' Shavuot is the festival of supreme intellect. The מקוה של שבועות is already in the C1 component (connected to מקוה של שער החמשים), so שבועות as שכל גדול naturally belongs in the same daat cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מצה", "מחלקת"], "reason": "Edge 1764: מצה ↔ מחלקת. Source text line 64: 'מַצָּה הִיא בְּחִינַת מַחֲלֹקֶת.' Matzah = controversy, and controversy (when for heaven's sake) = daat. This pair's shared nature is daat-in-exile — the holy machloket that even in Egypt (when daat was in exile) retained the taste of manna/daat. Belongs in the daat package."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "מַלְכוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה", "label_en": "Holy Kingdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The second largest component in Torah #56 centers on מלכות (kingship/sovereignty) as the dominant concept with 4 direct bechina connections. This package represents holy malkhut — the dimension of righteous sovereignty that every person possesses according to their level. Mordecai is the archetypal expression of holy malkhut (explicitly stated: 'מָרְדְּכָי – זֶה בְּחִינַת הַמַּלְכוּת'). The Rebbe teaches that holy malkhut is defined by being מאסף לכל המחנות דקדשה — gathering all of holiness's camps, like the tribe of Dan who was the rear-guard. This is expressed as סוף דבר הכל נשמע = ירא אלקים = the fear of God that holy malkhut embodies. The 'מחנה דן' node (which is the rear-guard tribe = prototype of gathering malkhut) belongs here as a direct expression of the same principle. Note: מלכות also appears in Package 3 (evil dimension) — the concept can manifest in both holy and evil orientations, following the teaching's explicit good/evil mirror structure.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "סוף דבר", "מאסף לכל המחנות דקדשה", "מחנה דן", "ירא אלקים", "מרדכי"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 4, "סוף דבר": 2, "מאסף לכל המחנות דקדשה": 2, "מחנה דן": 1, "ירא אלקים": 1, "מרדכי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תאות עשירות"], "reason": "Edge 1834: תאות עשירות ↔ מלכות. Although this edge connects to the מלכות node, the source text (line 45) places תאות עשירות explicitly in the evil/sitra achra context: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת תַּאֲוַת עֲשִׁירוּת, שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת' — where the referent is the evil kingdom's gathering of wealth. Therefore תאות עשירות is REASSIGNED to Package 3 (evil malkhut), not Package 2. Per the framework, מלכות appears in both good and evil packages in this Torah, reflecting the teaching's core good/evil mirror structure."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "מַלְכוּת הָרְשָׁעָה", "label_en": "Wicked Kingdom", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the evil mirror of Package 2 (Holy Kingdom), explicitly established by the Rebbe in section ה: 'וּכְנֶגֶד מַלְכוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה יֵשׁ מַלְכוּת הָרְשָׁעָה' (line 44). The evil kingdom is represented by Haman-Amalek. Where holy malkhut gathers all the holy camps, evil malkhut gathers money/mammon (the sparks of holiness trapped in material wealth). Haman IS evil malkhut = מלכות דסטרא אחרא. תאות עשירות (the craving for wealth) is the essential nature of evil malkhut in this Torah — it is what evil malkhut does: collect holy sparks via material greed. The parallel structure: Package 2 (מרדכי = holy malkhut, gathers souls for God) vs. Package 3 (המן = evil malkhut, gathers wealth/sparks for the Other Side).", "dominant_node": "מלכות דסטרא אחרא", "nodes": ["מלכות דסטרא אחרא", "המן", "תאות עשירות"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות דסטרא אחרא": 1, "המן": 1, "תאות עשירות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["המן", "מלכות דסטרא אחרא"], "reason": "Edge 1866: המן ↔ מלכות דסטרא אחרא. Direct bechina pair from C11. The teaching explicitly identifies Haman with evil malkhut. Merged with the תאות עשירות node (reassigned from the C2 component) to form the complete evil malkhut package."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תאות עשירות"], "reason": "Reassigned from the מלכות component (where it was connected by edge 1834) to this evil package. Source text context (line 45): the desire for wealth is the bechina of the evil kingdom gathering holy sparks — it is the evil analogue of מאסף לכל המחנות דקדשה."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "רוּחַ הַדּוֹפֵק וְדַרְכֵי ה'", "label_en": "The Pulse and the Ways of God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package encompasses the domain of the beating pulse (רוח הדופק) and its spiritual correlates — the heavenly realm (שמים), thunder/divine mystery (רעמים), and the purified hands (ידים). The teaching in sections ט-י develops a detailed anatomy of how the breath of life (רוח הדופק) circulates through the body, especially concentrating in the hands, which are themselves an aspect of shamayim (heaven = fire + water = right and left hands). The thunder (רעמים) is the form in which divine speech reaches us from the heavens, and the divine ways of God (דרכי ה' = צדיק וטוב לו etc.) are the mysterious dimension of the same thunder that cannot be comprehended. All nodes share the same essential nature: aspects of the spiritual-physical medium through which divine speech and holy controversy (מחלקת לשם שמים) are received and transmitted. The four tzaddik/rasha pairs (merged from C7-C10) belong here because the text explicitly identifies them as 'בחינת דרכי ה', בחינת רעמים' (line 84-86) — they are not a separate concept but the specific content of the divine ways that cannot be grasped.", "dominant_node": "רעמים", "nodes": ["רעמים", "רוח הדופק", "שמים", "ידים", "רוח הקדש", "דרכי ה'", "צדיק ורע לו", "צדיק שאין הלכה כמותו", "צדיק וטוב לו", "צדיק שהלכה כמותו", "רשע ורע לו", "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שאין הלכה כמותו", "רשע וטוב לו", "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שהלכה כמותו"], "node_degrees": {"רעמים": 3, "שמים": 2, "רוח הדופק": 2, "רוח הקדש": 1, "דרכי ה'": 1, "ידים": 1, "צדיק ורע לו": 1, "צדיק שאין הלכה כמותו": 1, "צדיק וטוב לו": 1, "צדיק שהלכה כמותו": 1, "רשע ורע לו": 1, "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שאין הלכה כמותו": 1, "רשע וטוב לו": 1, "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שהלכה כמותו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["צדיק ורע לו", "צדיק שאין הלכה כמותו", "צדיק וטוב לו", "צדיק שהלכה כמותו", "רשע ורע לו", "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שאין הלכה כמותו", "רשע וטוב לו", "רשע שמקרב להצדיק שהלכה כמותו"], "reason": "These four 2-node pairs (C7-C10) are all explicitly identified in the source text (lines 84-86) as 'בְּחִינַת דַּרְכֵי ה', בְּחִינַת רְעָמִים' — the mysterious divine ways that cannot be grasped, expressed as the four paradoxes of tzaddik/rasha with good/evil outcomes. They share the SAME essential nature as the dominant concept רעמים: they ARE the specific content of the thunder/divine ways, not a separate concept. The text says: 'כִּי זֶה בְּחִינַת דַּרְכֵי ה' שֶׁאִי אֶפְשָׁר לְהַשִּׂיג, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת: צַדִּיק וְטוֹב לוֹ, צַדִּיק וְרַע לוֹ; רָשָׁע וְטוֹב לוֹ, רָשָׁע וְרַע לוֹ' — the 'היינו' (that is = they are the same thing as divine ways/thunder). These are not separate bechina concepts; they are the CONTENT of what the thunder-mystery IS."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּמִדּוֹת", "label_en": "Torah and Measures", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the cluster centered on Torah as God's name and the vessel of life-force. The teaching in sections ג-ד develops the idea that Torah = God's name (שמו של הקב\"ה) because both are the vessel (כלי) that bounds and measures the divine vitality. The life-force can only be received in bounded measures (מדות), and the days (ימים) are themselves measures. Torah, as God's name, is the vessel of measures and days — and by learning Torah one draws life-force into the days/measures. All four nodes share the same essential nature: they are aspects of the vessel-and-measure structure through which divine vitality flows into finite existence.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "שמו של הקדוש ברוך הוא", "מדות", "ימים"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "שמו של הקדוש ברוך הוא": 1, "מדות": 1, "ימים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מדות", "ימים"], "reason": "Edge 1358: מדות ↔ ימים. Source text line 15-16: 'כִּי הַתּוֹרָה הִיא בְּחִינַת מִדּוֹת... שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת יָמִים' — Torah = מדות = ימים is the fundamental equation of section ג. The days and the measures are both vessels for the life-force that Torah draws down. This pair belongs with Torah/שמו because Torah IS the vessel of measures/days — they are all aspects of the same divine naming/bounding structure."}]}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "פְּנִימִיּוּת וְחִיצוֹנִיּוּת", "label_en": "Inner and Outer", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package captures the spatial/dimensional framework of inner and outer that the teaching uses to describe how God is present even in distant places. The 'חצר בית' (courtyard-house) is glossed as 'חיצוניות ופנימיות, היינו מחשבות ודיבורים' — the outer domain (חצר/חיצוניות/דבור) and the inner domain (בית/פנימיות/מחשבה). These are structural concepts — neutral vessels that can contain either holiness or the absence of holiness. The teaching stresses that God is hidden even within these outermost/innermost domains of even the most distant. Polarity is neutral because these are architectural/structural concepts (like body parts or letters) that serve as containers: the outer court can be a place of holiness or distance.", "dominant_node": "פנימיות", "nodes": ["פנימיות", "מחשבה", "בית", "חיצוניות", "דבור", "חצר"], "node_degrees": {"פנימיות": 2, "חיצוניות": 2, "מחשבה": 1, "בית": 1, "דבור": 1, "חצר": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חיצוניות", "דבור", "חצר", "פנימיות", "מחשבה", "בית"], "reason": "C5 (חיצוניות/דבור/חצר) and C6 (פנימיות/מחשבה/בית) are separate connected components in the graph (no direct bechina edge between them) but are thematically inseparable: the source text (line 35) explicitly presents them as a paired unit — 'חֲצַר בֵּית – זֶה בְּחִינַת חִיצוֹנִיּוּת וּפְנִימִיּוּת, הַיְנוּ מַחֲשָׁבוֹת וְדִבּוּרִים.' The outer (חצר=חיצוניות=דיבור) and inner (בית=פנימיות=מחשבה) are defined together as a single bi-polar domain. Their shared nature is the inner-outer spatial structure of divine concealment. Merging the two 3-node clusters into one 6-node package is correct because the framework states packages are thematic clusters sharing a common denominator — and these two clusters share the common denominator of 'the spatial/dimensional structure of inner/outer through which God is hidden.'"}]}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 56, "label": "זֻהֲמַת הַנָּחָשׁ", "label_en": "Pollution of the Serpent", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil counterpart to Package 4 (the holy ruach/pulse domain). The pollution of the serpent (זהמת הנחש) produces the 39 labors (ל'ט מלאכות = the disordered/corrupted creative work) which generates sadness/depression of spirit (עצבות). The teaching (lines 74-75) explains that there is a serpent whose influence causes עצבות רוח, and this עצבות is identified as 'בחינת ל'ט מלאכות שהוא זהמת הנחש' — the 39 forbidden Shabbat labors are the spiritual form of the serpent's pollution. When the ruach hadofek (the beating pulse of Package 4) is blocked by this sadness, the limbs become heavy. The evil mirror of the purified hands/pulse (Package 4) is the snake-pollution/depression/39-labors complex. The sighing (אנחה) that heals the pulse (an eitza in Package 4) is the corrective against this evil package — pointing to the correct eitza flow from Package 7 (evil) through Package 4 (good) to Package 1 (daat/machloket lishem shamayim).", "dominant_node": "ל\"ט מלאכות", "nodes": ["ל\"ט מלאכות", "זהמת הנחש", "עצבות"], "node_degrees": {"ל\"ט מלאכות": 2, "זהמת הנחש": 1, "עצבות": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 57, "label": "יַעֲקֹב הַנֶּדֶר וְהָאֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Jacob, the Vow, and Faith", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster unites the positive axis of Torah #57: Yaakov, whose essential nature IS the vow (neder), accesses the hidden root of the Sages (pele = wonder) through that vow. The lack of faith in the Sages IS the concealment of the Sages (hisatlkut ha-chakhamim), which IS pele — the wonder-state where wisdom is hidden. Thus the chain is: neder = Yaakov; neder = pele; pele = hisatlkut ha-chakhamim; hisatlkut = loss of emunah. The letter shin (three-pronged, representing the three Patriarchs) and bat (= emunah, as in 'a daughter was born to Avraham and her name was Bakol') are the cosmological encoding of this same domain: the Patriarchs are the three-pronged shin, and the bat (daughter/emunah) is what unites with them to produce 'shin-bat' = Shabbat. All nine nodes share the common denominator of the positive constructive domain: faith in the Sages, the vow that restores it, and the Patriarchal light that the vow draws down.", "dominant_node": "נדר", "nodes": ["יעקב", "נדר", "פלא", "הסתלקות החכמים", "אין לו אמונת חכמים", "אות שין", "אבות", "בת", "אמונה"], "node_degrees": {"נדר": 2, "פלא": 2, "הסתלקות החכמים": 2, "יעקב": 1, "אין לו אמונת חכמים": 1, "אות שין": 1, "אבות": 1, "בת": 1, "אמונה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אות שין", "אבות"], "from_component": "G", "reason": "The letter shin IS the Patriarchs ('שִׁין זֶה בְּחִינַת אָבוֹת'). The Torah explicitly states that the vow (neder = Yaakov) draws down the light of the Patriarchs (orot ha-avot) — the same Patriarchs that the shin encodes. Shared nature: both express the dimension of Patriarchal light and the role of Yaakov within the trio. Component G belongs with Component A because the Patriarchs/shin/Yaakov constellation is the same conceptual family."}, {"merged_nodes": ["בת", "אמונה"], "from_component": "H", "reason": "Bat = emunah ('בַּת זֶה בְּחִינוֹת אֱמוּנָה'). The Torah says: 'שִׁין בַּת' = Shabbat. Shin = the Patriarchs (Package 1); Bat = emunah (Package 1 — the same faith-in-Sages that the vow restores). Emunah is the daughter of the Patriarchs, just as the vow is the mode of Yaakov. Bat and emunah share the same essential nature as the faith dimension that the Yaakov/neder axis is designed to repair. They belong with the neder/pele/Patriarchs cluster."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 57, "label": "מַצָּה וְכֹחַ הַדִּבּוּר", "label_en": "Matzah and the Power of Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster encodes the highest level of peace — Shabbat-peace with speech (shalom she-yesh lo peh). Matzah is written with the letter heh (ה), which represents the five articulatory positions of the mouth (chamesh motza'ot ha-peh), which is the power of speech (koach ha-diburi). Matzah also IS the eating of Shabbat delight (akilat oneg Shabbat). Thus: matzah = Shabbat eating = letter heh = five mouth-positions = power of speech. All four nodes share the same essential nature: the domain of holy speech, the mouth's articulated capacity, which is the defining advantage of human over animal ('mevaker al chai'). This package represents the completion of the Shabbat tikkun at the level of human speech — the fullest expression of holiness.", "dominant_node": "מצה", "nodes": ["מצה", "אכילת ענג שבת", "חמשת מוצאות הפה", "כח הדבור"], "node_degrees": {"מצה": 2, "חמשת מוצאות הפה": 2, "אכילת ענג שבת": 1, "כח הדבור": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 57, "label": "עֹנֶג שַׁבָּת וְשָׁלוֹם", "label_en": "Shabbat Delight and Peace", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster centers on oneg Shabbat (Shabbat pleasure) as holy eating — eating from which the sitra achra receives no portion — and its production of shalom (peace). The gorel (lot) IS oneg Shabbat, because Daniel says 'you shall rest and stand for your lot at the end of days' (ve-tanuach ve-ta'amod le-goralekha le-ketz ha-yamin) — when all will be Shabbat. The sun (shemesh) IS peace, as the Sages state: 'just as the sun shines peace for all who enter the world.' Shemesh = shalom is merged into this package because the text explicitly connects the Shabbat-sun-peace triad: 'Shemesh be-Shabbat tzedaka la-aniyim' — the Shabbat sun is peace through charity. All five nodes share the essential nature of the Shabbat domain: the sacred, complete, sitra-achra-free dimension of holy pleasure, rest, and peace.", "dominant_node": "ענג שבת", "nodes": ["ענג שבת", "אכילה", "גורל", "שמש", "שלום"], "node_degrees": {"ענג שבת": 2, "אכילה": 1, "גורל": 1, "שמש": 1, "שלום": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שמש", "שלום"], "from_component": "I", "reason": "Shemesh = shalom is explicitly stated as a bechina ('שֶׁמֶשׁ זֶה בְּחִינַת שָׁלוֹם'). The context is the Shabbat teaching: 'Shemesh be-Shabbat tzedaka la-aniyim' — the sun on Shabbat IS peace given to the poor through charity. The sun-as-peace is the Shabbat dimension of peace (the full speech-peace), not the fasting-peace (which is animal-level/without speech, in Package 4). Shemesh/shalom share the essential nature of the Shabbat-complete-peace domain and belong with the oneg Shabbat package."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 57, "label": "חָמֵץ וְהַתַּעֲנִית", "label_en": "Chametz and the Fast", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This cluster represents the partial or lower-level peace achieved through fasting rather than Shabbat eating. Chametz IS fasting ('חָמֵץ זֶה בְּחִינַת הַכְנָעָתוֹ, בְּחִינַת תַּעֲנִית, בְּחִינַת הֶעְדֵּר אֲכִילָה'), which IS the tikkun of anger via fasting ('חָמֵץ זֶהוּ בְּחִינַת תִּקּוּן הַכַּעַס עַל יְדֵי הַתַּעֲנִית'). The peace produced by fasting IS 'shalom without speech' = chai (animal-level existence: 'בְּחִינַת חַי, בְּחִינַת וְחַיַּת הַשָּׂדֶה הָשְׁלְמָה לָּךְ — peace of the field animals, who have life but not speech'). The polarity is neutral because: (1) chametz as a concept is associated with the Edom/kaved domain (evil) in this Torah, yet (2) the tikkun of fasting is a positive corrective act, and (3) the peace achieved is real but partial — it subdues enemies but lacks the full power of speech. Chametz/fasting is neither the pure holy path (Shabbat/matzah) nor pure evil — it is the liminal, partial tikkun that reduces evil without achieving complete holiness.", "dominant_node": "חמץ", "nodes": ["חמץ", "שלום שאין לו פה", "חי", "תענית", "תקון הכעס על ידי התענית"], "node_degrees": {"חמץ": 3, "שלום שאין לו פה": 2, "חי": 1, "תענית": 1, "תקון הכעס על ידי התענית": 1}}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 57, "label": "עֵשָׂו הַכָּבֵד וְשַׁעַר אֲרָם", "label_en": "Esav, the Liver, and the Gate of Aram", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster unites all evil/opposing-force nodes in Torah #57. The liver (kaved) IS anger ('כָּבֵד כּוֹעֵס'), IS Edom/Esav ('אֱדוֹם זֶה בְּחִינַת כָּבֵד הַנַּ\"ל — Esav was called Admoni, red/full-of-blood like the liver'). The 'rulers of anger' (shultanei rugzin) ARE the enraged liver ('שׁוּלְטָנֵי רֻגְזִין, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת כָּבֵד כּוֹעֵס'). The gate of Aram IS the gate of the sitra achra ('שַׁעַר אֲרָם זֶה בְּחִינַת שַׁעַר דְּסִטְרָא-אָחֳרָא'). All six nodes share the same evil nature: the domain of the prosecuting force (Esav/Edom), whose grip is on the liver, who stirs up accusers from above and below, and whose gate must fall three times before Mashiach comes. The kaved is dominant because it is the physiological and cosmological root concept from which all the others branch: Esav = liver; anger = liver; rulers of anger = liver; Aram-gate = sitra achra gate that is nourished by the liver-anger complex.", "dominant_node": "כבד", "nodes": ["כבד", "אדום", "כבד כועס", "שולטני רגזין", "שער ארם", "שער דסטרא אחרא"], "node_degrees": {"כבד": 1, "אדום": 1, "כבד כועס": 1, "שולטני רגזין": 1, "שער ארם": 1, "שער דסטרא אחרא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אדום", "כבד"], "from_component": "E", "reason": "Edom = liver is an explicit bechina ('אֱדוֹם זֶה בְּחִינַת כָּבֵד'). Esav/Edom is the great accuser whose grip is on the liver. This pair is the core identification of the evil oppressor with the physiological seat of anger. It belongs in the evil package together with the other liver-anger nodes."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שולטני רגזין", "כבד כועס"], "from_component": "F", "reason": "The rulers-of-anger ARE the enraged liver ('שׁוּלְטָנֵי רֻגְזִין, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת כָּבֵד כּוֹעֵס'). They are the heavenly/cosmic forces empowered by human anger. Same evil domain as Component E — both describe the liver-anger-Esav nexus. Merged into the evil package as natural companions to אדום/כבד."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שער ארם", "שער דסטרא אחרא"], "from_component": "J", "reason": "The gate of Aram IS the gate of the Other Side — the evil structure that falls three times and enables Mashiach to come. The Aram-gate/sitra-achra-gate pair is the macro-cosmic expression of the same evil domain: the Esav/liver/anger power has its cosmic gate. When that gate falls (through Shabbat eating, vow, and peace-producing practices), the gate of holiness rises in its place. Shared nature with E+F: all are manifestations of the same evil force at different levels (physiological = liver, cosmological = sitra achra gate). Merged into the evil package."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 58, "label": "אָבוֹת וּמַתָּנוֹת הַמִּדְבָּר", "label_en": "The Patriarchs and the Wilderness Gifts", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's opening movement (sections א–ה) identifies the three wilderness gifts — Beer (water), Cloud, and Manna — as bechina-expressions of the three Patriarchs: Abraham/Chesed/water, Isaac/cloud/concealment, Jacob/manna/sustenance. Moses drew all three because his soul was comprised of all three Patriarchs. This cluster groups every node that shares the essential nature of patriarchal gift-giving: the water of Chesed (Abraham = 'מימי החסד'), the cloud as garment (Isaac = concealment/cloud/clothing), and the manna as Jacob's shepherding provision. חֶסֶד and מֵימֵי הַחֶסֶד are two explicit bechinas of אברהם, making Abraham the dominant hub. The small components {מן, יעקב} and {ענן, יצחק, לבוש} are merged here because they participate in precisely the same bechina domain: each gift is an aspect of one Patriarch's essential quality, and לבוש (garment) is a bechina of עָנָן, making it part of the Isaac/concealment cluster within this package.", "dominant_node": "אברהם", "nodes": ["\"באר מים חיים\"", "אברהם", "חסד", "מימי החסד", "מן", "יעקב", "ענן", "יצחק", "לבוש"], "node_degrees": {"אברהם": 3, "ענן": 2, "\"באר מים חיים\"": 1, "חסד": 1, "מימי החסד": 1, "מן": 1, "יעקב": 1, "יצחק": 1, "לבוש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Comp C", "merged_nodes": ["מן", "יעקב"], "reason": "מן and יעקב form a bechina-pair (edge 1376) stating that the Manna is the bechina of Jacob. This is the third of the three wilderness gifts paralleling the three Patriarchs. The shared nature is identical to the dominant cluster's theme: a wilderness gift expressing a Patriarch's essential quality. It is not cause-and-effect; both node pairs (מן↔יעקב and 'באר מים חיים'↔אברהם) state the same kind of thing from the same structural domain."}, {"merged_component": "Comp D", "merged_nodes": ["ענן", "יצחק", "לבוש"], "reason": "ענן is a bechina of יצחק (edge 1387) — the Cloud is the gift of concealment associated with Isaac's quality of darkness/cloud/blindness. ענן is also a bechina of לבוש (edge 1557) — the cloud is a garment, clothing the people. This triplet belongs in the patriarchal-gifts cluster: the Cloud as Isaac's domain, and Garment as the functional aspect of the Cloud. The shared nature across all three merged components is: 'one of the three wilderness gifts as an expression of a specific Patriarch's essential quality.'"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 58, "label": "גְּדֻלָּה וּמַלְכוּת וּשְׁכִינָה", "label_en": "Greatness, Kingship, and the Divine Presence", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's middle movement (sections ט–י) centers on the phenomenon of beauty, greatness, and honor that comes to the 'proper ones of the generation' as a sign (ציון) of the hidden Torah received through Shabbat. הַר is a bechina of גְּדֻלָּה (the mountain = greatness, per Sanhedrin 107); גְּדֻלָּה is a bechina of מַלְכוּת (kingship is greatness); מַלְכוּת is a bechina of שְׁכִינָה (kingship = the Divine Presence). All four nodes orbit the same essential domain: the outward mark of holy kingship that signals alignment with the concealed Torah. This is the positive form of malkhut — when the Shabbat light flows through properly, greatness becomes a vessel for Torah renewal. The polarity is 'good' because the dominant axis of these bechinas describes the proper function of malkhut as the outward sign of the double Torah portion; the 'exile of the Shechina' (when malkhut is damaged) is described in the text as a damage to this same package, not a separate evil cluster (no evil-domain bechina nodes appear for Torah #58 in the dataset).", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["הר", "גדלה", "מלכות", "שכינה"], "node_degrees": {"גדלה": 2, "מלכות": 2, "הר": 1, "שכינה": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 58, "label": "שַׁבָּת וּתְשׁוּבָה וְלֶחֶם מִשְׁנֶה", "label_en": "Shabbat, Repentance, and the Double Portion of Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's central positive outcome (sections ד–ז) is that one who guards the covenant and subdues evil merits Shabbat, which is a bechina of תְּשׁוּבָה (repentance — 'וְשַׁבְתָּ עַד ה''). Shabbat-repentance comes from love (תְּשׁוּבָה מֵאַהֲבָה), and this repentance-from-love is itself a bechina of לֶחֶם מִשְׁנֶה (the double portion of Shabbat bread). לֶחֶם מִשְׁנֶה is a bechina of מִשְׁנֵה תּוֹרָה (the second/double Torah — novel Torah insights renewed on Shabbat). These five nodes share a single essential nature: they are all expressions of the doubled, holy receiving that flows on Shabbat to one who is aligned with the covenant. The Zoharic formula 'תרין ינקין לחד' (two draw from one) describes this entire cluster. The small isolated pair {שבת, תשובה} (Comp F, edges 3245–3246, two parallel edges) is merged here because its essential nature — Shabbat as the bechina of repentance — is the same as the dominant cluster's theme, and תְּשׁוּבָה מֵאַהֲבָה is already in Comp E connecting directly to this pair's conceptual domain.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "תשובה", "תשובה מאהבה", "לחם משנה", "משנה תורה"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 2, "תשובה": 2, "לחם משנה": 2, "תשובה מאהבה": 1, "משנה תורה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Comp F", "merged_nodes": ["שבת", "תשובה"], "reason": "שבת and תשובה are linked by two parallel bechina edges (3245 and 3246), both quoting 'וְשַׁבְתָּ עַד ה' אֱלֹקֶיךָ'. Their shared nature — Shabbat as repentance — is identical in domain to Component E's dominant theme: תְּשׁוּבָה מֵאַהֲבָה (a specific form of תְּשׁוּבָה) is a bechina of לֶחֶם מִשְׁנֶה, the specific gift of שַׁבָּת. Since שבת and תשובה are each already members of Comp E's conceptual family by direct textual reference (the text explicitly equates all five nodes under the Zoharic phrase), merging them here is consistent with shared bechina nature, not a cause-effect merge."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 59, "label": "עִגּוּלָא וְרִבּוּעָא וְכִסֵּא הַכָּבוֹד", "label_en": "The Circle, the Square, and the Throne of Glory", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant node is עגול (degree 3), connecting to אות סמ\"ך, שם, and ששים. The teaching in sections ג–ד defines שַׁבָּת as a bechina of עיגולא ורבועא (Zohar on Vayakhel), and in sections ג–ד the Rebbe unpacks each pole: עִגּוּלָא = אות סמ\"ך (the round letter) = כסא כבוד (Throne of Glory) = שישים מלכות (sixty queens around the throne) = שם (God's Name, 'יַנְחֵנִי בְמַעְגְּלֵי צֶדֶק לְמַעַן שְׁמוֹ'). The square pole: רִבּוּעָא = מִשְׁפָּט = אות מם (the square letter) = יָד ('וְתֹאחֵז בְּמִשְׁפָּט יָדִי'). And בְּנֵס (the miracle of the two letters standing in the Tablets) = שַׁבָּת ('אוֹת הִיא לְעוֹלָם'). All ten nodes share a single essential bechina nature: they are all expressions of the cosmic circle-and-square duality that constitutes Shabbat, the Throne, and the divine Name. The polarity is good — this entire cluster describes the structure of divine honor, holy kingship, and the sacred sign of the covenant. The מם and סמ\"ך standing miraculously upright in the Tablets (section ד) IS the miraculous union of the square and the circle, which IS Shabbat, which IS the Throne of Glory.", "dominant_node": "עגול", "nodes": ["עגול", "אות סמ\"ך", "כסא כבוד", "שם", "ששים", "רבוע", "משפט", "יד", "בנס", "שבת"], "node_degrees": {"עגול": 3, "אות סמ\"ך": 2, "רבוע": 2, "כסא כבוד": 1, "שם": 1, "ששים": 1, "משפט": 1, "יד": 1, "בנס": 1, "שבת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component C", "merged_nodes": ["רבוע", "משפט", "יד"], "reason": "Component C (3 nodes) is not 'small' by strict count but shares the identical bechina nature as Component A. Section ג explicitly names both poles as a unit: 'שַׁבְּתוֹתַי דָּא עִגּוּלָא וְרִבּוּעַ דִּלְגוֹ', and section ד-ה treats the two letters מם (= ריבועא = משפט) and סמ\"ך (= עיגולא = כסא כבוד) as a single inseparable pair: 'מֵם וְסָמֶךְ שֶׁבַּלּוּחוֹת בְּנֵס הָיוּ עוֹמְדִים'. The ריבועא-משפט domain does not exist independently in this Torah — it is always the counterpart of עיגולא-כסא, forming one unit. יד = ריבועא = משפט completes the square pole. To separate them into two packages would fragment a structure the text treats as one."}, {"merged_component": "Component E", "merged_nodes": ["בנס", "שבת"], "reason": "Section ד-ה: 'מֵם וְסָמֶךְ שֶׁבַּלּוּחוֹת בְּנֵס הָיוּ עוֹמְדִים. מֵם זֶה רִבּוּעָא, וְסָמֶךְ זֶה עִגּוּלָא הַנַּ\"ל'. The miracle (בְּנֵס) of the two letters standing upright is explicitly the miracle of ריבועא and עיגולא standing together — which the Rebbe defines as שַׁבָּת ('אוֹת הִיא לְעוֹלָם'). The shared bechina nature is: בְּנֵס = the miraculous sign of the circle-and-square unity = שַׁבָּת. This is the same domain as the dominant package. The 2-node pair belongs here."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 59, "label": "הִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב וּמַלְאֲכֵי אֵשׁ", "label_en": "Heart-Fervor and Angels of Fire", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's central practical teaching (sections א–ב) describes the defense mechanism against the klipot that cling to one who draws people close to God: the solution (eitza) is to summon 'מַלְאֲכֵי לִבּוֹ' (the angels of his heart), which the text defines in a single sentence as two linked bechinot: (1) 'מַלְאֲכֵי לִבּוֹ, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת הִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב' — the heart-angels ARE the fervor of the heart; (2) 'שֶׁהַהִתְלַהֲבוּת הוּא בְחִינַת מַלְאָכִים, בִּבְחִינַת וַיֵּרָא אֵלָיו מַלְאַךְ ה' בְּלַבַּת אֵשׁ' — fervor IS angels, as seen from Moses at the burning bush. All four nodes — מלאכי לבו, התלהבות הלב, התלהבות, מלאכים — share one essential bechina nature: they are four names for the same spiritual phenomenon, the fiery ardor of the heart that manifests as angelic fire and burns away the klipot. The two dataset entries 'התלהבות הלב' and 'התלהבות' are aliases — the dataset captured one concept under two slightly different labels. Polarity is good: this fire is holy fire that protects the tzaddik and burns the negative forces.", "dominant_node": "התלהבות", "nodes": ["מלאכי לבו", "התלהבות הלב", "התלהבות", "מלאכים"], "node_degrees": {"התלהבות הלב": 1, "מלאכי לבו": 1, "התלהבות": 1, "מלאכים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "D1 + D2", "merged_nodes": ["מלאכי לבו", "התלהבות הלב", "התלהבות", "מלאכים"], "reason": "Component D1 (מלאכי לבו ↔ התלהבות הלב) and Component D2 (התלהבות ↔ מלאכים) are disconnected in the graph because 'התלהבות הלב' and 'התלהבות' are two node IDs for the same concept. The source text (section ב) presents a single bechina chain: 'מַלְאֲכֵי לִבּוֹ, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת הִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב, שֶׁהַהִתְלַהֲבוּת הוּא בְחִינַת מַלְאָכִים' — a continuous sentence that equates all four terms. The two components share the same essential nature (fiery fervor = angels) and represent a single conceptual unit artificially split by the dataset's node-naming. Merging is mandated by both the source text and bechina-identity."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 59, "label": "חוֹמָה וּכְבִישַׁת כַּעַס וַעֲשִׁירוּת", "label_en": "The Wall, Anger-Suppression, and Wealth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Section ה of the Torah contains one of the most explicit multi-way bechina statements in the teaching: 'עֲשִׁירוּת וּכְבִישַׁת כַּעַס – בְּחִינָה אַחַת, וְנִקְרָאִים חוֹמָה' — wealth and suppressing anger ARE a single bechina and are called 'wall.' The Rebbe then adds that the חוֹמָה and בַּיִת are a bechina of עֵינַיִם (eyes), completing the cluster. The essential shared nature of all four nodes: they are all expressions of the spiritual wall that protects the person and his soul (נפש = שם = the Name that grows in this Torah). Wealth is a wall (Prov 18:11: 'הוֹן עָשִׁיר קִרְיַת עֻזּוֹ וּכְחוֹמָה נִשְׂגָּבָה'); suppressing anger is a wall ('עִיר פְּרוּצָה אֵין חוֹמָה אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר אֵין מַעְצוֹר לְרוּחוֹ', Prov 25:28); eyes are the wall and house ('הַחוֹמָה וּבַיִת הַנַּ\"ל, שֶׁהֵם בְחִינַת עֵינַיִם'). The polarity is good: this entire cluster describes the positive spiritual structure that protects the Name/soul of the tzaddik. Wealth is not evil here — it is the right-side channel of divine influx that constitutes the wall of the soul.", "dominant_node": "חומה", "nodes": ["חומה", "ממון", "כבישת כעס", "עינים"], "node_degrees": {"חומה": 3, "ממון": 2, "כבישת כעס": 2, "עינים": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 60, "label": "יִרְאָה, זָקֵן וְעַתִּיק", "label_en": "Fear of God, the Elder, and Atik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant connected component of Torah #60 (13 nodes) is built around יראה, which connects to אריכות ימים, דמים וגבורות, and זקן. זקן forms the hub connecting to הדרת פנים, אבוה דאבא, חרוב, עתיק, and אריכות ימים. עתיק connects to ספורי מעשיות של שנים קדמוניות and רב חסד, and through רב חסד to מרדכי and ברושים. תקוני עתיק connects to אריכות ימים. The Rebbe's central teaching is: יראה (fear of God) IS the extension/length of days (אריכות ימים), which IS the quality of the Elder (זקן), which IS the Atik (עתיק — the Ancient of Days, the highest crown). These are all expressions of the same essential reality: sanctified time that expands through each act of holiness, the archetype of the aged sage whose every day broadens out with additional holiness. The blood-forces (דמים וגבורות) are the physical manifestation of this fear-energy released through birth. The ancient stories (ספורי מעשיות של שנים קדמוניות) ARE the Atik's face — they carry the vitality of all 70 faces of Torah. Mordechai and the carob-tree (חרוב) are both expressions of the Atik dimension: חרוב = ancient/long-standing tree = זקן; מרדכי = ברושים = the aromatic cypress that IS Mordechai (gematria = רב חסד). The entire cluster is positive/holy: it describes the domain of holiness, expanded time, and divine fear that enables the contemplation of Torah (hitbonenut). The shofar cluster is merged here: שופר is the bechina of both התגלות היראה and התעוררות השנה, and through דבור (speech of awakening) and תרועה (the speech-lips of the tzaddik) it is structurally part of the yirah-revelation domain. The small pairs are also merged: קצר מלמעלה ורחב מלמטה / יראת ה' היא אוצרו / עשאה כאוצר describe the formal structure of יראה (narrow at top, wide at bottom = the storehouse shape). שלש קוין / ישלש אדם מעותיו describe the three-fold structure of יראה corresponding to three-fold wealth. שבעים פנים / שבעים שנים are the measure of the sleep = the domain of contemplation the Atik oversees. ישראל המתעוררים מלמטה / אשריך ישראל and אתערותא דלעלא / מי כמוכה באלים are the two poles of the awakening structure — from-below and from-above — both expressions of yirah realized. חמרה / עשירות = Yissachar's donkey = the wealth that flows from Atik/contemplation. משא ומתן / רגל = commerce/the foot = one of the three thirds of wealth within the yirah-wealth domain. אות וא\"ו / הלוחות = the letter vav IS the tablets (both 6 handbreadths in length and breadth) = the instrument through which Moshe became wealthy = deep in the yirah-hitbonenut domain.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "אריכות ימים", "דמים וגבורות", "זקן", "הדרת פנים", "אבוה דאבא", "חרוב", "עתיק", "ספורי מעשיות של שנים קדמוניות", "רב חסד", "מרדכי", "ברושים", "תקוני עתיק", "שופר", "התגלות היראה", "התעוררות השנה", "דבור", "תרועה", "קצר מלמעלה ורחב מלמטה", "\"יראת ה' היא אוצרו\"", "\"עשאה כאוצר\"", "שלש קוין", "\"לעולם ישלש אדם מעותיו, שליש בפרקמטיא, ושליש בקרקע, ושליש בידו\"", "שבעים פנים לתורה", "שבעים שנים", "ישראל המתעוררים מלמטה", "\"אשריך ישראל מי כמוך\"", "אתערותא דלעלא", "\"מי כמוכה באלים ה'\"", "חמרה", "עשירות", "משא ומתן", "רגל", "אות וא\"ו", "הלוחות"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 3, "זקן": 5, "עתיק": 3, "אריכות ימים": 3, "רב חסד": 2, "מרדכי": 2, "שופר": 2, "התעוררות השנה": 2, "\"יראת ה' היא אוצרו\"": 2, "דמים וגבורות": 1, "הדרת פנים": 1, "אבוה דאבא": 1, "חרוב": 1, "ספורי מעשיות של שנים קדמוניות": 1, "ברושים": 1, "תקוני עתיק": 1, "התגלות היראה": 1, "דבור": 2, "תרועה": 1, "קצר מלמעלה ורחב מלמטה": 1, "\"עשאה כאוצר\"": 1, "שלש קוין": 1, "\"לעולם ישלש אדם מעותיו, שליש בפרקמטיא, ושליש בקרקע, ושליש בידו\"": 1, "שבעים פנים לתורה": 1, "שבעים שנים": 1, "ישראל המתעוררים מלמטה": 1, "\"אשריך ישראל מי כמוך\"": 1, "אתערותא דלעלא": 1, "\"מי כמוכה באלים ה'\"": 1, "חמרה": 1, "עשירות": 1, "משא ומתן": 1, "רגל": 1, "אות וא\"ו": 1, "הלוחות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שופר", "התגלות היראה", "התעוררות השנה", "דבור", "תרועה"], "reason": "The shofar cluster (Component A) forms a 5-node chain: שופר ↔ התגלות היראה AND שופר ↔ התעוררות השנה ↔ דבור ↔ תרועה. Its essential nature is 'the revelation and instrument of yirah' — the shofar IS the revelation of fear (התגלות היראה), and its sound IS the awakening from sleep that leads to speech (דבור = תרועה). The Rebbe explicitly states (line 72-78) that the shofar brings about the revelation of yirah and through that the extension of days (= the entire Pkg 1 domain). Shared nature with Pkg 1: all are expressions of yirah being revealed and operative in the world."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קצר מלמעלה ורחב מלמטה", "\"יראת ה' היא אוצרו\"", "\"עשאה כאוצר\""], "reason": "This 3-node chain describes the structural/formal property of yirah: the fear of God IS the storehouse (אוצרו) which IS the vessel that is narrow at top and wide at bottom (עשאה כאוצר). All three are different names for the same formal quality of yirah — its shape/structure. Belongs in Pkg 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שלש קוין", "\"לעולם ישלש אדם מעותיו...\""], "reason": "The three lines of yirah (שלש קוין) — fear of Heaven, of one's teacher, of one's parents — are a bechina of the three-fold division of wealth. This pair is at the heart of the yirah structure (section ד of the Torah). Its shared nature is 'the triadic structure of yirah expressed in wealth.' Belongs in Pkg 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שבעים פנים לתורה", "שבעים שנים"], "reason": "The 70 faces of Torah ARE 70 years — the measure of the sleep from which people must be awakened. This equivalence (line 46) is foundational to understanding what it means to sleep from all faces. The domain is: the full scope of Torah-faces/years that the Atik encompasses. Shared nature: the structure of Torah-time that the Atik (= Pkg 1) sustains. Belongs in Pkg 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ישראל המתעוררים מלמטה", "\"אשריך ישראל מי כמוך\"", "אתערותא דלעלא", "\"מי כמוכה באלים ה'\""], "reason": "These two pairs represent the two poles of the awakening structure (lines 100-102): Israel awakening from below (= its own yirah, self-arousal) and the divine awakening from above (= the transcendent completion). The Rebbe explicitly links these to the zikhronot/shofarot of Rosh Hashana (= yirah domain). Shared nature: expressions of yirah from below and above. Belong in Pkg 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חמרה", "עשירות"], "reason": "Choni's donkey giving birth to many foals = wealth (Yissachar's donkey = rich in possessions). The story's concluding image is the materialization of wealth through the chain: awakening → speech-liberation → pekidut akarot → yirah → length of days → wealth drawn into length of days. Wealth as the fruit of the Atik-yirah-zaken domain. Belongs in Pkg 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["משא ומתן", "רגל"], "reason": "Commerce (משא ומתן) IS the foot (רגל), which corresponds to the 'third invested in business' — one of the three divisions of wealth (שלש קוין) already in Pkg 1. Since שלש קוין is in Pkg 1, its component parts (the specific thirds) also belong there. Shared nature: one dimension of the three-fold structure of wealth within yirah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אות וא\"ו", "הלוחות"], "reason": "The letter vav IS the tablets (both 6 by 6 handbreadths) — the instrument through which Moshe became wealthy (the chips of the tablets = the pesoles = the rounds of contemplation = wealth). The vav/tablets are the structural expression of how Torah-contemplation produces wealth in the Atik domain. Shared nature with Pkg 1: the material/formal expression of the contemplation-wealth connection."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 60, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַרְקוּלִיס וְחִלּוּף הַכָּבוֹד", "label_en": "The Idol of Desecration and Inversion of Honor", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These four nodes are all expressions of the same essential reality: the desecration and inversion of Torah honor. שמרקולי\"ס is the idolatrous stone-heap whose very name encodes the concept; מ\"ר קלו\"ס (= mar kilus) is the etymology — 'bitter shame,' i.e., the inversion of honor; חלוף הכבוד is the explicit concept name — 'substitution of honor'; כסיל and תלמיד שאינו הגון are the same type of person — the fool/unworthy recipient who embodies this inversion of Torah's honor when Torah is taught to him. The Rebbe explicitly identifies כסיל as the תלמיד שאינו הגון via the verse 'נוֹתֵן לִכְסִיל כָּבוֹד,' and identifies שמרקולי\"ס as מ\"ר קלו\"ס as חלוף הכבוד. All five nodes share the same nature: they are forms and expressions of the anti-honor, the force that inverts and desecrates the holiness of Torah.", "dominant_node": "מ\"ר קלו\"ס", "nodes": ["שמרקולי\"ס", "מ\"ר קלו\"ס", "חלוף הכבוד", "כסיל", "תלמיד שאינו הגון"], "node_degrees": {"שמרקולי\"ס": 1, "מ\"ר קלו\"ס": 2, "חלוף הכבוד": 1, "כסיל": 1, "תלמיד שאינו הגון": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כסיל", "תלמיד שאינו הגון"], "reason": "This 2-node component is explicitly merged into Package 1 because the כסיל IS the תלמיד שאינו הגון — the Rebbe equates them via 'נוֹתֵן לִכְסִיל כָּבוֹד = לְתַלְמִיד שֶׁאֵינוֹ הָגוּן.' More critically, the כסיל as a type belongs to the same essential domain as שמרקולי\"ס and חלוף הכבוד: all are expressions of the anti-honor force. The unworthy student is the human embodiment of מרקוליס — teaching Torah to him IS throwing Torah honor to the idol of desecration. The shared nature (not cause-effect) is the essential property of being the 'anti-honor,' whether expressed as an idol name, an etymology, a concept, or a person-type."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 60, "label": "דִּבּוּר, הוֹלָדָה וְהַתְעוֹרְרוּת", "label_en": "Speech, Birth, and Awakening", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package brings together three connected clusters that all share the same essential nature: the holy domain of restoring and releasing speech, which leads to birth. Component B (הולדה/ארכבותה/זווג גופני/להתיר פה עקרות/פקידות עקרות) describes the birth-side: physical union and the visitation of barren women. Component C (להתיר פה אלמים/זווג נשיקין/כח הדבור) describes the speech-side: releasing the mute ones through the power of speech and the union of kisses. These two components are explicitly linked in the text (lines 63-64): 'כלי הדבור וכלי ההולדה הם בחינה אחת' — the vessels of speech and the vessels of birth ARE THE SAME THING. Component D (שברים/בטחון/כלי הולדה/כליות) describes the structural vessels: שברים (shofar sound) = בטחון (trust) = כלי הולדה (birth organs) = כליות (kidneys/creative organs). The kidneys are the organs of trust and birth-capacity. These 'vessels' are the very containers that receive the power of speech and translate it into birth. The four small pairs merged here: (K) התלבשות/כנף — the clothing/garbing of the face is the act of awakening (the panim must be clothed in stories to avoid blinding the sleeper; this is in the direct service of restoring speech-birth); (L) כשלומד עם אחר/כתיבה — teaching another IS writing on their heart = the speech-act of Torah transmission; (M) צדק/לבושין — tzedek IS garments (Job 29:14), and garments = the clothing of the face that enables gentle awakening = directly in the speech-awakening domain; all of these share the essential nature of 'the holy mechanics of restoring divine speech to those who have fallen silent'.", "dominant_node": "הולדה", "nodes": ["הולדה", "ארכבותה דא לדא נקשן", "זווג גופני", "להתיר פה עקרות", "פקידות עקרות", "להתיר פה אלמים", "זווג נשיקין", "כח הדבור", "שברים", "בטחון", "כלי הולדה", "כליות", "התלבשות", "כנף", "כשלומד עם אחר", "כתיבה", "צדק", "לבושין"], "node_degrees": {"הולדה": 3, "להתיר פה אלמים": 2, "שברים": 2, "בטחון": 2, "זווג גופני": 2, "ארכבותה דא לדא נקשן": 1, "להתיר פה עקרות": 1, "פקידות עקרות": 1, "זווג נשיקין": 1, "כח הדבור": 1, "כלי הולדה": 1, "כליות": 1, "התלבשות": 1, "כנף": 1, "כשלומד עם אחר": 1, "כתיבה": 1, "צדק": 1, "לבושין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["להתיר פה אלמים", "זווג נשיקין", "כח הדבור"], "reason": "Component C: כח הדבור ↔ להתיר פה אלמים ↔ זווג נשיקין. These three are expressions of the same reality: the power of speech (כח הדבור) = releasing the mute (להתיר פה אלמים) = the union of kisses (זווג הנשיקין precedes physical union). The text says (line 64): the speech-liberation זווג IS the kisses-union that enables the bodily-union. Merged with Component B because the text explicitly states (line 63): 'כלי הדבור וכלי ההולדה הם בחינה אחת' — the vessels of speech and birth are ONE bechina."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שברים", "בטחון", "כלי הולדה", "כליות"], "reason": "Component D: שברים (shofar-sound) ↔ בטחון ↔ כלי הולדה, and בטחון ↔ כליות. Trust (בטחון) = the kidneys (כליות) = the birth organs (כלי הולדה), which is what the shofar's 'shevarim' sound IS. The shevarim breaks and opens the birth vessels. The entire cluster is about the structural readiness for birth. Merged into Pkg 2 because shared essential nature: the vessels and organs of holy birth-capacity."}, {"merged_nodes": ["התלבשות", "כנף"], "reason": "Clothing/garbing (התלבשות) = the wing/hem (כנף), based on Isaiah 30:20 'וְלֹא יִכָּנֵף עוֹד מוֹרֶיךָ'. The teaching (section ו) states that the face (panim = sekhel) must be clothed in stories so it does not harm the awakening person. This clothing-act is the mechanism of the speech of awakening — it belongs with the speech-birth domain of Pkg 2."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כשלומד עם אחר", "כתיבה"], "reason": "Teaching another = writing (כתיבה), as the tongue IS a pen (Ps 45:2) engraving on the heart of the student. This is the bechina of the speech-act of Torah transmission. Belongs in Pkg 2 as a dimension of the power of holy speech to engrave (restore) the face/panim of students."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צדק", "לבושין"], "reason": "Tzedek = garments (Job 29:14 'צֶדֶק לָבַשְׁתִּי'). The garments here are the tzedek/justice-clothing through which the face is gradually revealed to the awakening person (Ps 17:15 'אֲנִי בְּצֶדֶק אֶחֱזֶה פָנֶיךָ'). This is the clothing-dimension of the speech-awakening process, directly in the domain of Pkg 2."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 60, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַלְכוּת וְגַדְלוּת", "label_en": "Kingdom and Greatness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes are bechina-equivalent: 'מוֹסֶרְךָ לַמַּלְכוּת' means bringing R' Akiva into a state of 'גַּדְלוּת' (greatness). Malkhut in this context is not the political kingdom but the elevated spiritual state — gadlut — the state of spiritual greatness that enables and compels Torah revelation. Both nodes share the same essential nature: they describe the elevated, expansive state of mind and spiritual standing from which Torah can be revealed. The Rebbe equates the word 'malkhut' (as used by R' Shimon) with the concept of gadlut throughout the passage.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "גדלות"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 1, "גדלות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 60, "label": "הֶעְדֵּר הַדִּבּוּר וְהַשֵּׁנָה", "label_en": "Absence of Speech and Sleep", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the evil mirror of Package 2 (speech and birth). Where Package 2 is the holy restoration of speech and birth, Package 3 is their absence — the state of sleeping (שנה), silence (שתיקה), muteness (אלמים), and the lack of speech (העדר הדבור). Component G: תקיעה ↔ העדר הדבור ↔ 'תקעת לזר כפיך נוקשת באמרי פיך'. The תקיעה (straight shofar-blast) IS the absence of speech — the state of being trapped in one's words (Proverbs 6:1-2). Component H: שתיקה ↔ אלמים — silence IS the mute ones. The Rebbe describes people who are in the state of שנה (sleep) as those who cannot hear or speak — חרשים and אלמים. Component P: מחין דקטנות ↔ שנה — small/contracted intellect IS sleep. These concepts all share the essential nature of 'the domain of contracted consciousness, impaired intellect, and silenced speech' — the state into which people fall when they lose their panim (face/intellect) through improper eating, desire, or spiritual neglect. This is explicitly the evil/negative state that the entire teaching works to correct. The polarity is evil because sleep (שנה), muteness (אלמים), and absence of speech (העדר הדבור) are the negative states in this Torah — the things to be overcome.", "dominant_node": "העדר הדבור", "nodes": ["העדר הדבור", "תקיעה", "\"תקעת לזר כפיך נוקשת באמרי פיך\"", "שתיקה", "אלמים", "מחין דקטנות", "שנה"], "node_degrees": {"העדר הדבור": 2, "תקיעה": 1, "\"תקעת לזר כפיך נוקשת באמרי פיך\"": 1, "שתיקה": 1, "אלמים": 1, "מחין דקטנות": 1, "שנה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שתיקה", "אלמים"], "reason": "Component H: שתיקה ↔ אלמים. Silence = the mute ones. This 2-node pair is the direct statement of the evil state: those in שנה are like חרשים and אלמים (the deaf and mute, who cannot hear and therefore cannot speak). The essential nature is 'the absence of holy speech' — same domain as העדר הדבור. Merged into Pkg 3."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מחין דקטנות", "שנה"], "reason": "Component P: מחין דקטנות ↔ שנה. Contracted intellect (small mind) IS sleep. The Rebbe's teaching (section ו, line 41) says: when the work (avoda) is done with intellect (sekhel), it can ascend; when one falls to מחין דקטנות, it is the state of שנה and cannot ascend. This is the intellectual-dimension of the evil/neutral state of sleep. Shared essential nature with Pkg 3: impaired consciousness that prevents speech and ascent."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 60, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מֶשִׁיכַת הָאֱלֹקוּת וּתְפִיסָה", "label_en": "Drawing of Divinity and Captivity", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes express the same essential reality from different angles: the drawing of Divine presence (אֱלֹקוּת) into the channels of the mind (רְהִיטֵי מֹחִין) through Torah IS a form of 'tephisa' — captivity, holding, containment. The Rebbe cites 'מֶלֶךְ אָסוּר בָּרְהָטִים — בְּרַהֲטֵי מֹחִין' to show that the Divine being drawn into the mind is inherently a bechina of tephisa. Both express the same phenomenon: the infinite held within the finite container of the mind. The Rebbe notes this is good and desirable to God even though it is a form of captivity. This is not cause-effect but two descriptions of the same event.", "dominant_node": "שמה שנמשך אלקותו יתברך, כביכול, ברהטי מחין", "nodes": ["שמה שנמשך אלקותו יתברך, כביכול, ברהטי מחין", "תפיסה"], "node_degrees": {"שמה שנמשך אלקותו יתברך, כביכול, ברהטי מחין": 1, "תפיסה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 60, "label": "הֶבֶל הַיֹּפִי וְהֶפְסֵק הַנְּשִׁימָה", "label_en": "The Vanity of Beauty and Interrupted Breath", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package centers on the evil domain of interrupted breath and physical beauty. The three nodes form a single connected component: הפסק הנשימה ↔ הבל היפי ↔ הבלי הגוים. The interrupted breath (הפסק הנשימה) IS the vanity of beauty (הבל היפי), which is also the vapor of the nations (הבלי הגוים). The Rebbe explains at length (sections ב and ג): the seminal drop exits with an interruption of breath; the vapor/breath that remains in the body clothes itself in the expulsive force and determines the beauty and purity of the offspring. Therefore, whoever desires the beauty of women (יפי הנשים) is receiving the bechina of interrupted breathing — the opposite of the long/continuous breathing that generates wealth (עשירות) and contemplation (hitbonenut). The nations' vapors (הבלי הגוים) cannot bring rain (גשמים) — they have no continuous breath to generate the atmospheric moisture that produces crops and wealth. All three nodes share the same essential nature: the evil/negative domain of physical attraction to beauty, interrupted spiritual breath, and the resulting poverty and spiritual aridity. This package is the direct evil mirror of Package 1 (יראה/עשירות = continuous breath = wealth) — the two are explicitly described as opposites in the text.", "dominant_node": "הפסק הנשימה", "nodes": ["הפסק הנשימה", "הבל היפי", "הבלי הגוים"], "node_degrees": {"הפסק הנשימה": 2, "הבל היפי": 1, "הבלי הגוים": 1}}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 60, "label": "שְׁבִירַת הַלֵּב וְשִׁכְחָה", "label_en": "Breaking of the Heart and Forgetting", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This 4-node component (שכחה/שבירת הלב/שבירת הלוחות/חרפה שברה לבי) forms a coherent evil cluster centered on the breaking that leads to forgetting. The chain: 'חרפה שברה לבי' (Psalm 69:21) = שכחה (forgetting), and שכחה = שבירת הלב (breaking of the heart), and שבירת הלב = שבירת הלוחות (breaking of the tablets). The Rebbe explains (section ח, line 71): 'חֶרְפָּה שָׁבְרָה לִבִּי' is forgetting — the breaking of the tablet-heart causes forgetting, just as the breaking of the First Tablets caused forgetting to enter the world (Eruvin 54). This breaking occurs when the face is written on an impure student (unworthy vessel) — the words are then forgotten from that student, and the teacher's 'tablets' (heart-inscription) are shattered. All four nodes share the same essential nature: 'the domain of spiritual fracture, forgetting, and the loss of Torah that comes from misplaced transmission or shame.' This is the evil mirror of Package 2's teaching/writing domain — where Package 2 is the holy inscription on worthy hearts (כתיבה/לבושין/speech), Package 5 is the shattering of those inscriptions (שבירת הלוחות/שכחה).", "dominant_node": "שכחה", "nodes": ["שכחה", "שבירת הלב", "שבירת הלוחות", "\"חרפה שברה לבי\""], "node_degrees": {"שכחה": 2, "שבירת הלב": 2, "שבירת הלוחות": 1, "\"חרפה שברה לבי\"": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 61, "label": "חָכְמָה עִלָּאָה וְהַצַּדִּיק — אֶבֶן שְׁתִיָּה", "label_en": "Supernal Wisdom and the Tzaddik — the Foundation Stone", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #61 centers on קָדְשֵׁי קֳדָשִׁים (the Holy of Holies) as the hub connecting four parallel aspects of the same supreme reality: חָכְמָה עִלָּאָה (supernal wisdom), צַּדִּיק הַדּוֹר (the tzaddik of the generation), שֵֹּכֶל הָעֶלְיוֹן (the supernal intellect), and אֶבֶן שְׁתִיָּה (the foundation stone). All are bechina of the same essential domain: the all-encompassing divine intellect that is the root of all creation, the source from which all sweetening of judgments flows, and the cosmic locus to which all souls return on Rosh Hashanah. לוּחוֹת הָאֶבֶן (the stone tablets) are also a bechina of חָכְמָה עִלָּאָה — the tablets receive from the foundation stone. Three isolated pairs are merged into this package on the basis of shared essential nature: (1) נְּפָשׁוֹת / אֲבָנִים — souls are 'stones,' sharing the 'stone' bechina with the foundation stone itself; (2) נֶּפֶשׁ / נֵר — the individual soul is a candle, the light-unit that when gathered with others forms the collective אוֹר צַדִּיקִים; (3) עֵזֶר וְהַיְשׁוּעָה / הַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים — salvation and the sweetening of judgments share the same essential nature as the outcome-function of this entire domain. The teaching states: 'יִשְׁלַח עֶזְרְךָ מִקֹּדֶשׁ — הַיְנוּ עַל-יְדֵי הַשֵּׂכֶל שֶׁנִּקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ'; salvation itself IS the holy intellect in its redemptive expression. All twelve nodes share one essential nature: the divine intellectual root that sustains creation, resides in the tzaddik, receives the souls, and sweetens all strict judgment.", "dominant_node": "קדשי קדשים", "nodes": ["קדשי קדשים", "אבן שתיה", "חכמה עלאה", "צדיק הדור", "שכל העליון", "לוחות האבן", "נפשות", "אבנים", "נפש", "נר", "עזר והישועה", "המתקת הדינים"], "node_degrees": {"קדשי קדשים": 4, "אבן שתיה": 2, "חכמה עלאה": 2, "צדיק הדור": 2, "שכל העליון": 1, "לוחות האבן": 1, "נפשות": 1, "אבנים": 1, "נפש": 1, "נר": 1, "עזר והישועה": 1, "המתקת הדינים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נפשות", "אבנים"], "reason": "Souls are explicitly called 'stones' (אֲבָנִים) via the verse 'תִּשְׁתַּפֵּכְנָה אַבְנֵי קֹדֶשׁ' (Eikhah 4:1). The bechina connection to Package 1 is direct and structural: the souls (= stones) come to the tzaddik who IS the אֶבֶן שְׁתִיָּה (= foundation stone) and are included within him. 'Stone' is the shared essential nature of both נְּפָשׁוֹת and the אֶבֶן שְׁתִיָּה cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נפש", "נר"], "reason": "The individual soul IS a candle: 'נֵר ה' נִשְׁמַת אָדָם' (Mishlei 20). This is a description of the soul's essential nature as a light-unit — the same domain as נְּפָשׁוֹת / אֲבָנִים (both describe what a soul fundamentally IS). The candle-soul joins the stone-soul in the same Package 1 domain of 'the nature and gathering of souls before the tzaddik.'"}, {"merged_nodes": ["עזר והישועה", "המתקת הדינים"], "reason": "The text states: 'עִקַּר הָעֵזֶר וְהַיְשׁוּעָה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים, הוּא מִקֹּדֶשׁ, הַיְנוּ עַל-יְדֵי הַשֵּׂכֶל שֶׁנִּקְרָא קֹדֶשׁ' (Section ו). Salvation and the sweetening of judgments share their essential nature with קֹדֶשׁ / שֵׂכֶל — they are the concrete expression of what the holy intellect (Package 1) IS and DOES. They are not an effect produced by Package 1 (eitza) but are the same reality described from the perspective of the recipient."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 61, "label": "מִשְׁפַּט אֱמֶת וֶאֱמוּנַת חֲכָמִים", "label_en": "True Judgment and Faith in the Sages", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all nodes in Torah #61 belonging to the domain of upright, balanced, uncorrupted judgment and the faith in sages that enables it. The bechina-pair {מִשְׁפָּט / דֶּרֶךְ הַמְמֻצָּע} establishes that justice/judgment IS the middle path (עַמּוּדָא דְּאֶמְצָעִיתָא) — it does not lean right or left, it is perfect balance. The pair {אֱמוּנַת חֲכָמִים / 'לֹא תָּסוּר...'} establishes that faith in the sages IS the biblical commandment of non-deviation — same essential quality: receiving from authoritative wisdom without personal distortion. The pair {מִשְׁפְּטֵי הַנְהָגוֹת יְשָׁרוֹת / מִשְׁפְּטֵי אֱמֶת} establishes that upright behavioral guidance IS true judgment. All six nodes share the same domain: the quality of uncorrupted, balanced reception of divine wisdom expressed as righteous leadership. This is the positive, holy pole of the teaching's central axis.", "dominant_node": "משפט", "nodes": ["משפט", "דרך הממצע", "אמונת חכמים", "\"לא תסור מן הדבר אשר יגידו לך ימין ושמאל\"", "משפטי הנהגות ישרות", "משפטי אמת"], "node_degrees": {"משפט": 1, "דרך הממצע": 1, "אמונת חכמים": 1, "\"לא תסור מן הדבר אשר יגידו לך ימין ושמאל\"": 1, "משפטי הנהגות ישרות": 1, "משפטי אמת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אמונת חכמים", "\"לא תסור מן הדבר אשר יגידו לך ימין ושמאל\""], "reason": "The Torah explicitly states אֱמוּנַת חֲכָמִים IS the commandment 'לֹא תָּסוּר מִן הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר יַגִּידוּ לְךָ יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל' (Devarim 17:11). This pair defines the same essential quality — faithful non-deviation from the sages' teaching — which is itself the precondition for משפט (true judgment). They belong together as the 'faith' side of the judgment cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["משפטי הנהגות ישרות", "משפטי אמת"], "reason": "Upright behavioral guidance and true judgment are the same reality: 'מִשְׁפְּטֵי הַנְהָגוֹת יְשָׁרוֹת, בִּבְחִינַת מִשְׁפְּטֵי אֱמֶת.' Both describe the product of uncorrupted reception of wisdom. They name the same quality from two perspectives (practical/behavioral vs. ontological/judicial) and belong in the judgment package."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 61, "label": "מוֹתָרוֹת וּמִשְׁפָּט מְעֻקָּל", "label_en": "Excess-Waste and Distorted Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package groups the evil/negative domain of Torah #61: the corruption of the mind and judgment through physical excess, and its downstream effects. The bechina-pair {עֲצִירוּת / מוֹתָרוֹת} establishes that internal blockage/constipation IS excess-waste — they describe the same evil quality: what is retained when proper flow is absent, the spiritual/physical impurity that clogs the body. The pair {'מִשְׁפָּטִים בַּל יְדָעוּם' / מִשְׁפָּט מְעֻקָּל} establishes that the 'judgments they do not know' IS distorted/bent judgment — both name the same evil: judgment corrupted by the toxic smoke (עֲשָׁנִים סְרוּחִים) of the waste rising to the brain. The pair {עֵצוֹת נִבְעָרוֹת / עֲצַת נָשִׁים} establishes that foolish counsel IS the 'women's counsel' (the kind spoken in the toilet, according to Gittin 45) — both name debased, corrupt counsel that emerges from a heart made impure by the filth of excess. These three pairs mirror the three positive expressions in Package 2: faith in sages (Package 2) vs. damage to faith in sages (this package); true judgment (Package 2) vs. distorted judgment (this package); upright guidance (Package 2) vs. foolish counsel (this package).", "dominant_node": "מותרות", "nodes": ["מותרות", "עצירות", "\"משפטים בל ידעום\"", "משפט מעקל", "עצות נבערות", "עצת נשים"], "node_degrees": {"מותרות": 1, "עצירות": 1, "\"משפטים בל ידעום\"": 1, "משפט מעקל": 1, "עצות נבערות": 1, "עצת נשים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"משפטים בל ידעום\"", "משפט מעקל"], "reason": "The Psalmist's phrase 'מִשְׁפָּטִים בַּל יְדָעוּם' is equated by the Rebbe with the result of confused/clouded intellect — 'בִּלְבּוּל הַמּוֹחִין' — which is itself bent judgment (מִשְׁפָּט מְעֻקָּל). Both nodes name the same evil: judgment so corrupted it can no longer be recognized as judgment. They are the evil mirror of Package 2's {מִשְׁפָּט / דֶּרֶךְ הַמְמֻצָּע}."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עצות נבערות", "עצת נשים"], "reason": "Foolish/bestial counsel IS the kind of counsel made in the toilet — both describe the same quality of corrupt advice that flows from a spiritually-degraded heart. The Rebbe explains that the heart of the person defiled by מוֹתָרוֹת becomes like a toilet, 'שֶׁשָּׁם כָּל עֲצָתָם.' This pair belongs squarely in the evil domain alongside מוֹתָרוֹת and מִשְׁפָּט מְעֻקָּל."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 61, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וְסֵפֶר", "label_en": "Controversy and the Book", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups the domain of halakhic controversy as the purifying mechanism that produces Torah literature and restores faith in the sages. The bechina-pair {מַחֲלֹקֶת / אֵלּוּ הַמַּיִם} establishes that controversy IS the purifying water (מֵי מְרִיבָה) — not destructive but cleansing, turning the accumulated impurity of Package 3 into spiritual purity. The pair {סֵפֶר / שְׁאֵלוֹת וּתְשׁוּבוֹת} establishes that a book (of Torah) IS a collection of questions and answers — the product of controversy processed through teshuvah. Together these four nodes define one essential domain: controversy as the instrument of purification and creative Torah renewal. The teaching's logic: מַחֲלֹקֶת (= purifying water) forces teshuvah, which generates ספרים (= שאלות ותשובות), which complete the Torah, which allows Torah to receive from חָכְמָה עִלָּאָה (Package 1), which sweetens all דינים. This is the process-package — the mechanism that links the fallen state (Package 3) back to the supreme domain (Package 1).", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "אלו המים", "ספר", "שאלות ותשובות"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 1, "אלו המים": 1, "ספר": 1, "שאלות ותשובות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ספר", "שאלות ותשובות"], "reason": "A Torah book IS a collection of questions and responses: 'כִּי מִכָּל מַחֲלֹקֶת נַעֲשֶׂה סֵפֶר, בְּחִינַת שְׁאֵלוֹת וּתְשׁוּבוֹת.' The ספר = שאלות ותשובות pair shares the same essential domain as {מַחֲלֹקֶת / אֵלּוּ הַמַּיִם}: the controversy-and-response process that generates Torah renewal. Both pairs are expressions of the same transformation process."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 62, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וְהַתַּעֲנִית", "label_en": "Faith and the Fast", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package unites the two positive-pole instruments of Torah #62. Component A: the maiden who has no eyes (עולימתא שפירתא דלית לה עינין) IS Faith — she is beautiful (she possesses certainty for the believer) yet has no sight (the unanswerable questions). Her essential nature IS אמונה. Component C: Hunger/fasting IS teshuvah's first-day power — 'רָעָב הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת תַּעֲנִית, שֶׁמַּרְעִיב אֶת עַצְמוֹ' (line 27). The Torah's structure makes these two nodes belong to the same domain: the ברור (purification) of food requires אמונה (section ב), and the technical instrument of that purification is the תענית which draws down waters of kindness to moisten the throat blocked by the desires (section ה). Both אמונה and תענית serve the same end: enabling holy eating that produces the yichud of the Holy One and the Shekhinah. They are both on the constructive/positive side of the teaching — the tools the person wields to overcome the opposing force. The עולימתא שפירתא is the Zoharic personification of the faith that operates in this Torah, linking the image to the concept. The rעב/תענית pair is the physical embodiment of the same spiritual posture: denying oneself food (as the rעב = fast) expresses the same faith-orientation as building one's life around אמונה rather than intellect for the unanswerable. Shared nature: both concepts express the CONSTRUCTIVE, HOLY, HUMAN-CAPACITY domain in Torah #62 — the positive tools through which the person achieves purification and ultimately holy eating.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["עולימתא שפירתא", "אמונה", "רעב", "תענית"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 1, "עולימתא שפירתא": 1, "תענית": 1, "רעב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["רעב", "תענית"], "from_component": "C", "reason": "The Torah explicitly states 'רָעָב הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת תַּעֲנִית' (line 27) — hunger IS fasting. This 2-node pair has the same essential nature as the faith/emunah cluster: both fasting and faith are the POSITIVE INSTRUMENTS of the teaching. The fast produces the healing of speech (moistens the dry throat = galut Mitzraim = מצר הגרון), just as faith produces the purification of food. Both fasting and faith are described in the Torah as the two enabling conditions for holy eating that brings about the yichud. The shared bechina nature is: 'positive, constructive practice that enables holy eating and overcomes the klipot.' Component C is merged into Package 1 with Component A because neither has a larger anchor, and their NATURE (both holy instruments in the same causal chain) is identical."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 62, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רַחֲמָנוּת — הַרַחֲמָנוּת שֶׁבְּיָדֵינוּ", "label_en": "Compassion — The Compassion Given Into Our Hands", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes are two faces of the same essential reality: what 'compassion' truly means in this Torah. The Rebbe states explicitly 'וְעִקָּר רַחֲמָנוּת הוּא בִּבְחִינַת... וְאֵל שַׁדַּי יִתֵּן לָכֶם רַחֲמִים – לָכֶם דַּיְקָא' — the essential nature (bechina) of compassion is that God gives it into our hands so we ourselves can exercise it in a simple, human form. 'רחמנות' and 'שהשם יתברך יתן לנו הרחמנות' are not cause and effect: they are two articulations of the same concept — the compassion-that-is-placed-in-human-hands is precisely what compassion IS in this teaching.", "dominant_node": "רחמנות", "nodes": ["רחמנות", "שהשם יתברך יתן לנו הרחמנות"], "node_degrees": {"רחמנות": 1, "שהשם יתברך יתן לנו הרחמנות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 62, "label": "תַּאֲווֹת וּקְלִפּוֹת", "label_en": "Desires and Husks", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package captures the negative/opposing-force pole of Torah #62. The Torah explicitly states: 'כָּל הַתַּאֲווֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת קְלִפּוֹת, כִּי הַתַּאֲווֹת הֵם מוֹתָרוֹת, כִּי בְּלֹא הַתַּאֲווֹת יָכוֹל הַגּוּף לְהִתְקַיֵּם — נִמְצָא שֶׁהֵם מוֹתָרוֹת לְקִיּוּם הַגּוּף, כְּמוֹ הַקְּלִפּוֹת הֵם מוֹתָרוֹת' (line 21). Desires ARE klipot because both are the excess beyond what is necessary for life — the husk is the surplus wrapping around the fruit; the desire is the surplus beyond bodily necessity. This is a fundamental ontological identification: the evil husks of the cosmic structure and the desires of the person ARE the same thing. Both are 'מוֹתָרוֹת' — excess/superfluous to the essential. When desires overpower the person, it IS the klipot overpowering holiness. This package represents the entire evil-domain structure of the Torah: the klipot that suck from behind holiness, the three ministers of Pharaoh (שר המשקים, אופים, טבחים) who are the root desires, and Pharaoh himself (= עורף = from behind) who feeds them. They are all the same essential category: the excess-husk-desire structure that is opposed to the holy eating the Torah aims to achieve.", "dominant_node": "קלפות", "nodes": ["תאוות", "קלפות"], "node_degrees": {"קלפות": 1, "תאוות": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 62, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה — קִלְקוּל הָאֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Idolatry — Corruption of Faith", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes are all bechina expressions of the same spiritual failure: turning away from God, corrupted faith, and idolatry are not three separate things but three names for one and the same negative reality. The Rebbe cites the Baal Shem Tov: 'כְּשֶׁסָּרִים מֵהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ הוּא בְּחִינַת עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה' — turning away from God IS (bechina of) idolatry. And 'קִלְקוּל הָאֱמוּנָה הִיא גַם-כֵּן בְּחִינַת עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה' — corrupted faith IS (bechina of) idolatry. All three share the same essential nature: the breakdown of one's connection to God, expressed as idolatry. Note: the dataset contains two slightly variant node IDs for idolatry ('עבודה  זרה' with double space and 'עבודה זרה' with single space) — these are the same concept, differing only by a data-entry spacing error; both are included here.", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה", "nodes": ["עבודה זרה", "עבודה  זרה", "קלקול האמונה", "כשסרים מהשם יתברך"], "node_degrees": {"עבודה זרה": 1, "עבודה  זרה": 1, "קלקול האמונה": 1, "כשסרים מהשם יתברך": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עבודה זרה", "עבודה  זרה"], "reason": "These are the same concept node — idolatry — differing only by a data-entry spacing artifact (single space vs. double space). They appear in two separate bechina edges (B2 and B3) both expressing the same equation. Treating them as one node merges those two edges into a single connected component of 3 distinct concepts."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 63, "label": "בְּרִית וְשִׁשָּׁה טְפָחִים", "label_en": "The Covenant and the Six Handbreadths", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the largest and most central cluster in Torah #63. The brit (covenant/circumcision) is the dominant node — the Rebbe opens with the declaration that ברית means 'ammah' (cubit = six tepachim) and that the brit contains all six. Every other node in this cluster is simply another name or face of the same essential reality: the cosmic six-dimensional structure hidden within the brit. לֶחֶם הַפָּנִים (showbread) is called a bechina of brit because it is arranged on Shabbat ('brit olam' — Lev 24) and its frame is one tefach wide (Exod 25). צַדִּיק is a bechina of brit because 'Tzaddik yesod olam' (Prov 10:25) — the righteous person IS the foundation/covenant of the world; he contains all six tepachim in his three pairs of revelation/concealment. אַמָּה בַּת שִׁשָּׁה טְפָחִים is the Aramaic-Talmudic definition of the brit itself (Shabbat 108b). And הַשֵּׁשֶׁת יָמִים / הַשִּׁשָּׁה טְפָחִים are explicitly equated: the six days of creation ARE the six handbreadths, all resting in the Shabbat (which is the brit). All of these concepts share one essential nature: the cosmic sixfold structure that is the brit.", "dominant_node": "ברית", "nodes": ["ברית", "אמה בת ששה טפחים", "לחם הפנים", "צדיק", "ששת ימים", "ששה טפחים"], "node_degrees": {"ברית": 3, "אמה בת ששה טפחים": 1, "לחם הפנים": 1, "צדיק": 1, "ששת ימים": 1, "ששה טפחים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ששת ימים", "ששה טפחים"], "from_component": "D", "reason": "Component D (ששת ימים ↔ ששה טפחים, edge id 3047) is a direct extension of Component A's brit-domain. The source text (lines 7–8) explicitly states: 'כָּל הַשִּׁשָּׁה טְפָחִים הַנַּ\"ל הֵם בְּחִינַת שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים... הִנִּיחַ כֻּלָּם בַּיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי, שֶׁהוּא שַׁבָּת, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת בְּרִית'. The six days and six tepachim are different expressions of exactly the same cosmic structure contained in the brit. They are bechina-equivalents of ברית and belong in the same package. The dominant node (ברית) already has an edge directly to ששה טפחים via the אמה → six tepachim → brit chain, and the explicit textual equation 'ששה טפחים = ששת ימים = שבת = ברית' makes this a single thematic family."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 63, "label": "חֲכָמִים לְהָרַע וְהַנָּחָשׁ", "label_en": "Those Wise to Do Evil and the Serpent", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The philosophizing heretics (מְחַקְּרֵי פִילוֹסוֹפְיָא וְאֶפִּיקוֹרְסוּת) are explicitly stated to be a bechina of the Nachash — the serpent of Genesis 3:1 ('והנחש היה ערום מכל חיות השדה'). These two nodes share the same essential nature: cunning intellect deployed for evil. The nachash here is not a metaphor for a minor evil — it is the defining figure of heretical wisdom, the entity that flies through the air (spreads) via evil speech, the entity that cannot be defeated through argumentation but only through holy speech. This is the evil mirror of Package 3 (the righteous sage/ant): just as the ant-in-the-serpent represents the good sage trapped by heretical pressure, the serpent-as-evil-sage represents the source of that pressure. Polarity is evil: these concepts belong entirely to the domain of the negative, the anti-Torah force in this teaching.", "dominant_node": "הנחש", "nodes": ["חכמים להרע", "הנחש"], "node_degrees": {"חכמים להרע": 1, "הנחש": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 63, "label": "חֲכַם הַדּוֹר וְהַנְּמָלָה", "label_en": "The Sage of the Generation and the Ant", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this cluster — הַנְּמָלָה הַמֻּנָּח בְּתוֹךְ פִּי הַנָּחָשׁ, חֲכַם הַדּוֹר, and נְמָלָה — all describe the same essential identity: the righteous sage who is beleaguered by heretical wisdom. The source text makes the bechinas explicit: 'הַנְּמָלָה הַמֻּנָּח בְּתוֹךְ פִּי הַנָּחָשׁ, הוּא בְחִינוֹת חֲכַם הַדּוֹר' (line 29) and 'הֶחָכָם הוּא בְחִינַת נְמָלָה' (line 33, citing Prov 6:6 'לֵךְ אֶל נְמָלָה עָצֵל — וַחֲכָם'). The dominant node is חֲכַם הַדּוֹר with degree 2, connecting both to the ant-in-the-serpent's-mouth image (the metaphor of his trapped suffering) and to the plain נמלה image (his positive role as teacher of God's ways to the people). This package is the good mirror of Package 2 (the serpent/evil sages): the ant inside the serpent's mouth is the righteous sage who endures צַעַר גָּדוֹל and מִלְחָמָה גְּדוֹלָה from the heretics' probing, but is ultimately the one who teaches דַּעַת וְדַרְכֵי ה' to the people.", "dominant_node": "חכם הדור", "nodes": ["הנמלה המנח בתוך פי הנחש", "חכם הדור", "נמלה"], "node_degrees": {"חכם הדור": 2, "הנמלה המנח בתוך פי הנחש": 1, "נמלה": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 64, "label": "חָלָל הַפָּנוּי וְהַצִּמְצוּם", "label_en": "The Primordial Void and the Contraction", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #64 centers on חלל הפנוי (the primordial empty space) as the structural pre-condition of all creation. Tzimtzum (divine contraction), levush (garment), and tzimtzum ha-or li-tzedadin are all different names for the same metaphysical act of God's self-withdrawal that creates the void. Pharaoh is explicitly equated to the void — 'paroh is an aspect of the chalal ha-panui' — because paroh means nullification and revelation, which is precisely the nature of the void: emptied of Divinity yet containing all creation. Arbeh (locust) is the kabbalistic symbol of the void as 'mineh u-veh' (garment from within itself). These are all parallel expressions — different faces of the same ontological structure. Five isolated pairs are merged here: (1) machloket–briat ha-olam: scholarly dispute creates a chalal ha-panui between sages, which is the same structure as the cosmic void that enables creation — the machloket IS the void-generating act in the human realm; (2) avadim–hakdamot shel kol ha-chokhmot: the servants of Pharaoh are a bechina of the introductory premises of all wisdom-systems — since Pharaoh = chalal ha-panui, his servants share that domain, being the preliminary propositions of those unanswerable heretical questions; (3) tzedek–levushin: righteousness is declared a bechina of vestments (Job 29:14) and levushin is simply the plural form of levush which is already the core of this component; (4) machar–le'atid la-vo: 'tomorrow' is a bechina of the eschatological future — the future is described as the time when the secret of the chalal ha-panui will finally be understood, placing 'machar' firmly within the void-domain as a temporal marker of its revelation; (5) the entire cluster coheres around a single neutral ontological reality: the void is neither holy nor evil — it is the structure of existence itself, the space God made to allow creation.", "dominant_node": "חלל הפנוי", "nodes": ["חלל הפנוי", "צמצום", "לבוש", "ארבה", "פרעה", "צמצום האור לצדדין", "צמצום של חלל הפנוי", "מחלקת", "בריאת העולם", "עבדים", "הקדמות של כל החכמות", "צדק", "לבושין", "מחר", "לעתיד לבוא"], "node_degrees": {"חלל הפנוי": 4, "צמצום": 2, "לבוש": 2, "ארבה": 1, "פרעה": 1, "צמצום האור לצדדין": 1, "צמצום של חלל הפנוי": 1, "מחלקת": 1, "בריאת העולם": 1, "עבדים": 1, "הקדמות של כל החכמות": 1, "צדק": 1, "לבושין": 1, "מחר": 1, "לעתיד לבוא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מחלקת", "בריאת העולם"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly states: 'machloket hi bechinot briat ha-olam' — scholarly dispute IS a bechina of the creation of the world, because dispute creates a chalal ha-panui between the sages (each sage pulls to his side, creating a void-space between them), which is the same structural act as the cosmic tzimtzum. The shared bechina-nature is: both are instances of creating a void that enables something to come into being. This pair belongs in the chalal ha-panui package because the machloket IS the human-realm instantiation of the cosmic void-creating act."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עבדים", "הקדמות של כל החכמות"], "reason": "The servants of Pharaoh are a bechina of the introductory premises of all wisdom-systems (hakdamot shel kol ha-chokhmot). Since Pharaoh is already in this package as a bechina of chalal ha-panui, his 'servants' naturally belong here — they are the propositions and starting-assumptions of those wisdom-systems that come from the void, which also have hardened hearts (unanswerable questions). The shared nature is: preliminary supports of a wisdom that comes from the void."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צדק", "לבושין"], "reason": "Tzedek (righteousness) is declared a bechina of levushin (vestments) via Job 29:14 — 'tzedek lavashti'. Levushin is the plural of levush, which is already the core of this component (levush = tzimtzum = chalal ha-panui). The word-root לבש (to wear/clothe) and the concept of the garment-as-contraction is the shared nature. Tzedek understood as a garment places it within the void-structure domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מחר", "לעתיד לבוא"], "reason": "Machar (tomorrow) is a bechina of le'atid la-vo (the eschatological future, the time of reward). In the Torah's argument, le'atid la-vo is specifically described as the time when humanity will finally understand the chalal ha-panui — 'then they will understand the aspect of the chalal ha-panui.' This makes le'atid la-vo a property of the void-domain: it is the future moment when the void's secret is revealed. Both nodes describe the temporal dimension of the void's self-disclosure."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 64, "label": "שְׁתִיקָה וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "Silence and Moses", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups the four nodes of the שתיקה component: silence (the transcendent mode above speech), Moses (whose defining trait is being 'heavy of mouth' = silent), the unanswerable confusions of heresy (which are themselves a bechina of silence because they have no verbal answer), and 'Be silent, thus it arose in thought' (the divine response to Moses that establishes silence as the proper stance before the unknowable). The essential shared nature is: all these nodes express the holy quality of being above speech — transcending language toward the highest level of faith. Moses is the archetypal silent-one; the heretical confusions are paradoxically silent because they have no verbal resolution; 'shetok kakh ala be-machashavah' is the template-statement of silence as divine wisdom. One isolated pair is merged here: (1) neginah–ruach: melody/song is a bechina of ruach (spirit). In Torah #64, the niggun of the tzaddik is the mechanism by which fallen souls are elevated from the heresy of the chalal ha-panui. The tzaddik who performs this is explicitly 'a bechina of Moses' — and the melody is specifically the 'song of supreme faith' which belongs to Moses-level. Neginah as a form of ruach (divine spirit flowing through music) shares the essential nature of the Moses-silence cluster: transcending speech to reach the level above language. It is not about the evil side nor the neutral structural void — it is the positive holy capacity to operate beyond verbal comprehension.", "dominant_node": "שתיקה", "nodes": ["שתיקה", "משה", "המבוכות והקשיות של האפיקורסית הזאת הבא מחלל הפנוי", "שתק כך עלה במחשבה", "נגינה", "רוח"], "node_degrees": {"שתיקה": 3, "משה": 1, "המבוכות והקשיות של האפיקורסית הזאת הבא מחלל הפנוי": 1, "שתק כך עלה במחשבה": 1, "נגינה": 1, "רוח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נגינה", "רוח"], "reason": "Neginah (melody) is a bechina of ruach (spirit/breath). In Torah #64, the niggun of the tzaddik is the supreme vehicle for raising souls from the heresy of the chalal ha-panui. The tzaddik performing this is 'a bechina of Moses' (line 47: 'tzaddik ha-dor she-hu bechinot Moshe'). The niggun belongs to 'rosh emunah' — the supreme faith that is above all other wisdoms. This is precisely the domain of shitika (silence above speech): the niggun of rosh emunah operates where speech cannot reach. The shared bechina-nature of this cluster is: the mode of holy transcendence above verbal-rational discourse. Neginah and ruach share this nature as much as Moses and 'shetok kakh ala be-machashavah'."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 64, "label": "שְׂחוֹק וּקְלִפּוֹת", "label_en": "Mockery and the Husks", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This isolated pair forms a small but self-standing evil package: mockery/play (שחוק) is a bechina of the kelipot (the husks of evil). The source text (line 60) explains: 'התעללתי — פירש רש\"י: שיחקתי... בחינת הקלפות, שהן כנגד הקדשה רק כמו מי שמדמה ומשחק ומעקם עצמו לחברו — כקוף המשחק ומדמה עצמו לאדם.' The kelipot mimic holiness like a monkey mimicking a man — they are the domain of false imitation and play. This is the evil dimension of creation, arising from the excess of light and the breaking of the vessels. The pair does NOT belong in Package 1 (the void) because the void is a neutral metaphysical structure, whereas the kelipot are the EVIL FORCE that arose as a consequence of the breaking of the vessels — a distinct ontological category with a negative valence. There is no larger evil component in this Torah to merge them into, so they stand as their own two-node evil package, representing the evil mirror of the holy creative process.", "dominant_node": "קלפות", "nodes": ["שחוק", "קלפות"], "node_degrees": {"שחוק": 1, "קלפות": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 65, "label": "תַּכְלִית וּבִטּוּל", "label_en": "The Ultimate Goal and Self-Nullification", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant hub תכלית (the ultimate purpose/goal of all existence) is directly equated with four parallel concepts that illuminate the same reality from different angles: בטול (self-nullification — the experiential state of dissolving into the infinite), כלו טוב (all-is-good — the moral dimension: from the vantage of the takhlit, everything is revealed as pure good), אחד (Oneness — the metaphysical dimension: ה' is One and His name is One), and עדן (the Garden of Eden — the eschatological/spatial name for the place no eye has seen). These five nodes are all expressions of the same transcendent reality. In Torah #65, Rebbe Nachman builds his central teaching around this concept: the takhlit is both the eschatological endpoint AND the perspective that dissolves all suffering. The good/evil mirror is visible: the opposite of takhlit is the condition of eyes-open to this-world's vanity, where suffering is felt as total — but that negative pole is handled through eitza connections, not bechina ones. This cluster is entirely in the domain of holiness and the divine perspective.", "dominant_node": "תכלית", "nodes": ["תכלית", "בטול", "כלו טוב", "אחד", "עדן"], "node_degrees": {"תכלית": 4, "בטול": 1, "כלו טוב": 1, "אחד": 1, "עדן": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 65, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קִנְאַת ה' וּצְדָקָה", "label_en": "Zealotry for God and Charity", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two concepts share the same essential spiritual nature: an act of fiery devotion to God's honor (קנאה) is structurally equivalent to an act of tzedakah. Both represent the channel through which a person elevates and sanctifies the world — one through passionate defense of God's name, the other through material generosity. The Rebbe's source (Tosafot on B.B. 10b, the case of Nevuzaradan) demonstrates that the act of 'being zealous for God' constitutes tzedakah in the fullest sense; they are two expressions of the same reality of sacred self-giving.", "dominant_node": "קנאת ה צבאות", "nodes": ["קנאת ה צבאות", "צדקה"], "node_degrees": {"קנאת ה צבאות": 1, "צדקה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 65, "label": "שִׂמְחָה וְקַבָּלַת הַתּוֹרָה", "label_en": "Joy and the Receiving of Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this component share the same essential domain: the joyful, wholehearted commitment expressed at Sinai as 'נַעֲשֶׂה וְנִשְׁמַע' (we will do and we will hear). All three are aspects of the same spiritual act and state: שמחה (joy) IS a bechina of נעשה ונשמע because the Gemara (Shabbat 88a) teaches that when Israel said נעשה ונשמע, 600,000 angels placed two crowns on their heads, and those crowns are called 'שמחת עולם' — eternal joy. The שמחה is not the cause of the מעשה; it IS the character of the act of acceptance. Similarly, נעשה ונשמע IS a bechina of קבלת התורה — it is the defining moment and mode of Torah-receiving. This cluster describes the essential nature of joyful Torah-reception. In the Torah's argument, שמחה serves as the vessel (כלי) for receiving chidushei Torah — the joy that comes from seeing all sufferings as good generates the crown-state that enables Torah novelty to flow. This is the positive renewal dimension of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "נעשה ונשמע", "nodes": ["נעשה ונשמע", "שמחה", "קבלת התורה"], "node_degrees": {"נעשה ונשמע": 2, "שמחה": 1, "קבלת התורה": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 65, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ", "label_en": "The Soul-Faculty of Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this component are all aspects of the same dimension: the nefesh as the faculty of speech, prayer, and song. נפש is the central hub: רות is explicitly stated to BE a bechina of the nefesh ('למה נקרא שמה רות שממנה יצא דוד שריוה להקב\"ה בשירות ותשבחות') — Ruth's essential nature is the soul-faculty that produces the songs and praises that satisfy the Divine. גן is also explicitly stated to be a bechina of the nefesh ('גן הוא בחינת הנפש, בחינת והיתה נפשם כגן רווה') — the garden-soul is the refreshed, watered soul. In Torah #65, the nefesh is the direct source of speech in prayer: the dibur (word) emerges from the nefesh (בראשית 2:7 — ויהי האדם לנפש חיה = לרוח ממללא). This cluster describes the essential nature of the nefesh as speech-origin and prayer-source — the figure of Ruth personifies it, and the garden-image captures its state of flourishing.", "dominant_node": "נפש", "nodes": ["נפש", "רות", "גן"], "node_degrees": {"נפש": 2, "רות": 1, "גן": 1}}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 65, "label": "יִסּוּרִין וְצִמְאוֹן הַנֶּפֶשׁ", "label_en": "Suffering and the Thirst of the Soul", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package is formed by merging two small isolated pairs (Component D: מליחות ↔ יסורין; Component E: הרגשת היסורין ↔ צמאון הנפש) because they share the same essential bechina-nature and the source text explicitly chains them together: 'הַרְגָּשַׁת הַיִּסּוּרִין הוּא בְּחִינַת צִמְאוֹן הַנֶּפֶשׁ, כִּי צִמָּאוֹן הוּא עַל יְדֵי מְלִיחוּת, וּמְלִיחוּת הוּא בְחִינַת יִסּוּרִין'. This creates a direct bechina-chain: יסורין = מליחות = צמאון = הרגשת היסורין. All four describe the same phenomenological reality — suffering as experienced by the soul — from different angles: the covenantal dimension (ברית במלח = ברית ביסורין), the physical sensation (saltiness causing thirst), the experiential name (thirst of the soul), and the direct sensory description (felt-suffering). Polarity is neutral because in this Torah, suffering is neither intrinsically good nor evil — it is the domain of divine refinement that from the perspective of the takhlit is revealed as pure good, but as experienced in the moment is the painful starting-point the teaching addresses. It is a neutral object that can be transformed.", "dominant_node": "יסורין", "nodes": ["יסורין", "מליחות", "הרגשת היסורין", "צמאון הנפש"], "node_degrees": {"יסורין": 1, "מליחות": 1, "הרגשת היסורין": 1, "צמאון הנפש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הרגשת היסורין", "צמאון הנפש"], "from_component": "E", "reason": "This isolated pair shares the same bechina-nature as Component D (יסורין/מליחות). The source text in section ד explicitly chains them: הרגשת היסורין = צמאון הנפש, because צמאון is caused by מליחות, and מליחות IS יסורין. All four nodes describe the same experiential domain of suffering-as-thirst-and-saltiness. They are NOT cause-effect (it is not that מליחות produces צמאון — it IS צמאון by nature). Merged into this package by shared bechina-nature, not by eitza."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 66, "label": "כֹּחַ וּפֹעַל — הָאָלֶ\"ף", "label_en": "Potential and Actualization — The Alef", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant conceptual structure of Torah #66 is the Alef-letter as the encoding of the koach-po'al (potential-actualization) duality. This package unifies five originally separate but inseparable components: (B) the koach-pole — יו\"ד בָּרֹאשׁ (the first yud = Shem haAtzem = beginning of thought = koach = upper spirit/ruach d'le'ela); (C) the po'al-pole — יו\"ד בַּסּוֹף (second yud = Shem Adni = end of action = po'al = lower spirit/ruach d'le'tata); (D) the vav/patach — the vav inside the alef that is a bechina of patach (opening), representing the act by which the two yuds are opened and separated when actualization occurs; (F) the combined כֹּחַ וּפֹעַל node equated with the two hands (yadayim = the instruments of manifestation); and (K) the chituch ha-yadayim equated with the verse 'פּוֹתֵחַ אֶת יָדֶךָ' (the opening of the hands = the actualization event). All five sub-clusters describe the same essential reality from different angles: the pre-actualization state (two yuds bound together, hands not yet open), the moment of actualization (patach/vav opens them), and the resulting structure (right hand = koach, left hand = po'al). The polarity is good because the entire teaching aims at enabling a person to bring their holy intentions from potential into actual practice — from machshavah to ma'aseh. The evil mirror of this package is the evil actualization: 'when a sinner actualizes his sin, he is completely wicked' (line 33) — but those nodes are not in the dataset as bechina edges.", "dominant_node": "כֹּחַ", "nodes": ["יוּ\"ד בָּרֹאשׁ", "כֹּחַ", "הַיּוּ\"ד שֶׁל שֵׁם הָעֶצֶם, שֶׁהוּא שֵׁם הֲוָיָה בָּרוּךְ הוּא", "רוּחַ דִּלְעֵלָּא", "תְּחִלַּת הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה", "מַחֲשָׁבָה תְּחִלָּה", "פֹּעַל", "יוּ\"ד בַּסּוֹף", "יוּ\"ד שֶׁל הַכִּנּוּי, שֶׁהוּא שֵׁם אֲדֹנָי, שֶׁהוּא יוּ\"ד בַּסּוֹף", "סוֹף הַמַּעֲשֶׂה", "רוּחַ דִּלְתַתָּא", "פַּתָּח", "וָּאו בְּאֶמְצַע", "וָּאו שֶׁבְּתוֹךְ הָאָלֶף", "וָּאו אוֹתִיּוֹת שֶׁיֵּשׁ בֵּין שְׁנֵי הַיּוּדִי\"ן שֶׁל שְׁנֵי הַשֵּׁמוֹת הַנַּ\"ל, כָּזֶה, יאקדונקי", "כֹּחַ וּפֹעַל", "שְׁנֵי הַיָּדַיִם יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל", "חִתּוּךְ הַיָּדַיִם", "'פּוֹתֵחַ אֶת יָדֶךָ'"], "node_degrees": {"כֹּחַ": 3, "יוּ\"ד בָּרֹאשׁ": 3, "פֹּעַל": 3, "יוּ\"ד שֶׁל הַכִּנּוּי, שֶׁהוּא שֵׁם אֲדֹנָי, שֶׁהוּא יוּ\"ד בַּסּוֹף": 2, "יוּ\"ד בַּסּוֹף": 2, "סוֹף הַמַּעֲשֶׂה": 2, "וָּאו שֶׁבְּתוֹךְ הָאָלֶף": 2, "פַּתָּח": 2, "הַיּוּ\"ד שֶׁל שֵׁם הָעֶצֶם, שֶׁהוּא שֵׁם הֲוָיָה בָּרוּךְ הוּא": 1, "רוּחַ דִּלְעֵלָּא": 1, "תְּחִלַּת הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה": 1, "מַחֲשָׁבָה תְּחִלָּה": 1, "רוּחַ דִּלְתַתָּא": 1, "וָּאו בְּאֶמְצַע": 1, "וָּאו אוֹתִיּוֹת שֶׁיֵּשׁ בֵּין שְׁנֵי הַיּוּדִי\"ן שֶׁל שְׁנֵי הַשֵּׁמוֹת הַנַּ\"ל, כָּזֶה, יאקדונקי": 1, "כֹּחַ וּפֹעַל": 1, "שְׁנֵי הַיָּדַיִם יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל": 1, "חִתּוּךְ הַיָּדַיִם": 1, "'פּוֹתֵחַ אֶת יָדֶךָ'": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_components": ["B (כח cluster)", "C (פועל cluster)"], "reason": "Components B and C are the two poles of the identical structural axis: the upper yud/koach and the lower yud/po'al. They are the two sub-structures that together constitute the alef-letter. In Torah #66 the Rebbe explicitly states 'שְׁנֵי הָרוּחוֹת...הֵם בְּחִינַת כֹּחַ וּפֹעַל' — the two spirits ARE koach and po'al. They are two bechina-components of the same concept, not cause-and-effect. They are separated in the raw graph only because no single bechina-edge directly links the two yuds (the text frames them as a duality, not equating them to each other). Merging them is required: they are both faces of the alef-structure and must be in the same package."}, {"merged_components": ["D (patach/vav cluster)"], "reason": "The vav-inside-the-alef IS a bechina of patach (opening). This component describes the same alef-structure as components B and C — it is the mediating element between the two yuds. Its essential nature is 'the opening act' that takes place within the alef, identical to the act of actualizing koach into po'al. Not a cause-and-effect (the vav does not produce the separation — it IS the separation, the opening, the patach state). Merged into Package 1."}, {"merged_components": ["F (כח ופועל/שני הידים)"], "reason": "The combined node 'כח ופועל' equated to 'שני הידים ימין ושמאל' describes the integrated, realized state of koach-po'al in the bodily instruments of action. This is the same domain as Package 1 — the hands are the site where the alef-structure becomes manifest in the world. The Rebbe explicitly states in lines 26-27: 'שְׁנֵי הָרוּחוֹת...בְּחִינַת שְׁתֵּי הַיָּדַיִם – יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל'. Merged into Package 1."}, {"merged_components": ["K (חתוך הידים / פותח את ידך)"], "reason": "Chituch ha-yadayim ('cutting/opening of the hands') is stated to be a bechina of 'Potei'ach et yadecha' (Ps 145:16). In the Torah's argument this is the actualization event — when the hands are cut/opened, koach is released into po'al. The acrostic of 'Potei'ach et yadecha' = cha-ta-kh = gematria of 2×ruach. This is entirely within the alef/koach-po'al domain. Its shared bechina-nature with Package 1 is the act-of-opening that is the essence of actualization. Merged into Package 1."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 66, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שֵׁם ה' וְהַחִיּוּת", "label_en": "The Divine Name and the Life-Force", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These four nodes all express the same essential reality: the Divine Name as the living essence of all existence. 'The Name is the life-force' (הַשֵּׁם הוּא הַחִיּוּת) and 'Shabbat is the Name of the Holy One' (שַׁבָּת שְׁמָא דְּקֻדְשָׁא בְּרִיךְ הוּא) — Name, life-force, and Shabbat are all parallel expressions of the same divine presence. Shabbat was commanded immediately after Yom Kippur precisely because after Yom Kippur the Name of God is revealed, and Shabbat itself IS that Name. The two raw components {שם, חיות} and {שבת, שמא דקדשא בריך הוא} are merged because their proof texts are drawn from the same passage and orbit the identical theological claim: the Name of God = life.", "dominant_node": "שם", "nodes": ["שם", "חיות", "שבת", "שמא דקדשא בריך הוא"], "node_degrees": {"שם": 1, "חיות": 1, "שבת": 1, "שמא דקדשא בריך הוא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שבת", "שמא דקדשא בריך הוא"], "reason": "The raw component {שבת, שמא דקדשא בריך הוא} is merged with {שם, חיות} because both components express the identical claim: the Divine Name = the living essence of reality. The proof text for both edges states that the Name is the life-force (נֶפֶשׁ חַיָה הוּא שְׁמוֹ) and that Shabbat is the Name of God (שַׁבָּת שְׁמָא דְּקֻדְשָׁא בְּרִיךְ הוּא). Shabbat, the Name, and life-force are three faces of the same divine presence — not cause-and-effect, but parallel aspects of one reality."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 66, "label": "אֱמֶת וְעוֹלָם הַבָּא", "label_en": "Truth and the World to Come", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The second large connected component of Torah #66 centers on אֱמֶת (Truth) as the portal through which the World to Come enters this world. All nodes in this cluster are different manifestations of the same ontological reality: divine truth as the foundation of genuine prayer. אֱמֶת = עוֹלָם הַבָּא = עָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי (the coming world) = אֵל (the divine name El, whose essence is truth — 'לֹא אִישׁ אֵל וִיכַזֵּב') = אֵל הֲוָיָה (the combination of El+YHVH in the world of Yetzirah) = יַעֲקֹב (whose seal is truth — 'תִּתֵּן אֱמֶת לְיַעֲקֹב') = פָּנִים (the Divine Face/Presence — truth is the face of God: 'מְבַקְּשֵׁי פָנֶיךָ יַעֲקֹב סֶלָה') = דִּבּוּר שֶׁל שַׁבָּת (Shabbat speech, which draws the coming-world dimension into weekday speech) = שַׁבָּת itself (which IS a bechina of the World to Come). The small pair הֵי / שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר (the letter Hei = payment to the wicked in the World to Come) is merged here because the 'payment' (שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר) of the wicked is precisely how truth/emet manifests as judgment. The Rebbe says: 'אֵל שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת אֱמֶת, שֶׁעַל יָדוֹ נַעֲשֶׂה שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר לָרְשָׁעִים' — El=Emet is the agent of the wicked's payment. Hei=payment is thus in the same essential truth-domain. The package describes the positive dimension: these concepts enable a person to pray in truth, free from dependence on other people.", "dominant_node": "אֱמֶת", "nodes": ["עָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי", "אֱמֶת", "אֵל", "יַעֲקֹב", "פָּנִים", "'אֵל הֲוָיָה'", "דִּבּוּר שֶׁל שַׁבָּת", "שַׁבָּת", "הֵי", "שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר"], "node_degrees": {"עָלְמָא דְּאָתֵי": 4, "אֱמֶת": 4, "אֵל": 1, "יַעֲקֹב": 1, "פָּנִים": 1, "'אֵל הֲוָיָה'": 1, "דִּבּוּר שֶׁל שַׁבָּת": 1, "שַׁבָּת": 1, "הֵי": 1, "שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הֵי", "שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר"], "from_component": "J (isolated pair)", "reason": "The letter Hei (in the milui/spelled-out form of Shem Havayah) = a bechina of shilum sakhar (payment of wages to the wicked in the World to Come). The source text (section ג, line 55-56) explains this in direct connection to the El-Emet nexus: 'אֵל שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת אֱמֶת, שֶׁעַל יָדוֹ נַעֲשֶׂה שִׁלּוּם שָׂכָר לָרְשָׁעִים, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת הֲוָיָה בְּמִלּוּי הֵהִין'. The Hei/payment pair describes how Emet (El=Emet=Package 2) is the agent that brings about the punishment of the wicked. The shared nature is: divine truth-reality as the ultimate arbiter of cosmic justice. This belongs in the truth/olam-haba package, not as an independent domain."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 66, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וְיְסוֹד הַפָּשׁוּט", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Simple Foundation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Tzaddik is explicitly identified as the 'Simple Foundation' (יְסוֹד הַפָּשׁוּט) — the single root from which the four elemental forces of all existence flow. These two nodes are two names for the same cosmic reality: the unitary source beneath all multiplicity. The verse 'צַדִּיק יְסוֹד עוֹלָם' (Proverbs 10) identifies the Tzaddik as the foundation of the world, and the teaching identifies this as the Simple Foundation — the unified root that precedes the split into four rivers/elements. This is the positive pole of the Torah: the Tzaddik as the world's sustaining root.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["יסוד הפשוט", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"יסוד הפשוט": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 66, "label": "שְׁלֵמוּת הַדִּבּוּר", "label_en": "The Perfection of Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all nodes describing the essential nature of perfected, complete speech in the weekday world. הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת IS a bechina of שָׂפָה בְּרוּרָה (clear/pure language — the prophetic phrase from Zephaniah 3:9 describing the messianic state when all peoples will call upon God's name). They are the same concept: full, uncorrupted speech. The isolated pair יְמֵי הַחֹל / יְצִירָה is merged here because weekdays = the world of Yetzirah (Formation), which IS the domain of speech in its incomplete form (as the Rebbe states in line 56: 'יְמֵי הַחֹל הֵם בְּחִינוֹת יְצִירָה'). The goal of drawing Shabbat-speech (Package 2's דִּבּוּר שֶׁל שַׁבָּת) into the weekday-speech context IS the achievement of שְׁלֵמוּת הַדִּבּוּר. This is the speech-domain: it describes the state and site of human speech awaiting perfection, as distinct from Package 2's divine truth-domain. The package's polarity is good because שָׂפָה בְּרוּרָה is the eschatological goal and הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת is its positive achievement.", "dominant_node": "הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת", "nodes": ["הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת", "שָׂפָה בְּרוּרָה", "יְמֵי הַחֹל", "יְצִירָה"], "node_degrees": {"הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת": 1, "שָׂפָה בְּרוּרָה": 1, "יְמֵי הַחֹל": 1, "יְצִירָה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יְמֵי הַחֹל", "יְצִירָה"], "from_component": "L (isolated pair)", "reason": "The weekdays (ימי החול) = a bechina of Yetzirah (the world of Formation). The source text (line 56) frames this in direct connection to the speech-perfection theme: 'יְמֵי הַחֹל הֵם בְּחִינוֹת יְצִירָה, כְּשֶׁאֵין אֶחָד בָּהֶם, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינוֹת שַׁבָּת.' The weekday-Yetzirah pair names the domain within which הַדִּבּוּר בִּשְׁלֵמוּת must be drawn into and achieved. They share the same essential nature: the incomplete-speech-world that needs completing. Merged into Package 3."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 66, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֳנָשִׁים וּמִיתָה", "label_en": "Punishments and Death", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These two nodes are parallel expressions of the same negative reality: punishment at any level is a form of death, because it uproots a person from the root of their life-force. The Gemara (Avodah Zarah 5a) interprets 'they died' as 'they became poor' — confirming that even loss of wealth or status constitutes a bechina of death. Since the Name/life-force is the positive pole (Package 1), punishment/death is its direct negative mirror: the domain where the life-connection is severed. This is the evil mirror of Package 1: just as the Name = life, punishment = death (severance from the Name).", "dominant_node": "מיתה", "nodes": ["ענשים", "מיתה"], "node_degrees": {"ענשים": 1, "מיתה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 66, "label": "נְקֻדּוֹת וּרְקִיעִין", "label_en": "Vowel-Points and Firmaments of Longing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This three-node component forms an independent and complete bechina-chain: נְקֻדּוֹת (vowel-points) = רְקִיעִין (firmaments) = מְנִיעוֹת (obstacles/barriers). The Rebbe establishes that the nine vowel-points are bechinat the nine firmaments (reki'im), and the firmaments = obstacles (meni'ot). The logic: a rakiya (firmament) is that which separates and blocks — 'יְהִי רָקִיעַ, וִיהִי מַבְדִּיל' — just as an obstacle blocks the person from the desired thing. The nekudah (vowel-point) is produced by desire (cheshek) which itself is intensified by the obstacle. The chain: nekudah = rakiya = meni'ah. This package describes the mechanism by which longing/desire is encoded structurally into the very vowel-points of speech — the 'sound-color' dimension of each letter that carries the intensity of the person's yearning. The polarity is good because the nekudot are the positive medium of genuine speech and the reki'im/meni'ot, although they appear as obstacles, serve the holy purpose of intensifying desire.", "dominant_node": "רְקִיעִין", "nodes": ["נְקֻדּוֹת", "רְקִיעִין", "מְנִיעוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"רְקִיעִין": 2, "נְקֻדּוֹת": 1, "מְנִיעוֹת": 1}}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 66, "label": "חֲרוֹן אַף וְנֶזֶק הַפַּרְנָסָה", "label_en": "Anger and the Damage to Livelihood", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package groups the two isolated pairs that together describe the negative/destructive domain of Torah #66: the bechina-chain of anger→smoke→eyes→livelihood. Charon af (anger) = the smoke that rises from the nostrils (based on Ps 18:9: 'עָלָה עָשָׁן בְּאַפּוֹ'). This smoke = a bechina of damage to parnasah (livelihood) because the eyes (which represent the expectation of livelihood: 'עֵינֵי כֹל אֵלֶיךָ יְשַׂבֵּרוּ' Ps 145:15) are damaged by smoke (Prov 10:26: 'כֶּעָשָׁן לָעֵינָיִם'). This creates the bechina chain: חֲרוֹן אַף = עָשָׁן = נֶזֶק לָעֵינַיִם = עֵינַיִם = פַּרְנָסָה. These four nodes share the same essential nature: the destructive force of anger that blocks the koach→po'al actualization by damaging livelihood (and without livelihood one cannot be free from dependence on others, and without that freedom one cannot pray in truth, and without truth one cannot actualize). This is the evil mirror of the entire teaching: while Package 1 describes holy actualization, Package 5 describes the anger that blocks it. Anger is characterized as 'evil' here because its bechina-language in the text aligns it with the destructive/blocking dimension. The Rebbe teaches that the sweetening of the anger (hamtakat charon af) transforms the smoke into ruach — it is the transition from Package 5 to Package 1.", "dominant_node": "חֲרוֹן אַף", "nodes": ["חֲרוֹן אַף", "עָשָׁן שֶׁיּוֹצֵא מִנְּחִירָיו", "עֵינַיִם", "פַּרְנָסָה"], "node_degrees": {"חֲרוֹן אַף": 1, "עָשָׁן שֶׁיּוֹצֵא מִנְּחִירָיו": 1, "עֵינַיִם": 1, "פַּרְנָסָה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עֵינַיִם", "פַּרְנָסָה"], "from_component": "H (isolated pair)", "reason": "Eyes = parnasah (livelihood) via Ps 145:15 ('עֵינֵי כֹל אֵלֶיךָ יְשַׂבֵּרוּ'). In the source text (line 24), the Rebbe explains this in direct sequence with the charon-af/smoke pair: 'הֶעָשָׁן מַזִּיק לָעֵינַיִם...שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת פַּרְנָסָה'. The eyes/parnasah pair is the downstream target of the anger-smoke. Their shared bechina-nature with Component I (charon af/smoke) is the destructive domain of anger. Merged into Package 5."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 67, "label": "כָּבוֹד וּמַלְכוּת", "label_en": "Divine Honor and Kingship", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #67 centers on כָּבוֹד (divine honor/glory) as the essential nature shared by מַלְכוּת (kingship), כֶּתֶר מַלְכוּת (the crown of kingship), and שֻׁלְחָנוֹ שֶׁל אָדָם (the person's table). These are all parallel expressions of the same royal/holy domain: the table is the crown of kingship (Yoma 72b), and the crown of kingship is the honor, and the honor is kingship (מֶלֶךְ הַכָּבוֹד, Psalm 24:7). Also in this component: כָּבוֹד is the bechina of אֵשׁ (fire, 'וּכְבוֹד ה׳ כְּאֵשׁ אֹכֶלֶת'), of קֹדֶשׁ (holiness, 'וְנִקְדַּשׁ בִּכְבוֹדִי'), and of חַוָּה (the mother of all life, since kavod is the root/source of all souls). The שֻׁלְחָן שֶׁבִּקְדֻשָּׁה (the table in holiness) is the bechina of מַלְכוּת from the side of מַלְכוּת itself, completing the ring. All these concepts share the single essential nature: the sacred presence of divine kingship as an anchoring, life-giving reality. Four 2-node pairs are merged here: (a) נֶפֶשׁ↔לֵּב — the heart/soul pair belongs in the kavod-kingship cluster because the Torah's entire argument is that the נֶפֶשׁ dwells within the כָּבוֹד, and when the heart distances itself from prayer the נֶפֶשׁ distances from כָּבוֹד — their essential bechina is that both are the 'inner life' that connects to כָּבוֹד; (b) שְׁמוּעָה טוֹבָה↔קוֹל הָרְעָמִים — thunder is the voice of divine honor ('אֵל הַכָּבוֹד הִרְעִים', Psalm 29:3), good tidings are the sound of thunder when the Rebbe reveals wisdom; these belong in the kavod cluster as the acoustic/communicative face of כָּבוֹד.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["כבוד", "מלכות", "כתר מלכות", "שלחנו של אדם", "שלחן שבקדשה", "אש", "קדש", "חוה", "נפש", "לב", "שמועה טובה", "קול הרעמים"], "node_degrees": {"כבוד": 5, "מלכות": 2, "כתר מלכות": 2, "שלחנו של אדם": 1, "שלחן שבקדשה": 1, "אש": 1, "קדש": 1, "חוה": 1, "נפש": 1, "לב": 1, "שמועה טובה": 1, "קול הרעמים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נפש", "לב"], "reason": "The heart (לֵּב) is a bechina of the soul (נֶפֶשׁ) — 'אֵלֶיךָ ה׳ נַפְשִׁי אֶשָּׂא, פֵּרוּשׁ רַשִׁ׳׳י: לִבִּי אֲכַוֵּן'. In Torah #67 the central drama is that the נֶפֶשׁ is rooted in כָּבוֹד (kavod is the mother and root of all souls), and the neglect of heartfelt prayer (כַּוָּנַת הַלֵּב) causes the נֶפֶשׁ to distance itself from its root, the כָּבוֹד. The nefesh/lev pair participates in the same essential domain as כָּבוֹד: the inner life of the human being whose source and home is the divine honor."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שמועה טובה", "קול הרעמים"], "reason": "Thunder is explicitly the voice of כָּבוֹד — 'אֵל הַכָּבוֹד הִרְעִים ה׳ עַל מַיִם רַבִּים' (Psalm 29:3). Good tidings (שְׁמוּעָה טוֹבָה) = the sound of thunder that heals the weary soul. The Torah #67 text (section 8) explicitly states: 'שְׁמוּעָה טוֹבָה זֶה בְחִינַת קוֹל הָרְעָמִים... שֶׁמְּתַקְּנִין הִתְרַחֲקוּת הַנֶּפֶשׁ מִן הַכָּבוֹד'. The sound-pair is a face of the divine honor domain."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 67, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שַׁבָּת וְהַצַּדִּיק — אוֹר הָעֵינַיִם", "label_en": "Shabbat and the Tzaddik — Light of the Eyes", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of divine illumination as channeled through the Tzaddik and through Shabbat. The core teaching equates the Tzaddik with Shabbat (both are 'Shem d'Kudsha Berikh Hu'), and both produce the three colors of the eye (תלת גונין דעינא ובת עין), which are the light of the eyes (מאור העינים), which is the light of the Temple (the Temple = עינים), which is the beauty and splendor of the Tzaddik (פאר), which is the intellect/mohin (מחין). All the structural equivalents — the house (בית), the tefillin-boxes (בתים של התפלין), the Tzaddik as primordial foundation (יסוד הפשוט), the four elements flowing from him, the holy name (שם ה / שם הקדש), and Noah as prototype of the departed Tzaddik — are all expressions of the same essential reality: the illumination that the true Tzaddik-head brings into the world, opening eyes and sustaining the Temple. The good/evil mirror: this entire package mirrors Package 2 (false heads, fire-lights) and Package 3 (the evil fire that burns when this light departs).", "dominant_node": "תלת גונין דעינא ובת עין", "nodes": ["שבת", "מאורי אור", "תלת גונין דעינא ובת עין", "כלליות הגונין", "ראש בית", "בעל הבית של העולם", "צדיק האמת", "היפי והפאר של העולם", "יוסף", "תפלין", "מחין", "המחין", "ראש", "פאר", "שם ה", "הצדיק", "נח", "כח הראות", "שמא דקדשא בריך הוא", "מאור העינים", "הגונין המאירין במקדש", "צדיק", "בית", "בית המקדש", "עינים", "בתים של התפלין", "דירה", "יסוד הפשוט", "צדיק יסוד עולם", "נהר יוצא מעדן", "בכיה", "הסתלקות מאורי אור", "מערב", "שקיעת האור", "מסתלק מאור עינים", "שקיעת האור במערב", "כל בני הדור", "בני בית", "שם הקדש", "פאר והדור", "הארבעה יסודות", "\"משם יפרד והיה לארבעה ראשים\""], "node_degrees": {"תלת גונין דעינא ובת עין": 6, "שבת": 5, "ראש בית": 5, "ראש": 4, "מאורי אור": 3, "פאר": 3, "מחין": 3, "הצדיק": 2, "כח הראות": 2, "שמא דקדשא בריך הוא": 2, "כלליות הגונין": 2, "בעל הבית של העולם": 2, "צדיק האמת": 2, "היפי והפאר של העולם": 2, "תפלין": 2, "מאור העינים": 2, "בית": 2, "בית המקדש": 2, "צדיק יסוד עולם": 2, "שם ה": 1, "יוסף": 1, "נח": 1, "הגונין המאירין במקדש": 1, "המחין": 1, "צדיק": 1, "עינים": 1, "בתים של התפלין": 1, "דירה": 1, "יסוד הפשוט": 1, "נהר יוצא מעדן": 1, "בכיה": 1, "הסתלקות מאורי אור": 1, "מערב": 1, "שקיעת האור": 1, "מסתלק מאור עינים": 1, "שקיעת האור במערב": 1, "כל בני הדור": 1, "בני בית": 1, "שם הקדש": 1, "פאר והדור": 1, "הארבעה יסודות": 1, "\"משם יפרד והיה לארבעה ראשים\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בית", "בית המקדש", "עינים", "בתים של התפלין", "דירה"], "reason": "These five nodes form a structural cluster around the Temple/House. The Torah explicitly equates בית המקדש with עינים (eyes); the בתים של התפלין are where the מחין dwell; the דירה is the dwelling-place produced by Shabbat. All are expressions of the same essential reality as the illuminated house that the Tzaddik-head sustains — same shared nature as the main cluster, not a causal relationship. Bechina: the house IS the eyes, the eyes IS the Temple."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יסוד הפשוט", "צדיק יסוד עולם", "נהר יוצא מעדן"], "reason": "These three are all bechina-expressions of the Tzaddik as the primordial simple foundation from which all four elements flow. The Torah says: 'נהר יוצא מעדן — זה בחינת צדיק יסוד עולם, שהוא בחינת יסוד הפשוט.' They share the same essential nature as the צדיק האמת / ראש בית nodes in the main cluster — all are facets of the Tzaddik's foundational role. Merged because they are bechina (same nature) with the Tzaddik-cluster, not eitza to it."}, {"merged_nodes": ["בכיה", "הסתלקות מאורי אור"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly states: 'הבכיה הוא בחינת הסתלקות מאורי אור' — crying IS the withdrawal of the lights of illumination. These are two aspects of the same reality. Thematically, this pair describes the *absence* of holy light (the negative face of the מאורי אור / תלת גונין cluster). Bechina: crying and light-withdrawal are not cause-and-effect but two expressions of the same inner reality of exile and concealment."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מערב", "שקיעת האור"], "reason": "West = setting of light. The Torah states: 'וזה בחינת שקיעת האור במערב' — the west IS the setting of light. These are two facets of the same geographic-spiritual reality. The West is the direction where the Shechina weeps (= מאור העינים departs), connecting this pair to the central eye-light theme of Package 1. Bechina: the west shares the same essential nature as the setting of divine light."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מסתלק מאור עינים", "שקיעת האור במערב"], "reason": "The Torah states: 'מסתלק מאור עינים — זה בחינת שקיעת האור במערב' — the withdrawal of the eye-light IS the setting of light in the west. These are two descriptions of the same withdrawal phenomenon, belonging to the same holy-light cluster as the absence-thereof."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כל בני הדור", "בני בית"], "reason": "When the Tzaddik (ראש בית) is present, the entire generation are 'children of the house.' The Torah states: 'כולנו, דהיינו כל בני הדור, כולם בחינת בני בית.' These two nodes are bechina-equivalent: all of the generation share the same essential nature as members of the Tzaddik-household. Merged into Package 1 since the concept only makes sense within the ראש בית / holy-Tzaddik reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שם הקדש", "פאר והדור"], "reason": "The Torah identifies Shem (שם, Noah's son) as שם הקדש = פאר והדור = the primary adornment. The holy name and the adornment-splendor are bechina-equivalent. These belong to the main cluster's שם ה / פאר / Tzaddik-name sub-theme: the same essential reality of the divine name embodied in the Tzaddik's fame."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הארבעה יסודות", "\"משם יפרד והיה לארבעה ראשים\""], "reason": "The Torah equates: 'ומשם יפרד והיה לארבעה ראשים — הינו בחינת הארבעה יסודות, שנמשכין מן הצדיק.' The scriptural phrase IS the four elements — both are expressions of the same structural reality of how multiplicity flows from the Tzaddik-unity. Bechina: the verse-phrase and the concept share the same nature. Merged into Package 1 as part of the Tzaddik-as-foundation sub-cluster."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 67, "label": "אַהֲבָה וְאַבְרָהָם", "label_en": "Love and Abraham", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Three-node component: אַבְרָהָם, אַהֲבָה, and יַד יָמִין are all bechina of each other. Abraham is the bechina of love (אַהֲבָה), which is the bechina of the right hand (יַד יָמִין). These three share a single essential nature: the quality of חֶסֶד — outpouring, expansive, nurturing love — as the power that grows and raises the new soul after birth. In Torah #67 section 7, after the soul is born through fear (יִרְאָה), it is raised and grown through love — 'מְגַדְּלִין אוֹתָהּ עַל־יְדֵי הָאַהֲבָה, בְּחִינַת אַבְרָהָם... בְּחִינַת יַד יָמִין.' All three are the same essential force — chesed/ahavah — viewed from different angles (patriarchal archetype, emotional quality, physical metaphor of the outstretched right hand). Merged from raw component C2 which already has 3 nodes; no additional merges needed.", "dominant_node": "אהבה", "nodes": ["אברהם", "אהבה", "יד ימין"], "node_degrees": {"אברהם": 1, "אהבה": 2, "יד ימין": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 67, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מְאוֹרֵי הָאֵשׁ וְהַחֻרְבָּן", "label_en": "Lights of Fire and Destruction", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes all share the essential nature of destructive fire-energy that is the direct opposite of the holy light (מאורי אור). The Torah states: 'יש מאורי אור ומאורי אש והם זה לעומת זה.' The ash (אפר) IS the fire-lights in their aftermath-form; the fire-lights ARE the burning of the Temple. They are three faces of the same destructive cosmic force that erupts when holy illumination withdraws. The Torah explicitly equates אפר with מאורי אש ('אפר — זה בחינת מאורי אש') and מאורי אש with שרפת בית המקדש. Good/evil mirror: this package is the exact negative mirror of the מאורי אור cluster in Package 1.", "dominant_node": "מאורי אש", "nodes": ["אפר", "מאורי אש", "שרפת בית המקדש"], "node_degrees": {"מאורי אש": 2, "אפר": 1, "שרפת בית המקדש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 67, "label": "צְדָקָה וְחֶסֶד", "label_en": "Charity and Lovingkindness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Three-node component: צְדָקָה (charity/righteousness), חֲמִשָּׁה חֲסָדִים (five acts of lovingkindness), and חֶסֶד (lovingkindness). The Torah #67 text (section 5) states explicitly: 'צְדָקָה הִיא בְחִינַת חֲמִשָּׁה חֲסָדִים, כִּי הַצְּדָקָה הִיא בְחִינַת חֶסֶד.' These three concepts share the essential nature of generous, outflowing divine grace that rescues the holy kingdom from the fallen state. Charity is the operative instrument by which the letter ה is added to צֶדֶק (justice) to form צְדָקָה — meaning the five hasadim (corresponding to the ה = five) transform bare justice into redemptive giving. This cluster is the 'rescue force' that lifts the Malkhut from exile. Note the relationship of this package to Package 2 (Love/Abraham): the two are distinct at the level of bechina — Package 2 captures the essential nature of love as the raising/nurturing force (right hand, Abraham, growth), while Package 3 captures charity as the specific redemptive instrument of the five hasadim that elevates the fallen kingdom from the sitra achra. They are parallel aspects of the chesed domain but each is a distinct essential nature in this Torah's argument.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "חמשה חסדים", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 2, "חמשה חסדים": 1, "חסד": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 67, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רֹאשׁ הַסִּטְרָא אָחֳרָא — הַמְפֻרְסָמִים שֶׁל שֶׁקֶר", "label_en": "Head of the Other Side — the False Famous Ones", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes are all expressions of the evil counterfeit of the holy Tzaddik-head (ראש בית). The Torah states: 'ראש של הסטרא אחרא — בחינת מפרסמים של שקר, בחינת שמות הטומאה של החיצונים.' They are three aspects of the same evil reality: the false famous ones who propagate the impure names of the sitra achra in place of God's name. Their essential nature is counterfeit leadership and impure naming. Good/evil mirror: this package directly mirrors Package 1's ראש בית / שם ה sub-cluster — same structure of 'a head whose name fills the world,' but aligned with evil.", "dominant_node": "מפרסמים של שקר", "nodes": ["ראש של הסטרא אחרא", "מפרסמים של שקר", "שמות הטמאה של החיצונים"], "node_degrees": {"מפרסמים של שקר": 2, "ראש של הסטרא אחרא": 1, "שמות הטמאה של החיצונים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 67, "label": "עַזֵּי פָּנִים וּמַלְכוּת הָרְשָׁעָה", "label_en": "The Brazen-Faced and the Evil Kingdom", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Core component: עַזֵּי פָּנִים (the insolent/brazen-faced) ↔ כְּלָבִים עַזֵּי נֶפֶשׁ (the dogs with brazen souls). These two are bechina of each other, both expressing the essential nature of impudent leadership that seizes honor when the holy Malkhut falls. In Torah #67 section 3, the Rebbe teaches: the fallen person who lacks kavod is like 'מַלְכוּתָא בְּלָא תָגָא' (a kingdom without a crown). The brazen-faced are the human embodiment of the evil kingdom that fills the vacuum when the table-of-holiness fails. They are also called by the verse 'פְּנֵי הַדּוֹר כִּפְנֵי הַכֶּלֶב' (the face of the generation is like the face of a dog, Sota 49b). Two small 2-node components are merged here: (a) שִׁכְחָה↔עֲנָנִין דִּמְכַסְּיָן עַל עֵינִין — forgetting/clouds-covering-the-eyes is the mechanism by which the wisdom of sages is hidden, which is what gives the brazen ones their power; the essential nature is 'the darkness that hides the holy light' and belongs in the evil domain alongside the brazen ones; (b) מוֹתָרוֹת שֶׁבַּמֹּחַ↔בִּינָה יְתֵרָה — the surplus brain matter from which the enemies suckle belongs in the evil domain because it is the material from which the שְׂעָרוֹת (hairs) grow, and those hairs are the channels through which enemies and adversaries draw their nourishment (Torah #67 section 6: 'מֵהֶם יוֹנְקִים הָאוֹיְבִים'). The essential nature of the excess-brain/binah-yeteira pair is 'the nutriment of the adversaries' — squarely in the evil domain as the food of the opposing forces.", "dominant_node": "עזי פנים", "nodes": ["עזי פנים", "כלבים עזי נפש", "שכחה", "עננין דמכסין על עינין", "מותרות שבמח", "בינה יתרה"], "node_degrees": {"עזי פנים": 1, "כלבים עזי נפש": 1, "שכחה": 1, "עננין דמכסין על עינין": 1, "מותרות שבמח": 1, "בינה יתרה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שכחה", "עננין דמכסין על עינין"], "reason": "Forgetting is the bechina of clouds-that-cover-the-eyes ('הַשִּׁכְחָה הוּא בְחִינַת עֲנָנִין דִּמְכַסְּיָן עַל עֵינִין'). When the elder forgets his learning, it is as if clouds have covered his inner sight. This is the mechanism by which holy wisdom disappears from the world, creating the void that the brazen ones exploit. The essential nature of both concepts is 'spiritual darkness / concealment of wisdom' — the evil-side condition that enables the brazen-faced to dominate. It belongs with the evil-domain cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מותרות שבמח", "בינה יתרה"], "reason": "'מוֹתָרוֹת שֶׁבַּמֹּחַ' = the surplus/excess of the brain matter = the 'בִּינָה יְתֵרָה' given to woman (Nidda 45b, quoted at Torah #67's opening). The Torah explains that from these brain excesses grow the שְׂעָרוֹת (hairs), and from those hairs the enemies (אוֹיְבִים, שׂוֹנְאִים) suckle their nourishment. This pair is therefore the root nutriment of the adversarial forces — it is an evil-domain concept in this Torah, not a neutral or positive one. It belongs alongside the brazen-ones and the forgetting/concealment cluster as part of the domain of 'forces that feed on holiness when it is mismanaged.'"}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 67, "label": "יִלֵּד וְגִדּוּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ", "label_en": "Birth-Difficulty and the Soul's Growth", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Three-node component: קִשּׁוּי לֵילֵד (difficulty in childbirth), גִּדּוּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ (raising/growing the soul), and 'צָרָה כְּמַבְכִּירָה' (anguish like a woman in first labor). These three share the essential domain of the birth-pain / growth-pain nexus. In Torah #67 section 7, the Rebbe states: 'קִשּׁוּי לֵילֵד, וְגַם הוּא בְחִינַת גִּדּוּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ, בְּחִינַת (זְכַרְיָה י׳׳ב): ׳כְּהָמִיר עַל הַבְּכוֹר׳, שֶׁהוּא צַעַר הַגִּדּוּל.' And 'צָרָה כְּמַבְכִּירָה' = the bechina of birth-difficulty. These three are all the same essential reality: the pain of bringing a new soul into the world and raising it. The polarity is neutral because the birth-pain is a structural feature of the spiritual process — it is not inherently evil (it is the resistance the soul meets when entering the world) nor is it simply 'good' (it is a form of suffering). It is the neutral force of the creative threshold — the birthpang that accompanies all significant spiritual emergence.", "dominant_node": "קשוי לילד", "nodes": ["קשוי לילד", "גדול הנפש", "\"צרה כמבכירה\""], "node_degrees": {"קשוי לילד": 2, "גדול הנפש": 1, "\"צרה כמבכירה\"": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 68, "label": "מָמוֹן וְשֶׁפַע קֹדֶשׁ", "label_en": "Wealth and Holy Overflow", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all nodes in Torah #68 that belong to the holy/positive dimension of wealth and material blessing. Component A (ממון ↔ חומה) identifies money as a protective wall — a spiritual fortification of the person. Component C (התחלת המקום שמשתלשל משם הממון ↔ קדושה ושפע קדש) identifies the supernal source from which money descends as a place of holiness and divine overflow. Both pairs share the same essential bechina nature: they describe the positive, sacred dimension of ממון. The origin-source (קדושה ושפע קדש) is the root; the terrestrial expression (חומה) is the fruit — different aspects of the same holy reality. The Torah's argument in §2 establishes that the soul and money come from the same supernal place, and §8 establishes that wealth, while it descends, manifests as a protective wall. These are two faces of one coin: wealth as holy inheritance flowing from the divine overflow and protecting the one who receives it.", "dominant_node": "ממון", "nodes": ["ממון", "חומה", "התחלת המקום שמשתלשל משם הממון", "קדשה ושפע קדש"], "node_degrees": {"ממון": 1, "חומה": 1, "התחלת המקום שמשתלשל משם הממון": 1, "קדשה ושפע קדש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_components": ["Component A: {ממון, חומה}", "Component C: {התחלת המקום שמשתלשל משם הממון, קדשה ושפע קדש}"], "reason": "Both components describe the same domain: the holy/positive face of ממון. Component C is the supernal root (money originates from a place of holiness and shefa). Component A is the earthly manifestation (money, once descended, functions as a protective wall). The Torah explicitly chains these: 'the beginning-place from which money descends is a bechina of holiness and shefa' (§2) and 'money and wealth is a bechina of a wall' (§8). These two bechina statements trace the same substance — holy divine overflow — through its descent: from sacred origin-point to protective wall. Merging on shared nature: both are expressions of ממון from the side of holiness."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 68, "label": "כַּעַס וְרָעָה", "label_en": "Anger and Evil", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package contains the evil mirror of Package 1. Edge 1517 states: 'from the north evil will open — that is, the bechina of anger.' Anger (כעס) and evil (רעה) are declared one bechina in Torah #68 via Yirmiyah 1:14 (from the north evil will open) cross-read with Kohelet 11:10 (remove anger from your heart and eliminate evil from your flesh). Both the evil that 'opens from the north' and the anger that must be removed are the SAME concept. This pair describes the dark inversion of the holy wealth-flow: where Package 1 shows the Tzafon as a source of holy gold (Iyov 37: 'from the north gold comes'), Package 2 shows the same Tzafon as the source of evil/anger (Yirmiyah 1). Both north-flows descend from Gevurot — the gold-from-north becomes holy ממון (Package 1); the evil-from-north becomes כעס that destroys the wall. The evil mirror is structurally exact: where ממון builds the חומה, כעס tears it down; where קדושה ושפע קדש is the holy root, רעה is the evil root.", "dominant_node": "כעס", "nodes": ["כעס", "רעה"], "node_degrees": {"כעס": 1, "רעה": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 69, "label": "מָמוֹן וְעֲנָפִים", "label_en": "Wealth and Branches", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #69 centers on ממון (wealth) as the core concept. The Torah teaches that money is not a material object but rather the manifestation — the 'shining-forth and spreading' (התנוצצות והתפשטות) — of the wife's soul-light. All nodes in this cluster share the same essential nature: they are different expressions of how the wife's soul-light extends outward as material wealth. The 'branches of the tree' (ענפים) = the soul-light's radiance = ממון. The 'wall' (חומה) = ממון (from Proverbs 18:11 'hon ashir kiryat uzo u'ke-chomah nisgavah'). The left hand (שמאל) = ממון (from Proverbs 3:16 'bismoalah osher ve-khavod' — wealth is on the left side of Wisdom). The tree-root (עקר האילן) = the wife's soul-light (אור נפשה) — explicitly: 'אוֹר נַפְשָׁהּ הוּא בְּחִינַת עִקַּר הָאִילָן'; this pair (originally isolated component D) is merged here because the tree-root's essential nature in this Torah is the SOURCE of the wealth-branches, placing it squarely in the tree/wealth metaphor domain rather than in the abstract soul/feminine domain. Tzedaka (צדקה) = שלח ('שַׁלַּח לַחְמְךָ עַל פְּנֵי הַמָּיִם') — giving forth/releasing wealth is the bechina of Kohelet's 'send forth.' This pair (originally isolated component H) is merged here because both concepts express the same essential nature: the voluntary release/transmission of wealth as a holy act that mirrors the soul-light's natural outflow. Polarity is neutral: ממון itself is not good or evil — the same soul-light framework describes both legitimate wealth (a gift from the wife's soul) and stolen wealth (a grasping of the neighbor's soul-light). The left-hand/שמאל designation is structural, not pejorative.", "dominant_node": "ממון", "nodes": ["ממון", "התנוצצות והתפשטות אורה", "ענפים", "ענפים של האילן", "חומה", "שמאל", "אור נפשה", "עקר האילן", "צדקה", "שלח"], "node_degrees": {"ממון": 4, "התנוצצות והתפשטות אורה": 2, "ענפים": 1, "ענפים של האילן": 1, "חומה": 1, "שמאל": 1, "אור נפשה": 1, "עקר האילן": 1, "צדקה": 1, "שלח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "D", "merged_nodes": ["אור נפשה", "עקר האילן"], "reason": "The pair 'wife's soul-light = root of the tree' is the hinge of the entire tree metaphor. In this Torah the soul-light is described exclusively in its role as the generative source that produces branches (ממון). The bechina declared is אור נפשה = עקר האילן; this root's NATURE in Torah #69 is 'the source from which wealth-branches emerge' — it belongs with the wealth/tree cluster, not with the abstract soul-ontology cluster (Package 2)."}, {"merged_component": "H", "merged_nodes": ["צדקה", "שלח"], "reason": "Tzedakah is equated to 'שַׁלַּח' (the act of sending forth, from Kohelet 11:1). The shared nature is: voluntarily releasing wealth is the same act as the natural outflow of soul-light. This is the positive counterpart to gzeilah — where theft takes the soul-light by force, tzedakah sends it forth willingly. Both concepts belong in the wealth-domain cluster because their essential nature is about the correct vs. incorrect relationship to ממון."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 69, "volume": "lm2", "label": "בְּרָכָה וְנִשֵּׂאת וְנוֹשֵׂאת", "label_en": "Blessing and the Carried-that-Carries", "polarity": "good", "reason": "ברכה (blessing) and נשאת ונושאת (carried-and-carries) are two expressions of the same essential reality. The Torah derives the word ברכה as an acronym for 'כִּי רוּחַ הַחַיָּה בָּאוֹפַנִּים' (Ezekiel 1:20), which is precisely the principle of נשאת ונושאת — the quality of simultaneously bearing and being borne, as embodied by the Merkavah. The blessing given to the drink-carrier IS the נשאת ונושאת quality: he carries the drink, and the drink in turn elevates him. These two nodes are not cause-and-effect — they are two names for the same spiritual principle of mutual elevation.", "dominant_node": "ברכה", "nodes": ["ברכה", "נשאת ונושאת"], "node_degrees": {"ברכה": 1, "נשאת ונושאת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 69, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ וּנְקֵבָה", "label_en": "Soul and the Feminine", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The second-largest connected component groups all nodes that describe the essential nature of the feminine/receptive dimension of the soul. The core insight: the nefesh (lowest soul-level) IS the feminine (nekeivah), because the lowest level of any hierarchy is — relative to the level above it — in the position of receiver/feminine. This yields: נפש = נקבה = רגלים (feet — the lowest part of the body, from Zohar Vayera: 'raglei chasidav yishmor — da ita'). The bat-zug (wife/soul-mate) = רגלין because she IS the nefesh-aspect of her husband. The entire lower-level hierarchy principle is captured in: 'כְּלַל הַמַּדְרֵגָה הַתַּחְתּוֹנָה הִיא בְחִינַת נְקֵבָה נֶגֶד מַדְרֵגָה שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה הֵימֶנָּה.' The isolated pair (component C) — 'כְּלַל נֶפֶשׁ רוּחַ וּנְשָׁמָה שֶׁלָּהּ' = 'נֶפֶשׁ נֶגְדּוֹ' — is merged here because it states the same principle at the meta-level: even the wife's ENTIRE nefesh-ruach-neshamah is considered 'nefesh' relative to her husband. This is a restatement of the fundamental nefesh=feminine principle and belongs in this package. Polarity is neutral: the nefesh/nekeivah/raglayim structure is ontological — it describes the soul's architecture. The same dimension can be in holiness (receiving from her husband, building children) or in captivity among the klipot (her feet descend to death).", "dominant_node": "נקבה", "nodes": ["נקבה", "נפש", "כלל המדרגה התחתונה", "רגלים", "בת זוגו", "כלל נפש רוח ונשמה שלה", "נפש נגדו"], "node_degrees": {"נקבה": 4, "נפש": 2, "כלל המדרגה התחתונה": 1, "רגלים": 2, "בת זוגו": 1, "כלל נפש רוח ונשמה שלה": 1, "נפש נגדו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "C", "merged_nodes": ["כלל נפש רוח ונשמה שלה", "נפש נגדו"], "reason": "This isolated pair states the ontological principle at the collective level: the wife's entire three-part soul (nefesh-ruach-neshamah) is considered 'nefesh' relative to the husband's higher standing. This is a direct extension of the nefesh=nekeivah principle already in Component B. The shared nature is identical: 'lowest/receptive dimension = nefesh = feminine.' Merges into Package 2."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 69, "label": "בָּנִים וְתוֹרָה", "label_en": "Children and Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups the fruit/children domain together with the Torah/right-hand domain. The core bechinas: בנים (children) = הפרות הגדלים בענפים האלו (the fruits growing on the tree's branches) = למודי ה' (Torah-students — from Isaiah 54: 'וְכָל בָּנַיִךְ לִמּוּדֵי ה''). Children are not merely biological offspring — they are Torah-students by essential nature. The isolated pair תורה = ימין (Torah is the right side, from Deuteronomy 33: 'מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ') is merged here because its shared nature is the same domain: Torah as the holy right-hand path that produces worthy children. In the Torah's argument: the wise man (chakham) marries for the sake of Torah (=ימין), and this produces worthy/fitting children — because learning Torah in purity = the proper intent for marriage. By contrast, the fool marries for ממון (=שמאל/left) and produces unfit children. The Torah=ימין pair belongs with the children=Torah-students cluster because both describe the positive goal of holy marriage: producing children who are Torah-students, achieved by orienting toward Torah (ימין) rather than money (שמאל). Polarity is good: all nodes here belong to the positive/holy domain — children as limudei Hashem, Torah as the right hand of God.", "dominant_node": "בנים", "nodes": ["בנים", "הפרות הגדלים בענפים האלו", "למודי ה'", "תורה", "ימין"], "node_degrees": {"בנים": 2, "הפרות הגדלים בענפים האלו": 1, "למודי ה'": 1, "תורה": 1, "ימין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "F", "merged_nodes": ["תורה", "ימין"], "reason": "Torah = ימין (the right hand/side) — this is the positive-orientation principle in Torah #69. In the teaching, the chakham who marries l'shem Torah orients to ימין and produces children who are limudei Hashem. The shared nature of תורה and ימין is 'the holy/right-hand path,' which is the same essential domain as בנים=למודי ה'. The Torah-right pair and the children-Torah-students pair both describe the same domain: holiness, learning, and righteous offspring as the proper goal of marriage. Merges into Package 3."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 69, "label": "שְׂעָרוֹת וּמֹחִין", "label_en": "Hairs and the Brain", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes belonging to the domain of brain-overflow (מותרי מחין) and its physical/spiritual manifestations. The core: מותרי מחין (surplus/overflow of the brain) = שערות (hairs, which are the physical manifestation of brain-overflow in kabbalistic anatomy) = מותרות (excesses). The tefillin-straps (רצועות) = מותרי מחין, because the straps are the 'overflow' portion of the tefillin, extending beyond the head-box. The brain (מחין) itself = תפלין (tefillin head-box), a well-established Lurianic equation. Two small isolated pairs are merged in: (1) מחין=תפלין (component G2) — shares the same essential nature as the whole cluster (brain anatomy expressed through tefillin). (2) שערות של החכם = שַׁעַר בְּיָמִין (component G3) — the wise man's hairs are not mere overflow but 'gates' (שערים) on the right side, through which he opens the gates of wisdom for those who need him to lower himself to their level. This is the POSITIVE pole of the שערות domain. Polarity is neutral because this cluster contains both poles: the wise man's שערות are holy gates (שַׁעַר בְּיָמִין), while the fool's שערות are empty sinah-chinam coming from brain-overflow that exceeds the brain's capacity. The same ontological structure (motarei mochin = hair) produces radically different outcomes depending on whether the person is a chakham or a kesil. This dual-pole structure within one cluster is marked as neutral.", "dominant_node": "שערות", "nodes": ["שערות", "מותרי מחין", "מותרות", "רצועה", "מחין", "תפלין", "שערות של החכם", "שער בימין"], "node_degrees": {"שערות": 2, "מותרי מחין": 2, "מותרות": 1, "רצועה": 1, "מחין": 1, "תפלין": 1, "שערות של החכם": 1, "שער בימין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "G2", "merged_nodes": ["מחין", "תפלין"], "reason": "The brain (מחין) = tefillin (the head-box) — a foundational Lurianic equation cited here as: 'מֹחִין, בְּחִינַת תְּפִלִּין, בְּחִינַת מֶלֶךְ אָסוּר בָּרְהָטִים, בְּרַהֲטֵי מֹחִין.' This pair shares the essential domain of the שערות/מותרי מחין cluster: both are about the brain and its kabbalistic-anatomical expression through tefillin. מחין is the inner substance; מותרי מחין is the overflow of that same substance; and תפלין is the structural container. All three are the same essential domain."}, {"merged_component": "G3", "merged_nodes": ["שערות של החכם", "שער בימין"], "reason": "The wise man's hairs (שערות של החכם) = a gate on the right (שַׁעַר בְּיָמִין) — explicitly: 'שְֹּעָרוֹת שֶׁל הֶחָכָם, הֵם בְחִינַת שַׁעַר בְּיָמִין.' This is the positive-pole expression of the שערות domain: the same מותרי מחין that produces sinah chinam in the fool becomes, in the wise man, 'gates' through which he humbles himself to bring people closer to God. The shared nature is identical — motarei mochin / שערות — but in the wise man it opens rather than closes. Merges into Package 4."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 70, "label": "צַדִּיק וְעָפָר וְכֹחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik, the Dust, and the Drawing Force", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #70 builds a single continuous bechina-chain: the tzaddik (צדיק) is an aspect of dust/earth (עפר), dust is an aspect of the drawing force (כח המושך), and the Mishkan (משכן) is an aspect of the drawing force. The entire teaching is a meditation on one essential nature seen from four angles: the humble person who makes himself like dust acquires the gravitational quality of earth — the capacity to draw everything toward himself. This is the same quality that earth has (it pulls all things downward), that the Tzaddik has (he draws all people toward holiness), and that the Mishkan had (it drew divine presence to wherever it stood). These four nodes are not cause-and-effect; they are parallel expressions of the same underlying reality: lowliness-as-gravitational-holiness. The Torah begins with physics (earth's gravity) and ends with history (Nissan as new year for kings), all unified through this one bechina-chain.", "dominant_node": "כח המושך", "nodes": ["צדיק", "עפר", "כח המושך", "משכן"], "node_degrees": {"כח המושך": 2, "עפר": 2, "צדיק": 1, "משכן": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 71, "label": "מְפֻרְסָם וְיִסּוּרִין בִּשְׁבִיל רַבִּים", "label_en": "The Famous One and Suffering on Behalf of the Many", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #71 teaches that public prominence (מְפֻרְסָם) carries an essential nature: the famous one becomes an aspect of Isaiah 53:12 — 'therefore I will divide him among the many' — and therefore must bear the quality of Isaiah 53:5 — 'by his stripes we are healed.' The famous tzaddik, the afflictions he suffers, the masses he heals, and the verse-formula of the Suffering Servant are all parallel expressions of one essential reality: public holiness requires absorbing the pain of others. The Rebbe says explicitly 'הוּא בִּבְחִינוֹת: לָכֵן אֲחַלֶּק לוֹ בָרַבִּים' — the famous person IS in the aspect of this verse, and 'עָלָיו נֶאֱמַר: וּבַחֲבֻרָתוֹ נִרְפָּא לָנוּ' — of him it is said, by his wounds we are healed. These are bechina-declarations (בְּחִינוֹת / נֶאֱמַר) linking the concept of public prominence to the entire Isaiah-53 theology of redemptive suffering. Paragraph 2 extends this to tzaddikim who voluntarily accept suffering to exchange the divine flow (שֶׁפַע), which is the same essential nature seen from a different angle: the tzaddik who absorbs communal suffering and thereby transforms the quality of the divine outpouring. All concepts belong to one unified domain — the structural reality of the tzaddik who bears the suffering of the many.", "dominant_node": "מְפֻרְסָם", "nodes": ["מְפֻרְסָם", "יִסּוּרִין בִּשְׁבִיל רַבִּים", "\"לָכֵן אֲחַלֶּק לוֹ בָרַבִּים\"", "\"וּבַחֲבֻרָתוֹ נִרְפָּא לָנוּ\"", "מְקַבְּלִים יִסּוּרִים בִּשְׁבִיל יִשְׂרָאֵל", "מַחֲלִיפִין הַשֶּׁפַע"], "node_degrees": {"מְפֻרְסָם": 2, "יִסּוּרִין בִּשְׁבִיל רַבִּים": 2, "\"לָכֵן אֲחַלֶּק לוֹ בָרַבִּים\"": 1, "\"וּבַחֲבֻרָתוֹ נִרְפָּא לָנוּ\"": 1, "מְקַבְּלִים יִסּוּרִים בִּשְׁבִיל יִשְׂרָאֵל": 1, "מַחֲלִיפִין הַשֶּׁפַע": 1}, "note_on_degrees": "Degrees are inferred from source-text bechina structure, not from torahData.js edges (which are absent for reference===71). מְפֻרְסָם holds the highest degree because it is the subject of both the בְּחִינוֹת declaration (linked to Isaiah 53:12) and the נֶאֱמַר formula (linked to Isaiah 53:5). יִסּוּרִין בִּשְׁבִיל רַבִּים bridges Paragraphs 1 and 2 — it is mentioned in both as the mechanism connecting the public tzaddik to the healing of the many, and as the voluntary act of the tzaddikim in Paragraph 2.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 71, "volume": "lm2", "label": "נֹעַם וּמוֹחִין שֶׁל אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "Pleasantness and the Minds of the Land of Israel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these nodes share the essential nature of נועם — the quality of harmonious, pleasant, luminous consciousness rooted in Eretz Yisrael. יוסף, described as יפה תואר (beautiful of appearance), is identified with נועם because 'תואר — שמראה פנים בהלכה', i.e., showing the pleasant/illuminating face of Torah. שפרה (beauty/pleasantness) links to שפיר ושליא (the afterbirth/vessel of birth) and to הולדה (procreation itself), all as expressions of the beautiful, nourishing, generative quality that flows from נועם. The 'loving flames' (שלהובין דרחימותא) and אהבה are the experiential form that נועם takes when it awakens the heart. מחין and מוחין של ארץ ישראל are all aspects of the same conscious luminosity. The good mirror of the חובלים package: where those minds are discordant and wounding, these minds are harmonious, pleasant, and life-generating.", "dominant_node": "נועם", "nodes": ["נועם", "מחין של ארץ ישראל", "יוסף", "שמנעימים בהלכה", "יפה תאר", "מחין", "שפרה", "שפיר ושליא", "הולדה", "אהבה", "שלהובין דרחימותא", "מוחין", "מוחין של ארץ ישראל"], "node_degrees": {"נועם": 4, "מחין של ארץ ישראל": 1, "יוסף": 2, "שמנעימים בהלכה": 2, "יפה תאר": 2, "מחין": 1, "שפרה": 4, "שפיר ושליא": 1, "הולדה": 1, "אהבה": 1, "שלהובין דרחימותא": 1, "מוחין": 1, "מוחין של ארץ ישראל": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אהבה", "שלהובין דרחימותא"], "reason": "This isolated pair (raw component D) was a two-node component. אהבה is a bechina of שלהובין דרחימותא (flames of desire). Both are the experiential quality of נועם as it arouses the heart — they are not a separate domain but the form נועם takes in the heart. The proof text reads: 'על ידי נועם העליון מתעורר האהבה, בחינת שלהובין דרחימותא'. They belong to Package 1 as the affective/inner dimension of נועם העליון."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מוחין", "מוחין של ארץ ישראל"], "reason": "This isolated pair (raw component E) records the equation 'כל המוחין הם בחינת מוחין של ארץ ישראל' from the opening of the Torah. מוחין and מחין are alternate spellings of the same word (minds/intellects). מוחין של ארץ ישראל and מחין של ארץ ישראל are the same concept. These nodes are aliases of nodes already in the main נועם component (component A), and the bechina they express — that all minds draw from the Eretz Yisrael source — is the same as edges 3 and 16 in that component. They belong in Package 1."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 71, "volume": "lm2", "label": "צְדָקָה וְיַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "Charity and Jacob", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of נדיבות לב — the quality of generous, open-hearted giving. יעקב is a bechina of צדקה (as written: 'משפט וצדקה ביעקב אתה עשית'). נחלת (inheritance/portion) is equated with יעקב via 'יעקב חבל נחלתו'. תרומה (the offering/contribution) is equated with צדקה because it is the paradigm of נדיבות לב: 'מאת כל איש אשר ידבנו לבו תקחו את תרומתי'. נדיבות לב and שנפתח ונתנדב הלב are two aspects of the same quality: the open, giving heart. All these nodes represent the vessel-making dimension of the Torah — the capacity to receive through generosity. צדקה is the instrument that creates the כלי (vessel) to receive נועם העליון, but the nodes within this package share the same NATURE (giving, open-heartedness, the Jacob-energy of generosity), even though צדקה's FUNCTION is to produce results (that is eitza, connecting this package to Package 1).", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "יעקב", "נחלת", "תרומה", "נדיבות לב", "שנפתח ונתנדב הלב"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 3, "יעקב": 2, "נחלת": 2, "תרומה": 2, "נדיבות לב": 2, "שנפתח ונתנדב הלב": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 71, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חוֹבְלִים וּמוֹחִין שֶׁל חוּץ לָאָרֶץ", "label_en": "Damaging Forces and Exile Minds", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes share the essential nature of חובלים — the quality of discordant, wounding, conflicting consciousness that results from פגם הכבוד (desecration of God's honor). מוחין של חוץ לארץ are called חובלים because they cannot harmonize with each other (the Gemara in Sanhedrin: 'חובלים — אלו תלמידי חכמים שבחוץ לארץ, שחובלים זה לזה בהלכה'). חבלי לדה (birth pangs) are identified as a bechina of חובלים because they represent the suffering/difficulty side of the birth process — the painful, convulsive dimension that needs to be repaired through נועם. This is the evil mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 is נועם/harmony/pleasant-minds, Package 3 is חובלים/discord/wounding-minds. The repair of these minds is the Torah's practical teaching.", "dominant_node": "חובלים", "nodes": ["חובלים", "ומחין של חוץ־לארץ", "חבלי לדה"], "node_degrees": {"חובלים": 2, "ומחין של חוץ־לארץ": 1, "חבלי לדה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 72, "label": "יֵצֶר הָרָע", "label_en": "The Evil Inclination — Its Levels and Root", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #72 is entirely devoted to a single domain: the taxonomy of the evil inclination across its multiple levels. Three bechina pairs are present in the dataset. (1) נשין שפירין ↔ אלילים — beautiful women are a bechina of idols; hirhurei znut equal hirhurei avoda zara; both are shtut (folly), the lower physical yetzer hara of coarse/turbid blood. (2) גבורות ↔ דינים — the higher yetzer hara of the spiritually advanced person is the bechina of gevurot and dinim, the forces of divine severity that must be sweetened. (3) מדת הדין ↔ יצר הרע — the measure of divine judgment is the root-bechina of the yetzer hara itself, the ultimate source from which all levels descend. All six nodes describe one overarching domain: the yetzer hara as it manifests across the levels of creation, from its highest spiritual root (midas hadin / gevurot) through the intermediate (klipa daka) down to the grossest physical level (turbid blood / shtut / dimyonot). In this Torah these are explicitly presented as a single continuous hierarchy, not distinct separate domains. The dominant node is יצר הרע — it is the concept that connects all three pairs through the teaching's narrative, appearing directly in one edge (מדת הדין ↔ יצר הרע) and serving as the referent of the other two pairs throughout the text.", "dominant_node": "יצר הרע", "nodes": ["יצר הרע", "מדת הדין", "גבורות", "דין", "נשין שפירין", "אלילים"], "node_degrees": {"יצר הרע": 1, "מדת הדין": 1, "גבורות": 1, "דין": 1, "נשין שפירין": 1, "אלילים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גבורות", "דין"], "reason": "Component B (edge 730, גבורות ↔ דינים) is a 2-node pair describing the upper yetzer hara — the same domain as Component C. The Torah explicitly says: 'הַיְנוּ גְּבוּרוֹת וְדִינִים' and 'שֶׁהֵם הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה'. Gevurot/dinim are the bechina of the upper yetzer hara, which in turn is rooted in midas hadin (Component C). These two pairs share the same essential nature — the upper level of the yetzer hara — and belong together."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נשין שפירין", "אלילים"], "reason": "Component A (edge 277, נשין שפירין ↔ אלילים) describes the lower physical yetzer hara: beautiful women are a bechina of idols because hirhurei znut = hirhurei avoda zara = shtut (Zohar Kedoshim 84). While this is the lower level of the yetzer hara, the Torah explicitly structures it as one unified taxonomy descending from midas hadin (the root) through gevurot/dinim (upper) down to shtut/blood/dimyonot (lower). The shared nature is: these are all aspects of the evil inclination. The Torah's argument is that ALL levels are the same root, so all belong in one package."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 72, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רוּחַ אֱלֹקִים וְהַמַּנְהִיג הָאֱמִתִּי", "label_en": "Divine Spirit and the True Leader", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: the Tzaddik who is the true leader (Moses-archetype) IS the universal intellect (מחין), which IS the divine spirit (רוח אלקים), which IS the spirit of holiness and prophecy, which IS the labor of building the Temple. The Rebbe equates every facet of the Tzaddik-as-leader with divine intellect and spirit: 'הרוח אלקים הוא בעצמו המחין.' The leader's gaze ignites each person's intellect because he himself is the universal mind. דעת and מלכות/גדולה are merged here because the text explicitly identifies דעת = בנין בית המקדש = מלאכת המחין, and גדולה/מלכות is distributed by the Tzaddik through his intellect-gaze. חכם הדור = חכם הכולל = מנהיג אמתי = משה are all synonyms for the same role.", "dominant_node": "רוח אלקים", "nodes": ["מחין", "רוח אלקים", "רוח הכולל", "רוח הקדש", "רוח נבואה", "מלאכת בנין בית המקדש", "והמנהיג האמתי של כל ישראל", "דעת הכולל", "המנהיג האמתי", "מנהיג אמתי", "משה", "חכם הדור", "חכם הכולל", "מלכות", "גדולה", "דעת", "בנין בית המקדש"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 3, "רוח אלקים": 4, "רוח הכולל": 4, "רוח הקדש": 1, "רוח נבואה": 1, "מלאכת בנין בית המקדש": 1, "והמנהיג האמתי של כל ישראל": 2, "דעת הכולל": 2, "המנהיג האמתי": 2, "מנהיג אמתי": 1, "משה": 3, "חכם הדור": 1, "חכם הכולל": 1, "מלכות": 1, "גדולה": 1, "דעת": 1, "בנין בית המקדש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חכם הדור", "חכם הכולל"], "reason": "חכם הדור and חכם הכולל are declared synonyms of the Tzaddik-as-universal-mind (explicitly: 'שהוא חכם הדור, בחינת חכם הכולל'). Their bechina edge connects them to each other; thematically they are another name for the same reality as מנהיג אמתי = משה. Sharing nature with the Tzaddik cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מלכות", "גדולה"], "reason": "מלכות = גדולה by explicit bechina ('בחינת המלכות, דהיינו גדולה'). גדולה in this Torah is the greatness distributed by the Tzaddik through his gaze — it is the output of the Tzaddik's intellect-illumination (מלך אסור ברהטים = מלכות is tied to the sparkling of the mind). The bechina pair stands closest to the Tzaddik package since their content is: the Tzaddik grants גדולה = מלכות through intellect. Not cause-effect with this package — rather, גדולה IS a mode of מלכות which IS the domain of the Tzaddik's distribution."}, {"merged_nodes": ["דעת", "בנין בית המקדש"], "reason": "The text states explicitly: 'כל מי שיש בו דעה, כאלו נבנה בית המקדש בימיו, כי עיקר בחינת בנין בית המקדש הוא הדעת.' דעת IS bechina of בנין בית המקדש. This pair belongs to the same domain as מחין = מלאכת בנין בית המקדש. Though they have different node IDs from מחין, they share the same conceptual identity and thematic home."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 72, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וְחַיִּים נִצְחִיִּים", "label_en": "Teshuvah and Eternal Life", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the same essential nature as expressions of the eternal, boundless life that comes through return to God. תשובה IS חיים (eternal life); חיים = חיים נצחיים; חיים נצחיים של עולם הבא = תשובה = שבת; שבת = יראה והבושה = תשובה; מתנה = שבת; נדיבי העם IS teshuvah. The entire cluster expresses the single reality: returning to God in fear and shame IS eternal life IS the gift of Shabbat IS the ultimate spiritual delight. בושה (shame/awe) and חפרוה are the experiential face of the same teshuvah reality: the verse 'וחפרה הלבנה ובושה החמה' uses חפרוה as bechina of בושה, and בושה is the inner mechanism of teshuvah throughout this Torah.", "dominant_node": "תשובה", "nodes": ["תשובה", "יראה והבושה", "חיים", "חיים נצחיים", "חיים נצחיים של עולם הבא", "שבת", "נדיבי העם", "מתנה", "חפרוה", "בושה"], "node_degrees": {"תשובה": 4, "יראה והבושה": 2, "חיים": 2, "חיים נצחיים": 1, "חיים נצחיים של עולם הבא": 2, "שבת": 3, "נדיבי העם": 1, "מתנה": 1, "חפרוה": 1, "בושה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חפרוה", "בושה"], "reason": "חפרוה IS bechina of בושה, drawn from the verse 'וחפרה הלבנה ובושה החמה' (Isa 24:23). They are two expressions of the same experience of shame/humility-awe. בושה is not connected to תשובה via bechina in the dataset (only via eitza), but the two-node component (חפרוה ↔ בושה) belongs unmistakably to the teshuvah domain: the entire Torah declares that בושה IS the mechanism of תשובה. Importantly, the test is shared NATURE not cause-effect: both חפרוה and בושה express the experience of shame-before-God, which is the same quality as the יראה-והבושה node already in this package."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 72, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שִׁפְלוּת וְאַיִן", "label_en": "Humility and Nothingness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of limitlessness through self-nullification. שפלות ועניוות IS בלתי גבול (without limit) because it IS אין (nothingness). This is the Torah's foundational teaching about humility: only that which has no ego-boundary (אין) can receive the infinite pleasure of the World to Come. מדבר (wilderness) = שפלות because one who makes oneself like a wilderness (without claims) achieves this quality of שפלות. The Torah declares: 'שפלות ועניוות הוא בחינת בלתי גבול, כי הוא בחינת אין ממש.' This whole cluster is about the single reality of true lowliness as infinite openness.", "dominant_node": "שפלות ועניוות", "nodes": ["שפלות ועניוות", "בלתי גבול", "אין", "מדבר", "שפלות"], "node_degrees": {"שפלות ועניוות": 2, "בלתי גבול": 1, "אין": 1, "מדבר": 1, "שפלות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מדבר", "שפלות"], "reason": "מדבר IS bechina of שפלות: 'מדבר היינו שפלות, שמשים עצמו כמדבר.' These two form a two-node bechina pair. Their thematic home is unmistakably the שפלות cluster: מדבר is wilderness-humility, and שפלות is humility — they are both expressions of the same quality of self-nullification. The node שפלות and the node שפלות ועניוות share the same essential nature (שפלות ועניוות is simply a fuller form of the same concept), confirming the merge."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 72, "volume": "lm2", "label": "בְּאֵר וּבֵאוּרֵי הַתּוֹרָה", "label_en": "The Well and Torah Explanations", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The well (באר), the rock (סלע), and Torah explanations (באורי התורה / חידושין דאוריתא) share the essential nature of the vessel and spring from which the Tzaddik's Torah flows to his followers. The text: 'באר זה בחינת באורי התורה... סלע ישכן — זה הבאר.' The three are mutually equated: סלע = באר = באורי התורה, forming a triangle of bechina relationships. These are all symbolic vessels for the same reality: the Torah novelties that flow from the Tzaddik's universal intellect and produce shame and teshuvah in those who receive them.", "dominant_node": "באורי התורה", "nodes": ["באר", "באורי התורה", "סלע"], "node_degrees": {"באר": 2, "באורי התורה": 2, "סלע": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 73, "label": "כְּלִי אֲנִי — אוֹתִיּוֹת הַתְּפִלָּה", "label_en": "The Vessel Ani — The Letters of Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the central conceptual cluster of Torah #73. The Rebbe builds one continuous bechina chain: every Jew is called a tzaddik (נִקְרָא צַדִּיק), the tzaddik is called Aleph (נִקְרָא אָלֶף); the Nun is called speech (נִקְרָא דִּבּוּר), speech IS malkhut (הַדִּבּוּר הוּא מַלְכוּת), malkhut IS called Nun (נִקְרָא נוּן); thought IS called Yud (הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה נִקְרָא יוּד); and the completed vessel IS called Ani (הַכְּלִי הַנִּקְרָא אֲנִי). These are all parallel aspects of the same essential structure: the three letters א-נ-י that form the word 'Ani' (I), each letter encoding a faculty of the person in prayer — the righteous soul (aleph), the speech/dibur/malkhut (nun), and the thought/intention (yud). They are different expressions of the same underlying reality: the formation of a receiving vessel for divine abundance through united conscious prayer. The 'mouth/peh' identity of malkhut is a further aspect of the same dibur cluster. Aleph, tzaddik, and ישראל are merged here because 'every Jew is called tzaddik' is the explicit bechina introduction to the aleph identity — they share the same nature as the human pole of the prayer-vessel construction.", "dominant_node": "אני", "nodes": ["אני", "אלף", "צדיק", "ישראל", "נון", "דיבור", "מלכות", "פה", "יוד", "מחשבה"], "node_degrees": {"אני": 3, "מלכות": 3, "דיבור": 2, "נון": 2, "צדיק": 2, "אלף": 2, "מחשבה": 2, "יוד": 2, "ישראל": 1, "פה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יוד", "מחשבה"], "reason": "The Yud is called thought (הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה נִקְרָא יוּד) and thought is one of the three letters forming the vessel Ani. Though a 2-node isolated cluster in raw terms, its essential nature is inseparable from the letter-formation of Ani — it is the yud-component of the same vessel. Merged into Package 1 rather than standing alone."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ישראל", "צדיק", "אלף"], "reason": "ישראל, צדיק, and אלף form a 3-node chain: every Jew is called tzaddik, every tzaddik is called Aleph. These three concepts share one nature: the human actor who forms the Aleph-component of the vessel Ani. ישראל is the empirical referent, צדיק is the identity declared, and אלף is the letter-dimension of that identity. All are aspects of the same bechina in this Torah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פה"], "reason": "Malkhut = peh (mouth) is stated as an equation in the text ('מַלְכוּת – פֶּה'). Peh is another name for dibur-malkhut in this Torah, the Nun-component of Ani. It shares the essential nature of the dibur/malkhut/nun cluster already in this package."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 73, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מ\"ט שַׁעֲרֵי תְּשׁוּבָה", "label_en": "The Forty-Nine Gates of Repentance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes are all different expressions of the same structural unit: the forty-nine-fold pathway through which teshuva unfolds. The forty-nine gates of teshuva ARE the forty-nine letters of the names of the twelve tribes (each gate has its corresponding tribal letter), and the forty-nine days of Sefirat HaOmer ARE those same gates. They are three faces of the same forty-nine-chambered reality — the human-accessible dimension of repentance.", "dominant_node": "המ\"ט שערי תשובה", "nodes": ["המ\"ט שערי תשובה", "מ\"ט אותיות שיש בשמות שנים־עשר שבטי י־ה", "מ\"ט ימי הספירה"], "node_degrees": {"המ\"ט שערי תשובה": 2, "מ\"ט אותיות שיש בשמות שנים־עשר שבטי י־ה": 1, "מ\"ט ימי הספירה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 73, "label": "סְתִים וְגַלְיָא — פְּנִימִיּוּת וְחִיצוֹנִיּוּת", "label_en": "Hidden and Revealed — Inner and Outer", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Torah opens with a fundamental ontological duality that applies equally to Torah and to God: both are simultaneously hidden (stim/penimit) and revealed (galya/chitzoniut). What is revealed IS the garment and the external dimension (מַה שֶּׁנִּגְלֶה לָנוּ הוּא הַלְּבוּשׁ וְחִיצוֹנִיּוּת); what is hidden IS the internal dimension (מַה שֶּׁנִּסְתָּר הוּא הַפְּנִימִיּוּת). These are bechina pairs: revealed=garment=external, hidden=inner. This is the structural frame of the entire Torah — the goal is to reach penimit through the tefila/vessel method. The polarity is neutral because this duality is a cosmic structural feature (not inherently good or evil in itself) — both Torah and God contain both dimensions, and the distinction applies symmetrically to the positive teaching.", "dominant_node": "פנימיות", "nodes": ["פנימיות", "סתים", "גליא", "חיצוניות", "לבוש"], "node_degrees": {"פנימיות": 2, "סתים": 2, "גליא": 2, "חיצוניות": 2, "לבוש": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 73, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שַׁעַר הַחֲמִשִּׁים וְתְּשׁוּבַת הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "The Fiftieth Gate and God's Own Repentance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes express the transcendent level of teshuva that is beyond any individual human's direct attainment: the fiftieth gate, the fiftieth day (Matan Torah / the descent of God onto Sinai), and the 'repentance of God Himself' (va'ashuva aleichem). They are identical: the fifty-day culmination of the Omer, the revelation at Sinai, and the divine 'return' to Israel are three descriptions of the same supernal, transcendent dimension of repentance.", "dominant_node": "יום החמשים", "nodes": ["יום החמשים", "תשובה של השם יתברך בעצמו", "שער החמשים"], "node_degrees": {"יום החמשים": 2, "תשובה של השם יתברך בעצמו": 1, "שער החמשים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 73, "label": "מַיִם וְתוֹרָה — הִתְגַּלּוּת", "label_en": "Water and Torah — Revelation", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Two bechina statements converge in the verse interpretation at the end of the Torah: (1) 'water' IS Torah — וְאֵין מַיִם אֶלָּא תּוֹרָה (a standard rabbinic bechina, Bava Kama 17a); (2) the word 'tavar' (תַּעֲבֹר) IS the language of revelation (מִלְּשׁוֹן הִתְגַּלּוּת), as Targum Onkelos renders it. These three concepts — מים, תורה, התגלות — share a single nature in this Torah's argument: they are the medium through which the hidden becomes accessible. When you 'pass through the water' (= when you desire that the Torah's hidden dimension be revealed to you), you need the vessel Ani. Torah/water and hitgalut are the domain of what is sought; the vessel Ani is the means. Polarity is neutral because Torah and revelation are cosmic structures, not inherently polarized in this teaching.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "מים", "התגלות"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 2, "מים": 2, "התגלות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים", "תורה", "התגלות"], "reason": "מים and תורה form a 2-node bechina pair (Bava Kama 17a; used explicitly in verse interpretation). התגלות is a 1-node isolated concept (the meaning of 'tavar' per Onkelos) but shares the same essential nature: in this Torah, Torah, water, and revelation are all expressions of the same thing — the medium of divine presence that becomes accessible. Per framework, 1-2 node isolated concepts are merged into thematically nearest package based on shared nature."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 73, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִצְרַיִם וּתְשׁוּבָה עִלָּאָה", "label_en": "Egypt as Supernal Repentance — The Narrow Throat", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes are all expressions of the mystical concept of the supernal / upper teshuva (teshuvah ilaah), rooted in the Ari's teaching. Egypt (Mitzrayim) IS the narrowing of the throat (metzar ha-garon) — the word Mitzrayim shares its root with metzar (constriction). The throat is the organ of speech and swallowing — both physiologically and spiritually, the site of constriction and refinement that purifies what passes through it. And this site of constriction IS teshuvah ilaah — the supernal, upper-dimension repentance that the Ari identifies with binah. The three nodes are one mystical identity: the place, its physical analogue, and its spiritual level.", "dominant_node": "מצרים", "nodes": ["מצרים", "מצר הגרון", "תשובה עלאה"], "node_degrees": {"מצרים": 2, "מצר הגרון": 1, "תשובה עלאה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 73, "label": "נוּקְבָא — קַבָּלַת תַּעֲנוּג", "label_en": "Nukva — Reception of Pleasure", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe introduces a distinct bechina domain: whoever receives pleasure from another IS called 'Nukva' (נִקְרָא נוּקְבָא, i.e. the feminine/receptive pole). When God receives pleasure from Israel's prayers (reakh nikoakh), God becomes, as it were, Nukva relative to Israel. The verse 'נְקֵבָה תְּסוֹבֵב גָּבֶר' confirms the inversion: the inner/penimit becomes the outer/chitzoniut. The 'feminine' pole here is not the evil/negative dimension but the holy receptive dimension — it is the 'secret of isha' (בְּסוֹד אִשָּׁה) in the positive sense. This cluster is distinct from Package 1 (vessel formation) because it describes the divine response to the vessel, not the construction of the vessel. It is the telos/outcome of the tefila rather than its structure. Polarity is good because this is the holy dimension of divine receptivity — the Shechina receiving Israel.", "dominant_node": "נוקבא", "nodes": ["נוקבא", "מקבל תענוג", "אישה", "ריח ניחוח"], "node_degrees": {"נוקבא": 2, "מקבל תענוג": 2, "אישה": 1, "ריח ניחוח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אישה", "ריח ניחוח"], "reason": "אישה and ריח ניחוח are both 1-node isolated bechina concepts. The Rebbe states that through the ריח ניחוח God receives (מְקַבֵּל) from Israel's prayers and thereby becomes 'בְּסוֹד אִשָּׁה' — in the secret of isha/woman. Isha and ריח ניחוח are the concrete names for the Nukva-state when God is the receiver. They share the essential nature of the Nukva cluster and are merged here per the framework rule on isolated 1-2 node components."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 74, "label": "יִצְחָק וְהַדִּין הַקָּדוֹשׁ — קַטְנוּת וְהַחֹשֶׁךְ", "label_en": "Yitzchak and the Holy Judgment — Smallness and Darkness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the core positive cluster of Torah #74's first movement. The Rebbe establishes a chain of bechina identities all sharing one essential nature: the dimension of holy judgment (דִּינָא קַדִּישָׁא), which is the starting state of every person's spiritual journey — the state of קַטְנוּת (contracted consciousness). Isaac IS the holy judgment; holy judgment IS the divine attribute Elohim; Elohim IS the Name of judgment; Malkhut IS judgment (dina d'malkhuta dina); Sarah IS malkhut; the moon (levanah) IS Yitzchak; the darkness of Isaac's dimmed eyes IS night; night IS the soul (nefesh) in its lower state; Gevurah IS Pachad Yitzchak; the prayer marker 'Hoshana Rabba' IS speech-without-knowledge (dibur b'lo da'at) = the state of din. All these concepts share one essential nature: the holy judgment that arrives as an apparent distancing, which is in reality the starting point of drawing near. The dominant node is יצחק with the highest degree of connections (5 direct bechina edges). אברהם-חסד is merged here because in the Torah's teaching, the holy judgment (Isaac) IS born from and contains Abraham's love/chesed — the very proof-text 'Abraham begat Isaac' is the bechina that connects chesed and din. The nature of the חסד here is not separate from the din-cluster but is its source and inner clothing. תפלה-פנים is merged here because the Torah explicitly presents prayer as the diagnostic sign (סימן) distinguishing dina d'kadisha from dina d'misa'ava — it belongs to the judgment domain's resolution mechanism and shares the nature of divine face/presence that is either revealed or hidden in the din.", "dominant_node": "יצחק", "nodes": ["יצחק", "אלהים", "גבורה", "דין", "דינא קדישא", "לבנה", "נפש", "לילה", "\"ותכהין עיניו מראות\"", "חשך", "הושענא רבא", "דבור בלא דעה", "ערבה", "מלכות", "שרה", "דינא דמלכותא דינא", "\"את אשר יאהב ה יוכיח\"", "פחד יצחק", "אברהם", "חסד", "תפלה", "פנים"], "node_degrees": {"יצחק": 5, "דין": 4, "אלהים": 3, "דינא קדישא": 3, "גבורה": 2, "הושענא רבא": 2, "דבור בלא דעה": 2, "מלכות": 2, "נפש": 2, "לילה": 2, "\"ותכהין עיניו מראות\"": 2, "לבנה": 1, "דינא דמלכותא דינא": 1, "\"את אשר יאהב ה יוכיח\"": 1, "שרה": 1, "פחד יצחק": 1, "חשך": 1, "ערבה": 1, "אברהם": 1, "חסד": 1, "תפלה": 1, "פנים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אברהם", "חסד"], "reason": "This 2-node pair shares its essential nature with the Package 1 cluster. The Torah's central bechina in this domain is 'Abraham begat Isaac' — Abraham (chesed) IS the source and inner clothing of the holy judgment (Isaac/din). In Torah #74 the chesed is not a separate positive domain but the hidden nature WITHIN the din cluster: 'כִּי בֶּאֱמֶת בְּזֶה הַדִּין מְלֻבָּשׁ חֶסֶד גָּדוֹל'. The bechina אברהם=חסד is the direct explanation of why the holy judgment IS loving discipline. Nearest large component by shared nature is Package 1 (the din-Isaac cluster)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["תפלה", "פנים"], "reason": "The Rebbe establishes תפלה IS פנים (Hezekiah 'turned his face and prayed'). This 2-node pair belongs in the Package 1 domain because in Torah #74 prayer serves as the diagnostic sign (סִימָן) distinguishing holy judgment from impure judgment — it is the indicator of whether one is inside the dina d'kadisha domain or outside it. Prayer/face belongs to the judgment cluster as its test and measure, not to the wisdom/healing cluster. Shared nature: the face of divine presence that is either revealed or hidden in the experience of judgment."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 74, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מָרְדֳּכַי וְחֵרוּת", "label_en": "Mordechai and Freedom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of freedom/liberation (חרות). Mordechai is called 'מר דרור' — the etymology of his name in Aramaic means freedom (Hullin 139b). 'חרות' and 'חרות של פסח' are the same concept of freedom — one stated in general and one in its specific Pesach context. The Rebbe explicitly equates Mordechai with the language of freedom (לשון חרות) and with the aspect of Pesach freedom (בחינת חרות של פסח). All three nodes are different expressions of the same liberating force.", "dominant_node": "מרדכי", "nodes": ["מרדכי", "חרות", "חרות של פסח"], "node_degrees": {"מרדכי": 3, "חרות": 1, "חרות של פסח": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חרות", "חרות של פסח"], "reason": "Both node_ids refer to the same conceptual reality — freedom. 'חרות של פסח' (the freedom of Pesach) is simply the contextually specified form of 'חרות' (freedom in general). In this Torah they are encoded as separate node_ids but the source text treats them as one bechina: Mordechai = מר דרור = לשון חרות = בחינת חרות של פסח. Both edges connect to מרדכי and belong to the same thematic cluster."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 74, "label": "יַעֲקֹב וְהַחָכְמָה — גַּדְלוּת וּרְפוּאַת הַנֶּפֶשׁ", "label_en": "Yaakov and Wisdom — Greatness and the Healing of the Soul", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the core positive cluster of Torah #74's second movement — the resolution and goal. All concepts share one essential nature: the dimension of wisdom-consciousness (מֹחִין דְּגַדְלוּת), which IS the healing of the soul, which IS the sun rising (Yaakov = the sun), which IS speech-with-knowledge (Simchat Torah), which IS the healing tree (ilana d'asuta), which IS Torah itself. Yaakov IS the sun (chamah); Yaakov IS the sky/shamayim; shamayim IS wisdom; wisdom IS gadlut of mind; gadlut IS healing of the soul; healing IS 'sun of righteousness with healing in its wings'; the 'Tree of Healing' IS Torah IS speech-with-knowledge IS Simchat Torah. Fatherly wisdom (Abba = Chokhmah) and Yaakov's sweetening role (hamtakat ha-din) are also bechina aspects of the same wisdom-domain. The dominant node is יעקב with the highest degree (6 direct bechina edges: davar b'da'at, chamah, shemesh x2, rachamim, shamayim, hamtakat). The concept of צדקה (speech-with-knowledge elevated from tzedek) belongs here because its bechina identity in this Torah is explicitly the domain of speech-with-wisdom (dibur b'yedia).", "dominant_node": "יעקב", "nodes": ["יעקב", "דבור בדעה", "אילנא דאסותא", "תורה", "צדקה", "שמחת תורה", "חמה", "שמש", "רחמים", "שמים", "חכמה", "אבא", "מחין דגדלות", "רפואת הנפש", "\"שמש צדקה ומרפא בכנפיה\"", "משיבת נפש", "המתקת הדין של יצחק"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב": 6, "דבור בדעה": 4, "רפואת הנפש": 3, "חכמה": 3, "אילנא דאסותא": 2, "שמחת תורה": 2, "שמש": 2, "מחין דגדלות": 2, "שמים": 2, "תורה": 1, "צדקה": 1, "חמה": 1, "רחמים": 1, "אבא": 1, "\"שמש צדקה ומרפא בכנפיה\"": 1, "משיבת נפש": 1, "המתקת הדין של יצחק": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 74, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שׁוֹשַׁנָּה וְאֶסְתֵּר", "label_en": "The Rose and Esther", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Zohar and the Arizal identify the שושנה (rose) of Song of Songs with Esther. Their bechina is grounded in gematria (שושנה = אסתר numerically) and the Kabbalistic tradition that Esther embodies the quality of the rose — hidden beauty, concealment followed by revelation, the Shekhinah in exile. In this Torah they are paired as the Purim dimension of the verse 'שפתותיו שושנים נוטפות מור עובר' — the rose (שושנה) is Esther (פורים).", "dominant_node": "שושנה", "nodes": ["שושנה", "אסתר"], "node_degrees": {"שושנה": 1, "אסתר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 74, "label": "סִטְרָא דִּמְסָאֳבָא — הַדִּין הַטָּמֵא", "label_en": "The Impure Side — The Defiled Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror cluster of Torah #74. The Rebbe establishes two parallel 2-node pairs and one further 2-node pair that together constitute the domain of dina d'misa'ava — the impure/defiled judgment. (1) סטרא דמסאבא IS Avimelech: the claim that Isaac's din originates from the impure side is the sitra d'misa'ava, embodied in Avimelech. (2) דינא דמסאבא IS 'the serpent injected filth into Eve': the impure judgment's prototype is the primordial defilement of the serpent. (3) עשו IS 'the dross and refuse': Esau IS the absolute dross/dregs that flows downward from Isaac's judgment — the evil separation from the holy din. These three pairs are united by one shared nature: the impure/evil side of judgment — the dimension that looks like judgment but is actually the sitra achra rather than dina d'kadisha. In the Torah's structure, these concepts are always presented as the negative pole contrasting with the holy judgment (Isaac/dina d'kadisha). They form one evil cluster because they are all bechina-aspects of the same corrupt dimension: impure judgment, its archetype (Avimelech), its cosmic root (the serpent), and its human embodiment (Esau). Dominant node is סטרא דמסאבא (which is the most abstract formulation covering all the others).", "dominant_node": "סטרא דמסאבא", "nodes": ["סטרא דמסאבא", "אבימלך", "דינא דמסאבא", "נחש הטיל זהמא בחוה", "עשו", "סיגים והפסלת גמור"], "node_degrees": {"סטרא דמסאבא": 1, "אבימלך": 1, "דינא דמסאבא": 1, "נחש הטיל זהמא בחוה": 1, "עשו": 1, "סיגים והפסלת גמור": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["סטרא דמסאבא", "אבימלך", "דינא דמסאבא", "נחש הטיל זהמא בחוה", "עשו", "סיגים והפסלת גמור"], "reason": "Three separate 2-node bechina pairs in the dataset, each isolated. All three share the essential nature of the evil/impure judgment domain: (A) סטרא דמסאבא=אבימלך: the historical embodiment of the impure claim against Isaac's lineage. (B) דינא דמסאבא=נחש הטיל זהמא בחוה: the cosmic root of impure judgment — the serpent's defilement. (C) עשו=סיגים: the human-dimensional consequence of the impure judgment — total dross. Per the framework, small (1-2 node) isolated components merge into thematically nearest package based on shared nature. All three pairs share the nature of the evil/impure judgment and together constitute the evil mirror of Package 1 (the holy judgment domain). This creates the good/evil mirror structure the framework mandates: Package 1 = dina d'kadisha; Package 3 = dina d'misa'ava. The structure is parallel: both are 'din' — one originates from Abraham/chesed and leads toward healing, the other originates from the serpent/Avimelech and leads to dross."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 74, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שְׂפָתַיִם וּפֶסַח", "label_en": "The Lips and Pesach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The verse says 'שִׂפְתוֹתָיו שׁוֹשַׁנִּים' — 'his lips.' The Rebbe identifies שפתותיו (the lips/speech) as a bechina of פסח through the well-known wordplay: פֶּסַח = פֶּה-סָח (the mouth that speaks). The essential nature shared is speech/verbal revelation — the mouth/lips that articulate and the festival whose name means 'the mouth speaks' are two expressions of the same dimension: divine speech and the liberation it enables.", "dominant_node": "פסח", "nodes": ["שפתותיו", "פסח"], "node_degrees": {"שפתותיו": 1, "פסח": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 75, "label": "הַעֲלָאַת מַיִּין נוּקְבִין וְהַשָּׁלוֹם", "label_en": "Elevation of Feminine Waters and Peace", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest connected component in Torah #75 centers on הַעֲלָאַת מַיִּין נוּקְבִין (elevation of feminine waters) as the master concept: it is a bechina of שלום (peace), of the act of the peacemaker (העושה שלום), of an answer to a question (תרוץ resolving apparent contradiction), and of Torah-and-Prayer speech in all its forms (תורה ותפלה, כל הדבורים שמדברים בתורה ותפלה). All of these concepts share the same essential nature: the unifying, integrative, upward-drawing quality that resolves fragmentation into coherence. Four small isolated components are merged here because their shared bechina nature aligns with this cluster: (A) דם + נפש — blood is the soul (נפש), which is the latent form of the sparks before they are brought into holy speech; this is the raw material of the elevation process and shares the essential nature of 'unspoken potential.' (B) העולמות + אותיות — the fallen worlds are letters, the scattered units that must be gathered and combined through holy Torah-Prayer speech; they share the nature of 'the material that undergoes the elevation/unification.' (C) צדק + דבור של תורה ותפלה — tzedek is the speech of Torah and prayer (as in צֶדֶק תְּדַבֵּרוּן); it is the righteous quality that Torah/Prayer speech embodies, directly part of the elevation domain. The עמידה node (standing) is included because תורה ותפלה = עמידה, which connects through this cluster via the תורה ותפלה node.", "dominant_node": "העלאת מיין נוקבין", "nodes": ["העלאת מיין נוקבין", "שלום", "תורה ותפלה", "עמידה", "העושה שלום", "תרוץ", "כל הדבורים שמדברים בתורה ותפלה", "דם", "נפש", "העולמות", "אותיות", "צדק", "דבור של תורה ותפלה"], "node_degrees": {"העלאת מיין נוקבין": 4, "תורה ותפלה": 2, "העושה שלום": 2, "תרוץ": 1, "כל הדבורים שמדברים בתורה ותפלה": 1, "שלום": 1, "עמידה": 1, "דם": 1, "נפש": 1, "העולמות": 1, "אותיות": 1, "צדק": 1, "דבור של תורה ותפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["דם", "נפש"], "reason": "Blood is a bechina of the soul (כִּי נֶפֶשׁ כָּל בָּשָׂר דָּמוֹ בְּנַפְשׁוֹ הוּא). In this Torah the Rebbe explains that the sparks/niצוצות, before being brought into holy speech, are in a state of דם (blood). Blood = soul = the latent, pre-verbal form of the divine sparks. This is the substrate of the entire elevation process. The domain is: sparks-before-elevation = blood = soul. This shares the essential nature of 'raw material that undergoes the הַעֲלָאַת מַיִּין נוּקְבִין process' and is most thematically cohesive with Package 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["העולמות", "אותיות"], "reason": "The fallen worlds are a bechina of letters (הָעוֹלָמוֹת הֵן בְחִינַת אוֹתִיּוֹת). The teaching states that at the time of creation the worlds fell and scattered as niצוצות/letters. These letters need to be combined (צֵרוּף) through Torah/Prayer speech. The domain is: scattered fallen worlds = unassembled letters = what must be gathered and unified. This shares the essential nature of 'the material of the elevation and unification process' — the same domain as Package 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צדק", "דבור של תורה ותפלה"], "reason": "Tzedek is a bechina of the speech of Torah and Prayer (צֶדֶק, הוּא בְחִינַת הַדִּבּוּר שֶׁל תּוֹרָה וּתְפִלָּה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב צֶדֶק תְּדַבֵּרוּן). This pair directly describes the righteous quality of holy speech, which is one of the core elements of the הַעֲלָאַת מַיִּין נוּקְבִין cluster. Tzedek = holy speech = the same domain as Torah/Prayer/elevation."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 75, "label": "רַגְלִין — תּוֹרָה וּתְפִלָּה כְּעֲמִידָה", "label_en": "Legs — Torah and Prayer as Standing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah and Prayer are both a bechina of רַגְלִין (legs/standing): Torah as in 'וְאַתָּה פֹּה עֲמֹד עִמָּדִי' and Prayer as in 'וַיַּעֲמֹד פִּינְחָס וַיְּפַלֵּל'. All three nodes (תורה, תפלה, רגלין) share the essential nature of 'spiritual standing/foundation' — the capacity to stand upright in divine service. The source text explicitly connects עמידה↔רגלין (תורה ותפלה הם בחינת עמידה, שהוא בחינת רגלין), though this bechina edge is missing from the dataset (see additions file). The small pair 'צולע על ירכו' + 'פגם הרגלין' is merged here because it shares the exact same domain — it describes the defective/broken form of legs (limping = defect of the legs), the negative counterstate of this very cluster. The Rebbe uses it to describe the state of the niצוצות before their tikkun: before holy speech they are 'limping on the hip' (the broken leg state), and after being brought to holy speech they are 'building the rib' (the repaired leg state). The domain is: legs/standing/foundation — both in its healthy form and its broken form.", "dominant_node": "רגלין", "nodes": ["תורה", "תפלה", "רגלין", "\"צולע על ירכו\"", "פגם הרגלין"], "node_degrees": {"רגלין": 2, "תורה": 1, "תפלה": 1, "\"צולע על ירכו\"": 1, "פגם הרגלין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"צולע על ירכו\"", "פגם הרגלין"], "reason": "'Limping on the hip' is a bechina of 'defect of the legs' (צוֹלֵעַ עַל יְרֵכוֹ, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת פְּגַם הָרַגְלִין). The Rebbe uses this to describe the state of the sparks before they are brought into holy speech — they are in a state of limping/broken legs. This is the negative/broken form of the same legs-domain that רגלין, תורה, and תפלה represent in their positive form. Thematically it is closest to Package 2 because the shared bechina nature is: the domain of legs, standing, and walking. The polarity note within the package is that this pair represents the broken/unrectified state of that domain."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 75, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "label_en": "Dispute and Strife", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "קשיא (a question/difficulty) is a bechina of מחלקת (dispute/strife/division): when a person encounters a difficulty, their heart is split — things appear to contradict each other and to be in opposition. This is the explicit negative pole of Torah #75's teaching. The entire Torah is about nullifying מדת הנצחון והמחלקת and pursuing שלום (Package 1). The teaching opens with the fact that the quality of strife and dispute comes from the blood-sparks that have not yet been used to serve God. These two nodes share the nature of 'fracture, division, and opposition' — they are the evil domain that Torah and Prayer must overcome. Although only 2 nodes, this component is maintained as a standalone evil package rather than merged into Package 1 because their essential nature (division/fracture) is the opposite of Package 1's nature (unification/peace) — merging them would conflate opposites. They are the evil mirror of Package 1's שלום.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "קשיא"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 1, "קשיא": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 76, "label": "הִסְתַּכְּלוּת — אוֹר הַיָּשָׁר וְאוֹר הַחוֹזֵר", "label_en": "Gazing — the Direct Light and the Returning Light", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The central organizing metaphor of Torah #76 is vision (ראיה / הסתכלות) and its two optical dimensions: the outgoing ray (אור הישר = direct light) and the returning image (אור החוזר = returning light). These are not cause-and-effect; they are two faces of the same act of seeing. The Rebbe declares that every act of sight has both dimensions — הסתכלות IS the unity of both. The two 3-node clusters (יראה and כח התפשטות הראות around אור הישר; הכאת הראות... and ממרחק ידע around אור החוזר) are both aspects of the single domain of vision-physics. They are merged here because they share one nature: the structure of divine and human sight. בטחון (trust) is equated explicitly with הסתכלות — 'בטחון הוא בחינת הסתכלות' — making it a direct member of this same bechina cluster. Polarity is neutral because vision is a structural/cosmic mechanism that applies equally to divine sight (כי רם ה' וישפל יראה) and human trust — it is not inherently good or evil but is the medium through which good flows.", "dominant_node": "הסתכלות", "nodes": ["הסתכלות", "בטחון", "אור הישר", "יראה", "כח התפשטות הראות מעיניו להדבר הנראה", "אור החוזר", "הכאת הראות בדבר הנראה, שעל ידי זה חוזר הדבר לעיניו ונצטיר בעיניו, שעל ידי זה עקר הראיה", "ממרחק ידע"], "node_degrees": {"הסתכלות": 1, "בטחון": 1, "אור הישר": 2, "יראה": 1, "כח התפשטות הראות מעיניו להדבר הנראה": 1, "אור החוזר": 2, "הכאת הראות בדבר הנראה, שעל ידי זה חוזר הדבר לעיניו ונצטיר בעיניו, שעל ידי זה עקר הראיה": 1, "ממרחק ידע": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אור הישר", "יראה", "כח התפשטות הראות מעיניו להדבר הנראה"], "reason": "Component A (3-node star on אור הישר) and Component B (3-node star on אור החוזר) are two separate connected components in the raw graph, but they share identical essential nature: both are optical dimensions of vision (ראיה). The source text explicitly presents them as paired aspects of the same phenomenon — 'יש בכח הראות בחינת אור הישר ואור החוזר' — meaning they are BOTH bechinot of the same act of seeing. Merging them into one package is therefore demanded by their shared nature (bechina), not by cause-effect. The two stars are simply the two wings of one conceptual domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הסתכלות", "בטחון"], "reason": "Component D (2-node pair בטחון/הסתכלות) is a small isolated component whose shared nature is perfectly aligned with the vision cluster. The Rebbe states 'הבטחון הוא בחינת הסתכלות' — trust IS gazing. Bטחון is the spiritual application of the same metaphysical structure: just as physical vision involves extending the power of sight toward what one desires to see, so too trust involves spiritually 'gazing' at God alone. הסתכלות is the bridge node connecting this pair to the wider vision cluster. Per the framework, 2-node isolated components are merged into the thematically nearest larger package based on shared nature."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 76, "label": "קַטְנוּת הַמֹּחִין — אֱלֹקִים וְדִינִין", "label_en": "Smallness of Mind — Elohim and Judgments", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the Torah's negative/constrained cluster. The Rebbe builds an explicit multi-node bechina chain: קטנות המחין (smallness of mind/consciousness) IS a bechina of אלהים (the divine name associated with judgment/concealment); אלהים IS a bechina of דין (judgment/strictness); דין IS a bechina of אחר הדברים (lit. 'after the words' — the state of being stuck in a static, unchanging condition, unable to refresh the mind). All four are different faces of the same spiritual reality: the constricted, judgmental, stagnant state of consciousness that cannot grow or renew itself. This is the Torah's evil/constrained pole — the condition one must overcome. Polarity is evil because: אלהים in this Torah specifically refers to the attribute of judgment (דינין), and קטנות המחין is described as the spiritual problem — the person stuck in 'אחר הדברים', unable to reach חידוש המחין. Note: node 'קטנות המחין' (edge 264) and node 'מחין דקטנות' (edges 724, 1757) are the same concept under two surface forms; treated as one node with combined degree 3.", "dominant_node": "דין", "nodes": ["קטנות המחין", "אלהים", "דין", "אחר הדברים"], "node_degrees": {"דין": 3, "קטנות המחין": 3, "אלהים": 2, "אחר הדברים": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 76, "label": "אַבְרָהָם וְאַהֲבָה — גַּדְלוּת הַמֹּחִין", "label_en": "Abraham and Love — Greatness of Mind", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the Torah's positive cluster, the mirror of Package 2. It contains three bechina-connected nodes: (1) גדלות המחין IS a bechina of רחמים וחסדים — 'מי ששכלו צח הוא בגדלות המחין, והוא בחינות רחמים וחסדים'; (2) אברהם IS a bechina of אהבה — 'אברהם, שהוא בחינת אהבה'. The connection between the two sub-pairs is that the source text explicitly presents אברהם/אהבה as the vehicle by which one reaches גדלות המחין/רחמים וחסדים — 'על ידי בחינת אברהם... זוכין למחין דגדלות, שהם בחינת חסדים ורחמים'. More fundamentally, these all share the same essential nature: the expansive, loving, merciful quality of the renewed/refreshed mind — the positive pole of consciousness in this Torah. All four nodes are different expressions of the same underlying reality: the state of גדלות (expansion), which is characterized by mercy, love, and clarity (שכל צח). The Zohar's citation — 'מסטרא דימינא מחא חיורא' — equates this luminous brain-state with the attribute of the right/love (אברהם = right = love = pure brain). Polarity is good: this is the Torah's destination-state, the positive dimension of consciousness and its divine attributes.", "dominant_node": "גדלות המחין", "nodes": ["גדלות המחין", "רחמים וחסדים", "אברהם", "אהבה"], "node_degrees": {"גדלות המחין": 1, "רחמים וחסדים": 1, "אברהם": 1, "אהבה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אברהם", "אהבה"], "reason": "Component F (2-node pair אברהם/אהבה) is a small isolated cluster. Its shared nature — love as the quality of expanded and luminous consciousness — is inseparable from the גדלות המחין cluster. The source text explicitly states that the expanded mind is called 'זרע אברהם אוהבי' (the seed of Abraham who loves me), confirming that אברהם and his quality of אהבה belong to the same bechina domain as גדלות המחין and רחמים וחסדים. Per the framework, 2-node isolated components are merged into the thematically nearest larger package based on shared nature."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 77, "label": "הֶבֶל פֶּה וְאוֹת הֵ\"א", "label_en": "The Breath of the Mouth and the Letter Heh", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single surviving bechina in Torah #77 equates the הֶבֶל פֶּה (breath/vapor of the mouth) with the letter הֵ\"א. In the surviving fragment the Rebbe frames the entire teaching around revealing God's kingship (מַלְכוּתוֹ יִתְבָּרַךְ). The breath of the mouth is the formless initial emanation of sound before it becomes structured voice; it corresponds to the first הֵ\"א of the divine Name (י-ה-ו-ה), which is the upper realm of divine expression. Both nodes describe the same essential phenomenon: the primordial, unstructured dimension of sacred speech/breath that is the substrate from which prayer and Torah emerge. The surviving context places these in the positive, holy domain — they are aspects of the human act of learning and praying that contributes to the revelation of God's malkhut. This is a good package, even though it is minimal due to the loss of the teaching's body.", "dominant_node": "הבל פה", "nodes": ["הבל פה", "אות ה\"א"], "node_degrees": {"הבל פה": 1, "אות ה\"א": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 77, "volume": "lm2", "label": "דַּעַת וּמֹחִין — בִּנְיָמִין", "label_en": "Da'at and Mochin — Binyamin", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of intellect, da'at, and the expanded mind (mochin). Binyamin embodies da'at because the Beit HaMikdash stood in his portion — and the Sages equate one who has da'at with one in whose days the Temple is built. Tefillin are the physical/spiritual structure of mochin. All four concepts are different expressions of the same domain: expanded divine knowledge seated in the head.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["בית המקדש", "דעת", "בנימין", "מחין", "תפלין"], "node_degrees": {"בית המקדש": 1, "דעת": 2, "בנימין": 2, "מחין": 2, "תפלין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 77, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יִרְאָה וּמַלְכוּת — יְהוּדָה", "label_en": "Yirah and Malkhut — Yehudah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of holy fear (yirat shamayim) and its structural embodiment. Malkhut is yirah — as the Mishnah says 'were it not for the fear of kingship.' Yehudah embodies malkhut and therefore yirah. The strap of tefillin (retzua) IS yirah — holy yirah specifically. The strap that passes through the passage of tefillin (maavarta) is the structural description of how yirah threads through mochin. All these are expressions of the same domain: holy awe as the receptive/binding force that accompanies da'at.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "הרצועה", "רצועה", "מלכות", "יהודה", "רצועה של תפלין", "יראה דקדשה", "רצועה דקדשה", "רצועה שעוברת בתוך המעברתא של תפלין"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 4, "הרצועה": 2, "רצועה": 2, "מלכות": 2, "יהודה": 2, "רצועה של תפלין": 1, "יראה דקדשה": 2, "רצועה דקדשה": 1, "רצועה שעוברת בתוך המעברתא של תפלין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["יראה דקדשה", "רצועה דקדשה", "רצועה שעוברת בתוך המעברתא של תפלין"], "reason": "These three nodes form a small sub-component (Component 3 in raw analysis) that is thematically inseparable from the holy yirah / malkhut cluster. 'יראה דקדשה' (holy yirah) is the positive specification of 'יראה' (fear/awe), and 'רצועה דקדשה' is its structural equivalent. 'רצועה שעוברת בתוך המעברתא של תפלין' is the specific image of holy yirah threading through the mochin. All three express the same essential nature as the main yirah package: they are holy awe in its active role passing through intellect. They are NOT eitza — they are not different stages; they are the same thing described from different angles (bechina)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הרצועה", "רצועה", "רצועה של תפלין"], "reason": "The dataset uses three spellings/forms of 'the strap': 'הרצועה' (with definite article), 'רצועה' (base form), and 'רצועה של תפלין' (specified as the tefillin strap). The proof texts treat these as identical — the strap in the aggadic story (רצועה שיוצאת מחלקו של יהודה לחלקו של בנימין) IS the retzua of tefillin. The bechina edge רצועה של תפלין — רצועה makes this explicit: the retzua of the story IS the retzua of tefillin. All three node_ids refer to the same essential concept."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 77, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יְרָאָה נְפוּלָה — רְצוּעָה בִּישָׁא", "label_en": "Fallen Yirah — Evil Strap", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These two nodes form the evil mirror of Package 2. 'רצועה בישא' (the evil strap) IS 'יראה נפולה' (fallen/debased fear) — as the Zohar explicitly states (Hakdamat haZohar 11b). Where Package 2 is the domain of holy awe that accompanies da'at and elevates, Package 3 is the domain of fallen fear — the same structural position (the strap threading through mochin) but now corrupted, producing suffering and pulling toward the sitra achra. This is the evil that the Torah is concerned with sweetening.", "dominant_node": "רצועה בישא", "nodes": ["רצועה בישא", "יראה נפולה"], "node_degrees": {"רצועה בישא": 1, "יראה נפולה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 78, "label": "מַלְכוּת וּשְׁכִינָה", "label_en": "Malkhut and the Shekhinah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All seven nodes are identified in the teaching as parallel aspects of a single divine reality: the Shekhinah/Malkhut dimension of holiness. The teaching explicitly states that דיבור (holy speech) is בחינת מלכות, שכינה is בחינת מלכות, רוח הקדש is בחינת מלכות, רוחו של משיח is בחינות רוח הקדש and שכינה, and תחיה is בחינות יחוד and רוח הקדש. They are not cause-and-effect relationships — they are multiple names and faces of the same underlying divine presence. The editorial note at the end of the Torah (§21) confirms this explicitly: 'הדבור דקדושה הוא בחינות השכינה, והוא בחינות רוחו של משיח בחינות רוח הקדש, בחינות תחיה, בחינות יחוד קדשא בריך הוא ושכינתה.'", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "שכינה", "דבור", "רוח הקדש", "רוחו של משיח", "תחיה", "יחוד קדשא בריך הוא ושכינתה"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 3, "שכינה": 3, "רוח הקדש": 3, "דבור": 2, "רוחו של משיח": 2, "תחיה": 2, "יחוד קדשא בריך הוא ושכינתה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component B — {מוציא ומכניס הרוח, 'החיות רצוא ושוב'}", "merge_reason": "Rejected — kept separate. Although the text says 'מוציא ומכניס הרוח... וזהו בחינת רוח הקדש,' this connection reads as an eitza bridge (the mechanism BY WHICH one draws holy spirit) rather than a shared essential nature. The physical breath-motion and the cosmic ratzoh-vashov motion share the same structural nature as each other (both are the back-and-forth rhythm of life-force), but their connection to the מלכות cluster in the text is presented as the *means* of attaining רוח הקדש, not as an equation of essential nature. They therefore remain a separate package describing the mechanism/medium of cosmic vital motion."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 78, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת, קִיּוּם הָעוֹלָם, וּמַתְּנַת חִנָּם", "label_en": "The Ten Utterances, Pre-Torah World-Sustenance, and the Free Gift", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These four nodes all express the same underlying spiritual reality: the dimension of existence that preceded the giving of the Torah, in which the world was sustained purely by God's free, unconditional grace (chesed chinam). The Ten Utterances of Creation are the vehicle through which this pre-Torah sustenance operated. The 'free gift' (matnat chinam) names the quality of that sustenance. The 'world-sustenance before the giving of the Torah' is the temporal/structural frame that encompasses it all. Eretz Yisrael's ten sanctities are identified as a bechina of those same Ten Utterances, meaning the land embodies the same primordial holiness. All four are expressions of the same cosmic domain — the stratum of reality beneath/before Torah-obligation, where divine grace alone holds everything in being.", "dominant_node": "עשרה מאמרות", "nodes": ["עשרה מאמרות", "קיום העולם קדם מתן־תורה", "מתנת חנם", "ארץ ישראל בעצמה יש בה גם כן בחינת עשר קדשות"], "node_degrees": {"עשרה מאמרות": 2, "קיום העולם קדם מתן־תורה": 2, "מתנת חנם": 1, "ארץ ישראל בעצמה יש בה גם כן בחינת עשר קדשות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 78, "label": "מוֹצִיא וּמַכְנִיס הָרוּחַ", "label_en": "The Breath-Motion — Ratzoh VaShov", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The two nodes share the same structural nature: both describe the reciprocal, oscillating motion of vital force — one on the physical level (inhaling and exhaling), the other on the cosmic level (the divine creatures' ratzoh vashov). They are two expressions of the same underlying rhythm. This is a neutral physical/cosmic description that serves as the medium through which holy spirit flows. The proof text reads: 'מוציא ומכניס הרוח, וזהו בחינת החיות רצוא ושוב.' The concept is neutral — the same breath-motion can carry holy spirit (when attached to Malkhut) or impure spirit (when attached to the old foolish king), so it cannot be assigned to the good domain on its own.", "dominant_node": "מוציא ומכניס הרוח", "nodes": ["מוציא ומכניס הרוח", "\"החיות רצוא ושוב\""], "node_degrees": {"מוציא ומכניס הרוח": 1, "\"החיות רצוא ושוב\"": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 78, "label": "מְקוֹמוֹת הַמְטֻנָּפִים", "label_en": "The Defiled / Lowly Places", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Both nodes are explicitly identified as aspects of the same spiritual domain: the realm of degradation and impurity — the 'place' of galut haShekhinah. 'מקומות הנמוכים, שהם בחינות מקומות המטנפים' — low spiritual places ARE the defiled places. These represent the evil/exile dimension: the domain of tumah and spiritual downfall where the Shekhinah must accompany Israel even in its fallen state. They stand as the dark counterpart to the holy מלכות/שכינה cluster. The teaching frames these places as precisely what the Shekhinah must endure in galut: 'השוכן אתם בתוך טומאותם.'", "dominant_node": "מקומות הנמוכים", "nodes": ["מקומות הנמוכים", "מקומות המטנפים"], "node_degrees": {"מקומות הנמוכים": 1, "מקומות המטנפים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 78, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְכַנְפֵי רֵאָה", "label_en": "Torah and the Wings of the Lung", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The two nodes share the essential nature of a protective, moderating, life-sustaining force. The lung-wings ARE Torah: 'כנפי ראה הוא בחינות התורה.' Both are the cooling/protecting presence that prevents the human heart from burning up — whether from excessive holy fire or from the fire of worldly desire. The Zohar source cited in the text (Tikkune Zohar 13; Zohar Pinchas) further identifies ריאה with מים and מים with תורה, forming a chain of bechina identities. Torah is the divine life-principle that moderates and sustains; the lung-wings are its physical analog.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "כנפי ראה"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "כנפי ראה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 79, "label": "שַׁבָּת וּתְשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה", "label_en": "Shabbat and Complete Teshuvah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this component share the essential nature of wholeness, rest (נַיְחָא), and completion. שַׁבָּת, שַׁדַּי, and תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה are each expressions of the same state: evil is fully expelled, inner flux ceases, and one dwells in total rest. מֶטַטְרוֹן (מט\"ט), מִשְׁנָה, and שֵׁשֶׁת יְמֵי הַחֹל describe the incomplete oscillating state that IS מט\"ט before Shabbat arrives — they are the same structural reality as שַׁבָּת/שַׁדַּי viewed from the weekday angle (the container that becomes complete). The node כָּשֵׁר וּפָסוּל טָמֵא וְטָהוֹר אָסוּר וּמֻתָּר names the six-category dual-polarity nature of the incomplete weekday state. The entire chain is a single thematic domain: the axis between incompleteness and completeness, with the teaching directed toward achieving the שַׁבָּת/שַׁדַּי pole. ארץ and נפש (Component B) are merged here because they share the same essential nature of rest-in-completeness: 'שְׁכָן אֶרֶץ – הַיְנוּ בְּחִינוֹת נֶפֶשׁ... הַנֶּפֶשׁ יֵשׁ לוֹ נַיְחָא, מֵחֲמַת שֶׁעָשָׂה תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה... הוּא בְּחִינוֹת שַׁבָּת' — the soul-at-rest is the same essential state as Shabbat-rest.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "שדי", "תשובה שלמה", "מט\"ט", "משנה", "ששת ימי החל", "כשר ופסול טמא וטהור אסור ומתר", "ארץ", "נפש"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 2, "שדי": 2, "תשובה שלמה": 1, "מט\"ט": 2, "משנה": 2, "ששת ימי החל": 2, "כשר ופסול טמא וטהור אסור ומתר": 1, "ארץ": 1, "נפש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component B (ארץ ↔ נפש)", "merged_into": "Component A (שבת cluster)", "reason": "ארץ and נפש form a 2-node component whose shared essential nature is identical to the dominant שבת cluster. The source text (line 22: 'שְׁכָן אֶרֶץ – הַיְנוּ בְּחִינוֹת נֶפֶשׁ... הַנֶּפֶשׁ יֵשׁ לוֹ נַיְחָא מֵחֲמַת שֶׁעָשָׂה תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה... וּכְשֶׁעוֹשֶׂה תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה הוּא בְּחִינוֹת שַׁבָּת') explicitly equates the נֶפֶשׁ-at-rest with the שַׁבָּת state. This is a BECHINA-based merge: ארץ/נפש are expressions of the same wholeness/rest nature (נַיְחָא) as שַׁבָּת/שַׁדַּי/תְּשׁוּבָה שְׁלֵמָה. They are not cause-and-effect links to this cluster but parallel aspects of the same inner reality."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 79, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וּבִטּוּל הַזְּמַן", "label_en": "Teshuvah and Nullification of Time", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: the transcendence of the bounded, finite dimension of time. Teshuvah is explicitly stated to BE bitul hazmanim — they are bechina achat (one aspect). From this same cluster: the 22 letters of Torah (aleph-beit) = stones = speech-acts = teshuvah, because 'take with you words and return'; the shortening of the sun (niskatzar hazman) = bitul hazman; the unification of time (hitachdut hazman) = teshuvah = love (Avraham's quality); the formula Yitzchak-ben-Avraham encodes the complete structure (time + teshuvah + above-time). Kriat Yam Suf and Milchemet Amalek are both bechina of bitul hazmanim and teshuvah. All nodes share the nature of breaking/transcending time's boundaries through return to God.", "dominant_node": "תשובה", "nodes": ["תשובה", "למעלה מהזמן", "בטול הזמנים", "קריעת ים סוף", "מלחמת עמלק", "בן", "דיבורים", "אבנים", "אותיות", "דבורים", "אברהם", "למעלה מן הזמן", "אהבה", "התאחדות הזמן", "יצחק בן אברהם", "התאחדות הזמן על ידי תשובה", "נתקצר הזמן", "בטול הזמן"], "node_degrees": {"תשובה": 4, "למעלה מהזמן": 1, "בטול הזמנים": 2, "קריעת ים סוף": 2, "מלחמת עמלק": 1, "בן": 1, "דיבורים": 2, "אבנים": 3, "אותיות": 1, "דבורים": 1, "אברהם": 2, "למעלה מן הזמן": 1, "אהבה": 2, "התאחדות הזמן": 1, "יצחק בן אברהם": 1, "התאחדות הזמן על ידי תשובה": 1, "נתקצר הזמן": 1, "בטול הזמן": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אברהם", "למעלה מן הזמן", "אהבה", "התאחדות הזמן"], "reason": "This 4-node component (raw Component B) is semantically inseparable from the teshuvah/bitul-hazman core. Avraham = above time (his attribute is love, and love = unity of time per 'in his eyes as a few days in his love'). 'למעלה מן הזמן' is the same concept as 'למעלה מהזמן' in Component A, just a slight variant spelling in the node_id. Ahavah = hitachdut hazman. These are all alternative names for the transcendence-of-time domain already occupied by teshuvah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["יצחק בן אברהם", "התאחדות הזמן על ידי תשובה"], "reason": "This isolated pair represents the Rebbe's key formula: 'Yitzchak ben Avraham' = time (Yitzchak) unified (ben = teshuvah) into above-time (Avraham). Both nodes are direct expressions of the central thesis — teshuvah = bitul hazman — and belong in the teshuvah cluster. The proof text explicitly states: 'Yitzchak ben Avraham alludes to the unity of time through teshuvah.'"}, {"merged_nodes": ["נתקצר הזמן", "בטול הזמן"], "reason": "These two nodes form a bechina pair: 'the shortening of time' = 'the nullification of time.' The proof text explains that when the sun set early for Jacob, 'the time was shortened, which is the bechina of bitul hazman.' This is the same concept as 'בטול הזמנים' in the main component, making this pair a natural merge into Package 1."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 79, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַזְּמַן — יִצְחָק, קֵץ, שֵׁנָה, אַחֲרִית הַיָּמִים", "label_en": "Time — Yitzhak, End, Sleep, End of Days", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These five nodes are all expressions of time as the finite, bounded structural dimension of reality. Yitzhak (יצחק) = the letters Keitz Chai = the personification of time in the patriarchal world. Ketz (קץ) = the end, which is the bechina of time as 'acharit hayamim' (End of Days). Sheinah (שנה/sleep) = the primary mode in which time is experienced (as cited: 'the essence of time is in the aspect of sleep'). These are not expressions of holiness or evil — they are structural/ontological faces of the temporal dimension itself. Time is what teshuvah transcends. This package represents the raw material that Package 1 works upon.", "dominant_node": "זמן", "nodes": ["זמן", "שנה", "קץ", "יצחק", "אחרית הימים"], "node_degrees": {"זמן": 3, "שנה": 1, "קץ": 2, "יצחק": 1, "אחרית הימים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 79, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קוֹל דִּקְדֻשָּׁה", "label_en": "The Holy Voice", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes are bechina of each other: 'His blessed voice' (קולו יתברך) and 'the voice of holiness' (קול דקדשה) are two names for the same divine reality — the sacred call that one merits to hear through teshuvah and bitul hazmanim. The Torah's core verse is 'וְשַׁבְתָּ עַד ה' אֱלֹקֶיךָ וְשָׁמַעְתָּ בְּקוֹלוֹ' — return to God and hear His voice. Hearing this voice is the culminating attainment of the entire teaching. This package is small but thematically distinct: it is the telos/destination, while Package 1 is the path. The evil mirror of this package is the 'kol d'sitra achra' — the voice of Esav — which tries to drown out the holy voice (discussed extensively in the source text but not represented as a bechina node in the dataset).", "dominant_node": "קולו יתברך", "nodes": ["קולו יתברך", "קול דקדשה"], "node_degrees": {"קולו יתברך": 1, "קול דקדשה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 80, "label": "שָׁלוֹם וְיוֹסֵף וְקִדּוּשׁ הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "Shalom, Yosef, and Kiddush Hashem", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of the union of opposites — chasadim (love/drawing-close) and gevurot (discipline/pushing-away) held together as one. Shalom IS the joining of chasadim and gevurot; Yosef IS shalom (he joins his brothers through both rebuke and love); kiddush Hashem IS the embodiment of chasadim+gevurot (first the flame of love, then the steel of self-sacrifice); the second vav IS Yosef (letter-identity); self-sacrifice IS Yosef's archetype; Israel and the first vav are merged here because the text teaches that the first vav (Israel) cannot be pronounced without the second vav (Yosef) — they are inseparable partners, two faces of the same reality. The composite nodes 'hands and feet' and 'chokhmah and speech' are included because they represent the same synthesis: the pairing of the two poles which is the very definition of shalom in this Torah.", "dominant_node": "שלום", "nodes": ["שלום", "יוסף", "קדוש השם", "חסדים וגבורות", "התחברות חסדים וגבורות", "אות וא\"ו שניה", "מסירת נפש על קדוש השם", "ישראל", "אות וא\"ו", "ידים ורגלים", "חכמה והדבור"], "node_degrees": {"שלום": 4, "יוסף": 3, "קדוש השם": 3, "חסדים וגבורות": 1, "התחברות חסדים וגבורות": 1, "אות וא\"ו שניה": 1, "מסירת נפש על קדוש השם": 1, "ישראל": 1, "אות וא\"ו": 1, "ידים ורגלים": 1, "חכמה והדבור": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component E (ישראל, אות וא\"ו)", "merge_reason": "The Torah teaches: 'Israel is bechina of vav (the first vav), and vav cannot be pronounced without a second vav, which is bechina of Yosef.' Israel-as-first-vav and Yosef-as-second-vav are two aspects of the same indissoluble reality. They share the same essential nature as the two poles that constitute shalom. Bechina basis: Israel's core identity IS its vav-nature, and the vav-nature IS the peace-union archetype. Merged into Package 1 on bechina (shared nature), not eitza."}, {"merged_component": "Component F (ידים ורגלים, חכמה והדבור)", "merge_reason": "These composite nodes articulate the paired structure that IS shalom in this Torah: the two extremities (hands=chokhmah, feet=speech) unified in the person of Yosef. The proof text explicitly states: 'Yosef encompasses hands and feet, which are chokhmah and speech.' The composite node's essential nature is the synthesis of the two poles — which is precisely what shalom/Yosef IS. Merged into Package 1 on bechina (the unity of both poles is what defines this package)."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 80, "volume": "lm2", "label": "סוֹדוֹת הַתּוֹרָה וְהַכֶּבֶשׁ", "label_en": "Secrets of Torah and the Ramp", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Temple altar ramp (כבש), the furnace/hidden depths of the world (כבשונו של עולם), and the secrets of Torah (סודות התורה) are three expressions of the same essential dimension: the deep, hidden, ascending dimension of Torah knowledge. Climbing the ramp = penetrating the concealed depths of the world = attaining Torah secrets. In the Torah's argument, whoever advanced further in Torah secrets was appointed leader — all three concepts name the same quality of depth-of-attainment that qualifies one for communal leadership.", "dominant_node": "כבש", "nodes": ["כבש", "כבשונו של עולם", "סודות התורה"], "node_degrees": {"כבש": 2, "כבשונו של עולם": 1, "סודות התורה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 80, "label": "חָכְמָה וּמֹחִין וְקֹדֶשׁ", "label_en": "Chokhmah, Mochin, and Holiness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of the primordial, inward, intellectual dimension of holiness — the 'head' of reality. Chokhmah IS the reishit (first-fruit, beginning); reishit IS kodesh (all beginnings are holy); kodesh IS chokhmah (the verse 'raise your hands in holiness' is interpreted as hands = kodesh = reishit = chokhmah). Mochin (the divine brain/intellect) IS chokhmah — the proof text for 'ido' (his hand) connects: hand → kodesh → reishit → chokhmah/mochah. YHVH IS mochin (as universally known in Kabbalah). The hands (yadim) are bechina of chokhmah. All are expressions of the same primal intelligent force: the upper, inward, masculine dimension of divine wisdom.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["חכמה", "ידים", "ראשית", "קדש", "מחין", "ידו", "יהוה"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה": 3, "ידים": 1, "ראשית": 1, "קדש": 1, "מחין": 2, "ידו": 1, "יהוה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component D (מחין, ידו, יהוה)", "merge_reason": "The proof text for edge 1742 explicitly traces the chain: 'his hand = mochin, because kodesh = reishit = chokhmah/mochah.' Mochin IS chokhmah (the terms are used interchangeably in the Zoharic tradition that underpins this Torah). YHVH = mochin is stated outright. The essential nature of mochin, chokhmah, kodesh, and ראשית is identical — all are the primordial divine intellectual faculty. Merged on bechina (shared essence), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 80, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מִקְדָּשׁ וְשֵׂכֶל", "label_en": "The Temple and the Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Temple (מקדש) and the intellect/mind of the tzadikim (שכלם) are two names for the same thing — derived directly from the Talmudic statement (Berakhot 33): 'Whoever has da'at, it is as if the Temple was built in his days.' In this Torah, it is their deep intellectual awareness of God ('the whole earth is full of His glory') that makes the tzadikim ashamed to display hypocrisy. The Temple = the mind that sees God everywhere = the sacred cognitive space that refuses pretense.", "dominant_node": "מקדש", "nodes": ["מקדש", "שכלם"], "node_degrees": {"מקדש": 1, "שכלם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 80, "label": "דִּבּוּר וּגְבוּרוֹת וִירוּשָׁלַיִם", "label_en": "Speech, Gevurot, and Yerushalayim", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of the outward, expressive, lower dimension — the faculty of holy speech and its structural manifestations. Speech IS gevurot (ugevuratcha yedaberu — your strength they will speak); speech IS Yerushalayim (the city of speech/tzedek); tzedek IS speech (tzedek tedaberun); legs/raglayim ARE speech (legs are the lower limbs that carry/express, parallel to speech as the outward expression of inner wisdom); the foot (raglo) IS gevurot (tzedek runs at his foot). All are aspects of the same expressive, outward, structuring force — the domain of the holy word, the mouth, the kingdom that receives and expresses what chokhmah/mochin sends. In this Torah's framework, speech in prayer and Torah study is the holy goal, and gevurot/legs/tzedek are its parallel aspects.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["דבור", "גבורות", "רגלים", "צדק", "ירושלים", "רגלו"], "node_degrees": {"דבור": 4, "גבורות": 2, "רגלים": 1, "צדק": 1, "ירושלים": 1, "רגלו": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 80, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פַּיִס וְיִפַּיְּסוּ", "label_en": "The Lottery and the Entreating", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The lottery/lot system (פיס) and the act of the community entreating/appeasing the leader (יפיסו) are two expressions of the same corrective principle: legitimate leadership can only come through communal consent and the leader's own reluctance. 'Lottery' (פיס) IS 'appeasement' (יפיסו) — in this Torah's reading, the Mishnah's word פיס is interpreted as meaning that the people must implore (יפיסו) the leader to accept office. The same root expresses both the institutional mechanism and the personal dynamic of proper, humble leadership.", "dominant_node": "פיס", "nodes": ["פיס", "יפיסו"], "node_degrees": {"פיס": 1, "יפיסו": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 80, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַמְמֻנֶּה וְיֵצֶר הָרָע", "label_en": "The Appointed Officer and the Evil Inclination", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The 'Appointed Officer' (ממונה) in the Mishnah who supervised the lottery procedure is read in this Torah as a bechina of the Yetzer Hara — both are the same controlling, supervising evil force that manages the competition for honor and exploits each candidate's desire for leadership. The ממונה IS the יצר הרע: the external figure of corrupt institutional authority is the inner voice of self-interest.", "dominant_node": "יצר הרע", "nodes": ["ממונה", "יצר הרע"], "node_degrees": {"ממונה": 1, "יצר הרע": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 80, "volume": "lm2", "label": "הַצְבִּיעוּ וְצְבִיעוּת", "label_en": "The Command to Display and Religious Hypocrisy", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Mishnaic command 'Raise your fingers!' (הצביעו — so others can count and determine the lot) is read in this Torah as a bechina for 'displaying some hypocrisy before the masses' (שיראה איזה צביעות בפני המון עם). The finger-raising IS the performance of false piety — both express the same act: putting on a public religious show to gain popularity. The double-meaning of the root צב״ע (finger / dyeing / hypocrisy) makes them parallel faces of the same fraudulent self-promotion.", "dominant_node": "הצביעו", "nodes": ["הצביעו", "שיראה איזה צביעות בפני המון עם"], "node_degrees": {"הצביעו": 1, "שיראה איזה צביעות בפני המון עם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 81, "label": "אֶרֶץ וְנֶפֶשׁ וְדִבּוּר — הַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "Earth, Soul and Speech — The Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of the Tzaddik's identity as a speaker. In this Torah, ארץ (earth/land) is a bechina of נֶפֶשׁ (soul), and נפש is a bechina of דִּבּוּר (speech). The Tzaddik's very being is speech — when he speaks Torah or prayer, it is called ארץ ישראל; when he speaks mundane speech (שיחת חולין) it is called פסולת ארץ ישראל. The word 'זֶה' (this) is a name for the Tzaddik himself (Kohelet 12: 'כִּי זֶה כָּל הָאָדָם'). The two 2-node components {תורה, ישראל} and {זה, צדיק} are merged into this cluster because: (a) תורה = ישראל is the holy/sacred dimension of the Tzaddik's speech — when he speaks Torah it IS ארץ ישראל, the bechina of holy dibur; (b) זה = צדיק is the subject of the entire teaching — the Tzaddik is the one whose nefesh/dibur/eretz cluster defines the Torah's main theme. All five concepts share the same essential domain: the Tzaddik as a being of speech and soul.", "dominant_node": "נפש", "nodes": ["ארץ", "נפש", "דבור", "תורה", "ישראל", "זה", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"ארץ": 1, "נפש": 2, "דבור": 1, "תורה": 1, "ישראל": 1, "זה": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": ["תורה", "ישראל"], "reason": "תורה = ישראל is the sacred nature of the Tzaddik's dibur. The teaching states that when the Tzaddik speaks Torah it is called ארץ ישראל, which is בחינת נפש. Torah shares the same essential domain as ארץ/נפש/דבור — it is the holy manifestation of the same speech-soul cluster. The merge is grounded in shared bechina nature: Torah IS the holy dibur, and dibur IS nefesh, and nefesh IS eretz. Torah and Yisrael are the sacred face of what the nefesh/dibur/eretz cluster IS."}, {"merged_component": ["זה", "צדיק"], "reason": "זה = צדיק and the Tzaddik is THE subject of the nefesh/dibur/eretz cluster in this Torah. The Tzaddik is literally defined here as the being whose speech (dibur) is either ארץ ישראל (Torah/prayer) or פסולת ארץ ישראל (mundane speech). His essential nature IS the nefesh/dibur/eretz domain. This merge is based on shared nature: the Tzaddik's essential character in this Torah is identical with the speech-soul-earth cluster."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 81, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רֶגֶל — הַמַּדְרֵגוֹת הַתַּחְתּוֹנוֹת", "label_en": "The Foot — Lower Spiritual Levels", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of 'רֶגֶל' (foot/lowest level). The Rebbe explicitly calls each one a 'bechina of foot': נצח והוד are the feet of the sefirotic body and are the bechina of prophets; a wife (אשה) is called רגל; faith (אמונה) is called רגל; evil speech (דבור הרע) is called רגל; money (ממון) is called רגל; all lower spiritual gradations (כל המדרגות התחתונות) go by the name 'feet'; a son (ברא) is the כרעא (Aramaic: foot/leg) of his father. All share the same structural position in their respective domains — the bottom, the outermost extension, the lowest gradation — yet all are capable of being elevated through joy of Torah and mitzvah. The metaphor cuts across human relationships (wife, son), spiritual faculties (faith), ethical behavior (speech), material life (money), and Kabbalistic anatomy (netzach and hod). The neutral polarity reflects that this cluster includes both positive elements (faith, prophets, wife, son) and negative elements (evil speech), unified by their common structural identity as 'foot.'", "dominant_node": "רגל", "nodes": ["רגל", "רגלים", "כל המדרגות התחתונות", "נצח והוד", "נביאים", "אמונה", "אשה", "דבור הרע", "ממון", "ברא", "כרעא"], "node_degrees": {"רגל": 5, "רגלים": 3, "כל המדרגות התחתונות": 1, "נצח והוד": 1, "נביאים": 1, "אמונה": 1, "אשה": 1, "דבור הרע": 1, "ממון": 1, "ברא": 1, "כרעא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ברא", "כרעא"], "reason": "The Talmudic dictum 'ברא כרעא דאבוה' (Eruvin 70b) — a son is the foot/leg of his father — is a bechina statement: the son and the foot share the same essential nature of being the continuation and extension of what came before. The proof text places this directly in the list of things called 'רגל.' Since ברא (son) IS a כרעא (foot) in the same sense all other items in this Torah are called רגל, this pair merges into the central רגל cluster. The relationship is bechina (shared nature), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 81, "label": "דַּעַת וְהַר וּבֵית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ", "label_en": "Knowledge, the Mountain, and the Temple", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts share the essential nature of transcendent spiritual knowledge and elevation. דַּעַת (divine knowledge/intimacy) is the bechina of הַר (the mountain) — the Gemara says 'ein har ela Beit HaMikdash.' Beit HaMikdash is in turn a bechina of דעת — 'whoever has daat it is as if the Temple was built in his days.' The mountain, the Temple, and daat are three expressions of the same elevated spiritual domain: the place of closeness to God, the summit of spiritual attainment, the inner sanctuary of consciousness. Together they define the goal toward which the Tzaddik's speech connects the masses.", "dominant_node": "דעת", "nodes": ["דעת", "הר", "בית המקדש"], "node_degrees": {"דעת": 2, "הר": 1, "בית המקדש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 81, "volume": "lm2", "label": "צֶדֶק וְתוֹרָה — אוֹת הֵ\"א", "label_en": "Righteousness and Torah — The Letter Heh", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts share the same essential holy nature: Torah and mitzvah (תורה והמצוה) ARE righteousness (צדק) — the Rebbe proves this from multiple verses ('כי כל מצותיך צדק'). Righteousness (צדק) IS the letter Heh (אות הא) — the Rebbe states that the wife is connected 'לבחינת צדק, הנקרא הא,' linking צדק to the letter Heh which is associated with the Shekhinah/malkhut. Furthermore, the supporters of Torah (תומכי דאוריתא) ARE called pillars of truth (סמכי קשוט) — this bechina pair is merged here because they belong to the same domain of Torah/mitzvah/righteousness, and the word 'קשוט' (truth/righteousness) directly connects them to the צדק theme. Together, this package defines the positive force of the teaching: Torah and mitzvah in their essential nature are righteousness, and righteousness is identified with the divine letter Heh (the Shekhinah). This is the 'good' pole of the Torah — the force that elevates all the 'lower levels.'", "dominant_node": "צדק", "nodes": ["צדק", "תורה והמצוה", "אות הא", "תומכי דאוריתא", "סמכי קשוט"], "node_degrees": {"צדק": 2, "תורה והמצוה": 1, "אות הא": 1, "תומכי דאוריתא": 1, "סמכי קשוט": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תומכי דאוריתא", "סמכי קשוט"], "reason": "The Rebbe states that when one learns Torah with joy that reaches the feet, the 'תומכי דאוריתא' (supporters of Torah) are elevated — they are called 'סמכי קשוט' (pillars of truth/righteousness). This is a pure bechina: supporters of Torah and pillars of truth are two names for the same thing. They merge into Package 2 (the Torah/Tzedek cluster) because 'קשוט' (Aramaic: truth/righteousness) is semantically equivalent to 'צדק,' placing them squarely in the righteousness domain. Their elevation through Torah joy does not describe a cause-effect from a different package; rather, it names what they already essentially are. The connection is bechina (shared nature), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 82, "label": "קְדֻשַּׁת הַבְּרִית וּמִילָה", "label_en": "Holiness of the Covenant and Circumcision", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes are different expressions of the same essential reality: the holiness of the covenant of circumcision as embodied by Joseph the Tzaddik. מ\"ל is explicitly called 'בחינת מילה'; קדשת הברית is called 'בחינת מילה'; and יוסף is called 'בחינת קדשת הברית, בחינת מילה כנודע'. They form a chain: יוסף — קדשת הברית — מילה — מ\"ל, all four being aspects of sexual purity and the holy covenant. The Torah explicitly identifies מ\"ל as the component within חשמ\"ל that represents מילה, i.e., the nullification of the ערלה. This is the positive pole of the teaching's central axis.", "dominant_node": "מילה", "nodes": ["מ\"ל", "מילה", "קדשת הברית", "יוסף"], "node_degrees": {"מ\"ל": 1, "מילה": 2, "קדשת הברית": 2, "יוסף": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 82, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אָדָם וְהַסֵּדֶר הָאֱלֹקִי", "label_en": "Adam and Divine Order", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: they are the divine principle of completeness, order, and life-force. 'Adam' = YHVH spelled out with alphs (gematria 45 = mah) = divine sekhel/wisdom that animates all things. 'Mah' (humility/divinity), 'sekhel', 'chiyut', 'shleimut', 'miluyi' are all different names for the same reality — divine wisdom that fills and gives life. The aleph-bet in order (kedem), the letter yud (=10, the wisdom of the divine), and kaf (= yud by gematria) represent the ordered, complete, masculine divine principle. 'Davar she-nimshakh mi-makom gavoah' and 'shvi' express the same reality of drawing down this divine order into one's consciousness.", "dominant_node": "אדם", "nodes": ["אדם", "הוי\"ה במלוי אלפין גימטריא מ\"ה", "סדר", "מה", "הקדוש ברוך הוא", "קדם", "אלף בית כסדר", "אות יוד", "כף", "שכל", "חיות", "שלמות", "מלוי", "דבר שנמשך ממקום גבוה", "שבי"], "node_degrees": {"אדם": 4, "הוי\"ה במלוי אלפין גימטריא מ\"ה": 1, "סדר": 3, "מה": 2, "הקדוש ברוך הוא": 1, "קדם": 2, "אלף בית כסדר": 1, "אות יוד": 2, "כף": 1, "שכל": 2, "חיות": 2, "שלמות": 2, "מלוי": 1, "דבר שנמשך ממקום גבוה": 1, "שבי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["דבר שנמשך ממקום גבוה", "שבי"], "reason": "This pair (Component F in the raw graph) is a small isolated component. The proof text explains 'ושבית שביו — כשתהיה בבחינת מה, כשתמשיך לך אלקות' — the shvi (captive/drawn-down thing) is explicitly equated with drawing down the 'mah' (Adam/divine sekhel). The concept of 'shvi' is the act of attaching divine order into one's consciousness, which is the very substance of this good package. Shvi is not a new domain — it is an aspect of how Adam/mah is drawn down."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 82, "label": "חַשְׁמַ\"ל וְנֹגַהּ", "label_en": "Chashmal and Nogah — The Intermediate Domain", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Both nodes represent the threshold domain between holiness and impurity — the fourth layer that can be absorbed into kedusha or fall into klipa. The Torah identifies נגה as the fourth klipa (corresponding to the fourth year fruit, revi'i) situated between the three impure klipot and kedusha, and states 'והוא בחינות חשמ\"ל, כי לפעמים נכלכלת במ\"ל אורות'. Nogah IS chashmal — the domain that sometimes contains lights of מ\"ל (kedusha) and sometimes falls into the klipot. This is the neutral, oscillating domain whose essential nature is ambiguity and potential. The three years of ערלה = three impure klipot; the fourth year (revi'i/nogah) = chashmal = the borderline that can go either way.", "dominant_node": "חשמ\"ל", "nodes": ["חשמ\"ל", "נגה"], "node_degrees": {"חשמ\"ל": 1, "נגה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["The source text (line 16) contains explicit bechina language linking שתיקה to this component: 'זאת השתיקה שהיא בחינת חשמ\"ל, זהו בחינת נגה'. This creates a bechina bridge to Package 5 not yet captured in the dataset. The gap analysis (torah_82_additions.json) documents this missing edge. If that edge were added, Package 2 and Package 5 would merge into a single larger component. For now, as separate bechina components, they remain separate packages.", "Similarly, the text says 'השתיקה, שהיא בחינת מילה' (line 15), which would bridge Package 2 (via its link to Package 5 through שתיקה=חשמ\"ל) to Package 1. These are missing bechina edges documented in additions."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 82, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חַוָּה וּמַלְכוּת", "label_en": "Chava and Malkhut — the Feminine Vessel", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: they are the feminine receptive principle — the vessel that receives from the masculine divine source. Chava = malkhut peh = Oral Torah = dibur (speech) = eretz (earth) = dalet (the letter that has nothing of its own). Ahor = Tashrak (reverse alphabet) = 'shelo ke-seder' (the disordered state of Chava when separated from Adam). Malkhut = dalet (as the source text says: 'malkhut ha-nikra dalet, leit lah mi-garmah klum'). This is a neutral package because the same vessel/entity (Chava/malkhut) can be either ordered (when connected to Adam/God) or disordered (when separated). The Torah's entire teaching revolves around this vessel and its two orientations.", "dominant_node": "חוה", "nodes": ["חוה", "מלכות פה", "שלא כסדר", "תורה שבעל פה", "ארץ", "דבור", "אחור", "תשר\"ק", "מלכות", "אות דלת"], "node_degrees": {"חוה": 4, "מלכות פה": 1, "שלא כסדר": 1, "תורה שבעל פה": 2, "ארץ": 1, "דבור": 1, "אחור": 1, "תשר\"ק": 1, "מלכות": 1, "אות דלת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אחור", "תשר\"ק"], "reason": "Component C in the raw graph. The proof text explicitly equates אחור = תשרק = שלא כסדר = חוה: 'אחור הוא בחינת תשרק שלא כסדר. וזה בחינת אדם וחוה, כסדר ושלא כסדר.' Tashrak (backward alphabet) is simply the same concept as 'ahor' and both are the same as Chava/malkhut in its disordered/reversed mode."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מלכות", "אות דלת"], "reason": "Component E in the raw graph. The proof text explicitly states: 'מלכות הנקרא דלת, לית לה מגרמה כלום' — malkhut is called dalet because it has nothing of its own. Malkhut here is the same concept as Chava (the Torah explicitly equates them throughout: 'זאת הבחינה מלכות, חוה'), and the dalet is its letter-symbol."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 82, "label": "שַׁלְהֶבֶת יָהּ", "label_en": "The Flame of God — Divine Protective Fire", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Both nodes are expressions of the divine flame of holy love that repels impurity from attaching to holiness on Shabbat eve. The proof text states: 'ואז יורדים בחינת שלהבת י\"ה, ומכוה אותן שלא יאחזו בקדשה, והוא בחינת מים חמין' — the hot water for bathing before Shabbat IS an aspect of the flame of Yah. The essential nature shared by both is: the burning divine love (stronger than death, per Shir HaShirim 8) that protects the boundary of holiness. The text further confirms at line 13 that this same שלהבת י\"ה is what the verse 'ואוהביו כצאת השמש בגבורתו' refers to — it is the blazing love of those who practice holy silence.", "dominant_node": "\"שלהבת י\"ה\"", "nodes": ["\"שלהבת י\"ה\"", "מים חמין"], "node_degrees": {"\"שלהבת י\"ה\"": 1, "מים חמין": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This component is a 2-node cluster. Its polarity is good (divine). While the eitza chain connects it to the נגה/חשמ\"ל domain (the flame acts on nogah to include it in kedusha), its essential NATURE — the divine flame of love stronger than death — is distinct from nogah's nature (the neutral threshold). A bechina-based merge into Package 2 is not warranted: שלהבת י\"ה IS the divine fire, not the neutral threshold. They are different aspects of the same teaching structure, connected by eitza (the flame acts on the nogah), not shared essence."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 82, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שֶׁלֹּא כְּסֵדֶר וּמִלְחָמָה", "label_en": "Disorder and War", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All these concepts share the same essential nature: they are the pathological state of separation from divine order. 'Lo ke-seder' (disorder) = 'milchama' (war) = 'hitnagdut ha-ratzon' (opposition of wills) — these are all the same experience of inner/outer conflict arising when Chava/malkhut is separated from Adam/sekhel. 'Davay' (sickness/faintness of heart) is the symptomatic suffering-state that results from this disorder. The Torah explicitly states: 'kol milchama hu hitnagdut ha-ratzon, hu bechina shelo ke-seder.'", "dominant_node": "לא כסדר", "nodes": ["לא כסדר", "דוי", "מלחמה", "התנגדות הרצון"], "node_degrees": {"לא כסדר": 3, "דוי": 1, "מלחמה": 1, "התנגדות הרצון": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 82, "label": "עָרְלָה וְשָׁלֹשׁ קְלִפּוֹת", "label_en": "The Foreskin and the Three Impure Klipot", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "ערלה and שלש קלפות are explicitly identified as the same essential reality: 'ערלה, שהיא בחינת שלש קלפות הנ\"ל'. The foreskin IS the three impure klipot. Both are the source of חרפה (shame/disgrace) — 'כי עיקר החרפות באים מבחינת ערלה, שהיא בחינת שלש קלפות'. This is the evil mirror of Package 1 (קדשת הברית/מילה): just as מילה is the holy covenant, ערלה is its opposite — the domain of impurity, the three klipot (רוח סערה, ענן גדול, אש מתלקחת), and the source of all shame. The circumcision/removal of the foreskin = the removal of the evil klipot = the gathering of all חרפות.", "dominant_node": "שלש קלפות הנ\"ל", "nodes": ["ערלה", "שלש קלפות הנ\"ל"], "node_degrees": {"ערלה": 1, "שלש קלפות הנ\"ל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 82, "label": "חַ\"שׁ וּשְׁתִיקָה", "label_en": "Chet-Shin and Holy Silence", "polarity": "good", "reason": "ח\"ש IS שתיקה — the proof text states this directly: 'ח\"ש, הוא שתיקה'. ח\"ש is the first component of the word חשמ\"ל (ח\"ש + מ\"ל), and it represents silence. In this Torah, silence is the holy response to insult — the behavior of those who are shamed and do not respond (הנעלבים ואינם עולבים). When practiced from love (מאהבה), this silence is holy, belonging to the domain of kedusha (nogah included in holiness). Both ח\"ש and שתיקה share the essential nature of holy silence that transcends speech — the aspect of חשמ\"ל that represents the nullification of the power to shame by withholding verbal retaliation. The polarity is good when practiced from love (the context of this Torah).", "dominant_node": "שתיקה", "nodes": ["ח\"ש", "שתיקה"], "node_degrees": {"ח\"ש": 1, "שתיקה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["The source text contains two explicit bechina statements linking שתיקה to Package 2 and Package 1 respectively: (1) 'זאת השתיקה שהיא בחינת חשמ\"ל' — שתיקה is called an aspect of חשמ\"ל (Package 2); (2) 'השתיקה, שהיא בחינת מילה' — שתיקה is called an aspect of מילה (Package 1). If these missing bechina edges were added to the dataset, Packages 1, 2, and 5 would form a single connected component. The gap analysis documents these missing edges. The decision to maintain Package 5 as separate is based strictly on the current dataset's bechina connections."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 83, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְתִקּוּן הַבְּרִית", "label_en": "Prayer and Tikkun HaBrit", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts are parallel expressions of the same spiritual act: the directed, faithful outpouring of the soul toward God. The bow (keshet) is the tikkun habrit from which arrows (prayer) are shot. The three vavs (chai birchan d'tzluta) are the three blessings-of-prayer which are the arrows. The voice (kol), the hand (yad), faith (emunah), the daughter (bat), and blessings (berachot) are all bechinot of prayer — different facets of the same reaching-upward. Mashiach = Dibur (speech) = the very act of praying (mesiach ilmim). The Bow/Tikkun HaBrit is the structural enabling apparatus, identical in nature to prayer itself.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "קול", "בת", "ידו", "אמונה", "חצים", "חי ברכן דצלותא", "תלת ווין", "ברכות", "תקון הברית", "קשת", "משיח", "דבור"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 5, "חצים": 2, "חי ברכן דצלותא": 2, "בת": 2, "קול": 1, "ידו": 1, "אמונה": 1, "תלת ווין": 1, "ברכות": 1, "תקון הברית": 1, "קשת": 1, "משיח": 1, "דבור": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תקון הברית", "קשת"], "reason": "The bow (keshet) IS the tikkun habrit — they share the same essential nature as the structural covenant-vessel that launches prayer. The proof text says 'תקון הברית שהוא קשת' directly identifying them. Merged into the Prayer package because the entire bow-and-arrow apparatus is a bechina of prayer itself."}, {"merged_nodes": ["משיח", "דבור"], "reason": "Mashiach = Dibur (speech) = the faculty by which one prays (מֵשִׂיחַ אִלְּמִים — he who speaks the mute). The proof text explicitly states 'מָשִׁיחַ שֶׁהוּא הַדִּבּוּר שֶׁבּוֹ מִתְפַּלֵּל.' Mashiach-as-speech is the same reality as prayer/communication with God."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 83, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שַׁבָּת וְתַכְלִית הַיְדִיעָה", "label_en": "Shabbat and Ultimate Knowledge", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all express the same transcendent domain: the holiness of Shabbat as the pinnacle of creation's wisdom. Kedushah shel Shabbat = Tachlit HaYediah (the ultimate knowing = knowing that we cannot know) = Mekomo Shel Olam (God as the Place that encompasses all). Shin = the three elements (fire, wind, water) that are the structural body of the word Shabbat (שַׁבָּת = שִׁין + בַּת), representing the three-dimensional holiness that fills creation. These are all faces of the same divine transcendence-that-encompasses-finitude.", "dominant_node": "תכלית הידיעה", "nodes": ["קדשת שבת", "תכלית הידיעה", "מקומו של עולם", "שין", "אש, רוח, מים"], "node_degrees": {"תכלית הידיעה": 2, "קדשת שבת": 1, "מקומו של עולם": 1, "שין": 1, "אש, רוח, מים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שין", "אש, רוח, מים"], "reason": "The letter Shin with its three branches = fire, wind, water. These are the first part of Shabbat (שַׁבָּת = שִׁין + בַּת). Shin represents the three-dimensional holiness of creation that Shabbat crowns. This small two-node component belongs to the Shabbat/Knowledge package because its proof text explicitly situates it inside the Shabbat word-analysis ('שַׁבָּת זוֹ שִׁין בַּת')."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 83, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גּוּף קָדוֹשׁ וּבִגְדֵי שַׁבָּת", "label_en": "Holy Body and Shabbat Garments", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Bigdei Shabbat (Shabbat garments) = Guf Kadosh m'Gan Eden (the holy body from the Garden of Eden) = Basar (flesh). These three concepts all name the same reality: the purified, holy body that a person receives after shedding the skin of the snake (mishkha d'chivya). 'Basar' in the verse 'bashar mibeshari' (flesh of my flesh) is the archetypal holy body. The Shabbat garments ARE that holy body — they are not clothes worn over a body but the holy body itself. The three are the same thing named from three angles.", "dominant_node": "בגדי שבת", "nodes": ["בגדי שבת", "גוף קדוש מגן־עדן", "בשר"], "node_degrees": {"בגדי שבת": 2, "גוף קדוש מגן־עדן": 1, "בשר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 83, "volume": "lm2", "label": "בּוּשָׁה וְכִסּוּי הַדָּם", "label_en": "Shame, Blood-Covering, and Atonement", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts express the same redemptive spiritual mechanism: the purifying act of shame and its divine response. Bushah (shame) = Shefichat Damim (shedding of blood) = Shechitah (ritual slaughter) — the same act of humiliation that releases the life-force (blood) for purification. The Shechina's covering of Israel's spilled blood with love = Kisui Dam (blood covering) = the very mitzvot themselves (which are 'delicacies' the Holy One tastes). Neshamot HaNefulot (fallen souls) = Pesha'im (transgressions) — the 'material' that this covering transforms: what was sin becomes merit. Together they form one integrated domain: the path of shame-as-purification and divine love-as-covering.", "dominant_node": "בושה", "nodes": ["בושה", "שפיכות דמים", "שחיטה", "שהשכינה מכסה דמהון של ישראל, שנשפך באהבה הזאת", "כסוי דם", "מצוות", "נשמות הנפולות", "פשעים"], "node_degrees": {"בושה": 2, "שהשכינה מכסה דמהון של ישראל, שנשפך באהבה הזאת": 2, "שפיכות דמים": 1, "שחיטה": 1, "כסוי דם": 1, "מצוות": 1, "נשמות הנפולות": 1, "פשעים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שהשכינה מכסה דמהון של ישראל, שנשפך באהבה הזאת", "כסוי דם", "מצוות"], "reason": "Component E (Shechina's covering / kisui dam / mitzvot) shares the same nature as Component D (bushah / shechitah / shefichat damim): both describe the same purification act from complementary angles. Shechitah requires both cutting (= bushah/shefichat damim) and covering the blood (= kisui dam/Shechina's love). The mitzvot are the 'flesh' that God can taste once the shechitah-act of shame has been performed. These are not eitza (cause→effect) but two faces of the same sacrificial-atonement reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["נשמות הנפולות", "פשעים"], "reason": "Component K belongs to the atonement domain: 'fallen souls are a bechina of transgressions' — they are the same spiritual 'substance' that the kisui dam/love covers and elevates. The falling souls ARE the pesha'im in their unredeemed form; after covering they become zechuyot. They share the same essential nature as the 'material of atonement' that this package addresses."}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 83, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַיִם רַבִּים וְזֶרַע מְרֵעִים", "label_en": "Many Waters and Evil Seed — External Destructive Forces", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These four concepts all represent the same domain: the external evil forces that try to destroy Israel's holiness. Mayim Rabbim (many waters) = Ahavot vYirot Chitzoniyot (external loves and fears) — the spiritual forces that try to drown and extinguish the holy fire of Torah. Zera Mereim (evil seed) = Mei HaMabul (Flood waters) — the destructive seed-force that seeks to overwhelm Israel, identical in nature to the primordial destructive deluge. Both pairs are 'evil water' — drowning, extinguishing, flooding forces that oppose the covenant.", "dominant_node": "מים רבים", "nodes": ["מים רבים", "אהבות ויראות חיצוניות", "זרע מרעים", "מי המבול"], "node_degrees": {"מים רבים": 1, "אהבות ויראות חיצוניות": 1, "זרע מרעים": 1, "מי המבול": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים רבים", "אהבות ויראות חיצוניות", "זרע מרעים", "מי המבול"], "reason": "Components F (זרע מרעים/מי המבול) and G (מים רבים/אהבות ויראות חיצוניות) both represent the same evil domain: external water-forces that try to extinguish, overwhelm, or pollute Israel's holiness. The Rebbe identifies all of them as the opposing force to the holy fire of Torah-love. They are two expressions of the same evil — 'raging external waters' — sharing essential nature as the anti-covenant flood."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 84, "label": "מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם וְסִטְרָא דִּימִינָא", "label_en": "The Trait of Abraham and the Right Side", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups five concepts that the Torah explicitly equates using successive 'is' statements (שֶׁזּוֹ הִיא / וְהִיא / וְהוּא / מִסִּטְרָא דִּימִינָא): being generous with money (וַתְּרָן בְּמָמוֹנִי), Abraham's characteristic trait (מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם), the gate called Love (תַּרְעָא דְּאַהֲבָה), the right side (סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא), and the white brain like silver (מֹחָא חִוָּרָא כְּכַסְפָּא). All five share a single essential nature: the divine quality of expansive love and generosity that belongs to the right column of the sefirot (Chesed/Abraham), which has the characteristic property of whitening and opening — turning accusation into defense, darkness into light, constricted thought into clarity. The chain is: generous money = Abraham's trait = the Love gate = the right side = white-silver intellect. Each is merely a different face of the same cosmic quality — the receptive-opening, chesed-oriented orientation of the soul.", "dominant_node": "מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם", "nodes": ["וַתְּרָן בְּמָמוֹנִי", "מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם", "תַּרְעָא דְּאַהֲבָה", "סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא", "מֹחָא חִוָּרָא כְּכַסְפָּא"], "node_degrees": {"מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם": 3, "וַתְּרָן בְּמָמוֹנִי": 1, "תַּרְעָא דְּאַהֲבָה": 2, "סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא": 2, "מֹחָא חִוָּרָא כְּכַסְפָּא": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "וַתְּרָן בְּמָמוֹנִי", "node2": "מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם", "proof": "וַתְּרָן הָיִיתִי בְּמָמוֹנִי – שֶׁזּוֹ הִיא מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם אָבִינוּ"}, {"node1": "מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם", "node2": "תַּרְעָא דְּאַהֲבָה", "proof": "שֶׁזּוֹ הִיא מִדַּת אַבְרָהָם אָבִינוּ, וְהִיא תַּרְעָא דְּאִתְקְרִי אַהֲבָה"}, {"node1": "תַּרְעָא דְּאַהֲבָה", "node2": "סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא", "proof": "וְהִיא תַּרְעָא דְּאִתְקְרִי אַהֲבָה, וְהוּא סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא"}, {"node1": "סִטְרָא דִּימִינָא", "node2": "מֹחָא חִוָּרָא כְּכַסְפָּא", "proof": "מִסִּטְרָא דִּימִינָא מֹחָא חִוָּרָא כְּכַסְפָּא (תיקוני זהר תיקון ע' דף קיח)"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 84, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּמַלְכוּת — הַשְּׁכִינָה הַקְּדוֹשָׁה", "label_en": "Prayer and Malkhut — The Holy Shekhinah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All ten nodes in this package share one essential nature: they are all expressions of the Shekhinah / holy Malkhut — the divine presence that is the primary subject of this Torah. The Rebbe explicitly equates all of them as bechinas of each other. תפלה IS מלכות (from the verse 'ואני תפלה'). תפלה IS אני — the divine 'I,' the first-person presence of God in the world. אלקים IS אני — the divine name Elokim is the bechina of 'ani' (the prayer text 'תנו עוז לאלקים' is explained as 'the ani'). אשת חיל IS מלכות — the Rebbe says explicitly: 'על-ידי הכח תתקן את אשת-חיל, בחינת מלכות.' התחברות IS תפלה — the word תפלה itself is linguistically 'לשון התחברות' (the language of bonding/attachment), as proven from the Aramaic translation. אתערותא דלתתא IS תקון השכינה — the Rebbe says 'כי מתחלה צריך אתערותא דלתתא, הינו תקון השכינה' — the arousal from below IS the repair of the Shekhinah; they are the same act seen from two perspectives. הקמת הברית IS זווג — the Rebbe says 'הקמת הברית רמז לזווג' — establishing the covenant IS the sacred union; they are the same divine reality described in two idioms. All ten concepts orbit one reality: the holy Shekhinah/Malkhut that is in exile and must be restored through powerful prayer. The eitza structure of the Torah is: pray forcefully → strengthen the Shekhinah → extract her from exile → achieve divine union. The bechina structure within this package reflects the single domain of holy divine presence in all its names and aspects.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "מלכות", "אני", "אלקים", "התחברות", "אשת חיל", "אתערותא דלתתא", "תקון השכינה", "הקמת הברית", "זווג"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 3, "מלכות": 2, "אני": 2, "אלקים": 1, "התחברות": 1, "אשת חיל": 1, "אתערותא דלתתא": 1, "תקון השכינה": 1, "הקמת הברית": 1, "זווג": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אתערותא דלתתא", "תקון השכינה"], "reason": "These two form their own raw connected component (a pair), but the Rebbe says directly: 'כי מתחלה צריך אתערותא דלתתא, הינו תקון השכינה' — the arousal from below IS the repair of the Shekhinah. The word 'הינו' (meaning 'that is' / 'namely') is the Rebbe's signature bechina marker: he is equating the two concepts as the same thing. The arousal from below does not cause the repair of the Shekhinah as a separate downstream effect — they are two descriptions of the same act. The Shekhinah (תקון השכינה) is the same entity as מלכות and תפלה in the main cluster (the Torah's entire subject is the Shekhinah = תפלה = מלכות). Merging this pair into Package 1 is a bechina merge, not an eitza merge."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הקמת הברית", "זווג"], "reason": "These two also form their own raw connected component (a pair). The Rebbe says: 'הקמת הברית רמז לזווג' — establishing the covenant is a symbol/indicator of sacred union. More precisely, it IS the union: both describe the culminating act of the Shekhinah being united with her divine partner after being repaired. This pair belongs in Package 1 because הקמת הברית / זווג is the culminating state of the entire process: the Shekhinah (מלכות / תפלה) being united with the Holy One. The domain is identical: holy malkhut and her union. The relationship between the two nodes is bechina (shared essential nature — covenant and union are two descriptions of the same divine reality), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 84, "label": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת וּמְקַטְרְגִים", "label_en": "Foreign Thoughts and Accusers", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups the blocking forces that oppose access to Torah secrets: the serpents and scorpions (נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים), the accusers (מְקַטְרְגִים), and foreign thoughts (מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת). The Torah states explicitly in line 10 that all three are the same thing: 'כָּל הַמְקַטְרְגִים וְכָל הַנְּחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים, הַיְנוּ הַמַּחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת שֶׁמְּבַלְבְּלִין מַחֲשַׁבְתֵּהּ' — all the accusers and all the serpents and scorpions, MEANING the foreign thoughts that confuse one's mind. This triple equation (מְקַטְרְגִים = נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים = מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת) is a classic bechina: three different conceptual registers (legal/prosecutorial, mythic/serpentine, psychological/cognitive) all describe the same essential blocking reality — the force that prevents a person from entering the secrets of Torah. This package is the evil mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 is the opening force (right side, love, whitening), Package 2 is the closing force (accusers, serpents, confusion).", "dominant_node": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת", "nodes": ["נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים", "מְקַטְרְגִים", "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת": 2, "נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים": 2, "מְקַטְרְגִים": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "מְקַטְרְגִים", "node2": "נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים", "proof": "כָּמָּה נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים מְבַלְבְּלִין מַחֲשַׁבְתֵּהּ [= the accusers and the serpents/scorpions are stated in apposition in the Zohar quote, then equated directly in Rebbe Nachman's commentary]"}, {"node1": "נְחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים", "node2": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת", "proof": "כָּל הַמְקַטְרְגִים וְכָל הַנְּחָשִׁים וְעַקְרַבִּים, הַיְנוּ הַמַּחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת שֶׁמְּבַלְבְּלִין מַחֲשַׁבְתֵּהּ"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 84, "label": "הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז וְרָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא", "label_en": "The Hidden Good and the Secrets of Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The two nodes in this package are equated by direct 'הַיְנוּ' language in line 6: 'הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז, הַיְנוּ רָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא' — the hidden good, MEANING the secrets of Torah. Their shared nature is: the concealed, sacred, day-specific content of reality that is accessible only to the righteous. This is kept as a standalone package rather than merged into Package 1 for the following reason: Package 1 describes the gate and the quality/path (love, generosity, right side, white mind). Package 3 describes the destination/content (what is revealed once the gate is opened). One enters Package 1 → then arrives at Package 3 — that is an eitza connection between the packages, not a shared nature within one package. The Torah explicitly frames them as two separate things: there is a gate (tara'a) and there is a good (tov ganuz). The gate and the good are not the same essential nature; they stand in a cause-and-effect relationship.", "dominant_node": "הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז", "nodes": ["הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז", "רָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא"], "node_degrees": {"הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז": 1, "רָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז", "node2": "רָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא", "proof": "רוֹאֶה וּמַשִּׂיג הַטּוֹב הַגָּנוּז, הַיְנוּ רָזִין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא, הַשַּׁיָּךְ לְאוֹתוֹ הַיּוֹם"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 85, "label": "אוֹר שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים וְהַמֹּחִין", "label_en": "The Light of the Seven Days and the Supernal Intellects", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The source text's single central bechina equation equates four things as expressions of the same supernal reality: (a) the four mochin — חכמה, בינה, חסד, גבורה — as they are clothed within נצח-הוד-יסוד of Binah; (b) the light of the seven primordial days (כְּאוֹר שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים); (c) the shin of three heads (שִׁין שֶׁל שְׁלֹשָׁה רָאשִׁין, corresponding to the standard shin ש); and (d) the shin of four heads (שִׁין שֶׁל אַרְבָּעָה רָאשִׁין, the shin found in tefillin shel rosh). All four are bechina-equivalent: they are all expressions of the same supernal intellectual light that flows through Ze'ir Anpin to illuminate and build Malkhut. The text explicitly says 'וְזֶה בְּחִינוֹת: כְּאוֹר שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים, שֶׁהֵם סוֹד שִׁין שֶׁל שְׁלֹשָׁה רָאשִׁין וְשִׁין שֶׁל אַרְבָּעָה רָאשִׁין'. The polarity is good: these are the holy intellectual lights (mochin d'gadlut) that flow from Binah into Ze'ir Anpin, enabling the blessing and building of Malkhut.", "dominant_node": "מֹחִין", "nodes": ["מֹחִין", "חָכְמָה", "בִּינָה", "חֶסֶד", "גְּבוּרָה", "נֶצַח-הוֹד-יְסוֹד דְּבִינָה", "כְּאוֹר שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים", "שִׁין שֶׁל שְׁלֹשָׁה רָאשִׁין", "שִׁין שֶׁל אַרְבָּעָה רָאשִׁין"], "node_degrees": {"מֹחִין": 2, "כְּאוֹר שִׁבְעַת הַיָּמִים": 2, "חָכְמָה": 1, "בִּינָה": 1, "חֶסֶד": 1, "גְּבוּרָה": 1, "נֶצַח-הוֹד-יְסוֹד דְּבִינָה": 1, "שִׁין שֶׁל שְׁלֹשָׁה רָאשִׁין": 1, "שִׁין שֶׁל אַרְבָּעָה רָאשִׁין": 1}, "merge_notes": ["חָכְמָה, בִּינָה, חֶסֶד, גְּבוּרָה are listed in the text as the four mochin. In the source they are enumerations of מֹחִין and share the same bechina-equivalent nature as the light of the seven days. They are merged into this package rather than treated as separate isolates, because the text itself groups them as a unit ('הַמֹּחִין הֵם אַרְבַּע: חָכְמָה בִּינָה חֶסֶד וּגְבוּרָה') and the bechina is stated for the group as a whole.", "שִׁין שֶׁל שְׁלֹשָׁה רָאשִׁין and שִׁין שֶׁל אַרְבָּעָה רָאשִׁין are presented as the secret (סוֹד) of the light of the seven days — they are therefore bechina-equivalent to the same light, expressed through the two forms of the letter shin."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 85, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֹרֶף וְלוּז — הַמָּקוֹם הַנֶּחְבָּא", "label_en": "The Nape and the Luz — The Hidden Place", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All these concepts designate the same anatomical-spiritual zone: the back-of-neck/nape region and its encoded names. עֹרֶף (nape) is the location of the Luz bone; לוּז is both the bone's name and the city adjacent to Beit El; קָדְקֹד (crown/skull) has the same gematria as 'נַחֲמוּ נַחֲמוּ' (two times 'be comforted') and is a bechina of the nape; שְׁתֵּי פְּעָמִים נַחֲמוּ equals קָדְקֹד by gematria and thus equals ערף; כְּתֵפָיו (shoulders — Benjamin's blessing) is cited alongside קָדְקֹד (Joseph's blessing) as the same Luz dimension; שַׁבַּת נַחֲמוּ has gematria ש״נ = ש״נ = the same letters as ערף, making it a bechina of ערף. These are structural neutral objects (body-part names, scriptural terms) that all point to the same hidden-yet-imperishable dimension. The pair אֱגוֹזִים הַנִּקְרָאִים לוּזִים — לֵאָה is merged here because: the text explicitly states the Luz-nuts 'hint at the bechina of Leah who is in the nape (עֹרֶף),' making both the nuts and Leah expressions of the same נֶפֶשׁ רִאשׁוֹנָה / ערף zone.", "dominant_node": "ערף", "nodes": ["ערף", "לוז", "קדקד", "שתי פעמים נחמו", "כתפיו", "שבת נחמו", "אגוזים הנקראים לוזים", "לאה"], "node_degrees": {"ערף": 3, "לוז": 2, "קדקד": 3, "שתי פעמים נחמו": 2, "כתפיו": 1, "שבת נחמו": 1, "אגוזים הנקראים לוזים": 1, "לאה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אגוזים הנקראים לוזים", "לאה"], "reason": "This isolated pair was a separate 2-node component. The proof text states explicitly: 'אֱגוֹזִים הַנִּקְרָאִים לוּזִים, הֵם רוֹמְזִים לִבְחִינַת לֵאָה שֶׁהִיא בָּעֹרֶף' — the Luz-nuts hint at Leah who is in the nape (ערף). Both the nuts and Leah are expressions of the same ערף / Luz-zone dimension, identical in essential nature to the Luz-nape cluster. Merged into Package 1 rather than Package 2 because Leah's nature here is as the first (ראשונה), the hidden preliminary zone, not the resurrection-consolation theme of Package 2."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 85, "label": "מַלְכוּת בִּבְחִינַת אוֹשִׁיט פְּסִיעָה לְבַר", "label_en": "Malkhut in Its Diminished State — Extending a Small Step Outward", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The opening line of the Torah establishes that Malkhut in its initial state is described 'בִּבְחִינַת אוֹשִׁיט פְּסִיעָה לְבַר' — the aspect of extending one small step outside, close to Ze'ir Anpin. This is a bechina description: Malkhut, in its state of smallness (katnut) and proximity to Ze'ir Anpin before it has been fully built and elevated, IS the aspect of 'extending a small step outward'. The Zohar phrase encapsulates the essential nature of Malkhut's initial, diminished condition. Polarity is neutral rather than evil: this diminished state of Malkhut is not itself the sitra achra — it is simply the starting condition of holy Malkhut before it receives mochin and grows. The Rebbe's teaching is about how to correct and grow it (לְתַקְּנָהּ וּלְגַדְּלָהּ), which presupposes it is holy Malkhut in a state of katnut.", "dominant_node": "מַלְכוּת", "nodes": ["מַלְכוּת", "\"אוֹשִׁיט פְּסִיעָה לְבַר\""], "node_degrees": {"מַלְכוּת": 1, "\"אוֹשִׁיט פְּסִיעָה לְבַר\"": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is a two-node component derived from the explicit bechina language in line 1. It stands apart from Package 1 because the mochin-light-shin cluster is about the supernal intellects that flow INTO Malkhut from above, whereas this package is about Malkhut's own starting condition. These are structurally different domains: the receiver (Malkhut) vs. the content received (the mochin/light). Merging would conflate the vessel with its filling."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 85, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֶצֶם לוּז וְנֶחָמָה — הַגּוּף הַנִּצְחִי", "label_en": "The Luz Bone and Consolation — The Eternal Body", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all express the same essential reality: latent indestructibility and the promise of future renewal. עֶצֶם לוּז is the imperishable bone in the human nape from which the body will be rebuilt at resurrection; אִילַן לוּז is the Luz tree whose 21-day cycle corresponds to the Three Weeks of mourning and the egg's incubation period, embodying the same pattern of survival-through-concealment; שְׁלֹשָׁה שָׁבוּעוֹת שֶׁהֵם עֶשְׂרִים וְאֶחָד יוֹם שֶׁבֵּין הַמְּצָרִים is the 21-day mourning period, identical in its numeric structure to the Luz tree's cycle; בֵּיצָה (egg) shares the same 21-day period of completion (a hen hatches in 21 days), making it a parallel expression of the same pattern of hidden development leading to emergence; עִקַּר נֶחָמוֹתֵינוּ (the essence of our consolation) is identified explicitly as the Luz bone itself — resurrection is the ultimate consolation. The pair בֵּית הַמִּקְדָּשׁ — בֵּית אֵל is merged here because: the Beit HaMikdash, called Beit El by Jacob, is the destination reached through the Luz pathway. The text states 'through the Luz one enters Beit El' and 'the Beit HaMikdash is the bechina of Beit El.' The Temple represents the same good/holy domain of consolation and divine dwelling that the resurrection/Luz bone promises.", "dominant_node": "עצם לוז", "nodes": ["עצם לוז", "אילן לוז", "שלשה שבועות שהם עשרים ואחד יום שבין המצרים", "ביצה", "עקר נחמותינו", "בית המקדש", "בית אל"], "node_degrees": {"עצם לוז": 2, "אילן לוז": 2, "שלשה שבועות שהם עשרים ואחד יום שבין המצרים": 2, "ביצה": 1, "עקר נחמותינו": 1, "בית המקדש": 1, "בית אל": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בית המקדש", "בית אל"], "reason": "This isolated pair was a separate 2-node component. The proof text establishes that these are bechina of each other — Jacob called the Beit HaMikdash 'Beit El.' The Temple's essential nature in this Torah is as the holy destination of consolation that the Luz-pathway leads to. It belongs thematically with the good/consolation cluster (Package 2) rather than the neutral anatomical cluster (Package 1). The connection is bechina (same sacred place, two names), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 86, "label": "שַׁבָּת וֶאֱמֶת", "label_en": "Shabbat and Truth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "שבת (Shabbat) and אמת (Truth) share a single essential nature in this Torah: they are both the same supportive, stabilizing force that enables a person to walk in God's ways. The Torah states explicitly 'שַׁבָּת הוּא בְחִינַת אֱמֶת' — Shabbat IS an aspect of truth — grounded in the Talmud's observation that even an unlearned person is afraid to lie on Shabbat (masechet Demai ch. 4). This is not a causal claim (Shabbat does not produce truth) but a statement of essential identity: they are the same stabilizing quality. The Talmud further states 'קֻשְׁטָא קָאֵי' — truth stands firm (on two legs; Shabbat 104), and that truth is itself a 'third leg' (רגל שלישי), the support (סעד) that a beginner-walker needs. Shabbat and truth are two names for the same supportive, anti-deception, stabilizing ground beneath the feet of the person who is just beginning to walk in God's ways.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "אמת"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 1, "אמת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 86, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֱמוּנָה שְׁלֵמָה וּמַאֲרִיךְ רוּחוֹ", "label_en": "Complete Faith and Length of Spirit", "polarity": "good", "reason": "אמונה שלמה and מאריך רוחו share the same essential nature: they are two expressions of the same positive spiritual reality. The Rebbe states explicitly — 'שאין להם אמונה שלמה, שהיא בחינת מאריך רוחו' — perfect faith IS a bechina of 'length of spirit.' The two concepts are not cause and effect (faith does not produce length of spirit; rather, full faith and long-spirit are the same interior quality described from two angles). This is the positive pole of the ruach-emunah spectrum that structures the entire Torah. The Rebbe cites Likutay Moharan I, 155 as a cross-reference for this identification. This package is the good mirror of Package 3 (the evil pole of heresy/no-ruach) and the contrast class for Package 2 (deficient faith/short-spirit).", "dominant_node": "אמונה שלמה", "nodes": ["אמונה שלמה", "מאריך רוחו"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה שלמה": 1, "מאריך רוחו": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 86, "label": "צְדָקָה וְשֶׁמֶשׁ", "label_en": "Charity and the Sun", "polarity": "good", "reason": "צדקה (charity) and שמש (the sun) share a single essential nature: they are both the same luminous, empowering, life-giving radiance that gives strength to movement and walking. The Torah states 'וּצְדָקָה הוּא בְחִינוֹת שֶׁמֶשׁ, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב (מַלְאָכִי ג): שֶׁמֶשׁ צְדָקָה' — charity IS an aspect of the sun, based on Malachi's 'a sun of righteousness.' This identity is not metaphor but ontological equivalence: the charitable act radiates the same essential quality as solar light. Within the Torah's framework, two solar instances are distinguished — the present-day sun (corresponding to weekday charity) and the future sun of Shabbat-charity (כאור שבעת הימים — 'as the light of the seven days'), but both are the same sun-nature. Charity and the sun are two names for the same luminous, energizing, walking-enabling force.", "dominant_node": "צדקה", "nodes": ["צדקה", "שמש"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 1, "שמש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 86, "volume": "lm2", "label": "קְטַנֵּי אֲמָנָה וְקֹצֶר רוּחַ", "label_en": "Little Faith and Shortness of Spirit", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "קטני אמנה and קצר רוח are two expressions of the same deficient spiritual state: lacking complete faith IS shortness of spirit. The Rebbe states — 'הקטני אמנה הם בחינת מקצר רוח' — and explains this is an intermediate position, neither the fullness of good (אמונה שלמה = מאריך רוחו) nor the total absence of spirit characteristic of heresy. The bechina identification is explicit: קצר רוח is the precise inner phenomenology of what it means to be a קטן אמנה. This is not an eitza relationship (failing faith does not cause short spirit; they are the same interior condition named twice). The Rebbe also notes that even righteous people can be in this state, and it is this deficiency that necessitates harsh avodah and fasting. The polarity is 'evil' in the sense of a spiritual flaw requiring correction — it is the domain of קלקול אמונה (impairment of faith), the middle zone on the spectrum between Package 1 (complete faith) and Package 3 (total heresy).", "dominant_node": "קטני אמנה", "nodes": ["קטני אמנה", "קצר רוח"], "node_degrees": {"קטני אמנה": 1, "קצר רוח": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 86, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה וְכְּפִירָה — אֵין רוּחַ", "label_en": "Idol Worship and Heresy — No Spirit", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "עבודה זרה והכפירות and 'אף אין יש רוח בהם' share the same essential nature: idolatry and heresy ARE the state of having absolutely no ruach whatsoever. The Rebbe cites Psalms 135 explicitly — 'העבודה זרה והכפירות הם בחינת: אף אין יש רוח בהם' — to establish that this describes the intrinsic nature of idols and the heretics who follow them: they are spirit-empty. This is not a consequence of heresy; it IS what heresy is at its core — the total absence of the ruach/emunah dimension. The node '\"אף אין יש רוח בהם\"' is a biblical phrase functioning as a concept-label: the defining characteristic of the evil pole on the ruach spectrum. This package is the evil mirror of Package 1 (אמונה שלמה = מאריך רוחו): the same ruach axis, but at its complete absence rather than its fullness.", "dominant_node": "עבודה זרה והכפירות", "nodes": ["עבודה זרה והכפירות", "\"אף אין יש רוח בהם\""], "node_degrees": {"עבודה זרה והכפירות": 1, "\"אף אין יש רוח בהם\"": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 87, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אִשָּׁה וָדֶרֶךְ", "label_en": "Woman and Path", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Torah explicitly states 'ki isha nikret derech' — a woman is called a 'path/way' (Kiddushin 2a). Both concepts express the same essential reality: the relational channel through which the covenant is expressed and through which divine light flows (or fails to flow in the case of pgam habrit). The same identification holds in both the holy and corrupted form: the holy wife/path vs. 'derech isha mena'efet' (the way of the adulteress, Proverbs 30:20). They are neutral in polarity because the same derech-isha nexus can be holy (proper marriage, the Elul tikkun) or evil (the pgam habrit). They are two names for one structural reality: the covenant relationship as a channel/path.", "dominant_node": "דרך", "nodes": ["אשה", "דרך"], "node_degrees": {"אשה": 1, "דרך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 87, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יָם וֶאֱמוּנָה", "label_en": "Sea and Faith", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah explicitly states 'bivchinат yam, bechinat emunah' — the yam (sea) is the bechina of emunah (faith). The 224 (rach'd) lights must shine in the 'sea,' which is identified as the dimension of faith. Both concepts express the same essential reality: the vast, boundless, receptive medium that holds divine light. The sea as a metaphor for emunah captures the quality of something that receives, contains, and extends beyond visible limits — exactly how faith functions in the soul. The kavvanot of Elul open the 'derech bа-yam,' illuminating this faith-sea. Polarity is good because the ים/אמונה nexus is the positive, receptive domain of holiness where the tikkun operates.", "dominant_node": "ים", "nodes": ["ים", "אמונה"], "node_degrees": {"ים": 1, "אמונה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 88, "label": "מָטָר וְהַשְׁפָּעוֹת", "label_en": "Rain and Divine Influx", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central bechina of Torah #88: rain (מטר) IS the divine influx and blessings (השפעות וברכות) that flow to the world through the tzaddik. The verse 'Who prepares rain for the earth' (המכין לארץ מטר) is interpreted as 'Who prepares the influx and blessings for the earth.' This single bechina is the load-bearing equitation of the entire teaching — the cosmic meaning of rain is precisely the divine overabundance that the tzaddik channels to the world. Both concepts share the same essential nature: the downward movement of divine blessing from the heavenly realm to the material world. This is an unambiguously positive, holy cluster.", "dominant_node": "מטר", "nodes": ["מטר", "השפעות וברכות"], "node_degrees": {"מטר": 1, "השפעות וברכות": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is a raw 2-node component from a single bechina edge (Edge 984). No merge from other components is applicable — the second component (שכינה↔אלהים) does not share the same essential nature as rain/influx. Rain/influx belongs to the realm of the tzaddik's output (drawing divine blessing downward), while שכינה/אלהים belongs to the realm of divine immanence/presence.", "Candidate for merger with the proposed gap-package 'שמים/אהבה/יראה' (see torah_88_additions.json) via the eitza chain: the tzaddik conceals his love/fear (Package 2 domain) in order to draw down the rain/influx (Package 1 domain). This is cause-and-effect, not shared nature — so the packages remain distinct per the bechina-only rule."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 88, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֲבֵדוֹת וּמַלְאָךְ הַשּׁוֹפָר", "label_en": "Lost Things and the Shofar Angel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The angel and his deputies holding shofars are explicitly called a bechina of 'תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד' (para 9) — meaning they are the celestial force that recovers what the wicked's desires cause to be lost. The אֲבֵדוֹת (lost things) are what the angel seeks. The shofar blasts TRT (Tekiah-Teruah-Tekiah) are how he signals the finding. These all share the same essential nature: they are expressions of the divine recovery system — the positive force that seeks, finds, and restores what has been spiritually lost. The great noise and joy upon finding an אֲבֵדָה (para 6) is the same domain. The righteous person (tzaddik) with strong avodah can also extract additional חִיּוּת from the fruit and 'find lost things there' (para 3) — meaning the tzaddik is a bechina of this same angel-function on earth.", "dominant_node": "אֲבֵדוֹת", "nodes": ["אֲבֵדוֹת", "מַלְאָךְ", "מְמֻנִּים", "שׁוֹפָרוֹת", "תְּקִיעָה תְּרוּעָה תְּקִיעָה", "חוֹפְרִים וּמְחַפְּשִׂים אַחַר אֲבֵדוֹת", "רַעַשׁ וְשִׂמְחָה גְּדוֹלָה"], "node_degrees": {"אֲבֵדוֹת": 7, "מַלְאָךְ": 4, "מְמֻנִּים": 3, "שׁוֹפָרוֹת": 4, "תְּקִיעָה תְּרוּעָה תְּקִיעָה": 4, "חוֹפְרִים וּמְחַפְּשִׂים אַחַר אֲבֵדוֹת": 3, "רַעַשׁ וְשִׂמְחָה גְּדוֹלָה": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["המלאכים המחפשים אחר האבדות, שהם בחינת תאוות רשעים תאבד — הם אוחזים בידם שופרות ותוקעים תר\"ת (para 9)", "אפילו הצדיקים החופרים ומבקשים אחר אבדות — הם אובדים גם כן לפעמים, בבחינת: יחפרו ויאבדו (para 8)", "וכשהם מוצאים איזה אבדה, נעשה רעש ושמחה גדולה (para 6)", "תאוות רשעים תאבד — ראשי תיבות: תקיעה, תרועה, תקיעה (para 9)"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מַלְאָךְ", "מְמֻנִּים"], "reason": "The text presents the מַלְאָךְ and the מְמֻנִּים under him as a single unified seeking-force: 'יש מלאך, ותחתיו כמה ממונים, וכולם אוחזים בידם שופרות' (para 6). They share exactly the same function and domain — seeking lost things with shofars. They are the same essential nature at different hierarchical levels."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חוֹפְרִים וּמְחַפְּשִׂים אַחַר אֲבֵדוֹת", "מַלְאָךְ"], "reason": "The tzaddikim who dig and seek after lost things (para 8: 'הצדיקים החופרים ומבקשים אחר אבדות') are performing the same function as the angel — they are the earthly bechina of the celestial recovery mission. Para 3 also states that the strong tzaddik 'can extract additional life-force from the fruit and find lost things there' — he IS operating in the angel's domain."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 88, "label": "שְׁכִינָה וֶאֱלֹקִים", "label_en": "The Divine Presence and the Name Elohim", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Shechinah (the Divine Presence immanent in the world) is identified as a bechina of the divine name אֱלֹקִים. This is a standard Kabbalistic equivalence: אלהים is the name associated with the sefirah of Malkhut (divine kingship / immanence in nature), and the Shechinah is the presence of God within the material world — they are two expressions of the same divine reality: God's presence as it contracts and manifests within creation. Both are squarely in the domain of holiness; this is not the name associated with the forces of evil but with the tzaddik's divine quality of justice/nature that he embodies.", "dominant_node": "שכינה", "nodes": ["שכינה", "אלהים"], "node_degrees": {"שכינה": 1, "אלהים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component (Edge 263) is attributed to reference 88 in the dataset, but the proof text 'בְּהַשְּׁכִינָה, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת אֱלֹקִים' does not appear in the Torah #88 source text file (torah_88.txt). The concepts שכינה and אלהים are entirely absent from the 9-line source text. This edge may represent: (a) a data entry error with the wrong reference number, (b) a background assumption Rebbe Nachman imports from another Torah, or (c) a proof-text from an external source that the editor associated with Torah #88. Per the algorithm, all edges with reference===88 AND type==='bechina' are included; this edge is therefore retained in the analysis but flagged as anomalous.", "No merge into Package 1 (מטר/השפעות וברכות): the shared-nature test fails. Divine Presence/Elohim is about the nature of God's immanent identity; rain/influx is about the flow of blessing through the tzaddik. These are categorically different bechinas — the Shechinah is not the same thing as rain, nor are they different faces of the same underlying reality within this Torah's conceptual framework.", "This package is retained as a standalone 2-node package. Its conceptual connection to the teaching may be that the Shechinah (= אלהים) is what the tzaddik draws down via his concealed love/fear — but this is an eitza connection (the Shechinah's influx flows as rain), not a bechina."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 88, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תַּאֲוָה וְאַבְדָּן", "label_en": "Desire and Loss", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. Just as the angel's domain is recovery of lost things, the domain of desires (תַּאֲווֹת) is the cause of spiritual loss. Para 7: 'מה שאובדים אבדות הרבה על ידי התאוות, בבחינת: תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד'. The wicked (רְשָׁעִים) are those driven by these desires. Loss (אַבְדָּן) is the shared essential nature — what happens when taavah operates. The tzaddik who loses despite his righteousness (para 8: 'יש צדיק אובד בצדקו') reflects the paradox of 'עֵת לְאַבֵּד' — even the good side can lose, which is itself a form of the losing-domain. The premature-fruit eating (para 1-2) is the concrete physical expression of this desire that causes loss of soul-life.", "dominant_node": "תַּאֲוָה", "nodes": ["תַּאֲוָה", "רְשָׁעִים", "תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד", "אֲכִילַת פְּרִי קֹדֶם גְּמַר בִּשּׁוּלוֹ", "עֵת לְאַבֵּד"], "node_degrees": {"תַּאֲוָה": 5, "רְשָׁעִים": 4, "תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד": 4, "אֲכִילַת פְּרִי קֹדֶם גְּמַר בִּשּׁוּלוֹ": 3, "עֵת לְאַבֵּד": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["אובדים אבדות הרבה על ידי התאוות, בבחינת: תאוות רשעים תאבד (para 7)", "האוכל פרי קודם גמר בשולה יכולה להזיק לו מאד לנשמתו, כי יוכל לאבד נפשו על ידי זה (para 1) — the desire to eat unripe fruit is the prototype taavah causing loss", "יש צדיק אובד בצדקו (Ecclesiastes 7:15, cited para 8) — the bechina of paradoxical loss", "עת לאבד (Ecclesiastes 3, cited para 8) — the time of losing is itself a bechina category"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד", "תַּאֲוָה"], "reason": "The verse 'תַּאֲוַת רְשָׁעִים תֹּאבֵד' is not just a proof text — it IS the essential description of this domain. The Rebbe treats it as both the category-name and the mechanism: desires of the wicked cause loss. It IS the same node as תַּאֲוָה in its fully expressed form."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אֲכִילַת פְּרִי קֹדֶם גְּמַר בִּשּׁוּלוֹ", "תַּאֲוָה"], "reason": "Eating unripe fruit before its time is the concrete embodiment of taavah in this Torah — the impulsive desire that does not wait for natural completion. The fruit-eating is not a separate domain; it is the physical instantiation of taavah as the cause of soul-loss. The entire first half of the Torah is the taavah that destroys in physical form."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 88, "volume": "lm2", "label": "בְּרָכָה וְיִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם", "label_en": "Blessing and Fear of Heaven", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Para 3 explicitly presents בְּרָכָה (the blessing over fruit) with כַּוָּנָה (intention) and יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם (fear of heaven) as the protective force that saves a person from the fruit's drawing power. These three are not cause-and-effect in sequence — they are three names for the same essential spiritual stance of mindful divine awareness. The same verse ('כַּוָּנָה וּבְיִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם') makes them coterminous. The חוֹזֵק בְּיוֹתֵר בַּעֲבוֹדַת ה' (one who is especially strong in divine service, para 3) is the fullest expression of this same domain — so strong that he can even draw out additional life-force and find lost things. This package is the middle ground between the loss-domain (Pkg 2) and the recovery-domain (Pkg 1).", "dominant_node": "בְּרָכָה", "nodes": ["בְּרָכָה", "כַּוָּנָה", "יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם", "חוֹזֵק בַּעֲבוֹדַת ה'", "הַנָּצָלוּת"], "node_degrees": {"בְּרָכָה": 4, "כַּוָּנָה": 3, "יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם": 3, "חוֹזֵק בַּעֲבוֹדַת ה'": 3, "הַנָּצָלוּת": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["אם מברך הברכה של הפרי בכוונה וביראת שמים, אזי יוכל להנצל מזה (para 3)", "גם אם הוא חזק ביותר בעבודת ה', יוכל גם כן להוציא עוד חיות מהפרי, ולמצא שם אבדות (para 3) — the fully realized brachah/yirah-domain enables even finding lost things", "כוונת אלול, שהם תיקון לפגם הברית (para 11) — the Rebbe connects this domain to the kavanot of Elul which rectify the covenant-blemish, showing that the blessing/awareness domain is the same as the Elul-teshuvah domain"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כַּוָּנָה", "יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם"], "reason": "The text joins them in one phrase: 'בכוונה וביראת שמים' (para 3). They are presented as a single combined state of consciousness, not as sequential steps. In Breslov teaching, intention and fear of heaven are two names for the same mindful awareness before God."}, {"merged_nodes": ["חוֹזֵק בַּעֲבוֹדַת ה'", "יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם"], "reason": "The one who is 'חזק ביותר בעבודת ה'' (para 3) is the fullest realization of יִרְאַת שָׁמַיִם — they are not different domains but different degrees of the same essential spiritual orientation. Being strong in avodah IS the full flowering of fear of heaven."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 88, "volume": "lm2", "label": "פְּרִי וְכֹחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ", "label_en": "Fruit and the Drawing Force", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The fruit (פְּרִי) and its drawing force (כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ) are structural/natural concepts that are the vehicle of this Torah's teaching. The fruit is not itself good or evil — it has a natural force when unripe that can harm, and a completed state that is permitted. The כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ is a natural property, like a physical organ, used as a spiritual metaphor. The ripening process (בִּשּׁוּל), the plucking (תְּלִישָׁה), and the waiting period (הַמְתָּנָה until the fruit ripens on its own in a detached state, para 5) are all facets of this neutral structural cluster. The human analogy given in para 5 — 'like a tired person who puffs many times (sappen/gasps) until he rests' — is itself a bechina of this fruit-rest process, showing the neutrality of the metaphor.", "dominant_node": "פְּרִי", "nodes": ["פְּרִי", "כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ", "בִּשּׁוּל", "תְּלִישָׁה", "חִיּוּת", "הַמְתָּנָה עַד שֶׁיָּנוּחוּ"], "node_degrees": {"פְּרִי": 8, "כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ": 6, "בִּשּׁוּל": 5, "תְּלִישָׁה": 3, "חִיּוּת": 4, "הַמְתָּנָה עַד שֶׁיָּנוּחוּ": 2}, "key_bechina_proofs": ["הפרי כל זמן שהיא צריכה להתגדל, יש לה כח המושך, כי היא צריכה חיות להתגדל (para 2)", "כשמתבשלת כל צרכה, שוב פסק ממנה כח המושך, כי אינה צריכה עוד למשוך חיות (para 2)", "תוכל הפרי למשוך לעצמה חיות הנפש של זה האדם (para 2) — the fruit's drawing force IS the life-force mechanism, they share the same nature", "זה דומה כמו האדם שהוא יגע, והוא מרחף רחיפות הרבה בפיו... עד אשר ינוח (para 5) — the fruit's rest-period IS a bechina of human panting/resting"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ", "חִיּוּת"], "reason": "The drawing force (כֹּחַ הַמּוֹשֵׁךְ) and the life-force (חִיּוּת) are co-defined: 'יש לה כח המושך, כי היא צריכה חיות להתגדל' (para 2). The drawing force IS the life-force-seeking mechanism. They are two aspects of the same natural property."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הַמְתָּנָה עַד שֶׁיָּנוּחוּ", "בִּשּׁוּל"], "reason": "The waiting period until the detached fruit ripens on its own (para 5) is a form of בִּשּׁוּל — natural ripening after plucking. The text says 'מותר לאכלן' after this wait, showing it achieves the same goal as full on-tree ripening. They are two modes of the same completion-process."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 89, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תּוֹרָה וְכַלָּה — שִׁדּוּכֵי הַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Torah and the Bride — The Matchmaking of Da'at", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah and kallah (bride) share the same essential nature: both are unions of two opposites. Torah contains two types of letters (two opposing dimensions), and a kallah is the product of a shidduch uniting two opposites. The Sages' word-play — read 'betrothed' (me'orasah) rather than 'inheritance' (morasha) — makes the equation explicit and textually grounded. Both concepts also participate in the central act of this Torah: the tzaddik who says Torah is acting as matchmaker, because Torah-as-bride is the object of the shidduch.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "כלה"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "כלה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 90, "label": "שְׁכִינָה וְאֶרֶץ הָעֶלְיוֹנָה", "label_en": "The Shekhinah and the Upper Earth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes share the essential nature of the divine indwelling presence — the Shekhinah. The Rebbe explicitly equates them: 'מִן הָאָרֶץ – הַיְנוּ אֶרֶץ הָעֶלְיוֹנָה, בְּחִינַת הַשְּׁכִינָה'. In kabbalistic terminology, אֶרֶץ הָעֶלְיוֹנָה (the upper earth / supernal earth) is a standard name for the sefirah of Malkhut, which is itself identified with the Shekhinah — the indwelling divine presence that receives the sparks (ניצוצות) fallen in the Breaking. Both nodes express the same single reality: the receptive, immanent dimension of divinity that is the locus of cosmic deficiency and repair. The teaching turns on the verse 'יִתַּמּוּ חַטָּאִים מִן הָאָרֶץ' — when one rejoices in God, the חטאים/חסרונות (deficiencies) are completed 'from' the Shekhinah, meaning the Shekhinah herself is made whole. The bechina ארץ העליונה = שכינה is the conceptual anchor of the entire Torah.", "dominant_node": "שכינה", "nodes": ["שכינה", "ארץ העליונה"], "node_degrees": {"שכינה": 1, "ארץ העליונה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["No merges performed — this Torah has exactly one bechina edge and one raw connected component of 2 nodes. The component is already its own coherent package.", "שכינה is designated dominant node over ארץ העליונה because שכינה is the theologically primary concept; ארץ העליונה functions as the interpretive gloss on the verse's 'מִן הָאָרֶץ'."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 90, "volume": "lm2", "label": "שְׁבִירַת כְּלִי חֶרֶס וּפִּתְחָהּ שֶׁל גֵּיהִנֹּם", "label_en": "Breaking the Clay Vessel and the Entrance to Gehinnom", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "These concepts form a single symbolic complex: the act of breaking a clay vessel at a betrothal (shidukh) is a bechina of the Pottery Gate (Shaar HaCharsit) in Jerusalem, which itself faced the entrance to Gehinnom. The broken shards of pottery reference the gate through which pottery shards were thrown, and that gate corresponds to the entrance of Gehinnom. All three nodes are different expressions of the same symbolic warning-sign — physical act, its geographic referent, and the spiritual reality it points toward. Polarity is neutral because the clay vessel and the gate are physical/geographic objects used as symbolic reminders rather than intrinsically aligned with good or evil.", "dominant_node": "שבירת כלי חרס", "nodes": ["שבירת כלי חרס", "שער החרסית", "פתחה של גיהנם", "גי בן הנם", "גיהנם"], "node_degrees": {"שבירת כלי חרס": 2, "שער החרסית": 1, "פתחה של גיהנם": 1, "גי בן הנם": 1, "גיהנם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["גי בן הנם", "גיהנם"], "reason": "This 2-node component is explicitly identified as a bechina pair in the proof text: 'וְגֵי בֶן הִנֹּם זֶהוּ בְּחִינַת הַגֵּיהִנֹּם'. Both nodes refer to the same spiritual reality of punishment/Gehinnom and belong to the same symbolic domain as Component A, since 'פתחה של גיהנם' in Component A is precisely the entrance to this very Gehinnom. All five nodes form a single unbroken symbolic chain: breaking clay → Pottery Gate → entrance to Gehinnom → Gei Ben Hinnom = Gehinnom. Merging is based on shared nature (all are faces of the Gehinnom-reminder domain), not cause-and-effect."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 91, "label": "שֶׁמֶשׁ וְחָכְמָה — The Sun and Wisdom", "label_en": "The Sun as the Dimension of Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #91 is built on a single structural identity: שמש (the sun) IS a bechina of חכמה (wisdom/sekhel). This is not a cause-and-effect statement — it is an ontological equation. The verse 'עַד בֹּא הַשָּׁמֶשׁ' (until the sun came) is interpreted to mean 'until the sekhel of the matter [was reached],' and the ground for this interpretation is precisely that 'the sun is a bechina of chokhmah, as cited above [in Torah #1].' The sun and wisdom share the same essential nature: they are both the illuminating, penetrating, crowning light that dispels darkness and reveals what was hidden. The sun-wisdom identity is the destination-point of the Torah's entire argument: sustained faith (אמונה) that spreads through all the limbs is what carries a person 'until the sun comes' — i.e., until the level of chokhmah is attained. Both שמש and חכמה represent the same ontological reality: the highest light-dimension of consciousness, the illuminating sekhel that is the 'sun' of spiritual life. Polarity is good: this is the domain of holy wisdom, divine light, and intellectual attainment — the positive telos of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["שמש", "חכמה"], "node_degrees": {"שמש": 1, "חכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 91, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חָכְמָה עִלָּאָה וְהָרַב", "label_en": "Upper Wisdom and the Teacher", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of the upper, giving, divine wisdom-pole: חכמה עלאה is the supernal wisdom; כלל התורה הקדושה is its full embodiment; the letter את (as in 'את השמים') encodes that upper pole; the רב (teacher) is its human image; אות יוד is its letter-form; לי/למד-יוד are the scriptural word encoding the union of upper and lower (ל=למד, י=יוד) in which the upper pole יוד is primary; חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה names the unified pair with the upper as the goal; וידבר/זווג is the act of divine union that brings the two wisdoms together; and לאמר/הלבשה/לבוש is the garment-mechanism by which the Tzaddik clothes Torah speech to effect that union. All these are expressions of the same upper-wisdom reality that the Tzaddik seeks to connect to the lower.", "dominant_node": "חכמה עלאה", "nodes": ["חכמה עלאה", "כלל התורה הקדושה", "את", "רב", "אות יוד", "לי", "למד יוד", "חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה", "וידבר", "זווג", "לאמר", "הלבשה", "לבוש"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה עלאה": 4, "אות יוד": 2, "רב": 2, "כלל התורה הקדושה": 1, "את": 1, "לי": 2, "למד יוד": 1, "חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה": 1, "וידבר": 1, "זווג": 1, "לאמר": 2, "הלבשה": 1, "לבוש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["למד יוד", "לי", "חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה"], "reason": "These three nodes form a tight cluster encoding the UNION of upper and lower wisdom: 'לי' is the word God cried out (מי יקום לי) which encodes the union of ל (=חכמה תתאה) and י (=חכמה עלאה); 'למד יוד' are the two letter-names that spell לי; 'חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה' is the composite concept. They share the essential nature of the upper wisdom as the goal-pole of the union, making them bechina of the upper wisdom cluster rather than the lower. The proof text states explicitly: 'לי דייקא, בחינת חכמה עלאה וחכמה תתאה שהם בחינת למד יוד'. This is not eitza — the word 'לי' does not produce the two wisdoms, it IS the encoding of them."}, {"merged_nodes": ["וידבר", "זווג"], "reason": "וידבר (the opening word of the verse) is a bechina of זווג (union/coupling) — the proof text states 'וידבר — לשון זווג והתקשרות'. This pair encodes the divine speech-act that unites the two wisdoms. It belongs to Package 1 (the upper wisdom cluster) because the union-act is the expression of upper wisdom joining to lower, and the positive thrust of the teaching is that connection. Merging into Package 1 because the coupling is initiated from the divine/upper side."}, {"merged_nodes": ["לאמר", "הלבשה", "לבוש"], "reason": "לאמר (the word 'to say/speak') is a bechina of הלבשה (clothing/garment); and לבוש (garment) is equated with לאמר via the verse 'בצע אמרתו' (Lamentations 2:17) which the Torah interprets as 'garment'. These three form the cluster of the Tzaddik's speech-garment by which Torah is clothed in ordinary stories. This is the mechanism by which חכמה עלאה reaches downward — so it belongs with the upper wisdom package as the expressive vehicle of that upper wisdom entering the world."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 91, "volume": "lm2", "label": "חָכְמָה תַּתָּאָה וְהַתַּלְמִיד", "label_en": "Lower Wisdom and the Student", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of the lower, receiving, worldly wisdom-pole: חכמה תתאה is the lower wisdom; אלקים (the divine name associated with nature/judgment) is its well-known kabbalistic encoding; the letter אות למד is its letter-form (the letter ל is the bechina of the student learning from the teacher — 'מגדל הפורח באויר'); תלמיד is its human image (the student who receives from the teacher); תלמיד הלמד מהרב is the full description of that student; and מגדל הפורח באויר (a tower floating in the air, from Chagigah 15b) is the image of the heart reaching upward to receive the teacher's words. All these are expressions of the same receiving, lower wisdom-pole that the Tzaddik seeks to elevate and connect to the upper.", "dominant_node": "חכמה תתאה", "nodes": ["חכמה תתאה", "אלקים", "תלמיד", "אות למד", "מגדל הפורח באויר", "תלמיד הלמד מהרב"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה תתאה": 4, "אות למד": 3, "תלמיד": 2, "אלקים": 1, "מגדל הפורח באויר": 1, "תלמיד הלמד מהרב": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 91, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גָּלוּת הַשְּׁכִינָה וּמִעוּט הַלְּבָנָה", "label_en": "Exile of the Shekhinah and Diminishment of the Moon", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of separation and fracture: 'וכשהחכמות שבעולם שיונקים מחכמה תתאה הם נפרדים מהתורה ומהשם יתברך' is the condition of separation — worldly wisdom cut off from its upper source; 'מעוט הלבנה' (diminishment of the moon) is the cosmic expression of that separation (the Shekhinah-moon loses its light when the lower world is disconnected from the upper); 'גלות השכינה' (exile of the Divine Presence) is the same condition named differently — both are aspects of the same fracture. This is the exact evil mirror of Package 1+2: when the Tzaddik successfully connects lower to upper wisdom (Packages 1 and 2), the result is מלוי הלבנה (fullness of the moon); when that connection is severed, the result is מעוט הלבנה and גלות השכינה.", "dominant_node": "וכשהחכמות שבעולם שיונקים מחכמה תתאה הם נפרדים מהתורה ומהשם יתברך", "nodes": ["וכשהחכמות שבעולם שיונקים מחכמה תתאה הם נפרדים מהתורה ומהשם יתברך", "מעוט הלבנה", "גלות השכינה"], "node_degrees": {"וכשהחכמות שבעולם שיונקים מחכמה תתאה הם נפרדים מהתורה ומהשם יתברך": 2, "מעוט הלבנה": 1, "גלות השכינה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 92, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְיַעֲקֹב — תּוֹרָה, אֱמֶת, רֵאָה, יַעֲקֹב, אָדָם", "label_en": "Torah and Jacob — Torah, Truth, Lung, Jacob, Man", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five concepts share a single essential nature: divine truth as expressed through the living body of Torah-learning man. The chain is triply reinforced by explicit bechina language: (1) יעקב הוא בחינת אמת — Jacob is truth itself; (2) ואמת הוא בחינת תורה — truth is Torah; (3) ובהתורה יש חמישה ספרים כנגד חמישה כנפי ראה — the five books of Torah correspond to the five wings of the lung, making Torah and the lung structurally identical; (4) הראה והלב הם בחינות יעקב ויוסף — the lung is the bechina of Jacob, bringing ראה into the cluster via Jacob; (5) אדם שהוא בחינת תורה (זאת התורה אדם, במדבר יט) — Man himself is a bechina of Torah. These five nodes are thus all expressions of the same positive cosmic force: the Torah-truth-breath that flows through Jacob/Man and cools the fires of harsh judgment. The dominant node תורה is the conceptual hub with three bechina connections (to ראה, אמת, and אדם) in this Torah.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "יעקב", "אמת", "ראה", "אדם"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 3, "יעקב": 2, "אמת": 2, "ראה": 2, "אדם": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is the largest raw connected component (5 nodes) and stands on its own without any merges.", "The five-node cluster is fully connected by bechina edges: יעקב–אמת (id 348), אמת–תורה (id 3092), תורה–ראה (id 2652), ראה–יעקב (id 1371), תורה–אדם (id 3091). The result is a densely interconnected good package.", "Components B (יוסף/לב) and C (אש/דין) are NOT merged into this package. The connection from this cluster to לב/יוסף runs through eitza (the lungs cool the heart), not bechina. The proof text for the ראה–יעקב edge (id 1371) mentions that 'הראה והלב הם בחינות יעקב ויוסף' but this appears as a contextual introduction; in the dataset the בחינה ראה↔יעקב is the captured equivalence, while יוסף↔לב is separately recorded as its own edge. The cross-component relationship is causal (ראה cools לב), which is eitza, not bechina — the two sides are not the same essential nature but structural opposites.", "אדם is the weakest node by degree (1) but its bechina with תורה is the Torah's closing punch-line: 'אדם שהוא בחינת תורה' is the conceptual key that explains why a man walking in his home can revive the dead — because Man IS Torah, and Torah cools the dinin. The node is correctly placed here."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 92, "volume": "lm2", "label": "עֲשָׂרָה קַפִּיטְל תְּהִלִּים וְעֲשָׂרָה מִינֵי נְגִינָה", "label_en": "Ten Psalms Chapters and Ten Types of Melody", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The ten chapters of Psalms and the ten types of melody are two expressions of the same corrective instrument of holiness. The Torah explicitly equates them structurally: the ten Psalm chapters correspond to (כנגד) the ten types of melody enumerated in Tehillim. Both are the same holy power of song that opposes and nullifies the klipa of nocturnal emission — they are not cause-and-effect but two names for the same tikkun-instrument, one describing its external form (chapters of a book) and one its inner musical essence (types of melody).", "dominant_node": "עשרה קפיטל תהלים", "nodes": ["עשרה קפיטל תהלים", "עשרה מיני נגינה"], "node_degrees": {"עשרה קפיטל תהלים": 1, "עשרה מיני נגינה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 92, "label": "יוֹסֵף וְלֵב — הַלֵּב הַבּוֹעֵר", "label_en": "Joseph and the Heart — the Burning Heart", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "יוסף and לב share the essential nature of the hidden-burning interior of the person. The bechina is explicit: יוסף נקרא צפנת פענח (Genesis 41), and כתיב בלבי צפנתי (Psalm 119:11) — the very name 'Tzafnat Pa'aneach' means 'the one who hides/stores in the heart,' equating יוסף with לב via the root צ.פ.נ. (to hide/store). In the physiology of this Torah, the לב is not evil by nature — it is a bodily organ that contains heat (חמימות), as all hearts do. The danger is excess: 'כשבוער האש שבלב, הוה אוקיד כל גופא.' The Rebbe frames this heat not as intrinsically sinful but as the natural force of vitality that must be balanced and cooled by the lungs/Torah. יוסף, who was known for managing passion (resisting Potiphar's wife), embodies the heart that contains fire without being consumed by it. The polarity is therefore neutral — these are structural/bodily forces that can tip to harm (if the fire is uncontrolled) or to vitality (when balanced). They are not placed as 'evil' because the teaching does not condemn the heart; it simply describes what cools it.", "dominant_node": "יוסף", "nodes": ["יוסף", "לב"], "node_degrees": {"יוסף": 1, "לב": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This two-node component was evaluated for merge into Package 1 (Torah/Jacob cluster) and Package 3 (Fire/Dinin cluster).", "Merge into Package 1 rejected: The connection between ראה (Package 1) and לב is eitza — the lungs cool the heart. The two clusters are structurally opposite (lung vs. heart, cooling vs. heating, Jacob vs. Joseph), not parallel aspects of the same thing. Bechina merges require shared essential nature, not opposition.", "Merge into Package 3 rejected: Although 'כשבוער האש שבלב' links the heart to the fire/dinin (Package 3), this is again eitza-like: the heart contains the fire, but the heart is not the fire. יוסף is not a bechina of דין — his essential nature is 'hidden/stored' (צפון), not 'judgment/severity'. The relationship between לב and אש/דין is containment (the heart houses the fire), not identity.", "יוסף is designated dominant node over לב because יוסף is the named personality who anchors the bechina — the proof text works by identifying יוסף's given name (צפנת פענח) with the Psalm phrase (בלבי צפנתי). The locus of identity is יוסף; לב is the concept he is equated to."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 92, "label": "אֵשׁ וְדִינִים — הָאֵשׁ הַשּׂוֹרֶפֶת", "label_en": "Fire and Strict Judgments — the Consuming Fire", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "אש and דין share the essential nature of harsh, consuming divine severity. The bechina is explicit and precise: 'האש שהוא בחינות דינים' — the fire IS dinin (strict judgments). In Kabbalistic thought, דינים (Din, strict judgment, the Gevurah axis) is the source of all destructive and burning forces. Fire is the physical manifestation of this metaphysical severity. The Torah presents this pair as the dangerous force that threatens to consume the entire body ('אוקיד כל גופא') — the very thing that the Torah-movement of Man/Jacob must quell. This is the evil (or at minimum severely negative) counter-pole to the good cluster of Torah/Truth/Lung. The polarity is evil not because fire or even judgment is intrinsically demonic, but because in the context of this Torah they represent the force that kills and destroys, and the entire teaching is structured around overcoming them.", "dominant_node": "דין", "nodes": ["אש", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"אש": 1, "דין": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This two-node component was evaluated for merge into Package 1 (Torah/Jacob cluster) and Package 2 (Joseph/Heart cluster).", "Merge into Package 2 rejected: While the heart (Package 2) contains the fire, the fire and the heart are not the same thing in this Torah. The relationship is structural/eitza (the heart houses the fire), not identity. The shared nature of Package 2 is 'hidden/stored interior' (צפון); the shared nature of Package 3 is 'consuming severity.' These are different essential natures.", "Merge into Package 1 rejected: Fire and dinin are the exact opposite of Torah and Truth. They cannot share a package with the cooling/good cluster.", "דין is designated dominant node over אש because דין is the primary kabbalistic concept — it is the metaphysical reality; אש is its physical expression. The proof text reads 'האש שהוא בחינות דינים' — the אש is defined in terms of דין, not the reverse. דין is the deeper, more abstract, and theologically primary term."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 94, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וֶאֱלֹקֵי אָבִיךָ", "label_en": "Faith and the God of Your Father", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina-connected component in Torah #94 equates אֱמוּנָה (faith) with אֱלֹקֵי אָבִיךָ ('the God of your father'). The Rebbe derives this from the Talmudic principle 'מַעֲשֵׂה אֲבוֹתֵיהֶם בִּידֵיהֶם' (Chullin 13b) — the ancestral practice is in their hands, meaning: our faith IS the inherited tradition of believing in the God of our fathers. The proof text reads: 'וֶאֱלֹקֵי אָבִיךָ, הִיא בְחִינַת אֱמוּנָה'. The two nodes share exactly the same essential nature: trust in the God who revealed Himself to the patriarchs, the root-faith that precedes intellectual investigation. This is the good/holy domain — it is the positive foundation of the teaching. In Torah #94 the Rebbe presents אמונה as the seventh quality (השביעית), parallel to Shabbat, which is added to the six days of action (עשייה = ששת ימי החול). This framing places faith in the transcendent/holy domain.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "אלקי אביך"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 1, "אלקי אביך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 94, "volume": "lm2", "label": "רֹאשׁ — הַצַּדִּיק, רֹאשׁ הַשָּׁנָה, וְהַמֹּחַ", "label_en": "The Head — the Tzaddik, Rosh Hashanah, and the Mind", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All five nodes in this Torah share the same essential quality of 'ראש' (head/beginning/summit). The Tzaddik is explicitly called a bechina of ראש because he is ראש בני ישראל; Rosh Hashanah is a bechina of ראש because it is the beginning of the year; and the person's mind and da'at (מחו ודעתו) brought to the Tzaddik is a bechina of ראש because it resides in the head. The teaching's entire point is that these three ראשים converge when one travels to the Tzaddik on Rosh Hashanah. They are not cause and effect — they are three parallel expressions of the same essential quality of 'ראשיות' (headship/primacy). ראש בני ישראל is an epithet of הצדיק, naming the specific dimension in which he is a ראש.", "dominant_node": "הצדיק", "nodes": ["הצדיק", "ראש", "ראש בני ישראל", "ראש השנה", "מחו ודעתו"], "node_degrees": {"הצדיק": 2, "ראש": 1, "ראש בני ישראל": 2, "ראש השנה": 2, "מחו ודעתו": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 95, "label": "מָה וְאֶפֶס וָאַיִן — בְּחִינַת הַבִּיטּוּל", "label_en": "Mah, Ephes, and Ayin — The Aspect of Self-Nullification", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe presents מה (nothingness/what), אפס (zero/nothing), and אין (non-being) as a triple compound phrase for one essential state: the attitude of complete self-nullification that a leader must embody. These three terms share the same essential domain — they are all names for the experience of egolessness and divine smallness. The Rebbe uses all three in one breath: 'מַחֲזִיק אֶת עַצְמוֹ בִּבְחִינַת מָה וְאֶפֶס וָאַיִן,' establishing them as co-referential aspects of the same reality. This is the positive pole of the Torah: the state a leader should maintain. The good/evil polarity is clear — this cluster represents the ideal of humility before God (good) in contrast to the arrogance (גַּאֲוָה) that destroys leaders (evil mirror, see Package 2).", "dominant_node": "מה", "nodes": ["מה", "אפס", "אין"], "node_degrees": {"מה": 2, "אפס": 1, "אין": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_basis": "GAP_FROM_SOURCE_TEXT", "source_quote": "מַחֲזִיק אֶת עַצְמוֹ בִּבְחִינַת מָה וְאֶפֶס וָאַיִן", "dataset_edges": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 95, "label": "קֻפָּה שֶׁל שְׁרָצִים וְאֲחוֹרַיִם דְּמָ\"ה", "label_en": "The Basket of Creeping Things and the Back-Aspect of MaH", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly interprets the talmudic 'basket of creeping things hanging behind him' (Yoma 22b) as the kabbalistic 'אֲחוֹרַיִם דְּמ\"ה' from Etz Chaim (Sha'ar Arich Anpin, ch. 12). The word 'פֵּרוּשׁ' (meaning/explanation) is the Rebbe's standard indicator that two things share the same identity — the basket IS the back-of-MaH, and the back-of-MaH IS the basket. This is a genuine bechina: the talmudic image and the kabbalistic structure are two faces of the same reality. Polarity is neutral: the basket of creeping things represents the humbling mechanism — it is an instrument that can preserve a good leader or, in its absence, expose an arrogant one. The concept itself is neither holy nor evil; it is a structural device. This package is closely related to Package 1 (the state of mah/humility that results) via an eitza connection: the basket → maintaining the state of mah.", "dominant_node": "קופה של שרצים", "nodes": ["קופה של שרצים", "אחוריים דמ\"ה"], "node_degrees": {"קופה של שרצים": 1, "אחוריים דמ\"ה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קופה של שרצים", "אחוריים דמ\"ה"], "reason": "These two nodes are explicitly equated in the source text via the word 'פֵּרוּשׁ' (explanation/interpretation): 'וְזֶה פֵּרוּשׁ סוֹד שֶׁמּוּבָא בְּעֵץ הַחַיִּים... אֲחוֹרַיִם דְּמ\"ה, תָּסִיר מ\"ה נִשְׁאָר פ\"ה. פֵּרוּשׁ, עַל יְדֵי קֻפָּה שֶׁל שְׁרָצִים שֶׁתָּלוּי מֵאֲחוֹרָיו.' The Rebbe says: the meaning of the Etz Chaim secret is: through the basket hanging behind him. The basket = the back-of-MaH. These are the same concept viewed from the rabbinic domain and the kabbalistic domain respectively. This is a classic bechina: two names from two different knowledge-systems for the same underlying reality. They belong in one package. Note: this pair has only 2 nodes (below the typical threshold for an independent package), but since there are no bechina edges in the dataset for Torah #95, and these two are the only kabbalistic-structural concepts in the Torah, they form their own package here. The merge is not into Package 1 (mah/ephes/ayin) because: the basket is the CAUSE of the mah-state, not an expression OF the mah-state. The mechanism of humility shares a different nature from the experience of humility. Per framework: do not group cause-and-effect (eitza) into the same package."}], "source_basis": "GAP_FROM_SOURCE_TEXT", "source_quote": "פֵּרוּשׁ, עַל יְדֵי קֻפָּה שֶׁל שְׁרָצִים שֶׁתָּלוּי מֵאֲחוֹרָיו, עַל יְדֵי זֶה מַחֲזִיק אֶת עַצְמוֹ בִּבְחִינַת מָה", "dataset_edges": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 95, "label": "גַּאֲוָה וּפֶה — הַמַּנְהִיג הַמִּתְגָּאֶה", "label_en": "Arrogance and the Mouth — The Leader Who Becomes Proud", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "When the leader removes מה from himself — when he stops holding himself as nothing and becomes arrogant — what remains is פה (mouth). The פה here is the mouth of the public who shame and disgrace him: 'חֲזֹר לַאֲחוֹרֶיךָ, וּמְבַזִּים אוֹתוֹ.' The Rebbe constructs this as an IS-statement: 'אֲזַי נִשְׁאָר פֶּה, הַיְנוּ שֶׁבְּנֵי אָדָם פּוֹתְחִים אֶת פִּיהֶם' — 'then PeH remains, MEANING people open their mouths.' The word הַיְנוּ (meaning) is a bechina-identifier: the remaining-PeH IS the opening of mouths against him. This is the evil mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 (good) = the leader in the state of mah, Package 3 (evil) = the leader in the state of gaava, whose elevation produces his public disgrace. The components of this package are: גַּאֲוָה (arrogance, the sin), פֶּה (the mouth that opens against him), and בִּזָּיוֹן (disgrace, the outcome that the mouth produces). Note: the connection between גאוה and פה is a bechina — 'when mah is removed, peh IS what remains;' not merely a consequence. The peh is not caused by the gaava; the peh IS the reality-of-gaava expressed in the public sphere.", "dominant_node": "פה", "nodes": ["פה", "גאוה", "בזיון"], "node_degrees": {"פה": 2, "גאוה": 1, "בזיון": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_basis": "GAP_FROM_SOURCE_TEXT", "source_quote": "וּכְשֶׁמֵּסִיר מִמֶּנּוּ מָה, שֶׁאֵין מַחֲזִיק אֶת עַצְמוֹ לְמָה, אֶלָּא שֶׁהוּא מִתְגָּאֶה, אֲזַי נִשְׁאָר פֶּה, הַיְנוּ שֶׁבְּנֵי אָדָם פּוֹתְחִים אֶת פִּיהֶם וְאוֹמְרִים חֲזֹר לַאֲחוֹרֶיךָ, וּמְבַזִּים אוֹתוֹ", "dataset_edges": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 96, "label": "מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה וּמַחֲלֹקֶת — הַכֹּחַ הָרָע", "label_en": "Foreign Thought and Conflict — The Evil Force", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package collects all five nodes of the large connected component — the cluster of concepts that the Torah equates with the forces opposing the tzaddik's prayer. The central bechina chain is: (1) 'זֹמֵם רָשָׁע – הַיְנוּ מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה': the wicked plotter IS the machshava zara (foreign/distracting thought during prayer); (2) 'חֶרֶב פָּתְחוּ רְשָׁעִים – לְשׁוֹן מִלְחָמָה, הַיְנוּ שֶׁנִּתְעוֹרֵר מַחֲלֹקֶת': the sword IS conflict/controversy (machloket) against a tzaddik; (3) 'חַרְבָּם – הַיְנוּ הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה שֶׁל אוֹתָהּ הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת': their sword (חרבם) IS the machshava zara that belongs to that conflict; (4) 'וְקַשְּׁתוֹתָם תִּשָּׁבַרְנָה – כְּלוֹמַר, הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת הַנַּ\"ל': their bows (קשתותם) IS the machloket that was mentioned. The bechina chain thus makes all five nodes expressions of the same fundamental spiritual reality: the evil force that opposes the tzaddik's spiritual work takes the form of machshava zara (when experienced internally during prayer) and machloket (when experienced externally as controversy from enemies). They are the same phenomenon at different levels — the inner disruption (machshava zara) and the outer controversy (machloket) are both bechina expressions of the evil's weapon against the tzaddik. The verse's imagery — sword, bow, wicked plotter — are all different facets of this one evil spiritual reality. This is the evil mirror of Package 2 (the tzaddik's spiritual world): where Package 2 describes the tzaddik's inner capacity and struggle, this package describes the opposing force he faces.", "dominant_node": "מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה", "nodes": ["מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה", "זֹמֵם רָשָׁע", "חֶרֶב", "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "קַשְּׁתוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה": 2, "זֹמֵם רָשָׁע": 1, "חֶרֶב": 2, "מַחֲלֹקֶת": 2, "קַשְּׁתוֹת": 1}, "source_bechina_edges": [{"node1": "זֹמֵם רָשָׁע", "node2": "מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה", "proof": "זֹמֵם רָשָׁע – הַיְנוּ מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה", "verse": "תהילים לז:יב"}, {"node1": "חֶרֶב", "node2": "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "proof": "חֶרֶב פָּתְחוּ רְשָׁעִים, וּפֵרֵשׁ רַשִׁ\"י: חֶרֶב – לְשׁוֹן מִלְחָמָה, הַיְנוּ שֶׁנִּתְעוֹרֵר מַחֲלֹקֶת עַל אֵיזֶה צַדִּיק", "verse": "תהילים לז:יד"}, {"node1": "חֶרֶב", "node2": "מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה", "proof": "חַרְבָּם – הַיְנוּ הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה שֶׁל אוֹתָהּ הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת", "verse": "תהילים לז:טו", "note": "חרבם is the possessive form of חרב — the same node with a possessive suffix, identifying the sword (= machshava zara) as belonging to the conflict"}, {"node1": "קַשְּׁתוֹת", "node2": "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "proof": "וְקַשְּׁתוֹתָם תִּשָּׁבַרְנָה; כְּלוֹמַר, הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת הַנַּ\"ל", "verse": "תהילים לז:טו"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 96, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק — לִבּוֹ, רְצוֹנוֹ, וּמַדְרֵגָתוֹ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik — His Heart, His Will, and His Spiritual Level", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package is formed by merging three small (2-node) connected components, all of which share the essential nature of 'the tzaddik's inner spiritual world as the arena of his struggle with the machshava zara.' The three components are: (A) לִבָּם ↔ לֵב הַצַּדִּיק — 'תָּבוֹא בְלִבָּם – הַיְנוּ בְּלֵב צַדִּיק זֶה': the 'their heart' of the verse IS the heart of this tzaddik. The lev (heart) is the sacred vessel of tefilla and the place where the machshava zara penetrates. It belongs to the tzaddik's positive domain — his heart is the seat of prayer and spiritual work, not an evil concept; (B) חֹרֵק שִׁנַּיִם ↔ רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת — 'וְחֹרֵק עָלָיו שִׁנָּיו – הַיְנוּ שֶׁרוֹצֶה לְהַעֲלוֹתָהּ': the gnashing of teeth IS the tzaddik's desire/will to elevate the machshava zara back to its source. This is an explicitly positive act — the Rebbe teaches that even without succeeding, this רָצוֹן (will) alone breaks all the baalei machloket; (C) יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ ↔ מַדְרֵגָה — 'וַה' יִשְׂחַק לוֹ, כִּי רָאָה כִּי יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ – הַיְנוּ שֶׁעֲדַיִן לֹא בָּא לְמַדְרֵגַת הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה זוֹ': the 'day that will come' IS the spiritual level (madrega) of that machshava zara — i.e., the level to which the tzaddik must eventually ascend. All three components describe the tzaddik's inner spiritual reality: his heart (vessel), his will (response), and his spiritual level (destination). They are three facets of the same essential reality — the tzaddik's capacity to engage the spiritual battle. The merge is grounded in bechina (shared nature): the heart, the will, and the level all describe the tzaddik's spiritual self during tefilla. They are not connected to each other by eitza in this Torah — the will does not produce the level, nor does the heart produce the will. They are parallel aspects of who the tzaddik IS in this moment of prayer. CRITICAL MERGE NOTE: The three components were merged into one package rather than left as three isolated 2-node packages because: (1) they all share the same polarity (good/tzaddik-positive), (2) they all describe the same subject (the tzaddik in prayer), and (3) no larger component with the same polarity exists to absorb them individually.", "dominant_node": "רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת", "nodes": ["לִבָּם", "לֵב הַצַּדִּיק", "חֹרֵק שִׁנַּיִם", "רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת", "יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ", "מַדְרֵגָה"], "node_degrees": {"לִבָּם": 1, "לֵב הַצַּדִּיק": 1, "חֹרֵק שִׁנַּיִם": 1, "רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת": 1, "יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ": 1, "מַדְרֵגָה": 1}, "source_bechina_edges": [{"node1": "לִבָּם", "node2": "לֵב הַצַּדִּיק", "proof": "תָּבוֹא בְלִבָּם – הַיְנוּ בְּלֵב צַדִּיק זֶה", "verse": "תהילים לז:טו", "note": "The verse says 'their heart' (לבם) = the heart of the tzaddik who is the subject of the teaching. The enemy's heart and the tzaddik's heart are equated as the venue — the machine zara enters the tzaddik's heart, so 'their heart' is identified as 'the heart of this tzaddik.'"}, {"node1": "חֹרֵק שִׁנַּיִם", "node2": "רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת", "proof": "וְחֹרֵק עָלָיו שִׁנָּיו – הַיְנוּ שֶׁרוֹצֶה לְהַעֲלוֹתָהּ", "verse": "תהילים לז:יב", "note": "The verse speaks of the wicked gnashing his teeth; Rebbe Nachman re-reads the subject as the tzaddik grinding his teeth in the effort to elevate the machshava zara. The gnashing IS the will/desire to elevate."}, {"node1": "יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ", "node2": "מַדְרֵגָה", "proof": "וַה' יִשְׂחַק לוֹ, כִּי רָאָה כִּי יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ – הַיְנוּ שֶׁעֲדַיִן לֹא בָּא לְמַדְרֵגַת הַמַּחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה זוֹ", "verse": "תהילים לז:יג", "note": "God's 'seeing that his day would come' is interpreted as: the tzaddik has not yet reached the spiritual level (madrega) from which this machshava zara descended. 'His day' = the madrega/spiritual level that awaits him."}], "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "לִבָּם ↔ לֵב הַצַּדִּיק", "original_component_size": 2, "merged_into": "Package 2", "reason": "The lev (heart) of the tzaddik is the vessel of prayer and the location where the machshava zara enters. It belongs to the tzaddik's inner world, not to the evil force cluster. The lev is a sacred concept (lev = makon of tefilla) and its polarity is good/neutral. It cannot merge into Package 1 (evil) because the tzaddik's heart is the antithesis of the evil force. It merges with Package 2 because all three small components describe the tzaddik as subject: his heart, his will, his level.", "merge_basis": "shared nature (bechina): the lev, the ratzon, and the madrega are all facets of the tzaddik's inner spiritual identity"}, {"merged_component": "חֹרֵק שִׁנַּיִם ↔ רָצוֹן לְהַעֲלוֹת", "original_component_size": 2, "merged_into": "Package 2", "reason": "The will to elevate (ratzon lehaalot) is the tzaddik's positive spiritual response — a sacred act of will. The Rebbe teaches that this ratzon alone, even without success, breaks the power of the machloket. This is explicitly a good-polarity concept. It merges with Package 2 because it shares the essential nature of the tzaddik's active spiritual capacity. It cannot merge with Package 1 because the ratzon is the tzaddik's response to the evil, not a component of the evil itself.", "merge_basis": "shared nature (bechina): the will to elevate IS the tzaddik's spiritual effort, same domain as his heart and his madrega"}, {"merged_component": "יָבֹא יוֹמוֹ ↔ מַדְרֵגָה", "original_component_size": 2, "merged_into": "Package 2", "reason": "The madrega (spiritual level) is the structural framework of the tzaddik's ascent. The 'day that will come' is the tzaddik's future spiritual level — a positive concept of growth and ascent. The madrega is where the tzaddik is going, and also the origin of the machshava zara that challenges him. In both framings, the madrega is the tzaddik's domain — his present station and his future destination. It merges into Package 2 as the structural/level dimension of the tzaddik's spiritual world.", "merge_basis": "shared nature (bechina): the madrega IS the measure of the tzaddik's spiritual standing — same domain as his heart and his will"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 97, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּמַלְכוּת", "label_en": "Prayer and Kingship", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest bechina cluster in Torah #97 unifies all concepts whose essential nature is 'prayer that has sovereignty' (ממשלה לתפלה). The Rebbe states in line 10: 'וְהַיִּרְאָה נִקְרֵאת מַלְכוּת' — fear/awe IS malkhut. Then: 'הַתְּפִלָּה יֵשׁ לָהּ בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת' — prayer itself shares the bechina of malkhut. In line 15, יְהוּדָה is declared 'הַיְנוּ הַתְּפִלָּה' (= prayer). In line 16, מְחוֹקְקִי is glossed as 'לְשׁוֹן שְׂרָרָה וּמַלְכוּת' (language of dominion and malkhut). All four concepts — prayer, awe/fear, Yehudah (the one who praises), and the lawgiver-as-sovereign — share the single essential nature of 'sovereign prayer': prayer that rules because it is imbued with Malkhut. The two bechinot of Menashe and Efraim (small components C and D) are merged here because the Rebbe says 'כְּשֶׁמִּתְפַּלֵּל בִּשְׁתֵּי בְּחִינוֹת אֵלּוּ' — when one prays WITH these two bechinot, meaning Menashe and Efraim describe the inner posture of the prayer itself, not its external cause. They are faces of the same domain: prayer conducted with the proper inner quality. Forgetting one's status (Menashe) and transforming foreign thoughts (Efraim) are two aspects of what prayer-with-malkhut essentially IS, not what merely produces it.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "יראה", "מלכות", "יהודה", "מחוקקי", "מנשה", "שכחה", "אפרים"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 3, "תפלה": 2, "יראה": 2, "יהודה": 1, "מחוקקי": 1, "מנשה": 1, "שכחה": 1, "אפרים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מנשה", "שכחה"], "reason": "Line 6: 'וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינַת מְנַשֶּׁה, לְשׁוֹן נִשְׁיוֹן וְשִׁכְחָה' — Menashe IS forgetting. These two nodes form a bechina pair. The Rebbe is describing the state required BEFORE prayer: forget one's yichus and toil so that prayer is not blocked by pride. The proof quote 'כִּי נַשַּׁנִי אֱלֹקִים אֶת כָּל בֵּית אָבִי' describes forgetting one's lineage (= yichus = gadlut). This is a description of the inner nature of prayer — the prayer conducted with Menashe-quality IS the prayer that has malkhut. It is not an eitza (forgetting does not merely produce prayer; forgetting IS the form of prayer). Merged into Package 1."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אפרים"], "reason": "Line 8: 'וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינַת אֶפְרַיִם, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: כִּי הִפְרַנִי אֱלֹקִים בְּאֶרֶץ עָנְיִי' — Efraim is the bechina of fruitfulness from poverty/dryness. During prayer, foreign thoughts (from previous intentional sins) fall upon a person. The act of breaking them via holy thoughts transforms the dry, barren matter into merit. This transformation IS the bechina of Efraim — it is not a result of prayer; it is a description of what prayer essentially does in this condition. Efraim therefore shares the essential nature of prayer-as-malkhut (prayer that rules over foreign thoughts). Merged into Package 1 as the second inner posture of sovereign prayer."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 97, "label": "שֶׁפַע וְאוֹתִיּוֹת הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "Divine Flow and the Letters of the Name", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package unifies all concepts whose essential nature is 'the divine flow of abundance as expressed through the letters of the divine Names.' The Rebbe builds an intricate kabbalistic structure: (a) הַשֶּׁפַע נִקְרֵאת ה' (line 2) — the shefa IS the letter Heh, confirmed in line 23 'ה' הִיא הַשֶּׁפַע'; (b) the letter Heh in its three written forms has a gematria equaling חָלָב (milk) — line 23; (c) the letters א\"ל (= the word El, composed of Alef-Lamed) ARE דָּד (nipple/source of nourishment — line 2 and restated in line 22); (d) similarly מ\"י (the two letters Mem-Yod) ARE דָּד; (e) עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת (the Ten Utterances of Creation) ARE the letter יוּ\"ד (line 17: 'עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת הַנַּ\"ל הֵם יוּ\"ד'); (f) חֶסֶד IS called אֵל (line 18: 'חֶסֶד נִקְרָא אֵל'), linking Chesed to the א\"ל cluster. All these concepts share the same essential domain: divine shefa as it descends through the letter-structure of Elkim/El. The letter Heh = shefa = milk (chalav), and the letters of the Name El and Mi each = dad (nipple). The shefa-as-nurture metaphor (milk, nipple/dad) runs through all nodes, binding them into one conceptual family. This is also the cosmic frame: shefa is what prayer restores (when ה' becomes ד' through sin, prayer must restore it to ה').", "dominant_node": "שפע", "nodes": ["שפע", "ה' (אות)", "חלב", "א\"ל (אותיות)", "מ\"י (אותיות)", "דד", "עשרה מאמרות", "יו\"ד (אות)", "חסד", "אל"], "node_degrees": {"שפע": 2, "ה' (אות)": 3, "דד": 2, "א\"ל (אותיות)": 2, "מ\"י (אותיות)": 2, "חסד": 1, "אל": 2, "עשרה מאמרות": 1, "יו\"ד (אות)": 1, "חלב": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חסד", "אל"], "reason": "Line 18: 'חֶסֶד נִקְרָא אֵל, שֶׁנֶּאֱמַר: חֶסֶד אֵל כָּל הַיּוֹם.' Chesed IS called El. El in this Torah is identified with the two letters Alef-Lamed (= א\"ל = dad). The Rebbe's conclusion in line 21-22 is: awaken the bechina of 'El' through prayer — and 'א\"ל בְּחִינַת דַּד.' Chesed-El-dad form a single conceptual node: the loving, nourishing divine attribute that flows as shefa. They are merged with the main shefa/letters cluster because all share the same essential nature (the divine nurturing flow) and because the Rebbe himself connects them via niqra and the proof texts."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עשרה מאמרות", "יו\"ד (אות)"], "reason": "Line 17: 'וַעֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת הַנַּ\"ל הֵם יוּ\"ד' — the Ten Utterances of Creation ARE the letter Yud. And then: 'יוּ\"ד וְד' הַנַּ\"ל — נַעֲשֶׂה ה'' — Yud + Dalet = Heh (the letter Heh = shefa). This bechina-pair connects directly into the main shefa/letter cluster: Ten Utterances = Yud; Yud + Dalet = Heh = shefa. The chain is clear: prayer activates chesed (El), which flows as shefa (Heh), sustained by the Ten Utterances (Yud), which through renewal re-creates the world. All share the nature of 'divine creative speech as the vessel of shefa.'"}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 97, "label": "גִּלְעָד וְהַתַּעֲנוּג הָאֱלֹקִי", "label_en": "Gilad and the Divine Pleasure", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Line 13 states: 'לִי גִלְעָד – לְשׁוֹן גַּל עֵד, שֶׁנִּתְגַּלָּה הַתַּעֲנוּג שֶׁהָיָה לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ קֹדֶם בְּרִיאַת הָעוֹלָם, שֶׁעִדֵּן וְקִשֵּׁט בִּתְפִלָּתָן שֶׁל צַדִּיקִים.' Gilad (גִּלְעָד) is etymologically decoded as gal-ed (גַּל עֵד = witness-heap), meaning the revelation of the divine pleasure (taanug) that existed before creation. This taanug was God's 'adorning Himself' with the prayers and good deeds of the righteous that He foresaw. The word Gilad thus IS the revealed divine taanug. These two nodes (Gilad and Taanug) form a bechina pair that represents a distinct thematic domain: the cosmic dimension of prayer — namely that prayer is not just personally transformative but cosmically restorative of the pre-creation divine pleasure. This is conceptually distinct from Package 1 (what prayer IS for the one praying) and Package 2 (the kabbalistic mechanics of shefa). The Taanug domain describes WHY the world was created and what prayer re-activates — a unique metaphysical dimension of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "גלעד", "nodes": ["גלעד", "תענוג"], "node_degrees": {"גלעד": 1, "תענוג": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 98, "label": "עֵינֵי הַצַּדִּיק וּרְאִיַּת הַפְּגָם", "label_en": "The Tzaddik's Eyes and Seeing the Damage of Sin", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central cluster of Torah #98 orbits the concept of visionary perception belonging to the Tzaddik. The Rebbe establishes that the Tzaddik possesses 'עֵינֵי ה'' (the eyes of God), citing the verse 'עֵינֵי ה' אֶל צַדִּיקִים'. These 'eyes of God' are further defined by the verse 'וְעֵינֵי ה' הֵמָּה מְשׁוֹטְטוֹת בְּכָל הָאָרֶץ' — they roam the entire earth and therefore see the full extent of any sin's damage. The Tzaddik, possessing these eyes, can see 'עַד הֵיכָן הַפְּגָם מַגִּיעַ' (how far the damage reaches). The Gemara phrase 'נָתַן בּוֹ עֵינָיו' is re-read as: the sinner comes to see through the eyes of the Tzaddik. 'גַּל' is decoded as 'הִתְגַּלּוּת' (revelation) — the revelation of what was hidden. These concepts all share the same essential nature: the domain of divine, penetrating, Torah-vision that reveals hidden spiritual reality. This is the positive pole of the teaching — the power of the Tzaddik's gaze to reveal truth.", "dominant_node": "עיני ה' (עיני הצדיק)", "nodes": ["עיני ה'", "הצדיק", "עיני הצדיק", "ראיה עד היכן הפגם מגיע", "נתן עיניו בו (מבט הצדיק)", "גל (התגלות)"], "node_degrees": {"עיני ה'": 3, "הצדיק": 3, "עיני הצדיק": 2, "ראיה עד היכן הפגם מגיע": 2, "נתן עיניו בו (מבט הצדיק)": 1, "גל (התגלות)": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 98, "label": "הַפְּגָם הַנִּסְתָּר וְהָעֹנֶשׁ שֶׁל הָרְאִיָּה", "label_en": "The Hidden Damage of Sin and the Punishment of Seeing", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The second conceptual cluster in Torah #98 contains the evil-side of the teaching: the sinner (אָדָם who transgresses), his inability to see the full extent of his own spiritual damage (הַפְּגָם), the concept of concealment (נִסְתָּר — what was hidden from him), and the phrase 'עֲצָמוֹת' decoded via 'עוֹצֵם עֵינָיו מֵרְאוֹת בְּרָע' — the closing of eyes from evil, which paradoxically becomes the greatest punishment when those eyes are forced open. The Rebbe says there is no greater punishment than when a person sees what he has damaged ('אֵין עֹנֶשׁ גָּדוֹל מִזֶּה, כְּשֶׁאָדָם רוֹאֶה מַה שֶּׁפָּגַם'). The 'גַּל שֶׁל עֲצָמוֹת' in the negative reading is the revelation of the sinner's own spiritual ruin to himself. These nodes share the nature of concealed spiritual damage, transgression, and the consequences of sin. This is the evil mirror to the Tzaddik's divine vision in Package 1: where the Tzaddik's eyes reveal, the sinner's eyes are sealed — until the moment of forced revelation which is the punishment itself.", "dominant_node": "הפגם (עד היכן הפגם מגיע)", "nodes": ["הפגם", "עובר עברה", "נסתר ממנו (לא ראה)", "עצמות (עוצם עיניו מראות ברע)", "גל של עצמות (עונש הראיה)"], "node_degrees": {"הפגם": 3, "עובר עברה": 2, "נסתר ממנו (לא ראה)": 2, "עצמות (עוצם עיניו מראות ברע)": 2, "גל של עצמות (עונש הראיה)": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 99, "label": "תְּפִלָּה בִּדְבֵקוּת וּשְׁגִירַת הַפֶּה", "label_en": "Prayer with Devekut and Fluency of the Mouth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina equation in Torah #99 links two expressions of the same spiritual quality: prayer conducted with devekut (attachment to God) and the experience of prayer being שְׁגוּרָה בְּפִיו — fluent, flowing, and easily articulated in one's mouth. The Rebbe cites Rabbi Chanina ben Dosa's rule (Berakhot 34b): when his prayer was fluent in his mouth he knew it was accepted; when not, he knew it was not. The Rebbe interprets this as a single bechina: fluency of prayer IS devekut, and devekut IS fluency of prayer. They are two faces of the same inner state — the condition in which the soul's attachment to God expresses itself as words that pour forth naturally and freely from the mouth. Neither produces the other; they are the same reality seen from two angles: the inner spiritual state (devekut) and its outer linguistic expression (shlimut/shgura). Both nodes are positive, belonging to the domain of authentic prayer and cleaving to God.", "dominant_node": "תפלה בדבקות", "nodes": ["תפלה בדבקות", "שגורה תפלתי בפי"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה בדבקות": 1, "שגורה תפלתי בפי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 101, "label": "אַנְפִּין נְהִירִין", "label_en": "Luminous Faces — Torah Intellect and Holiness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire positive teaching of Torah #101 orbits a single concept: אנפין נהירין (luminous faces), which the Rebbe identifies as Torah knowledge, the divine intellect, and the holiness of Israel. The word 'אנפין' (Aramaic for 'faces/aspects') plus 'נהירין' (illuminated) describes what happens when a person grasps the wisdom of Torah: they attain the state of בְּחִינַת אנפין נהירין. Everything in this package is a face or expression of that same luminous-intellect reality. The three cognitive sekhel-faculties — חכמה (raw intellect/initial flash of wisdom), בינה (understanding/learning and comprehending), and דעת (knowledge/knowing Torah fully) — are three stages of the same luminous-intellect event. They are called 'ידים לתורה' (hands for receiving Torah), which are the three hands of the verse: יד הגדולה, יד החזקה, יד הרמה. The three faculties together constitute שלש פעמים י\"ק = gematria of אדם, which is why 'אַתֶּם קְרוּיִין אָדָם' — it is Torah-intellect that defines a human being. Torah itself is a bechina of י\"ה (the Yud-Heh of the Divine Name = chokhmah + binah), and the five books of Torah are the five articulatory positions of the mouth. חכמות התורה (the wisdom-dimensions of Torah) is the content of י\"ה. תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין (the two inseparable companions — chokhmah and binah) are the same pair. מה שאדם משיג בשכלו (what a person grasps with his intellect) is directly identified as אנפין נהירין. ידיעת והשגת התורה (Torah knowledge and attainment) is named as the content of אנפין נהירין. קדשת ישראל עם קדוש (the holiness of Israel as a holy people) is declared a bechina of אנפין נהירין — because Israel's root-nature is removed from desires and bad traits. All these nodes share the same essential nature: luminous intellect, Torah comprehension, divine image (צֶלֶם אֱלֹקִים realized through knowing Torah). The good/evil mirror for this package is Package 2 (אנפין חשוכין).", "dominant_node": "אנפין נהירין", "nodes": ["אנפין נהירין", "מה שאדם מבין ומשיג בשכלו", "תורה", "י\"ה", "חכמות התורה", "תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין", "ידיעת והשגת התורה", "קדשת ישראל עם קדוש", "שכל פשוט", "חכמה", "כשלומד ומבין", "בינה", "כשיודע התורה", "דעת", "חמשה חמשי תורה", "חמשת מוצאות הפה", "שלש שכליות אלו", "ידים לתורה", "יד הגדולה ויד החזקה ויד הרמה", "אלו השלשה שכליים", "שלש פעמים י\"ק"], "node_degrees": {"אנפין נהירין": 4, "תורה": 2, "י\"ה": 3, "ידים לתורה": 2, "מה שאדם מבין ומשיג בשכלו": 1, "חכמות התורה": 1, "תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין": 1, "ידיעת והשגת התורה": 1, "קדשת ישראל עם קדוש": 1, "שכל פשוט": 1, "חכמה": 1, "כשלומד ומבין": 1, "בינה": 1, "כשיודע התורה": 1, "דעת": 1, "חמשה חמשי תורה": 1, "חמשת מוצאות הפה": 1, "שלש שכליות אלו": 1, "יד הגדולה ויד החזקה ויד הרמה": 1, "אלו השלשה שכליים": 1, "שלש פעמים י\"ק": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component 3 — {שכל פשוט, חכמה}", "source_edge": "Edge 1146: שכל פשוט ↔ חכמה (proof: שֵׂכֶל פָּשׁוּט, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת חָכְמָה)", "reason": "The Torah explicitly equates שכל פשוט (primary/raw intellect) with חכמה (the first sefira of intellect). This is Stage 1 of the three-stage intellect described in Torah #101 (Section 15: 'שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת חָכְמָה, בְּחִינַת: כֻּלָּם בְּחָכְמָה עָשִׂיתָ'). חכמה is itself a face of the same luminous-intellect reality that constitutes אנפין נהירין — the Torah says 'חָכְמַת אָדָם תָּאִיר פָּנָיו', i.e., wisdom illuminates the face, which IS אנפין נהירין. Merge into Package 1."}, {"merged_component": "Component 4 — {כשלומד ומבין, בינה}", "source_edge": "Edge 525: כשלומד ומבין ↔ בינה (proof: וּכְשֶׁלּוֹמֵד וּמֵבִין, זֶה נִקְרָא בִּינָה)", "reason": "Stage 2 of the three-stage intellect. בינה is the second faculty: when you study and understand, that act of comprehension is called בינה. The Torah (Section 15) lists it as the second of the three sekhel-faculties. בינה is itself part of י\"ה (Yud-Heh = chokhmah + binah, the inseparable pair), and the entire package centers on the intellect that produces אנפין נהירין. This small component belongs with the other intellect-nodes in Package 1."}, {"merged_component": "Component 5 — {כשיודע התורה, דעת}", "source_edge": "Edge 759: כשיודע התורה ↔ דעת (proof: כְּשֶׁיּוֹדֵעַ הַתּוֹרָה, זֶה נִקְרָא דַּעַת)", "reason": "Stage 3 of the three-stage intellect. When one fully knows Torah — that is called דעת. The Torah (Section 15) explicitly names all three stages together: חכמה, בינה, דעת as the 'three intellectuals' that are 'three times יק = Adam'. This triplet is the backbone of the positive teaching. Merge into Package 1."}, {"merged_component": "Component 6 — {חמשה חמשי תורה, חמשת מוצאות הפה}", "source_edge": "Edge 1191: חמשה חמשי תורה ↔ חמשת מוצאות הפה (proof: חֲמִשָּׁה חֻמְּשֵׁי תּוֹרָה, בְחִינַת חֲמֵשֶׁת מוֹצָאוֹת הַפֶּה)", "reason": "The five books of Torah are a bechina of the five articulatory positions of the mouth (guttural, palatal, lingual, dental, labial). This bechina identifies Torah as the verbalized intellect — speech given form through mouth-positions. In Torah #101 (Section 3), the Heh of the Divine Name (הֵא הוּא אוֹתִיּוֹת שֶׁל הַתּוֹרָה, שֶׁהוּא חֲמִשָּׁה חֻמְּשֵׁי תּוֹרָה בְּחִינַת חֲמֵשֶׁת מוֹצָאוֹת הַפֶּה) — the Heh = Torah = five books = five mouth-positions. This belongs with Package 1 because Torah is the central bechina of אנפין נהירין in this teaching. The letters of Torah are the very substance of luminous-face reality."}, {"merged_component": "Component 7 — {שלש שכליות אלו, ידים לתורה, יד הגדולה ויד החזקה ויד הרמה}", "source_edges": "Edge 1256: שלש שכליות אלו ↔ ידים לתורה; Edge 1246: ידים לתורה ↔ יד הגדולה ויד החזקה ויד הרמה", "reason": "This 3-node chain expresses that the three intellect-stages (chokhmah, binah, daat) serve as 'hands for receiving Torah' — metaphorically the three extended hands by which Torah is grasped and held. This is an aspect of the same intellectual-receptivity domain as אנפין נהירין. The Torah (Section 15) says explicitly: 'שָׁלֹשׁ שִׂכְלִיּוּת אֵלּוּ, הֵם בְחִינַת יָדַיִם לַתּוֹרָה, בְּחִינַת יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה וְיָד הַחֲזָקָה וְיָד הָרָמָה, שֶׁבָּהֶם מִתְקַבֶּלֶת הַתּוֹרָה'. All three concepts share the nature: 'the intellect that receives Torah.' Merge into Package 1."}, {"merged_component": "Component 10 — {אלו השלשה שכליים, שלש פעמים י\"ק}", "source_edge": "Edge 2953: אלו השלשה שכליים ↔ שלש פעמים י\"ק (proof: אֵלּוּ הַשְּׁלֹשָׁה שִׂכְלִיִּים, הֵם בְחִינַת שָׁלֹשׁ פְּעָמִים י\"ק... שָׁלֹשׁ פְּעָמִים י\"ק הוּא גִּימַטְרִיָּא אָדָם)", "reason": "The three intellectual stages (חכמה, בינה, דעת) are equated with three repetitions of יק (Yud-Heh, the Divine Name fragment that represents intellect). Three times 26 (actually יק = 15, three times 15 = 45 = אדם in gematria). This bechina directly links the three-intellect structure to the cosmic number אדם, making it the summit of the positive teaching. It is an elaboration of the same luminous-intellect cluster. Merge into Package 1."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 101, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וּפָרֵשׁ שִׂיחָה", "label_en": "Prayer and Expressing One's Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the same essential nature: they are modes of a person speaking directly and personally to God, expressing the fullness of one's heart. The Torah explicitly equates Tikkun Chatzot with hitbodedut ('like hitbodedut'), and groups Tehillim, supplications, requests, slichot, and hitbodedut as parallel expressions of the same root practice — פָּרֵשׁ שִׂיחָתוֹ בֵּינוֹ לְבֵין קוֹנוֹ (expressing one's speech between oneself and one's Maker). The Torah states these are the one foundation and root of all advice for drawing close to God, signaling they share the same essential dimension.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תקון חצות", "התבודדות", "אמירת תהלים", "תחנות ובקשות", "תפלה", "פרש שיחתו", "חיזוק בתפלה"], "node_degrees": {"תקון חצות": 2, "התבודדות": 3, "אמירת תהלים": 3, "תחנות ובקשות": 3, "תפלה": 4, "פרש שיחתו": 2, "חיזוק בתפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 101, "label": "אַנְפִּין חֲשׁוּכִין", "label_en": "Dark Faces — Passions, Bad Traits, and Subjugation", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The negative pole of Torah #101 is equally unified: everything converges on אנפין חשוכין (dark/obscured faces), which is the domain of what is not grasped by Torah-intellect. The Rebbe identifies: what one does not comprehend (מה שאינו משיג) as a bechina of אנפין חשוכין; the seventy nations (שבעים עכו\"ם) as a bechina of אנפין חשוכין (because they grasp only the dark-face domains of passion and bad traits); concealment of knowledge (חשכות והסתרת הידיעה) as a bechina of אנפין חשוכין; and desires and bad traits (תאוות ומדות רעות) as a bechina of אנפין חשוכין. The burdens of exile — physical subjugation (שעבוד בגשמיות = על מלכות) and bad traits of the nations (מדות רעות של העכו\"ם = על דרך ארץ) — are the concrete historical manifestation of the same dark-face domain operating in the world. They describe what falls upon someone when they abandon Torah (the 'ol Torah) and thereby come under the dominion of אנפין חשוכין. These are not separate domains but specific expressions of the same fundamental reality: the domain of passion, ignorance, and foreign domination that opposes Torah-intellect. The evil mirror for Package 2 (dark faces) is Package 1 (luminous faces) — the same spectrum of reality, aligned opposite ways. The 70 nations are the cosmic dimension of dark-faces; שעבוד and מדות רעות are the individual and national manifestations.", "dominant_node": "אנפין חשוכין", "nodes": ["אנפין חשוכין", "מה שאינו משיג", "שבעים עכו\"ם", "חשכות והסתרת הידיעה", "תאוות ומדות רעות", "מדות רעות של העכו\"ם", "על דרך ארץ", "שעבוד בגשמיות", "על מלכות"], "node_degrees": {"אנפין חשוכין": 4, "מה שאינו משיג": 1, "שבעים עכו\"ם": 1, "חשכות והסתרת הידיעה": 1, "תאוות ומדות רעות": 1, "מדות רעות של העכו\"ם": 1, "על דרך ארץ": 1, "שעבוד בגשמיות": 1, "על מלכות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_component": "Component 8 — {מדות רעות של העכו\"ם, על דרך ארץ}", "source_edge": "Edge 2390: מדות רעות של העכו\"ם ↔ על דרך ארץ (proof: הַמִּדּוֹת רָעוֹת שֶׁל הָעַכּוּ\"ם, שֶׁזֶּהוּ בְחִינַת עֹל דֶּרֶךְ אֶרֶץ)", "reason": "The bad traits of the nations ARE identified as the 'yoke of earth-ways' (על דרך ארץ). The source text (Section 7) explains: 'וְגַם דֶּרֶךְ אַרְצִיּוּת, דְּהַיְנוּ הַמִּדּוֹת רָעוֹת שֶׁל הָעַכּוּ\"ם, שֶׁזֶּהוּ בְּחִינַת עֹל דֶּרֶךְ אֶרֶץ'. This pair expresses the same reality as שבעים עכו\"ם and תאוות ומדות רעות in Component 2 — both are faces of the dark-face domain. Merge into Package 2."}, {"merged_component": "Component 9 — {שעבוד בגשמיות, על מלכות}", "source_edge": "Edge 2392: שעבוד בגשמיות ↔ על מלכות (proof: שִׁעְבּוּד בְּגַשְׁמִיּוּת, שֶׁזֶּהוּ בְחִינַת עֹל מַלְכוּת)", "reason": "Physical subjugation to foreign rule (שעבוד בגשמיות) is identified as the 'yoke of kingship' (על מלכות). The source text (Section 7) explains that when someone abandons the yoke of Torah (which IS אנפין נהירין), the yoke of foreign dominion (אנפין חשוכין in its political expression) takes over. שעבוד בגשמיות is the outer, political manifestation of the dark-face domain's dominion. It belongs with אנפין חשוכין, whose defining feature is the lack of Torah-intellect and the resulting grip of the nations' reality over the individual. Merge into Package 2."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 101, "volume": "lm2", "label": "יֵצֶר הָרָע וּמִלְחַמְתּוֹ", "label_en": "The Evil Inclination and Its Warfare", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: they are all expressions of the opposing force that seeks to prevent a person from drawing close to God. The Torah identifies the yetzer hara and its armies as 'the principal enemies and haters of a person' — one domain of opposition. Chalishut (weakness) and bilbulim (confusions) are explicitly grouped together as the yetzer's weapons — the countless forms of inner resistance to prayer. They are two faces of the same obstructing force. The Torah also equates this with choshekh (darkness) — the state of being cut off from God.", "dominant_node": "יצר הרע", "nodes": ["יצר הרע", "מלחמות היצר", "חילות היצר", "אויבים ושונאים", "חלישות", "בלבולים", "חשך"], "node_degrees": {"יצר הרע": 3, "מלחמות היצר": 2, "חילות היצר": 2, "אויבים ושונאים": 2, "חלישות": 2, "בלבולים": 2, "חשך": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חלישות", "בלבולים"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly lists them together ('כַּמָּה וְכַמָּה מִינֵי חֲלִישׁוּת וּבִלְבּוּלִים') as two expressions of the same inner obstacle to prayer — they are not cause-and-effect but two manifestations of the same blocking force of the yetzer hara."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אויבים ושונאים", "יצר הרע"], "reason": "The Torah explicitly states that the yetzer hara and its armies 'הֵם עִקָּר הָאוֹיְבִים וְהַשּׂוֹנְאִים שֶׁל הָאָדָם' — they ARE the enemies and haters. This is a bechina equivalence: the yetzer IS the enemy."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 102, "label": "יִרְאָה וּבֵית", "label_en": "Fear of God and the House", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central teaching of Torah #102 is that earned, self-generated fear of God (יִרְאָה שֶׁבָּאָה לָאָדָם מִכֹּחַ אָדָם עַצְמוֹ) is the condition under which prayer is heard and divine abundance descends. The Rebbe explicitly equates this yira with three things that share the same essential nature: (1) the 'house' (בֵּית) in the verse 'You shall be over MY HOUSE' — יִרְאָה is bechina of בֵּית, because the Talmud says 'woe to one who has a house but no courtyard' (i.e., Torah without yira is a building without a perimeter fence — yira IS the containing structure); (2) the צַדִּיק who 'rules through the fear of God' — this earned yira is called/named צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹקִים; (3) implicitly, the 'higher fear' (יִרְאָה עֶלְיוֹנָה) is the counterpart — same word, different essential nature, hence the contrast between the two types of yira is structural to the Torah. All these concepts share the domain of yira as a containing, ruling, earned quality — they are different expressions of the same essential dimension.", "dominant_node": "יִרְאָה", "nodes": ["יִרְאָה", "בֵּית", "צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹקִים"], "node_degrees": {"יִרְאָה": 3, "בֵּית": 1, "צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹקִים": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_evidence": [{"quote": "אַתָּה תִּהְיֶה עַל בֵּיתִי. בֵּיתִי הוּא יִרְאָה", "bechina_statement": "בֵּיתִי = יִרְאָה (explicit equation using הוּא)"}, {"quote": "וְיִרְאָה זוֹ נִקְרָא: צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹקִים", "bechina_statement": "יִרְאָה עַצְמִית = צַדִּיק מוֹשֵׁל יִרְאַת אֱלֹקִים (explicit equation using נִקְרָא)"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 102, "volume": "lm2", "label": "גְּאֻלָּה וּבֹעַז", "label_en": "Redemption and Boaz", "polarity": "good", "reason": "בֹּעַז and גּוֹאֵל share the same essential nature: Boaz is called a bechina of redeemer by the explicit verse 'כִּי גוֹאֵל אָנֹכִי'. They are two expressions of the same redemptive reality — the personal (Boaz) and the functional title (Redeemer) are one and the same thing in this context. This component represents the domain of גאולה (redemption).", "dominant_node": "בעז", "nodes": ["בעז", "גואל"], "node_degrees": {"בעז": 1, "גואל": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 102, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְכִסֵּא", "label_en": "Kingship and the Throne", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #102 is explicitly about the revelation and growth of divine malkhut (God's kingship). The Rebbe equates the כִּסֵּא (throne) with מַלְכוּת — they share the same essential nature: both denote the royal/governing dimension. The 'diminution' (מִעוּט הַמַּלְכוּת) described by 'רַק הַכִּסֵּא' (only the throne exceeds you) is also the same concept expressed as meiut — reduced but inherent malkhut. Furthermore, the entire purpose of the teaching is that everything God created was for the revelation of His malkhut, and when He sends shefa to Israel they acknowledge 'מַלְכוּתוֹ בַּכֹּל מָשָׁלָה'. Malkhut is the destination-concept of the Torah's entire argument. The kisse and malkhut share the same essential domain: the governing, royal, feminine-receptive divine dimension.", "dominant_node": "מַלְכוּת", "nodes": ["מַלְכוּת", "כִּסֵּא", "מִעוּט הַמַּלְכוּת"], "node_degrees": {"מַלְכוּת": 3, "כִּסֵּא": 2, "מִעוּט הַמַּלְכוּת": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_evidence": [{"quote": "רַק הַכִּסֵּא – הַיְנוּ מִעוּט הַמַּלְכוּת, כִּי 'רַק' הוּא מִעוּט, 'כִּסֵּא' הוּא מַלְכוּת", "bechina_statement": "כִּסֵּא = מַלְכוּת (explicit equation using הוּא)"}, {"quote": "כְּדֵי לְגַלּוֹת מַלְכוּתוֹ", "bechina_statement": "The entire creation is bechina of the vessel/domain of revealing malkhut"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 102, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְרוּת", "label_en": "Prayer and Ruth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "רוּת and תְּפִלָּה share the same essential nature: Ruth is called a bechina of prayer because from her descended David, who saturated the Holy One Blessed be He with songs and praises. Both Ruth and תפלה belong to the domain of supplication and praise before God — Ruth embodies the very quality that makes prayer efficacious (devoted beseeching), and the Sages confirm this through her name's etymology. They are two faces of the same devotional reality.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["רות", "תפלה"], "node_degrees": {"רות": 1, "תפלה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 102, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְתֵבוֹת", "label_en": "Prayer and the Words as Vessels", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe teaches that prayer-words (תֵּבוֹת) are vessels (כֵּלִים) for receiving divine abundance (שֶׁפַע). The mouth-speech (פֶּה/פִּיךָ) is equated with prayer through the Onkelos translation ('וְעַל מֵימַר פֻּמָּךְ' = the prayer). These three — תְּפִלָּה, תֵּבוֹת, and פֶּה/מֵימַר פֻּמָּךְ — are all expressions of the same essential domain: the verbal/prayer dimension through which shefa is drawn down. They are not cause-and-effect (one does not produce the other); they are parallel aspects of the same thing — prayer IS words, words ARE vessels, and the mouth IS the prayer instrument. שֶׁפַע (abundance) is the content that flows through the vessel; it is included here as the paired concept to תֵּבוֹת-as-vessel.", "dominant_node": "תְּפִלָּה", "nodes": ["תְּפִלָּה", "תֵּבוֹת", "שֶׁפַע", "פֶּה"], "node_degrees": {"תְּפִלָּה": 3, "תֵּבוֹת": 2, "שֶׁפַע": 2, "פֶּה": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_evidence": [{"quote": "הַתֵּבוֹת הֵם כֵּלִים לְקַבֵּל הַשֶּׁפַע", "bechina_statement": "תֵּבוֹת = כֵּלִים לְקַבֵּל הַשֶּׁפַע (explicit equation: words ARE vessels)"}, {"quote": "וְעַל פִּיךָ יִשַּׁק כָּל עַמִּי, וְתַרְגּוּם אוּנְקְלוֹס: וְעַל מֵימַר פֻּמָּךְ, הַיְנוּ הַתְּפִלָּה", "bechina_statement": "פִּיךָ / מֵימַר פֻּמָּךְ = תְּפִלָּה (explicit equation using הַיְנוּ)"}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 102, "label": "קְּלִפּוֹת תַּחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה", "label_en": "The Klipot Subdued under the Shechina", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #102 opens with the statement that God created everything only to reveal His kingship, 'and through this the klipot are subdued under the Shechina.' The klipot are the evil counterpart to malkhut — they are the domain of spiritual husks/impurity that oppose the Shechina's revelation. This is a two-concept package: the klipot themselves, and their subdued state (כְּנִיפִיּוּת / נִכְפָּפִין) under the Shechina. Though small (only 2 explicit nodes), this constitutes a genuine evil package because: (a) it is explicitly named in the source text in contrast to the positive malkhut revelation, (b) it reflects the good/evil mirror structure — Package 2 (malkhut revealed) mirrors Package 4 (klipot subdued). The klipot are not merely an eitza-obstacle; they are a bechina domain of the evil/husks dimension that the Torah structurally opposes to the Shechina.", "dominant_node": "קְּלִפּוֹת", "nodes": ["קְּלִפּוֹת", "נִכְפָּפִין תַּחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה"], "node_degrees": {"קְּלִפּוֹת": 2, "נִכְפָּפִין תַּחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_evidence": [{"quote": "וְעַל יְדֵי זֶה, נִכְפָפִין הַקְּלִפּוֹת תַּחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה", "bechina_statement": "The klipot subdued state is the evil-mirror of malkhut's revelation — it names the domain that is the structural opposite of holy kingship in this Torah"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 103, "label": "הֲלָכוֹת וְנַחֲלַת אָבוֹת", "label_en": "Halakhot and Ancestral Inheritance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central domain of Torah #103's positive teaching is the equation: הֲלָכוֹת (halakhot) ARE נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת (ancestral inheritance). The Gemara (Sanhedrin 91b) establishes this as an explicit identity — Torah/halakhot are not merely commanded but are the inheritance of Israel, received through the lineage of ancestors. The righteous teacher (מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים) who transmits halakhot to his students is identified in Bava Batra 8b as מְלַמְּדֵי תִּינוֹקוֹת (children's teachers), and they are compared to the stars (כַּכּוֹכָבִים) — an explicit bechina-equivalence using 'אֵלּוּ' (these are...). Stars are the bechina of the teachers who make many righteous, because they illuminate the night and give light to many. The dominant node is הֲלָכוֹת (the teaching content = the inheritance) because the entire teaching pivots on whether halachic content is transmitted or withheld. Good/evil mirror: the good teacher who gives halakhot (this package) is the mirror of the corrupt teacher who withholds/steals them (Package 2).", "dominant_node": "הֲלָכוֹת", "nodes": ["הֲלָכוֹת", "נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת", "מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים", "מְלַמְּדֵי תִּינוֹקוֹת", "כַּכּוֹכָבִים"], "node_degrees": {"הֲלָכוֹת": 2, "נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת": 2, "מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים": 2, "מְלַמְּדֵי תִּינוֹקוֹת": 1, "כַּכּוֹכָבִים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים", "מְלַמְּדֵי תִּינוֹקוֹת", "כַּכּוֹכָבִים"], "reason": "These three form a tight bechina cluster: Bava Batra 8b declares 'מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים כַּכּוֹכָבִים — אֵלּוּ מְלַמְּדֵי תִּינוֹקוֹת'. The 'אֵלּוּ' is the classical bechina-declaration formula: these (stars) = these (teachers). All three — the act of making-many-righteous, the role of children's teacher, and the star-symbol — share the same essential nature: the teacher who freely transmits Torah/halakhot to many students and thereby righteous them. They belong in Package 1 because the defining shared nature is positive transmission of halakhot (= nachalat avot)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הֲלָכוֹת", "נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת"], "reason": "Sanhedrin 91b equates these directly: 'הֲלָכוֹת הֵם נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת'. This is not cause-and-effect; it is an identity statement. Halakhot ARE the inheritance — they are two names for the same reality: the Torah content that passes from generation to generation as a sacred patrimony. The shared nature is: Torah-knowledge-as-patrimony."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 103, "label": "תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים", "label_en": "Brood of Sinful Men — The Corrupt Teacher Cluster", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. Where Package 1 is the teacher who transmits Torah-as-inheritance, Package 2 is the teacher who withholds or steals it. The text explicitly names this cluster using Moses's condemnation: תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים (Numbers 32:14), which Onkelos translates as תַּלְמִידֵי גֻּבְרַיָּא חַיָּבַיָּא (disciples of wicked men). These two phrases are explicit bechina-equivalences (the Hebrew verse and its Aramaic translation name the same essential reality). The evil teacher's actions are described through two more bechina-equivalent phrases: מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים (those who make many guilty — the exact evil mirror of מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים) and מְנִיאֵי לֵב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל (those who dissuade the hearts of Israel from their inheritance). Both phrases name the same essential reality from different angles: the teacher who corrupts rather than rectifies his students. The Rebbe applies this to teachers who 'stole halakhot' from students — גּוֹזֵל נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת (robber of ancestral inheritance) is the identity assigned to such a teacher. Dominant node: מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים (most structurally connected, as it is the direct evil counterpart of מַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים which is the dominant-adjacent node in Package 1).", "dominant_node": "מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים", "nodes": ["מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים", "מְנִיאֵי לֵב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל", "תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים", "תַּלְמִידֵי גֻּבְרַיָּא חַיָּבַיָּא", "גּוֹזֵל נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים": 2, "מְנִיאֵי לֵב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל": 2, "תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים": 2, "תַּלְמִידֵי גֻּבְרַיָּא חַיָּבַיָּא": 1, "גּוֹזֵל נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תַּרְבּוּת אֲנָשִׁים חַטָּאִים", "תַּלְמִידֵי גֻּבְרַיָּא חַיָּבַיָּא"], "reason": "These are the same condemnation in two languages: the biblical Hebrew (Numbers 32:14) and Onkelos's Aramaic translation. Onkelos's translation is an explicit bechina-equivalence — the targum declares what the Hebrew 'IS' in meaning. Both name the same essential reality: the student-group shaped by a wicked teacher. Shared nature: the disciple-body that carries forward the corruption of its teacher, perpetuating the theft of Torah from Israel."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מְחַיְּבֵי הָרַבִּים", "מְנִיאֵי לֵב בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל"], "reason": "These are two descriptions of the same action of the evil teacher: 'making many guilty' and 'dissuading the hearts of Israel from their ancestral inheritance'. They are not cause-and-effect (one does not produce the other); they are two faces of the same reality. The teacher who dissuades Israel IS the one who makes them guilty. Shared nature: the destructive-teacher action that corrupts the many."}, {"merged_nodes": ["גּוֹזֵל נַחֲלַת אָבוֹת"], "reason": "The Gemara states the teacher who withholds halakha IS as if he robbed the student of the ancestral inheritance ('כְּאִלּוּ גּוֹזְלוֹ מִנַּחֲלַת אָבוֹת'). The Rebbe drops the 'כְּאִלּוּ' (as if) and treats this as a real identity — the withholding-teacher IS a robber of inheritance. This node belongs in the evil package because it names the essential nature of the corrupt teacher from the perspective of the damage done: he is, at his core, a thief of Torah-patrimony."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 104, "label": "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל וְחֵלֶק בֵּאלֹהִים", "label_en": "Eretz Yisrael and a Portion in God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The positive pole of Torah #104's teaching centers on the essential equivalence between desiring a portion in Eretz Yisrael and having a relationship with God. The Gemara (Ketubot 110b) — 'כָּל הַדָּר בְּחוּץ לָאָרֶץ כְּאִלּוּ אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ' — is a bechina-declaration in the classic Talmudic 'כְּאִלּוּ' form: the inverse of this statement is the positive identity: dwelling IN Eretz Yisrael IS, in its essential nature, having God. The Torah explicitly states that Bnei Reuven and Bnei Gad WERE כְּשֵׁרִים (innocent/worthy) because they DID want a portion in the Land — their wanting a portion in the Land is what makes them people who have God. These three concepts share a single essential nature: the connection between Israel's people and their divine inheritance through the Land. כְּשֵׁרִים (worthy people) is the human embodiment of this connection — those who have God because they cleave to the Land. חֵלֶק בָּאָרֶץ (a portion in the Land) is the concrete form of that divine connection. The dominant node is אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל because the entire teaching's structure pivots on the Land as the medium of divine presence — it is the concept with the highest connectivity in this cluster (connecting both to 'having God' and to the innocent people who want it).", "dominant_node": "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "nodes": ["אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "יֶשׁ לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ", "כְּשֵׁרִים"], "node_degrees": {"אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל": 2, "יֶשׁ לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ": 2, "כְּשֵׁרִים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "יֶשׁ לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ"], "reason": "The Gemara (Ketubot 110b) declares: 'כָּל הַדָּר בְּחוּץ לָאָרֶץ כְּאִלּוּ אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ'. The inverse is the positive bechina: dwelling in the Land = having God. The Land and divine connection are two faces of the same essential reality — not one causing the other, but both expressing the same spiritual state of belonging to the covenant. This 'כְּאִלּוּ' identity is treated as a true bechina, consistent with the framework's treatment of 'כְּאִלּוּ' in Torah #103."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כְּשֵׁרִים"], "reason": "Line 5 explicitly establishes the innocence (כְּשֵׁרוּת) of Bnei Reuven and Bnei Gad by the criterion of their desiring a portion in Eretz Yisrael. The shared nature: כְּשֵׁרִים (those who are worthy) ARE those who have God because they want Eretz Yisrael. כְּשֵׁרוּת in this Torah is not a general moral term but a specific identity — those who cleave to the divine inheritance of the Land. It belongs in the good cluster because it names the human type who embodies the Land-God connection."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 104, "label": "חוּץ לָאָרֶץ וְאֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ", "label_en": "Exile and Having No God — The Disconnection Cluster", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. The Gemara's identity declaration (Ketubot 110b) makes exile from the Land equivalent in essential nature to having no God. This cluster groups three concepts that are all facets of the same essential reality: spiritual disconnection from the divine. 'אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ' (has no God) is the central concept because the whole teaching's structure depends on it — it is what Moshe falsely attributed to Bnei Gad (they have no God because they don't want the Land), and it is what his grandson truly embodied through idol worship. Dwelling in exile (חוּץ לָאָרֶץ) IS, in its essential nature, having no God — they are the same spiritual state viewed from different dimensions (geographic vs. theological). Idol worship (עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה / עֲבַד לְפֶסֶל) is the concrete, visible expression of having no God — not the cause or consequence but the direct embodiment. All three nodes share the same essential nature: absence of divine connection. The dominant node is 'אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ' because it is the conceptual hub that connects both the geographic (exile) and the ritual (idol worship) dimensions. The good/evil mirror is perfect and explicit: Package 1 (אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל → יֶשׁ לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ → כְּשֵׁרִים) mirrors Package 2 (חוּץ לָאָרֶץ → אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ → עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה) exactly.", "dominant_node": "אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ", "nodes": ["אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ", "חוּץ לָאָרֶץ", "עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה"], "node_degrees": {"אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ": 2, "חוּץ לָאָרֶץ": 1, "עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חוּץ לָאָרֶץ", "אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ"], "reason": "Directly declared by the Gemara (Ketubot 110b): 'כָּל הַדָּר בְּחוּץ לָאָרֶץ כְּאִלּוּ אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ'. The 'כְּאִלּוּ' (as if) is a bechina-identity in Talmudic language — the Rabbis are declaring that these two states share the same essential spiritual nature. Exile from the Land IS, ontologically, being without God. Not cause-and-effect: one who lives outside the Land does not thereby LOSE God sequentially — rather, the state of exile and the state of having no God are the same spiritual condition expressed in two registers (geographic and theological)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עֲבוֹדָה זָרָה"], "reason": "The grandson's idol worship (עֲבַד לְפֶסֶל מִיכָה — 'served Micha's idol') is explicitly presented as the concrete realization of 'אֵין לוֹ אֱלוֹהַּ'. It is not that idol worship CAUSES having no God; rather, idol worship IS having no God in its most visible form — they share the same essential nature. The punishment's fitness (middah kenegged middah) works precisely because the grandson's idol worship is not merely a consequence of something but is the same state (having no God) that Moshe falsely attributed to Bnei Gad. The structural identity of sin and punishment is itself a bechina: they are the same essential thing manifested at two different points in time."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 105, "label": "רַחֲמִים פְּשׁוּטִים וּתְפִלַּת הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "Simple Mercy and God's Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this Torah's bechina subgraph all express the same essential identity: the dimension of simple/plain divine mercy (רחמים פשוטים), which the Zohar identifies with Zeir Anpin. The Rebbe teaches that this mercy is structurally identical to 'God's prayer' — the dynamic by which the simple mercy of Zeir Anpin intercedes upward to arouse the Great Mercy of Atika Stima'ah. רחמים פשוטים is the pivot: it is what we can arouse through prayer, it is called 'rachamin stam d'Zeir Anpin' by the Zohar, and it is what constitutes God's own 'prayer' (God's simple mercy beseeching His own Great Mercy on our behalf). All three nodes share the same essential nature — they are different names and descriptions of the same cosmic dimension of accessible, bounded mercy that serves as the vehicle for infinite redemption.", "dominant_node": "רחמים פשוטים", "nodes": ["רחמים פשוטים", "רחמים סתם דזעיר אנפין", "תפלת הקדוש ברוך הוא"], "node_degrees": {"רחמים פשוטים": 2, "רחמים סתם דזעיר אנפין": 1, "תפלת הקדוש ברוך הוא": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 106, "label": "גַּדְלוּת הַמֹּחִין וְהַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Expanded Consciousness and Da'at", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster represents the positive pole of Torah #106. The Rebbe's teaching centers on the move from katnut (diminished consciousness) to gadlut hamochin (expanded consciousness). When gadlut hamochin is reached, da'at is actualized — the Rebbe cites the Talmudic saying 'Great is da'at that was given between two letters' (Berakhot 33) and interprets this as da'at being placed between El (=chesed) and YHVH (=rachamim). The two divine Names are explicit bechinot: 'El, hu chesed' and 'YHVH, hu rachamim'. The result of reaching gadlut — the sweetening of all judgments (nimtakin kol hadinim) — flows via eitza from this cluster but is not itself a bechina node. The dominant node is gadlut hamochin, which is the most central concept, directly correlated with da'at and mediating between the two Names.", "dominant_node": "גדלות המוחין", "nodes": ["גדלות המוחין", "דעת", "אל", "חסד", "ה'", "רחמים"], "node_degrees": {"גדלות המוחין": 3, "דעת": 3, "אל": 2, "חסד": 2, "ה'": 2, "רחמים": 2}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": [{"pair": ["אל", "חסד"], "source_quote": "אֵל, הוּא חֶסֶד, עַל דֶּרֶךְ: חֶסֶד אֵל כָּל הַיּוֹם", "bechina_marker": "הוּא"}, {"pair": ["ה'", "רחמים"], "source_quote": "וַה', הוּא רַחֲמִים, עַל דֶּרֶךְ: רַחֲמֶיךָ רַבִּים ה'", "bechina_marker": "הוּא"}, {"pair": ["גדלות המוחין", "דעת"], "source_quote": "גְּדוֹלָה דֵּעָה שֶׁנִּתְּנָה בֵּין שְׁנֵי אוֹתִיּוֹת... כְּשֶׁיָּבוֹא לְגַדְלוּת הַמֹּחִין, נִתְעוֹרֵר עָלָיו רַחֲמִים וַחֲסָדִים", "bechina_marker": "implicit equivalence: gadlut = the state in which da'at is present between the Names"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 106, "label": "קַטְנוּת הַמֹּחִין וְהָעֲנִיּוּת", "label_en": "Diminished Consciousness and Poverty", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster represents the negative pole of Torah #106 — the evil/deficiency mirror of Package 1. The Rebbe opens with the Talmudic principle 'there is no poverty except poverty of da'at' (Nedarim 41), establishing that spiritual poverty (aniyut) IS lack of da'at. The state of lacking sekhel/da'at IS CALLED katnut hamochin ('he is called katnut hamochin'). The word 'dal' in the verse 'ashrei maskil el dal' is explicitly interpreted as 'when he is in katnut hamochin' — making dal a bechina of katnut. This cluster contains the direct evil mirrors of Package 1: where gadlut = da'at present = chesed + rachamim flow, katnut = da'at absent = judgments not sweetened. The cluster is 'evil' in the sense of deficiency/din — it is the negative pole the teaching instructs us to escape from. Dominant node is katnut hamochin, which is most central, explicitly equated to aniyut, dal, and chisaron sekhel.", "dominant_node": "קטנות המוחין", "nodes": ["קטנות המוחין", "עניות", "דל", "חסרון דעת"], "node_degrees": {"קטנות המוחין": 3, "עניות": 2, "דל": 2, "חסרון דעת": 2}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": [{"pair": ["עניות", "חסרון דעת"], "source_quote": "אֵין עֲנִיּוּת אֶלָּא מִן הַדַּעַת", "bechina_marker": "אֵין... אֶלָּא (exclusive identification)"}, {"pair": ["קטנות המוחין", "חסרון דעת"], "source_quote": "כִּי יֵשׁ זְמַן שֶׁאֵין לוֹ שֵׂכֶל, וְהוּא נִקְרָא קַטְנוּת הַמֹּחִין", "bechina_marker": "נִקְרָא"}, {"pair": ["דל", "קטנות המוחין"], "source_quote": "אַשְׁרֵי מַשְׂכִּיל אֶל דָּל – הַיְנוּ כְּשֶׁהוּא בְּקַטְנוּת הַמֹּחִין", "bechina_marker": "הַיְנוּ (explicative equation)"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "מֹחִין וּמַנְהִיג אֱמֶת — הַנּוֹעַם וְהַיִּרְאָה", "label_en": "Holy Intellect and the True Leader — Pleasantness and Fear of God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the largest package in Torah #107, spanning sections 1071, 1072, and 1077. The central hub is the node מחין (holy consciousness/brain-light), which appears across all three sections and serves as the structural connector for the entire cluster. In section 1071, מחין is identified with the nועם העליון (supernal pleasantness) that is the nature of the Land of Israel's consciousness, as opposed to the damaging consciousness of exile. The midwife Shifra (=Joseph=pleasant teaching) and the beauty of Halacha all connect to this domain of holy נועם. In section 1072, מחין connects to the Ruach haKodesh, the prophetic spirit (ruach nevuah), the all-encompassing spirit (ruach hakolel), and the true leader Moses. The Temple building, da'at, and the encompassing sage (chakham hakolel) all belong to this cluster. In section 1077, the tefillin are identified as a bechina of מחין; the strap (retzua) that passes through the tefillin is identified as yirah (fear of God); malkut and Yehudah are identified as yirah; Binyamin as da'at/mochin; and the Holy Temple as da'at. All these nodes form one connected component through מחין as the central bridge. The dominant node is מחין with the highest connectivity (7+ edges). The package is entirely positive/good — every node represents an aspect of holy consciousness, authentic leadership, the beauty of Torah, and the fear of Heaven.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["מחין", "נועם", "מחין של ארץ ישראל", "יוסף", "שפרה", "יפה תאר", "שמנעימים בהלכה", "נעם", "הולדה", "שפיר ושליא", "מוחין", "מוחין של ארץ ישראל", "רוח אלקים", "רוח הקדש", "מלאכת בנין בית המקדש", "רוח הכולל", "והמנהיג האמתי של כל ישראל", "משה", "מנהיג אמתי", "דעת הכולל", "המנהיג האמתי", "חכם הדור", "חכם הכולל", "רוח נבואה", "דעת", "בנין בית המקדש", "בית המקדש", "בנימין", "תפלין", "מוחין (1077)", "יראה", "הרצועה", "יראה דקדשה", "רצועה דקדשה", "רצועה בישא", "יראה נפולה", "רצועה", "יהודה", "מלכות", "גדולה", "רצועה של תפלין", "רצועה שעוברת בתוך המעברתא של תפלין"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 8, "דעת": 4, "מלכות": 3, "רוח אלקים": 4, "רוח הכולל": 4, "משה": 3, "יראה": 4, "תפלין": 3, "יראה דקדשה": 2, "רצועה": 3, "שפרה": 4, "נועם": 2, "נעם": 2}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "יַעֲקֹב וּצְדָקָה — נְדִיבוּת הַלֵּב", "label_en": "Jacob and Charity — Generosity of Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package from section 1071 centers on the patriarchal figure Jacob and the concept of charity (tzedakah). Jacob is identified as a bechina of tzedakah (via their shared connection to nachalah/inheritance — the land inheritance of Jacob is the bechina of tzedakah). Tzedakah is connected to terumah (the priestly tithe — giving generously) and to nedivut lev (generosity/openhearted giving). The phrase 'when the heart opens and gives willingly' (sheniftach venitmadved halev) is a bechina of nedivut lev. The package represents the ethical-spiritual dimension of giving, generosity, and the connection of the patriarchal soul to the flow of divine inheritance. It is entirely positive/good. The dominant node is יעקב or צדקה — both have the same degree (4) within this cluster.", "dominant_node": "יעקב", "nodes": ["יעקב", "צדקה", "נחלת", "תרומה", "נדיבות לב", "שנפתח ונתנדב הלב"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב": 2, "צדקה": 3, "נחלת": 2, "תרומה": 2, "נדיבות לב": 2, "שנפתח ונתנדב הלב": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "חוֹבְלִים — מֹחִין שֶׁל חוּץ לָאָרֶץ", "label_en": "The Damagers — Consciousness of Exile", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This small package from section 1071 is the exact evil mirror of Package 1's נועם/מחין-of-holiness dimension. Where Package 1 has מחין של ארץ ישראל (the consciousness of the Land of Israel, which is identified with pleasantness/נועם), Package 3 has מחין של חוץ לארץ (the consciousness of exile), which is identified with חובלים (the damagers/destroyers). The birth pangs of labor (חבלי לדה) are also a bechina of חובלים — they represent the painful, damaging dimension of emergence into the world, as opposed to the pleasantness of Shifra's holy midwifery. This is the evil counterpart: where Israel's consciousness produces beauty and נועם, exile's consciousness produces damage and pain. The dominant node is חובלים with the highest connectivity (2 edges) in this tiny cluster.", "dominant_node": "חובלים", "nodes": ["מחין של חוץ לארץ", "חובלים", "חבלי לדה"], "node_degrees": {"חובלים": 2, "מחין של חוץ לארץ": 1, "חבלי לדה": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "תְּשׁוּבָה וּבִטּוּל הַזְּמַן — חַיִּים נִצְחִיִּים", "label_en": "Repentance and Transcendence of Time — Eternal Life", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the second largest package in Torah #107, uniting all the repentance-related concepts from sections 1072 and 1079, connected through the central node תשובה. In section 1072, the cluster includes: repentance (teshuvah) producing life (chayim) and eternal life (chayim nitzchiyim), Shabbat as a foretaste of the World to Come, humility (shiflet) as the condition for eternal life, the gates of repentance (nedivei ha'am opening them through shame), and the well (be'er) and rock (sela) as bechinot of Torah interpretations (be'urei Torah). In section 1079, Rebbe Nachman's editor (Reb Nosson) connects teshuvah with bitul hazmanim (transcendence of time), showing that repentance lifts one above temporal constraints. Abraham is identified as a bechina of 'above time' (through his attribute of ahavah/love which unifies time). Yitzchak is a bechina of zman (time), and the redemptive events of kri'at yam suf (splitting of the Sea) and milchemet Amalek are bechinot of bitul hazmanim. The Word/letters of speech (dibburim/otiyot) are connected to teshuvah as the means of return. Also included from section 1071: ahavah (love) is connected to shalhavot di-re'himuta (flames of love), and through the 1079 chain ahavah ↔ Abraham ↔ above-time ↔ teshuvah, the flames of love enter this package. The dominant node is תשובה with the highest connectivity in the cluster (5+ edges).", "dominant_node": "תשובה", "nodes": ["תשובה", "חיים", "חיים נצחיים", "חיים נצחיים של עולם הבא", "שבת", "מתנה", "יראה והבושה", "נדיבי העם", "חפרוה", "בושה", "מדבר", "שפלות", "שפלות ועניוות", "בלתי גבול", "אין", "למעלה מהזמן", "למעלה מן הזמן", "בטול הזמנים", "בטול הזמן", "קריעת ים סוף", "מלחמת עמלק", "נתקצר הזמן", "אברהם", "אהבה", "התאחדות הזמן", "יצחק בן אברהם", "התאחדות הזמן על ידי תשובה", "בן", "קול דקדשה", "קולו יתברך", "זמן", "שנה", "קץ", "יצחק", "אחרית הימים", "אבנים", "אותיות", "דבורים", "דיבורים", "שלהובין דרחימותא"], "node_degrees": {"תשובה": 7, "אהבה": 3, "אברהם": 2, "בטול הזמנים": 4, "קריעת ים סוף": 3, "זמן": 3, "קץ": 2, "אבנים": 3, "דיבורים": 2}, "merge_notes": ["Clusters from sections 1072 (teshuvah/shabbat/chayim) and 1079 (teshuvah/zman/avraham) are united through the shared node תשובה.", "The node אהבה from section 1071 (אהבה ↔ שלהובין דרחימותא) is pulled into this cluster via the 1079 chain אהבה ↔ אברהם ↔ למעלה מן הזמן ↔ תשובה.", "The nodes 'למעלה מהזמן' and 'למעלה מן הזמן' are slightly different node_ids expressing the same concept; similarly 'בטול הזמנים' and 'בטול הזמן'. Both variants are included and should be considered for aliasing."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "בְּאֵר וּבְאוּרֵי תוֹרָה — סֶלַע", "label_en": "The Well and Torah Interpretations — The Rock", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This small three-node cluster from section 1072 connects the well (be'er) with Torah interpretations (be'urei Torah) and the rock (sela). The be'er (well) is a bechina of be'urei Torah: the well digs down to living waters just as Torah commentary uncovers deep meaning. The sela (rock) is a bechina of the be'er: both are sources of hidden living water that can be 'opened' to flow. Together these three form a compact metaphorical cluster around the theme of accessing hidden wisdom. Although small (3 nodes), the cluster is thematically distinct from the larger teshuvah cluster and not connected to it via bechina. Dominant node: באר or באורי התורה (equal degree).", "dominant_node": "באר", "nodes": ["באר", "באורי התורה", "סלע"], "node_degrees": {"באר": 2, "באורי התורה": 2, "סלע": 1}, "merge_notes": ["PROPOSED MERGE INTO PACKAGE 4: Thematically, the be'er/Torah-interpretation cluster is connected to the broader teshuvah/repentance package (repentance is accessed through the 49 gates — like wells of water). However, there is no bechina edge connecting this cluster to the teshuvah cluster in the dataset. If a bechina edge were to be established (e.g., be'urei Torah ↔ teshuvah or be'er ↔ מ'ט שערי תשובה), this cluster should be merged into Package 4. For now it remains independent."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 6, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "מ\"ט שַׁעֲרֵי תְּשׁוּבָה — אוֹתִיּוֹת הַשְּׁבָטִים", "label_en": "The 49 Gates of Repentance — Letters of the Tribes", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This three-node cluster from section 1073 identifies the 49 gates of repentance (the complete structure of how all Jews can return) with the 49 letters in the names of the twelve tribes of Israel, and equates the 49 days of the Omer count with the 49 gates. The connection: each gate corresponds to a letter, each letter to a day of counting. This is a distinct cluster about the structural framework of communal repentance (different from the individual teshuvah in Package 4). The cluster stands alone in the dataset without bechina edges connecting it to Package 4 or other packages.", "dominant_node": "המ\"ט שערי תשובה", "nodes": ["המ\"ט שערי תשובה", "מ\"ט אותיות שיש בשמות שנים־עשר שבטי י־ה", "מ\"ט ימי הספירה"], "node_degrees": {"המ\"ט שערי תשובה": 2, "מ\"ט אותיות שיש בשמות שנים־עשר שבטי י־ה": 1, "מ\"ט ימי הספירה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["PROPOSED MERGE INTO PACKAGE 4: The 49 gates of repentance are structurally connected to the teshuvah theme. The 'שלהובין דרחימותא' node already resides in Package 4 via the אהבה chain. Thematically, this cluster elaborates the structural dimension of teshuvah. A merge into Package 4 is recommended if a bechina edge can be established between 'המ\"ט שערי תשובה' and 'תשובה'."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 7, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "שַׁעַר הַחֲמִשִּׁים — תְּשׁוּבַת הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ", "label_en": "The Fiftieth Gate — God's Own Repentance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This three-node cluster from section 1073 identifies the 50th day of the Omer (Shavuot / Matan Torah) as a bechina of the 50th gate — which is the gate of God's own teshuvah (as it were): 'shuvu elai va'ashuva aleikhem' (Malachi 3). The descent of God onto Sinai ('vayered Hashem al Har Sinai') is the bechina of God's own teshuvah toward Israel. This is a structurally distinct and theologically elevated concept, separate from the 49-gate structure of Package 6 and from the individual teshuvah of Package 4. Dominant node: שער החמשים.", "dominant_node": "שער החמשים", "nodes": ["שער החמשים", "יום החמשים", "תשובה של השם יתברך בעצמו"], "node_degrees": {"שער החמשים": 1, "יום החמשים": 2, "תשובה של השם יתברך בעצמו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["PROPOSED MERGE INTO PACKAGE 6: Packages 6 and 7 together form the complete structure of the 50 gates — the 49 accessible gates (Package 6) and the transcendent 50th gate (Package 7). They should logically be merged into one 'שערי תשובה' package. No bechina edge currently links them in the dataset, but thematically they are one teaching."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 8, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "מִצְרַיִם וּתְשׁוּבָה עִלָּאָה", "label_en": "Egypt and Supreme Repentance", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This three-node cluster from section 1073 identifies Egypt (Mitzrayim) with the constriction of the throat (meitzar hagaron), and this constriction is a bechina of teshuvah ila'ah (the highest/supreme level of repentance). Egypt is the place where the twelve tribes were purified — it is both the place of constriction and the crucible of supreme return. The cluster is marked 'neutral' because Egypt as a geographic/historical entity is neutral — it is the site of both oppression and purification, serving as the context for the most elevated repentance. The dominant node is מצרים.", "dominant_node": "מצרים", "nodes": ["מצרים", "מצר הגרון", "תשובה עלאה"], "node_degrees": {"מצרים": 2, "מצר הגרון": 1, "תשובה עלאה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["PROPOSED MERGE INTO PACKAGE 4: The concept of teshuvah ila'ah is a variant/sub-type of teshuvah, and the Egypt-purification motif feeds directly into the 49-gates structure (the purification in Egypt was what enabled the 49 days of counting). A merge into the teshuvah mega-cluster (Package 4) is recommended."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 9, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "פּוּרִים וּפֶסַח — מָרְדְּכַי וְאֶסְתֵּר", "label_en": "Purim and Passover — Mordechai and Esther", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package consolidates three small 1074 bechina clusters that all belong to the same Purim-Passover teaching. The three mini-clusters are: (a) Mordechai ↔ cheirus (freedom) / cheirus shel Pesach; (b) Shoshanah ↔ Esther; (c) Shiftotav ↔ Pesach. In the source text (section 1074), these all appear in a single teaching about how Purim leads to Pesach and how the verse from Shir HaShirim 'siftatav shoshanim notfot mor over' contains all three: siftatav = peh sach = Pesach; shoshanah = Esther; notfot mor over = Mordechai = mar deror = cheirus of Pesach. The three separate bechina chains are thematically one teaching and should be one package. The dominant node is מרדכי (most connected across the teaching).", "dominant_node": "מרדכי", "nodes": ["מרדכי", "חרות", "חרות של פסח", "שושנה", "אסתר", "שפתותיו", "פסח"], "node_degrees": {"מרדכי": 2, "חרות": 1, "חרות של פסח": 1, "שושנה": 1, "אסתר": 1, "שפתותיו": 1, "פסח": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Three separate 2–3 node clusters from section 1074 consolidated into one package based on shared thematic content and shared source context (all from same proof text).", "Note: 'חרות' and 'חרות של פסח' are very likely aliases of the same concept and should be considered for node deduplication."], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 10, "torah_ref": 107, "label": "עֲשָׂרָה מַאֲמָרוֹת וְאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "The Ten Divine Utterances and the Land of Israel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This four-node cluster from section 1078 connects the Ten Divine Utterances (asarah ma'amarot, the ten statements by which the world was created — Genesis) with the sustaining of the world before the Torah was given (kiyvum ha'olam kodem matan Torah) as a bechina of matnat chinam (gift of grace, which is how Moses asked to enter the Land). The Land of Israel's ten degrees of holiness (asarah kedushut) are also identified as a bechina of the ten utterances. The teaching is about accessing Eretz Yisrael through grace/gift (chinam) — the power of the pre-Torah era of free divine gift. This cluster is compact and internally coherent, representing the domain of creation-speech and the sanctity of the Land. Dominant node: עשרה מאמרות.", "dominant_node": "עשרה מאמרות", "nodes": ["עשרה מאמרות", "קיום העולם קדם מתן־תורה", "מתנת חנם", "ארץ ישראל בעצמה יש בה גם כן בחינת עשר קדשות"], "node_degrees": {"עשרה מאמרות": 2, "קיום העולם קדם מתן־תורה": 2, "מתנת חנם": 1, "ארץ ישראל בעצמה יש בה גם כן בחינת עשר קדשות": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 108, "label": "עוֹלָה וְרוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה", "label_en": "The Burnt Offering and the Broken Spirit", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All nodes in this package share the essential nature of the inward act of self-breaking/self-severing that constitutes genuine repentance and spiritual re-ascent. The Torah establishes a series of explicit bechina equations: זבחי אלהים = עולה = רוח נשברה = the act of breaking one's spirit in shame after a fall. The nesira (נסירה) and שם חת\"ך are the kabbalistic mechanism underlying this same act — the Name חת\"ך (= 2 × רוח in gematria) IS the nesira, and the nesira IS what the עולה accomplishes (transformation from back-to-back to face-to-face). All nodes thus share the same essential nature: the spiritual technology of self-breaking as the vehicle for reconciliation with God.", "dominant_node": "עולה", "nodes": ["עולה", "זבחי אלהים", "רוח נשברה", "כשמשבר את רוחו בקרבו, ומתביש וחושב בעצמו איך נפלתי ונשלכתי משמים לארץ", "נסירה", "שם חת\"ך"], "node_degrees": {"עולה": 3, "זבחי אלהים": 1, "רוח נשברה": 1, "כשמשבר את רוחו בקרבו, ומתביש וחושב בעצמו איך נפלתי ונשלכתי משמים לארץ": 1, "נסירה": 1, "שם חת\"ך": 1}, "raw_component_membership": {"component_A_nodes": ["עולה", "זבחי אלהים", "רוח נשברה", "כשמשבר את רוחו בקרבו, ומתביש וחושב בעצמו איך נפלתי ונשלכתי משמים לארץ"], "component_B_nodes": ["נסירה", "שם חת\"ך"], "merge_basis": "Component B merged into Component A: the source text states 'וְזֶהוּ בְחִינוֹת נְסִירָה, וּבָא אֶל בְּחִינוֹת פָּנִים בְּפָנִים, וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינוֹת עוֹלָה' — nesira and שם חת\"ך are explicitly identified as bechinot of עולה. The eitza edge (id 2958: משבר את לבו → שם חת\"ך) shows the causal mechanism, but the bechina identity ('וְהַנְּסִירָה הוּא בְּחִינַת שֵׁם חֲתַ\"ךְ') and the equation of nesira with עולה confirm shared essential nature."}, "merge_notes": ["Component B (נסירה ↔ שם חת\"ך) merged into Package 1. Basis: SHARED NATURE — all are expressions of the same spiritual reality: the inward cutting/severing/breaking that turns back-to-back into face-to-face. The source text makes this explicit: 'וְזֶהוּ בְחִינוֹת נְסִירָה, וּבָא אֶל בְּחִינוֹת פָּנִים בְּפָנִים, וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינוֹת עוֹלָה'. The Name חת\"ך (whose gematria = 2×רוח, the two breaths of a sighing heart) IS the nesira, and the nesira IS the עולה. These are not cause-and-effect but different linguistic faces of the same act."], "source_bechina_edges": [{"edge_id": 1525, "node1": "עולה", "node2": "כשמשבר את רוחו בקרבו, ומתביש וחושב בעצמו איך נפלתי ונשלכתי משמים לארץ", "proof": "וּבְחִינוֹת עוֹלָה הוּא כְּשֶׁמְּשַׁבֵּר אֶת רוּחוֹ בְּקִרְבּוֹ, וּמִתְבַּיֵּשׁ וְחוֹשֵׁב בְּעַצְמוֹ אֵיךְ נָפַלְתִּי וְנִשְׁלַכְתִּי מִשָּׁמַיִם לָאָרֶץ"}, {"edge_id": 2352, "node1": "זבחי אלהים", "node2": "עולה", "proof": "וְזֶהוּ זִבְחֵי אֶלֹהִים, הַיְנוּ בְחִינוֹת עוֹלָה, הוּא רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה כַּנַּ\"ל"}, {"edge_id": 2712, "node1": "עולה", "node2": "רוח נשברה", "proof": "וְזֶהוּ בְחִינוֹת עוֹלָה: וְזֶהוּ זִבְחֵי אֶלֹהִים, הַיְנוּ בְחִינוֹת עוֹלָה, הוּא רוּחַ נִשְׁבָּרָה כַּנַּ\"ל"}, {"edge_id": 2959, "node1": "נסירה", "node2": "שם חת\"ך", "proof": "וְהַנְּסִירָה הוּא בְחִינַת שֵׁם חֲתַ\"ךְ, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב בַּכַּוָּנוֹת 'פּוֹתֵ'חַ אֶ'ת יָדֶ'ךָ', סוֹפֵי-תֵּבוֹת חֲתַ\"ךְ, הַיְנוּ שֶׁחוֹתֵךְ וּמְנַסֵּר אֶת עַצְמוֹ"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 109, "label": "נֹּהַּ וְהָאֲנָחָה — הָאֲנָחָה כְּמִיתָה", "label_en": "The Sigh as Death — Groaning and the Death-Process", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This 2-node component is the only connected component in the bechina subgraph for Torah #109. The Rebbe establishes an explicit bechina: 'הַנֹּהַּ וְהָאֲנָחָה הוּא בְּחִינַת מִיתָה בְּגוּף וָנֶפֶשׁ' — the groaning and sighing IS a bechina of death in body and soul. The shared essential nature is the process of vital-force gathering and then releasing/expiring. The Rebbe supports this from Berakhot 58b ('a sigh breaks a person's body') for the bodily dimension, and from Tehillim 104:29 ('when You gather their spirit, they expire') for the soul dimension. Polarity is neutral because this death-process operates in BOTH directions: sighing over sins leads to dying away from evil and attaching to holiness; sighing over good leads to dying away from holiness and attaching to impurity. The sigh-as-death is the mechanism that can go either way — it is the pivot of the entire Torah. The node with greatest centrality-to-teaching is נה והאנחה (groaning and sighing), which is the active agent; מיתה בגוף ונפש describes its essential nature.", "dominant_node": "נה והאנחה", "nodes": ["נה והאנחה", "מיתה בגוף ונפש"], "node_degrees": {"נה והאנחה": 1, "מיתה בגוף ונפש": 1}, "dataset_edge": {"edge_id": 1795, "node1_id": "נה והאנחה", "node2_id": "מיתה בגוף ונפש", "node1_text": "נֹּהַּ וְהָאֲנָחָה", "node2_text": "מִיתָה בְּגוּף וָנֶפֶשׁ", "bechina_marker": "הוּא בְּחִינַת"}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component is the only component in the dataset. The source-text gap analysis (see additions file) reveals two additional bechina clusters: (1) הבל / חבל הנשמה / תוסף רוחם — the breath as a soul-connecting rope; (2) חבל דקדשה / חבל דטמאה — the two opposing rope-domains. These would constitute Package 2 (good) and Package 3 (evil) respectively. See torah_109_additions.json and torah_109_new_packages.json."], "source_bechinot": [{"pair": ["נה והאנחה", "מיתה בגוף ונפש"], "source_quote": "הַנֹּהַּ וְהָאֲנָחָה הוּא בְּחִינַת מִיתָה בְּגוּף וָנֶפֶשׁ", "bechina_marker": "הוּא בְּחִינַת"}], "good_evil_mirror": {"note": "This neutral package sits at the center of the Torah's good/evil mirror. The 'death' enacted by the sigh can sever connection to evil (→ good outcome) or sever connection to good (→ evil outcome). The sigh itself is the pivot, not polarized. The two rope-clusters (חבל דקדשה / חבל דטמאה) ARE the polar mirror — see gap analysis."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 109, "volume": "lm2", "label": "אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל וּמְקוֹם גְּנִיזַת הַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "Eretz Yisrael and the Tzaddik's Burial Place", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the same essential nature: the domain of the holy land and its spiritual reality as expressed in the burial place of the true tzaddik. The Rebbe states that the burial place of true tzaddikim IS holy with the actual holiness of Eretz Yisrael itself ('מְקוֹם גְּנִיזָתָם הוּא קָדוֹשׁ בִּקְדֻשַּׁת אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל מַמָּשׁ'). This is a bechina relationship: the burial place and Eretz Yisrael are two expressions of the same holiness. The tzaddikim are the ones through whom this identity is realized — they are the ones who 'inherit' Eretz Yisrael through the transference of its holiness to their burial place. Thus ארץ ישראל, מקום גניזת הצדיק, and קדושת ארץ ישראל all belong to one domain: the living reality of the holy land, which is not merely a geographical location but a quality of holiness that true tzaddikim carry with them even in death.", "dominant_node": "ארץ ישראל", "nodes": ["ארץ ישראל", "מקום גניזה", "קדושת ארץ ישראל", "צדיקים אמיתיים"], "node_degrees": {"ארץ ישראל": 3, "מקום גניזה": 2, "קדושת ארץ ישראל": 1, "צדיקים אמיתיים": 1}, "mirror_package_id": null, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מקום גניזה", "ארץ ישראל"], "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly states that the burial place of true tzaddikim IS Eretz Yisrael in holiness — 'מְקוֹם גְּנִיזָתָם הוּא קָדוֹשׁ בִּקְדֻשַּׁת אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל מַמָּשׁ'. The word 'ממש' (literally/actually) underscores that this is not metaphorical equivalence but an identity of essential nature. This is an unambiguous bechina."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קדושת ארץ ישראל", "ארץ ישראל"], "reason": "The Rebbe frames the tzaddikim's inheritance as acquiring the specific holiness-quality of Eretz Yisrael. The holiness of Eretz Yisrael is not a separate concept from Eretz Yisrael itself — it is its essential nature. Both belong in the same package as different faces of the same holy-land reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["צדיקים אמיתיים", "מקום גניזה"], "reason": "The tzaddik and his burial place are inseparable in this Torah: it is specifically the 'true tzaddik' whose burial place acquires the holiness of Eretz Yisrael. The tzaddik is the agent through whom the identity of burial place with Eretz Yisrael is actualized. They belong to the same domain of holy-land identity."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 110, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְרוּחָנִיּוּת", "label_en": "Torah and Spirituality", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster represents the positive pole of Torah #110. The Rebbe opens with a definitional identity: 'הַתּוֹרָה הִיא רוּחָנִיּוּת' — Torah IS spirituality. The bechina marker 'הִיא' (IS) is the strongest possible identification in Hebrew — Torah and ruchaniyut are the same essential reality viewed from different angles. From this identity flows the next bechina: 'דָּבָר רוּחָנִי אֵינוֹ תּוֹפֵס מָקוֹם' — a spiritual thing takes no space. The phrase 'eino tofes makom' is not a consequence of ruchaniyut but a DEFINITION of it — what it means to be spiritual IS to be without spatial limit. The 'lo tofes makom' quality is therefore a bechina of ruchaniyut, not an eitza from it. The third node is 'sekhel ruchani' — the spiritual intellect of the person whose 'sikhlо ruchani' (intellect is spiritual). The teaching establishes that this person's sekhel shares the same essential nature as Torah's spirituality: 'yakhol litpos kol haTorah kulah ve-lo yishkach davar' — because the vessel (sekhel) and the content (Torah) are the same spiritual nature, they can coexist without displacement. The dominant node is Torah (תּוֹרָה) because it is named in the opening verse and is the hub connecting both ruchaniyut and sekhel ruchani through the identity relationship. The entire teaching circles back to Torah: at the end (line 6), the Rav Yosef story concludes 'ki lo yihyeh shikhah, ki davar ruchani eino tofes makom' — restating the Torah-Ruchaniyut-Lo Tofes Makom triple identity as the reason learning endures.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "רוחניות", "שכל רוחני", "לא תופס מקום"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 3, "רוחניות": 3, "שכל רוחני": 2, "לא תופס מקום": 2}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": [{"pair": ["תורה", "רוחניות"], "source_quote": "כִּי הַתּוֹרָה הִיא רוּחָנִיּוּת", "bechina_marker": "הִיא (identity copula — Torah IS spirituality)"}, {"pair": ["רוחניות", "לא תופס מקום"], "source_quote": "כִּי דָּבָר רוּחָנִי אֵינוֹ תּוֹפֵס מָקוֹם", "bechina_marker": "definitional identity — to be spiritual IS to take no space; repeated twice (lines 2 and 6) as the teaching's axiom"}, {"pair": ["תורה", "שכל רוחני"], "source_quote": "וַאֲשֶׁר זַךְ יָשָׁר פָּעֳלוֹ, וְשִׂכְלוֹ רוּחָנִי, יָכוֹל לִתְפֹּס כָּל הַתּוֹרָה כֻּלָּהּ", "bechina_marker": "parallel nature — the spiritual sekhel can hold all of Torah because both share the same ruchani essence; a physical vessel cannot hold a spiritual content unlimited, but a spiritual vessel can"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 110, "label": "מַמָּשׁוּת וְגַשְׁמִיּוּת", "label_en": "Materiality and Physicality", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster represents the negative pole of Torah #110 — the evil/deficiency mirror of Package 1. The Rebbe explicitly equates mamashut (substance/materiality) and gashmiyut (physicality) throughout the teaching, using them interchangeably in two parallel formulations: 'mamashut ve-gashmiyut be-divrei haTorah' (line 4) and 'mamashut ve-gashmiyut' (line 5). This repeated coupling is the Rebbe's own declaration that these are two expressions of the same essential nature — the antithesis of ruchaniyut. The third node is the word 'yamush' itself, which the Rebbe explicitly derives from the root of mamashut: 'yamush — mileshon mamashut' (yamush IS from the language of mamashut). This is a classic bechina move: deriving the essential nature of a biblical term from its etymological root, establishing that 'yamush' (departing from the mouth) IS mamashut (materialization). The bechina marker 'mileshon' (from the language of) functions identically to 'bechina' — it identifies the conceptual essence embedded in the word's form. The dominant node is mamashut (מַמָּשׁוּת) because it is the most frequently mentioned concept (appears 5 times across lines 3-6), it is the etymological source of 'yamush', and it is explicitly paired with gashmiyut as the negative counterforce to Torah's ruchaniyut. The good/evil mirror is perfect: Torah = ruchaniyut = spiritual sekhel = lo tofes makom (Package 1) vs. materialized Torah = mamashut = gashmiyut = yamush (Package 2).", "dominant_node": "מַמָּשׁוּת", "nodes": ["מַמָּשׁוּת", "גַּשְׁמִיּוּת", "יָמוּשׁ"], "node_degrees": {"מַמָּשׁוּת": 2, "גַּשְׁמִיּוּת": 2, "יָמוּשׁ": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechinot": [{"pair": ["מַמָּשׁוּת", "גַּשְׁמִיּוּת"], "source_quote": "מַמָּשׁוּת וְגַשְׁמִיּוּת בְּדִבְרֵי הַתּוֹרָה", "bechina_marker": "repeated coupling in two parallel formulations (lines 4 and 5) — the Rebbe uses them as synonyms, establishing shared essential nature"}, {"pair": ["יָמוּשׁ", "מַמָּשׁוּת"], "source_quote": "יָמוּשׁ – מִלְּשׁוֹן מַמָּשׁוּת. כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְיָמֵשׁ חֹשֶׁךְ", "bechina_marker": "מִלְּשׁוֹן (from the language/root of) — etymological bechina: yamush IS from mamashut, identifying the word's essential conceptual nature"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 112, "label": "דֶּרֶךְ וְזִוּוּג", "label_en": "The Path and the Celestial Union", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina edge in the Torah 112 dataset equates דרך (path/treading) with זיווג (celestial union). The context in line 31 of the source text is: 'דָּרַךְ כּוֹכָב מִיַּעֲקֹב וְקָם שֵׁבֶט מִיִּשְׂרָאֵל' (Numbers 24:17) — when prayer ascends through the correct tribal gate (שבט), the treading/path of the star from Jacob produces a celestial union above. דרך and זיווג share the same essential nature here: they are both expressions of the upward movement of prayer that connects and unites above. This is a positive, holy domain — the proper channeling of prayer through one's tribal path produces divine union above.", "dominant_node": "דרך", "nodes": ["דרך", "זווג"], "node_degrees": {"דרך": 1, "זווג": 1}, "source_edge": {"id": 1047, "proof": "דֶרֶךְ הוּא בְחִינוֹת זִוּוּג, מִלְּשׁוֹן (בְּרֵאשִׁית ו): \"כִּי הִשְׁחִית כָּל בָּשָׂר אֶת דַּרְכּוֹ\"."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 114, "label": "שָׁמַיִם וְצַדִּיק", "label_en": "Heaven and the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single existing bechina edge in the dataset (id: 2528) equates שמים (heaven/sky) with צדיק (the righteous person). The proof text is: 'שָׁמַיִם הוּא בְּחִינַת הַצַּדִּיק, עַל־שֵׁם אֵשׁ וּמַיִם, הַיְנוּ אַהֲבָה וְיִרְאָה, וְעַל־שֵׁם זֶה נִקְרָא הַצַּדִּיק שָׁמַיִם.' This is an explicit, canonical bechina statement: the sky is named heaven (שמים) because it contains fire (אש) and water (מים) — i.e., the two primordial opposites held in union — and the tzaddik embodies this same union of opposites as love (אהבה = fire) and awe/fear (יראה = water). The tzaddik IS the heavens because both are the union of fire and water, love and awe. The existing edge captures the direct pair; the gap analysis (torah_114_additions.json) proposes extending this component to include אש ומים and אהבה ויראה as bechina nodes within this same cluster. The dominant node is צדיק by centrality and because the entire Torah's argument revolves around the tzaddik's ability to draw down abundance for the world. This is firmly a 'good' polarity cluster — it represents the holy channel between heaven and earth.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["שמים", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"שמים": 1, "צדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["The source text extends this component: line 4 further equates שמים with אש ומים and those with אהבה ויראה. These are missing bechina edges that would expand this package to 4 nodes (שמים, צדיק, אש ומים, אהבה ויראה). See torah_114_additions.json for the proposed new edges and torah_114_new_packages.json for the expanded package."], "source_edge": {"id": 2528, "proof": "שָׁמַיִם הוּא בְחִינַת הַצַּדִּיק, עַל - שֵׁם אֵשׁ וּמַיִם, הַיְנוּ אַהֲבָה וְיִרְאָה, וְעַל - שֵׁם זֶה נִקְרָא הַצַּדִּיק שָׁמַיִם."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 115, "label": "מֹשֶׁה וְדַעַת כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "Moses and the Da'at of All Israel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina in this component (edge 774) equates Moshe with 'da'at kol Yisrael' — Moses IS the da'at (knowledge/wisdom) of all Israel. The teaching makes this identification in two places: line 7 (commentary on 'u'Moshe nigash el ha-arafel') and line 8 (restating the same point). The bechina marker is 'שֶׁהוּא בְחִינוֹת' — Moshe is an aspect of / is essentially da'at kol Yisrael. Moshe is the dominant node: he is named in the verse (Exodus 20) that anchors the entire teaching, he is the person who can approach the arafel while the nation stands back, and his essential quality is precisely da'at — the wisdom/discernment that enables a bar da'at to find God within the obstacle. The polarity is clearly good: Moshe approaching the arafel is the positive act of the teaching, the eitza recommendation. The da'at that belongs to Moshe is the quality needed to find God within the meni'ah. This is the holy wisdom pole of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "משה", "nodes": ["משה", "דעת כל ישראל"], "node_degrees": {"משה": 1, "דעת כל ישראל": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component cannot be merged with Package 2 by shared nature. Moshe/da'at and meni'ah/arafel/choshekh are the opposite poles of the teaching. Their connection is eitza (through da'at one can enter the meni'ah and find God there), not bechina. They do not share the same essential nature."], "source_edge": {"id": 774, "proof": "מֹשֶׁה, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת דַּעַת כָּל יִשְׂרָאֵל"}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 115, "label": "מְנִיעָה וַעֲרָפֶל וְחֹשֶׁךְ", "label_en": "The Obstacle, the Cloud-Darkness, and Concealment", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package clusters the three expressions of the same paradoxical reality: the obstacle (מניעה), the cloud-and-thick-darkness (ענן וערפל), and darkness (חושך). The dataset contains edge 2410 equating מניעה ↔ ענן וערפל. The source text provides the chain: 'מניעה הוא בחינת ענן וערפל, כי ענן וערפל הַיְנוּ חֹשֶׁךְ, חֹשֶׁךְ הוּא לְשׁוֹן מְנִיעָה' (line 4) — making all three a single conceptual cluster by two additional bechina markers (הַיְנוּ and לְשׁוֹן). The dominant node is מניעה: it is the teaching's central subject, named most frequently (appears 8+ times across lines 2-13), and it is what the arafel and choshekh are said to BE. The polarity is neutral rather than purely evil because of the teaching's crucial paradox: the meni'ah is simultaneously (a) the obstacle erected by middat hadin to prevent the unworthy from approaching God, AND (b) the hiding-place where God Himself conceals His presence. The same arafel is both the instrument of judgment and the vessel of divine love. God hides WITHIN the very obstacle (line 12: 'be-tokef ha-meni'ot be-atsman nistar HaShem'). This dual nature — obstacle AND divine hiding-place — prevents a simple 'evil' classification. The meni'ah is the locus of the teaching's tension: it is the negative pole relative to the nation that stands back, but it contains the positive divine presence relative to the one who has da'at and enters it.", "dominant_node": "מניעה", "nodes": ["מניעה", "ענן וערפל", "חושך"], "node_degrees": {"מניעה": 2, "ענן וערפל": 2, "חושך": 1}, "merge_notes": ["חושך is not in the dataset for reference 115 but is identified from the source text gap analysis (see torah_115_additions.json). It is included in this package because the source text explicitly equates it with ענן וערפל (via הַיְנוּ) and with מניעה (via לְשׁוֹן מְנִיעָה), making it the third node of the same essential cluster.", "This 3-node component (2 from dataset + 1 from gap analysis) is not merged with Package 1 because Moshe/da'at and meni'ah/arafel/choshekh are structurally opposite poles. They are joined by eitza, not bechina."], "source_edges": [{"id": 2410, "proof": "וּמְנִיעָה הוּא בְחִינַת עָנָן וַעֲרָפֶל, כִּי עָנָן וַעֲרָפֶל הַיְנוּ חׂשֶׁךְ, חׂשֶׁךְ הוּא לְשׁוֹן מְנִיעָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב (בְּרֵאשִׁית כ\"ב): \"וְלֹא חָשַֹכְתָּ\"."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 117, "label": "נָחָשׁ וְהַקְּלָלָה", "label_en": "The Serpent and the Curse", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "All three existing bechina edges in the dataset form a single connected chain: עֶבֶד↔אָרוּר (edge 431), אָרוּר↔נָחָשׁ (edge 2057), נָחָשׁ↔כְּנַעַן עֶבֶד עֲבָדִים (edge 1510). The chain reads: slave = cursed = serpent = Canaan slave of slaves. These are all explicitly identified as aspects of the same evil reality in Torah 117. The teaching declares (line 7): 'falsehood (שקר) is from the bechina of the serpent, which is called arur (cursed)' and (line 8): 'the slave is the bechina of Canaan slave of slaves, and he is the bechina of arur, the bechina of the serpent.' This cluster represents the evil principle — the serpent who brought death into the world, whose essence persists as curse, slavery, and the 9 out of 10 measures of sleep that came to the world. The dominant node is נָחָשׁ because it is the named archetypal figure (the serpent of Genesis 3) from which all other members of this cluster are derived as aspects. It connects to the most other nodes in the gap-expanded graph (degree 3: connected to כנען עבד עבדים, ארור, שקר). The gap analysis adds שֶׁקֶר (falsehood) and makes the direct עֶבֶד↔נָחָשׁ link explicit. The evil polarity is unambiguous: נחש, ארור, עבד, שקר are all marked as the negative force that Elijah will come to repel.", "dominant_node": "נחש", "nodes": ["נחש", "ארור", "עבד", "כנען עבד עבדים"], "node_degrees": {"נחש": 2, "ארור": 2, "עבד": 1, "כנען עבד עבדים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["All 4 nodes are in a single connected component from the 3 existing bechina edges. No merging of separate components was required.", "The gap analysis (torah_117_additions.json) proposes adding שֶׁקֶר (falsehood) as a 5th node in this package, based on line 7: 'הַשֶּׁקֶר, שֶׁהוּא מִבְּחִינַת הַנָּחָשׁ'. This would raise נָחָשׁ to degree 3.", "The gap analysis also proposes a direct עֶבֶד↔נָחָשׁ edge, making the chain fully explicit. With that addition, עֶבֶד would have degree 2.", "שֵׁנָה (sleep) is causally related to this package (the serpent brought death; sleep is 1/60 of death) but the relationship is eitza (causal), not bechina — sleep is NOT declared to be the bechina of the serpent in this Torah. Therefore שֵׁנָה belongs to a different analysis layer."], "source_edges": [{"id": 431, "node1_id": "עבד", "node2_id": "ארור", "proof": "וְעֶבֶד הוּא בְחִינַת (בְּרֵאשִׁית ט): \"כְּנַעַן עֶבֶד עֲבָדִים\", וְהוּא בְחִינַת אָרוּר (שָׁם)"}, {"id": 1510, "node1_id": "נחש", "node2_id": "כנען עבד עבדים", "proof": "הַנָּחָשׁ, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת כְּנַעַן עֶבֶד עֲבָדִים"}, {"id": 2057, "node1_id": "ארור", "node2_id": "נחש", "proof": "אָרוּר (שָׁם), בְחִינַת הַנָּחָשׁ"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 118, "label": "צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר, מֹשֶׁה, וּמָשִׁיחַ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik of the Generation, Moses, and Mashiach", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #118 establishes a single, unified identity equation: every tzaddik of the generation is a bechina of Moses, Moses is a bechina of Mashiach, and therefore the Torah novelties (chidushei Torah) that the tzaddik innovates are themselves a bechina of Mashiach — because Mashiach's spirit hovers over them. The gematria proof (Shiloh = Moshe) and the Talmudic practice of Tannaim calling each other 'Moshe' are both cited to ground this identity. All four nodes (צדיק הדור, משה, משיח, חדושי תורה שמחדש) form one connected component via three bechina edges and are expressions of a single domain: the perpetual re-embodiment of the Moshe-Mashiach archetype in every generation through Torah renewal. The dominant node is משיח/משה (co-dominant, each with degree 2), with the tzaddik and the chidushei Torah as the two poles by which this archetype is accessed in lived reality.", "dominant_node": "משה", "nodes": ["צדיק הדור", "משה", "משיח", "חדושי תורה שמחדש"], "node_degrees": {"משה": 2, "משיח": 2, "צדיק הדור": 1, "חדושי תורה שמחדש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 119, "label": "שַׁבָּת וְדַעַת", "label_en": "Shabbat and Divine Knowledge", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina in Torah #119 is the equation: שַׁבָּת הוּא בְּחִינוֹת דַּעַת. Shabbat is the aspect/dimension of daas (divine knowledge/intellect) — on ordinary weekdays the people 'wander and gather in foolishness' (שָׁטוּ הָעָם וְלָקְטוּ — which the Zohar reads as שְׁטוּתָא, folly), but on Shabbat daas flows to every person. These two nodes are structurally identical: Shabbat is the temporal vessel of daas, and daas is the spiritual content of Shabbat. Together they form the single bechina cluster of this Torah, which is the foundation from which the eitza chain of compassion is derived.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "דעת"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 1, "דעת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 120, "label": "סֵפֶר וַעֲשִׂיָּה וְנַעֲשֶׂה", "label_en": "The Book, Action, and Na'aseh", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package groups three nodes that are all aspects of the same structural domain: the written/material dimension of Torah engagement. The dataset chains them as follows — learning from a book IS a bechina of עֲשִׂיָּה (action/making, edge 2454), and עֲשִׂיָּה IS a bechina of נַעֲשֶׂה (the 'we will do' commitment given at Sinai, edge 2114). The teaching draws on the Midrash: Israel forfeited נַעֲשֶׂה by making the golden calf, and this forfeiture means that learning from a book — which is of the same essential dimension as that forfeited commitment — no longer carries the same spiritual power to arouse the person. The polarity is 'neutral' rather than 'evil' because the Torah does not condemn books or action — it simply explains their structural limitation after the sin of the golden calf. The book and the act of learning from it are not inherently evil; they represent the outer, physical, action-layer of Torah engagement. The dominant node is עֲשִׂיָּה because it is the hub connecting both other nodes (degree 2): it is the concept-bridge between the act of reading a book and the biblical category of נַעֲשֶׂה.", "dominant_node": "עשיה", "nodes": ["כשלומד מתוך הספר", "עשיה", "נעשה"], "node_degrees": {"עשיה": 2, "כשלומד מתוך הספר": 1, "נעשה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This component of 3 nodes is internally connected through the shared hub עֲשִׂיָּה. No external merges were performed.", "The component was NOT merged with Package 2 (נִשְׁמַע cluster) despite both being small (2-3 node) components. The reason: the two packages are the structural OPPOSITES of each other within this Torah's argument — Package 1 is the fallen/limited dimension (action/book, lost at the Calf), Package 2 is the surviving/empowered dimension (hearing, the crown that remained). Merging them would destroy the essential teaching. Per framework.md §3.2 rule 2 and the merge criteria (shared nature only, not eitza), they may not be merged: their relationship is one of contrast and opposition within the teaching's argument, not shared essential nature.", "The Torah does not assign an evil counterpart or mirror to this package. The 'falling short' of book-learning is a structural/historical limitation, not a demonic force."], "source_edges": [{"id": 2454, "node1_id": "כשלומד מתוך הספר", "node2_id": "עשיה", "proof": "כְּשֶׁלּוֹמֵד מִתּוֹךְ הַסֵּפֶר שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת עֲשִׂיָּה"}, {"id": 2114, "node1_id": "עשיה", "node2_id": "נעשה", "proof": "עֲשִׂיָּה, שֶׁהוּא בְחִינַת נַעֲשֶׂה"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 120, "label": "שְׁמִיעָה מִפֶּה הַצַּדִּיק וְנִשְׁמַע", "label_en": "Hearing from the Tzaddik's Mouth and Nishma", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups the two nodes that are both aspects of the same holy, surviving dimension of Torah engagement: the oral/aural transmission from the tzaddik. The dataset records: hearing from the mouth of the tzaddik IS a bechina of נִשְׁמַע (the 'we will hear' commitment from Sinai, edge 2231). The teaching explains that while Israel forfeited נַעֲשֶׂה (action/book, Package 1), the crown of נִשְׁמַע remained with them. Therefore, hearing the tzaddik speak — which is an act of the same essential dimension as that preserved crown — retains the full spiritual power to arouse and awaken the person. The polarity is 'good' because this is the preserved holy dimension: the keter (crown) that survived the sin of the golden calf. The dominant node is נִשְׁמַע because it is the biblical/Sinaitic category that gives the entire package its theological grounding; without it, the statement about the tzaddik's mouth would be a practical observation rather than a cosmic-structural claim. The node כְּשֶׁשּׁוֹמֵעַ מִפֶּה הַצַּדִּיק is the existential application of that Sinaitic crown in the present generation.", "dominant_node": "נשמע", "nodes": ["כששומע מפה הצדיק", "נשמע"], "node_degrees": {"נשמע": 1, "כששומע מפה הצדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is a 2-node component from a single bechina edge (id: 2231). Per the algorithm, small (1-2 node) components are candidates for merging by shared nature only. This component was NOT merged with Package 1 for the reason stated above: these two components are the two opposing structural poles of the Torah's argument.", "The gap analysis confirms no additional bechina edges involving נִשְׁמַע exist in the source text for Torah 120. The teaching does not add further bechinot to this cluster — it states the equation and draws the practical conclusion (therefore one must travel to the tzaddik rather than rely only on books).", "The good/evil mirror: Package 1 (עֲשִׂיָּה/נַעֲשֶׂה) is the dimmed/lost dimension — while not evil, it is the 'crown removed' side. Package 2 (נִשְׁמַע) is the preserved crown. The teaching's structure is: lost crown vs. preserved crown, not good vs. evil per se. However, Package 2 clearly represents the path of holiness and is therefore marked 'good'."], "source_edges": [{"id": 2231, "node1_id": "כששומע מפה הצדיק", "node2_id": "נשמע", "proof": "כְּשֶׁשּׁוֹמֵעַ מִפֶּה הַצַּדִּיק, הוּא בְחִינַת נִשְׁמַע"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 122, "label": "נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל וֶאֱמֶת", "label_en": "The Eternal of Israel and Truth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's central positive thesis is that God's nitzachon — 'נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל' — IS truth (אֱמֶת). The verse is re-read: the Eternal One of Israel does not lie. God does not abandon truth even when contending or prevailing. These two concepts — divine nitzachon (נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל / מִדַּת הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא בְּנִצָּחוֹן) and אֱמֶת — are the two faces of the same reality in this Torah: God's eternal nature is not separable from His truthfulness. The structural equivalence 'הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא אַף בְּהַנִּצָּחוֹן הוּא אֱמֶת' operates exactly as a bechina identification: it does not say that divine nitzachon produces truth (eitza), but rather that divine nitzachon IS truth — they are the same thing from two angles. The polarity is 'good' because this is the holy dimension of the teaching: the attribute of the divine that transcends the human failing. The dominant node is נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל because the entire Torah is an explication of this phrase from the verse, and because it carries the double-meaning (eternal nature + winning) that makes the teaching possible. Within this teaching, אֱמֶת is the paired counterpart: 'does not lie' = IS truth.", "dominant_node": "נצח ישראל", "nodes": ["נצח ישראל", "אמת"], "node_degrees": {"נצח ישראל": 1, "אמת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is a 2-node component derived from the implicit bechina structure of the source text (no formal בְּחִינַת keyword present — see torah_122_additions.json for proposed edge). The two nodes form the positive pole of the teaching's binary axis.", "This package was NOT merged with Package 2 (the human nitzachon/falsehood cluster) despite both being minimal 2-node components. The reason: these two packages are the two structural poles of the Torah's argument — divine truth vs. human falsehood. They are definitionally opposed; merging them would obliterate the teaching's entire point. Their relationship is one of radical contrast, not shared essential nature. Per framework.md §3.2 and the merge criterion (shared nature only, not opposition), no merge is warranted.", "The good/evil mirror: Package 1 (divine nitzachon = truth) is directly mirrored by Package 2 (human nitzachon = inability to bear truth). This is one of the most explicit good/evil mirror structures in the corpus — the same concept (nitzachon) appears in both its holy form (God's eternal nature) and its fallen form (human contentiousness that rejects truth). The two packages are the two faces of the same word from the verse, split along the axis of holy vs. human.", "Note: The dataset for Torah #122 contains zero edges (reference 122 is not yet populated in torahData.js). The packages in this file are derived entirely from the gap analysis of the source text and represent the proposed dataset enrichment. The source edges listed below are proposed additions."], "source_edges": [{"id": "proposed_122_01", "node1_id": "נצח ישראל", "node2_id": "אמת", "proof": "הַקָּדוֹשׁ בָּרוּךְ הוּא אַף בְּהַנִּצָּחוֹן הוּא אֱמֶת, וְאֵינוֹ מְשַׁקֵּר — וְזֶהוּ: וְגַם נֵצַח יִשְׂרָאֵל לֹא יְשַׁקֵּר", "status": "proposed — no formal בְּחִינַת keyword in source; structural bechina equation inferred from text logic and hermeneutic re-reading of verse"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 122, "volume": "lm2", "label": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת זָרוֹת וּקְלִפּוֹת", "label_en": "Foreign Thoughts and Husks", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly equates three concepts using 'הוּא' (IS) language — the language of bechina: (1) every מַחֲשָׁבָה זָרָה (foreign thought) IS a קְלִפָּה (spiritual husk), and (2) the קְלִפָּה IS a קוֹמָה שְׁלֵמָה (complete spiritual stature/form). These three are explicitly stated to be the same thing. All belong to the evil domain: they are the opposing forces that invade the space of prayer and must be defeated. The foreign thought, the husk, and the complete-form are three faces of the same spiritual opponent.", "dominant_node": "מחשבות זרות", "nodes": ["מחשבות זרות", "קליפה", "קומה שלמה"], "node_degrees": {"מחשבות זרות": 2, "קליפה": 2, "קומה שלמה": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 122, "label": "נִצָּחוֹן בָּשָׂר וָדָם וְדַחִיַּת הָאֱמֶת", "label_en": "Human Contentiousness and the Rejection of Truth", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The teaching's negative thesis is that the attribute of nitzachon (contentiousness, the drive to win) in human beings is structurally incompatible with truth. 'מִדַּת הַנִּצָּחוֹן אֵינָהּ סוֹבֶלֶת הָאֱמֶת' — the attribute of winning/contention does not tolerate truth. Even when truth is plainly visible, a contentious person will reject it because of their nitzachon. The teaching names this explicitly and declares it 'מְבֹרָר מְאֹד' (very well-established). The two concepts in this cluster — מִדַּת הַנִּצָּחוֹן (the human attribute of contentiousness) and דְּחִיַּת הָאֱמֶת / שֶׁקֶר (the rejection of truth / falsehood) — are structurally paired: in this Torah, the contentious mode IS the truth-rejecting mode. They share the same essential nature in the domain of human failing. The polarity is 'evil' because this is the negative human attribute — the teaching presents it as a known flaw, something that departs from God's standard. The dominant node is מדת הנצחון because it is the named quality being dissected; the rejection of truth is its necessary consequence/manifestation (which at the bechina level means: human nitzachon IS the rejection of truth — they are two descriptions of the same human failing).", "dominant_node": "מדת הנצחון", "nodes": ["מדת הנצחון", "דחיית האמת"], "node_degrees": {"מדת הנצחון": 1, "דחיית האמת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is a 2-node component derived from the implicit bechina structure of the source text. No formal בְּחִינַת language is present in the source for this pair — see torah_122_additions.json for proposed edge and the careful distinction between bechina and eitza formulations in the source.", "NOT merged with Package 1 — see Package 1 merge_notes for full reasoning.", "This package is the evil mirror of Package 1. The same word (נִצָּחוֹן) takes a holy form in Package 1 (God's eternal winning = truth) and a fallen form here (human contentiousness = rejection of truth). This is the good/evil mirror operating at maximum compression — the entire teaching is structured around the bifurcation of a single concept into its divine and human expressions.", "Node naming note: The source text says 'יִדְחֶה אוֹתוֹ מֵחֲמַת הַנִּצָּחוֹן' — he pushes truth away because of the contentiousness. The active noun 'דְּחִיַּת הָאֱמֶת' (rejection of truth) is the implicit complementary node to מִדַּת הַנִּצָּחוֹן in this Torah. The phrase שֶׁקֶר (falsehood) is not used explicitly in the text (only 'לֹא יְשַׁקֵּר' — 'does not lie' — as the negation in the verse), but the semantic content is equivalent: human nitzachon = lying = rejection of truth. The node is proposed as 'דחיית האמת' to match the source's specific phrasing."], "source_edges": [{"id": "proposed_122_02", "node1_id": "מדת הנצחון", "node2_id": "דחיית האמת", "proof": "מִדַּת הַנִּצָּחוֹן אֵינָהּ סוֹבֶלֶת הָאֱמֶת, כִּי אַף אִם יֵרָאֶה לָעֵינַיִם דְּבַר אֱמֶת, יִדְחֶה אוֹתוֹ מֵחֲמַת הַנִּצָּחוֹן", "status": "proposed — no formal בְּחִינַת keyword in source; the relationship is stated as causal/behavioral in the text but the structural equivalence (human nitzachon = truth-rejection) justifies a bechina classification at the level of shared essential nature — see torah_122_additions.json for full analysis"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 122, "volume": "lm2", "label": "תְּפִלָּה כְּסֵדֶר וְגִבּוּרַת הַהִלּוּךְ", "label_en": "Ordered Prayer and the Strength of Forward Movement", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe presents a set of concepts that all share the same essential nature — the positive force that defeats the foreign thoughts: תְּפִלָּה כְּסֵדֶר (prayer in order) IS the warrior's march (דֶּרֶךְ הִלּוּכוֹ), and this movement IS itself the defeat. The גִּבּוּר (warrior/brave person) who passes through the ambushers without looking back (אֵינוֹ מַשְׁגִּיחַ / לִבְלִי לְהִסְתַּכֵּל) is the same essential figure as the person praying in order. The not-looking (אֵינוֹ מַשְׁגִּיחַ) is the bechina of the forward movement — they are two expressions of the same stance of non-engagement. Together these concepts define the domain of victorious prayer as a single unbroken forward movement.", "dominant_node": "תפלה כסדר", "nodes": ["תפלה כסדר", "דרך הילוכו", "גיבור", "אינו משגיח"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה כסדר": 3, "דרך הילוכו": 2, "גיבור": 1, "אינו משגיח": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 123, "label": "נָבָל וְהַתּוֹרָה", "label_en": "Nabal and Torah — True Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The only bechina edge in Torah #123 equates נָבָל with תּוֹרָה. The Rebbe establishes the equation in two complementary ways: (1) נָבָל as an acrostic — ל\"ב נְתִיבוֹת (the 32 Paths of Wisdom), which is the totality/generality (כְּלָלִיּוּת) of Torah; (2) נָבָל as linguistically related to נוֹבְלוֹת — 'Torah is called noblot chochma ha'elyona' (Bereishit Rabbah 17), meaning Torah is the fallen-off sparks of the supernal wisdom, the accessible face of divine Chochma. Both paths arrive at the same conclusion: naval IS a bechina of Torah — they share the essential nature of true, divine wisdom that supersedes and nullifies all external wisdoms. This is the structural heart of the teaching: the word 'naval' (seemingly 'vile/degraded') in Onkelos's translation of Devarim 32:6 encodes the highest reality — Torah, the true wisdom before which all human wisdoms are null.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "נבל"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "נבל": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw connected component: single edge {נבל, תורה} — one component of 2 nodes. No merging required or possible; this is the entire bechina graph for Torah #123.", "Although the Torah discusses חָכְמוֹת (wisdoms), כְּפִירָה (heresy), אֱמֶת (truth), תַּם וְיָשָׁר (simplicity), and קַבָּלַת הַתּוֹרָה (receiving the Torah), none of these appear as nodes in bechina edges for reference 123 — they operate exclusively through eitza (cause-and-effect) edges or narrative description. Per framework rules, packages are built on bechina edges only; eitza-only nodes are not assigned to packages."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 126, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ יְתֵרָה וַהֲסְתַּלְּקוּת הַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "The Extra Soul and the Departure of the Tzaddik", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The entire teaching rests on a single structural bechina: the grief of the Chavrayya upon Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai's impending departure IS a bechina of Shabbat-departure grief — specifically, the grief over losing the Nefesh Yeteirah (extra Shabbat soul) at Motzaei Shabbat. The Talmud (Beitzah 16) derives from 'שָׁבַת וַיִּנָּפַשׁ' (Exodus 31:17) that 'כֵּיוָן שֶׁשָּׁבַת וַי אָבְדָה נֶפֶשׁ' — when Shabbat rests/ceases, 'vay' (woe) — the soul departs. The Rebbe's teaching draws the structural equivalence: the Chavrayya's relationship with Rabbi Shimon parallels Israel's relationship with the Nefesh Yeteirah on Shabbat. Both involve an elevated spiritual state of great pleasure and delight (תַּעֲנוּג / שַׁעֲשׁוּעִים) given through a sacred channel (Shabbat / the Tzaddik), and both produce intense anticipatory grief (גַּעֲגּוּעִים / צַעַר הַהִסְתַּלְּקוּת) upon the perception that this elevated state will end. The polarity is 'neutral' rather than 'good' or 'evil' because the teaching is a structural observation — it identifies a universal phenomenology of elevated spiritual experience and the longing it generates, not a prescription for or against anything. The Tzaddik himself (Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai), the Nefesh Yeteirah, Shabbat, and the quality of spiritual delight (תַּעֲנוּג) all belong to the domain of holiness, but the teaching's argument is structural/descriptive rather than normative/prescriptive. No evil mirror is present in this Torah — it is a short, purely structural analogy without any opposing force or negative polarity.", "dominant_node": "נפש יתרה", "nodes": ["נפש יתרה", "הסתלקות הצדיק", "שבת וינפש", "געגועים על אבדת הנפש", "תענוג מהצדיק"], "node_degrees": {"נפש יתרה": 2, "שבת וינפש": 2, "הסתלקות הצדיק": 1, "געגועים על אבדת הנפש": 1, "תענוג מהצדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["There are no dataset-derived components to merge. This package is constructed entirely from the gap analysis of the source text.", "All nodes in this package share the same essential nature: they are aspects of the structural phenomenon of 'elevated spiritual intimacy generating anticipatory grief upon its perceived loss.' The Nefesh Yeteirah, the Tzaddik's teachings, Shabbat-departure, and longing/yearning are all aspects of the same underlying reality that the Rebbe is equating through his bechina statement.", "No merging decision needed — only one package exists for this Torah."], "source_edges": [], "note_on_source_edges": "No bechina edges for reference 126 exist in torahData.js. The nodes and their relationships identified here are drawn from the gap analysis (torah_126_additions.json) and reflect the bechina statement in line 2 of the source text: 'וְהוּא בְּחִינַת שָׁבַת וַיִּנָּפַשׁ — כֵּיוָן שֶׁשָּׁבַת וַי אָבְדָה נֶפֶשׁ.' These nodes and edges should be added to torahData.js."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 128, "label": "הַשְׁפָּלַת הָעֵינַיִם וְעֵינֵי הַיֵּצֶר", "label_en": "Lowering the Eyes and the Yetzer's Eyes", "polarity": "mixed", "reason": "Torah #128 presents a single conceptual equation: the act of a partially-righteous person who lowers his eyes to avoid gazing at women (מַשְׁפִּיל עֵינָיו) is a bechina of 'כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא' — blinding the eyes of the yetzer hara (of sexual desire). These two concepts share the same essential nature: they are both the same act of ocular suppression, one framed in human behavioral terms and the other in the Talmudic metaphor of blinding the captured yetzer. The package is 'mixed' in polarity because it encompasses both the good impulse (yirat Hashem causing the lowering of eyes, the positive act of restraint) and the evil force (the yetzer hara of niyuf that remains and looks sideways even after its eyes are blinded). The dominant node is the Talmudic phrase כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא since the entire Torah is structured as an explanation of what that Aramaic phrase means behaviorally. The yetzer's persistence — that it 'looks from the side' (מִסְתַּכֵּל מִן הַצַּד) even after its eyes are blinded — is not a separate package but the completing half of the same bechina: the blinded yetzer = the somewhat-righteous man who steals a sideways glance. Both aspects belong to one cluster because they are two descriptions of the same reality.", "dominant_node": "כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא", "nodes": ["כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא", "הַשְׁפָּלַת עֵינַיִם", "מְסַמֵּא עֵינֵי הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע", "יֵצֶר הָרָע דְּנִאוּף", "מִסְתַּכֵּל מִן הַצַּד"], "node_degrees": {"כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא": 3, "הַשְׁפָּלַת עֵינַיִם": 2, "מְסַמֵּא עֵינֵי הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע": 2, "יֵצֶר הָרָע דְּנִאוּף": 1, "מִסְתַּכֵּל מִן הַצַּד": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_derived": true, "source_note": "All nodes and edges for this package are derived from source-text analysis only. None exist in torahData.js. The bechina connections inferred from the text are: (A) כַּחְלִינְהוּ לְעֵינֵהּ דְּיִצְרָא ↔ הַשְׁפָּלַת עֵינַיִם [Line 2-3: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת... שֶׁמְּסַמֵּא עֵינֵי הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע']; (B) הַשְׁפָּלַת עֵינַיִם ↔ מְסַמֵּא עֵינֵי הַיֵּצֶר הָרָע [same passage — the act of suppressing one's gaze IS the blinding of the yetzer's eyes]; (C) יֵצֶר הָרָע דְּנִאוּף ↔ מִסְתַּכֵּל מִן הַצַּד [Line 3: even after blinding, the yetzer remains and looks sideways — this sideways-glancing IS the blinded-yet-surviving yetzer]."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 129, "label": "אֶרֶץ וֶאֱמוּנָה — הַצַּדִּיק כְּאֶרֶץ", "label_en": "Land and Faith — the Tzaddik as Land", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina edge in the dataset establishes the foundational identity of this Torah: ארץ ↔ אמונה. The teaching opens with this identification (Psalm 37: 'שְׁכָן אֶרֶץ וּרְעֵה אֱמוּנָה') and the entire Torah is built upon it. Crucially, the source text adds a second-order bechina that is not yet in the dataset: the Tzaddik is a bechina of ארץ ('כְּשֶׁדָּבוּק לְהַצַּדִּיק וּמַאֲמִין בּוֹ, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת אֶרֶץ'). This three-node cluster — ארץ, אמונה, and הצדיק — forms one conceptual package: they all share the same essential nature. They are the 'nourishing reality' that absorbs and transforms whoever enters/attaches to it. ארץ is the pivot node connecting אמונה (equated directly) and הצדיק (equated as a bechina of ארץ). Eretz Yisrael is also explicitly brought as a parallel (line 7: 'וְכֵן אֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל בְּעַצְמָהּ, יֵשׁ לָהּ גַּם כֵּן הַכֹּחַ הַזֶּה') — it participates in the same ארץ nature. ארץ ישראל is therefore the extension of ארץ in the physical domain, and belongs to this same package.", "dominant_node": "ארץ", "nodes": ["ארץ", "אמונה", "הצדיק", "ארץ ישראל"], "node_degrees": {"ארץ": 3, "אמונה": 2, "הצדיק": 1, "ארץ ישראל": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package is formed from one explicit bechina edge in the dataset (ארץ↔אמונה, edge #294) plus two bechina identifications found in the source text not yet in the dataset.", "הצדיק is identified as a bechina of ארץ in line 6: 'כְּשֶׁדָּבוּק לְהַצַּדִּיק וּמַאֲמִין בּוֹ, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת אֶרֶץ'. This is explicit bechina language. The Tzaddik IS ארץ — he has the same transformative absorption capacity. This bechina edge (הצדיק↔ארץ) is missing from the dataset and is proposed in torah_129_additions.json.", "ארץ ישראל is treated as co-extensive with ארץ in this Torah. Line 7 says Eretz Yisrael 'has this same power' (the power of ארץ/אמונה to absorb and transform inhabitants). It is the same package membership — a face of ארץ in the physical/national domain.", "The teaching's analogies (vegetation eaten by animals, animals eaten by humans, food distributing to organs) are all metaphors for the MECHANISM — they are not bechina-equated nodes but illustrative comparisons. They do not form a separate bechina package.", "No small components to merge — this Torah has only 1 bechina edge in the dataset; the package is formed by combining the dataset edge with the gap-analysis findings."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 129, "label": "מֹחַ וְדַעַת קְדוֹשָׁה — תְּרֵין רֵעִין", "label_en": "Holy Mind and Knowledge — the Two Inseparable Companions", "polarity": "good", "reason": "In line 9, the Rebbe explicitly identifies 'תְּרֵין רֵעִין דְּלָא מִתְפָּרְשִׁין' (the two inseparable companions from Shir HaShirim 5:1 'אִכְלוּ רֵעִים') as 'הַמֹּחִין הַקְּדוֹשִׁים' — the holy minds/intellect: 'שֶׁהֵם הַמֹּחִין הַקְּדוֹשִׁים'. This is bechina identification language ('שֶׁהֵם' = they are). The Rebbe then further specifies: 'שֶׁהוּא הַמֹּחַ וְהַדַּעַת' — the mochin are specifically מוח (mind) and דעת (knowledge/awareness). These are not cause-and-effect; they are three names for the same spiritual reality: the two-companions = the holy mochin = מוח + דעת. This cluster — תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין, מוחין קדושים, מוח, דעת — forms the second package of this Torah. It represents the spiritual 'dominant partner' in the eating/transformation metaphor: the holy mind that absorbs the body and transforms it into its own nature, just as ארץ/אמונה absorbs those who dwell within it.", "dominant_node": "מוחין קדושים", "nodes": ["תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין", "מוחין קדושים", "מוח", "דעת"], "node_degrees": {"מוחין קדושים": 2, "תרין רעין דלא מתפרשין": 1, "מוח": 1, "דעת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["All four bechina identifications for this package are from the source text (not yet in the dataset). They belong in torah_129_additions.json as proposed new bechina edges.", "The Rebbe explicitly says 'תְּרֵין רֵעִין דְּלָא מִתְפָּרְשִׁין, שֶׁהֵם הַמֹּחִין הַקְּדוֹשִׁים' — this is canonical bechina language (שהם = they are).", "Then: 'שֶׁהוּא הַמֹּחַ וְהַדַּעַת' — the holy mochin = מוח + דעת specifically.", "This package is the parallel to the צדיק/ארץ/אמונה package: just as ארץ/אמונה/הצדיק is the external transformative reality (one attaches to a Tzaddik), מוח/דעת/תרין רעין is the internal transformative reality (one's mind absorbs and transforms the body). The teaching bridges both: external closeness to the Tzaddik (Package 1) AND internal dominance of mind over body (Package 2) both work through the same eatting/transformation principle.", "No merge with Package 1: these are distinct conceptual clusters. Package 1 concerns the Tzaddik/Land/Faith as the 'nourishing reality'. Package 2 concerns the mind/intellect as the internal agent of transformation. They are connected by the common mechanism (bechina of eating) but are not themselves bechinot of each other in this Torah."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 131, "label": "כָּבוֹד וּמַלְכוּת וְצֶדֶק", "label_en": "Honor, Kingship, and Justice", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #131 opens with the injunction to fear and dread כבוד (honor/glory) because it is a great danger. The Rebbe's first conceptual move is to equate כבוד with מלכות: 'כָּבוֹד הוּא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת הַדָּן אֶת הַכֹּל' (Honor is a bechina of Kingship, which judges all things — citing Psalm 24:7 'מֶלֶךְ הַכָּבוֹד'). In line 3, the Rebbe continues: 'צֶדֶק מַלְכוּתָא קַדִּישָׁא, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת כָּבוֹד, מֶלֶךְ הַכָּבוֹד כַּנַּ\"ל' — identifying צדק as מלכותא קדישא (holy kingship, the standard Zoharic equation of the sefirah Malkhut with the name צדק), and then identifying this entire cluster as a bechina of כבוד. The phrase 'שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת כָּבוֹד' is unambiguous bechina language. The מאזני צדק (scales of justice) then become the weighing instrument of this holy-kingship/honor — it is through כבוד/מלכות/צדק that every person is weighed and judged. These three concepts — כבוד (honor/glory), מלכות (kingship), and צדק (justice / holy kingship) — share the same essential nature: they are three faces of the divine attribute of sovereign judgment. They are all expressions of the same underlying reality: God's royal glory that scrutinizes and weighs all things. The polarity is 'good' because these all refer to the holy (קדישא) dimension: צדק מלכותא קדישא, כבוד as divine glory (מֶלֶךְ הַכָּבוֹד), and מלכות as the holy attribute. The danger of כבוד discussed in the teaching is not that כבוד itself is evil — it is that receiving honor improperly can trigger the judgment of this holy sovereignty against the person. כבוד is the dominant node because the Torah is introduced as a teaching about כבוד specifically, and all other equivalences are anchored to it.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["כבוד", "מלכות", "צדק"], "node_degrees": {"כבוד": 2, "מלכות": 2, "צדק": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package begins with one dataset bechina edge (ID 1826: כבוד ↔ מלכות) forming a 2-node component.", "The source text line 3 contains the additional bechina: 'צֶדֶק מַלְכוּתָא קַדִּישָׁא, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת כָּבוֹד' — equating צדק (= מלכות קדישא) with כבוד. This uses explicit bechina language ('שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת') and expands the component by one node: צדק is connected to both מלכות (by the Zoharic equation צדק = מלכות קדישא) and to כבוד (by the Rebbe's explicit bechina statement).", "מאזני צדק (scales of justice) appears in line 3 but is the instrument of judgment — it is named for צדק but is itself a mechanism (eitza context: through the מאזנים one is weighed). It is not equated with any of the three bechina nodes via bechina language, so it is excluded from the package nodes.", "מכובדי (line 2, from the Talmudic drasha on Shemot 29:43 — 'do not read bichvodi but bimchubadai') appears as a homiletic pivot, not a conceptually equated node.", "No small components require merging — Torah 131 has only one connected component in the dataset.", "The source text appears truncated (ends mid-sentence at line 3). Additional bechina statements may exist in the missing text that are not yet captured. The gap analysis reflects only what is available in the extant 3 lines."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 132, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וְהַמַּעְיָן", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Spring", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah consists of a single bechina equation: the tzaddik IS the spring (מעיין). Both share the same essential nature — a spiritual/life-giving force that does not flow uniformly or visibly but follows hidden underground channels, emerging with full power in distant places while simultaneously nourishing (invisibly) those over whom it passes underground. The tzaddik who is 'famous' in one place and 'unknown' in the adjacent place IS the spring that gushes visibly in one location but flows hidden underground through the neighboring terrain. The specific nodes that share this nature are: (1) הצדיק — the tzaddik himself as the carrier of the spiritual force; (2) המעיין — the spring/fountain as the Zoharic image of the same reality; (3) פרסום הצדיק — the tzaddik's fame/public recognition, which IS the spring's visible gushing forth (נביעה); (4) היעלמות הצדיק — the tzaddik's hiddenness/lack of recognition, which IS the spring flowing underground (תחות ארעא); (5) השפעה נסתרת — the hidden influence, which IS the watering of tree roots underground (השקאת שרשי אילנא) — even in the hidden phase the spring/tzaddik nourishes. These five concepts are all different expressions of the same metaphysical reality: a tzaddik's spiritual power flows like water, undiminished by hiddenness, nourishing the world through both visible and invisible channels. The package polarity is 'good' because the tzaddik and his influence are the subject of the teaching, and the underground phase is not evil but mysterious/hidden-good (the tree roots are nourished even then).", "dominant_node": "הצדיק", "nodes": ["הצדיק", "המעיין", "פרסום הצדיק", "היעלמות הצדיק", "השפעה נסתרת"], "node_degrees": {"הצדיק": 4, "המעיין": 4, "פרסום הצדיק": 1, "היעלמות הצדיק": 1, "השפעה נסתרת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["No dataset edges exist for Torah #132. All nodes are proposed from source text analysis. The five nodes form a single natural bechina cluster: tzaddik = spring, tzaddik's fame = spring gushing visibly, tzaddik's hiddenness = spring underground, hidden influence = watering of tree roots. No small isolated components require merging — the entire teaching is one integrated metaphor.", "הצדיק and המעיין are co-dominant (both have degree 4 conceptually — each maps to all aspects of the other), but הצדיק is chosen as dominant_node because the tzaddik is the explicit human subject of the teaching; the spring is the metaphorical image.", "פרסום הצדיק (the tzaddik's fame/recognition) = נביעת המעיין בגלוי (the spring's visible gushing): these are the same reality — the moment when the hidden spiritual force breaks through to visible public recognition.", "היעלמות הצדיק (the tzaddik's being hidden/unrecognized in the neighboring country) = הליכה תחות ארעא (the spring flowing underground): same nature — the force is present but invisible to those directly above it.", "השפעה נסתרת (hidden influence) = השקאת שרשי אילנא (watering tree roots underground): even in the hidden/underground phase, the tzaddik's force nourishes those spiritually rooted in his path — this is the positive resolution of the apparent paradox of hiddenness."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 134, "label": "אֲמִירַת תּוֹרָה וְנִאוּף", "label_en": "Torah Teaching and Adultery — Sekhel as Seed", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This Torah is built on a single central bechina: the act of saying Torah to someone whose intellect cannot receive it IS בְּחִינַת נִאוּף (adultery/illegitimate seminal emission). The Rebbe equates the process of Torah transmission with a biological-spiritual generative act: the teacher transmits 'drops of sekhel' (טִפֵּי הַשֵּׂכֶל) into the student, exactly as a father transmits seed into a mother. When the recipient's sekhel is fit, this generates spiritual offspring (new understanding, new deeds at the correct level). When it is not fit, the transmission is either (a) entirely barren — 'נִקְרָא לְבַטָּלָה' (called in vain — nothing is generated at all), or (b) generates something pasul/pagum (flawed/disqualified) — 'נִאוּף מַמָּשׁ' (actual adultery: the student acts on the teaching but at the wrong level for his madrega). The package is marked NEUTRAL because the core bechina (Torah-saying = seed-transmission) is a structural description of how teaching works — it is neither intrinsically good nor evil. Both the positive dimension (appropriate transmission generates proper offspring) and the negative dimension (inappropriate transmission is niuf) emerge from the same neutral structural fact. The teacher-student-sekhel triad functions exactly like father-mother-seed.", "dominant_node": "נִאוּף", "nodes": ["אמירת תורה לבלתי ראוי", "ניאוף", "טיפי השכל", "לבטלה", "ניאוף ממש", "פסול ופגום"], "node_degrees": {"ניאוף": 3, "אמירת תורה לבלתי ראוי": 2, "טיפי השכל": 2, "לבטלה": 1, "ניאוף ממש": 1, "פסול ופגום": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["לבטלה", "ניאוף ממש", "פסול ופגום"], "reason": "These three nodes each appear in the text as qualifications of the single bechina 'inappropriate Torah-saying IS niuf'. They are not independent bechina components — the text names two varieties of the niuf-situation: (1) the barren case (לבטלה — nothing is produced), and (2) the producing-something-flawed case (ניאוף ממש / פסול ופגום). Both are aspects of the core niuf concept: the first describes niuf-without-offspring, the second describes niuf-with-corrupt-offspring. They share the same essential domain — the illegitimate spiritual generative act — and are not large enough (2 nodes together) to stand alone. They are merged into Package 1 per the merge rule for 1-2-node components that share the same essential nature."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 134, "label": "תַּאֲוַת נִאוּף וְהַיֵּצֶר הָרָע", "label_en": "The Desire for Adultery and the Evil Inclination", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package captures the evil mirror and consequence of the core teaching: because saying Torah inappropriately IS a form of niuf, the yetzer hara can exploit the very act of Torah-saying to strengthen itself in the domain of actual sexual desire (תַּאֲוַת נִאוּף). The text states explicitly: 'וְעַל כֵּן עַל יְדֵי שֶׁאוֹמֵר תּוֹרָה, יוּכַל, חַס וְשָׁלוֹם, לְהִתְגַּבֵּר עָלָיו הַיֵּצֶר בְּתַאֲוַת נִאוּף.' This is the evil side of the Torah's structural insight: the same metaphysical channel that should carry sekhel as holy seed becomes the very opening through which the yetzer penetrates when the teacher is careless. The Rebbe returns to this in line 5-6: 'כָּל הַגָּדוֹל מֵחֲבֵרוֹ... יִצְרוֹ גָּדוֹל מִמֶּנּוּ' — the greater the Torah scholar, the greater his yetzer specifically in teshuka/niuf. This is the evil package: it groups the yetzer, its specific manifestation in sexual desire, and the teaching that the greater one is, the larger this yetzer becomes. The bechina here is implicit but structurally present: the yetzer hara IS בְּחִינַת תַּאֲוַת נִאוּף for the Torah teacher — the two are equated through the same structural logic that equates inappropriate teaching with niuf.", "dominant_node": "יצר הרע", "nodes": ["יצר הרע", "תאוות ניאוף", "כל הגדול מחברו יצרו גדול ממנו"], "node_degrees": {"יצר הרע": 2, "תאוות ניאוף": 2, "כל הגדול מחברו יצרו גדול ממנו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כל הגדול מחברו יצרו גדול ממנו"], "reason": "This Talmudic principle (Sukkah 52a), cited in line 5-6 of the Torah text, is applied specifically to the context of Torah-saying and the niuf-yetzer. The Rebbe interprets it as: the one who is 'gadol' (i.e., the great Torah teacher who says Torah) has a yetzer that is specifically stronger in taavas niuf — because the careless act of Torah-saying opened the channel. This principle IS an aspect of the yetzer hara's specific character in this Torah context. It is a 1-node component that shares the essential nature of the yetzer-taavas-niuf cluster and is merged here per the merge rule."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 135, "label": "מַלְכוּת וַעֲנָוָה", "label_en": "Kingship and Humility", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all nodes in Torah #135 whose essential nature is the dimension of holy kingship / receptive humility. מַלְכוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה is the central concept — the divine kingdom that has no substance of its own ('דְּלֵית לָהּ מִגַּרְמַהּ כְּלוּם') and is therefore called דָּלֶ\"ת (the letter of nothingness and receptivity). יִרְאָה (fear of God) is stated as a bechina of מַלְכוּת (line 26, 30: 'יִרְאָה הוּא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת'), and תַּלְמִידֵי-חֲכָמִים are also a bechina of מַלְכוּת ('מָאן מַלְכֵי — רַבָּנָן', line 29). The 2-node component ענוה ↔ מישרים אשפט is merged here: ענוה (humility) is the subjective, experiential face of the same domain — it is the inner quality of the person who has truly internalized malkhut. Line 30 makes this connection explicit: 'עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה׳ — כִּי יִרְאָה הוּא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת.' Humility and fear of God are presented as consecutive aspects of the same essential domain of malkhut. The verse מֵישָׁרִים אֶשְׁפֹּט (judging with equity/uprightness) is named as a bechina of ענוה (line 12, 14): one who judges with complete equity, without self-aggrandizement, expresses the same essential quality as humility. Both ענוה and יראה/מלכות belong to the domain of 'smallness before greatness' — the structural emptiness that makes holy reception possible.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "אות דל\"ת", "יראה", "תלמידי חכמים", "ענוה", "מישרים אשפט"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 3, "אות דל\"ת": 1, "יראה": 1, "תלמידי חכמים": 1, "ענוה": 1, "מישרים אשפט": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ענוה", "מישרים אשפט"], "from_component": "B", "reason": "The 2-node bechina pair ענוה ↔ מישרים אשפט (edge 1792) forms its own raw connected component. However, the source text (line 30) directly joins ענוה to the malkhut/yirah cluster: 'עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה׳ — כִּי יִרְאָה הוּא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת.' Humility (ענוה), fear of God (יראה), and malkhut are all expressions of the same fundamental quality — the emptiness of self, the receptivity, the acknowledgment that greatness does not come from oneself. These are parallel aspects of the same cosmic structure: dalet, the empty vessel. Merging per shared-nature rule; the link between ענוה and יראה/מלכות is shared nature (both describe the quality of receptive smallness), not merely a causal chain."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 135, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וּקְדֻשַּׁת יוֹם-טוֹב", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Holiness of Yom Tov", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups the concepts whose essential nature is the luminous, outward-shining aspect of holiness — the tzaddik as the vessel and embodiment of Yom Tov's holiness, and the 'great light of Yom Tov' as both the face of the tzaddik and the holiness of Yom Tov itself. Three bechina edges connect four nodes: (1) אוֹר הַגָּדוֹל שֶׁל יוֹם טוֹב ↔ צַדִּיק (edge 2527: the great light of Yom Tov is a bechina of the tzaddik); (2) צַדִּיק ↔ קְדֻשַּׁת יוֹם טוֹב (edge 2610: the tzaddik is a bechina of the holiness of Yom Tov); (3) אוֹר הַגָּדוֹל שֶׁל יוֹם טוֹב ↔ פְּנֵי רַבּוֹ (edge 2490: the great light of Yom Tov is a bechina of the face of one's teacher/master). The dominant concept is that the tzaddik IS the Yom Tov — they share the same essential nature. When one 'receives the face of one's teacher on the festival' (הַקְבָּלַת פְּנֵי רַבּוֹ בָּרֶגֶל), one is actually receiving the holiness of Yom Tov, since the tzaddik's face and Yom Tov's light are the same essential thing. This package is the positive, illuminating pole of the teaching — the source of light that annuls גַּדְלוּת (arrogance) by its sheer greatness. Its dominant node is צַדִּיק (two bechina connections), but אוֹר הַגָּדוֹל שֶׁל יוֹם טוֹב is equally central (also two connections) — the package is a unified cluster of three names for the same reality.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "קדשת יום טוב", "אור הגדול של יום טוב", "פני רבו"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 2, "אור הגדול של יום טוב": 2, "קדשת יום טוב": 1, "פני רבו": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 136, "label": "דָּן לְכַף זְכוּת וְדִין אֱמֶת", "label_en": "Judging Favorably and True Justice", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The explicit and central bechina of this Torah is stated on line 9: 'הֱוֵי דָּן אֶת כָּל אָדָם לְכַף זְכוּת — דָּן דַּיְקָא, כִּי הוּא בְּחִינַת דִּין וּמִשְׁפָּט מַמָּשׁ.' Judging favorably (דָּן לְכַף זְכוּת) IS, in essence, actual judgment and justice (דִּין וּמִשְׁפָּט). This is not cause-and-effect — it is an identification of shared nature: the act of favorable judging IS a judicial act. The cluster includes the הַיְנוּ statement that one who judges favorably 'raises his fellow to a higher madrege' (מַעֲלֶה חֲבֵרוֹ לְמַדְרֵגָה יוֹתֵר גָּבוֹהַּ), which the Rebbe identifies as the mechanism by which the heavenly court judges the person. Before any elevation in madrege, a din (heavenly judgment) takes place — so the favorable judgment the person below enacts on earth directly triggers and shapes the heavenly judgment above. The result: 'וּכְפִי שֶׁיֵּצֵא הַמִּשְׁפָּט כֵּן יָקוּם' — as the verdict issues, so it stands. The dominant concept is דָּן לְכַף זְכוּת as the most active and instructionally central node.", "dominant_node": "דן לכף זכות", "nodes": ["דן לכף זכות", "דין ומשפט", "מעלה חברו למדרגה יותר גבוהה", "קודם שעולין לאיזו מדרגה דנין את האדם", "וכפי שיצא המשפט כן יקום"], "node_degrees": {"דן לכף זכות": 3, "דין ומשפט": 2, "מעלה חברו למדרגה יותר גבוהה": 2, "קודם שעולין לאיזו מדרגה דנין את האדם": 1, "וכפי שיצא המשפט כן יקום": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 136, "label": "מָקוֹם וּמַדְרֵגָה", "label_en": "Place and Spiritual Level", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The Mishnah's phrase 'עַד שֶׁתַּגִּיעַ לִמְקוֹמוֹ' (until you reach his place) is interpreted by the Rebbe through a הַיְנוּ equality: 'הַיְנוּ שֶׁתִּשְׁתַּדֵּל שֶׁתִּהְיֶה עִמּוֹ בְּמָקוֹם אֶחָד, בְּמַדְרֵגָה אַחַת' (line 7). This makes מָקוֹם (place) and מַדְרֵגָה (spiritual level/standing) structural equivalents — two expressions of the same locational reality. The Torah consistently uses these two terms as parallel: 'בְּמָקוֹם אֶחָד הַיְנוּ בְּמַדְרֵגָה אַחַת' (lines 4, 5, 7). This cluster is polarity-neutral because the concept of madrege-equality is the structural mechanism the Torah describes — it is neither good nor evil in itself; it is the condition that either resolves machlokes (when present) or generates it (when absent). The dominant concept is מַדְרֵגָה, which appears more often and carries the heavier explanatory weight.", "dominant_node": "מדרגה", "nodes": ["מדרגה", "מקום", "במקום אחד במדרגה אחת"], "node_degrees": {"מדרגה": 3, "מקום": 2, "במקום אחד במדרגה אחת": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 136, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "label_en": "Conflict and Dispute", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "מַחֲלֹקֶת (dispute/conflict) is the problem the entire Torah is designed to resolve. The Rebbe identifies its cause: 'מַחֲלֹקֶת הוּא רַק מֵחֲמַת שֶׁהֵם מְשֻׁנִּים זֶה מִזֶּה' — conflict exists only because people differ from each other (line 5). Two related bechina-adjacent concepts belong here: (1) the aggressor who stirs conflict from jealousy ('מִתְקַנֵּא בּוֹ' — envying the other's greater level, line 4), and (2) the person whose machlokes arises because he has not reached the other's madrege (lines 3-4). This package is polarity 'evil' because machlokes is the opposing force in this Torah — the thing that is overcome by the practice of favorable judgment. The dominant concept is מַחֲלֹקֶת itself.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "משונים זה מזה", "מתקנא בו"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 4, "משונים זה מזה": 1, "מתקנא בו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["משונים זה מזה", "מתקנא בו"], "reason": "Both are defined as bechina-expressions of the underlying cause of machlokes: difference in madrege produces either envy (jealousy at not reaching the other's level) or the other's failure to recognize the lower person's stage. Both share the essential nature of 'separation between people due to unequal standing' and are therefore part of the conflict-cluster, not independent packages."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 137, "label": "חֵלֶק אֱלֹקִּי מִמַּעַל וְהַקּוֹל הַפְּנִימִי", "label_en": "The Divine Portion from Above and the Inner Voice", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah rests on a single bechina: 'חֶלְקִי יְיָ' (My portion is God, Psalms 119:57) IS 'הַחֵלֶק אֱלֹקַּי מִמַּעַל שֶׁיֵּשׁ לִי' — the divine spark that each person carries from above. The Rebbe reinterprets the verse's 'אָמַרְתִּי' (I said) not as the human speaker declaring his intention, but as the divine portion SPEAKING to the person: it tells him and teaches him 'לִשְׁמֹר דְּבָרֶיךָ' (to keep Your words). This yields two interrelated bechina identities: (1) 'חֶלְקִי יְיָ' IS 'הַחֵלֶק אֱלֹקַּי מִמַּעַל' — the verse-phrase and the interpretive phrase are two names for the same metaphysical reality: the divine portion within the person. (2) 'אָמַרְתִּי' (the saying/teaching) IS attributed to this divine portion — the divine portion shares the nature of an inner teacher who speaks and instructs. These three concepts — the divine portion, the inner voice/saying, and the teaching/instruction — are all aspects of one thematic cluster: the divine inner-teacher who guides the soul toward observance of the commandments. The polarity is entirely positive (good): this is a teaching about the divine light within every person guiding him toward Torah and mitzvot; there is no evil mirror developed in this two-line Torah.", "dominant_node": "חלק אלקי ממעל", "nodes": ["חלק אלקי ממעל", "חלקי ה'", "אמרתי — קול הנשמה המלמד"], "node_degrees": {"חלק אלקי ממעל": 2, "חלקי ה'": 1, "אמרתי — קול הנשמה המלמד": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 138, "label": "הַלֵּב וּדְבַר ה'", "label_en": "The Heart and the Word of God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This Torah presents a single unified teaching: the heart (לֵב) is the dwelling-place of the essence of divinity (עִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת), and therefore whatever the heart speaks is literally the word of God (דִּבְרֵי ה' מַמָּשׁ). The heart is not merely a metaphor — it is identified directly with the divine presence. The Rebbe proves this from the verse 'צוּר לְבָבִי' (Rock of my heart = God) and from Rashi's reading of the opening verse. Within this cluster: בַּר לֵבָב (pure/empty of heart) is the human subject who has purified the heart, characterized as being בִּבְחִינַת 'וְלִבִּי חָלַל בְּקִרְבִּי' — whose heart is hollow/empty within him, making it a perfect vessel for divine speech. The Torah's conceptual equation is: emptied heart = divine speech = prophetic knowledge of the future. All these concepts share the same essential nature: the heart as a transparent channel for the divine voice. The emptying (חָלַל) is not a separate idea from being 'bar levav' — it IS what being bar levav means, hence the explicit bechina relationship. 'לְךָ' (to You) = 'בִּשְׁלִיחוּתְךָ' (in Your commission) is Rashi's bechina-equivalence, identifying the heart's speech as delegated divine speech. This entire cluster belongs to the positive/holy domain: the heart is the good vessel for God's presence.", "dominant_node": "לֵב", "nodes": ["לֵב", "עִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת", "דִּבְרֵי ה' מַמָּשׁ", "בַּר לֵבָב", "וְלִבִּי חָלַל בְּקִרְבִּי", "בִּשְׁלִיחוּתְךָ"], "node_degrees": {"לֵב": 3, "דִּבְרֵי ה' מַמָּשׁ": 2, "בַּר לֵבָב": 2, "וְלִבִּי חָלַל בְּקִרְבִּי": 2, "עִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת": 1, "בִּשְׁלִיחוּתְךָ": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 138, "label": "יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת", "label_en": "Knowledge of the Future", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Knowing the future (יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת) is the eitza-result produced by the hollow heart receiving divine speech — 'יוּכַל לֵידַע עֲתִידוֹת, עַל־יְדֵי מַה שֶּׁהַלֵּב אוֹמֵר לוֹ'. The language 'עַל־יְדֵי' (through/by means of) is the standard eitza marker in Rebbe Nachman's teachings, not a bechina-equivalence marker. Strictly speaking, this is a single-node concept that receives an eitza arrow from Package 1. However, within the topology of this Torah, the teaching culminates in this concept: knowing the future IS what divine speech from the heart produces. The verse 'לְךָ אָמַר לִבִּי בַּקְּשׁוּ פָנָי' is the textual proof that the heart commissions/speaks prophetically. As a standalone concept with no bechina peers, it is listed here as a separate minimal package to preserve the framework rule that every node belongs to exactly one package. It cannot be merged into Package 1 by shared-nature bechina — the connection is explicitly eitza ('through what the heart says → one can know the future'). It is good polarity: prophetic knowledge is a gift of the holy domain.", "dominant_node": "יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת", "nodes": ["יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת"], "node_degrees": {"יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת": 0}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": [], "reason": "No bechina peers found for יְדִיעַת עֲתִידוֹת in this Torah. It is the terminus of the eitza chain from Package 1. Listed as a minimal package to preserve node-coverage. If future dataset encoding produces bechina edges connecting this to another prophetic-knowledge concept within this Torah, this package should be revisited."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 139, "label": "מִצְוָה, צֶדֶק, וְיִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא", "label_en": "The Mitzva, Righteousness, and the Honor of Shabbat", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package clusters all the bechina-equivalent concepts that constitute the positive subject of Torah #139. Three explicit bechina relationships anchor the cluster: (A) 'כָּל מִצְוָה הִיא קוֹמָה שְׁלֵמָה' (line 3) — every mitzva IS a complete stature/form. This is a strict identity statement: the mitzva and the komah sh'lemah are two names for the same essential reality. (B) 'וְהַמִּצְווֹת נִקְרָאִים צֶדֶק' (line 7) — mitzvot are CALLED tzedek. The 'נִקְרָאִים' formula is a canonical bechina marker in Rebbe Nachman's language, equating mitzvot and tzedek in essential nature. This identification is proven from Psalms 119:172 ('כָּל מִצְוֹתֶיךָ צֶדֶק') and allows the Rebbe to re-read the verse 'צֶדֶק לְפָנָיו יְהַלֵּךְ' as 'the mitzva walks before God.' (C) 'אֵין לְהַמִּצְוָה הַהִיא בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין, לַעֲלוֹת בָּהֶם וְלֵילֵךְ לִפְנֵי הַקָּדוֹשׁ-בָּרוּךְ-הוּא' (line 3) — the mitzva has, as its ascending dimension, a 'bechina of raglayim' (feet). The raglayim are not a separate object but a modal aspect (bechina) of the mitzva itself, through which it rises and walks before God. The parallel: just as a person walks on their feet, the mitzva's 'feet' are its mode of ascent. The connection of Shabbat to this cluster: line 2 states 'כִּי יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא הוּא' — the verse (and therefore its subject, tzedek/the mitzva going before God) IS what constitutes the 'Yikra d'Shabata,' the honor/aura of Shabbat. This is an identification: the spectacle of the mitzva ascending before God IS the glory of Shabbat. Lines 4-5 confirm this structurally — it is on Shabbat that this ascent takes place. The verse-content (tzedek walking before God) and Shabbat's honor are two names for the same event. Thus שַׁבָּת / יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא belongs to this cluster by shared nature: both are expressions of the same holy reality — the mitzva's ascent before God. The dominant node is מִצְוָה, with the most bechina connections within this Torah: it is equated to קוֹמָה שְׁלֵמָה, to צֶדֶק, and characterized as having בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין.", "dominant_node": "מִצְוָה", "nodes": ["מִצְוָה", "קוֹמָה שְׁלֵמָה", "צֶדֶק", "בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין", "שַׁבָּת", "יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא", "פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלִין"], "node_degrees": {"מִצְוָה": 4, "צֶדֶק": 2, "קוֹמָה שְׁלֵמָה": 2, "בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין": 2, "שַׁבָּת": 2, "יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא": 2, "פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלִין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שַׁבָּת", "יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא"], "reason": "שַׁבָּת and יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא have no bechina peers outside of their identification with the mitzva-ascent. Line 2 ('כִּי יִקְרָא דְּשַׁבַּתָּא הוּא') identifies the verse's content — i.e., tzedek/the mitzva walking before God — with the honor of Shabbat. The ascending mitzva before God IS Shabbat's glory. This is a bechina (shared nature): the mitzva-ascent and the honor of Shabbat are two faces of the same reality. Shabbat is not merely the mechanism that enables ascent (that would be eitza); it IS the occasion whose essential meaning consists in the mitzva's ascent. Because no second bechina domain exists for Shabbat in this Torah, and because its essential nature is identified with the mitzva's ascent, it merges into Package 1 by shared nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלִין"], "reason": "פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלִין (the footsteps/foot-beats of the mitzva) is the cumulative form of the mitzva's bechina-raglayim. The verse 'וְיָשֵׂם לְדֶרֶךְ פְּעָמָיו' is read as referring to the mitzva's foot-movements. As such, פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלִין is not a new concept but the collective expression of 'בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין' — the same ascending mode, now accumulated across many mitzvot, generating the communal path. Its essential nature (ascending movement of the mitzva before God) is the same as the broader mitzva cluster. Merged into Package 1 by shared nature."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 139, "label": "קְלִפָּה וְחִיצוֹנִים — רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת", "label_en": "The Klipa and External Forces — 'Her Feet Go Down to Death'", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 1. Where Package 1 consists of the mitzva's feet ascending before God, Package 2 consists of the klipa's feet descending toward death. The anchor bechina is explicit in line 3: '[בִּבְחִינַת (משלי ה׳:ה׳): רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת]' — the klipa's possession of the mitzva's raglayim is characterized by the verse 'her feet go down to death' (Proverbs 5:5, referring originally to the 'foreign woman,' the feminine embodiment of the sitra achra). The 'בִּבְחִינַת' formula equates the klipa to the entity whose feet descend to death — not an incidental proof-text, but a structural identification: the klipa IS the entity described by this verse. The חִיצוֹנִים (external/alien forces) and the קְלִפָּה are used interchangeably throughout this Torah (lines 3, 4, 5 — 'שְׁלִיטַת הַחִיצוֹנִים,' 'הַחִיצוֹנִים,' 'יוֹנֶקֶת הַקְּלִפָּה'). They are different names for the same negative reality: the domain of impurity that is active during the weekdays and sucks vitality from the mitzva's ascending dimension. The dominant node is קְלִפָּה, the most active and explicitly named agent in the evil cluster. The good/evil structural mirror: Package 1's 'בְּחִינַת רַגְלִין לַעֲלוֹת' (feet ascending) is the direct mirror-opposite of Package 2's 'רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת' (feet descending to death). The mitzva's feet rise; the klipa's feet fall. Shabbat's role in the teaching is precisely to remove the raglayim from Package 2's grip (the klipa's downward pull) and restore them to Package 1's upward movement.", "dominant_node": "קְלִפָּה", "nodes": ["קְלִפָּה", "חִיצוֹנִים", "רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת"], "node_degrees": {"קְלִפָּה": 3, "חִיצוֹנִים": 2, "רַגְלֶיהָ יוֹרְדוֹת מָוֶת": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 139, "label": "תַּעֲנוּג הַקָּדוֹשׁ-בָּרוּךְ-הוּא וְדֶרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה", "label_en": "God's Delight and the Established Communal Path", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package clusters the two culminating nodes of the teaching — both expressing the divine response to the mitzva's ascent before God. (A) תַּעֲנוּג / שַׁעֲשׁוּעִים (divine pleasure/delight): Lines 6 and 9 repeatedly emphasize that God derives 'תַּעֲנוּג גָּדוֹל' (great pleasure) and 'שַׁעֲשׁוּעִים' (delight) from every mitzva, even one performed imperfectly. The analogy is a father whose delight in his child's first steps surpasses his concern about their clumsiness. This delight is the immediate divine response to the ascending mitzva. (B) דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה (well-established communal path): Lines 7-9 develop the walking metaphor — just as a person walking repeatedly through a pathless wilderness eventually creates a worn path for others, God, from the accumulated 'פַּעֲמֵי רַגְלָיו' (foot-movements of the mitzva), makes a 'דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה' (a trampled, established path) accessible to the many. The re-reading of 'וְיָשֵׂם לְדֶרֶךְ פְּעָמָיו' is: God MAKES a derech from the footsteps. The text links taanug to derech via eitza: 'מֵחֲמַת גֹּדֶל הַתַּעֲנוּגִים... עוֹשֶׂה מִפַּעֲמֵי רַגְלָיו דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה' (line 9) — BECAUSE of the great pleasure, God makes the established path. The connection between taanug and derech k'vushah within this package is not explicit bechina but shared-nature grouping of two singleton concepts that both belong to the same conceptual domain: the divine engagement with the mitzva produces delight AND an enduring communal path. Neither taanug nor derech k'vushah has a bechina peer elsewhere in this Torah. By shared-nature merge: both are expressions of how God's relationship to the ascending mitzva manifests — one as immediate subjective response (delight), one as objective communal outcome (established path). The dominant node is תַּעֲנוּג, which is the stated cause of the derech.", "dominant_node": "תַּעֲנוּג", "nodes": ["תַּעֲנוּג", "דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה"], "node_degrees": {"תַּעֲנוּג": 2, "דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תַּעֲנוּג", "דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה"], "reason": "Neither תַּעֲנוּג nor דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה has a bechina peer within Torah #139. Their connection is eitza (taanug → derech) per line 9 ('מֵחֲמַת גֹּדֶל הַתַּעֲנוּגִים... עוֹשֶׂה... דֶּרֶךְ כְּבוּשָׁה'). However, both are singleton-node eitza-downstream concepts that share the essential domain of 'divine response to the ascending mitzva.' The framework instructs: 'Merge small (1-2 node) components by SHARED NATURE only (not eitza/cause-effect).' Their shared nature is: they are both expressions of how God's engagement with the mitzva manifests outwardly — the immediate response (taanug/delight) and the enduring outcome (derech k'vushah). Both are 'what God produces from the mitzva's ascent.' This is a conceptual family by shared nature, not by eitza (the eitza relationship is between them internally, but the reason for their grouping into one package is their common membership in the domain of 'God's mitzva-response'). Merged into Package 3 as a two-node small package."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 140, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וְהַחוֹתָם", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and the Seal", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #140 teaches a single, tightly focused idea: the Tzaddik is beyond ordinary intellectual grasp ('לְמַעְלָה מִשִּׂכְלוֹ'), and yet his nature can be inferred — the way a seal's engraved letters can only be read by pressing it into wax. The Tzaddik is explicitly called a בְּחִינַת חוֹתָם (seal), because just as the seal's inscription is hidden in its reversed form, the Tzaddik's inner reality is concealed yet can be known through his disciples (the 'wax impression'). The dominant node is צדיק, which is connected to חותם (the seal). The same צדיק is also called 'כָּל הָאָדָם' (all of humanity) via the Kohelet verse — this is an additional bechina in the source text not yet in the dataset (see torah_140_additions.json). The package is good/holy: the Tzaddik and his seal are expressions of the covenant of holiness ('בְּרִית קֹדֶשׁ').", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "חותם"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 1, "חותם": 1}, "merge_notes": "No merging was performed. There is only one bechina component in this Torah. The two nodes (צדיק, חותם) are directly connected by edge 1113. There are no orphan or isolated nodes in the dataset for this Torah. The eitza edge (edge 1271: אנשיו המקרבין אליו → יוכל להבין מעלת הצדיק) does NOT contribute to package formation — it is a directional cause-and-effect relationship showing how the Tzaddik's stature becomes knowable, not a shared-nature parallel. It flows between the domain of this package and an implied external practical result."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 141, "label": "מִילַת הַלֵּב וְהַחֲרָטָה", "label_en": "Circumcision of the Heart and Repentance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The core positive teaching of Torah #141 is that a person must circumcise the foreskin of his heart (מִילַת עָרְלַת הַלֵּב), creating an opening (חָלָל בַּלֵּב) through which he can genuinely feel the pain of his sins (כְּאֵב חֲטָאָיו) and arrive at true regret (חֲרָטָה אֲמִתִּית). These are not three separate causal steps but three aspects of the same reality: the opened heart IS the capacity to feel genuine pain IS the state of true teshuvah. The Torah makes this explicit: the blocked/sealed heart (לֵב עָרֵל וְאָטוּם) prevents genuine feeling, and the moment it is opened/circumcised, pain, awareness, and regret all arrive together. In the framework's terms: feeling pain, having the heart opening, and being in a state of genuine contrition are expressions of the same essential quality — the heart that has been made receptive to divine truth. The dominant concept is מִילַת הַלֵּב (circumcision of the heart), which is the term that unifies all the others. The verse anchor (Deuteronomy 30:6) 'וּמָל ה' אֶת לְבָבְךָ' names this act in its divine form. The month of Elul (אֱלוּל) is the bechina of this circumcision — the text explicitly states its acronym maps to 'אֶת לְבָבְךָ וְאֶת לְבַב', making Elul and the divine circumcision of hearts the same essential concept in the domain of time. Package is good/holy: circumcising the heart is the positive spiritual act of teshuvah, oriented toward God.", "dominant_node": "מִילַת הַלֵּב", "nodes": ["כשימול את ערלת לבבו, ויהיה לו חלל בלב", "ירגיש לבבו באמת גדל כאבו, ויצטער ויתחרט באמת", "כשימול את לבבו, וירגיש לבבו גדל כאבו, ויתחיל להצטער ולהתחרט באמת", "נמול לבב אביהם", "אלול"], "node_degrees": {"כשימול את ערלת לבבו, ויהיה לו חלל בלב": 1, "ירגיש לבבו באמת גדל כאבו, ויצטער ויתחרט באמת": 2, "כשימול את לבבו, וירגיש לבבו גדל כאבו, ויתחיל להצטער ולהתחרט באמת": 1, "נמול לבב אביהם": 2, "אלול": 0}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כשימול את ערלת לבבו, ויהיה לו חלל בלב", "ירגיש לבבו באמת גדל כאבו, ויצטער ויתחרט באמת", "כשימול את לבבו, וירגיש לבבו גדל כאבו, ויתחיל להצטער ולהתחרט באמת", "נמול לבב אביהם"], "reason": "These four dataset nodes (from eitza edges 1416, 1417, 1418, 1945, 2085) all describe the same essential spiritual state: the heart that has been opened through circumcision and is therefore capable of genuine repentance. While their dataset connections are eitza (causal), they share the same essential nature — they are all aspects of מִילַת הַלֵּב / תְּשׁוּבָה אֲמִתִּית. 'כשימול את ערלת לבבו ויהיה לו חלל בלב' (the act of circumcising the heart-foreskin, creating the hollow) is a bechina of 'ירגיש לבבו באמת גדל כאבו ויצטער ויתחרט באמת' (the resulting state of genuine pain and regret) — they describe the same moment of spiritual opening from two angles: the action and the inner experience. The near-duplicate node 'כשימול את לבבו וירגיש לבבו גדל כאבו ויתחיל להצטער ולהתחרט באמת' is effectively the same concept as the first node (opening the heart) fused with the second node (the resulting pain) — it is a compound statement of the same reality. 'נמול לבב אביהם' (the father's heart was circumcised) is the same act as 'כשימול את ערלת לבבו' — it is the same circumcision of the heart viewed from the children's perspective. All share the SAME NATURE. Merge is by shared-nature (bechina), not cause-and-effect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["אלול"], "reason": "The month of Elul is introduced via an explicit bechina-equivalence: 'אֱלוּל רָאשֵׁי תֵּבוֹת: אֶת לְבָבְךָ וְאֶת לְבַב' — the acronym equation identifies Elul AS the time-aspect of divine heart-circumcision. This is a bechina in the classical sense: Elul and the divine act of circumcising hearts are the same essential reality expressed through the dimension of time. The month of Elul IS the bechina of 'וּמָל ה' אֶת לְבָבְךָ וְאֶת לְבַב זַרְעֲךָ'. Elul is not a cause-and-effect intermediary; it IS the temporal vessel of the same spiritual quality. It belongs in Package 1 alongside the other heart-circumcision concepts."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 141, "label": "לְבָבוֹת הַטִּפּוֹת וְהַנְּשָׁמוֹת הַנִּדָּחוֹת", "label_en": "Hearts of the Scattered Drops and the Estranged Souls", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #141 describes a second class of entities: the 'drops' (טִפּוֹת) emitted by the man in sin. These drops have hearts (לְבָבוֹת) and other organs. They exist in two forms: (a) children born in the normal way (בָּנָיו מִמִּין בְּנֵי אָדָם), and (b) demonic beings who went to impure places (לְמָקוֹם אַחֵר חַ\"ו) and now dwell in filth in Sheol (שְׁאוֹל תַּחְתִּית). The teaching explicitly says the latter initially think they are doing well, because they are 'מַזִּיקֵי עָלְמָא' (destroyers of the world). When the man's heart is circumcised, their hearts too are circumcised, and they realize their true state and begin to lament. When the man dies, they follow his bier and mourn — but this is a source of shame and disgrace to him, not honor. The dominant concept of this cluster is the טִפּוֹת and their hearts (לְבָבוֹת הַטִּפּוֹת) — the soul-fragments scattered into impurity. These are not simply a neutral object; the teaching frames them as the evil dimension of sin's aftermath: they are demonic, they damage the world (מַזִּיקֵי עָלְמָא), they dwell in the place of filth. The polarity is evil: they represent the spiritual children of sin, the side-effect of uncircumcised-heart living, dwelling in the realm of impurity. The node 'מרגישין היכן הם, ומתחילין לקונן ולהצטער, ונעשה שם רעש גדול ביניהם' and 'נמול לבבם' represent the moment when these evil/impure soul-fragments come to recognize their true state — a transitional moment that is the mirror-result of the good act in Package 1. They are the evil mirror of Package 1: just as there is a good circumcision of the heart (Package 1), there is a mirrored circumcision of the hearts of these scattered soul-fragments in the evil realm (Package 2). Same structural act, opposite polarity. The text uses explicit 'כַּנַּ\"ל' (as above) to link the two circumcisions symmetrically.", "dominant_node": "לְבָבוֹת הַטִּפּוֹת", "nodes": ["ירגישו גם כל הלבבות של כל הטפות שנמשכו ממנו, ולכל מקום שנמשכו, ירגישו שם במקום שהם, הן אותם שנמשכו ממנו ונתהוו מהם בניו ממין בני אדם, והן אותם שנמשכו ממנו למקום אחר חס ושלום", "ירגישו שם כל הלבבות של הטפות, ויתודע להם האמת איך הם מוטלים במקום טנופות בשאול תחתית", "מרגישין היכן הם, ומתחילין לקונן ולהצטער, ונעשה שם רעש גדול ביניהם", "נמול לבבם"], "node_degrees": {"ירגישו גם כל הלבבות של כל הטפות שנמשכו ממנו, ולכל מקום שנמשכו, ירגישו שם במקום שהם, הן אותם שנמשכו ממנו ונתהוו מהם בניו ממין בני אדם, והן אותם שנמשכו ממנו למקום אחר חס ושלום": 2, "ירגישו שם כל הלבבות של הטפות, ויתודע להם האמת איך הם מוטלים במקום טנופות בשאול תחתית": 0, "מרגישין היכן הם, ומתחילין לקונן ולהצטער, ונעשה שם רעש גדול ביניהם": 1, "נמול לבבם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ירגישו גם כל הלבבות של כל הטפות שנמשכו ממנו, ולכל מקום שנמשכו, ירגישו שם במקום שהם, הן אותם שנמשכו ממנו ונתהוו מהם בניו ממין בני אדם, והן אותם שנמשכו ממנו למקום אחר חס ושלום", "ירגישו שם כל הלבבות של הטפות, ויתודע להם האמת איך הם מוטלים במקום טנופות בשאול תחתית", "מרגישין היכן הם, ומתחילין לקונן ולהצטער, ונעשה שם רעש גדול ביניהם", "נמול לבבם"], "reason": "These four concepts are all aspects of the same thing: the scattered soul-fragments (the 'hearts' of the drops) and their spiritual state. They are not a causal chain but different angles on the same reality. 'All the hearts of the drops that were drawn from him' (the entity), 'they become aware of the truth of how they lie in filth in Sheol' (what that entity knows), 'they begin to lament and cry' (what that entity does when awakened), and 'their hearts were circumcised' (the same act, in the passive form — the structural twin of 'נמול לבב אביהם' in Package 1) — all share the essential nature of 'the soul-fragments of sin awakening to their true station'. These are bechina-compatible on shared nature: they all describe what it means to be one of these scattered entities."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 142, "label": "לֵב שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה וְסֵפֶר הַזִּכָּרוֹן", "label_en": "The Supernal Heart and the Book of Remembrance", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #142 teaches that one who yearns to learn Torah but cannot (due to being unlearned, lacking books, or being in the wilderness) is credited as if he actually studied — because his yearning heart draws from the supernal heart (לֵב שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה), which is itself the Book of Remembrance (סֵפֶר זִכָּרוֹן). This book is composed from the Torah discussions of two distant tzaddikim whose questions and answers complete each other across hundreds of miles, heard only by God. The core bechina identifications are: (1) what the heart yearns to learn IS study from the book (מַה שֶּׁהַלֵּב חוֹמֵד לִלְמֹד = לִמּוּד מִתּוֹךְ הַסֵּפֶר), and (2) the supernal heart IS the Book of Remembrance (לֵב שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה = סֵפֶר זִכָּרוֹן). Together these four nodes are all expressions of the same spiritual reality: the heart and the book are identical — yearning IS learning, and the supernal heart IS the book. The two 2-node components share essential nature (not cause-and-effect), warranting merger. The dominant node is לֵב שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה, which structurally anchors the entire teaching: it is the source both of the yearning heart's longing and of the book in which the tzaddikim's Torah is written. The polarity is good: all four concepts belong to the holy domain — the mechanism by which God credits sincere yearning as actual performance.", "dominant_node": "לב שלמעלה", "nodes": ["לב שלמעלה", "ספר זכרון", "מה שהלב חומד ללמד", "למוד מתוך הספר"], "node_degrees": {"לב שלמעלה": 1, "ספר זכרון": 1, "מה שהלב חומד ללמד": 1, "למוד מתוך הספר": 1}, "merge_notes": "Two 2-node components were found in the dataset for this Torah: Component A (edge 1632: מה שהלב חומד ללמד ↔ למוד מתוך הספר) and Component B (edge 2323: לב שלמעלה ↔ ספר זכרון). These are merged into a single package on the grounds of SHARED NATURE — not cause-and-effect. The justification is that all four nodes are different expressions of the same underlying spiritual reality: the heart and the book are identical in this Torah's teaching. Component A declares that the yearning to learn IS actual learning; Component B declares that the supernal heart IS the Book of Remembrance. In the source text, these two identifications are explicitly joined: 'הוּא מְקַבֵּל לֵב מִלֵּב שֶׁלְּמַעְלָה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת סֵפֶר זִכָּרוֹן... וְהוּא בְּחִינַת לִמּוּד מִתּוֹךְ הַסֵּפֶר מַמָּשׁ' (line 5). The Rebbe himself equates all four within one continuous bechina chain. This is shared-nature merger, not a cause-effect chain. The two nodes that 'connect' the components (the lower heart = the upper heart in essence) are both aspects of the same lev/sefer identity. The merge is fully consistent with the framework rule: merge by shared nature only."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 143, "label": "צִמְצוּם וְדִינִים", "label_en": "Contraction and Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster contains all concepts that share the essential nature of 'not knowing / constricted intellect / strict judgment.' The state of needing advice and not knowing what to do IS tzimtzum (contraction of the sekhel) and tzimtzum IS dinim. These three nodes are all expressions of the same underlying reality: the contracted, un-illuminated, strict-judgment dimension of a person's inner state. The Torah identifies this triad using explicit bechina language twice: 'היא בחינת צמצום ... והוא בחינת דינים' and 'הוא בחינת צמצום'. Polarity is evil/problematic — these are the conditions of spiritual constriction that the Torah describes as the danger one faces without the Tzaddik's counsel. The dinim here are not cosmic divine justice but the internal judgment-dimension (strict, contracted) that afflicts a person who lacks guidance.", "dominant_node": "צמצום", "nodes": ["כשצריך לעצה, ואינו יודע איך לעשות", "צמצום", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"צמצום": 2, "כשצריך לעצה, ואינו יודע איך לעשות": 1, "דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 143, "label": "חֲסָדִים וְהֶאָרַת הַחָכְמָה", "label_en": "Chasadim and the Illumination of Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This pair contains two nodes that share the essential nature of 'the wise man's counsel illuminating the recipient's sekhel.' The action of the wise man — he expands (מרחיב) and illuminates (מאיר) the person's wisdom — IS chasadim, because chesed is 'nehiru d'chokhmeta' (the illumination of wisdom), as cited from the Zohar (Lech Lecha 94a) and Psalms 52:3. Both nodes are different expressions of the same positive reality: when the Tzaddik/Chacham gives advice, it is not merely practical guidance but the flow of chesed, which is the light of wisdom itself pouring into the contracted intellect. Polarity is good — this is the remedy, the expansion, the chesed-dimension that sweetens the dinim of Package 1. CC2 is retained as its own package rather than absorbed into CC1, because the two poles (dinim/tzimtzum and chasadim/expansion) are structurally opposed in Kabbalistic teaching — they must not be collapsed into a single package. Their connection is causal (eitza): receiving counsel FROM the Tzaddik PRODUCES (eitza edge 888) the sweetening of dinim.", "dominant_node": "חסדים", "nodes": ["על ידי שהחכם נותן לו עצה, הוא מרחיב לו, ומאיר בו חכמתו", "חסדים"], "node_degrees": {"חסדים": 1, "על ידי שהחכם נותן לו עצה, הוא מרחיב לו, ומאיר בו חכמתו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["CC2 not merged into CC1 despite being only 2 nodes: the two components represent opposite poles (dinim/tzimtzum vs. chasadim) that are explicitly distinguished in the Torah. Merging by 'shared nature' would be a category error — these concepts do not share nature, they are structural opposites connected by causal flow (eitza). The 2-node threshold for merging applies only when the shared nature is genuine; here the natures are antithetical."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 144, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק וְהַחַיִּים", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and Life", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of 'the one who controls his desire and therefore is always alive.' The teaching's central bechina declaration is: the Tzaddik is never dead — he is תָּמִיד חַי (always alive), even after physical death. This is because for him, life consists in serving God (עֲבוֹדַת הַשֵּׁם), which continues uninterrupted beyond the grave. The text further defines the Tzaddik's quality: he כּוֹבֵשׁ אֶת יִצְרוֹ (subdues his inclination) and אוֹחֵז אֶת תַּאֲוָתוֹ בְּיָדוֹ (holds his desire in his hand) — even regarding the permitted half (חֶצְיוֹ לָכֶם), he maintains mastery. These three dimensions — Tzaddik, life/always-alive, and the subduing of desire — all express the same essential reality: the axis of holiness and spiritual vitality that transcends death. The explicit bechina marker is the statement 'בְּוַדַּאי זֶה אֵין מֵת, אֶלָּא תָּמִיד חַי' — this person IS not dead, IS always alive. This IS-language equates the Tzaddik's type with the concept of enduring life. The axis of this package is: Tzaddik = control of desire = life. Polarity is good — this is the positive pole of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "תמיד חי", "כובש יצרו / אוחז תאוותו בידו"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 2, "תמיד חי": 1, "כובש יצרו / אוחז תאוותו בידו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Package 1 is derived from the source text, not from dataset bechina edges (none exist for reference 144). The three nodes all share the same essential nature: the domain of 'life through mastery of desire.' The Tzaddik IS the one who subdues desire; that subduing IS the reason he IS always alive. These are three facets of a single reality, not a causal chain — the Tzaddik does not 'produce' life by subduing desire in an eitza sense within this package; rather, they are all bechina-expressions of the same person/quality."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 144, "label": "תַּאֲוַת אֲכִילָה וּשְׁתִיָּה וְהַמָּוֶת", "label_en": "Desires of Eating and Drinking and Death", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of 'being enslaved to physical desire, which constitutes a form of death.' The teaching's most explicit bechina statement is: 'זֶה נִקְרָא מֵת' — the person who followed desires of eating and drinking all his days IS CALLED (נִקְרָא) dead. The word נִקְרָא is one of the canonical bechina markers in the framework ('X נִקְרָא Y — X is called Y'). The equation made here is: the desire-chaser = מֵת (the dead). After physical death his lack of eating/drinking shows that death has occurred — death is recognizable (נִכֶּרֶת) in him, because his entire identity was eating and drinking, and now that is gone. The two concepts in this package — תַּאֲווֹת אֲכִילָה וּשְׁתִיָּה (desires of eating and drinking) and מֵת (the dead / death) — share the same essential nature: both represent existence without inner life, without transcendence, without the axis that connects a person to God. The desire is the death-orientation expressed in life; the death is the desire-orientation made manifest. This is not a causal chain (desires do not 'produce' death as an eitza mechanism in this text) — rather, the Rebbe is saying they ARE the same thing: to live for desire IS death. Polarity is evil — this is the negative pole of the teaching, the anti-life axis.", "dominant_node": "תאוות אכילה ושתיה", "nodes": ["האדם שנמשך אחר תאוות אכילה ושתיה", "מת"], "node_degrees": {"האדם שנמשך אחר תאוות אכילה ושתיה": 1, "מת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Package 2 is derived from the source text's explicit bechina statement 'זֶה נִקְרָא מֵת.' This is a 2-node component. The merge question is: should it be merged into Package 1? Answer: NO. These two packages represent structurally opposed poles of the teaching — good vs. evil, life vs. death. They share NO nature (one is the axis of life/mastery, the other is the axis of desire/death). The connection between them is one of contrast/opposition, not shared essence. Merging them would violate the fundamental rule that packages are built on shared nature (bechina), not on contrast."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 145, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וּמוֹת הָרוּחָנִי", "label_en": "Controversy and Spiritual Death", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The teaching's single bechina equates the talmudic principle 'אֵין אָדָם מֵת וַחֲצִי' (no person dies halfway — i.e., one either attains full spiritual continuity or is fully cut off) with machloket (strife, controversy). The logic is that machloket IS the mode of dying in full — it erases one's name from the tradition entirely. David's request to 'dwell in Your tent forever' is fulfilled through disciples quoting halachah in one's name; machloket prevents this, reducing a sage to the anonymous labels 'אחרים' or 'יש אומרים'. Both nodes — the rabbinic death-principle and machloket — share the same essential nature: the domain of complete severance from life and continuity. This is unambiguously an evil package: machloket belongs to the side of the sitra achra (as confirmed by Torah #277 in the same dataset, where 'סטרא אחרא הוא בחינת מחלקת'). The talmudic phrase 'אין אדם מת וחצי' is invoked here in its dark corollary — when machloket is present, there is no 'halfway' reprieve; the result is complete obliteration from the chain of tradition.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["אֵין אָדָם מֵת וַחֲצִי וְכוּ'", "מַחֲלֹקֶת"], "node_degrees": {"מַחֲלֹקֶת": 1, "אֵין אָדָם מֵת וַחֲצִי וְכוּ'": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 146, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּמַחְכִּימַת פֶּתִי", "label_en": "Torah and Making the Simple Wise", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of Torah as the faithful witness and maker of wisdom in the hidden domain. The teaching's core bechina declarations define this package: (1) Torah IS CALLED (הַנִּקְרֵאת) a woman — an אִשָּׁה — which is why she can testify in the place where men are not accustomed to be; (2) 'הַחֲרָטָה, זֶה בְּחִינַת מַחְכִּימַת פֶּתִי' — the regret after a transgression IS a bechina of 'making the simple wise.' These two bechina statements link five concepts into a single nature-family: Torah is the woman who stands in the secret place (the locus of lust) and whose calling elicits wisdom-regret from the transgressor. Torah, her identity as אִשָּׁה, her function as עֵדוּת (testimony), the capacity of מַחְכִּימַת פֶּתִי (making the peti wise), and the concrete result חֲרָטָה (regret after the transgression) all share the same essential domain: the redemptive power of Torah that reaches the sinner even in the hidden world of desire. These are not cause-and-effect (the regret is not produced by Torah in an eitza sense within this package — rather, the regret IS the בחינת of the Torah's making-wise action, i.e., they are the same thing seen from different angles). Polarity is good: this cluster belongs entirely to the domain of holiness, Torah, and spiritual recovery.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אישה", "עדות", "מחכימת פתי", "חרטה"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 4, "מחכימת פתי": 3, "אישה": 2, "עדות": 2, "חרטה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Package 1 is derived from the source text, not from dataset bechina edges (none exist for reference 146). The five nodes all share the same essential nature: the domain of 'Torah as faithful witness who makes the simple wise and draws forth regret.' The key linguistic anchors are (a) 'הַתּוֹרָה הַנִּקְרֵאת אִשָּׁה' — Torah IS CALLED a woman; (b) 'וְזֹאת הַחֲרָטָה, זֶה בְּחִינַת מַחְכִּימַת פֶּתִי' — regret IS the bechina of making-the-simple-wise. The concept of פֶּתִי (the simple/fool) is the person who is the object of both Torah's calling and the ruach shtut's domination; in this Torah his peti-nature is what Torah redeems (מַחְכִּימַת פֶּתִי), so he is assigned here as neutral-to-good rather than to Package 2. The node עֵדוּת appears because the Torah's identity as testimony (עֵדוּת ה' נֶאֱמָנָה) is the opening premise and defines why Torah can be trusted as a witness — it is a face of Torah's essential nature in this teaching."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 146, "label": "תַּאֲווֹת וְרוּחַ שְׁטוּת", "label_en": "Lusts and the Spirit of Folly", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of the hidden domain of lust and spiritual folly — the realm that Torah must penetrate to testify. The teaching's two bechina statements for this package are: (1) 'עִקַּר תַּאֲווֹת הֵם בַּסֵּתֶר, בִּבְחִינַת מַיִם גְּנוּבִים יִמְתָּקוּ' — the essential nature of lusts operating in secret IS (בְּחִינַת) 'stolen waters are sweet' (Prov. 9:17); (2) 'כִּי בִּשְׁעַת הָעֲבֵרָה הָיָה בּוֹ רוּחַ שְׁטוּת' — at the moment of the transgression, the spirit of folly inhabits him [implied IS-equation between the sinner's state and ruach shtut, grounded in the Talmudic dictum of Sota 3a: 'ein adam over aveira ela im ken nikhnas bo ruach shtut']. The four concepts in this package — תַּאֲווֹת (lusts), מַיִם גְּנוּבִים (stolen waters), הַסֵּתֶר (the hidden/secret domain), and רוּחַ שְׁטוּת (spirit of folly) — all share the same essential domain: the hidden world of desire, sin, and spiritual blindness that is the antithesis of Torah wisdom. The connection between them is one of shared essential nature, not cause-and-effect: lust IS the stolen-waters quality; the secret-hiding IS where lust lives; the ruach shtut IS the inner state of the person in the moment of lust-driven transgression. The concept עֲבֵרָה (transgression) belongs here because the Rebbe explicitly defines it as the moment of ruach shtut — the transgression IS the ruach-shtut event. All four form a single bechina-family of the evil pole. Polarity is evil: this cluster belongs to the domain of the sitra achra, desire, and spiritual folly.", "dominant_node": "תאוות", "nodes": ["תאוות", "מים גנובים", "הסתר", "רוח שטות", "עברה"], "node_degrees": {"תאוות": 3, "מים גנובים": 2, "הסתר": 2, "רוח שטות": 2, "עברה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Package 2 is derived from the source text's explicit bechina statements and their implied equivalences. All five nodes express the same essential reality: the domain of lust-in-hiding and spirit-of-folly that opposes Torah's wisdom. The merge question is: should any node from Package 2 be merged into Package 1? Answer: NO. The two packages represent structurally opposed poles. Torah's entering into the secret place (הַסֵּתֶר) is an eitza connection between packages (Torah goes there → she can testify), not a shared nature. The הַסֵּתֶר IS the domain of תַּאֲווֹת (shared nature, bechina, Package 2); Torah ENTERS the seter (cause-effect / narrative relation, eitza between packages). These must remain separate."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 147, "label": "עַזּוּת דִּקְדֻשָּׁה וְאִתְעָרוּתָא דִּלְתַתָּא", "label_en": "Holy Boldness and Arousal from Below", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package centers on עזות דקדשה — the holy boldness that enables a person to stand firm against all opponents of his Torah-study and Divine service. It is the only bechina cluster in the dataset for this Torah: the verse-phrase 'תנו עז לאלקים' is declared a bechina of אתערותא דלתתא (arousal from below), meaning that when Israel exercises holy boldness in standing against their adversaries, this IS the lower-world arousal that draws down corresponding Divine strength from above. The two concepts share the same essential nature: both are the 'empowering push' that originates in the human realm. The eitza structure of the Torah (עזות דקדשה → תנו עז → Divine overflow back) is the causal flow between packages, not within them. The essential nature shared within this package is: holy strength / willpower that rises upward and draws down blessing. This Torah's positive teaching culminates in the concept that holy boldness is not merely a personal virtue but a cosmic act — it is how human will strengthens the Divine throne (כביכול).", "dominant_node": "אתערותא דלתתא", "nodes": ["תנו עז לאלקים", "אתערותא דלתתא"], "node_degrees": {"תנו עז לאלקים": 1, "אתערותא דלתתא": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_edge": {"id": 488, "proof_summary": "תְּנוּ עֹז לֵאלקִים — זֶה בְּחִינַת אִתְעָרוּתָא דִּלְתַתָּא: 'give strength to God' is declared a bechina of arousal-from-below, because our holy boldness in standing against opponents IS the act of raising strength from below."}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 147, "label": "עַזּוּת פָּנִים וְעַזּוּת דְּסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "label_en": "Brazen Impudence and Evil Boldness", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror package. Torah #147 explicitly describes the two opposing forms of boldness: עזות דקדשה (Package 1 — holy) and עזות פנים / עזות דסטרא אחרא (this package — evil). The text names both as belonging to the same structural type (both are 'brazenness') but oriented in opposite directions. עזות פנים is the impudence of those who have no share in Torah — described as the 974 pre-creation generations who were unfit to receive Torah and were 'planted' in each generation as the brazen ones. The Rebbe explicitly identifies these as the source of obstruction against Torah and Divine service. The evil boldness of the sitra achra (עזות דסטרא אחרא) is the force that believers must stand against — it is the evil mirror of the עזות דקדשה of Package 1. Both belong here because they share the same essential nature: brazenness oriented toward the side of evil / obstruction / disconnection from Torah. This package has no dataset edges yet — it requires the additions listed in torah_147_additions.json to be populated. See that file for the proposed nodes.", "dominant_node": "עזות פנים", "nodes": ["עזות פנים", "עזות דסטרא אחרא"], "node_degrees": {"עזות פנים": 0, "עזות דסטרא אחרא": 0}, "merge_notes": [], "note": "These nodes are not yet in the dataset — they are proposed additions (see torah_147_additions.json). Their package assignment is established here based on the source text's explicit structural opposition. Once the bechina edges connecting them are added to the dataset, their node_degrees will update accordingly."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 148, "label": "יִרְאָה וְאֵין סוֹף", "label_en": "Fear of God and the Infinite", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah #148 is a single unified teaching about the infinite recursive nature of יִרְאָה (fear of God). The Rebbe's central claim is that יִרְאָה itself is afraid of God — the midah has its own yirah — and so each level of fear contains a higher fear, ascending endlessly. From this he draws the two supporting verses: (1) 'עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה'' — the heel of humility is fear, meaning יִרְאָה is the essential nature and endpoint of עֲנָוָה; (2) 'רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה יִרְאַת ה'' — the beginning of wisdom is fear, meaning יִרְאָה is also the starting-point of חָכְמָה. Both verses show יִרְאָה appearing at a different position relative to its neighboring virtue, which the Rebbe reads as evidence that fear has an infinite depth: it is simultaneously the heel/outcome (עָקֵב) of humility AND the crown/beginning (רֵאשִׁית/עֲטָרָה) of wisdom — positions that cannot coexist unless fear is infinite and transcends fixed hierarchical location. All three concepts — יִרְאָה, עֲנָוָה, and חָכְמָה — are aspects of the same positive domain of divine service. חָכְמָה and עֲנָוָה are the two virtues that meet יִרְאָה from opposite directions (one from above/beginning, one from below/heel), and they are both faces of the same holy reality described by יִרְאָה. The concept of אֵין סוֹף (infinity) is the structural descriptor of how high yirah reaches — it is the qualitative dimension of the package. All nodes are positive/good.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "ענוה", "חכמה", "אין סוף"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 3, "ענוה": 1, "חכמה": 1, "אין סוף": 1}, "source_edges": [{"node1": "ענוה", "node2": "יראה", "proof": "נִמְצָא שֶׁעֲקֵב הָעֲנָוָה הִיא יִרְאָה", "source_quote": "עֵקֶב עֲנָוָה יִרְאַת ה'"}, {"node1": "חכמה", "node2": "יראה", "proof": "כֵן רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה הִיא גַּם־כֵּן יִרְאָה", "source_quote": "רֵאשִׁית חָכְמָה יִרְאַת ה'"}, {"node1": "יראה", "node2": "אין סוף", "proof": "וְכֵן נִכְלָל יִרְאָה אַחַת בַּחֲבֶרְתָּהּ, לְמַעְלָה לְמַעְלָה עַד אֵין סוֹף", "source_quote": "לְמַעְלָה לְמַעְלָה עַד אֵין סוֹף"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 149, "label": "חֲצוֹת וְהַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים", "label_en": "Midnight Prayer and the Sweetening of Judgments", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package centers on חֲצוֹת לַיְלָה (the midnight hour / midnight prayer practice) as the practice that sweetens din (strict judgment) and achieves the rectification of the verse. The text declares 'כִּי הוּא הַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים' — IT IS the sweetening of judgments — using the bechina formula. Chatzot shares its essential nature with המתקת הדינים: they are two expressions of the same rectifying reality. The analogy with פִּדְיוֹן (redemption/ransom payment) further enriches this cluster: chatzot functions with the same essential dynamic as pidyon — a payment/neutralization that releases the held captive (here: souls held under strict din). The practical halakhic detail in the teaching (chatzot always begins 6 hours after nightfall, extends through the second watch) belongs to this same thematic cluster as the contextual definition of the practice. The dominant node is חֲצוֹת because it is the named subject of the entire Torah and has the most conceptual connections within this package. The polarity is good: chatzot is an act of holiness, arising to praise God at midnight (as David did — cf. Psalm 119:62, the verse cited), and it achieves sweetening of judgments, a positive rectification.", "dominant_node": "חצות", "nodes": ["חצות", "המתקת הדינים", "פדיון, כי הוא המתקת הדינים"], "node_degrees": {"חצות": 0, "המתקת הדינים": 0, "פדיון, כי הוא המתקת הדינים": 0}, "merge_notes": ["The node 'פדיון, כי הוא המתקת הדינים' (id node2 of edge 2477) already includes the identification with המתקת הדינים in its node label. Once the bechina edge {חצות ↔ המתקת הדינים} is added to the dataset (see torah_149_additions.json, addition #3), this package will have one bechina edge anchoring its two primary nodes. The pidyon analogy is structurally close to a bechina but the source text uses 'מסגל כמו' (is endowed/suited like) for pidyon, which is analogical rather than an identity statement. The true bechina identity is chatzot ↔ המתקת הדינים."], "note": "חצות is the only node currently in the dataset for reference 149 (as node1 of the eitza edge 2477). The other two nodes in this package are proposed additions from the gap analysis. Once the bechina edge is added, the component will have 2 directly connected nodes (חצות and המתקת הדינים) with פדיון serving as a closely related concept in the same domain."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 149, "label": "צֶדֶק וָדִין", "label_en": "Tzedek and Strict Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The text states explicitly: 'כִּי צֶדֶק – דִּינָא' — Tzedek IS din. This is a bechina equation in the classic 'X – Y' formula that Rebbe Nachman uses throughout Likutay Moharan. Tzedek (the quality of righteousness/justice, also associated with the sefirah Malkhut in its strict aspect) and דין (strict judgment, the harsh face of heavenly law) share the same essential nature in this Torah's context: they are both the force of strict accounting that requires sweetening through chatzot. The polarity of this package is evil (or more precisely: the side that requires rectification) — not that tzedek is intrinsically evil, but in this Torah's structural argument, tzedek/din is the force of harshness that suppresses and constricts, the force that chatzot (Package 1) is brought to sweeten and overcome. The teaching frames the verse 'על מִשְׁפְּטֵי צִדְקֶךָ' as 'mishpat prevailing over tzedek' — i.e., rachamim overcoming din. Tzedek/din is the force being overcome: it is the negative pole in this Torah's polarity structure. Both nodes (צדק and דין) are proposed additions to the dataset for this Torah (see torah_149_additions.json, additions #1 and related). They share a single essential nature: strict judgment / the attribute of stern divine accounting.", "dominant_node": "צדק", "nodes": ["צדק", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"צדק": 0, "דין": 0}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component is self-sufficient. Merge with Package 3 (משפט/רחמים) is explicitly ruled out: they represent opposite cosmic poles (din vs. rachamim). They are the structural opposites of the teaching, not the same essential nature. Merge with Package 1 is also ruled out: chatzot sweetens din but is not the same essential nature as din."], "note": "Both nodes צדק and דין are already present in the broader dataset for other Torahs but have no bechina edge for reference 149. A new bechina edge {צדק ↔ דין} for reference 149 is proposed in the additions file."}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 149, "label": "מִשְׁפָּט וְרַחֲמִים", "label_en": "Mishpat and Divine Mercy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The text states explicitly: 'וּמִשְׁפָּט – רַחֲמֵי' — Mishpat IS rachamim. This is the second bechina equation in the Torah, parallel in structure to the צדק–דין equation of Package 2. Mishpat (legal rule / divine judgment in its merciful mode) and רַחֲמִים (compassion/mercy) share the same essential nature in this context. The teaching's entire resolution — 'שֶׁצֶּדֶק גּוֹבֵר עַל מִשְׁפָּט' is explained as tzedek/din being overridden by mishpat/rachamim — depends on this identification. Mishpat here is the merciful counterpart: it is the divine attribute that looks beyond strict accounting toward compassion. The polarity is good: rachamim and mishpat in this Torah's usage are the positive, life-giving divine attribute that the practice of chatzot activates against the harshness of din/tzedek. Package 3 is the direct good-mirror of Package 2 (evil): same structural category (divine attribute/judgment mode), opposite cosmic orientation. Together, Packages 2 and 3 constitute the good/evil mirror structure that the framework mandates identifying. Both nodes (משפט and רחמים/רחמי) are proposed additions for reference 149 (see torah_149_additions.json, additions #2 and related).", "dominant_node": "משפט", "nodes": ["משפט", "רחמים"], "node_degrees": {"משפט": 0, "רחמים": 0}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component is self-sufficient as the direct good-mirror of Package 2. Merge with Package 2 is ruled out (opposite polarities; the framework forbids merging across the good/evil polarity boundary when they represent opposing cosmic forces). Merge with Package 1 is possible conceptually (chatzot achieves rachamim), but that is an eitza relationship (cause-effect), not a bechina (shared nature). The essential nature of Package 1 (chatzot / the practice of sweetening) is distinct from the essential nature of Package 3 (the attribute of mercy itself). They are kept separate."], "note": "The existing dataset edge 2477 (type: eitza) already encodes the causal flow from chatzot to המתקת הדינים, which structurally connects Package 1 → Package 2/3 axis (chatzot sweetens din and activates rachamim). The eitza flows between packages; the bechina pairs are within packages. This structural separation is maintained here."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 150, "label": "דְּמוּת דְּיוֹקֶן וְנִסָּיוֹן הַנִּסְתָּר", "label_en": "The Hidden Image and the Concealed Trial", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This teaching contains a single thematic cluster, centered on three mutually illuminating concepts: (1) דְּמוּת דְּיוֹקְנוֹ שֶׁל אָבִיו — the image of his father's form, which appeared to Yosef to protect him from sin; (2) נִסָּיוֹן — the trial/test, specifically the trial of sexual temptation that Yosef faced; (3) נִסְתָּר / דְּבָרִים נִסְתָּרִים וּמְכֻסִּים — the hidden and concealed nature of how the image appeared. All three share the same essential nature within this teaching: they all belong to the domain of the miraculous hidden dimension of spiritual protection. The implicit bechina declared by the Rebbe is: מַעֲלַת הַנִּסָּיוֹן (the excellence/quality of the trial) IS constituted by the hiddenness — the very fact that these are דְּבָרִים נִסְתָּרִים וּמְכֻסִּים mִבְּנֵי אָדָם IS the greatness of what occurred. The image (דְּמוּת דְּיוֹקֶן) IS a hidden matter (דָּבָר נִסְתָּר) by its very nature — not explainable, not knowable even to Yaakov himself. The Rebbe does not explain the mechanism; he declares it to be opaque and presents that opacity as the defining feature. These three dimensions — the image, the trial, and the hiddenness — all refer to the same essential phenomenon from different angles. They cannot be separated into distinct packages because the teaching is too brief and does not develop independent conceptual clusters. This is a good package because the entire discourse is about a positive spiritual protection: the father's image appearing to save his son from sin. The polarity is unambiguously good: Yosef withstands temptation, the miracle is of the side of holiness, the father-image is the tzaddik/patriarch protecting his descendants.", "dominant_node": "דמות דיוקן", "nodes": ["דמות דיוקנו של אביו", "נסיון", "דברים נסתרים ומכוסים"], "node_degrees": {"דמות דיוקנו של אביו": 2, "נסיון": 1, "דברים נסתרים ומכוסים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["The three nodes form one inseparable cluster — the teaching is too short and thematically unified to split into sub-packages. The implicit bechina structure is: דמות דיוקן ↔ דבר נסתר (the image IS a hidden matter), and מעלת הנסיון ↔ דברים נסתרים ומכוסים (the greatness of the trial IS constituted by the hiddenness). Since the single teaching makes all three concepts facets of one reality — the miraculous hidden protection that enabled Yosef's trial — they belong together in one package."], "note": "These nodes are proposed additions — none exist in the dataset for reference 150. The node 'דמות דיוקן' (image of the form) likely already exists in torahData.js for other references (the concept of the 'image of the father' appears in multiple teachings about Yosef). The node 'נסיון' (trial) also likely exists for other references. 'דברים נסתרים ומכוסים' is likely a new node specific to this Torah's formulation. Once bechina edges are added to the dataset for reference 150, node_degrees will update accordingly."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 151, "label": "וָאו וְהַחִיּוּת", "label_en": "The Letter Vav and the Life-Force", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's central positive assertion is that the letter vav IS the life-force of all things: 'כִּי הַוָּא\"ו הוּא הַחִיּוּת שֶׁל כָּל דָּבָר.' This is stated twice (lines 2 and 3) as the structural key to the entire teaching — everything in the Torah's argument pivots on this equation. The vav is not an instrument that produces life-force, nor a symbol that points toward it: it IS the life-force. Vav and חִיּוּת are two names for the same essential reality, made explicit by the word 'הוּא' (IS), which is the Rebbe's standard bechina-identity formula. The polarity is good because the life-force (חִיּוּת) is the positive, vital, divine energy that sustains children and all living things. The vav as life-force is the root of health, vitality, and existence — the positive pole of this Torah's cosmic axis.", "dominant_node": "וָאו", "nodes": ["וָאו", "חִיּוּת"], "node_degrees": {"וָאו": 1, "חִיּוּת": 1}, "source_edges": [{"node1": "וָאו", "node2": "חִיּוּת", "proof": "כִּי הַוָּא\"ו הוּא הַחִיּוּת שֶׁל כָּל דָּבָר", "source_quote": "הַוָּא\"ו הוּא הַחִיּוּת שֶׁל כָּל דָּבָר"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 151, "label": "פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה וְחֶסְרוֹן הַחִיּוּת", "label_en": "The Moon's Blemish and the Deficiency of Life-Force", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package groups all nodes in Torah #151 that represent the negative/harmful pole: the blemished/deficient state of the moon (פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה), the deficient spelling of the word 'lights' (מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו), and the absence of life-force (חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת). All three are different expressions of the same underlying cosmic defect — the moon's blemish — which the Torah explicitly identifies using bechina language. The text says 'כָּל זֶה הוּא סוֹד פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה, שֶׁמִּשָּׁם נִמְשָׁךְ בְּחִינַת מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו' — the me'orot deficiency IS a bechina of (= shares the essential nature of) the moon's blemish. And separately: 'חֶסְרוֹן הַוָּא\"ו מְרַמֵּז עַל חֶסְרוֹן הַחִיּוּת' — the absence of vav = the absence of life-force. These two sub-components (מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר ↔ פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה, and חֶסְרוֹן וָאו ↔ חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת) are merged into one package because they share the same essential nature: the domain of cosmic deficiency, the harmful/evil pole in this Torah. The merge is valid per the framework's 'shared nature' rule: both sub-components describe the same fundamental reality from different angles (the specific scriptural instance of the deficiency vs. the general principle of what the deficiency means). The polarity is evil: this package is the domain of spiritual/cosmic damage — the moon's flaw that causes askarah and death in children. Note: the node לְבָנָה (the moon) appears in Package 3 as well, but in its deficiency aspect (פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה) it is distinctly the evil-pole version of the same moon-concept.", "dominant_node": "פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה", "nodes": ["פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה", "מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו", "חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת"], "node_degrees": {"פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה": 1, "מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו": 1, "חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת": 1}, "source_edges": [{"node1": "מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו", "node2": "פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה", "proof": "וְכָל זֶה הוּא סוֹד פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה, שֶׁמִּשָּׁם נִמְשָׁךְ בְּחִינַת מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו", "source_quote": "כָּל זֶה הוּא סוֹד פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה"}, {"node1": "חֶסְרוֹן וָאו", "node2": "חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת", "proof": "חֶסְרוֹן הַוָּא\"ו מְרַמֵּז עַל חֶסְרוֹן הַחִיּוּת כַּנַּ\"ל", "source_quote": "חֶסְרוֹן הַוָּא\"ו מְרַמֵּז עַל חֶסְרוֹן הַחִיּוּת"}], "merge_notes": [{"merged_components": [{"nodes": ["מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר וָאו", "פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה"], "source_edge": "E2"}, {"nodes": ["חֶסְרוֹן וָאו", "חֶסְרוֹן חִיּוּת"], "source_edge": "E3"}], "reason": "Both 2-node components describe the same essential domain: the harmful deficiency that kills children. מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר and חֶסְרוֹן וָאו are variant expressions of the same vav-deficiency (one the specific scriptural instance, one the abstract principle). Both are identified as expressions of the moon's blemish. Merge is by shared nature (same domain of cosmic deficiency), never by cause-effect. The two edges point to the same underlying reality from different angles: the specific deficient word in the Torah (מְאֹרֹת) vs. the general structural principle (absence of vav = absence of life-force). Note: 'חֶסְרוֹן וָאו' is included in this package as it is the negative pole of 'וָאו' (Package 1's positive node) — a classic good/evil mirror: the vav = life-force (good), the absence of vav = absence of life-force (evil)."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 151, "label": "כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה וְתִקּוּן הַלְּבָנָה", "label_en": "Kalot Moshe and the Rectification of the Moon", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups all nodes in Torah #151 that represent the positive healing/tikkun pole: כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה (the moment when Moses completed the Tabernacle — a word written without vav in the Torah, like מְאֹרֹת), the moon (לְבָנָה) in its aspect of being filled on Rosh Chodesh, and כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם (atonement for all offspring). Two explicit bechina statements connect these: (1) 'שֶׁנִּתְתַּקֵּן וְנִמְתָּק בְּחִינַת כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַלְּבָנָה' — Kalot Moshe IS a bechina of the moon; (2) 'כַּלֹּת – רָאשֵׁי תֵּבוֹת: כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם' — the word kalot IS (acronymically) atonement for all offspring. These three nodes form one connected component: לְבָנָה ↔ כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה ↔ כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם. The dominant node is כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה because it is the specific phrase that generates both connections (it IS the moon, and its letters ARE the atonement). The polarity is good: this is the tikkun — the rectification of the moon's deficiency, the source of atonement, forgiveness, sweetening, and long life for Israel's offspring. This package is the positive resolution of Package 2 (the evil pole). Note: לְבָנָה appears in both Package 2 (as פְּגִימַת הַלְּבָנָה, its deficient/evil aspect) and Package 3 (as the moon being filled, its tikkun/good aspect) — precisely the dual-polarity structure the framework describes for nodes that can appear in both a good and an evil package.", "dominant_node": "כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה", "nodes": ["כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה", "לְבָנָה", "כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם"], "node_degrees": {"כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה": 2, "לְבָנָה": 1, "כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם": 1}, "source_edges": [{"node1": "כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה", "node2": "לְבָנָה", "proof": "שֶׁנִּתְתַּקֵּן וְנִמְתָּק בְּחִינַת כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַלְּבָנָה", "source_quote": "כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַלְּבָנָה"}, {"node1": "כַּלֹּת מֹשֶׁה", "node2": "כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם", "proof": "וְזֶהוּ: כַּלֹּת – רָאשֵׁי תֵּבוֹת: כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם", "source_quote": "כַּלֹּת – רָאשֵׁי תֵּבוֹת: כַּפָּרָה לְכָל תּוֹלְדוֹתָם"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 152, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּנְשָׁמָה וְאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "Emuna, the Holy Soul, and Eretz Yisrael", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The largest conceptual cluster in Torah #152 centers on the holy soul (נְשָׁמָה קְדוֹשָׁה) as the sacred inner sanctum, with emuna (אֱמוּנָה) as the accessible gateway-face of the soul and the gateway itself (פֶּתַח) as the structural expression of emuna. These three share the same essential nature: they are all aspects of the holy center that the klipa surrounds but cannot fully close. The text establishes the identity in line 4 with the classic dehavinu formula: 'סְתִימַת הַפֶּתַח הַנַּ\"ל, דְּהַיְנוּ הָאֱמוּנָה' — the gateway IS emuna. The soul is identified as the root in line 2: 'שָׁרְשָׁם, שֶׁהוּא הַנְּשָׁמָה הַנַּ\"ל' — their root, which IS the holy soul. Eretz Yisrael is explicitly merged into this cluster in line 9: 'כִּי הַכֹּל אֶחָד עִם הַנַּ\"ל' — 'for everything is one with the above' — meaning Eretz Yisrael's gatekeeping dynamic (the boundary that admits the worthy and rejects the unworthy, drawn from Zohar Acharei) is the same essential structure as the emuna/soul gateway. The yirei shamayim / perisa kalisha pair (raw component C) is merged into this package because: (a) the perisa kalisha IS the membrane spread over the gateway (פֶּתַח) — it is the emuna-boundary made structural (Zohar Teruma, cited in line 8); (b) the yirei shamayim IS a bechina of the perisa kalisha (explicit bechina language in line 8); (c) the Rebbe concludes line 8 with 'וְהוּא מַמָּשׁ מַה שֶּׁכָּתוּב לְעֵיל בְּעִנְיַן הַיְרֵא שָׁמַיִם' — it is exactly what was described regarding the yirei shamayim — asserting that the perisa kalisha and the yirei shamayim are the same reality as the emuna gateway. All seven nodes in this package share the essential nature of 'the sacred inner domain and its protective boundary-face.'", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "פתח (שער האמונה)", "נשמה קדושה", "שרש (של הענפים)", "ארץ ישראל", "ירא שמים", "פריסא קלישא"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 3, "פתח (שער האמונה)": 3, "נשמה קדושה": 2, "שרש (של הענפים)": 2, "ארץ ישראל": 1, "ירא שמים": 2, "פריסא קלישא": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נשמה קדושה", "שרש (של הענפים)"], "reason": "Line 2: 'שָׁרְשָׁם, שֶׁהוּא הַנְּשָׁמָה הַנַּ\"ל' — 'their root, which IS the holy soul.' The שֶׁהוּא (which is) formula establishes bechina identity. The soul IS the root of the branches; the root IS the soul. These are the same reality described from two directions — the soul from above (the root) and the branches' goal from below (the root they are trying to reach). Both belong to the holy-center cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ארץ ישראל"], "reason": "Line 9 explicitly states: 'כִּי הַכֹּל אֶחָד עִם הַנַּ\"ל' — 'for everything is one with the above.' The Rebbe is equating the Eretz Yisrael gatekeeping dynamic (from Zohar Acharei, cited in line 3) with the emuna/soul/gateway structure of the entire Torah. Eretz Yisrael shares the essential nature of the emuna cluster: a holy domain with a gateway that admits the worthy (those with intact branches) and rejects the unworthy (those whose branches are blemished). The parallelism is exact: ארץ ישראל = the holy domain; those cast out from emuna = the dead unworthy of burial in Eretz Yisrael. By the explicit 'כִּי הַכֹּל אֶחָד' declaration, this is a bechina-level identity, not merely an analogy."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ירא שמים", "פריסא קלישא"], "reason": "Line 8: 'הוּא בְּחִינַת הַיְרֵא שָׁמַיִם, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת: פְּרִיסָא קַלִּישָׁא' — this is the only explicitly labeled 'בְּחִינַת' statement in the entire Torah, making it the most certain bechina pair. The yirei shamayim IS a bechina of the perisa kalisha; the perisa kalisha IS spread over the gateway (פֶּתַח). The Rebbe concludes: 'וְהוּא מַמָּשׁ מַה שֶּׁכָּתוּב לְעֵיל בְּעִנְיַן הַיְרֵא שָׁמַיִם' — it is exactly what was described above regarding the yirei shamayim. This merge into Package 1 is justified because the perisa kalisha is a structural aspect of the gateway (פֶּתַח/אֱמוּנָה) itself — it is the emuna-boundary made tangible. The yirei shamayim, by being a bechina of the perisa kalisha, thus shares the essential nature of the emuna-gateway domain, not merely a functional relationship to it. They are not guardians OF emuna from outside; they ARE the emuna boundary in its active-protective form."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 152, "label": "חָכְמָה וּמֹחַ", "label_en": "Wisdom and the Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #152 concludes its main teaching in line 7 with a crucial limitation statement: 'אַךְ אִי אֶפְשָׁר שֶׁיָּאִיר אֵלָיו חָכְמָה, דְּהַיְנוּ הַמֹּחַ בְּעַצְמוֹ' — 'But it is impossible for wisdom (chokhmah) to shine upon him — that is (dehavinu), the intellect (moch) itself.' The dehavinu formula establishes the bechina identity: chokhmah IS the moch. These two nodes are two expressions of the same essential reality: the highest level of divine intellect, the innermost illumination of the soul that transcends the emuna-gateway transmission path. This package is kept separate from Package 1 precisely because the Rebbe distinguishes it: mesirut nefesh enables attachment (debikut) to the tzaddik, but it CANNOT cause chokhmah/moch to shine. The essential nature of chokhmah/moch is 'the transcendent intellect-beyond-attachment' — it stands above the emuna-gateway system, not within it. This is a 2-node package but is not merged with any other package because its domain is structurally distinct: it is the unreachable upper level, the limit of what can be transmitted, the horizon the teaching points toward but acknowledges cannot be crossed through ordinary mesirut nefesh.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["חכמה", "מוח"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה": 1, "מוח": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": [], "reason": "Merge with Package 1 (emuna/soul) ruled out: chokhmah/moch is the domain ABOVE the emuna-gateway transmission. The text's structure places chokhmah as what CANNOT be received even through mesirut nefesh — it is definitionally beyond the emuna-gateway system. Merging it into Package 1 would collapse the Torah's key distinction. Merge with Package 3 (mesirut nefesh) ruled out: chokhmah/moch is the goal that mesirut nefesh cannot achieve; the essential nature of Package 3 is the path of approach, while Package 2 is the unreachable destination. These are cause-and-effect limitations, not shared essential natures — the Rebbe says mesirut nefesh DOES NOT produce chokhmah, precisely because they are different things."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 152, "label": "מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ וְהִתְקָרְבוּת לְהַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "Self-Sacrifice and Drawing Close to the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Line 7: 'וְעַל יְדֵי מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ, דְּהַיְנוּ שֶׁמּוֹסֵר נַפְשׁוֹ בִּשְׁבִיל לִנְסֹעַ וּלְהִתְקָרֵב לְהַצַּדִּיק' — 'And through mesirut nefesh (self-sacrifice), that is (dehavinu), one who devotes his life to travel and draw close to the tzaddik.' The dehavinu establishes the bechina identity: mesirut nefesh IS, in this Torah's context, the act of traveling and drawing close to the tzaddik. These two nodes express the same essential reality: the consecrated devotional act of approaching holiness through total self-giving. Mesirut nefesh names the inner quality (total self-dedication); traveling to the tzaddik names the outer expression of that quality. They are two faces of the same thing. This package is kept separate from Package 1 (emuna/soul/gateway) because: (a) the Torah's structure presents mesirut nefesh as the practice that enables debikut — this is an eitza relationship (mesirut nefesh → attachment to the tzaddik), not a shared nature with the emuna-gateway domain itself; (b) the essential nature of Package 3 is the active-seeker's path, while Package 1 is the holy-center domain that the seeker aims toward. These are structurally distinct domains: the seeker-path and the destination-domain.", "dominant_node": "מסירת נפש", "nodes": ["מסירת נפש", "נסיעה והתקרבות לצדיק"], "node_degrees": {"מסירת נפש": 1, "נסיעה והתקרבות לצדיק": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": [], "reason": "Merge with Package 1 (emuna/soul) ruled out: mesirut nefesh is the practice/path of approaching the emuna-gateway; it is not the same essential nature as the gateway itself. The Rebbe's phrasing 'על ידי מסירת נפש ... אפשר לדבק עצמו אליו' (through mesirut nefesh it is possible to attach) is causal/eitza language, not bechina identity. Merge with Package 2 (chokhmah/moch) ruled out: the Rebbe explicitly distinguishes mesirut nefesh from chokhmah — the former enables debikut (attachment) but CANNOT enable chokhmah to shine. They are defined by their contrast, not their shared nature."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 153, "label": "רַב וְתַלְמִיד — אוֹר הַחַמָּה וְהַמַּרְאָה הַמְּלֻטֶּשֶׁת", "label_en": "Rav and Talmid — The Sunlight and the Polished Mirror", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package contains the entire positive teaching cluster of Torah #153. The Torah opens with the chamah/levanah metaphor: the moon has no light of its own but receives light from the sun because it is like a polished mirror. The Rebbe then declares the Rav and Talmid to be bechina chamah and levanah respectively ('הַתַּלְמִיד וְהָרַב הֵם בְּחִינַת חַמָּה וּלְבָנָה' — edge 1185 and edge 1564). The talmid's face having אנפין נהירין is immediately identified as being בחינת מראה מלטשת (edge 1944). These three pairs all share the same essential nature: they are all different expressions of the one phenomenon of a receiver that is refined enough to accept and reflect back the light of its source. The Rav IS the chamah — the light-giver; the Talmid IS the levanah — the receiver; the Talmid's אנפין נהירין IS the polished mirror — the condition of refined, transparent receptivity. All six nodes exist on the positive side: they all describe the state of holy reception, the capacity of the student to receive the face/light of the teacher and reflect it back ('שֶׁיִּתְרָאֶה בְּתוֹכוֹ פְּנֵי הָרַב'). The text reinforces this by citing the Sinai theophany: 'פָּנִים בְּפָנִים' — at the moment of Torah-giving, Israel had אנפין נהירין and could receive the divine face. The nodes רב, חמה, תלמיד, לבנה, אנפין נהירין, and מראה מלטשת all illuminate the same cosmic structure from different domains: human relationship, celestial metaphor, and facial-aura language from the kabbalistic tradition. The dominant node is רב because it is the source/transmitter of the light — all other nodes in the cluster are defined relative to what the Rav represents. Equivalently, תלמיד could be considered central as the subject-agent of the receiving act. The Rav has 1 direct bechina edge (to חמה); the Talmid has 1 direct bechina edge (to לבנה); but the Rav and Talmid appear together in the same bechina sentence and are structurally symmetric. The אנפין נהירין ↔ מראה מלטשת pair is subordinate — it describes the quality of the תלמיד side of the dyad. Merge rationale: Components A (רב/חמה) and B (תלמיד/לבנה) must merge because the original bechina sentence declares them jointly ('הַתַּלְמִיד וְהָרַב הֵם בְּחִינַת חַמָּה וּלְבָנָה') — they share the same essential nature as the two poles of a single light-transmission dyad. Component C (אנפין נהירין/מראה מלטשת) merges into this cluster by shared nature: the teaching explicitly identifies אנפין נהירין as the quality that defines the תלמיד/לבנה when she is capable of receiving — they are the same thing from different angles (the talmid's face with אנפין נהירין IS the lev'anah-as-polished-mirror).", "dominant_node": "רב", "nodes": ["רב", "חמה", "תלמיד", "לבנה", "אנפין נהירין", "מראה מלטשת"], "node_degrees": {"רב": 1, "חמה": 1, "תלמיד": 1, "לבנה": 1, "אנפין נהירין": 1, "מראה מלטשת": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Components A (רב/חמה, edge 1185) and B (תלמיד/לבנה, edge 1564) are merged into one package because the source text declares them jointly in a single bechina sentence. They represent the two poles of the same phenomenon: the teacher-student light-transmission dyad. The Rav being chamah and the Talmid being levanah are not independent facts — they are a paired statement about a single relational structure. Neither can stand alone as a package without the other.", "Component C (אנפין נהירין/מראה מלטשת, edge 1944) merges with A+B because the text explicitly equates אנפין נהירין with the quality of the Talmid/Levanah that enables reception: 'אִם הַתַּלְמִיד יֵשׁ לוֹ פָּנִים, הַיְנוּ בְּחִינַת אַנְפִּין נְהִירִין, בְּחִינַת מַרְאָה מְלֻטֶּשֶׁת.' The three concepts (face, shining face, polished mirror) are introduced in the same sentence as sequential bechina identifications: having face = having אנפין נהירין = being a מראה מלטשת. These are the same thing viewed from three angles. Merging C into A+B by SHARED NATURE: the polished mirror IS the levanah IS the talmid's shining face — they are different vocabulary for the same essential quality."], "note": "All 6 nodes already exist in the dataset with bechina edges for reference 153 (edges 1185, 1564, 1944). An additional bechina edge connecting אנפין נהירין to תלמיד, or to לבנה directly, is not in the dataset but is strongly implied by the text — see torah_153_additions.json for proposed additions. The nodes פנים (face) and קבלת פנים (reception of the face) appear prominently in the source text but are not currently in the dataset as bechina nodes for reference 153 — these are candidates for addition (gap analysis)."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 153, "label": "אַנְפִּין חֲשׁוּכִין וְתַאֲוַת מָמוֹן", "label_en": "Darkened Face and the Lust for Money", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package contains the evil-mirror cluster of Torah #153. Edge 2002 declares: 'אַנְפִּין חֲשׁוּכִין, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת הַמְשֻׁקָּע בְּתַאֲוַת מָמוֹן' — the darkened face IS the state of being sunk in money-lust. This is the direct structural mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 has אנפין נהירין (shining face = polished mirror = good receptivity), Package 2 has אנפין חשוכין (darkened face = inability to receive = sunk in monetary desire). The teaching makes the mirror explicit: the Rav who does not see his reflection in the Talmid's face knows the Talmid is in a state of אנפין חשוכין, which IS being 'מְשֻׁקָּע בְּתַאֲוַת מָמוֹן.' The metaphor is taken from the opening celestial passage: just as a body that is 'עָב וְחָשׁוּךְ' (opaque and dark) cannot receive the sun's light, the talmid with a darkened face (metaphysical opacity) cannot receive the Rav's face. The two nodes are: אנפין חשוכין (the structural negative mirror of אנפין נהירין in Package 1) and משקע בתאות ממון (the specific moral cause of the darkness — sinking in money-lust). These share the same essential nature: they are both descriptions of spiritual opacity from different angles — one structural (darkened face), one etiological (the cause: money-lust). The Rebbe cites Torah 23 for further elaboration ('כַּמְבֹאָר בְּמָקוֹם אַחֵר — לְעֵיל בְּסִימָן כ\"ג'). Also related: the text introduces 'עַזּוּת פָּנִים' (brazenness) at the end of the teaching, stating that עזות corresponds to lacking holy face and inability to receive. This is a third node in the evil cluster — see gap analysis.", "dominant_node": "אנפין חשוכין", "nodes": ["אנפין חשוכין", "משקע בתאות ממון"], "node_degrees": {"אנפין חשוכין": 1, "משקע בתאות ממון": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component stands on its own as the evil mirror of Package 1's אנפין נהירין/מראה מלטשת sub-cluster. No merge into Package 1 is possible: they represent opposite polarities (the framework explicitly identifies these as the good/evil mirror pair). The merge rule (by SHARED NATURE only) actually reinforces their separation: אנפין חשוכין and אנפין נהירין do NOT share the same nature — they are precise negations of each other. Component D is kept as a standalone 2-node evil package.", "A potential third node for this package is עזות פנים — the text explicitly states that one with עזות has no holy face (אין לו פנים דקדשה) and cannot receive faces (Nedarin 20 is cited). The teaching says those with עזות did not have their ancestors stand at Mount Sinai — the inverse of Israel at Sinai having אנפין נהירין (Package 1). עזות פנים IS the condition of having אנפין חשוכין, just as משקע בתאות ממון IS. See gap analysis for proposed addition of this node to Package 2."], "note": "Both nodes in this package are currently in the dataset for reference 153 (edge 2002). The addition of עזות פנים as a third node is proposed in torah_153_additions.json — it would expand this evil package to 3 nodes and one additional bechina edge."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 154, "label": "יִרְאָה וְאֶרֶץ", "label_en": "Fear and Earth", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina edge in the dataset (edge 444) connects יִרְאָה (divine fear/awe) with אֶרֶץ (the earth). This represents the Torah's core bechina equation: fear of God is the earth-quality — grounded, receptive, the lowest dimension that holds everything above it. The Rebbe derives this from Tehillim 76: 'אֶרֶץ יָרְאָה' — the earth itself is fear. This is the positive package: it describes the desired state of elevated yirah, namely יִרְאַת הָרוֹמְמוּת (transcendent awe of God's majesty), which is the root and proper home of all fear. The lev (heart) is identified as the root-place of yirah, and daat as the quality that must accompany it. The dataset captures only the outer shell of this teaching — the full component (after gap-filling) would include יִרְאָה, אֶרֶץ, לֵב, דַּעַת, and יִרְאַת הָרוֹמְמוּת as the apex concept.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "ארץ"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 1, "ארץ": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 155, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וְאֶרֶץ יִשְׂרָאֵל", "label_en": "Faith and the Land of Israel", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #155 teaches a single integrated thesis: אמונה (faith/trust in God) is the life-force of divine service, manifested identically as ארך אפים (patient forbearance), as כח הגודל וכח הצומח (the power of growth and flourishing — like wheat planted in good soil), and as ארץ ישראל (the Land of Israel, which is the spiritual source and locus of this quality). The node אמת (truth) enters through Eretz Yisrael's second bechina-identity (the Talmudic tradition that 'Moses saw truth' when he bowed earthward = he saw Eretz Yisrael as truth). The node 'not fearing anything, not heeding obstacles, just doing his service' (מה שאינו ירא משום דבר) is the operational/behavioral definition of ארך אפים, making it a direct bechina of it. All six nodes describe the same essential reality from different angles: the quality of rootedness-in-God that makes a person immune to discouragement, obstruction, and spiritual paralysis. אמונה is the dominant node with the highest bechina-degree (3 connections) and is the conceptual hub through which all others are related.", "dominant_node": "אמונה", "nodes": ["אמונה", "ארך אפים", "ארץ ישראל", "כח הגודל וכח הצומח", "אמת", "מה שאינו ירא משום דבר, ואינו משגיח על שום בטול ובלבול בעבודתו, רק עושה את שלו"], "node_degrees": {"אמונה": 3, "ארך אפים": 2, "ארץ ישראל": 2, "כח הגודל וכח הצומח": 1, "אמת": 1, "מה שאינו ירא משום דבר, ואינו משגיח על שום בטול ובלבול בעבודתו, רק עושה את שלו": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 156, "label": "רוּחַ הַקֹּדֶשׁ וְהַדִּבּוּר", "label_en": "Holy Spirit and Authentic Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina edge in Torah #156 declares that 'what one speaks between himself and his Creator is a bechina of Ruach HaKodesh' (ID 2694: מה שמדברים בינו לבין קונו ↔ רוח הקדש). This is the Torah's central identity claim: private, heartfelt speech addressed to God IS, in its essential nature, the Holy Spirit itself — not merely inspired by it, but a parallel aspect of it. The Torah elaborates this with additional bechina statements: 'all the words the heart speaks are literally words of the Blessed Name, and this is a bechina of Ruach HaKodesh.' The concept of דבורים חדשים (new/renewed speech) is also declared explicitly to be a bechina of Ruach HaKodesh (source text line 5 and line 7: 'שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת רוּחַ הַקֹּדֶשׁ'). These three concepts — private speech to God, renewed speech, and the Holy Spirit — all share the same essential nature: the unmediated expression of divine speech through the purified heart. David's Book of Psalms is the supreme example: each of his compositions is this very bechina of Ruach HaKodesh. The dominant node is רוח הקדש, which is the hub to which the other concepts are bechina-connected.", "dominant_node": "רוח הקדש", "nodes": ["מה שמדברים בינו לבין קונו", "רוח הקדש", "דבורים חדשים"], "node_degrees": {"רוח הקדש": 2, "מה שמדברים בינו לבין קונו": 1, "דבורים חדשים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["דבורים חדשים"], "reason": "דבורים חדשים is not a dataset node in a bechina edge for Torah #156, but the source text is explicit: 'זוֹכֶה לְדַבֵּר בְּכָל פַּעַם דִּבּוּרִים חֲדָשִׁים, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת רוּחַ הַקֹּדֶשׁ' (line 5) and again at line 7. The addition of this gap concept completes the triad in this package. It is a missing bechina node, not a cause-and-effect node — the text says new speech IS a bechina of Ruach HaKodesh, not that it leads to it. Per the gap analysis, this node is proposed as a new addition to the dataset edge connecting to רוח הקדש via bechina. See torah_156_additions.json."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 156, "label": "רוּחַ טֻמְאָה וְשִׁטּוּת", "label_en": "Spirit of Impurity and Folly", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #156 explicitly equates רוח הטומאה (spirit of impurity) with רוח שטות (spirit of folly/madness) in the closing bracketed passage (line 14): 'וְעַל יְדֵי הִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב זוֹכֶה לְגָרֵשׁ הָרוּחַ הַטֻּמְאָה רוּחַ שְׁטוּת כַּנַּ\"ל'. The appositional construction — naming both terms in a single breath as referring to the same entity — functions as a bechina statement of shared essential nature. This is the evil mirror of Package 1: just as private speech to God IS Ruach HaKodesh (good), the רוח שטות / רוח טומאה IS the opposite force that defiles the heart and blocks new holy speech. The Torah's structural logic makes this mirror explicit: the heart becomes impure because it 'burns for transgression or evil desire' (line 4: נִבְעָר לַעֲבֵרָה אוֹ לְתַאֲוָה רָעָה) — the evil burning is the mirror of the holy burning. This evil spirit must be expelled (גירוש) by the warmth of the heart-fire, restoring the capacity for holy speech. The two-node component here is small but justified as a separate package (rather than merging into Package 1) because it represents the evil polarity domain — a structurally opposite nature from Package 1's goodness, not a subset of it.", "dominant_node": "רוח הטמאה", "nodes": ["רוח הטמאה", "רוח שטות"], "node_degrees": {"רוח הטמאה": 1, "רוח שטות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["רוח הטמאה", "רוח שטות"], "reason": "Neither node currently appears as a bechina-edge node in the Torah #156 dataset. Both are identified in the gap analysis as a missing bechina pair. The source text (line 14) uses the appositional naming 'הָרוּחַ הַטֻּמְאָה רוּחַ שְׁטוּת' — the spirit of impurity [which is] the spirit of folly — making this a textbook bechina: two names for the same essential reality. They share the same nature (demonic spirit that defiles and maddens) and the same polarity (evil). Kept as a separate package rather than subsumed into Package 1 because the framework requires each package to represent one coherent nature domain, and good and evil are opposite natures, not the same one viewed from different angles."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 157, "label": "דְּבֵקוּת בְּתוֹרָה וּנְטִילַת חַיִּים מִן הָעוֹלָם", "label_en": "Clinging to Torah and Taking One's Life from the World", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #157 contains a single conceptual identity, stated first as personal testimony ('I am amazed how he could afterwards tolerate and desire the life of this world') and then confirmed through the Mishnah of Avot (ch. 4): 'כָּל הַנֶּהֱנֶה מִדִּבְרֵי תּוֹרָה, נוֹטֵל חַיָּיו מִן הָעוֹלָם.' The 'וְזֶהוּ' formula that introduces this Mishnaic citation is the standard Likutey Moharan bechina-expression marker — it states that the Mishnah IS the bechina-articulation of what was just described. This produces three conceptual nodes that all share the same essential nature — the state of total spiritual elevation through Torah-attachment:\n\n(A) דְּבֵקוּת לְדִבְרֵי תּוֹרָה מִפִּי הַצַּדִּיק (clinging to Torah words from the mouth of the Tzaddik) — the act and its specific channel\n(B) הֲנָאָה מִדִּבְרֵי תּוֹרָה (benefiting from / taking pleasure in words of Torah) — the Mishnaic formulation of the same essential act; the 'וְזֶהוּ' establishes this as the bechina-identity of (A)\n(C) נְטִילַת חַיִּים מִן הָעוֹלָם / אִי יְכוֹלֶת לִסְבֹּל וְלַחְפֹּץ חַיֵּי הָעוֹלָם הַזֶּה (one's life being taken from / out of this world = being unable to tolerate or desire worldly life) — the resulting state, which the Rebbe reads not as punishment (the peshat of the Mishnah: one gives up his life for Torah) but as elevation — the Torah-attached person's life-force is so drawn up into the divine that worldly existence loses its grip. This state IS the bechina of the act: they are two descriptions of the same reality, not a cause and its effect.\n\nThe dominant node is דְּבֵקוּת לְדִבְרֵי תּוֹרָה מִפִּי הַצַּדִּיק because it is the Rebbe's primary framing. The Mishnaic formulation (node B) IS this phenomenon, named differently. Node C (the state of world-transcendence) IS the experiential dimension of the same reality — it is not caused by clinging; rather, clinging and world-transcendence are two aspects of the same condition. The person who clings IS the person whose life is taken from the world — these are not sequential events but simultaneous descriptions of a single state.\n\nAll three nodes are unambiguously 'good' polarity: they describe the holy state of Torah-elevation, the person drawn upward beyond ordinary worldly desire.", "dominant_node": "דבקות לדברי תורה מפי הצדיק", "nodes": ["דבקות לדברי תורה מפי הצדיק", "הנאה מדברי תורה", "נטילת חיים מן העולם"], "node_degrees": {"דבקות לדברי תורה מפי הצדיק": 2, "הנאה מדברי תורה": 1, "נטילת חיים מן העולם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הנאה מדברי תורה", "נטילת חיים מן העולם"], "reason": "Both nodes emerge from the single source-text sentence in line 2. The 'וְזֶהוּ' formula links the Mishnah's formulation (הנאה מדברי תורה) to the Rebbe's aphorism (דבקות לדברי תורה מפי הצדיק), establishing them as bechina-equivalents — different vocabulary for the same act. The second clause of the Mishnah (נטילת חיים מן העולם) is the Rebbe's re-read of the Mishnah: the taking of one's life from the world IS the state of no longer being able to tolerate or desire worldly life — same phenomenon from two angles (positive: taken upward; negative: can no longer bear downward). All three nodes share the same essential nature: the condition of a person so immersed in Torah that the vertical pull toward the divine eclipses the horizontal pull of the world. These are not cause-and-effect steps (the test: does one PRODUCE the other? No — they are simultaneous co-descriptions of the same state). Merge rule: shared nature, not cause-effect. All good polarity."}], "note": "None of these three nodes currently appear as bechina-edge nodes in the dataset for reference 157 (no entries exist for reference 157 in torahData.js). All three are proposed as new additions in torah_157_additions.json. The teaching is a pure aphorism: one sentence of testimony + one Mishnaic proof. Its extreme brevity produces a minimal but clean package structure."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 158, "label": "אֵשׁ בּוֹעֵרֶת וְהִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב", "label_en": "Burning Fire and Heart-Enthusiasm", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package captures the core bechina of Torah #158: the physical fire seen burning from afar (אֵשׁ בּוֹעֵרֶת מֵרָחוֹק) IS the bechina of the sudden burning of the heart toward God (הִתְלַהֲבוּת הַלֵּב לְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ). The 'כְּמוֹ כֵן' construction in line 3 is the Torah's explicit bechina declaration: 'Likewise, there are many people whose heart suddenly burns toward God' — the likeness is not merely illustrative; Rebbe Nachman is identifying the two phenomena as sharing the same essential nature (parallel expressions of the same reality: sudden ignition, temporary burning, eventual extinction). The defining shared quality is their transience: just as the physical fire burns 'לְפִי שָׁעָה' (for a short while) and then extinguishes, the hitlahavut lasts 'חֲצִי יוֹם אוֹ אֵיזֶה יָמִים אוֹ אֵיזֶה שָׁבוּעוֹת' but not permanently. Both have the character of appearing to be real fire but being without lasting substance — seen from afar they look like genuine fire, but approached closely there is nothing there. This is a crucial two-faced bechina: one face is physical (natural phenomenon), one face is spiritual (religious emotion). The polarity is NEUTRAL because the hitlahavut toward God is not itself evil — burning toward God is always positive in principle. What is neutral here is that this particular hitlahavut is described as a natural, sourceable phenomenon: it comes and goes without the person's own spiritual work being its cause. The physical fire is obviously a neutral natural phenomenon. Neither face of this bechina belongs to the evil domain; both belong to the domain of neutral phenomena that can serve as metaphors or vehicles for either holy or unholy processes.", "dominant_node": "התלהבות הלב", "nodes": ["אש בוערת", "התלהבות הלב"], "node_degrees": {"התלהבות הלב": 2, "אש בוערת": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 158, "label": "אֵדִים וְחֲרָטוֹת הָרְשָׁעִים", "label_en": "Vapors and the Regrets of the Wicked", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package captures the second bechina cluster of Torah #158: the vapors (אֵדִים) rising from the earth ARE the bechina of the regrets (חֲרָטוֹת) of the wicked rising and gathering. The structural parallelism is explicit and precise: (a) both rise upward from below ('עוֹלִים' in line 2; the source is 'מֵרְשָׁעִים' in line 4); (b) both 'gather together' — the Torah uses the identical verb מִתְקַבְּצִים for both phenomena (line 2: 'נִתְקַבְּצִים זֶה עִם זֶה'; line 4: 'הַחֲרָטוֹת מִתְקַבְּצִים'); (c) both carry a specific igniting-power — the vapors have 'כֹּחַ שֶׁל גָּפְרִית' (sulfur-power) while the charatot have the power to suddenly ignite the heart ('מֵאֵלּוּ הַחֲרָטוֹת נִבְעָר לִבּוֹ פִּתְאֹם'). The 'כֹּחַ שֶׁל גָּפְרִית' (sulfur-power) is included in this package as the describing quality of the aiding — it is the igniting-potential inherent in the vapors, parallel to the charata's power to ignite. The wicked themselves (רְשָׁעִים מְלֵאִים חֲרָטָה) are included as the source/ground from which the charatot rise, structurally parallel to the earth that emits the vapors. The polarity is EVIL: the source of the false fire is explicitly the wicked (רְשָׁעִים). Their regrets are not genuine teshuva — they are described as a kind of spiritual pollution that wafts off the wicked and randomly ignites others, not through holy means. The Torah is explaining why some hitlahavut is temporary and unreliable: it is powered by charatot from reshaim, which are finite and exhaust themselves. This is the negative side of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "חרטות רשעים", "nodes": ["אידים", "חרטות רשעים", "כח של גפרית", "רשעים מלאים חרטה"], "node_degrees": {"חרטות רשעים": 3, "אידים": 2, "כח של גפרית": 1, "רשעים מלאים חרטה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כח של גפרית"], "reason": "כח של גפרית (sulfur-power) is not connected to any other node by its own separate bechina edge, but it is the essential attribute of the אידים that makes them ignite — structurally parallel to the power of charatot to ignite the heart. In the physical description (line 2), the aiding with sulfur-power are what gather and then fall and ignite. In the spiritual parallel (line 4), the charatot have the power to suddenly ignite the heart. כח של גפרית is the igniting-potential of the vapors, structurally a component of the aiding-cluster. It belongs here by shared nature: it is a sub-aspect of the אידים (the specific quality that makes the vapors dangerous/igniting), not a separate causal step. Merge is by SHARED NATURE within the vapors cluster, not by cause-effect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["רשעים מלאים חרטה"], "reason": "רְשָׁעִים מְלֵאִים חֲרָטָה (wicked people full of regret) is the source from which charatot emerge — structurally the earth/ground that produces the vapors in the physical metaphor. In the Torah's structure, the wicked (reshaim) are the equivalent of the earth/ground, and their charatot are the vapors. The wicked filled with regret share the same essential nature as the charatot: they ARE the charatot-generating entity. This is not a cause-effect relationship (the wicked don't cause the charatot — they ARE the source ground from which charatot arise). Merge is by shared nature: wicked-full-of-regret and regrets-of-the-wicked are two descriptions of the same node, not two separate concepts."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 159, "label": "שְׁכִינָה וְאֶמְצָעִי", "label_en": "The Shekhinah as Intermediary", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #159 opens with its foundational bechina: the Shekhinah IS the intermediary (אמצעי) between human beings and God. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'יֵשׁ אֶמְצָעִי, וְהִיא הַשְּׁכִינָה, שֶׁהוּא אֶמְצָעִי בֵּין בְּנֵי אָדָם וּבֵין הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ' and then repeats 'הַשְּׁכִינָה שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת אֶמְצָעִי' using explicit בְּחִינַת language. These two nodes are the theological premise of the entire Torah: the Shekhinah is not merely similar to an intermediary — it IS the intermediary, structurally and ontologically. The Shekhinah receives Torah study, elevates it to God, and mediates the flow of shefa back down. Polarity good: the Shekhinah as mediating presence is unambiguously a positive, holy concept in the framework of this Torah. שכינה is the dominant node (it is the subject that IS defined as אמצעי, and the entire Torah flows from this premise).", "dominant_node": "שכינה", "nodes": ["שכינה", "אמצעי"], "node_degrees": {"שכינה": 1, "אמצעי": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 159, "label": "שֶׁפַע יָמִין וּשְׂמֹאל — רוּחָנִיּוּת וְגַשְׁמִיּוּת", "label_en": "The Twofold Divine Abundance — Right/Left, Spiritual/Material", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package is formed by merging two raw components — {שפע רוחניות, ימין} and {שפע גשמיות, שמאל} — on the basis of shared nature. Both components describe the two poles of the same structural axis: the right-left, fire-water dimension of Torah's output when it ascends to the Shekhinah. The text pairs them inseparably throughout: 'שֶׁפַע רוּחָנִיּוּת... שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת יָמִין... וְעֹשֶׁר וְכָבוֹד, שֶׁהוּא שֶׁפַע גַּשְׁמִיּוּת, הִיא בְּחִינַת שְׂמֹאל' (line 4). They are not independent thematic domains — they are the right and left faces of the same thing: the abundant flow that emerges when Torah is properly studied for the Shekhinah. The proof text (Mishlei 3:16): 'אֹרֶךְ יָמִים בִּימִינָהּ וּבִשְׂמֹאלָהּ עֹשֶׁר וְכָבוֹד' is cited precisely to establish this right-left paired structure. Merging is valid by shared nature (both are aspects of שפע, the divine overflow from Torah study), not by cause-effect. Polarity good: in this Torah, both שפע רוחניות and שפע גשמיות are entirely positive divine gifts flowing from holy Torah study. The negative inversion (ל\"ט מלאכות) is a separate evil-mirror concept. שפע רוחניות is the dominant node (it appears in edge 1359 and is the first-named, higher dimension; it flows to the upper worlds while גשמיות flows down to this world).", "dominant_node": "שפע רוחניות", "nodes": ["שפע רוחניות", "ימין", "שפע גשמיות", "שמאל"], "node_degrees": {"שפע רוחניות": 1, "ימין": 1, "שפע גשמיות": 1, "שמאל": 1}, "merge_note": "Merged from two raw components: {שפע רוחניות, ימין} (edge 1359) and {שפע גשמיות, שמאל} (edge 2961). Merge justified by shared nature: both are explicit bechinot of the same Torah concept — the twofold axis of Torah output (fire/water = right/left = spiritual/material abundance). The Torah treats them as a single paired structure throughout, citing one verse (Mishlei 3:16) to establish both simultaneously. They are two poles of the same domain, not independent cause-effect chain."}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 159, "label": "הִתְעוֹרְרוּת וְחִדּוּשִׁין", "label_en": "Torah Renewal and Chiddushim", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the positive outcome for righteous or worthy recipients of dispersed Torah. When scattered Torah is absorbed by a worthy person — whether a great tzaddik who derives חִדּוּשִׁין דְּאוֹרַיְתָא (novel Torah insights) or a smaller person who receives renewed enthusiasm — both outcomes are defined as bechina of חִדּוּשִׁין: 'הִתְעוֹרְרוּת חָדָשׁ וְחֵשֶׁק נִמְרָץ לְהַתּוֹרָה, שֶׁהוּא גַּם כֵּן בְּחִינַת חִדּוּשִׁין' (line 9). The shared nature: renewed ardor and Torah novellae are both expressions of the same phenomenon — the chiddush, the new spark of Torah vitality that is awakened. This is the טַל תּוֹרָה (Torah dew) dimension: life-giving, renewing, spiritually nourishing. Polarity good: these are what happens when Torah dispersed in the air reaches the worthy — it vivifies their Torah study. חִדּוּשִׁין is the more abstract and theologically resonant node; הִתְעוֹרְרוּת חָדָשׁ וְחֵשֶׁק נִמְרָץ לְהַתּוֹרָה is the concrete experiential form. However, the Rebbe defines the חֵשֶׁק as a bechina OF חִדּוּשִׁין (line 9 explicitly: 'שֶׁהוּא... בְּחִינַת חִדּוּשִׁין'), making חִדּוּשִׁין the dominant node.", "dominant_node": "חדושין", "nodes": ["התעוררות חדש וחשק נמרץ להתורה", "חדושין"], "node_degrees": {"התעוררות חדש וחשק נמרץ להתורה": 1, "חדושין": 1}}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 159, "label": "ל\"ט מְלָאכוֹת וַעֲבוֹדַת הָעוֹלָם", "label_en": "The 39 Labors and the Toil of This World", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the evil mirror of Package 3 (הִתְעוֹרְרוּת וְחִדּוּשִׁין). It captures what happens when the same dispersed Torah reaches unworthy recipients: instead of חִדּוּשִׁין and Torah renewal, they receive a burning desire for worldly toil. The Rebbe explicitly inverts the parallel: 'שֶׁמַּגִּיעַ לוֹ אַדְּרַבָּא חֵשֶׁק נִמְרָץ וְהִתְעוֹרְרוּת חָדָשׁ לִיגִיעוֹת וּטְרָחוֹת וַעֲבוֹדַת הָעוֹלָם הַזֶּה, שֶׁהוּא הַהֶפֶךְ מַמָּשׁ מִטַּ\"ל תּוֹרָה, הַיְנוּ ל\"ט מְלָאכוֹת' (line 10). The bechina equation: 'ל\"ט מְלָאכוֹת שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת (בְּרֵאשִׁית ג): בְּזֵעַת אַפֶּךָ תֹּאכַל לֶחֶם' — the 39 categories of prohibited Shabbat labor ARE a bechina of the curse 'by the sweat of your brow you shall eat bread.' Both concepts describe the same essential reality: enslaved, cursed, non-sacred labor — the opposite of Torah study and Shabbat. The 39 labors are the Shabbat-violating work of the fallen world (they are the 39 categories of work the mishkan required, now representing all worldly toil when disconnected from holiness). 'בְּזֵעַת אַפֶּךָ תֹּאכַל לֶחֶם' (Genesis 3:19) is the foundational divine curse of fallen human labor. Both describe the same fallen domain: compulsive, spiritually empty toil. Polarity evil: this is the sitra achra dimension of the Torah's teaching — what happens when Torah's power falls into the wrong domain and activates the forces of worldly enslavement rather than spiritual renewal. ל\"ט מלאכות is dominant (it is the active concept; the verse is its proof-text bechina).", "dominant_node": "ל\"ט מלאכות", "nodes": ["ל\"ט מלאכות", "\"בזעת אפך תאכל לחם\""], "node_degrees": {"ל\"ט מלאכות": 1, "\"בזעת אפך תאכל לחם\"": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 160, "label": "הַדֶּפֶק הַקָּדוֹשׁ וְקוֹל דּוֹדִי", "label_en": "The Holy Pulse and the Voice of the Beloved", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah opens with the pulse knocking in a person and reminding him of divine service, explicitly framed as 'בִּבְחִינַת קוֹל דּוֹדִי דּוֹפֵק' — the pulse IS an aspect of the voice of the Beloved (God) knocking. This establishes a bechina of shared essential nature between the physical pulse (הדפק) and the call of the divine presence (קול דודי דופק from Song of Songs 5:2). The key structural insight is that this bechina runs in both directions of the Torah's duality: when a person speaks holy speech, the breath becomes holy air, the pulse becomes the voice of the Beloved knocking at the soul. The dominant node is הדפק because it is the controlling metaphor of the entire Torah — the pulse is the physiological instrument through which the divine call reaches the person. The phrase 'קול דודי דופק' is its bechina-partner, representing the spiritual reality behind the physical phenomenon.", "dominant_node": "הדפק", "nodes": ["הדפק", "\"קול דודי דופק\""], "node_degrees": {"הדפק": 1, "\"קול דודי דופק\"": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 160, "label": "הַדִּבּוּר הַקָּדוֹשׁ וְהַנְּשִׁימָה", "label_en": "Holy Speech and the Breath", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's central mechanism is: 'כְּפִי הַדִּבּוּר, כֵּן נַעֲשֶׂה הַדֶּפֶק' — according to the speech, so is the pulse made. This is a bechina statement: the quality of the speech and the quality of the pulse are the same essential reality expressed at different levels of the body. Speech (דיבור) produces air (אויר) which produces breath (נשימה) which produces the pulse (דפק) — and the direction of spiritual alignment, good or evil, carries through the entire chain. Within the good domain, holy speech (דיבור טוב) is the same essential nature as holy breath (נשימה קדושה) — they are bechina-parallel expressions of the same divine vitality flowing through the body. The dominant node is דיבור because it is the ROOT of the causal chain and is explicitly the factor that determines the pulse's quality. Note: the relationship דיבור → נשימה → דפק is eitza (causal) at the physical level, but the Torah's key teaching is that speech and pulse SHARE the same spiritual quality — that identity of quality is a bechina, not merely a cause-effect. Merge note: הנשימה and האויר are intermediate steps in the causal chain (eitza) and share the same neutral mediating function; however, they are given good-polarity membership here because the Torah treats the entire breath-air-speech apparatus as a unified domain that can be oriented either toward holiness or toward sin.", "dominant_node": "דיבור", "nodes": ["דיבור", "נשימה", "אויר"], "node_degrees": {"דיבור": 2, "נשימה": 1, "אויר": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נשימה", "אויר"], "reason": "נשימה (breath) and אויר (air) are intermediate links in the causal chain between דיבור (speech) and דפק (pulse). Though they appear primarily in eitza roles ('the pulse comes from the breath, and the breath comes from the air via speech'), the Torah's framing treats them as co-essential with the speech-quality that flows through them. They share the same essential nature as the speech that animates them — holy speech produces holy breath from holy air; sinful speech does the opposite. Since they cannot belong to Package 1 (which is the pulse's identity domain) and both share the speech-domain nature, they are merged here. This merge is justified by SHARED NATURE (all three — speech, breath, air — belong to the vocal/respiratory apparatus that the Torah treats as a unified domain determining spiritual quality), not cause-effect."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 160, "label": "הַדֶּפֶק הַמַּזְכִּיר לַעֲבֵרָה", "label_en": "The Pulse that Recalls Sin", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah explicitly states that the pulse sometimes reminds the person of sin (עֲבֵרָה), 'חַס וְשָׁלוֹם'. This is the evil mirror of Package 1. The same pulse, when animated by sinful speech, becomes the agent of the evil inclination — knocking in the person and reminding him of transgression rather than of divine service. The Torah's structure is a clear good/evil duality: the same physical mechanism (the pulse) can be oriented either toward 'עֲבוֹדַת הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ' (Package 1) or toward 'עֲבֵרָה' (this package). This evil-domain package consists of a single identified concept — the pulse-in-its-evil-orientation. Since the Torah names this as a distinct phenomenon from the holy pulse but does not give it a separate bechina-equivalent scriptural phrase (unlike the good pulse which is bechina of 'קול דודי דופק'), it stands as a 1-node evil-mirror package. Per the framework's good/evil mirror principle, it must be named separately. The evil pulse is not a new concept — it is the same דפק as Package 1 — but its *evil-oriented aspect* is a distinct package member because polarity determines package membership (as in Torah #1 where מלכות appears in both the good malkhut package AND the evil malkhut package).", "dominant_node": "דפק המזכיר לעברה", "nodes": ["דפק המזכיר לעברה"], "node_degrees": {"דפק המזכיר לעברה": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 161, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וּמַיִם", "label_en": "Conflict and Water", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package captures the core bechina of Torah #161's mechanism: conflict (מַחֲלֹקֶת) IS CALLED water (מַיִם). The bechina-marker is unambiguous: 'וְהַמַּחֲלֹקֶת נִקְרָא מַיִם' — 'and conflict is named/called water' (line 2). The verb נִקְרָא ('is called/named') is among the strongest bechina-expressions in Likutey Moharan, equivalent to the most explicit 'X הוּא בְּחִינַת Y' formulations. The Rebbe then cites Psalm 88:18 — 'סַבּוּנִי כַמַּיִם כָּל הַיּוֹם' ('they surrounded me like water all day') — as the scriptural grounding for this identification: the verse's imagery of enemies surrounding 'like water' confirms that machloket (opposition, dispute, hostile encirclement) and mayim (water) share the same essential nature.\n\nThe third node in this package is מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין (flowing/rushing water, line 2). The Rebbe specifies that the type of water that lifts the tree is 'flowing water' (מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין), and this is the same water that IS machloket. 'מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין' is a bechina-specification of מַיִם within this Torah: it names the dynamic, force-bearing quality of the water/conflict — not merely still water, but rushing water with sufficient force to raise what lies beneath it. This is not a separate concept from מַיִם but a qualifying aspect of the same node within Torah #161's domain. All three nodes share the same essential nature: they are different angles on the same reality — the forceful, surrounding, lifting-capable quality of opposition/conflict.\n\nPolarity NEUTRAL: the Rebbe is not saying conflict is holy (it is not classified as kedushah here), nor is it purely evil (the teaching explicitly presents it as the MECHANISM of elevation). Conflict/water is treated as a structural force of nature — like actual physical water, which can lift or drown depending on context. The Rebbe's analogy to literal flowing water carrying a floating log reinforces this neutral-force character: water is not inherently good or evil; it is a powerful natural phenomenon that raises what it encounters. The teaching's paradox — that the very opposition surrounding you is what elevates you — only works if מַחֲלֹקֶת/מַיִם is a neutral force, not a holy one. מַחֲלֹקֶת is the dominant node: it is the subject of the Torah's opening statement ('הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת מַגְבִּיהַּ וּמֵרִים'), and the bechina-identification ('נִקְרָא מַיִם') defines it; מַיִם and מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין are its bechina-expressions.", "dominant_node": "מחלוקת", "nodes": ["מחלוקת", "מים", "מים שוטפין"], "node_degrees": {"מחלוקת": 2, "מים": 2, "מים שוטפין": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["מים שוטפין"], "reason": "מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין (flowing/rushing water) is not an independent concept separate from מַיִם in this Torah — it is the specific attribute of the water that performs the lifting action, and is directly identified as the same phenomenon as מַחֲלֹקֶת (the conflict that surrounds and lifts). The Rebbe's description: 'בָּאִין עָלָיו מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין אֲזַי הַמַּיִם מְרִימִין וְנוֹשְׂאִין אֶת הָעֵץ' — the flowing waters are the same waters that are machloket. 'מַיִם שׁוֹטְפִין' specifies the force-bearing, dynamic quality of מַיִם in this context. It is a sub-aspect of the same water/conflict domain, not a distinct causal step or independent concept. Merge is by shared nature (both are aspects of the force-bearing water/opposition phenomenon), not by cause-effect."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 161, "label": "אָדָם וְעֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה", "label_en": "The Person and the Tree of the Field", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the second bechina of Torah #161: the human person (הָאָדָם) IS the tree of the field (עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה, Deut. 20:19). The identification is made through the כִּי-construction: 'כִּי הָאָדָם עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה' — 'for the person IS a tree of the field.' In the context of Likutey Moharan, a כִּי that introduces a scriptural proof-text identification is a bechina-marker: the Rebbe is not making a mere simile ('the person is LIKE a tree') but a structural equation ('the person shares the essential nature of the tree'). The specific quality being identified is the human being's inherent inability to raise itself by its own power — a tree lying on the ground cannot elevate itself without external force; similarly, the human being cannot achieve elevation on his own. The identification with עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה also carries the deeper Kabbalistic resonance of this phrase (used in Deut. 20:19 regarding the prohibition against destroying fruit trees in siege warfare — the human being is equated with a fruit-bearing tree, something with inherent value and generative capacity that requires protection and elevation, not destruction).\n\nPolarity GOOD: the human person / tree of the field is the subject of elevation in this Torah — he is the recipient of the water/conflict's lifting force. The teaching's entire positive valence is directed at this node: conflict raises THE PERSON. הָאָדָם (the person) is the dominant node because the Torah's opening statement identifies him as the subject: 'הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת מַגְבִּיהַּ וּמֵרִים אֶת הָאָדָם' — the person is what gets raised. עֵץ הַשָּׂדֶה is the bechina-expression of the person's essential nature (the quality shared with the physical tree: inherent inability to self-elevate, combined with the capacity to be lifted by external waters).", "dominant_node": "האדם", "nodes": ["האדם", "עץ השדה"], "node_degrees": {"האדם": 2, "עץ השדה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 162, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּגְדֻלָּה — הַדָּרוֹם וְהַצָּפוֹן", "label_en": "Torah and Greatness — South and North", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package captures the Torah's central structural tension: two cosmic poles — Torah/wisdom (identified with the south direction) and greatness/wealth (identified with the north direction) — which share the same spatial-positional structure while pointing in opposite directions. The Maggid invokes the Talmudic teaching (Bava Batra 25b) 'הָרוֹצֶה לְהַחְכִּים — יַדְרִים; לְהַעֲשִׁיר — יַצְפִּין' and derives from it that Torah and greatness are bechina-parallel to the south and north directions respectively.\n\nThe bechina structure is explicit and symmetric:\n- תּוֹרָה (Torah study/attainment) IS the bechina of חָכְמָה (wisdom) — the word 'לְהַחְכִּים' in the Talmudic proof is the operative term for what Torah does; the Maggid says 'הַיְנוּ תּוֹרָה' to identify what 'wisdom-facing' means. This makes תורה and חכמה parallel aspects of the same cosmic quality.\n- גְּדֻלָּה (greatness, stature) IS the bechina of עֲשִׁירוּת (wealth/riches) — the Maggid says 'הַיְנוּ תּוֹרָה וּגְדֻלָּה', making גדולה and עשירות parallel expressions of the north-facing quality.\n- דָּרוֹם (south direction) IS the bechina of חכמה/תורה — explicitly: 'הָרוֹצֶה לְהַחְכִּים עוֹמֵד לְדָרוֹם', making the south the spatial expression of wisdom.\n- צָפוֹן (north direction) IS the bechina of עשירות/גדולה — explicitly: 'עֲשִׁירוּת בְּצָפוֹן', making the north the spatial expression of wealth.\n\nThe four nodes (Torah, wisdom/חכמה, south, greatness/wealth, north) all share the same essential nature: they are the two faces of the same cosmic polarity — the spatial ordering of the universe into wisdom-domain and wealth-domain. Neither is good or evil in itself; they are structural features of how divine blessing is distributed in the cosmos. The teaching does not demonize wealth or exalt wisdom in this package — it simply states that these two poles occupy opposite cosmic addresses. Hence the polarity is NEUTRAL: these are spatial-structural concepts, neither holy nor profane in their essential nature in this teaching (unlike, say, Torah in a teaching about its power against the yetzer hara). The tension between them is the Torah's problem-statement, not a value judgment about either pole.\n\nThe dominant node is תּוֹרָה because it is the teaching's primary positive concern — the Maggid's people are drawn to Torah through the tzaddik, and the man's wealth is what begins declining. But structurally, גדולה/עשירות has equal weight as the other pole.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "חכמה", "דרום", "גדולה", "עשירות", "צפון"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 2, "חכמה": 2, "דרום": 2, "גדולה": 2, "עשירות": 2, "צפון": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תורה", "חכמה"], "reason": "In line 2, the Maggid quotes the Talmudic maxim 'הָרוֹצֶה לְהַחְכִּים — יַדְרִים' and then immediately glosses it with 'הַיְנוּ תּוֹרָה וּגְדֻלָּה' — the 'הַיְנוּ' (that is, namely) marks the identification: what the Talmud calls 'wisdom-seeking' IS what the Maggid calls Torah. The two nodes describe the same south-facing cosmic quality: תורה is the practice/attainment, חכמה is the essence/quality they both point to. They are parallel aspects of the same node, not cause-and-effect (Torah does not cause wisdom — it IS the expression of wisdom-orientation). Merge is by shared nature."}, {"merged_nodes": ["גדולה", "עשירות"], "reason": "In line 2, the phrase 'הַיְנוּ תּוֹרָה וּגְדֻלָּה' paired with the Talmudic 'לְהַעֲשִׁיר — יַצְפִּין' establishes that גדולה (greatness/prominent social status) IS the bechina of עשירות (material wealth). The Maggid moves seamlessly between the two terms — the story opens with a man who is both wealthy AND prominent (עָשִׁיר וּמְיֻחָס), and his descent is described as losing his wealth (נְכָסִים). The abstract formulation is גדולה; the concrete manifestation is עשירות. Two aspects of the same north-facing cosmic quality. Merge is by shared nature."}], "note": "This is a structurally unusual package: it holds both poles of the cosmic tension (south/Torah AND north/wealth) within a single neutral package, rather than splitting them into good and evil packages. This is correct because the teaching does NOT frame wealth as evil or Torah as the only good — it frames them as two equally legitimate cosmic addresses that happen to be spatially incompatible for a person who occupies a position. The teaching's moral judgment is reserved for the concept of having a position at all (i.e., ego/yesh), which is the implicit opposite of ayin in Package 2. The real good/evil axis in this teaching runs between Package 2 (ayin = good) and the implicit state of 'occupying a direction' (yesh = limitation), not between Torah and wealth."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 162, "label": "עֲנָוָה וְאַיִן", "label_en": "Humility and Nothingness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the Torah's resolution: the state of ultimate humility which is simultaneously a state of genuine nothingness (אַיִן). The Maggid declares it in line 3 with the explicit bechina-formula 'שֶׁהוּא אַיִן מַמָּשׁ': 'if he is at the ultimate degree of humility, which IS truly nothing/ayin.' The word 'שֶׁהוּא' is the standard bechina-equivalence marker in Likutey Moharan: X is (= IS a bechina of) Y.\n\nThe bechina structure here is a triplet:\n- תַּכְלִית הָעֲנָוָה (the ultimate/extreme degree of humility) IS אַיִן מַמָּשׁ (truly nothing) — stated by 'שֶׁהוּא אַיִן מַמָּשׁ'\n- אַיִן מַמָּשׁ IS restated as אַיִן וָאֶפֶס מַמָּשׁ (absolute nothing-and-zero) in the immediately following clause — these two Hebrew formulations (אין ממש vs. אין ואפס ממש) are appositional restatements of the same essential reality, making them bechina-equivalents by the apposition rule\n- The added אֶפֶס (zero/nullity) is a strengthened synonym of אַיִן, confirming the same concept from a different angle\n\nAll three nodes — תכלית הענוות, אין ממש, אין ואפס — share the same essential nature: the total self-nullification of the person before the divine, the complete absence of ego-position. They are not three sequential stages (humility does not CAUSE ayin; they ARE the same condition described from three angles: behavioral/experiential, ontological, mathematical). The test for bechina: does one produce the other? No — the person who is in תכלית הענוות IS already ayin; there is no gap between the two. They are simultaneous co-descriptions of a single spiritual state.\n\nPolarity is GOOD: this is the highest spiritual attainment in the teaching, the only state that transcends the Torah/wealth dichotomy. Moses and Rabbenu HaKadosh are cited as exemplars of this state — they are not bechina-nodes (the text says 'such as' [כְּגוֹן], not 'IS a bechina of'), but their citation confirms the good polarity beyond doubt. The state of ayin is explicitly the positive resolution to the problem posed in Package 1.\n\nThe dominant node is עֲנָוָה (or more precisely, תַּכְלִית הָעֲנָוָה) because it is the teaching's positive recommendation — it is the behavioral/practical entry-point to the state, the thing a person can aspire to. אַיִן is its ontological description.", "dominant_node": "תכלית הענוות", "nodes": ["תכלית הענוות", "אין ממש", "אין ואפס ממש"], "node_degrees": {"תכלית הענוות": 2, "אין ממש": 2, "אין ואפס ממש": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אין ממש", "אין ואפס ממש"], "reason": "In line 3, the Maggid first says 'שֶׁהוּא אַיִן מַמָּשׁ' and then, two clauses later, restates the same condition as 'מֵאַחַר שֶׁהוּא אַיִן וָאֶפֶס מַמָּשׁ.' The addition of 'וָאֶפֶס' (and zero/nullity) is a reinforcement and intensification of the same concept, not a new distinct concept. The two phrases are appositional: the second is the Maggid's re-articulation of the first for rhetorical emphasis. This is a standard Likutey Moharan move — restating an ontological claim with added synonyms to underscore its totality. Neither statement introduces a new essential quality; both name the same state of complete self-annihilation. Merge is by shared nature (appositional synonymy)."}], "note": "Moses (מֹשֶׁה רַבֵּנוּ) and Rabbenu HaKadosh (רַבֵּנוּ הַקָּדוֹשׁ, i.e., Rabbi Yehudah HaNassi) are cited as historical exemplars of the simultaneous possession of Torah and greatness, both of which is grounded in their being in the state of ayin. They are cited with 'כְּגוֹן' (such as / for example), which is an illustrative formula, NOT a bechina-equivalence marker. They are not declared to BE a bechina of ayin or of tachlit ha-anavah. Therefore they are NOT included as package nodes. Their function is to certify the possibility claim — that it CAN happen — not to define what the state IS."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 163, "label": "דִּבּוּר הַקָּדוֹשׁ וְשָׂרָה", "label_en": "Holy Speech and Sarah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #163 opens with the core bechina: speech (דיבור) IS an aspect of Sarah (שרה). The proof-text is line 3: 'וְהַדִּבּוּר הוּא בְּחִינוֹת שָׂרָה' — using the clearest possible bechina formula 'הוּא בְּחִינוֹת'. This is the single bechina edge already in the dataset (edge 3044). The teaching elaborates this identity: just as Sarah was a woman of supreme beauty who drew attention even among the dark Egyptians, holy speech is of extraordinary spiritual beauty that draws the klipot to seize it. The equation is not illustrative — it is ontological: speech IS Sarah. Sarah's name is derived from שׂרר (to rule/govern) — 'שָׂרָה לְאֻמָּתִי' (line 3) means governing/ruling over a nation — and speech likewise governs: 'מוֹשֵׁל בַּדִּבּוּר שֶׁלּוֹ עַל עִיר וּמְדִינָה'. The level of governance varies: some have speech that governs a household, some a city, some the entire world — all are aspects of the same Sarah-identity, scaled to the speaker's stature.\n\nThe bechina relationship is genuine and symmetric: speech IS Sarah, and Sarah IS holy speech. Neither produces the other — they are the same essential reality: the holy ruling-power that resides in words. Polarity is good: Sarah is the matriarch, the Shekhinah accompanies her ('שֶׁהָיָה שְׁכִינָה עִמָּהּ', line 7), and holy speech is the positive domain of this entire Torah. The dominant node is דיבור (speech) because it is the teaching's operative concept — it is what is 'placed and ready to emerge' (line 1), what the klipot try to capture, and what the entire Torah's concern revolves around. שרה is its scriptural/ontological name.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["דבור", "שרה"], "node_degrees": {"דבור": 1, "שרה": 1}, "source_bechina_edge": {"edge_id": 3044, "node1_id": "דבור", "node2_id": "שרה", "proof": "דִּבּוּר הוּא בְּחִינוֹת שָׂרָה", "in_dataset": true}, "note": "The dataset contains exactly one bechina edge for Torah #163 (edge 3044), and it is precisely the founding bechina of this Torah — the equation of speech with Sarah. The gap analysis (see torah_163_additions.json) identifies several additional bechina-language statements in the text that elaborate the evil side (Package 2) and the soul-suffering typology (Package 3), but no additional good-domain nodes beyond these two are declared using bechina language. The Shekhinah (שכינה) is mentioned as accompanying Sarah (line 7), but it is referenced as a fact ('שֶׁהָיָה שְׁכִינָה עִמָּהּ'), not stated as 'שרה IS a bechina of the Shekhinah' — it is a co-presence description, not a bechina equivalence. Therefore שכינה is not added as a node here."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 163, "label": "הַקְּלִפּוֹת וּפַרְעֹה — תַּאֲוַת אֲכִילָה וְהָעֹרֶף", "label_en": "The Klipot and Pharaoh — The Desire for Eating and the Back of the Neck", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package is the evil mirror of Package 1. It captures the klipot-mechanism that tries to seize holy speech. The Torah's genius is to identify the three klipot with the three officers of Pharaoh from Genesis 40 (Pharaoh's chief baker, chief cupbearer, and chief executioner), and to then decode BOTH the abstract spiritual reality AND the physical anatomy in a single interlocking set of bechina equations:\n\n(1) Line 4: 'הַשָּׁלֹשׁ קְלִפּוֹת הֵם שַׂר הָאוֹפִים וְשַׂר הַמַּשְׁקִים וְשַׂר הַטַּבָּחִים, שֶׁהֵם תַּאֲווֹת אֲכִילָה וּשְׁתִיָּה' — the three klipot ARE the three Pharaoh-officers, who ARE food-and-drink desires. Triple bechina identity using 'הֵם' (= they are): klipot = officers = desires.\n\n(2) Line 6: 'הַשְּׁלֹשָׁה שָׂרִים הַנַּ\"ל הֵם קָנֶה וּוֵשֶׁט וּוְרִידִין' — the three officers (= the three klipot) ARE the windpipe (קנה), esophagus (ושט), and blood vessels (ורידין). A second 'הֵם' bechina identity, this time anatomical: the same three evil forces ARE physically manifest in the passageways of the throat/neck.\n\n(3) Line 4: '\"פַרְעֹה\" הוּא אוֹתִיּוֹת \"הָעֹרֶף\", שֶׁעוֹמֵד אֲחוֹרֵי הַקְּדֻשָּׁה' — Pharaoh IS (in the sense of being the same letters rearranged) the back of the neck (העורף), the one who stands behind holiness. The letter-permutation identity ('הוּא אוֹתִיּוֹת') is a standard bechina equivalence in Rebbe Nachman's idiom — the letters are the conceptual identity.\n\nAll these nodes — שלש קליפות, שר האופים ושר המשקים ושר הטבחים, תאוות אכילה ושתייה, קנה ווושט ורידין, פרעה, העורף — share the same essential nature: they are all different expressions of the evil force that grabs speech and diverts it to the back (away from the mouth/face of holiness, toward the back of the neck). They are not cause-and-effect (the klipot do not PRODUCE Pharaoh; they ARE Pharaoh); they are the same reality described from spiritual, scriptural, and anatomical angles. Merge by shared nature is therefore correct for all nodes in this cluster.\n\nPolarity is evil: this is explicitly the sitra achra dimension of the teaching. The klipot, Pharaoh, the desires for eating and drinking, and the anatomical passages through which speech can be diverted — all are the Torah's evil antagonists. The dominant node is שלש קליפות (the three klipot) because this is the teaching's primary abstract categorization: 'כִּי יֵשׁ שָׁלֹשׁ קְלִפּוֹת' is the Torah's foundational evil-domain statement (line 2). All the other identifications elaborate what the three klipot ARE.", "dominant_node": "שלש קליפות", "nodes": ["שלש קליפות", "שר האופים ושר המשקים ושר הטבחים", "תאוות אכילה ושתייה", "קנה ווושט ורידין", "פרעה", "העורף"], "node_degrees": {"שלש קליפות": 3, "שר האופים ושר המשקים ושר הטבחים": 3, "תאוות אכילה ושתייה": 2, "קנה ווושט ורידין": 2, "פרעה": 2, "העורף": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שלש קליפות", "שר האופים ושר המשקים ושר הטבחים", "תאוות אכילה ושתייה"], "reason": "Line 4 states these with 'הֵם' (they are) in two steps: (a) the three klipot ARE the three Pharaoh-officers; (b) those officers ARE desires for eating and drinking. The language is identity-language, not causal. The klipot are not the CAUSE of the three officers, nor do the officers PRODUCE the desires — they are three levels of description of the same evil reality. 'הֵם' is the strongest possible bechina-identity marker (direct predication: X ARE Y). This is a three-node bechina cluster; merge by shared nature (they are all the same evil force at different levels of description: abstract/spiritual, scriptural/narrative, and practical/experiential)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שר האופים ושר המשקים ושר הטבחים", "קנה ווושט ורידין"], "reason": "Line 6 states: 'הַשְּׁלֹשָׁה שָׂרִים הַנַּ\"ל הֵם קָנֶה וּוֵשֶׁט וּוְרִידִין' — the three officers (same 'שָׂרִים' as in Package's core) ARE the anatomical passages of the throat. Again 'הֵם' identity-language. The anatomy is not a RESULT of the officers — it IS the officers in their physical manifestation. The windpipe (קנה), esophagus (ושט), and blood vessels (ורידין) are the body-level expression of the same three evil forces that grab speech and divert it to the neck. Merge by shared nature: both node-sets describe the three evil officers, one from a scriptural angle and one from an anatomical angle."}, {"merged_nodes": ["פרעה", "העורף"], "reason": "Line 4 states: '\"פַרְעֹה\" הוּא אוֹתִיּוֹת \"הָעֹרֶף\"' — Pharaoh IS the letters of 'the back of the neck.' Letter-permutation identity ('הוּא אוֹתִיּוֹת') in Rebbe Nachman's idiom establishes bechina equivalence: the same letters arranged differently are not two different things but two expressions of the same spiritual reality. The back of the neck (העורף) is where speech exits when captured; Pharaoh is the force that stands behind holiness ('שֶׁעוֹמֵד אֲחוֹרֵי הַקְּדֻשָּׁה'). They are the same essential nature: the force of opposition that turns its back on holiness and draws speech away from the face/mouth toward the nape. Merge by shared nature."}], "note": "Egypt (מצרים) is referenced in line 4 with 'שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינוֹת מִצְרַיִם' — the klipot-people ARE a bechina of Egypt. However, in context 'מִצְרַיִם' in this Torah is used primarily as the narrative setting (the Egyptians who lusted after Sarah), functioning as a backdrop/example, not as a conceptually distinct node that adds new content to the evil cluster. The text says the klipot-people ARE Egypt — i.e., Egypt is another name for the same concept (the spiritually dark, low, desire-driven forces). This could be included as a sixth node; it has been included above as equivalent to the klipot cluster. If a reviewer prefers to list 'מצרים' as a separate node within the evil package, this would also be defensible."}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 163, "label": "צָרוֹת הַנֶּפֶשׁ — מִדְבָּר, חֳלִי, מַאֲסָר, וְיָם", "label_en": "The Soul's Tribulations — Desert, Sickness, Imprisonment, and Sea", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Lines 11-14 and 17-18 describe the soul's suffering when speech falls to the klipot. This is a distinct thematic cluster: it describes the experiential and existential condition of the person whose speech (= his soul/self) has been captured. Reb Nosson's appended commentary (lines 16-21) makes explicit that these descriptions ARE a bechina of the four categories in Psalm 107 ('the four who must give thanks': desert travelers, prisoners, the sick, and seafarers).\n\nThe bechina connections are explicit:\n- Line 18: 'הַנֶּפֶשׁ הוֹלֶכֶת נָעָה וְנָדָה... עָיֵף וְרָעֵב וְצָמֵא בַּמִּדְבָּר — זֶה בְּחִינַת תָּעוּ בַּמִּדְבָּר' — the wandering, exhausted, hungry, thirsty soul IS a bechina of 'they wandered in the desert' (Psalm 107:4)\n- Line 18: 'חֳלִי הַנֶּפֶשׁ... כָּל אֹכֶל תְּתַעֵב נַפְשָׁם — זֶה בְּחִינַת... חוֹלֶה' — the soul so faint it cannot eat IS a bechina of 'their soul loathed all food' (Psalm 107:18, the sick man's verse)\n- Line 13: the captured soul IS described as 'חָבוּשׁ בְּבֵית־הָאֲסוּרִין' — imprisoned; and line 18: 'מֵעִנְיַן חָבוּשׁ בְּבֵית־הָאֲסוּרִים' — a bechina of the prisoner of Psalm 107\n- Line 14 and 18: 'יַעֲלוּ שָׁמַיִם יֵרְדוּ תְהוֹמוֹת — זֶהוּ בְּחִינַת יוֹרְדֵי הַיָּם' — the soul rising and plunging IS a bechina of 'those who go down to the sea' (Psalm 107:23-26)\n\nAll four types from Psalm 107 are declared explicitly by the commentary (line 17: 'הַכֹּל מְרַמֵּז עַל צָרוֹת הַנֶּפֶשׁ' — all of it hints at the soul's tribulations). The four types are parallel aspects of the same reality: the soul's condition when scattered, exiled, and trapped. They are not sequential stages (the desert wanderer does not PRODUCE the prisoner; all four describe the same essential state from different angles). This makes them bechina-related, not eitza-related.\n\nLine 12 also uses explicit bechina language: 'הִיא בְּחִינַת: נַפְשָׁם בָּהֶם תִּתְעַטָּף' — the soul wrapping itself in itself IS a bechina of the verse 'their soul fainted within them' (Psalm 107:5). This adds a fifth bechina identity within the cluster.\n\nPolarity is evil/negative: this entire cluster describes the soul's suffering, exile, and spiritual degradation — not a positive spiritual state. While God ultimately saves the soul ('הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ מַצִּיל אוֹתָהּ', line 19), the cluster itself describes the PROBLEM, not the solution. In the framework's terms, it occupies the 'afflicted' domain — not the domain of holiness or the domain of the klipot-as-captor (Package 2), but the domain of the captured/suffering entity. Polarity is designated 'evil' (negative consequence domain) rather than 'good' because all these nodes describe spiritual damage and exile.\n\nThe dominant node is צרות הנפש (the soul's tribulations) because it is the explicit umbrella concept named in line 17: 'הַכֹּל מְרַמֵּז עַל צָרוֹת הַנֶּפֶשׁ' — all of it alludes to this.\n\nStructural note: there is a question whether Package 3 should be 'evil' or 'neutral.' It does not belong to the klipot; it belongs to the victim. However, in the framework's polarity scheme: good = holiness/positive side; evil = negative/suffering/opposing-force side; neutral = structural objects. The soul's tribulations are clearly in the negative/suffering domain (they result from the evil klipot's activity), not neutral. 'Evil' here means 'the suffering/negative side of the spiritual map' rather than the klipot themselves.", "dominant_node": "צרות הנפש", "nodes": ["צרות הנפש", "תועים במדבר", "חולי הנפש", "חבוש בבית האסורים", "יורדי הים", "נפשם בהם תתעטף"], "node_degrees": {"צרות הנפש": 4, "תועים במדבר": 2, "חולי הנפש": 2, "חבוש בבית האסורים": 2, "יורדי הים": 2, "נפשם בהם תתעטף": 1}, "note": "The four Psalm 107 types (desert travelers, prisoners, sick, seafarers) are identified as a unified set by the commentary's explicit statement: 'כָּל אַרְבָּעָה הַנַּ\"ל... הַכֹּל מְרַמֵּז עַל צָרוֹת הַנֶּפֶשׁ' (line 17). They are bechina-equivalents of one another in the sense that they all express the same reality (the soul's tribulations in divine service) from four different angles. The dataset does not currently contain any of these bechina edges for reference 163. See torah_163_additions.json for the proposed new edges. Note: 'נפשם בהם תתעטף' (Psalm 107:5 — 'their soul fainted/wrapped within them') appears in line 12 with explicit 'בְּחִינַת' language ('וְהִיא בְּחִינַת: נַפְשָׁם בָּהֶם תִּתְעַטָּף') applied to the soul's inability to receive nourishment — this is a fifth bechina identity within the soul-suffering cluster."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 164, "label": "צַדִּיק וְרוֹפֵא הַנְּפָשׁוֹת", "label_en": "The Tzaddik as Healer of Souls", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's mashal-nimshal structure establishes a direct bechina between the Tzaddik-of-the-generation, the talmid-chacham, and the great doctor (דאקטיר הגדול): all three ARE the master healer who knows the deep, precious medicines. Line 3 uses the defining formula: 'הוּא רוֹפֵא חֳלִי הַנְּפָשׁוֹת' — the Tzaddik IS the healer of sick souls. This is not cause-and-effect (he does not produce healing through some other route); he IS essentially a healer, in the same way the great doctor IS essentially a healer. The mashal figure (the great doctor) and the nimshal figure (the Tzaddik/talmid-chacham) are different expressions of the same essential role. The defining quality of this cluster is mastery of hidden, precious remedies — the Tzaddik knows the deep Torah medicine (הנהגות ודרכים ישרים) that the patient would never prescribe for himself. This package is unambiguously positive — the Tzaddik is the axis of good in the teaching.", "dominant_node": "צדיק הדור", "nodes": ["צדיק הדור", "תלמיד-חכם", "רופא חולי הנפשות", "הדאקטיר הגדול"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק הדור": 3, "תלמיד-חכם": 2, "רופא חולי הנפשות": 2, "הדאקטיר הגדול": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הדאקטיר הגדול"], "reason": "הדאקטיר הגדול is the mashal-level counterpart of the צדיק הדור in the nimshal. The Rebbe uses 'כֵּן יִזְדַּמֵּן' to establish a direct mashal-nimshal equivalence. The great doctor and the Tzaddik share the same essential nature: mastery of precious hidden medicines that the patient himself does not know he needs. This is a shared-nature bechina (both ARE the master healer), not cause-and-effect. The mashal figure belongs in the same package as its nimshal counterpart."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 164, "label": "תּוֹרָה וּרְפוּאָה", "label_en": "Torah and Healing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central bechina of Torah #164 is stated explicitly in line 5: 'כִּי הַתּוֹרָה הִיא רְפוּאָה' — Torah IS healing. This is the clearest possible bechina statement (using the copula 'הִיא'), establishing Torah and healing as two expressions of the same essential reality. From this hub, several further bechinas radiate: (1) פְּנִימִיּוּת הַתּוֹרָה (the inner Torah) IS the powerful drug (סַם) by mashal-nimshal: the dangerous drug in the parable that must be diluted corresponds directly to the inner dimension of Torah that cannot be given to the unready. (2) רְפוּאָה גְּנוּזָה (the hidden healing) IS פְּנִימִיּוּת הַתּוֹרָה — line 7 explicitly identifies the inner Torah with the healing that is hidden inside the stories. (3) סַם חַיִּים (elixir of life) IS Torah-for-the-one-who-merited — the Talmud quote (Yoma 72b) confirms this bechina directly. This package represents the positive, holy dimension of the Torah-medicine: the real healing power, the inner Torah, the elixir of life for those who merit it.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "רפואה", "פנימיות התורה", "סם", "סם חיים", "רפואה גנוזה"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 4, "פנימיות התורה": 3, "רפואה": 3, "סם": 2, "רפואה גנוזה": 2, "סם חיים": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 164, "label": "לְבוּשׁ וְסִפּוּרֵי מַעֲשִׂיּוֹת", "label_en": "The Garment and the Story-Wrapping", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This cluster groups the concepts that describe the external medium through which inner Torah (Package 2) is delivered to the unready receiver (Package 4). The fundamental bechina in this package is that sippurei ma'asiot (outer stories), sippurei dvarim of the Tzaddik, and other Torah words all share the same essential nature: they are garments/wrappings (לבוש, עירוב) for the inner Torah. Lines 6-7 present a gradient of three levels of garment: (1) דִּבְרֵי תוֹרָה אֲחֵרִים (other Torah teachings) — the first level of wrapping; (2) סִפּוּרֵי דְּבָרִים חִיצוֹנִיִּים (outer stories) — the second, more external wrapping; (3) סִפּוּרֵי מַעֲשִׂיּוֹת that the Torah itself is already clothed in. All three share the same essential nature: they are לְבוּשׁ (garment). The עִירוּב (mixing) of the parable (mixing the drug with other substances) IS a bechina of this very act of wrapping inner Torah. This package is marked neutral because the garment itself is neither inherently holy nor unholy — it is the structural device that can make either a good delivery (Torah received safely) or a necessary compromise; the stories of the Torah are themselves this neutral vehicle. The title 'לְעִנְיַן סִפּוּרֵי דְּבָרִים שֶׁל הַצַּדִּיק הָאֱמֶת' announces this package as the Torah's central subject.", "dominant_node": "סיפורי מעשיות", "nodes": ["סיפורי מעשיות", "סיפורי דברים", "לבוש", "עירוב", "דברי תורה אחרים"], "node_degrees": {"סיפורי מעשיות": 3, "סיפורי דברים": 2, "לבוש": 2, "עירוב": 2, "דברי תורה אחרים": 1}}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 164, "label": "הַחוֹלֶה וּמִי שֶׁלֹּא זָכָה", "label_en": "The Sick Receiver — One Who Has Not Merited", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package groups all the nodes that describe the person on the receiving end who cannot accept inner Torah directly. The core bechina is established by the mashal-nimshal structure: הַחוֹלֶה (the sick patient in the parable) IS the person who has not merited (מִי שֶׁלֹּא זָכָה) — both describe the same essential spiritual state: one who would be spiritually destroyed by direct encounter with the inner Torah. Line 5 establishes this via the Talmud (Yoma 72b): 'לֹא זָכָה – נַעֲשֶׂה לוֹ סַם הַמָּוֶת'. The concept of סַם הַמָּוֶת (the death-drug/poison) belongs here as a bechina of what Torah becomes for this person — Torah-as-poison IS the spiritual condition of the unworthy receiver, the same essential reality viewed from the Torah's effect-on-them angle. This package is marked evil not because the person is inherently evil but because the dominant quality described — spiritual unworthiness, inability to receive holiness, Torah-turns-to-poison — is the negative/evil pole of the teaching. The teaching's entire argument (why stories are necessary) is driven by this package's reality. The רוֹפֵא שֶׁנֶּחֱלָה (the doctor who himself fell sick) of the parable also belongs here: he represents the person with partial knowledge who nonetheless needs someone greater — he IS a bechina of the חוֹלֶה at the level of one who thinks he knows what medicine he needs.", "dominant_node": "החולה", "nodes": ["החולה", "מי שלא זכה", "סם המוות", "הרופא שנחלה"], "node_degrees": {"החולה": 3, "מי שלא זכה": 2, "סם המוות": 2, "הרופא שנחלה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["סם המוות"], "reason": "סַם הַמָּוֶת is not simply a consequence of Torah meeting the unworthy person — it IS that person's essential relationship with Torah. The Talmud says Torah 'becomes' poison for him; this 'becoming' is a bechina statement (the Torah IS a death-drug to him), not merely a causal outcome. The death-drug and the unworthy receiver share the same essential spiritual reality: mutual incompatibility with holiness in this state. Placing סם המוות in Package 2 (Torah) would be wrong because it represents Torah distorted by the receiver's state, not Torah in its positive essence."}, {"merged_nodes": ["הרופא שנחלה"], "reason": "The sick doctor of the mashal is distinguished from the great doctor precisely because he is now on the patient's side — despite his partial knowledge of medicine, he himself needs healing and cannot self-prescribe properly. He IS a bechina of the חוֹלֶה (the receiver who needs the Tzaddik's higher guidance), not the great doctor. His presence in the parable specifically illustrates the spiritual state of someone who thinks their own partial Torah-knowledge is sufficient remedy, which is the defining characteristic of the 'unready receiver' this package describes."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 165, "label": "הָרָע וְהַיִּסּוּרִין", "label_en": "Evil and Afflictions", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This package captures the Torah's central bechina-cluster for the domain of suffering. In line 3, the Rebbe identifies the word 'רֵעֲךָ' (your neighbor/friend) with 'הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ' (the evil that comes to you), using the formula: 'וְאָהַבְתָּ לְרֵעֲךָ – שֶׁתֶּאֱהוֹב הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ.' The reinterpretation via 'שֶׁתֶּאֱהוֹב' provides the bechina-ground: the Rebbe is declaring that 'רֵעֲךָ' IS 'הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ' — they are the same referent (the word רֵעֲךָ is being equated with its new meaning הרע). Immediately following, the same construction repeats: 'הַיְנוּ הָרָעוֹת וְהַיִּסּוּרִין הַבָּאִים עָלֶיךָ' — the marker 'הַיְנוּ' (that is / namely) equates 'הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ' with 'הָרָעוֹת וְהַיִּסּוּרִין הַבָּאִים עָלֶיךָ.' The three nodes form a connected chain: (1) רֵעֲךָ IS הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ [by the 'שֶׁתֶּאֱהוֹב הָרָע' reinterpretation of the verse]; (2) הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ IS הָרָעוֹת וְהַיִּסּוּרִין [by the 'הַיְנוּ' marker in line 3]. All three share one essential nature: they name the domain of divinely-sent affliction from different angles — the grammatical form of the verse (רֵעֲךָ), the shorthand (הָרָע שֶׁבָּא לְךָ), and the full explicit description (הָרָעוֹת וְהַיִּסּוּרִין). This package is marked evil because the dominant content — suffering, pain, affliction, evil occurrences — is the negative pole of the teaching. The instruction to LOVE this domain does not change its intrinsic nature; the package is still the evil pole that the teaching teaches the person to accept. The dominant node is 'הָרָעוֹת וְהַיִּסּוּרִין' because it is the most fully articulated description of the domain, appears in both line 2 (the teaching's opening statement) and line 3 (the hermeneutical proof), and has the highest effective degree of conceptual connectivity within the teaching.", "dominant_node": "הרעות והיסורין", "nodes": ["הרעות והיסורין", "הרע שבא לך", "רעך"], "node_degrees": {"הרעות והיסורין": 2, "הרע שבא לך": 2, "רעך": 1}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 165, "label": "כָּמוֹךָ וּמַעֲשֶׂיךָ", "label_en": "As You Are — Your Deeds and Measure", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "This package captures the Torah's second bechina-cluster: the measure of a person's actions and true state as the yardstick of divine justice. In line 4, the Rebbe reinterprets the word 'כָּמוֹךָ' (as yourself): 'כִּי כָּמוֹךָ – כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה הוּא, הַיְנוּ לְפִי מַעֲשֶׂיךָ.' Two bechina markers fire in sequence: (1) the dash construction 'כָּמוֹךָ – כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה הוּא' equates the verse-word with its new meaning: 'as yourself' IS 'as you truly are (כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה הוּא)'; (2) the 'הַיְנוּ' marker then equates 'כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה הוּא' with 'לְפִי מַעֲשֶׂיךָ' (according to your deeds). The chain: כָּמוֹךָ IS כְּמוֹ שֶׁאַתָּה הוּא IS לְפִי מַעֲשֶׂיךָ. All three share one essential nature: the self-referential measure — what you are, how you stand before God, defined by your actual deeds. This is the standard of justice by which the magnitude of deserved-suffering is reckoned. The polarity is neutral: 'מַעֲשֶׂיךָ' (your deeds) is a structural concept — it is the human side of the justice calculus, neither inherently holy nor evil. The same concept would apply equally to a person with good deeds or bad deeds; it is the yardstick, not the verdict. The dominant node is 'לְפִי מַעֲשֶׂיךָ' — it is the most concrete and active of the three formulations, appearing in both line 2 (the teaching's first explanation) and line 5 (the verse-conclusion), and it is the explicit substantive content that the other two are equated to.", "dominant_node": "לפי מעשיך", "nodes": ["לפי מעשיך", "כמו שאתה הוא", "כמוך"], "node_degrees": {"לפי מעשיך": 2, "כמו שאתה הוא": 2, "כמוך": 1}}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 165, "label": "אֲנִי ה' — בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים", "label_en": "I am God — The Master of Mercy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package captures the Torah's third bechina-cluster: the divine identity as explicitly the Master of Mercy. In line 5, the Rebbe reinterprets the verse's closing phrase: 'אֲנִי ה' – בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים, עֲדַיִן אֲנִי מִתְנַהֵג עִמְּךָ בְּרַחֲמִים, כַּנַּ\"ל.' The dash construction 'אֲנִי ה' – בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים' is the bechina marker: 'I am God' IS 'the Master of Mercy.' The Rebbe equates the divine name (אֲנִי ה') with the divine quality (בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים). This is the same structural move as in line 3 and 4 — the verse is being hermeneutically re-read, and each clause-meaning IS declared to be the same as the deeper concept. The two nodes share one essential nature: the God who spoke the verse IS the Master of Mercy — both designations name the same reality from different angles (the divine name versus the divine quality). The polarity is good: this is the positive pole of the teaching — God's mercy, God's compassionate governance, is unambiguously the holy positive force. The entire teaching's argument rests on this: God IS mercy, and therefore even painful sufferings are expressions of that mercy (less than deserved). The dominant node is 'בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים' because it is the substantive theological content being declared — the new understanding of who God IS in this context. 'אֲנִי ה'' is the verse-anchor; 'בַּעַל הָרַחֲמִים' is the bechina-declaration that expands its meaning.", "dominant_node": "בעל הרחמים", "nodes": ["בעל הרחמים", "אני ה'"], "node_degrees": {"בעל הרחמים": 1, "אני ה'": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 166, "label": "אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים וּמֶמְשָׁלַת הַצַּדִּיק", "label_en": "Man of God and the Dominion of the Tzaddik", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's central bechina: the tzaddik-with-community IS 'Ish HaElohim' (Man of God), and both share the same essential nature as memshala (dominion/rulership manifest through divine service). The Rebbe uses 'נִקְרָא' — 'was called / is called' — which is the standard bechina declaration formula in LM1 ('X is called Y' = X IS an aspect of Y; compare Torah #1's 'שֶׁנִּקְרֵאת חֵן', Torah #163's 'הָיָה נִקְרָא'). The statement is: when הָעוֹלָם (the world/students) is אֵצֶל הַצַּדִּיק (gathered at the tzaddik), he becomes נִקְרָא אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים — IS called Man of God — and this is IS a state of מֶמְשָׁלָה (dominion, rulership). Three nodes share the same essential nature: (1) אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים — the scriptural title Elisha receives when surrounded, which IS the teaching's name for the enhanced state of tzaddik-plus-community; (2) מֶמְשָׁלָה — the conceptual term the Rebbe introduces in his opening statement as what the tzaddik ACQUIRES / IS in this state; (3) צַדִּיק כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֶצְלוֹ — the tzaddik in this state of having the world gathered around him. All three are different descriptions of the same essential reality: the tzaddik-in-his-full-governance-dimension, the point at which human leadership and divine authority merge. The first sentence ('כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֵצֶל הַצַּדִּיק, אָז יֵשׁ לוֹ מֶמְשָׁלָה') and the second ('כְּשֶׁהָיוּ בְּנֵי הַנְּבִיאִים אֶצְלוֹ, הָיָה נִקְרָא אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים') are bechina-parallel: both state the same identity — tzaddik surrounded = governed by / identical to the Ish HaElohim quality. The dominant node is אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים because it is the teaching's scriptural name for the reality being described, and it IS the explicit predicate of the 'נִקְרָא' (was called) statement that the Rebbe brings as his proof text.", "dominant_node": "אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים", "nodes": ["אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים", "מֶמְשָׁלָה", "צַדִּיק כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֶצְלוֹ"], "node_degrees": {"אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים": 2, "מֶמְשָׁלָה": 2, "צַדִּיק כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֶצְלוֹ": 2}, "source_bechina_statements": [{"text": "כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֵצֶל הַצַּדִּיק, אָז יֵשׁ לוֹ מֶמְשָׁלָה", "analysis": "Direct identity: the tzaddik-with-world IS (has / is-defined-by) memshala. The copula 'יֵשׁ לוֹ' in this context functions as a bechina: this condition IS the condition of memshala, they share the same essential nature.", "nodes_linked": ["צַדִּיק כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֶצְלוֹ", "מֶמְשָׁלָה"]}, {"text": "כְּשֶׁהָיוּ בְּנֵי הַנְּבִיאִים אֶצְלוֹ, הָיָה נִקְרָא אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים", "analysis": "Naming statement: Elisha-with-students WAS CALLED / IS an aspect of 'Ish HaElohim'. 'נִקְרָא' is a standard bechina declaration in LM1 — to be called something IS to be that thing essentially. The title 'Man of God' is the scriptural expression of the memshala quality.", "nodes_linked": ["צַדִּיק כְּשֶׁהָעוֹלָם אֶצְלוֹ", "אִישׁ הָאֱלֹקִים"]}], "note": "The three nodes form a tight triangle of bechina equivalences: (a) Tzaddik-with-world IS memshala; (b) Tzaddik-with-world IS Ish HaElohim; (c) by transitivity, memshala IS Ish HaElohim — both name the same elevated state. This is not a cause-effect chain (the community does not PRODUCE Ish HaElohim status through some mechanism; the community's presence IS the condition that defines the state — the state IS memshala, and memshala IS Ish HaElohim). The Rebbe's two sentences are parallel bechina declarations, not a two-step causal sequence. All three nodes belong in one package by shared nature."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 166, "label": "אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם — הַצַּדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ", "label_en": "Plain Elisha — The Tzaddik in His Solitude", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The teaching contrasts the Ish HaElohim state (Package 1) with the state of 'Elisha alone' — 'כְּשֶׁהָיָה לְבַדּוֹ נִקְרָא אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם' (when he was alone he was called simply Elisha). The word 'נִקְרָא' again marks this as a bechina statement: the tzaddik-in-solitude IS called / IS an aspect of 'Elisha stam' (plain/bare Elisha). Two nodes share this essential nature: (1) אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם — the scriptural name for the tzaddik-alone state; (2) צַדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ — the tzaddik in solitude, without the world gathered around him. Both describe the same essential condition: the tzaddik at his base level, possessing his own spiritual qualities but not yet manifesting the additional dimension of memshala that emerges only in relationship to the community. This package is marked neutral (not evil) because: (a) the solitary tzaddik is still the tzaddik — a holy person with genuine spiritual stature; he is not diminished into evil or disorder; (b) the Rebbe does not frame the lone-Elisha state as negative, destructive, or opposed to holiness — it is simply the baseline state, the tzaddik's ordinary self; (c) in the framework's polarity scheme, neutral applies to concepts that can serve either domain or that describe a condition without inherent holiness/evil valence. The tzaddik-alone is a holy person in a neutral structural condition (not yet in the memshala relationship). The dominant node is אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם because 'Elisha stam' (plain/simply Elisha) is the text's explicit naming formula for this state and captures its essential quality: plain/bare/not-yet-activated-with-memshala.", "dominant_node": "אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם", "nodes": ["אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם", "צַדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ"], "node_degrees": {"אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם": 1, "צַדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ": 1}, "source_bechina_statements": [{"text": "כְּשֶׁהָיָה לְבַדּוֹ נִקְרָא אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם", "analysis": "Naming statement: Elisha-alone WAS CALLED / IS an aspect of 'plain Elisha'. The 'סְתָם' (plain/simply/without further qualification) is the Rebbe's conceptual marker for the baseline state without memshala. The lone tzaddik IS this baseline, and the baseline IS 'Elisha stam.'", "nodes_linked": ["צַדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ", "אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם"]}], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אֱלִישָׁע סְתָם", "צַדִּיק בִּבְדִידוּתוֹ"], "reason": "Two nodes, one raw component. They share the same essential nature: the tzaddik-without-community IS 'plain Elisha', and 'plain Elisha' IS the tzaddik-without-community. Both name the same baseline condition from two angles (the scriptural-narrative figure from Tanach/Zohar, and the conceptual category the Rebbe is teaching about). Merge by shared nature."}], "note": "This package is structurally the 'other pole' of Package 1 — not its evil mirror but its baseline contrast. The teaching is not a good/evil confrontation; it is a description of two conditions of the same person (the tzaddik), distinguished by the presence or absence of the world gathered around him. This makes Torah #166 structurally unusual: it has a good package and a neutral baseline package rather than the typical good/evil mirror pair. The absence of an evil mirror is genuine — the text contains no evil domain, no opposing force, no sitra achra. It is a teaching purely about the positive dimension of tzaddik-community relationship."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 167, "label": "שַׁבָּת אֵצֶל הַצַּדִּיק וְתַעֲנִית", "label_en": "Shabbat with the Tzaddik and Fasting — Shared Spiritual Refinement", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #167 contains a single bechina equation: the Shabbatot spent with the true Talmid Chacham (the true tzaddik) ARE LIKE a fast (תַּעֲנִית). The language 'הֵם כְּמוֹ' declares essential identity of spiritual nature — not mere resemblance, but ontological equivalence at the level of their spiritual effect on the soul. Both nodes — שַׁבָּת אֵצֶל הַתַּלְמִיד-חָכָם and תַּעֲנִית — share the same essential quality: the suspension of the body's ordinary activity in order to elevate the soul. A fast (תַּעֲנִית) works by withdrawing the body from its regular nourishment, thereby redirecting energy from the physical to the spiritual, refining the soul and atoning for sin. Shabbat spent with the tzaddik works through an identical spiritual mechanism: the ordinary weekday self — its thoughts, its physicality, its everyday concerns — is 'fasted' from, while the soul feeds on the tzaddik's presence, Torah, and the holiness of Shabbat itself. The result is the same spiritual tikun as a fast, achieved through the opposite physical means (Shabbat is when one must eat and rejoice, yet it achieves what the fast achieves through restraint). This equivalence is the paradox that requires 'דַּע וְהַאֲמֵן' — knowledge and faith — because it is counterintuitive: the feast-day achieves what the fast-day achieves.\n\nBoth nodes belong entirely to the domain of holiness and positive spiritual practice (polarity: good). A fast is a holy practice for tikun and spiritual elevation. Shabbat is the holy seventh day. The tzaddik's presence transforms Shabbat into an intensified vehicle of the same spiritual work that fasting performs. There is no evil mirror in this Torah — no opposing force is named, no klipah is discussed, no negative counterpart is introduced. The Torah is a pure affirmation of the spiritual power of the tzaddik-Shabbat nexus.\n\nThe dominant node is תַּעֲנִית (fasting) rather than שַׁבָּת, because the teaching's rhetorical force is the revelation that Shabbat achieves what we ordinarily attribute exclusively to fasting — the fast is the known reference point that the Shabbat-tzaddik combination is declared equivalent to. The entire surprise of the Torah lies in the elevation of Shabbat-with-tzaddik to the status of תַּעֲנִית. However, the node שַׁבָּת אֵצֶל הַתַּלְמִיד-חָכָם הָאֱמֶת is the subject (the thing being elevated), so in a sense it is the operative concept the teaching wishes to promote.", "dominant_node": "תענית", "nodes": ["שבת אצל תלמיד חכם אמת", "תענית"], "node_degrees": {"שבת אצל תלמיד חכם אמת": 1, "תענית": 1}, "merge_notes": ["No merges. Torah #167 contains exactly one bechina equation, yielding one 2-node component. There are no raw components to merge — the two nodes are the entire bechina topology of this Torah. The teaching's architecture is a pure bechina statement with no elaboration, sub-clusters, or causal chains requiring package-merge analysis."], "source_bechina_statement": {"in_dataset": false, "text": "הַשַּׁבָּתִים שֶׁשּׁוֹבְתִים אֵצֶל הַתַּלְמִיד-חָכָם הָאֱמֶת, הֵם כְּמוֹ תַּעֲנִית", "formula": "הֵם כְּמוֹ (they are like)", "proposed_edge": {"node1_id": "שבת אצל תלמיד חכם אמת", "node2_id": "תענית", "type": "bechina", "reference": 167}, "note": "The bechina edge for this Torah's central equation is not yet in the dataset. See torah_167_additions.json."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 168, "label": "גַּאֲוָה וְגַדְלוּת", "label_en": "Pride and Self-Inflation", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Torah #168 opens with 'כְּשֶׁבָּא לָאָדָם גַּדְלוּת' (when pride/greatness comes to a person) and then cites Proverbs 16:18: 'לִפְנֵי שֶׁבֶר גָּאוֹן' — before destruction comes gaon (pride/arrogance). The Torah uses 'גַּדְלוּת' (greatness/pride) and 'גָּאוֹן' (arrogance) as two names for the same spiritual quality: the swelling of self-regard, the attitude of self-inflation. This is the implicit bechina at the heart of the teaching — it is not stated with 'בְּחִינַת' but is implicit in the structure: the verse is cited specifically to explain the phenomenon of גדלות, meaning the Rebbe holds that גאון IS גדלות. The shared essential nature is: a state of self-inflation that PRECEDES and signals its own destruction. Polarity is evil: pride/arrogance is consistently treated in Breslov teaching as the spiritual danger-state, the opposite of the servant's proper nullification before God. The Talmud (Sotah 5a) identifies pride as a form of avodah zarah (idolatry) and states that the presence of pride in a Torah scholar is itself a form of spiritual destruction. The verse 'לִפְנֵי שֶׁבֶר גָּאוֹן' makes this explicit: the gaon is already 'before' the shever, meaning the pride itself contains the seed of its own ruin. Note: שֶׁבֶר (destruction/fracture) and צָרָה (trouble/affliction) are the eitza-consequences of this cluster, not bechina-equivalents. They are what pride LEADS TO, not what pride IS. They are documented in the eitza_summary below.", "dominant_node": "גאוה", "nodes": ["גאוה", "גדלות"], "node_degrees": {"גאוה": 1, "גדלות": 1}, "source_bechina_evidence": {"type": "implicit_bechina_via_verse_citation", "description": "The Rebbe cites Proverbs 16:18 ('לִפְנֵי שֶׁבֶר גָּאוֹן') specifically as the prooftext for the phenomenon of 'גדלות' arriving. This is the Rebbe's standard hermeneutic move — quote a verse that uses a near-synonymous word to establish conceptual identity. The verse's 'גָּאוֹן' IS the teaching's 'גַּדְלוּת'. Explicit bechina-formula is absent, but the equivalence is structurally established.", "source_quote": "כְּשֶׁבָּא לָאָדָם גַּדְלוּת, סִימָן שֶׁיָּבוֹא לוֹ צָרָה... כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: לִפְנֵי שֶׁבֶר גָּאוֹן"}, "note": "This is a 2-node package built from a single implicit bechina equivalence. The teaching contains no further bechina-language concepts on the evil side. שבר and צרה are excluded from this package because they are the eitza-result of pride, not parallel aspects sharing the same nature as pride."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 168, "label": "עֲנָוָה וְשִׁפְלוּת", "label_en": "Humility and Lowliness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #168's second arm presents 'כְּשֶׁהוּא שָׁפָל' (when a person is lowly/humble) and cites Proverbs 16:15: 'וְלִפְנֵי כָבוֹד עֲנָוָה' — before honor comes humility (anavah). The Torah uses 'שָׁפָל' (lowly) and 'עֲנָוָה' (humility) as two names for the same spiritual quality: the voluntary lowering of self, the orientation of smallness before God and others. Just as Package 1 pairs גדלות/גאון, this package pairs שפלות/ענוה — the Rebbe cites the verse to explain the phenomenon of שפלות, establishing that ענוה IS שפלות. The shared essential nature is: a state of self-lowering that PRECEDES and signals the arrival of honor. Polarity is good: humility is the cardinal virtue in Breslov teaching. Rebbe Nachman's consistent emphasis is on shpilut (lowliness) as the foundation of genuine avodas Hashem — the Talmud (Avodah Zarah 20b) frames anavah as the highest level of character development, and the Psalms repeatedly identify the humble (ענוים) as those who inherit the earth and receive God's grace. The verse 'וְלִפְנֵי כָבוֹד עֲנָוָה' makes this explicit: the humility precedes, signals, and makes room for the honor. Note: כָּבוֹד גָּדוֹל (great honor) is the eitza-consequence of this cluster — what humility LEADS TO — not a bechina-equivalent of humility. It is documented in the eitza_summary below.", "dominant_node": "ענוה", "nodes": ["ענוה", "שפלות"], "node_degrees": {"ענוה": 1, "שפלות": 1}, "source_bechina_evidence": {"type": "implicit_bechina_via_verse_citation", "description": "The Rebbe cites Proverbs 16:15 ('וְלִפְנֵי כָבוֹד עֲנָוָה') specifically as the prooftext for the phenomenon of 'שָׁפָל' (lowliness). The verse's 'עֲנָוָה' IS the teaching's 'שָׁפָל'. The equivalence is structurally established through verse-citation hermeneutic.", "source_quote": "כְּשֶׁהוּא שָׁפָל, סִימָן שֶׁיָּבוֹא לְכָבוֹד גָּדוֹל... כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְלִפְנֵי כָבוֹד עֲנָוָה"}, "note": "This is a 2-node package built from a single implicit bechina equivalence — the mirror of Package 1. כבוד (honor) is excluded from this package because it is the eitza-result of humility, not a parallel aspect sharing the same nature as humility."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 169, "label": "דִּין וְדָמִים וְרַגְלִין", "label_en": "Judgment, Blood, and Legs — The Din Cycle", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All seven nodes share the essential nature of the din-cycle: the metaphysical mechanism by which divine judgment (דין) manifests in the physical body and is ultimately sweetened. The Rebbe explicitly equates: דָּמִים = דִּינִים (blood is a bechina of judgments — the blood that flows into the legs IS the judgments settling there); עִבּוּר = דִּין (pregnancy is a bechina of judgment — 'as a pregnant woman is filled with wrath', Berakhot 29b); לֵדָה = הַמְתָּקַת הַדִּין (childbirth is a bechina of the sweetening of judgment — when birth occurs, the blood/din exits the legs); שְׁלוּחֵי הַדִּין = רַגְלִין (the agents of judgment are called 'runners' in the Zohar, and they are a bechina of legs — the legs are where judgment concentrates). The polarity is neutral because this cluster represents a structural metaphysical mechanism — divine judgment and its biological/bodily correlates — rather than intrinsically good or evil content. The same leg-system that is heavy with din is the one that dances with joy when din is sweetened. The pregnancy-birth metaphor covers both poles (din = gestation, sweetening = delivery) as phases of one cycle, making the cluster structurally bilateral rather than polarity-fixed.", "dominant_node": "דין", "nodes": ["דין", "דם", "עבור", "לדה", "המתקת הדינים", "שלוחי הדין", "רגלים"], "node_degrees": {"דין": 2, "דם": 1, "עבור": 1, "לדה": 1, "המתקת הדינים": 1, "שלוחי הדין": 1, "רגלים": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw Component A {דין, דם, עבור} (3 nodes): the core bechina hub. דין is the hub with degree 2; דם and עבור each equated directly with דין.", "Raw Component B {לדה, המתקת הדינים} (2-node pair): merged here. לדה = המתקת הדין is a direct, explicit bechina. The shared nature with Component A is the din-cycle: עבור IS din (the concentration phase) and לדה IS the sweetening of din (the release phase) — both are facets of the same din-cycle metaphysical structure, not a cause-and-effect relationship between them. The merge is by shared domain: the pregnancy-birth-judgment metaphor is one integrated structure.", "Raw Component C {שלוחי הדין, רגלים} (2-node pair): merged here. שלוחי הדין = רגלים by explicit bechina (Zohar Bereshit 43). The shared nature with Component A is direct: the legs ARE where din settles (the blood/din flows to the legs), and the agents of din ARE the legs. This is the same din-body-mechanism that Components A and B describe. Shared nature: the bodily location and physical carrier of din, not a separate thematic domain."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 170, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ, צוּרָה, וְיִסּוּרִין", "label_en": "Soul, Form, and Affliction — The Suffering-Soul Continuum", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #170's central insight is that affliction (יסורין/צרות) and soul/form (נֶפֶשׁ/צוּרָה) are not opposites but two aspects of a single spiritual reality — revealed by the fact that 'tzarah' is the language of 'tzurah.' The teaching presents suffering not as evil but as the positive mechanism through which the soul is revealed and grows. Polarity is 'good' because: (a) the entire teaching valorizes yisurin as a spiritual gift — they enable the tzaddik and spiritual person to carry and elevate many souls; (b) the psalm citation ('Lord, how many are my oppressors — the many who rise against me I carry and elevate to their root') transforms suffering into a redemptive act; (c) the Zohar proof-text frames the body as wood that must be struck to produce fire — presenting the process positively as spiritual illumination. The afflictions are not the evil force in this Torah; they are the positive mechanism of soul-revelation. There is no 'evil affliction' counterpart introduced — the teaching treats all forms of suffering (whether from family, neighbors, city, or the world) as part of the same positive schema of soul-growth. The dominant node is נֶפֶשׁ (soul) — it is the goal-state and ultimate carrier of other souls, and its equation with צוּרָה (form) is the climax of the teaching. צוּרָה is the most-connected node within the bechina chain (it connects to both צרות via 'לְשׁוֹן' and to נֶפֶשׁ via 'הַיְנוּ'), making it structurally central. The chain shows the four-stage continuum: suffering (יסורין) = affliction (צרות) = form (צוּרָה) = soul (נֶפֶשׁ). This is the Torah's fundamental bechina insight: suffering IS, in its deepest nature, a form-revealing and soul-revealing process.", "dominant_node": "נפש", "nodes": ["יסורין", "צרות", "צורה", "נפש"], "node_degrees": {"יסורין": 1, "צרות": 2, "צורה": 2, "נפש": 1}, "source_bechina_statements": [{"edge": "יסורין — צרות", "formula": "נִקְרָאִים (are called)", "quote": "כָּל הַיִּסּוּרִין נִקְרָאִים צָרוֹת", "type": "explicit_bechina"}, {"edge": "צרות — צורה", "formula": "לְשׁוֹן (language of / named after)", "quote": "נִקְרֵאת צָרָה, לְשׁוֹן צוּרָה", "type": "etymological_bechina"}, {"edge": "צורה — נפש", "formula": "הַיְנוּ (namely / that is)", "quote": "מְאִירָה הַצּוּרָה, הַיְנוּ הַנֶּפֶשׁ", "type": "explicit_bechina"}], "merge_notes": ["No merges into this package. The גּוּף/חֹמֶר component (Package 2) has a causal relationship to the יסורין/צרות end of this chain — afflictions act upon the body — but they do not share the nature of suffering or soul. Merging would violate the 'shared nature only' criterion."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 170, "label": "גּוּף וְחֹמֶר", "label_en": "Body and Matter — The Physical Substrate", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "In Torah #170, the body (גּוּף) and matter (חֹמֶר) are presented as the physical substrate that is subdued by suffering and thereby enables the soul to shine. They are not evil — the teaching does not demonize the body or present it as the enemy. Rather, the body is the 'wood' (as the Zohar's metaphor of 'אָעָא' suggests) that must be struck in order to catch fire. The body is a neutral medium: it can either block the soul's light or, when subdued through suffering, allow it to shine. The Zohar passage ('גּוּפָא דְּלָא סָלֵק בֵּהּ נְהוֹרָא דְּנִשְׁמְתָא' — a body that cannot receive the soul's light) presents the body as neutral material whose state determines whether the soul's light passes through. Polarity is therefore 'neutral' — not evil (as the body would be in a teaching about sin or yetzer hara) and not positive (as the body becomes a vehicle for spiritual service in other Torahs). The bechina between גּוּף and חֹמֶר is structural: throughout the teaching, the Rebbe uses גּוּף (body) and חֹמֶר (matter/material) interchangeably as synonyms for the physical dimension — 'נִכְנָע גּוּפוֹ' and 'בְּהִכָּנַע הַחֹמֶר' describe the same event with the same vocabulary of submission. The kabbalistic identification of body=matter (חומר) and soul=form (צוּרָה) is the classical Aristotelian-kabbalistic dyad adopted by Rebbe Nachman here. The dominant node is גּוּף (body) as the more frequently used term in the teaching's narrative.", "dominant_node": "גוף", "nodes": ["גוף", "חומר"], "node_degrees": {"גוף": 1, "חומר": 1}, "source_bechina_statements": [{"edge": "גוף — חומר", "formula": "structural parallel (interchangeable use in identical context)", "quote": "נִכְנָע גּוּפוֹ... בְּהִכָּנַע הַחֹמֶר תִּגְדַּל הַצּוּרָה", "type": "structural_bechina_via_parallel_usage"}], "merge_notes": ["Merge considered and rejected. גּוּף/חֹמֶר cannot merge with Package 1 (יסורין/צרות/צוּרָה/נֶפֶשׁ) because the relationship between them is causal, not bechina: the afflictions (יסורין/צרות) act ON the body (גּוּף/חֹמֶר) to subdue it, which then enables the soul (נֶפֶשׁ/צוּרָה) to shine. Cause-and-effect. The body does not share the essential nature of suffering or soul. גּוּף/חֹמֶר stands alone as a 2-node package representing the material substrate that is the target of the purification process described in the Torah."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 172, "label": "אוֹר הַשֵּׁם וְהַכָּבוֹד", "label_en": "Divine Light and Honor", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly states: 'וְעִקַּר אוֹר הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ הוּא הַכָּבוֹד' (line 4) — the essence of divine light IS kavod. This is the central bechina equivalence of the entire Torah. Later (line 5) the Rebbe repeats 'אֲזַי מְקַבֵּל אוֹר הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ, שֶׁהוּא הַכָּבוֹד' — confirming the equation. The divine light and divine honor are two names for the same reality: the continuous divine flow that God pours out upon creation. This package also naturally absorbs שפע (divine abundance/flow), which the Rebbe mentions in line 3 ('אוֹר וְשֶׁפַע שֶׁל הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ') as the two aspects of the divine outpouring — light and shefa are treated as equivalent outflow terms in this Torah.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["אור השם יתברך", "כבוד", "שפע"], "node_degrees": {"כבוד": 2, "אור השם יתברך": 2, "שפע": 1}, "source_basis": [{"quote": "וְעִקַּר אוֹר הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ הוּא הַכָּבוֹד", "line": 4, "bechina_type": "explicit equivalence — 'the essence of X is Y'"}, {"quote": "אֲזַי מְקַבֵּל אוֹר הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ, שֶׁהוּא הַכָּבוֹד", "line": 5, "bechina_type": "explicit equivalence — 'X which is Y'"}, {"quote": "אוֹר וְשֶׁפַע שֶׁל הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ", "line": 3, "bechina_type": "paired terms — light and shefa treated as co-referential"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 172, "label": "חָכְמָה וְאַיִן — הַבִּיטּוּל", "label_en": "Wisdom and Nothingness — Self-Nullification", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Three concepts cluster around the domain of self-nullification and its equation with chokhmah: (1) חכמה is explicitly equated with אין via the verse 'הַחָכְמָה מֵאַיִן תִּמָּצֵא' (Job 28:12, line 6) — wisdom is found from/in nothingness, meaning chokhmah IS ayin; (2) the wise (חכמים) are called 'אַיִן' directly: 'הַחֲכָמִים שֶׁהֵם אַיִן'; (3) the practice of ביטול עצמו / אינו כלל מזה העולם (lines 3 and 5) is the human expression of this same ayin-quality — the person who is nothing from this world embodies the chokhmah/ayin principle. All three are bechinas of the same underlying state: the nullified, physicality-free orientation that blocks nothing and receives everything. This is structurally independent from Package 1 (divine light/kavod) because chokhmah/bitul is the quality of the RECEIVER, while kavod/light is the quality of what is RECEIVED — they are related by eitza (nullification enables reception), not by shared nature.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["חכמה", "אין", "ביטול עצמו / אינו כלל מזה העולם"], "node_degrees": {"חכמה": 2, "אין": 2, "ביטול עצמו / אינו כלל מזה העולם": 1}, "source_basis": [{"quote": "כִּי הַחָכְמָה מֵאַיִן תִּמָּצֵא", "line": 6, "bechina_type": "verse-based equivalence — wisdom comes from/is ayin"}, {"quote": "הַחֲכָמִים שֶׁהֵם אַיִן", "line": 6, "bechina_type": "explicit predication — 'the wise who ARE ayin'"}, {"quote": "אִם הָאָדָם מְבַטֵּל עַצְמוֹ, וְאֵינוֹ כְּלָל מִזֶּה הָעוֹלָם", "line": 3, "bechina_type": "the practical form of the ayin-state; line 5 equates this with 'מְלֹא כָל הָאָרֶץ'"}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 172, "label": "פָּנִים שׂוֹחֲקוֹת — חַיִּים וָטוֹב", "label_en": "The Joyful Face — Life and Good", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Line 7 states: 'כְּשֶׁהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ מַרְאֶה פָּנִים שׂוֹחֲקוֹת – חַיִּים וְטוֹבָה לָעוֹלָם'. The showing of a joyful face IS life and good — this is an explicit bechina equation: פנים שוחקות = חיים וטובה. Line 8 confirms with the verse: 'רְאֵה נָתַתִּי לִפְנֵיכֶם הַיּוֹם אֶת הַחַיִּים וְאֶת הַטּוֹב... הַיְנוּ לַפָּנִים שֶׁלָּכֶם' — life and good ARE the faces you show. The Rebbe also applies this to the tzaddik: 'וְכֵן הַצַּדִּיק, כְּשֶׁמַּרְאֶה פָּנִים שׂוֹחֲקוֹת, הוּא טוֹב' — the tzaddik's joyful face = good. This package covers the positive pole of the face-domain. It is distinct from Package 1 (divine light/kavod) in that panim is the *mode of revelation* of kavod — the way the light reaches the world — rather than being the light itself.", "dominant_node": "פנים שוחקות", "nodes": ["פנים שוחקות", "חיים וטובה"], "node_degrees": {"פנים שוחקות": 2, "חיים וטובה": 1}, "source_basis": [{"quote": "כְּשֶׁהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ מַרְאֶה פָּנִים שׂוֹחֲקוֹת – חַיִּים וְטוֹבָה לָעוֹלָם", "line": 7, "bechina_type": "explicit equivalence — showing joyful face = life and good"}, {"quote": "הַיְנוּ לַפָּנִים שֶׁלָּכֶם", "line": 8, "bechina_type": "'הַיְנוּ' = definitional bechina — life/good IS the panim"}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 172, "label": "פָּנִים זוֹעֲפוֹת — מָוֶת וָרָע", "label_en": "The Averted Face — Death and Evil", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 3 is explicitly stated in the same verses. Line 7: 'וּלְהֵפֶךְ – לְהֵפֶךְ, חַס וְשָׁלוֹם' — the reverse face = the reverse outcome (death and evil). Line 8 cites 'אֶת הַמָּוֶת וְכוּ'' in the same 'הַיְנוּ לַפָּנִים שֶׁלָּכֶם' formula — death/evil also IS the face shown. This is the structural evil mirror of Package 3 (the good face/life package): the same panim concept oriented negatively produces its opposite. The Rebbe applies this symmetry to God and to the tzaddik alike. Per the framework's good/evil mirror principle (§4), every good package has an exact evil counterpart — this is that counterpart for Package 3.", "dominant_node": "פנים זועפות / ולהפך", "nodes": ["פנים זועפות / ולהפך", "מות ורע"], "node_degrees": {"פנים זועפות / ולהפך": 2, "מות ורע": 1}, "source_basis": [{"quote": "וּלְהֵפֶךְ – לְהֵפֶךְ, חַס וְשָׁלוֹם", "line": 7, "bechina_type": "mirror inversion — the negative face = the negative outcome"}, {"quote": "אֶת הַמָּוֶת וְכוּ', הַיְנוּ לַפָּנִים שֶׁלָּכֶם", "line": 8, "bechina_type": "הַיְנוּ bechina — death is the averted/negative face"}]}, {"id": 5, "torah_ref": 172, "label": "גַּשְׁמִיּוּת וְהַצֵּל", "label_en": "Physicality and the Shadow", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Lines 2-3 establish a sustained metaphysical bechina: the shadow (צל) is caused by a physical/material thing (דָּבָר גַּשְׁמִי) standing opposite a spiritual/finer thing. The Rebbe gives three examples — wood and stone vs sunlight, earth's shadow causing eclipses, the sun itself relative to what is above it — all to establish that גשמיות (physicality/materiality) and צל (shadow) are the same reality: 'הָאָדָם כְּפִי גַּשְׁמִיּוּתוֹ... כֵּן עוֹשֶׂה צֵל'. This is a sustained bechina teaching: a person's degree of physicality IS their degree of shadow-casting. The Rebbe uses 'כְּפִי' (according to) throughout — the measure of physicality = the measure of shadow. מעשים רעים (evil deeds) are the third element: they are what increases/generates the גשמיות-צל. All three belong to the same domain: the negative material-obstacle realm. This is the evil/neutral blocking-mechanism cluster of Torah #172.", "dominant_node": "צל", "nodes": ["צל", "גשמיות", "מעשים רעים"], "node_degrees": {"צל": 3, "גשמיות": 2, "מעשים רעים": 1}, "source_basis": [{"quote": "הַצֵּל הוּא מִדָּבָר גַּשְׁמִי שֶׁעוֹמֵד נֶגֶד דָּבָר רוּחָנִי", "line": 2, "bechina_type": "definitional — shadow IS the physical standing against the spiritual"}, {"quote": "הָאָדָם כְּפִי גַּשְׁמִיּוּתוֹ וּכְפִי מַעֲשָׂיו, כֵּן עוֹשֶׂה צֵל", "line": 3, "bechina_type": "proportional equivalence — physicality and evil deeds = shadow"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 173, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וּפְנִימִיּוּת הַנֶּפֶשׁ", "label_en": "Faith and the Inwardness of the Soul", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire teaching of Torah #173 revolves around one central identification: the root of faith (שרש האמונה), the inwardness of faith (פנימיות האמונה), and the inwardness of the soul (פנימיות הנפש) are declared to be one and the same reality — 'הַנֶּפֶשׁ וְהָאֱמוּנָה הֵם בְּחִינָה אַחַת'. These three nodes form a tight bechina chain (edges 2503 and 2504). The כ' letter (אות כ\"ף) enters this cluster because the Rebbe states it 'hints at the bechina of Shekhinah Pnimiyut' (line 4) and the Etz Chaim confirms 'הַכֶּתֶר הִיא פְּנִימִיּוּת הַנֶּפֶשׁ' while 'הַכ' הִיא בְּחִינַת כֶּתֶר' (line 5, edge 1529). Thus כתר is a bechina of פנימיות הנפש by transitivity confirmed in the same text, and אות כ\"ף is a bechina of כתר. All five nodes share the same essential nature: the hidden, innermost dimension of the soul and faith, which writing (כתב) both expresses and reveals to the true tzaddik. This is a wholly positive/good domain — it concerns the innermost holiness of the Jewish soul.", "dominant_node": "פנימיות הנפש", "nodes": ["שרש האמונה", "פנימיות האמונה", "פנימיות הנפש", "כתר", "אות כ\"ף"], "node_degrees": {"פנימיות האמונה": 2, "שרש האמונה": 1, "פנימיות הנפש": 1, "כתר": 1, "אות כ\"ף": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אות כ\"ף", "כתר"], "reason": "Raw Component A (2 nodes) merged into the faith/soul cluster by shared nature. The Torah text (line 5) explicitly states 'הַכֶּתֶר הִיא פְּנִימִיּוּת הַנֶּפֶשׁ' — making כתר and פנימיות הנפש parallel aspects of the same thing. אות כ\"ף is a bechina of כתר (edge 1529). Therefore both אות כ\"ף and כתר share essential nature with the three faith/soul nodes. This is a shared-nature merge, not cause-effect: the letter כ', Keter, and the inwardness of the soul are all expressions of the same uppermost hidden dimension."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 175, "label": "בְּכִיָּה וּבִינָה וְשִׂמְחָה", "label_en": "Weeping, Binah, and Joy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #175 presents a single interlocking bechina chain in which weeping (בְּכִיָּה), the Divine Name of 63 (שֵׁם ס\"ג), the sefira of Binah (בִּינָה), the heart (לֵב), and joy (שִׂמְחָה) are all shown to share the same essential nature. The teaching's central bechina declarations are: (1) בְּכִיָּה corresponds to שֵׁם ס\"ג — proven by gematria (the word בְּכִיָּה equals the miluy of ס\"ג) and by the acronym (the letters בּ-כ-י-ה stand for 'בְּשִׁמְךָ יְגִילוּן כָּל הַיּוֹם', which encodes the Name-joy connection in the word itself); (2) שֵׁם ס\"ג IS in Binah — 'שֶׁהוּא בְּבִינָה', an identification drawn from Ari z\"l; (3) בִּינָה IS the heart — 'בִּינָה – לִבָּא', the classic Zoharic identification cited via the verse 'הַלֵּב מֵבִין'; (4) שִׂמְחָה IS [essentially] in the heart — 'עִקַּר הַשִּׂמְחָה הִיא בַּלֵּב', cited from Psalms 4:8 'נָתַתָּ שִׂמְחָה בְלִבִּי'. Because בְּכִיָּה = שֵׁם ס\"ג = בִּינָה = לֵב, and שִׂמְחָה IS the domain of לֵב = בִּינָה, all five concepts share the same essential kabbalistic locus: Binah/the heart. The practical teaching follows necessarily: the proper arousal of weeping must originate in joy, because both crying and joy belong to the same sefirotic domain (Binah/heart). They are not opposites but two expressions of the same inner reality. חֶדְוָה (gladness/delight) is merged into this package — see note below. Polarity is good throughout: all named concepts are positive spiritual states or holy kabbalistic structures (Binah is the mother-sefira of holiness; Shem 63 is the Name of divine chesed/rachamim; the heart as Binah is the seat of genuine spiritual feeling; joy and crying-from-joy are both positive spiritual achievements in Breslov teaching).", "dominant_node": "בינה", "nodes": ["בכיה", "בינה", "שם ס\"ג", "מילוי ס\"ג", "לב", "שמחה", "חדוה"], "node_degrees": {"בינה": 3, "לב": 2, "שם ס\"ג": 2, "בכיה": 2, "שמחה": 1, "מילוי ס\"ג": 1, "חדוה": 1}, "source_bechina_evidence": {"bechina_1": {"node1": "בינה", "node2": "שם ס\"ג", "formula": "explicit: 'שֶׁהוּא בְּבִינָה'", "source_quote": "כִּי בְּכִיָּה הוּא מִתְעוֹרֵר מִשֵּׁם ס\"ג, שֶׁהוּא בְּבִינָה"}, "bechina_2": {"node1": "בינה", "node2": "לב", "formula": "explicit nominal identity: 'בִּינָה – לִבָּא'", "source_quote": "כִּי בִּינָה – לִבָּא, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: הַלֵּב מֵבִין"}, "bechina_3": {"node1": "בכיה", "node2": "מילוי ס\"ג", "formula": "gematria identity: 'בְּגִימַטְרִיָּא הַמִּלּוּי שֶׁל ס\"ג'", "source_quote": "בְּכִיָּה בְּגִימַטְרִיָּא הַמִּלּוּי שֶׁל ס\"ג, כַּמּוּבָא בְּכִתְבֵי הָאֲרִ\"י"}, "bechina_4": {"node1": "שמחה", "node2": "לב", "formula": "essence statement: 'עִקַּר הַשִּׂמְחָה הִיא בַּלֵּב'", "source_quote": "וְעִקַּר הַשִּׂמְחָה הִיא בַּלֵּב, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: נָתַתָּ שִׂמְחָה בְלִבִּי"}, "bechina_5_implicit": {"node1": "שמחה", "node2": "חדוה", "formula": "implicit: hendiadys pair — used together as a compound expression naming the same state", "source_quote": "עִקַּר מַעֲלַת הַבְּכִיָה הוּא כְּשֶׁהִיא מֵחֲמַת שִׂמְחָה וְחֶדְוָה"}}, "note_on_merge": "חֶדְוָה (gladness/delight) is treated here as merged into Package 1 rather than as a separate 1-node isolated component. The merge is justified by shared nature: חֶדְוָה and שִׂמְחָה are used as a hendiadys pair in the Torah's opening clause — 'מֵחֲמַת שִׂמְחָה וְחֶדְוָה' — designating a single compound spiritual state. They share the essential nature of inner gladness/rejoicing, and neither is the cause of the other. The framework rule against cause-effect merges is not violated: this is a merge by shared nature (both are expressions of spiritual joy). The dominant node of the merged package is בִּינָה (3 direct bechina connections: to שֵׁם ס\"ג, to לֵב, and indirectly through לֵב to שִׂמְחָה and through שֵׁם ס\"ג to בְּכִיָּה/מִלּוּי ס\"ג).", "note_on_polarity": "All concepts in this package are oriented toward holiness and positive spiritual achievement. בִּינָה is the mother-sefira of holiness (Ima ila'ah). שֵׁם ס\"ג is the Divine Name associated with chesed and divine mercy. לֵב/heart is the seat of sincere spiritual feeling. בְּכִיָּה from joy is explicitly characterized as 'עִקַּר מַעֲלַת הַבְּכִיָּה' — the highest level of weeping. שִׂמְחָה is a cardinal Breslov virtue. There is no evil mirror package in this Torah — the teaching presents only the positive ideal, without naming the negative counterpart explicitly. The negative state (crying from sadness or crying NOT rooted in joy) is implied as the lesser form but not given a name or bechina structure in this teaching.", "note_on_single_package": "Torah #175 produces exactly one package because its bechina chain is a single unified cluster. All concepts connect through the hub nodes of בִּינָה and לֵב. The teaching is architecturally simple compared to longer LM1 teachings: it is a single conceptual argument with one destination (crying must come from joy) demonstrated through a single kabbalistic chain. There is no second independent domain of bechina-connected concepts."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 176, "label": "רוּחַ שְׁטוּת וְאֲוִיר", "label_en": "Spirit of Folly and Air", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The source text explicitly equates ruach shtut (the spirit of folly) with avir (air), using the direct identity statement 'הָרוּחַ הוּא הָאֲוִיר' — 'the spirit IS the air.' This is the bechina-language identifier: ruach shtut and avir share the same essential nature; they are not cause and effect but two names for the same thing. The teaching's entire physical mechanism — that the spirit of folly pushes outward seeking an empty space to escape through, just as physical air does — depends on this identity. Together these two concepts form the evil/negative domain cluster: the antagonist force that fills a person's heart and must be expelled. Ruach shtut is the dominant node (it is named in the title of the Torah and drives the entire argument); avir is its physical-nature bechina, explaining why it behaves as it does.", "dominant_node": "רוח שטות", "nodes": ["רוח שטות", "אויר"], "node_degrees": {"רוח שטות": 1, "אויר": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_note": "Both nodes derived from source text gap analysis (neither appears as a bechina-edge node in the dataset for ref 176). The bechina identity 'הָרוּחַ הוּא הָאֲוִיר' is explicitly stated in paragraph 2 of the source text. Ruach shtut already exists as a node in the broader dataset (e.g., Torah #1 Package 3), confirming it is a valid concept-level node."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 177, "label": "עָקֵב וְיַעֲקֹב", "label_en": "The Heel and Jacob", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three nodes share the essential nature of the humble, lowly dimension that is paradoxically exalted — the 'dal/akev' quality of Yaakov. The text states explicitly: 'יתגדל — תגי דל, שהוא תגי = כתר, דל = בחינת יעקב שהוא עקב בחינת דל.' The heel (עקב) is Jacob's essence — the lowest limb, the heel that trails behind — and that lowliness is the same as 'dal' (the poor one). The Kaddish word 'yitgadal' is parsed as תגי+דל, where 'dalet' specifically represents Jacob-as-heel-as-poor. These three concepts are different expressions of the same essential quality: humble smallness that is Israel's foundational identity and the basis of Kaddish's opening word.", "dominant_node": "דל", "nodes": ["עקב", "דל", "יעקב"], "node_degrees": {"עקב": 1, "דל": 2, "יעקב": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: {עקב, דל, יעקב} — 3 nodes, 2 edges (id=746: עקב↔דל; id=1370: דל↔יעקב). No merges needed; the component is fully formed from the dataset."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 177, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְרָצוֹן", "label_en": "Kingship and Will", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All three nodes share the essential nature of divine malkhut as rooted in ratzon (will/desire). The text teaches: 'מלכות הוא בחינת כבוד' (Tehilim 145:11); 'והוא בחינת רצון, כי רצונו של אדם זהו כבודו' (Yerushalmi Peah 1). The Kaddish phrase 'ויַמְלִיךְ מַלְכוּתֵהּ' is conditioned on a person submitting their ratzon entirely to God's ratzon — 'עיקר המלכות מהרצון.' These three concepts are structurally identical: kavod = the outward glory of the king; malkhut = the institution of kingship; ratzon = the inner volitional essence that makes both possible. They are three faces of one domain: the holy kingdom that is constituted and expanded by total submission of will to God.", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["מלכות", "כבוד", "רצון"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 1, "כבוד": 2, "רצון": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: {מלכות, כבוד, רצון} — 3 nodes, 2 edges (id=1443: מלכות↔כבוד; id=2778: כבוד↔רצון). No merges needed; component is fully formed."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 177, "label": "רֹאשׁ וּמֹחִין", "label_en": "Head and Brains", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes share the essential nature of the intellectual/spiritual consciousness dimension — the supreme faculty through which a true tzaddik receives holy awareness and can atone for sin. The text states: 'כשנעשה ראש שהוא בחינות מחין, על-ידי-זה הוא יכול לכפר עוונות.' The rosh is not merely a physical head but the spiritual state of 'mochin' — expanded holy consciousness, described also as 'שמן משחת קדש' and 'כשמן הטוב על הראש.' Rosh and mochin are directly equated in the text as the same bechina: being a rosh = having mochin. This is the apex of the Torah's vertical axis — the faculty that enables everything else.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["ראש", "מחין"], "node_degrees": {"ראש": 1, "מחין": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina component: {ראש, מחין} — 2 nodes, 1 edge (id=1741: ראש↔מחין). This is a small isolated pair.", "Merge candidate considered: Could ראש/מחין merge with מלכות/כבוד/רצון? Rejected — they operate in different domains. Mochin/rosh = the cognitive/spiritual faculty; malkhut/ratzon = the volitional/royal dimension. These are not the same essential nature; they stand in causal relation (mochin → capacity for kavod/ratzon alignment) rather than parallel identity.", "Merge candidate considered: Could ראש/מחין merge with עקב/דל/יעקב? Rejected — opposite ends of the vertical axis. עקב/דל = the low/humble; ראש/מחין = the high/expanded. Their relationship is structural (head vs. heel of the same body = the full Kaddish: יתגדל is about the dalet/heel while רבא relates to mochin), not shared nature.", "Verdict: Keep as standalone 2-node package. The ראש/מחין pair is a genuine, self-contained conceptual unit central to the Torah's opening argument."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 178, "label": "נֶּפֶשׁ וְדִבּוּר", "label_en": "The Soul and Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single bechina of Torah #178 is the identity of nefesh (soul) with dibur (speech): 'וְהַנֶּפֶשׁ הוּא בְּחִינַת הַדִּבּוּר, בִּבְחִינַת נַפְשִׁי יָצְאָה בְדַבְּרוֹ.' This is the theological foundation of the entire Torah: because the soul IS speech, the damage of sin is located in speech, and therefore verbal confession (viduy devarim) is precisely the repair mechanism for the nefesh. Both nodes are expressions of the same spiritual reality — the innermost voice of a person. This is a positive (good) package because the Torah is teaching the path of repair through confession: the nefesh-as-dibur IS the sacred domain that can be restored through holy speech. The nefesh here is not damaged or evil; it is the holy vessel that needs healing, and dibur is the means of that healing because they share the same essential nature.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["נפש", "דבור"], "node_degrees": {"נפש": 1, "דבור": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is the sole bechina-connected component in Torah #178. No other components exist in the dataset to merge with. The pair is retained as its own standalone package. Per the algorithm, small components (1-2 nodes) are candidates for merging into larger components based on SHARED NATURE — but with no other component present, this pair is Package 1 by default.", "Note: The Torah's broader conceptual structure (simcha, mitzvot, viduy, kol, yichud) operates through eitza (directional cause-and-effect) chains. The simcha cluster and viduy cluster are eitza-connected to the dibur node but do not share a bechina (parallel-nature) relationship with it as represented in the dataset for Torah #178. See torah_178_additions.json for gap analysis."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 179, "label": "נֶפֶשׁ וּמַחֲלֹקֶת — הָרָצוֹן", "label_en": "Soul and Strife — The Will", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All four nodes in this package share the essential nature of רָצוֹן (will/desire) as their common denominator. The Rebbe explicitly states: 'נֶפֶשׁ הוּא בְּחִינַת רָצוֹן' (soul is an aspect of will) in the context of Yom Kippur, where 'afflict your souls' means subdue your will. Simultaneously he equates מַחֲלֹקֶת with 'רְצוֹנוֹת אֲחֵרִים שֶׁלֹּא כִּרְצוֹנוֹ' — strife is nothing other than alien wills opposing one's will. The package is marked neutral because the concept of רָצוֹן itself is structurally neutral: it can be one's own holy will (oriented toward God, expressed as נֶפֶשׁ), or the alien wills of others (expressed as מַחֲלֹקֶת). The teaching's central pivot is precisely this identification: fasting subdues the נֶפֶשׁ/רָצוֹן, which thereby annuls the רְצוֹנוֹת אֲחֵרִים/מַחֲלֹקֶת.", "dominant_node": "רצון", "nodes": ["נפש", "רצון", "מחלקת", "רצונות אחרים שלא כרצונו"], "node_degrees": {"נפש": 1, "רצון": 1, "מחלקת": 1, "רצונות אחרים שלא כרצונו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw Component A {נפש, רצון} (edge ID 2777) and Raw Component B {מחלקת, רצונות אחרים שלא כרצונו} (edge ID 2781) merged on shared nature.", "Both components are expressions of the same underlying domain: the nature of רָצוֹן (will). Component A defines the positive pole: soul = will, the inner orientation of the person. Component B defines the negative pole: strife = alien/other wills. These are not cause-and-effect — they are two faces of the same structural reality of 'will'. The Rebbe's entire argument rests on this identification: fasting subdues the נֶפֶשׁ (= רָצוֹן), and this is exactly what nullifies מַחֲלֹקֶת (= רְצוֹנוֹת אֲחֵרִים). Same essential nature, different orientation.", "The Zohar passage cited (Acharei 68) confirms the identity: 'תְּעַנּוּ אֶת נַפְשֹׁתֵיכֶם' means to subdue the רָצוֹן — both נֶפֶשׁ and מַחֲלֹקֶת are defined in terms of רָצוֹן in the same passage. This shared bechina-definition justifies the merge."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 179, "label": "צְדָקָה וְתַעֲנִית", "label_en": "Charity and Fasting", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two nodes share the essential nature of the spiritual practice of self-denial and giving from oneself. The Rebbe equates them explicitly via the Talmudic statement (Berakhot 6): 'אַגְרָא דְּתַעֲנִיתָא צִדְקְתָא' — the reward/merit of fasting is charity. He explains: the essential quality of fasting IS charity (צְדָקָה הוּא בְּחִינַת תַּעֲנִית). Both express the same underlying act: withdrawing from self-gratification and redirecting toward a higher good — in fasting, one withholds food from oneself; in charity, one withholds money for oneself and gives to another. Both are the same essential practice of self-transcendence in service of the higher will. The polarity is good because both are positive spiritual practices in the Torah's framework.", "dominant_node": "תענית", "nodes": ["צדקה", "תענית"], "node_degrees": {"צדקה": 1, "תענית": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 180, "label": "מָעוֹת וּמַלְכוּתָא קַדִּישָׁא", "label_en": "Money and the Holy Kingdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #180 is a single tight chain of bechina-equivalences all serving one teaching: money (מָעוֹת) is a bechina of feet (רַגְלִין), which is a bechina of tzedek (צֶדֶק), which is the holy malkhut (מַלְכוּתָא קַדִּישָׁא), which is a bechina of judgments (דִּינִין). The entire network forms one cycle: ממון—דין—מלכות—צדק—רגלים—ממון. All five nodes describe the same spiritual structure — the domain of divine judgment/kingship as it manifests in the material world through money. The polarity is 'good' because: (1) the node2 of the צדק↔מלכות edge is explicitly 'מַלְכוּתָא קַדִּישָׁא' (holy kingdom); (2) the teaching aims to demonstrate how to SWEETEN judgments, not to describe the evil side; (3) the feet/tzedek/malkhut cluster is the redemptive channel through which judgments are returned to their root in Binah. The judgments (דינין) are not presented as an evil force to be defeated but as a spiritual reality whose root is holy (in Binah), and the whole chain explains the mechanism of pidyon as a spiritual practice.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "צדק", "רגלים", "ממון", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 2, "צדק": 2, "רגלים": 2, "ממון": 2, "דין": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 181, "label": "יַעֲקֹב וְהַכָּבוֹד", "label_en": "Jacob and Honor", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire teaching of Torah #181 is built on the identity declared in line 6: Jacob is a bechina of honor (כָּבוֹד), because Jacob is a bechina of the soul (נֶפֶשׁ). The verse cited — 'כָּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַבָּאָה לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב' (Gen. 46) — grounds both equalities in scripture: Jacob's house is where the nefesh comes home, therefore Jacob = nefesh = kavod. The teaching's entire argument about honor (the conspiracy against a leader works by aggregating collective shares of honor; righteousness preserves one's share of honor; falling spiritually means losing one's portion of honor and falling into lust) depends on kavod as a spiritual-ontological quality. Kavod is the dominant node: it appears 9 times in the 8-line text and drives the whole argument. Jacob is the biblical embodiment of kavod and nefesh — Jacob prays (line 5: 'אַל תֵּחַד כְּבוֹדִי') specifically to protect Moses from the aggregated honor of Korach's assembly. Nefesh is the metaphysical ground: having vitality from the nefesh means having a share in kavod (line 4). All three nodes share the same essential nature — the spiritual dimension of a person that is connected to holiness, honor, and the soul, and which is diminished or protected in the cosmic struggle between Torah leadership and opposition. This is wholly a good/holy domain: the nefesh-kavod-Yaakov cluster represents the positive pole of the teaching (the tzaddik's spiritual stature that must be preserved), in direct contrast to the evil pole (the conspiracy of the wicked, and falling to lust — line 7).", "dominant_node": "כבוד", "nodes": ["יעקב", "כבוד", "נפש"], "node_degrees": {"יעקב": 2, "כבוד": 1, "נפש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 183, "label": "הַצַּדִּיקִים בְּעִגּוּל — בֵּית דִּין", "label_en": "The Tzaddikim in a Circle — The Court", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two concepts share the same essential nature: the tzaddikim who sit in a circle in the world ARE the heavenly court — they are not the cause of the court but they ARE the court. The teaching states explicitly: 'those tzaddikim who sit in a circle are the aspect of a court (beit din), from which go out all judgments and all sustenance.' The formation (the circle) and the function (judgment + sustenance) are both inherent to the same reality — the tzaddikim-as-beit-din. Both are positive, holy expressions of the guiding, sustaining principle that governs the world.", "dominant_node": "בית דין", "nodes": ["אלו הצדיקים שיושבים בעגול", "בית דין"], "node_degrees": {"אלו הצדיקים שיושבים בעגול": 1, "בית דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 183, "label": "אַהֲבָה וּרְאִיָּה", "label_en": "Love and Vision", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes — אהבה (love), ראיה (vision/seeing), אוהבים זה את זה (loving one another), and רואים זה את זה (seeing one another) — express the same single essential insight: love and seeing are the same thing. The teaching defines love precisely as 'seeing one another' and then argues both ways: strong love = you cannot bear not to see your companion; even distant love = you love from afar = a form of seeing. The opposite of love is the idiom 'I cannot look at him.' Love IS seeing; seeing IS love. The two dataset components (אהבה ↔ ראיה and אוהבים זה את זה ↔ רואים זה את זה) are the same bechina stated twice — once abstractly and once in its inter-personal application. They share identical essential nature and must be merged into one package. Polarity is good: love and vision are qualities of holiness and of the tzaddikim's bond.", "dominant_node": "אהבה", "nodes": ["אהבה", "ראיה", "אוהבים זה את זה", "רואים זה את זה"], "node_degrees": {"אהבה": 1, "ראיה": 1, "אוהבים זה את זה": 1, "רואים זה את זה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["אהבה", "ראיה", "אוהבים זה את זה", "רואים זה את זה"], "reason": "The dataset has two separate 2-node components: (1) אהבה ↔ ראיה (edge 2653) and (2) אוהבים זה את זה ↔ רואים זה את זה (edge 2680). These two components express the same essential bechina — love = seeing — at two levels of abstraction: the abstract principle (love / vision) and the interpersonal application (loving one another / seeing one another). The source text explicitly links them: 'and this is the aspect of seeing one another, which is the aspect of love as stated above' (line 5). אוהבים זה את זה is simply the applied form of אהבה, and רואים זה את זה is simply the applied form of ראיה. There is no cause-and-effect relation between the pairs — they are all expressions of the same underlying reality. Merging is mandated by shared essential nature."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 185, "label": "יִרְאָה וְאוֹת אָלֶ\"ף", "label_en": "Fear of God and the Letter Alef", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah #185 revolves entirely around the upper yir'ah (fear arising from intellectual contemplation of God's greatness), which the Rebbe declares to be a bechina of the letter alef (א). This alef is itself composed of three structural elements: the yud (sekhel/intellect, the top point of the letter), the vav (the diagonal — the 'drawing' or המשכה from sekhel to yir'ah), and the dalet (the lower arm — yir'ah itself, which is bechina of malkhut, the receptive kingdom that has nothing of its own). All five nodes in this package are aspects of this single conceptual structure: the holy upper fear that completes the divine Name. The package is unambiguously positive — it describes the ideal form of yir'ah that the Zohar says completes God's holy name when Israel achieves it.", "dominant_node": "יראה", "nodes": ["יראה", "מלכות", "אות דל\"ת", "המשכה מהשכל להיראה", "אות וא\"ו"], "node_degrees": {"יראה": 2, "מלכות": 1, "אות דל\"ת": 1, "המשכה מהשכל להיראה": 1, "אות וא\"ו": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Raw Component A (3 nodes): {יראה, אות דל\"ת, מלכות} — connected via edges יראה↔אות דל\"ת and יראה↔מלכות.", "Raw Component B (2 nodes): {המשכה מהשכל להיראה, אות וא\"ו} — isolated pair connected via המשכה מהשכל להיראה↔אות וא\"ו.", "Merge justification: Both components are sub-structures of the letter alef (א), explicitly equated in the source text with יראה ראשונה / יראה עליונה. The Torah states: 'יִרְאָה הָרִאשׁוֹנָה... הִיא בְּחִינוֹת א. כִּי הַיִּרְאָה הִיא בְּחִינַת מַלְכוּת... וְהִיא בְּחִינוֹת דָּלֶת... וְהַשֵּׂכֶל נִקְרָא יוּד... וְהַהַמְשָׁכָה מֵהַשֵּׂכֶל לְהַיִּרְאָה הוּא בְּחִינַת וָאו. וְזֶה א.' The vav (Component B) and the dalet/malkhut (Component A) are both named aspects of the same alef-structure. They share the nature of being components of the upper yir'ah, not cause-and-effect stages.", "No evil mirror package: Torah #185 does not develop an evil counterpart. The second (lower) yir'ah is described as a lower-grade positive phenomenon, not an evil one — it is still a valid path to yir'ah, simply drawing from lower sources rather than from sekhel. It is not classified as evil but as a lesser grade of the same good."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 186, "label": "דִּבְרֵי הַצַּדִּיק וּנְבוּאָה", "label_en": "The Tzaddik's Words and Prophecy", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's central structural insight is that the tzaddik's words (דברי הצדיק) share the same essential nature as prophecy (נבואה): both are communicated in remez (hint/allusion), both are not understood at the time they are spoken, and both are understood retroactively (lemafrea) only after the reality they hint at has been fulfilled. The teaching does not frame this as tzaddik producing prophecy or prophecy causing the tzaddik's words — it explicitly says 'וְכֵן מָצִינוּ אֵצֶל הַנְּבִיאִים' (and so we find with the prophets), establishing that the two phenomena share the same essential structure. The tzaddik's word IS a kind of prophecy; prophecy IS the paradigm of which the tzaddik's word is the living instance. The three nodes (דברי הצדיק, נבואה, רמז) are all expressions of the same underlying reality: hidden truth embedded in allusive speech that is decoded only after the fact. רמז (hint/allusion) is the shared essential medium — it is both what the tzaddik's words ARE and what prophecy IS. Daniel's sealed words are the explicit example cited, establishing that even the most obscure prophetic hints share this same bechina. All concepts are positive, belonging to the domain of tzaddikim and prophets who communicate divine truth.", "dominant_node": "נבואה", "nodes": ["דברי הצדיק", "נבואה", "רמז"], "node_degrees": {"נבואה": 2, "דברי הצדיק": 1, "רמז": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_bechina_evidence": {"bechina_1": {"node1": "דברי הצדיק", "node2": "נבואה", "formula": "structural parallel: 'וְכֵן מָצִינוּ אֵצֶל הַנְּבִיאִים' — 'and so we find with the prophets' — the tzaddik's mode of speech is explicitly equated to the prophets' mode", "source_quote": "וְכֵן מָצִינוּ אֵצֶל הַנְּבִיאִים, שֶׁבְּעֵת שֶׁאָמַר הַנָּבִיא הַנְּבוּאָה הָיְתָה בְּרֶמֶז"}, "bechina_2": {"node1": "נבואה", "node2": "רמז", "formula": "identity statement: 'הָיְתָה בְּרֶמֶז' — the prophecy WAS in hint/allusion — remez is the essential nature of prophecy", "source_quote": "בְּעֵת שֶׁאָמַר הַנָּבִיא הַנְּבוּאָה הָיְתָה בְּרֶמֶז"}}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 186, "label": "אֱמוּנָה בַּצַּדִּיק וְמוֹפְתִים", "label_en": "Faith in the Tzaddik and Miracles", "polarity": "good", "reason": "אמונה שהם מאמינים בדברי הצדיק (the faith of the believers in the tzaddik's words) and מופתים (miracles) are presented in the opening of the teaching as a pair whose relationship the teaching seeks to explain. The edge in the dataset (id 1720) classifies this as eitza — faith PRODUCES the revelation of miracles — which is correct for the causal direction. However, examining the teaching more closely, there is also a bechina dimension: the teaching states 'כִּי בֶּאֱמֶת הַצַּדִּיק בְּוַדַּאי מָלֵא מוֹפְתִים' (for in truth the tzaddik is certainly full of miracles) — this establishes that the tzaddik's nature IS miracle: he does not produce miracles from outside himself, he IS full of miracles. The faith does not create the miracles (they pre-exist in the tzaddik); faith REVEALS what is already there. The אמונה and the מופתים are both expressions of the same hidden reality that is already present in the tzaddik. This note documents the bechina dimension: צדיק (tzaddik) shares essential nature with מלא מופתים (fullness of miracles) — the tzaddik IS the miraculous. However, since the dataset edge 1720 already captures the eitza relationship between אמונה and מופתים, and the bechina of צדיק = מלא מופתים is the more precise statement of the teaching, this package is proposed to capture the bechina side. Polarity: good.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "מלא מופתים"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 1, "מלא מופתים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"note": "The dataset edge 1720 captures the eitza relationship אמונה → מופתים. This package proposes the bechina dimension: צדיק = מלא מופתים (the tzaddik's essential nature is to be full of miracles). The concept אמונה belongs in the eitza flow (it is the cause that reveals, not a parallel expression of the same thing as מופתים). The bechina proposed here is narrower and more precise: the tzaddik himself shares essential nature with the miraculous."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 188, "label": "שִׁכְחָה וַאֲבֵדָה", "label_en": "Forgetting and the Lost Item", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "שכחה (forgetting) and אבדה (the lost item / being in a state of loss) are declared explicitly equal by the Rebbe: 'הַשִּׁכְחָה הִיא בְּחִינַת אֲבֵדָה'. They are two aspects of the same spiritual reality — the condition of having lost one's pre-birth knowledge upon entering the world. שכחה is the mechanism (the act of forgetting); אבדה is the resulting state (being lost, having lost something). Neither produces the other — they are the same phenomenon viewed from different angles, making this a classic bechina. Polarity is neutral rather than evil: this is the natural human condition described in Niddah 30b, not an adversarial force. It is the problem the Torah addresses — the universal human amnesia about one's spiritual task — but it is not labeled as evil (sitra achra). The tzaddik's role (eitza, not bechina) is to reverse this state.", "dominant_node": "אבדה", "nodes": ["שכחה", "אבדה"], "node_degrees": {"שכחה": 1, "אבדה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is the only connected component in the dataset for Torah #188 (1 bechina edge total). There is nothing to merge with — no other components exist in the current dataset.", "The 2-node component is retained as-is. Both nodes share identical essential nature: the spiritual amnesia that afflicts every person from birth. No merger is possible or needed.", "The dominant node is designated as אבדה rather than שכחה because the entire teaching is structured around the concept of the אבדה — the lost item that must be recovered. שכחה is introduced as an explanation of what kind of thing the אבדה is; אבדה is the primary concept driving the teaching's practical directive (travel to the tzaddik to recover your loss)."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 189, "label": "עָפָר וְעַצְבוּת — יְסוֹד הַנְּשִׁיכָה", "label_en": "Dust and Sadness — The Foundation of the Bite", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Both עָפָר (dust) and עַצְבוּת וְעַצְלוּת (sadness and laziness) are expressions of the same evil, spiritually deadening reality. The teaching states explicitly: 'עָפָר הִיא בְּחִינַת עַצְבוּת וְעַצְלוּת' — dust IS the aspect of sadness and laziness, because sadness and laziness originate in the foundation of dust (יְסוֹד הֶעָפָר). Dust is the material substrate of spiritual deadness; sadness and laziness are its experiential expression in the soul. Neither produces the other — they are two faces of the same downward, earthbound reality. The entire teaching frames this cluster as the evil force to be avoided: 'one must be very careful' (צָרִיךְ לִזָּהֵר מְאֹד) from these things. Polarity is evil: dust, sadness, and laziness together constitute the domain of the snake — the sitra achra's primary weapon against spiritual life.", "dominant_node": "עצבות ועצלות", "nodes": ["עפר", "עצבות ועצלות"], "node_degrees": {"עפר": 1, "עצבות ועצלות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 191, "label": "הַלֵּב וְהָאֱלֹקוּת — הָאֵין סוֹף שֶׁבִּפְנִים", "label_en": "The Heart and Divinity — The Inner Infinity", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's central structural insight is that the heart (לב) and the brain/mind (מוח) are parallel expressions of the same inner capacity — together they form the faculty that contains infinite reality. The teaching then grounds this infinite capacity in a single cause: Divinity (אלקות). Line 4 states explicitly: 'וְזֶה רַק מֵחֲמַת שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם הָאֱלֹקוּת' — 'and this is only because the Divinity is found there.' This is an essence statement: the heart's unbounded capacity IS Divinity; Divinity IS what the heart essentially contains. Line 5 deepens this: 'אִם בְּהַלֵּב, שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם רַק בְּחִינוֹת אֱלֹקוּת' — 'if in the heart, where there are only aspects of Divinity (bechinot elokut)' — using the explicit bechina-formula word 'בְּחִינוֹת', declaring the heart's contents to be aspects/dimensions of Divinity. The heart (לב) and the mind/brain (מוח) are treated throughout the teaching as a single inner faculty — lines 3 and 4 link them together structurally as the small physical vessel that contains the infinite. They are not presented as cause and effect (the brain does not produce the heart, nor vice versa); they are co-expressions of the same inner spiritual faculty. Similarly, the 'heart of kings' (לב מלכים) cited from Proverbs 25 is used as the paradigm of this infinite inner capacity — it is the same thing as 'לב' (the heart of anyone who has Divinity in it). The אין חקר (unsearchability/infinity) is not something the heart produces — it IS the heart's essential nature, stated three times in the text as an identity ('לב מלכים אין חקר'). All four nodes — לב, מוח, אלקות, and לב מלכים/אין חקר — are parallel aspects of the same spiritual reality: the infinite inner capacity rooted in Divinity's presence within the human person. Polarity is unambiguously good: this package describes the domain of holiness, Divinity, and the soul's boundless capacity for spiritual reality.", "dominant_node": "לב", "nodes": ["לב", "מוח", "אלקות", "לב מלכים"], "node_degrees": {"לב": 3, "מוח": 1, "אלקות": 2, "לב מלכים": 2}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina cluster from source text analysis — no pre-existing dataset edges to merge. All four nodes are derived from direct reading of Torah #191.", "לב and מוח: Lines 3-4 treat heart and brain as a single inner faculty that together 'contain everything' (תּוֹפְסִים הַכֹּל). They are described in the same breath: 'הַלֵּב וְהַמֹּחַ שֶׁקְּטַנִּים מְאֹד בִּמְקוֹמָם, הֵם תּוֹפְסִים הַכֹּל'. They are not cause and effect (the brain does not cause the heart's capacity); they are co-expressions of the same capacity — both small physically, both infinite in spiritual content. The bechina is symmetric: heart = mind as the inner faculty of containing.", "לב and אלקות: Line 4 states 'וְזֶה רַק מֵחֲמַת שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם הָאֱלֹקוּת' — the heart's infinite capacity IS Divinity's presence. Line 5 uses 'בְּחִינוֹת אֱלֹקוּת' explicitly — the heart contains 'aspects of Divinity.' The heart's essential nature is to be the vessel/expression of Divinity; Divinity's essential presence in the human being IS the heart. This is a genuine bechina, not an eitza: Divinity does not produce the heart's capacity as a separate downstream effect — Divinity IS the heart's inner nature.", "לב and לב מלכים: The teaching uses 'לב מלכים אין חקר' (Proverbs 25) three times as the definitive characterization of the heart's infinite nature. The verse is not cited as a cause that produces the heart's capacity — it IS the identity statement: the heart of kings IS unsearchable, meaning the heart's essential quality IS אין חקר (infinity/unsearchability). 'לב מלכים' is the paradigmatic expression of 'לב' — both refer to the same inner faculty, with 'מלכים' (kings) representing those who fully actualize the heart's infinite capacity (as line 8 explains: the king's greatness is entirely in the heart's knowledge of its dominion).", "No evil mirror: Torah #191 does not develop an explicit evil counterpart. The teaching presents the contrast between the צדיק whose heart is full (all 310 worlds, all pleasures) and the person whose heart is empty (sits next to him but feels nothing). However, this contrast is expressed through the eitza/narrative structure of the teaching — it is not developed into a named bechina cluster for the evil domain. The empty-hearted person is not given a set of parallel bechina concepts; he is simply the negative case used to highlight the positive reality. Consistent with other short aphoristic teachings in LM that present the positive ideal without structuring the negative into a separate bechina family."], "source_bechina_evidence": {"bechina_1": {"node1": "לב", "node2": "מוח", "formula": "co-expression: 'הַלֵּב וְהַמֹּחַ שֶׁקְּטַנִּים מְאֹד בִּמְקוֹמָם, הֵם תּוֹפְסִים הַכֹּל' — heart and brain together constitute the single inner faculty that contains all", "source_quote": "הַלֵּב וְהַמֹּחַ שֶׁקְּטַנִּים מְאֹד בִּמְקוֹמָם, הֵם תּוֹפְסִים הַכֹּל"}, "bechina_2": {"node1": "לב", "node2": "אלקות", "formula": "explicit bechina-formula: 'שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם רַק בְּחִינוֹת אֱלֹקוּת' — the heart's content IS aspects/dimensions of Divinity; the word 'בְּחִינוֹת' (aspects/dimensions — the core bechina vocabulary) is used explicitly", "source_quote": "אִם בְּהַלֵּב, שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם רַק בְּחִינוֹת אֱלֹקוּת"}, "bechina_3": {"node1": "לב", "node2": "לב מלכים", "formula": "identity through repetition: 'לֵב מְלָכִים אֵין חֵקֶר' stated three times as the defining identity of the heart's infinite nature — not an analogy but the name of the heart's essential quality", "source_quote": "כִּי לֵב מְלָכִים אֵין חֵקֶר"}, "bechina_4": {"node1": "אלקות", "node2": "לב", "formula": "causal essence statement: 'וְזֶה רַק מֵחֲמַת שֶׁנִּמְצָא שָׁם הָאֱלֹקוּת. כִּי עִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת הוּא בְּהַלֵּב' — Divinity's essential place IS the heart; this is an identity statement, not causal production", "source_quote": "כִּי עִקַּר הָאֱלֹקוּת הוּא בְּהַלֵּב"}}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 191, "label": "עוֹלָם הַבָּא וְשַׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת — הַתַּעֲנוּגִים שֶׁבַּלֵּב", "label_en": "The World to Come and 310 Worlds — The Inner Pleasures", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching's opening and closing frame the same cluster of concepts: תענוגים ושעשועים (pleasures and delights), שי\"ט עולמות (310 worlds), and עולם הבא (World to Come). These are presented as parallel expressions of the same spiritual reality — not as cause-and-effect stages. Line 1 introduces them as a single unit: 'כָּל הַתַּעֲנוּגִים וְהַשַּׁעֲשׁוּעִים וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת' — pleasures, delights, and 310 worlds are listed together as the content of the full heart. Line 10 closes with the definitive identity statement: 'עִקַּר הַרְגָּשַׁת תַּעֲנוּג וְשַׁעֲשוּעַ עוֹלָם הַבָּא וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת – הַכֹּל הוּא בַּלֵּב' — 'the essential feeling of pleasure and delight of the World to Come and all the 310 worlds — all of it is in the heart.' This is an explicit identity/bechina statement: the World to Come IS what is experienced as pleasure in the heart; the 310 worlds ARE the pleasures and delights. They do not produce one another sequentially — they are three names for the same spiritual content that fills the heart of the tzaddik. The dominant node is עולם הבא because it is the eschatological frame (what awaits the righteous) and because the teaching's apex statement ('אַשְׁרֵי לוֹ' — praiseworthy is he) concludes by identifying עולם הבא as the content of the full heart. Polarity is good: these are entirely positive concepts belonging to the domain of holiness, the righteous, and spiritual reward.", "dominant_node": "עולם הבא", "nodes": ["תענוגים ושעשועים", "שי\"ט עולמות", "עולם הבא"], "node_degrees": {"עולם הבא": 2, "תענוגים ושעשועים": 2, "שי\"ט עולמות": 2}, "merge_notes": ["Raw bechina cluster from source text analysis — no pre-existing dataset edges. All three nodes co-appear in lines 1, 9, and 10 as parallel descriptors of the same spiritual content.", "תענוגים ושעשועים and שי\"ט עולמות: Listed together four times in the text (lines 1, 9, 10 and implicitly in line 5 'עוֹלָמוֹת אֵין מִסְפָּר'). They are not cause and effect — they co-describe the same reality. The '310 worlds' (שי\"ט עולמות — from the gematria of the word שי\"ט) is a Kabbalistic idiom for the complete spiritual inheritance of the righteous; 'pleasures and delights' is the experiential descriptor of that inheritance. They are two faces of the same spiritual content.", "עולם הבא and תענוגים ושעשועים: Line 10 explicitly equates them: 'עִקַּר הַרְגָּשַׁת תַּעֲנוּג וְשַׁעֲשוּעַ עוֹלָם הַבָּא' — 'the essential feeling of pleasure and delight of the World to Come.' The World to Come IS essentially experienced as pleasure and delight; pleasure and delight IS the experiential essence of the World to Come. Neither produces the other — they are the same reality described from different angles.", "Connection to Package 1: Packages 1 and 2 are connected through the node לב (the heart). Package 1 describes what the heart IS (Divinity, infinite capacity); Package 2 describes what the heart CONTAINS (the pleasures/310 worlds/World to Come). The flow between them is eitza: the heart's infinite capacity (Package 1) enables the possession of all the pleasures and worlds (Package 2). This is a cross-package eitza, not a within-package bechina — correctly keeping the two packages separate."], "source_bechina_evidence": {"bechina_1": {"node1": "תענוגים ושעשועים", "node2": "שי\"ט עולמות", "formula": "co-listing as identical content: listed together four times as the parallel description of the same spiritual possession ('כָּל הַתַּעֲנוּגִים וְהַשַּׁעֲשׁוּעִים וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת')", "source_quote": "כָּל הַתַּעֲנוּגִים וְהַשַּׁעֲשׁוּעִים וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת"}, "bechina_2": {"node1": "עולם הבא", "node2": "תענוגים ושעשועים", "formula": "identity statement: 'עִקַּר הַרְגָּשַׁת תַּעֲנוּג וְשַׁעֲשוּעַ עוֹלָם הַבָּא' — the essential feeling of the World to Come IS pleasure and delight", "source_quote": "עִקַּר הַרְגָּשַׁת תַּעֲנוּג וְשַׁעֲשוּעַ עוֹלָם הַבָּא וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת – הַכֹּל הוּא בַּלֵּב"}, "bechina_3": {"node1": "שי\"ט עולמות", "node2": "עולם הבא", "formula": "co-identity: in the closing statement (line 10), all three — pleasures, the World to Come, and 310 worlds — are declared to be one and the same thing ('הַכֹּל הוּא בַּלֵּב' — all of it is in the heart)", "source_quote": "עוֹלָם הַבָּא וְכָל הַשַּׁ\"י עוֹלָמוֹת – הַכֹּל הוּא בַּלֵּב"}}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 192, "label": "נְשָׁמָה וְשֵׂכֶל", "label_en": "Soul and Intellect — The Tzaddik's Inner Essence", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dataset contains exactly one bechina edge for Torah #192: נשמה ↔ חכמה (soul = sekhel/intellect), stated in line 3: 'הַנְּשָׁמָה הִיא בְּחִינַת הַשֵּׂכֶל כְּמָה שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְנִשְׁמַת שַׁדַּי תְּבִינֵם'. These two nodes share the essential nature of the inner light of the tzaddik that is transmitted through speech. The Rebbe equates soul and intellect because both refer to the same non-physical inner substance of the sage — when a student receives the tzaddik's speech, he receives 'his intellect (שכלו) and his soul (נשמתו)' which are one and the same thing (line 2-3). This equation is also the foundation of the teaching's central claim: the face (פנים), the intellect (שכל), and the soul (נשמה) of the tzaddik are transmitted as a single unit through true speech. Both nodes are expressions of the same divine inner quality — the tzaddik's essence that can be received by the student.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["נשמה", "חכמה"], "node_degrees": {"נשמה": 1, "חכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This is the only connected component in the Torah #192 bechina graph. No merges are applicable — there is only one edge and two nodes.", "NOTE: This package represents only 1 of the ~7 bechina relationships explicitly stated in the Torah's source text. The dataset for Torah #192 is severely under-represented in bechina edges. See torah_192_additions.json for the full gap analysis."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 193, "label": "מַחֲשָׁבָה וּמְצִיאוּת בְּפֹעַל", "label_en": "Thought and Actual Reality", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The central declaration of Torah #193 is that there is no difference between actual death and the pain of death felt through intense thought: 'אֵין הֶפְרֵשׁ בֵּין הַמִּיתָה בְּפֹעַל לְהַצַּעַר שֶׁמַּרְגִּישׁ מֵהַמִּיתָה בְּמַחֲשָׁבָה'. This is a classic bechina declaration — the teaching equates מחשבה (thought / mental reality) with מציאות בפועל (actual physical reality). They are not cause and effect; thought does not produce reality here — they ARE the same thing at the level of experience. The Rebbe opens with the premise that if one strengthens thought on any matter it will happen ('יוּכַל לִפְעֹל שֶׁיִּהְיֶה כָךְ'), but this is presented not as causation but as identity: the thought, when fully intensified, is the thing itself. The polarity is neutral because thought (מחשבה) is a faculty that can be directed toward any domain — good (martyrdom for God's name) or harmful (excessive wealth-seeking). The concept of מחשבה here is the universal mechanism, not inherently holy or evil.", "dominant_node": "מחשבה", "nodes": ["מחשבה", "מציאות בפועל", "צער המיתה במחשבה", "מיתה בפועל"], "node_degrees": {"מחשבה": 3, "מציאות בפועל": 1, "צער המיתה במחשבה": 2, "מיתה בפועל": 2}, "merge_notes": ["This package is derived entirely from the source text — there are no bechina edges in torahData.js for reference 193 to serve as the algorithmic basis.", "The bechina equation in the source text: 'אֵין הֶפְרֵשׁ בֵּין הַמִּיתָה בְּפֹעַל לְהַצַּעַר שֶׁמַּרְגִּישׁ מֵהַמִּיתָה בְּמַחֲשָׁבָה' — 'there is no difference between actual death and the pain felt from death through thought.' This is the primary bechina of the Torah: מיתה בפועל ↔ צער המיתה במחשבה.", "The opening statement — 'if one strengthens thought on any matter one can make it happen' — generalizes the same identity: מחשבה תקיפה ↔ מציאות בפועל. The mechanism is not causation but ontological identity: fully-actualized thought IS reality.", "These four concepts form one connected cluster because they all express the same essential nature: the identity of mental reality with physical reality. No evil mirror exists in this Torah — the text does not discuss a negative counterpart to thought-power."]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 193, "label": "מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ וְקִדּוּשׁ הַשֵּׁם", "label_en": "Martyrdom and Sanctification of God's Name", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The specific application of the thought-reality identity in this Torah is מסירת נפש על קידוש השם (self-sacrifice for the sanctification of God's name). The text explicitly links: קבלת מסירת נפש (the acceptance of martyrdom) ↔ ארבע מיתות בית דין (the four court-decreed deaths accepted by Rabbi Akiva during Shema) ↔ קריאת שמע (the recitation of the Shema, which is the formal context for this acceptance). All three are identified as the same act — they are parallel expressions of the same spiritual devotion, not a sequence of causes and effects. Rabbi Akiva's statement ('כל ימי הייתי מצטער מתי יבוא לידי ואקיימנו') is interpreted to mean that every time he recited Shema and accepted martyrdom in thought, he actually felt the pain of death — meaning the mental acceptance and the physical reality were one. This cluster is unambiguously good-polarity: it describes the highest level of devotion to God, the complete identification with the Shema declaration.", "dominant_node": "מסירת נפש", "nodes": ["מסירת נפש", "קידוש השם", "ארבע מיתות בית דין", "קריאת שמע"], "node_degrees": {"מסירת נפש": 3, "קידוש השם": 1, "ארבע מיתות בית דין": 1, "קריאת שמע": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package is derived from the source text — no bechina edges exist in torahData.js for this reference.", "The bechina relationships: (1) Rabbi Akiva's acceptance of martyrdom during Shema IS the ארבע מיתות בית דין — 'בְּשָׁעַת קְרִיאַת שְׁמַע שֶׁהָיָה רַבִּי עֲקִיבָא מְקַבֵּל עַל עַצְמוֹ אַרְבַּע מִיתוֹת בֵּית דִּין, הָיָה מְקַבֵּל עַל עַצְמוֹ מְסִירַת נַפְשׁוֹ'. The text treats these as identical, not sequential. (2) קריאת שמע is the context/bechina of מסירת נפש — during Shema one accepts martyrdom; they are the same spiritual act from two sides.", "This is a separate package from Package 1 because the relationship between the two packages is eitza, not bechina: the power of thought (Package 1) is what ENABLES the full experience of מסירת נפש (Package 2). Package 1 is the mechanism; Package 2 is the holy domain in which that mechanism is applied."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 194, "label": "דִּבּוּר וְהֵיכַל הַמֶּלֶךְ", "label_en": "Speech and the Palace of the King", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The sole connected component in Torah #194 contains two nodes: אדני and דבור. The Rebbe explicitly declares: 'אדני, בחינת הדבור' — the Divine Name אדני (associated with the mouth and speech, as in 'אדני שפתי תפתח', Psalm 51:17) is a bechina of speech. This is the conceptual core of the entire Torah: speech (דבור) is the היכל המלך (palace of the King), the sacred domain that belongs to God alone. דבור is dominant (it is the hub around which the teaching revolves), while אדני is the Divine-Name expression of that same reality. The polarity is 'good' because this cluster describes holy speech — the King's own palace — as distinct from the degraded honor-seeking that the Torah condemns. The human who distinguishes himself from animals through speech (גדר האדם = דבור) participates in this holy domain only insofar as he speaks truthfully and humbly before the King.", "dominant_node": "דבור", "nodes": ["אדני", "דבור"], "node_degrees": {"דבור": 1, "אדני": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 196, "label": "תְּפִלַּת קֶבַע וּגְזֵלָה", "label_en": "Fixed Demanded Prayer and Robbery", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the central and only substantive bechina cluster in Torah #196. The Rebbe explicitly equates תפלת קבע (the mode of prayer in which one demands that God must fulfill the request) with גזלה (robbery, taking by force). The equation is stated twice and anchored in a verse: 'אַל תַּעַשֶׂה תְּפִלָּתְךָ קֶבַע, מִלְּשׁוֹן גְּזֵלָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְקָבַע אֶת קֹבְעֵיהֶם נָפֶשׁ.' The word קֶבַע is identified as derived from and synonymous with robbery — the act of taking by force what has not been freely given. The additional nodes in this cluster share this same essential nature: לְהִתְעַקֵּשׁ בִּתְפִלָּתוֹ (to be stubborn/insistent in prayer) IS the mode of תפלת קבע — the Rebbe defines them as identical ('שֶׁאָסוּר לְהִתְעַקֵּשׁ בִּתְפִלָּתוֹ... כִּי זֶה הוּא תְּפִלַּת קֶבַע'). לוֹקֵחַ דָּבָר בְּחָזְקָה (taking by force) IS גזלה — both describe the same act of forced acquisition. All four nodes (תפלת קבע, גזלה, להתעקש בתפלתו, לוקח דבר בחזקה) are different formulations of the same sinful spiritual posture: demanding from God rather than supplicating. The dominant node is תפלת קבע because it is the primary concept the teaching revolves around (mentioned three times) and is the node through which the others are defined.", "dominant_node": "תפלת קבע", "nodes": ["תפלת קבע", "גזלה", "להתעקש בתפלתו", "לוקח דבר בחזקה"], "node_degrees": {"תפלת קבע": 3, "גזלה": 2, "להתעקש בתפלתו": 2, "לוקח דבר בחזקה": 1}, "source_quotes": ["כִּי זֶה הוּא כְּמוֹ לוֹקֵחַ דָּבָר בְּחָזְקָה, בִּגְזֵלָה", "אַל תַּעַשֶׂה תְּפִלָּתְךָ קֶבַע, מִלְּשׁוֹן גְּזֵלָה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְקָבַע אֶת קֹבְעֵיהֶם נָפֶשׁ", "כִּי זֶה הוּא תְּפִלַּת קֶבַע, שֶׁלּוֹקֵחַ הַדָּבָר בְּחָזְקָה, בִּגְזֵלָה"], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["להתעקש בתפלתו", "לוקח דבר בחזקה"], "reason": "These two nodes form the descriptive cluster of תפלת קבע. להתעקש בתפלתו is explicitly defined BY the Rebbe as the meaning of תפלת קבע ('שֶׁאָסוּר לְהִתְעַקֵּשׁ בִּתְפִלָּתוֹ... כִּי זֶה הוּא תְּפִלַּת קֶבַע'). לוקח דבר בחזקה is the metaphorical description of what גזלה is. The four-node cluster represents one unified domain: the sinful posture of demanding prayer. They share the same essential nature — not a cause-effect chain — and merge per the shared-nature rule."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 196, "label": "תְּפִלָּה בְּרַחֲמִים וְתַחֲנוּנִים", "label_en": "Prayer with Mercy and Supplication", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the good counterpart cluster — the proper mode of prayer that the Torah prescribes. The Rebbe states explicitly: 'רַק צָרִיךְ לְהִתְפַּלֵּל וּלְהִתְחַנֵּן לִפְנֵי הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּרַחֲמִים וְתַחֲנוּנִים.' תפלה (prayer) in its proper mode IS רחמים ותחנונים (mercy and supplication) — both are expressions of the same spiritual posture: approaching God as a petitioner who accepts the outcome either way, not as a creditor who demands repayment. The two nodes are joined in direct bechina: the opening Mishnah quote itself frames them as the positive definition of prayer (אֶלָּא רַחֲמִים וְתַחֲנוּנִים). This is the structural mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 is the evil posture of prayer (forced/demanded), Package 2 is the holy posture of prayer (merciful/supplicating). The same concept תפלה appears in both packages as the common element — this is the classic Good/Evil mirror structure where the same concept (prayer) can be oriented either toward holiness (Package 2) or toward the evil posture (Package 1, as תפלת קבע).", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "רחמים ותחנונים"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 2, "רחמים ותחנונים": 1}, "source_quotes": ["אַל תַּעַשֶׂה תְּפִלָּתְךָ קֶבַע, אֶלָּא רַחֲמִים וְתַחֲנוּנִים", "רַק צָרִיךְ לְהִתְפַּלֵּל וּלְהִתְחַנֵּן לִפְנֵי הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּרַחֲמִים וְתַחֲנוּנִים"], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 197, "label": "עֲנָוָה וְחָכְמָה", "label_en": "Humility and Wisdom", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central claim of Torah #197 is that true humility and wisdom share an inseparable essential nature — 'וְעִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה – כְּשֶׁהִיא בְּחָכְמָה' (the essence of humility is when it is with wisdom). Rebbe Nachman does not say wisdom *causes* humility or that humility *produces* wisdom; he says the essential form of humility IS humility-in-wisdom. These two concepts are different expressions of the same spiritual reality: the quality of the tzaddik who genuinely knows how small he is because he genuinely perceives how great Wisdom is. Moshe Rabbeinu is included in this package because the Torah cites 'וְהָאִישׁ מֹשֶׁה עָנָו מְאֹד' precisely as the proof-case that ענוה in its highest form cannot be damaged — Moshe IS the archetype of ענוה, and therefore IS a bechina of this quality. עִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה and עֲנָוָה בְּחָכְמָה are the same concept stated in two formulations by the Rebbe himself and constitute a single bechina pair that is the spine of the entire teaching.", "dominant_node": "ענוה", "nodes": ["ענוה", "חכמה", "עִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה", "עֲנָוָה בְּחָכְמָה", "מֹשֶׁה רַבֵּנוּ"], "node_degrees": {"ענוה": 3, "חכמה": 2, "עִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה": 1, "עֲנָוָה בְּחָכְמָה": 1, "מֹשֶׁה רַבֵּנוּ": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "ענוה", "node2": "חכמה", "proof": "וְעִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה – כְּשֶׁהִיא בְּחָכְמָה", "note": "The Rebbe defines true anava as inseparable from chokhmah; they are co-essential aspects of the same spiritual quality in the tzaddik"}, {"node1": "עִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה", "node2": "עֲנָוָה בְּחָכְמָה", "proof": "וְעִקַּר הָעֲנָוָה – כְּשֶׁהִיא בְּחָכְמָה", "note": "The Rebbe directly equates these: the essence of humility IS humility-with-wisdom — two names for one reality"}, {"node1": "מֹשֶׁה רַבֵּנוּ", "node2": "ענוה", "proof": "וְהָאִישׁ מֹשֶׁה עָנָו מְאֹד — cited as the supreme exemplar whose humility could not be damaged even by lashon hara", "note": "Moshe IS the bechina of ענוה in its highest form — the Torah praises him for precisely this quality, making him the archetype"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 197, "label": "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה וּלְשׁוֹן הָרָע", "label_en": "False Humility and Evil Speech", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This is the evil mirror of Package 1. Just as Package 1 identifies the true/holy form of anava (bonded with chokhmah, exemplified by Moshe), Package 2 identifies the damaged/false form (anava severed from chokhmah, produced by lashon hara). The Rebbe explicitly equates עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה (false/invalid humility) with כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת (bowing one's head in a way of foolishness): 'כִּי זֶה עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה' — this IS false humility. That is a bechina statement. לָשׁוֹן הָרָע belongs in this package as the dominant evil force of the teaching — it is the anti-ענוה force that severs humility from wisdom. While its primary relationship to the other concepts is eitza (it causes the damage), its essential nature IS the domain of the anti-holiness forces that oppose the ענוה-חכמה union. Lashon hara, false humility, and foolish head-bowing are all expressions of the same domain: the spiritual reality that is the counterforce to true humility.", "dominant_node": "לשון הרע", "nodes": ["לשון הרע", "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה", "כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת"], "node_degrees": {"לשון הרע": 2, "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה": 1, "כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה", "node2": "כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת", "proof": "לְהַרְאוֹת עַצְמוֹ בִּכְפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת כְּאִלּוּ הוּא עָנָו, כִּי זֶה עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה", "note": "The Rebbe directly states: bowing one's head in the manner of foolishness IS false humility — an explicit definitional bechina"}, {"node1": "לשון הרע", "node2": "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה", "proof": "עַל יְדֵי פְּגַם לָשׁוֹן הָרָע נַעֲשֶׂה פֵּרוּד בֵּין עֲנָוָה לְחָכְמָה — leaving only the degraded form", "note": "Lashon hara IS the essential anti-force to ענוה; what remains after its damage is precisely עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה — they share the same domain of opposition to true humility-wisdom"}], "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["לשון הרע", "עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה", "כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת"], "reason": "These three form the evil cluster by virtue of sharing the same essential domain: the domain of false/damaged humility that opposes the ענוה-חכמה union of Package 1. עֲנָוָה פְּסוּלָה and כְּפִיפַת רֹאשׁ בְּדֶרֶךְ שְׁטוּת are directly equated by bechina; לשון הרע is the dominant force of that same domain — the active evil whose essence is the severing of the ענוה-חכמה bond."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 199, "label": "דְּבַשׁ וְאִשָּׁה", "label_en": "Honey and Woman", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The only explicit identity (bechina) statement in Torah #199 is the gematria equation: 'דְּבַשׁ בְּגִימַטְרִיָּא אִשָּׁה' — honey and woman share the same numerical value (306). The Rebbe cites this as a foundational equivalence grounding the entire teaching's mechanism: the taste of sweetness in Torah IS the bechina of דבש, and since דבש = אשה, that sweetness acts directly on the spiritual domain of אשה (wife, woman). The polarity is neutral: both דבש and אשה are natural/relational concepts that are neither inherently holy nor evil. They function here as the shared pivot between the eitza-domain of Torah-study and the eitza-domain of marital life. The teaching does not assign positive or negative valence to דבש or אשה themselves — they are the structural link through which the practice (feeling sweetness in Torah) operates on the protective domain.", "dominant_node": "דבש", "nodes": ["דבש", "אשה"], "node_degrees": {"דבש": 1, "אשה": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package is derived from close reading of the source text. There are zero bechina edges in torahData.js for reference 199 — the single existing edge (id null, שמרגישין טעם מתיקות בהתורה → להנצל מענש של אלמן) is type 'eitza'.", "The bechina equation is stated explicitly in line 3: 'כִּי דְּבַשׁ בְּגִימַטְרִיָּא אִשָּׁה כַּמּוּבָא בַּכְּתָבִים'. The Rebbe cites kabbalistic writings (כתבים = the Ari's writings) as the source for this equation.", "The gematria proof: ד+ב+ש = 4+2+300 = 306; א+ש+ה = 1+300+5 = 306. Equal gematria in Rebbe Nachman's methodology functions as a bechina — the two words share the same essential numerical-spiritual root.", "This two-node package is the smallest possible package; the entire Torah's structure radiates from this single bechina pivot. The teaching exists to communicate one thing: because of this identity (דבש = אשה), sweetness in Torah sweetens the judgment of one's wife."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 203, "label": "אֶסְתֵּר וְהַשְּׁכִינָה", "label_en": "Esther and the Shechina", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly declares 'וְאֶסְתֵּר הִיא הַשְּׁכִינָה' — Esther IS the Shechina. This is the one and only bechina equation in Torah #203. The identification uses the copula 'הִיא' (is), the strongest form of bechina declaration. Esther and the Shechina are two names for the same essential reality: the hidden divine presence dwelling among Israel. The name אסתר itself derives from הַסְתָּרָה (concealment), pointing to the concealed/hidden nature of the Shechina in exile. The entire teaching's practical insight — that by listening to women's stories one can know the state of the Shechina — rests on this identification: Esther (= Shechina) had her welfare/peace known through the court of women, which is the model Mordechai used. The polarity is 'good': both אסתר and שכינה belong to the domain of holiness — Esther as the embodiment of divine royalty and hidden sanctity, שכינה as the divine presence itself. There is no evil package: Torah #203 presents a positive teaching about accessing knowledge of the Shechina through the medium of women's stories, with no opposing evil domain described.", "dominant_node": "שכינה", "nodes": ["שכינה", "אסתר"], "node_degrees": {"שכינה": 1, "אסתר": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 204, "label": "מָעוֹת וְתוֹרָה", "label_en": "Money for a Torah Scholar and Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The single connected component in Torah #204 groups three concepts that share the same essential nature: (1) מעות שנותנין לתלמיד חכם — money given to a Torah scholar, (2) תמכי אוריתא — supporters of Torah (the Aramaic designation from Proverbs 3:18 as understood in rabbinic literature), and (3) תורה — Torah itself. The Rebbe's teaching makes an escalating bechina claim: first, that this money is a bechina of tamchei oraita (expected, conventional), and second, the deeper revelation that this money is literally bechina of Torah itself (surprising, elevated). The notarikon proof — that מָעוֹת is an acronym for וְאֵין עֲבֵירָה מְכַבָּה תּוֹרָה — confirms and seals the bechina equation rather than adding a new independent bechina. All three nodes are in the domain of holiness (Torah, study, its supporters) and share the same positive polarity. The dominant node (most connected) is מעות שנותנין לתלמיד חכם, which serves as the bridge between the known identification (tamchei oraita) and the deeper identification (Torah itself).", "dominant_node": "מעות שנותנין לתלמיד חכם", "nodes": ["מעות שנותנין לתלמיד חכם", "תורה", "תמכי אוריתא"], "node_degrees": {"מעות שנותנין לתלמיד חכם": 2, "תורה": 1, "תמכי אוריתא": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 205, "label": "חֶסֶד וּגְבוּרָה וְהַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Chesed and Gevurah, and Da'at", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The central teaching of Torah #205 is that the word תהלים (gematria תפ\"ה) equals the two divine names אל אלקים spelled out — and those names are a bechina of חסד וגבורה. The lost seed (טפה) itself is also a bechina of חסד וגבורה (it contains the forces of fire and water, warmth and moisture). The seed originates from דעת, which is equally a bechina of חסד וגבורה. And דעת is further specified as חמשה חסדים וחמש גבורות — ten elements — which is why ten chapters must be recited. The isolated pair עשרה מיני זמרה / עשרה לשונות is merged here because 'ten' is the structural key that connects them: the 10 types of song = the 10 languages of Psalms, and the reason there are 10 chapters is precisely that דעת = 5+5 = ten. They share the same essential domain of the complete ten-structure of da'at expressed as chesed and gevurah. חסד וגבורה is the dominant hub (4 direct bechina connections); דעת is secondary (2 direct connections). All nodes in this package describe the same cosmic duality that both defines the problem (the fallen seed is chesed-gevurah captured by the klipa) and defines the solution (תהלים = אל אלקים = chesed-gevurah that can extract the seed from the klipa).", "dominant_node": "חסד וגבורה", "nodes": ["חסד וגבורה", "אל אלקים", "דעת", "חמימות ולחות", "טפה", "חמשה חסדים וחמש גבורות", "עשרה מיני זמרה", "עשרה לשונות שנאמר בהם ספר תהלים"], "node_degrees": {"חסד וגבורה": 4, "דעת": 2, "אל אלקים": 1, "חמימות ולחות": 1, "טפה": 1, "חמשה חסדים וחמש גבורות": 1, "עשרה מיני זמרה": 1, "עשרה לשונות שנאמר בהם ספר תהלים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עשרה מיני זמרה", "עשרה לשונות שנאמר בהם ספר תהלים"], "reason": "This isolated pair is a bechina-pair (edge 2460): the 10 types of song are the 10 languages of the book of Psalms. Their connection to Package 1 is through the number ten: the Rebbe explicitly explains that one recites ten chapters because דעת = חמשה חסדים וחמש גבורות (ten elements). The 10 songs/languages are the practical expression of that same ten-structure. Their essential nature — the completeness of ten as the structure of da'at — places them within the same thematic family as חמשה חסדים וחמש גבורות, חסד וגבורה, and דעת. An eitza flow connects this pair to the Package 1 hub (the reason for ten chapters flows from דעת = ten), making their package membership unambiguous."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 206, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְהַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ", "label_en": "Torah and God", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package is formed from two explicit bechina identifications in the source text. First, line 3 states directly: 'הַתּוֹרָה הוּא הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּעַצְמוֹ' — Torah IS God Himself. The word 'הוּא' is the strongest possible bechina declaration: not an analogy, not a metaphor, but direct essential identity. Second, lines 9–10 state: 'קוֹל הַקְּרִיאָה שֶׁל הַמִּצְווֹת, שֶׁהֵם הַתּוֹרָה' — the commandments ARE the Torah (שֶׁהֵם = 'they are'). By transitivity of bechina: if Torah = God and mitzvot = Torah, then the three concepts — תורה (Torah), השם יתברך (God Himself), and מצוות (the commandments) — form one bechina cluster. They are three expressions of the same underlying reality: the divine calling-voice that calls the sinner back from the path of error. The polarity is 'good': all three concepts belong to the domain of holiness, the positive teaching, the calling that draws the soul toward the straight path (דֶּרֶךְ הַיָּשָׁר). The dominant node is תורה, which is connected to both other nodes and also appears in the dataset for many other Torahs, making it the central bridge concept.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "השם יתברך", "מצוות"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 2, "השם יתברך": 1, "מצוות": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package is derived from close reading of the source text. There are zero bechina edges in torahData.js for reference 206.", "The first bechina (תורה = השם יתברך) is stated in line 3: 'וְהַתּוֹרָה הוּא הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּעַצְמוֹ, שֶׁהוּא קוֹרֵא אוֹתָם וּמְבַקְּשָׁם שֶׁיַּחְזְרוּ אֵלָיו.' The Rebbe has just cited the Talmudic Aramaic formula 'אוֹרַיְתָא מַכְרֶזֶת קַמַּיְהוּ' (Torah proclaims before them) and then explains: Torah IS God Himself, who calls them and seeks them to return.", "The second bechina (מצוות = תורה) is stated in lines 9–10: 'קוֹל הַקְּרִיאָה שֶׁל הַמִּצְווֹת, שֶׁהֵם הַתּוֹרָה' — the calling-voice of the commandments, which ARE Torah. The Rebbe identifies mitzvot and Torah as the same reality: hearing/remembering the mitzvot is hearing/remembering the Torah is hearing God's calling voice.", "The node 'השם יתברך' does not appear as a standalone node_id in the existing dataset (God is typically referenced as the source/context, not as a node). For this Torah's context, the identification 'הַתּוֹרָה הוּא הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ בְּעַצְמוֹ' makes 'השם יתברך' an explicit bechina node — the one who IS Torah, who IS the calling voice. This is a new node that appears in the dataset as a concept but not as a standalone node_id in the bechina graph.", "The 'קוֹל הַקְּרִיאָה' (voice of the calling) and 'קוֹל הַתּוֹרָה' (voice of Torah) are used interchangeably throughout the text (lines 4, 9, 10, 11), but they are not a separate bechina pair — they are descriptions of the same Torah/God/mitzvot node in its functional aspect (calling the sinner). They do not constitute a fourth node but rather describe the activity of the Package 1 nodes."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 207, "label": "דִּבּוּרִים וּגְבוּרוֹת — הַדִּבּוּר כְּבָחִינַת גְּבוּרָה", "label_en": "Speech and Gevurot — Speech as an Aspect of Divine Severity", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The foundational bechina of Torah #207 is the opening statement: 'כָּל הַדִּבּוּרִים הֵם בְּחִינַת גְּבוּרוֹת' — all speech is an aspect of gevurot. The proof is twofold: the verse 'וּגְבוּרָתְךָ יְדַבֵּרוּ' (Psalm 145:11) links speaking and gevurot linguistically; and the kabbalistic writings (the Ari) teach that the five gevurot of da'at break through the mouth and become the five articulatory organs of speech (five motzaot ha-peh). Neither produces the other — they are two faces of the same spiritual reality: speech is the external, audible form of the gevurot that exist within da'at. This is a textbook bechina: symmetric, reversible, identity of essential nature across two domains (the physical domain of speech / the Kabbalistic domain of the sefirot's gevurot). The polarity is neutral rather than good or evil because speech itself is an object or instrument — it can serve either holiness (Torah study and good words that sweeten the gevurot) or evil (harsh speech that falls on the tzaddik). The teaching makes this ambivalence explicit: the same reality (speech = gevurot) can be sweetened through Torah and good words, or remain unsweetened and cause damage. The five motzaot ha-peh (five organs of speech: aleph/glottal, bet/labial, gimel/palatal, dalet/dental, vav/lingual) are implicitly included in this package as the structural bridge between the internal gevurot and the external speech — they are not given an independent bechina node but are referenced as the mechanism by which gevurot emerge as speech.", "dominant_node": "גבורות", "nodes": ["דבור", "גבורות"], "node_degrees": {"גבורות": 1, "דבור": 1}, "merge_notes": ["Dataset edge id 602 directly supplies this package: node1_id='דבור', node2_id='גבורות', type='bechina', reference=207.", "The teaching returns to this bechina equation five times across the nine lines (lines 1, 2, 3, 4, 5), each time restating it in a new context (normal speech, harsh gevurot, the tzaddik's burden). This repeated restatement confirms the centrality of this package — it is the Torah's foundational axiom.", "The term 'גבורות קשות' (harsh gevurot) in lines 2–7 is a specification of גבורות (the same node, at the extreme end of severity) rather than a separate node. The Rebbe says 'sometimes harsh gevurot emerge' — indicating that 'גבורות קשות' is a mode or intensity of the same category of גבורות, not a distinct concept with different essential nature.", "The five motzaot ha-peh (מוצאות הפה) and the five gevurot of da'at are cited as the mechanistic explanation for the bechina, not as independent bechina nodes themselves. They appear in line 1 as proof ('כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב בַּכְּתָבִים') and are absorbed into the foundational equation.", "No evil mirror is developed for this package. The 'evil' dimension of this Torah is expressed through the eitza flow (harsh gevurot → unsweetened speech → falls on tzaddik), not through a separate bechina cluster. The polarity of speech/gevurot is appropriately neutral."], "dataset_edge": {"id": 602, "node1_id": "דבור", "node2_id": "גבורות", "type": "bechina", "reference": 207, "proof": "הַדִּבּוּרִים הֵם בְּחִינַת גְּבוּרוֹת כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב (תְּהִלִּים קמ\"ה): \"וּגְבוּרָתְךָ יְדַבֵּרוּ\", וּכְמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב בַּכְּתָבִים שֶׁהַה' גְּבוּרוֹת שֶׁבַּדַּעַת בּוֹקְעִין וְיוֹצְאִין בְּהַפֶּה וְנַעֲשֶׂה מֵהֶם ה' מוֹצָאוֹת הַפֶּה, נִמְצָא שֶׁכָּל הַדִּבּוּרִים הֵם בְּחִינַת גְּבוּרוֹת"}}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 207, "label": "תּוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל פֶּה, הַצַּדִּיק, וְתוֹרַת חֶסֶד", "label_en": "Oral Torah, the Tzaddik, and the Torah of Lovingkindness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Line 8 contains the Torah's second structural bechina cluster, built on two explicit identity declarations: (1) speaking against Torah she-be'al peh and speaking against the tzaddik ha-dor 'is literally one thing' (הוּא דָּבָר אֶחָד מַמָּשׁ) — because the essence of Oral Torah depends entirely on the tzaddik ha-dor, and (2) 'הַתַּלְמִיד חָכָם הוּא בְּעַצְמוֹ הַתּוֹרָה' — the Torah scholar IS himself the Torah (from the Talmudic statement in Makkot 22b: 'How foolish are those who stand before the Torah scroll but not before a Torah scholar'). These are bechina equations of essential nature: Torah she-be'al peh and the tzaddik ha-dor are not in a cause-effect relationship — they are two expressions of the same spiritual reality. Neither produces the other; both are manifestations of the living, embodied divine teaching-presence. Line 9 extends this cluster: when the tzaddik sweetens the harsh speech, he 'transforms the words into Torah' — and this Torah is specifically תּוֹרַת חֶסֶד (Torah of lovingkindness). The definition cited from Sukkah 49b ('זֶה הַלּוֹמֵד עַל מְנָת לְלַמְּדָהּ' — one who studies in order to teach others) is a bechina identity statement: torat chesed IS Torah that is taught to others, and this IS what the sweetened speech becomes. Thus three nodes share the same essential nature: תורה שבעל פה (the living Oral Torah), צדיק הדור (the tzaddik who embodies it), and תורת חסד (the Torah of kindness produced when he sweetens harsh gevurot and teaches them). The dominant node is תורה שבעל פה because it appears at the center of both bechina statements in line 8, and because the entire teaching builds toward explaining why speaking against it is so devastating. The polarity is unambiguously good: this package defines the holy domain — Torah, its human embodiment, and its most sublime form (torat chesed).", "dominant_node": "תורה שבעל פה", "nodes": ["תורה שבעל פה", "צדיק הדור", "תלמיד חכם", "תורת חסד"], "node_degrees": {"תורה שבעל פה": 3, "צדיק הדור": 2, "תלמיד חכם": 1, "תורת חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This package has zero pre-existing bechina edges in the dataset for reference 207. All nodes are derived from source text gap analysis.", "תורה שבעל פה and צדיק הדור: Line 8 states explicitly: 'כְּשֶׁמְּדַבְּרִים עַל תּוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל פֶּה, אוֹ כְּשֶׁמְּדַבְּרִים עַל הַצַּדִּיק הַדּוֹר בְּעַצְמוֹ, הוּא דָּבָר אֶחָד מַמָּשׁ' — speaking against Oral Torah or against the tzaddik of the generation is one and the same thing. The reasoning given: 'עִקַּר תּוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל פֶּה הוּא תָּלוּי בְּצַדִּיק הַדּוֹר' (the essence of Oral Torah depends on/is rooted in the tzaddik of the generation). This is a bechina of shared essence: Oral Torah and the tzaddik are not cause-and-effect — they are co-expressions of the same living divine teaching-presence. The Zohar proof cited ('שְׁכִינְתָּא בֵּין תְּרֵין צַדִּיקַיָּא יָתֵבָא') is given as the mystical basis for this identity.", "תורה שבעל פה and תלמיד חכם: Line 8 continues: 'הַתַּלְמִיד חָכָם הוּא בְּעַצְמוֹ הַתּוֹרָה' — the Torah scholar is himself the Torah. The Talmud (Makkot 22b) proves this: one should stand before a Torah scholar just as one stands before a Torah scroll, because the scholar IS the Torah. This is the same identity as between תורה שבעל פה and צדיק הדור, expressed from the angle of the individual scholar rather than the generation's leader.", "תורה שבעל פה and תורת חסד: Line 9 states that when the tzaddik sweetens the harsh words, he 'makes Torah from the words' — and this is specifically תּוֹרַת חֶסֶד, defined via Sukkah 49b as 'the one who studies in order to teach others.' The sweetened Torah is the ultimate form of Torah she-be'al peh — the living Torah that the tzaddik transmits outward. The connection: the same Oral Torah that was attacked by harsh speech, when sweetened and transformed, becomes the Torah of lovingkindness that the tzaddik teaches. This is a within-domain bechina: the sweetened form (torat chesed) IS the positive actualization of the same reality (Torah she-be'al peh).", "צדיק הדור vs. גדול הדור: The teaching uses both terms. 'גדול הדור' appears in lines 2, 4, 5, 7 and refers to the same person as 'צַדִּיק הַדּוֹר' (lines 5, 8). In line 7 the Baal Shem Tov is called 'הַגָּדוֹל הַדּוֹר'. These are treated as synonyms — the same conceptual node, the leader of the generation who carries the burden of the gevurot. No separate bechina is needed; they are textual aliases for the same node.", "Evil mirror check: This Torah does not develop an explicit evil bechina counterpart for Package 2. The opposing force is the Sabbatean heretics (Sha'tz) and the scholars who left the fold — but these are described as eitza-agents (they caused harsh speech to fall on the Besht) rather than as a conceptual bechina cluster. There is no equation of the form 'the false sage IS an aspect of X.' The evil dimension of this Torah operates entirely at the eitza level, not through a bechina package."], "source_bechina_evidence": {"bechina_1": {"node1": "תורה שבעל פה", "node2": "צדיק הדור", "formula": "explicit identity declaration: 'הוּא דָּבָר אֶחָד מַמָּשׁ' (it is literally one thing)", "source_quote": "כְּשֶׁמְּדַבְּרִים עַל תּוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל פֶּה, אוֹ כְּשֶׁמְּדַבְּרִים עַל הַצַּדִּיק הַדּוֹר בְּעַצְמוֹ, הוּא דָּבָר אֶחָד מַמָּשׁ"}, "bechina_2": {"node1": "תלמיד חכם", "node2": "תורה שבעל פה", "formula": "explicit essence identity: 'הַתַּלְמִיד חָכָם הוּא בְּעַצְמוֹ הַתּוֹרָה'", "source_quote": "הַתַּלְמִיד חָכָם הוּא בְּעַצְמוֹ הַתּוֹרָה"}, "bechina_3": {"node1": "תורה שבעל פה", "node2": "תורת חסד", "formula": "transformative identity: sweetened speech becomes Torah, which is specifically torat chesed; defined via Sukkah 49b as 'זֶה הַלּוֹמֵד עַל מְנָת לְלַמְּדָהּ'", "source_quote": "אֲזַי הוּא חוֹזֵר וְעוֹשֶׂה מֵהַדִּבּוּרִים תּוֹרָה... וַאֲזַי עוֹשֶׂה תּוֹרַת חֶסֶד, הַיְנוּ לְלַמֵּד לַאֲחֵרִים"}}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 208, "label": "רָשָׁע כְּצוֹפֶה וּמַחֲלֹקֶת כְּמִכְסֶה", "label_en": "The Wicked as Watchman and Controversy as Covering", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Torah #208 presents a striking paradox: the wicked opponent (רָשָׁע) and the controversy directed at the tzaddik (מַחֲלֹקֶת) are each declared to be, in their essential function, a covering and protection — a מִכְסֶה/צִפּוּי. The bechina network is unified through the pivot word צוֹפֶה: in its first reading, the רָשָׁע IS the צוֹפֶה (the city-watchman who guards); in its second reading, צוֹפֶה means מִכְסֶה (covering), and the מַחֲלֹקֶת IS the מִכְסֶה. These two chains share the node צוֹפֶה, creating one connected bechina cluster. The polarity is neutral — not 'evil' and not 'good' — because the same concepts (wicked, controversy, covering) serve different functions depending on degree: in small measure, the wicked watchman and the controversy-covering are beneficial and protective (guards the tzaddik from gashmiyut / shields him from overexposure); in excess, the same covering suffocates. The teaching does not present a good vs. evil mirror structure in the usual sense; instead, it presents a single set of concepts that are inherently ambiguous — the same opposition that protects in small doses destroys in large ones. The dominant node is צוֹפֶה, which is the structural hinge linking the watchman-cluster and the covering-cluster across both interpretations. רָשָׁע and צוֹפֶה/שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר are explicitly equated ('הוּא כְּמוֹ צוֹפֶה... כֵּן הָרָשָׁע הוּא שׁוֹמֵר'). מַחֲלֹקֶת and מִכְסֶה are explicitly equated ('הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת הִיא כְּמוֹ מִכְסֶה לָהֶם'). צוֹפֶה and מִכְסֶה are explicitly equated as a linguistic identity ('צוֹפֶה לְשׁוֹן מִכְסֶה'). These three bechina equations form one connected component of four-to-five nodes. All concepts in this package operate within the domain of opposition/protection — a structural reality (how Providence uses negative forces for positive ends) that is neutral in nature: neither purely holy nor purely evil, but a mechanism that can serve either purpose depending on degree.", "dominant_node": "צוֹפֶה", "nodes": ["צוֹפֶה", "רָשָׁע", "שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר", "מַחֲלֹקֶת", "מִכְסֶה"], "node_degrees": {"צוֹפֶה": 3, "רָשָׁע": 1, "שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר": 1, "מַחֲלֹקֶת": 1, "מִכְסֶה": 2}, "merge_notes": ["This package is derived from close reading of the source text. There are zero bechina edges in torahData.js for reference 208.", "Bechina 1 (line 2): רָשָׁע ↔ צוֹפֶה/שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר — 'הָרָשָׁע הוּא כְּמוֹ צוֹפֶה, כְּשׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר שֶׁנִּקְרָא צוֹפֶה, כֵּן הָרָשָׁע הוּא שׁוֹמֵר אֶת הַצַּדִּיק'. The language 'הוּא כְּמוֹ... כֵּן הוּא שׁוֹמֵר' is classic bechina identification.", "Bechina 2 (line 3): מַחֲלֹקֶת ↔ מִכְסֶה — 'כִּי הַמַּחֲלֹקֶת הִיא כְּמוֹ מִכְסֶה לָהֶם'. The language 'הִיא כְּמוֹ מִכְסֶה' is explicit bechina.", "Bechina 3 (line 4): צוֹפֶה ↔ מִכְסֶה — 'צוֹפֶה לְשׁוֹן מִכְסֶה, כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב: וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתוֹ זָהָב'. The language 'לְשׁוֹן מִכְסֶה' (the word means covering) is a linguistic bechina declaration — the Rebbe equates two words as expressions of the same root-concept.", "The three bechina equations form one connected component: [רָשָׁע — צוֹפֶה — שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר] via Bechina 1, [מַחֲלֹקֶת — מִכְסֶה] via Bechina 2, and [צוֹפֶה — מִכְסֶה] via Bechina 3. The shared node צוֹפֶה bridges the watchman-cluster (first interpretation) and the covering-cluster (second interpretation).", "The node שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר is closely identified with צוֹפֶה in the text ('שׁוֹמֵר הָעִיר שֶׁנִּקְרָא צוֹפֶה'). These could be treated as two nodes (the city-guard role and the term tzofeh) or as one node with two names (aliases). They are retained as separate nodes here because the text first equates רָשָׁע to שׁוֹמֵר and then calls that שׁוֹמֵר by the name צוֹפֶה — making both identification steps explicit.", "צִפּוּי (overlay/plating) from 'וְצִפִּיתָ אוֹתוֹ זָהָב' is treated as a variant of מִכְסֶה (they are the same concept — physical covering) rather than a separate fifth concept. The Rebbe uses the verse as a proof that צוֹפֶה = מִכְסֶה, not to introduce צִפּוּי as an independent node."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 209, "label": "אוֹרֵחַ וּבְרָכוֹת הַתְּפִלָּה", "label_en": "The Guest and the Blessings of Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster unifies all the positive conceptual nodes in Torah #209 around the central word-bridge of אוֹרֵחַ / אֹרַח (guest / path). The Rebbe plays on the same root: the physical 'guest' (אוֹרֵחַ) who blesses his host and the spiritual 'path of the righteous' (אֹרַח צַדִּיקִים) that shines like a great light are the same essential reality — the illuminated trajectory of holiness that produces blessing. The verse 'בְּרָכוֹת לְרֹאשׁ צַדִּיק' (blessings upon the head of the righteous) is an explicit bechina of the שְׁמוֹנֶה-עֶשְׂרֵה בְּרָכוֹת — the righteous man's blessings ARE the Amidah's 18 blessings (Zohar Pinchas 244, Tikun 47). The תְּמִידִין תִּקְּנוּם (prayers established like the Tamid) and הַכְנָסַת אוֹרֵחַ תַּלְמִיד-חָכָם (hosting a Torah scholar) are merged into this cluster because the Talmud (Berakhot 10a) explicitly equates them: one who hosts Torah scholars is as if he offered the Tamid. This means the Tamid and hachnasat orchim are parallel aspects of the same spiritual act — the one that establishes and repairs prayer. The dominant node is תְּפִלָּה (prayer/18-blessings) because every concept in this cluster ultimately defines itself as a face of proper prayer: the guest who blesses, the path of light, the blessings on the righteous, and the Tamid all converge on the reality of the Amidah.", "dominant_node": "שְׁמוֹנֶה-עֶשְׂרֵה בְּרָכוֹת שֶׁבַּתְּפִלָּה", "nodes": ["אוֹרֵחַ", "אֹרַח צַדִּיקִים כְּאוֹר נֹגַהּ", "בְּרָכוֹת לְרֹאשׁ צַדִּיק", "שְׁמוֹנֶה-עֶשְׂרֵה בְּרָכוֹת שֶׁבַּתְּפִלָּה", "הַכְנָסַת אוֹרֵחַ תַּלְמִיד-חָכָם", "תְּמִידִין תִּקְּנוּם"], "node_degrees": {"אוֹרֵחַ": 2, "אֹרַח צַדִּיקִים כְּאוֹר נֹגַהּ": 1, "בְּרָכוֹת לְרֹאשׁ צַדִּיק": 1, "שְׁמוֹנֶה-עֶשְׂרֵה בְּרָכוֹת שֶׁבַּתְּפִלָּה": 1, "הַכְנָסַת אוֹרֵחַ תַּלְמִיד-חָכָם": 1, "תְּמִידִין תִּקְּנוּם": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הַכְנָסַת אוֹרֵחַ תַּלְמִיד-חָכָם", "תְּמִידִין תִּקְּנוּם"], "reason": "These two nodes form a raw 2-node bechina pair (line 4: 'תְּמִידִין תִּקְּנוּם — זֶה בְּחִינַת הַכְנָסַת אוֹרֵחַ תַּלְמִיד-חָכָם'). They are the operational/ritual face of the same prayer-establishment act. The Tamid offering corresponds to prayer (the premise of the entire Torah from Berakhot 26b), and hosting a Torah scholar corresponds to the Tamid (Berakhot 10a). Both are therefore parallel expressions of the prayer-act. They belong in the good prayer cluster with the guest/blessings nodes, since all six nodes share the essential domain of prayer-establishment and blessing. The eitza relationship (hosting → repairs prayer) is the directional arrow between this merged group and the evil package; the bechina of Tamid=hosting is the parallel-nature relationship within the good package."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 209, "label": "תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת", "label_en": "Evil Prayers", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster contains the two names given in the text for the same evil phenomenon: the תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת (evil prayers that everyone accumulates — line 2: 'יֵשׁ לְכָל אָדָם תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת') and their functional definition תְּפִלּוֹת כְּנֶגֶד (opposing prayers — line 2: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת תְּפִלּוֹת כְּנֶגֶד שֶׁהִיא מִתְנַגֶּדֶת לוֹ'). The Rebbe explicitly labels the second as the bechina of the first: the evil prayers ARE the 'opposing prayers' — they are two names for the same essential reality, the spiritual force that opposes proper prayer. This is the sole evil package in Torah #209 and mirrors Package 1: where Package 1 is about prayers that bless and ascend, Package 2 is about prayers that oppose and disrupt. The proof-text is the rabbinic statement about the thief who prays before breaking in (Berakhot 63, Ein Yaakov version: 'גַּנָּבָא עַל מַחְתַּרְתָּא רַחֲמָנָא קַרְיָא') — this is the paradigm of the evil prayer that inverts the proper relationship between a person and the Divine.", "dominant_node": "תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת", "nodes": ["תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת", "תְּפִלּוֹת כְּנֶגֶד"], "node_degrees": {"תְּפִלּוֹת רָעוֹת": 1, "תְּפִלּוֹת כְּנֶגֶד": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 210, "label": "וְאָהַבְתָּ וְאַבְרָהָם", "label_en": "The Love of God and Abraham", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The sole bechina edge in Torah #210 equates 'וְאָהַבְתָּ' (the mitzvah of loving God, Devarim 6:5) with אַבְרָהָם (the patriarch Abraham). The teaching's proof is twofold: (1) The Sages (Yoma 86) interpret 'וְאָהַבְתָּ' as causing Hashem's name to become beloved through one's conduct — and Abraham is the paradigm of this conduct. (2) The splitting of the Red Sea occurred through 'זְכוּת אַבְרָהָם בְּחִינַת וְאָהַבְתָּ' (Abraham's merit, which is the bechina of ואהבת), and the verse confirms this: 'וַיָּשָׁב הַיָּם לִפְנוֹת בֹּקֶר' = 'דָּא בֹּקֶר דְּאַבְרָהָם'. Abraham and ואהבת share the same essential quality: the love that makes Hashem's name beloved in the world. Abraham is not the cause of ואהבת nor its product — he IS the living embodiment of that quality. They are two faces of the same divine love-force: ואהבת as commandment/ideal, Abraham as its archetypal human expression.", "dominant_node": "\"ואהבת\"", "nodes": ["\"ואהבת\"", "אברהם"], "node_degrees": {"\"ואהבת\"": 1, "אברהם": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 211, "label": "מֹחִין וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "Intellect and Moses", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups the two concepts that share the essential nature of 'pure intellect / clarity of mind.' Moshe (Moses) IS mochin — the Zohar source cited in the Torah explicitly states 'מֹשֶׁה הוּא בְּחִינַת מֹחִין.' These two nodes are different expressions of the same metaphysical reality: the faculty of clear, refined, holy thought. The broader thematic context of this package encompasses מחין זכים (pure intellect) and קדשת וטהרת המחשבות (holiness and purity of thought) — concepts that appear in the eitza edges of this Torah (edges 884, 1556, 2945) but not in bechina edges. Moshe serves as the personification of mochin in this Torah: just as Moshe carried Yosef's bones out of Egypt, the intellect attaches itself to the tzaddik in order to reach its completion. The polarity is unambiguously good — mochin/intellect in the domain of holiness is the primary positive value of this teaching.", "dominant_node": "מחין", "nodes": ["משה", "מחין"], "node_degrees": {"מחין": 1, "משה": 1}, "source_edges": [{"edge_id": 1740, "node1_id": "משה", "node2_id": "מחין", "proof": "'מֹשֶׁה הוּא בְּחִינַת מֹחִין"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 211, "label": "צַדִּיק וְיוֹסֵף", "label_en": "The Tzaddik and Joseph", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package groups the two concepts that share the essential nature of 'the tzaddik / the righteous one.' Yosef (Joseph) IS tzaddik — the Torah explicitly states 'וְיוֹסֵף הוּא בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק.' Both nodes are different expressions of the same archetype: the righteous leader to whom one attaches in order to acquire holy intellect. Joseph is the biblical personification of the tzaddik principle in this Torah; the tzaddik-concept in turn is what the entire practical teaching is about — the reason people travel on Rosh Hashana. The broader thematic context encompasses הִתְקַשְּׁרוּת לְצַדִּיקִים (attachment to tzaddikim), which appears in the eitza edges for this Torah (edges 1556, 2945) as the means by which one arrives at pure intellect. The polarity is good — the tzaddik in Torah #211 is purely a positive, redemptive figure, the vehicle for holiness of thought.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["יוסף", "צדיק"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 1, "יוסף": 1}, "source_edges": [{"edge_id": 2526, "node1_id": "יוסף", "node2_id": "צדיק", "proof": "וְיוֹסֵף הוּא בְּחִינַת צַדִּיק"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 212, "label": "מְחָאַת כַּפַּיִם וּדְבַר הַנְּבִיאִים", "label_en": "Clapping of Hands and Prophetic Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The teaching opens with the bold identification: clapping hands is a bechina of 'ידי אדם מתחת כנפיהם' (the human hands beneath the wings of the divine creatures, Ezekiel 10:8). The Rebbe immediately unpacks this by equating the wings (כנפים) with speech/words (דבורים): 'הכנפים הם הדבורים, בבחינת ובעל כנפים יגיד דבר'. Simultaneously, prophets (נביאים) are identified as a bechina of the speech of prayer (דבור התפלה): 'נביאים זה בחינת דבור התפלה, כמו ניב שפתים'. The palms (כפים) are identified as the hand of the prophets: 'הכפים הם יד הנביאים'. These four identifications form a single connected cluster through bechina: מחאת כפים — כפים — יד הנביאים — נביאים — דבור התפלה — כנפים — דבורים — all expressions of the same spiritual reality: the act of clapping that releases prophetic speech-power and reveals the divine image. The cluster is entirely in the domain of holiness. The dominant node is מחאת כפים, which connects directly to ידי אדם מתחת כנפיהם and serves as the hinge between the physical act and its prophetic-speech essence.", "dominant_node": "מחאת כפים", "nodes": ["מחאת כפים", "ידי אדם מתחת כנפיהם", "כנפים", "דבורים", "נביאים", "דבור התפלה", "כפים", "יד הנביאים"], "node_degrees": {"מחאת כפים": 3, "ידי אדם מתחת כנפיהם": 1, "כנפים": 2, "דבורים": 2, "נביאים": 2, "דבור התפלה": 1, "כפים": 1, "יד הנביאים": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "מחאת כפים", "node2": "ידי אדם מתחת כנפיהם", "proof": "גַּם מְחָאַת כַּף זֶה בְּחִינַת (יחזקאל י׳:ח׳): וִידֵי אָדָם מִתַּחַת כַּנְפֵיהֶם", "note": "Direct bechina statement: clapping IS the bechina of 'human hands beneath their wings'"}, {"node1": "כנפים", "node2": "דבורים", "proof": "כִּי הַכְּנָפַיִם הֵם הַדִּבּוּרִים, בִּבְחִינַת (קהלת י׳:כ׳): וּבַעַל כְּנָפַיִם יַגֵּיד דָּבָר", "note": "Explicit bechina: the wings ARE the speeches/words — two names for the same spiritual reality"}, {"node1": "נביאים", "node2": "דבור התפלה", "proof": "וּנְבִיאִים זֶה בְּחִינַת דִּבּוּר הַתְּפִלָּה, כְּמוֹ נִיב שְׂפָתַיִם", "note": "Direct bechina: 'prophets' IS a bechina of 'the speech of prayer' — the verse 'ניב שפתים' links the two"}, {"node1": "כפים", "node2": "יד הנביאים", "proof": "כִּי הַנְּבִיאִים הֵם הַדִּבּוּרִים, וְהַכַּפַּיִם הֵם יַד הַנְּבִיאִים", "note": "The palms ARE the hand of the prophets — bechina identification of two aspects of the same act"}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 212, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבִּכְתָב וְשֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה", "label_en": "Prayer and Written and Oral Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The third clause of the teaching reveals that prayer is encompassed/included within Torah she-bikhsav and Torah she-be'al-peh, which together constitute the two hands: 'גַּם נִכְלָל הַתְּפִלָּה בְּתוֹךְ תּוֹרָה שֶׁבִּכְתָב וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה שֶׁהֵם שְׁנֵי יָדַיִם: יָד כּוֹתֶבֶת וְיָד חוֹתֶמֶת'. The word 'נכלל' (is included/encompassed) indicates that prayer shares the same essential nature as — is contained in the same bechina-domain as — Torah she-bikhsav and Torah she-be'al-peh. The two Torah-modes are identified with two hands: writing hand (יד כותבת) and sealing hand (יד חותמת), which are their essential bechina-definition. This cluster forms a second connected component: תפלה — תורה שבכתב — יד כותבת — תורה שבעל-פה — יד חותמת. All are in the domain of holiness. The dominant node is תפלה, which is the subject of the 'being included' and thus is the most significant conceptual pivot of this cluster.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "תורה שבכתב", "יד כותבת", "תורה שבעל-פה", "יד חותמת"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "תורה שבכתב": 2, "יד כותבת": 1, "תורה שבעל-פה": 2, "יד חותמת": 1}, "bechina_edges": [{"node1": "תורה שבכתב", "node2": "יד כותבת", "proof": "תוֹרָה שֶׁבִּכְתָב וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה שֶׁהֵם שְׁנֵי יָדַיִם: יָד כּוֹתֶבֶת וְיָד חוֹתֶמֶת", "note": "Written Torah IS the writing hand — explicit bechina equation of mode of Torah with its hand-aspect"}, {"node1": "תורה שבעל-פה", "node2": "יד חותמת", "proof": "תוֹרָה שֶׁבִּכְתָב וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה שֶׁהֵם שְׁנֵי יָדַיִם: יָד כּוֹתֶבֶת וְיָד חוֹתֶמֶת", "note": "Oral Torah IS the sealing hand — explicit bechina equation of mode of Torah with its hand-aspect"}, {"node1": "תפלה", "node2": "תורה שבכתב", "proof": "גַּם נִכְלָל הַתְּפִלָּה בְּתוֹךְ תּוֹרָה שֶׁבִּכְתָב וְתוֹרָה שֶׁבְּעַל־פֶּה", "note": "Prayer is encompassed within Written and Oral Torah — prayer shares the essential domain of Torah; 'נכלל' signals bechina inclusion not eitza causation"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 213, "label": "שֵׁם סָאל וְהַהַסְתָּרָה", "label_en": "The Divine Name SAL and the Concealment", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all share the same essential nature: the protective, concealing dimension of the Divine Name SAL. The Name SAL (סאל) is formed from the initial letters of 'אַתָּה סֵתֶר לִי מִצַּר תִּצְּרֵנִי' (Psalm 32:7). The Torah explicitly states: 'אֲזַי הַשֵּׁם הַנַּ\"ל הוּא מִכְסֶה וּמִסְתָּר, בְּחִינַת: אַתָּה סֵתֶר לִי' — the Name IS the covering/concealment, a bechina of 'atah seter li.' The concealment (מסתיר ומכסה), the verse-phrase 'אתה סתר לי,' the verse-phrase 'מצר תצרני ותשמרני,' and the Name SAL itself are all faces of the same protective reality. They are not a cause-and-effect chain — the Name IS the concealment and the concealment IS the Name. The verse is both the source of the Name's letters AND the bechina of what the Name is. 'מצר תצרני ותשמרני' belongs here (in its protective valence) because when the Name functions correctly it encompasses both halves of the verse: 'אתה סתר לי' AND 'מצר תצרני ותשמרני' — and the Mem of מצר remains safely within the protective verse rather than defecting to the Accuser.", "dominant_node": "שם סאל", "nodes": ["שם סאל", "מסתיר ומכסה", "\"אתה סתר לי\"", "\"מצר תצרני ותשמרני\""], "node_degrees": {"שם סאל": 3, "מסתיר ומכסה": 2, "\"אתה סתר לי\"": 2, "\"מצר תצרני ותשמרני\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["\"מצר תצרני ותשמרני\""], "reason": "The phrase 'מצר תצרני ותשמרני' is the continuation of the protective verse 'אתה סתר לי.' In the Torah's conclusion (line 4), when the Name functions protectively, it is described as BOTH 'אתה סתר לי' AND 'מצר תצרני ותשמרני.' The verse's second half is thus a bechina of the same protective reality as the first half, and belongs in the same package. It is the Mem of this phrase that is at risk — but the phrase itself, when functioning, is protective. Its valence here is good."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 213, "label": "סָמֶ\"ךְ-מֵ\"ם וְאוֹת מֵ\"ם", "label_en": "Samael and the Letter Mem", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These concepts all share the essential nature of the Accuser — the destructive spiritual force that circles the protected person seeking entry. The Torah's two explicit bechina statements both concern this cluster: (1) 'הַמֵּ\"ם הִיא בְּחִינַת הַסָּמֶ\"ךְ-מֵ\"ם הַמְשׁוֹטֵט סָבִיב סָבִיב' — the letter Mem IS Samael the circler; (2) 'הַמֵּם שֶׁל מִצַּר תִּצְּרֵנִי הוּא בְּחִינַת הַמֵּ\"ם הַנַּ\"ל, בְּחִינַת הַסָּמֶ\"ךְ-מֵ\"ם' — the Mem of 'mitzar' IS that same Mem, IS Samael. The Zohar citation confirms: 'Pinchas saw the Mem of מות circling' — so the Mem of death and the Mem of mitzar are both bechinot of Samael who circles. בעל דבר (the Accuser) is explicitly identified as the entity that roams/searches/circles around the covered person — the same entity as Samael. 'סמאל' (the full name formed when Mem joins SAL) is this package's dominant outcome concept. 'סוף אדם למות' (the talmudic phrase, Berakhot 17) is the inverted acrostic of שם סאל when Samael gains power — it is the evil manifestation of the same letters, and thus a bechina of Samael's victory/authority.", "dominant_node": "סמ\"ך-מ\"ם", "nodes": ["סמ\"ך-מ\"ם", "בעל דבר", "אות מ\"ם", "מם של מות", "\"סוף אדם למות\""], "node_degrees": {"סמ\"ך-מ\"ם": 4, "בעל דבר": 2, "אות מ\"ם": 3, "מם של מות": 2, "\"סוף אדם למות\"": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בעל דבר"], "reason": "The Torah opens with the Accuser (בעל דבר) as the entity from whom the Name conceals and protects. Line 2 equates the circling presence explicitly: 'הַבַּעַל דָּבָר מְשׁוֹטֵט... וְזֶה בְּחִינַת... הַסָּמֶ\"ךְ-מֵ\"ם הַמְשׁוֹטֵט.' The circling of the בעל דבר IS the circling of the סמ\"ך-מ\"ם — they are the same entity in different naming registers. בעל דבר belongs in this evil package as a bechina of Samael."}, {"merged_nodes": ["מם של מות"], "reason": "The Zohar passage cited (Pinchas 236) describes Pinchas seeing 'the Mem of מות circling' — this Mem is immediately glossed by the Rebbe as 'הַיְנוּ כִּי הַמֵּ\"ם הִיא בְּחִינַת הַסָּמֶ\"ךְ-מֵ\"ם.' So the Mem of death is a bechina of the Mem-letter and of Samael. It is a distinct formulation ('מם של מות') but shares the exact same essential nature as the evil Mem cluster."}, {"merged_nodes": ["\"סוף אדם למות\""], "reason": "The phrase 'סוֹף אָדָם לָמוּת' (Berakhot 17) is the acrostic שם סאל (ס-א-ל) in its inverted, evil state — when Samael gains power over the person, the very same Name that was protective becomes a death-sentence. The Rebbe explicitly identifies this phrase as sharing the same acrostic letters as שם סאל, making it the evil bechina-twin of the protective Name. It belongs with the evil package because it IS the outcome of Samael's victory."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 214, "label": "תַּלְמוּד וְלִילִית", "label_en": "Talmud and Lilith", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The sole bechina in Torah #214 is a gematria identity: תַּלְמוּד = לִילִית = 480. The Rebbe explicitly grounds this as a structural equivalence: 'תַּלְמוּד בְּגִימַטְרִיָּא אוֹתִיּוֹת שֶׁל שְׁמָהּ לִילִית'. This is the classic Rebbe Nachman gematria-bechina: identical numerical value reveals deep structural kinship — the same underlying reality expressed through two names. The package is marked neutral because this bechina is the Torah's entire point: the Talmud and Lilith share the same structural nature (the letters of one are the gematria of the other), and this shared nature is precisely what makes Talmud study a double-edged spiritual force. Properly oriented learning (כַּהֹגֶן) subdues Lilith by the power of this identity — it turns her own gematria-signature back against her. Improperly oriented learning empowers her through the same mechanism. The package therefore does not belong to the good domain alone (תַּלְמוּד as pure Torah study) nor to the evil domain alone (לִילִית as pure adversary), but occupies the structural boundary between both poles. This is not a contradiction but a deep structural feature: the same neutral vessel can be filled with holiness or with evil depending on orientation, and its power in either direction derives from the same shared nature.", "dominant_node": "תלמוד", "nodes": ["תלמוד", "לילית"], "node_degrees": {"תלמוד": 1, "לילית": 1}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 215, "label": "עֵת רָצוֹן וּמֵצַח הָרָצוֹן", "label_en": "Time of Divine Favor and the Forehead of Will", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes two chained bechina equations in a single passage: first, that the universal redemption (which encompasses all 24 courts of judgment) requires an eit ratzon — and eit ratzon is explicitly declared to be 'בְּחִינַת הִתְגַּלּוּת מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן' (a bechina of the revelation of the forehead of will); second, that Shabbat Mincha is given as the prime example of this state: 'כְּמוֹ בְּשַׁבָּת בְּמִנְחָה, בְּחִינַת: וַאֲנִי תְפִלָּתִי... עֵת רָצוֹן'. These two bechina declarations connect three nodes: (a) עֵת רָצוֹן — the time of divine favor, the spiritual window when God's will is revealed; (b) הִתְגַּלּוּת מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן — the revelation of the forehead of will, the Kabbalistic concept of divine willingness radiating from above; (c) שַׁבָּת בְּמִנְחָה — Shabbat afternoon prayer, the specific temporal-liturgical moment that embodies eit ratzon. All three share the same essential nature: they are different expressions of the single reality of divine favor/willingness being manifest and accessible. The Rebbe uses the forehead metaphor (מֵצַח) because the forehead (metzach) in Kabbalistic thought is associated with ratzon (will) — the forehead is the place where will is 'written' or revealed on the divine countenance. Shabbat Mincha is the earthly time-window that corresponds to this supernal revelation. The polarity is 'good': these are all positive, sacred moments/qualities — divine will, sacred time, the channel through which the universal redemption operates.", "dominant_node": "עֵת רָצוֹן", "nodes": ["עֵת רָצוֹן", "הִתְגַּלּוּת מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן", "שַׁבָּת בְּמִנְחָה"], "node_degrees": {"עֵת רָצוֹן": 2, "הִתְגַּלּוּת מֵצַח הָרָצוֹן": 1, "שַׁבָּת בְּמִנְחָה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 215, "label": "מֹשֶׁה בֵּין שְׁמָד לְרָצוֹן", "label_en": "Moses Between Destruction and Will", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe makes a striking bechina claim about Moses' essential nature using gematria: 'מֹשֶׁה עוֹמֵד בֵּין שְׁמָד לְרָצוֹן, כִּי מִסְפַּר מֹשֶׁה הוּא מְמֻצָּע בֵּין מִסְפַּר שְׁמָד לְמִסְפַּר רָצוֹן' — Moses stands between shemad and ratzon, because Moses' gematria is the midpoint between the gematria of shemad and the gematria of ratzon. Gematria: מֹשֶׁה = 345, שְׁמָד = 344, רָצוֹן = 346. Moses is literally the median — the exact intermediate number — between destruction (344) and will (346). This is not merely a biographical observation but a deep bechina declaration: Moses' essential nature is that of the mediator who transforms shemad (coerced apostasy, destruction) into ratzon (divine will, voluntary acceptance). His very name-number encodes this function. The Rebbe then summarizes: 'כָּל עֲבוֹדַת מֹשֶׁה – לְהַמְתִּיק הַחֲרוֹן אַף שֶׁל הָעַכּוּ\"ם לְרָצוֹן' — Moses' entire service is sweetening the idol-worshippers' wrath into ratzon. This confirms the bechina: Moses IS the sweetening force between these two poles. The cluster of מֹשֶׁה, שְׁמָד, and רָצוֹן form a bechina unit — they are three aspects of the same structural reality in Moses' spiritual identity. The polarity is 'good': Moses represents the redemptive, sweetening force. Note: שְׁמָד appears here as a concept that Moses' nature encompasses and mediates — it is part of his identity as the polar opposite he stands against, making it a 'good' package concept despite shemad's negative content, because the package's nature is Moses-as-mediator.", "dominant_node": "מֹשֶׁה", "nodes": ["מֹשֶׁה", "שְׁמָד", "רָצוֹן"], "node_degrees": {"מֹשֶׁה": 2, "שְׁמָד": 1, "רָצוֹן": 1}, "merge_notes": ["שְׁמָד and רָצוֹן are here explicitly bechina-connected to מֹשֶׁה via gematria equivalence, not as a cause-and-effect (that would be eitza). Moses' number being the midpoint establishes a parallel-essence: his identity IS the median between these two values. Note that רָצוֹן in Package 1 and רָצוֹן in Package 2 are the same concept-node but serve slightly different roles: in Package 1 it appears as part of the phrase 'עֵת רָצוֹן' (time of will), while here it appears as the standalone concept 'רָצוֹן' (will) to which Moses' gematria is adjacent. These should be treated as potentially aliased nodes in the full dataset — the same conceptual reality expressed in two ways."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 215, "label": "עַכּוּ\"ם וְחֲרוֹן אַף", "label_en": "Idol Worshippers and Divine Wrath", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Rebbe establishes an explicit bechina equation: 'כָּל זְמַן שֶׁיֵּשׁ עַכּוּ\"ם בָּעוֹלָם חֲרוֹן אַף בָּעוֹלָם' — as long as idol worshippers exist in the world, there is divine wrath (חֲרוֹן אַף) in the world. This is the bechina equation underlying Torah 215: עַכּוּ\"ם (idol worshippers) and חֲרוֹן אַף (divine wrath) are parallel expressions of the same negative spiritual reality. The presence of one entails the presence of the other — they are bechina equivalents within the domain of evil/opposition. The phrase is quoted as a known rabbinic dictum ('כִּי כָּל זְמַן שֶׁיֵּשׁ עַכּוּ\"ם בָּעוֹלָם...') indicating it describes an essential ontological link, not a mere causal observation. The 24 courts of judgment (כ\"ד בָּתֵּי דִּינִים) are the structural apparatus through which this wrath operates — they are the framework of judgment that must be sweetened. These three concepts (עַכּוּ\"ם, חֲרוֹן אַף, כ\"ד בָּתֵּי דִּינִים) share the same essential nature: they are all expressions of the negative/opposing domain that Moses' entire service is directed toward sweetening. The polarity is 'evil': these concepts belong to the domain of opposition, judgment, and spiritual distance from God. This package is the evil mirror of Package 1 (eit ratzon / ratzon) — ratzon (divine will/favor) vs. charon af (divine wrath); and the evil mirror of Package 2's Moses-as-sweetener: the thing being sweetened.", "dominant_node": "חֲרוֹן אַף", "nodes": ["חֲרוֹן אַף", "עַכּוּ\"ם", "כ\"ד בָּתֵּי דִּינִים"], "node_degrees": {"חֲרוֹן אַף": 2, "עַכּוּ\"ם": 1, "כ\"ד בָּתֵּי דִּינִים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 216, "label": "תְּפִלָּה וְחַי בִּרְכוֹת", "label_en": "Prayer and the Eighteen Blessings", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The core positive cluster of this teaching. The Rebbe states: 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת חַ\"י בִּרְכוֹת הַתְּפִלָּה חוּץ בִּרְכַּת הַמִּינִים, שֶׁעַל־יְדֵי חַי בִּרְכָאָן, מְבַטְּלִין הַטֶּבַע אֵם כָּל חָי.' The explicit bechina language 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת' connects the act of nullifying nature directly to the 18 blessings — they share the same essential nature as the transcendent force that breaks through natural determination. תפלה (prayer), תפלותינו (our prayers), and חי ברכאן (18 blessings of the Amidah) are all different faces of the same reality: the human act of turning toward the divine and thereby overturning the fixed order of nature. מבטלין הטבע (nullifying nature) is what the 18 blessings essentially ARE in this teaching — not merely what they produce — making it a bechina relationship, not only eitza. The three dataset nodes for this Torah on the prayer side (תפלה, תפלותינו, חי ברכאן) all inhabit this single domain of transcendent prayer-as-nature-breaker.", "dominant_node": "תפלה", "nodes": ["תפלה", "תפלותינו", "חי ברכאן", "מבטלים הטבע", "מבטלין הטבע אם כל חי. ומכניעין ומבטלין המינים והאפיקורסים", "נשתנה הטבע"], "node_degrees": {"תפלה": 1, "תפלותינו": 1, "חי ברכאן": 1, "מבטלים הטבע": 1, "מבטלין הטבע אם כל חי. ומכניעין ומבטלין המינים והאפיקורסים": 1, "נשתנה הטבע": 1}, "note_on_nodes": "תפלה and תפלותינו (edges 2241 and 1690 respectively) are the same canonical concept (prayer) appearing in two grammatical forms — the dataset records them as separate node_ids but they represent the same domain. All three 'nullification' nodes (מבטלים הטבע, מבטלין הטבע אם כל חי..., נשתנה הטבע) describe the essential quality of prayer in this Torah: its nature as nature-transcending power. The bechina 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת חַ\"י בִּרְכָאָן' declares that the 18 blessings ARE this nullifying power by essential identity."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 216, "label": "הַטֶּבַע וְהַכּוֹפְרִים", "label_en": "Nature and the Deniers — The Anti-Prayer Force", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. The philosophers call nature 'אֵם כָּל חָי' — 'the mother of all living.' This teaching frames nature (הטבע) as the opposing force to prayer, the fixed deterministic order that prayer must transcend. The equation הטבע = אם כל חי is an explicit identity established by the philosophers and cited by the Rebbe — it is the conceptual anchor of the opposition. The מינים (heretics) and אפיקורסים (Epicureans) represent the human embodiment of this naturalistic worldview: those who deny divine intervention and hold that nature is the ultimate authority. They are precisely the group that the 18 blessings (Package 1) were designed to 'subdue and nullify' (מַכְנִיעִין וּמְבַטְּלִין). The structural parallel: just as Package 1's 18 blessings nullify the טבע, the inclusion of Birkat HaMinim (the blessing against heretics — excluded from the count of 18) targets the מינים/אפיקורסים. The Rebbe explicitly pairs these two acts ('מְבַטְּלִין הַטֶּבַע אֵם כָּל חָי. וּמַכְנִיעִין וּמְבַטְּלִין הַמִּינִים וְהָאֶפִּיקוֹרְסִים') — nullifying nature and subduing heretics are the paired outcomes, confirming that heretics/Epicureans are the human expression of the טבע-force. This is the good/evil mirror pattern per framework §4: Package 1 (prayer/18 blessings) → Package 2 (nature/heretics) as the structural opponent.", "dominant_node": "הטבע", "nodes": ["הטבע", "אם כל חי", "המינים והאפיקורסים"], "node_degrees": {"הטבע": 2, "אם כל חי": 1, "המינים והאפיקורסים": 1}, "note_on_nodes": "הטבע is the dominant node as it is the concept the Rebbe defines at the outset and the one that prayer opposes throughout. אם כל חי is the philosophers' name for הטבע — an explicit bechina identity within the source text ('הַפִילוֹסוֹפִים קוֹרִים לְהַטֶּבַע אֵם כָּל חָי'). המינים והאפיקורסים are the human representatives of the naturalistic worldview — those who deny providence and miracle, the living embodiment of the 'nature is supreme' claim. These three nodes share the essential nature of nature-as-supreme-law, the domain that prayer overturns. Note: הטבע and אם כל חי and המינים והאפיקורסים do not currently appear as named node_ids in the dataset's Torah-216 edges (the eitza edges record only the prayer-side nodes plus the compound result phrases). These are proposed as new nodes whose bechina relationships are explicit in the source text."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 217, "label": "הַלּוּחוֹת וָאו", "label_en": "The Tablets and the Letter Vav", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The sole bechina in Torah #217 is the explicit identification: 'הַלּוּחוֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת וָאו' — the Tablets ARE a bechina of the letter vav (stated twice in the text, lines 2 and 3). The proof is the Talmudic tradition (Bava Batra 14) that the Tablets were 6 cubits long and 6 cubits wide — their essential nature is sixness, and six is the numerical value of the letter vav. These two concepts — the Tablets and the letter vav — are different expressions of the same underlying reality: the sixfold structure that carried the Torah from Sinai. The package is marked good because both nodes belong entirely to the domain of holiness. The Tablets are the holy vessel of Torah par excellence. The letter vav in this Torah's context represents the spiritual dimension of Torah-transmission — its removal (via the breaking of the Tablets) is precisely what caused שִׁכְחָה (forgetting of Torah). There is no evil mirror in this teaching. The opposites — שִׁכְחָה and the broken state of the Tablets — are not developed as a parallel evil package; they are consequences described through eitza language ('עַל יְדֵי הַלּוּחוֹת שֶׁנִּשְׁתַּבְּרוּ נִתְהַוָּה הַשִּׁכְחָה'), not bechina identifications. The dominant node is הַלּוּחוֹת because it is the Rebbe's operative concept — all the causal chains in the teaching (broken → forgetting; given → Tammuz) flow from the Tablets, and the bechina equation with vav is the teaching's theoretical foundation.", "dominant_node": "הלוחות", "nodes": ["הלוחות", "אות וא\"ו"], "node_degrees": {"הלוחות": 1, "אות וא\"ו": 1}, "source_edges": [{"edge_id": 180, "node1_id": "הלוחות", "node2_id": "אות וא\"ו", "proof": "הַלּוּחוֹת הֵם בְּחִינַת וָאו, כְּמוֹ שֶׁאָמְרוּ רַבּוֹתֵינוּ זִכְרוֹנָם לִבְרָכָה (בָּבָא בַּתְרָא י\"ד): הַלּוּחוֹת אָרְכָּן שִׁשָּׁה וְרָחְבָּן שִׁשָּׁה"}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 219, "label": "אַיִן וָאֶפֶס וַעֲנָוָה", "label_en": "Nothingness and Humility", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts are all expressions of the same essential quality of self-nullification and non-being. 'לא' (the word 'not/no') is the linguistic form in which God reveals Himself to the humble person; 'אין ואפס' (nothingness and nullity) is the ontological reality that 'לא' points to; 'ענוה ושפלות' (humility and lowliness) is that same nothingness as a human spiritual posture. The Torah text explicitly chains all three: 'בחינת לא, שהוא בחינת אין ואפס, בחינת ענוה ושפלות'. These are not cause-and-effect — they are three faces of the same inner reality of spiritual self-erasure.", "dominant_node": "אין", "nodes": ["לא", "אין", "ענוה ושפלות"], "node_degrees": {"לא": 1, "אין": 2, "ענוה ושפלות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 219, "label": "מַלְכוּת וְיִרְאָה וְהַכְנָעָה", "label_en": "Kingship, Fear, and Submission", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three concepts share the essential nature of relational receptivity and self-emptying in the context of divine sovereignty. 'מלכות' (kingship) has no independent substance — it exists only through the people ('אין מלך בלא עם'), making it intrinsically a quality of receptive poverty. 'יראה' (fear/awe) is the subjective response that mirrors this same quality in the human soul — the soul holding itself as nothing before the King. 'הכנעה' (submission/humiliation) names the inner disposition that both Malkhut and Yirah share: the quality of bowing, yielding, having no self-assertion. The Torah explicitly states 'יראה בחינת מלכות הוא בחינת הכנעה', making this a single thematic family of concepts that all express the same essential mode of being — existing-for-another, not for oneself. The good/evil mirror for this package would be pride (גאוה) and self-assertion, which the Torah discusses as the force that destroys the Temple when unclothed — but גאוה itself is not equated via bechina to any nodes in this Torah.", "dominant_node": "מלכות", "nodes": ["מלכות", "יראה", "הכנעה"], "node_degrees": {"מלכות": 2, "יראה": 1, "הכנעה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 221, "label": "יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה וְהִסְתַּפְּקוּת", "label_en": "The Great Hand and Contentment/Sufficiency", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Both concepts express the same essential reality: divine infinite provision. The 'Great Hand' (יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה) is God's boundless capacity to provide — proven by the verse 'הֲיַד ה' תִּקְצָר' (Is God's hand too short? — Numbers 11:23). Contentment/sufficiency (הִסְתַּפְּקוּת) is the state of satiated abundance that proceeds from that infinite divine provision. The Rebbe equates them directly: 'יָד הַגְּדוֹלָה הוּא בְּחִינַת הִסְתַּפְּקוּת'. They are two faces of the same divine quality — inexhaustible adequacy. The one is the metaphysical/divine dimension (God's hand that never falls short), the other is the experiential/human dimension (the feeling of having enough). Together they form the positive domain of this Torah: the blessing that flows to the one who gives maaser (tithe).", "dominant_node": "יד הגדולה", "nodes": ["יד הגדולה", "הסתפקות"], "node_degrees": {"יד הגדולה": 1, "הסתפקות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 223, "label": "גְּזֵרַת הַצַּדִּיק וּבִרְכַּת כֹּהֲנִים", "label_en": "The Tzaddik's Decree and the Priestly Blessing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's sole bechina equates the tzaddik who can decree — who says 'I say it shall be so' — with the formula of the Priestly Blessing: 'So shall you bless... say to them (אָמוֹר לָהֶם).' The shared essential nature is authoritative, reality-creating declarative speech by the holy person. The Kohen does not petition God to bless; he declares the blessing as an already-established fact. The higher tzaddik similarly does not request — he decrees. Both are two expressions of the same capacity: holy speech that is performative, not petitionary. This contrasts structurally with the ordinary tzaddik who petitions (בַּקָּשָׁה), whose request may or may not be heard — that mode belongs outside this package as the narrative foil.", "dominant_node": "צדיק שיכול לגזור", "nodes": ["צדיק שיכול לגזור", "כֹּה תְבָרְכוּ... אָמוֹר לָהֶם"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק שיכול לגזור": 1, "כֹּה תְבָרְכוּ... אָמוֹר לָהֶם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 224, "label": "צַּדִּיק וְאִילָן — הַצַּדִּיק כְּבְחִינַת עֵץ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik as Tree — the Righteous One as Life-Giving Organism", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's sole teaching rests on a single extended bechina: the tzaddik shares the essential nature of a tree. A tree gives vitality to everything attached to it (branches, bark, leaves) and even to grasses far away by sheltering them from the sun. The tzaddik similarly gives vitality (חִיּוּת) and illumination (הֶאָרָה) to all who are attached to him, and even to those who are spiritually distant, by covering and protecting them. The tree-parts (branches, bark, leaves) are explicitly called a 'bechina' that the tzaddik possesses — they are structural aspects of the same life-distributing organism. The grasses (עֲשָׂבִים) = the distant ones (רְחוֹקִים) share the same nature: apparently-disconnected recipients who are in fact sustained by the central source through the mechanism of sheltering/covering (כִּסּוּי). All these concepts — tzaddik, tree, branches, bark, leaves, distant grasses, vitality, covering — are expressions of one domain: the reach of the tzaddik's life-force beyond his visible sphere of influence.", "dominant_node": "צדיק", "nodes": ["צדיק", "אילן", "ענפים", "קליפה", "עלין", "עשבים", "רחוקים מן הצדיק", "חיות והארה", "מכסה / מגן"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק": 4, "אילן": 4, "ענפים": 2, "קליפה": 1, "עלין": 2, "עשבים": 2, "רחוקים מן הצדיק": 2, "חיות והארה": 2, "מכסה / מגן": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 225, "label": "שֵׂכֶל וְנֵר הַדּוֹלֵק", "label_en": "Intellect and the Burning Candle", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All these concepts share the essential nature of the intellect (שֵׂכֶל) and its metaphorical inner structure: the eyes ARE the intellect ('עֵינֵי זֶה בְּחִינַת הַשֵּׂכֶל'), the sustained intellect IS a burning candle, and the moisture that rises to the brain IS the oil that feeds that candle. The Rebbe develops a unified lamp metaphor: the brain is the wick, the soul is the flame, the moisture (= oil) feeds it, and the eyes are the outward expression of this light. These three bechina pairs are all faces of the same essential domain — the intellect in its wholeness — and not a chain of cause-and-effect. The lungs sustain the moisture (an eitza), but the moisture being oil, the sekhel being a candle, and the eyes being sekhel are all parallel expressions of the same reality.", "dominant_node": "חכמה", "nodes": ["עיני", "חכמה", "קיום השכל", "נר דולק", "לחלוחית העולים אל המח", "שמן"], "node_degrees": {"עיני": 1, "חכמה": 1, "קיום השכל": 1, "נר דולק": 1, "לחלוחית העולים אל המח": 1, "שמן": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עיני", "חכמה"], "reason": "Eyes and sekhel/chokhmah are a direct bechina pair (edge 1144): 'עֵינֵי זֶה בְּחִינַת הַשֵּׂכֶל' — the eyes in the verse Ps. 145:15 are the intellect. Both belong to the intellect-domain."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קיום השכל", "נר דולק"], "reason": "The sustaining of the intellect IS a burning candle (edge 2200): 'קִיּוּם הַשֵּׂכֶל שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת נֵר דּוֹלֵק.' This is not a cause-effect but a parallel description — both the sustained sekhel and the burning candle are expressions of the same ignited-intellect reality."}, {"merged_nodes": ["לחלוחית העולים אל המח", "שמן"], "reason": "The moisture that rises to the brain IS the oil (edge null-id): 'הַלַּחְלוּחִית הָעוֹלִים אֶל הַמֹּחַ, שֶׁהֵם בְּחִינַת הַשֶּׁמֶן.' The moisture and the oil are two names for the same substance in the candle metaphor. Both belong to Package 1 because the candle (= sekhel) and its oil (= moisture/brain-nutrition) are the same essential domain: the intellect and what sustains it."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 225, "label": "אָדָם וְדִבּוּר", "label_en": "Man and Speech", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These two concepts share the essential nature of the human being defined by articulate speech: 'אָדָם שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת דִּבּוּר' (man is the bechina of speech). The power to cut letters from raw sound (חִתּוּךְ הַדִּבּוּר) is what distinguishes man from animal. They are not cause-and-effect — being a human does not produce speech as an external result; rather, speech IS what man IS. The same concept is expressed by the verse 'מִי שָׂם פֶּה לָאָדָם': the mouth-speech-humanity complex is one integral reality.", "dominant_node": "אדם", "nodes": ["אדם", "דבור"], "node_degrees": {"אדם": 1, "דבור": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 225, "label": "אֵין הַשֵּׂכֶל וּכְלוֹת הָעֵינַיִם", "label_en": "Failing Intellect and Exhausted Eyes", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This pair is the evil mirror of Package 1. When the sekhel is not whole ('אֵין הַשֵּׂכֶל בִּשְׁלֵמוּת'), it is expressed by the verse 'כָּלוּ עֵינַי' (my eyes have failed — Ps. 69:4). Just as in Package 1 the eyes ARE the intellect in its positive aspect ('עֵינֵי כֹל אֵלֶיךָ יְשַׂבֵּרוּ' = trusting through intellect), here the failed eyes express the intellect's absence. These are not cause-and-effect but the same negative reality seen from two angles: the deficiency of sekhel IS the failing of the eyes.", "dominant_node": "אין השכל בשלמות", "nodes": ["אין השכל בשלמות", "\"כלו עיני\""], "node_degrees": {"אין השכל בשלמות": 1, "\"כלו עיני\"": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 226, "label": "שַׁבָּת וְתִקּוּן הָרְאוּת", "label_en": "Shabbat and the Complete Repair of Sight", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Shabbat and 'the complete repair of sight' (תִּקּוּן הָרְאוּת בִּשְׁלֵמוּת) are two expressions of the same essential reality: the domain of holy, complete light. The Rebbe states explicitly 'שַׁבָּת שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת תִּקּוּן הָרְאוּת בִּשְׁלֵמוּת' — Shabbat IS the complete repair of sight. Both are facets of the same quality: the restoration of vision to its full, uncorrupted state. In the Torah's architecture, this package is the GOOD pole — the counterweight to the corrupted sight (קִלְקוּל הָרְאוּת) that is Lilit's domain. On Shabbat the defective luminaries (מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר, which the text identifies as Lilit) are restored to fullness. The two concepts are symmetric and mutually illuminating: Shabbat is the time when sight is whole; complete sight is what Shabbat embodies.", "dominant_node": "שבת", "nodes": ["שבת", "תקון הראות בשלמות"], "node_degrees": {"שבת": 1, "תקון הראות בשלמות": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "evil_mirror": "The evil counterpart of this package is the domain of קִלְקוּל הָרְאוּת (corruption of sight), which includes לִילִית (Lilit), מְאֹרֹת חָסֵר (defectively-spelled luminaries), עֵינֵי לֵאָה רַכּוֹת (the soft eyes of Leah), and נִשְׁמַת עֵרֶב רַב (the souls of the Erev Rav). This evil package is entirely absent from the dataset (see torah_226_additions.json and torah_226_new_packages.json for proposals). The structural mirror: Shabbat = complete light/sight vs. Lilit = defective/corrupted light/sight."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 227, "label": "רְשָׁעִים וַעֲשָׂבֵי הַגַּגּוֹת", "label_en": "The Wicked and the Rooftop Grasses", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups the wicked souls who lack elevation with their symbolic counterpart — grasses growing on rooftops. The Torah explicitly states (זֶה בְּחִינַת) that the verse 'יִהְיוּ כְּחָצִיר גַּגּוֹת' (Psalms 129:6) is the bechina of these reincarnated wicked souls. All three nodes share the same essential nature: they are beings that cannot be elevated because no one harvests them and no blessing is uttered over them. The rooftop location (גַּגּוֹת) is significant — they grow in isolation, untouched by human hands and divine blessing. The phrase 'שֶׁלֹּא מִלֵּא כַפּוֹ קוֹצֵר' (who does not fill his hand with harvesting) from Psalms confirms that the absence of reaping is their defining characteristic. These three expressions — the wicked person, the rooftop grass, and the Psalm's image — are all faces of the same metaphysical reality: existence without selection, without blessing, without ascent.", "dominant_node": "רשעים מגולגלים בעשבים על הגגות", "nodes": ["רשעים מגולגלים בעשבים על הגגות", "עשבים הגדלים על הגגות", "חציר גגות"], "node_degrees": {"רשעים מגולגלים בעשבים על הגגות": 1, "עשבים הגדלים על הגגות": 2, "חציר גגות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 227, "label": "תְּבוּאָה וַעֲלִיָּה", "label_en": "The Harvested Grain and Elevation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This cluster groups the concepts belonging to the positive pole of the teaching: the grain that is reaped, the blessing of God said over it, and the elevation it achieves. These three concepts share the same essential nature — they are expressions of the same reality: a being that is chosen (harvested), acknowledged (blessed), and thereby elevated. The Torah presents them as a unified mechanism: קְצִירָה (harvesting) → בִּרְכַּת ה' (the blessing said by passersby) → עֲלִיָּה (elevation/ascent of the soul). However, within the bechina logic, the harvested grain and elevation share identical nature — to be harvested IS to have elevation; the grain-that-rises is the same entity as the grain-that-is-reaped and the grain-that-is-blessed. These are three faces of the same spiritual reality: a soul that is selected by the tzaddik (represented by the reaper), blessed, and lifted. בִּרְכַּת ה' here functions as the vehicle of bechina — it is the identifying mark of the grain that merits elevation, and its absence from the rooftop grasses defines their opposite nature.", "dominant_node": "תבואה הנקצרת", "nodes": ["תבואה הנקצרת", "ברכת ה'", "עליה"], "node_degrees": {"תבואה הנקצרת": 2, "ברכת ה'": 2, "עליה": 2}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 233, "label": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת קְדוֹשׁוֹת וְחַיּוֹת טְהוֹרוֹת", "label_en": "Holy Thoughts and Pure Animals", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Holy thoughts and pure animals are two expressions of the same positive spiritual quality — the living volitional force (chayot) of the soul when it is oriented toward holiness. The Rebbe uses the metaphor of pure animals fighting in the arena to illuminate the nature of holy thoughts: both are the same spiritual force (chayot = living force) expressed in two registers — the metaphorical-physical (pure animals) and the mental-spiritual (holy thoughts). They are not cause and effect; they are the same essential reality seen from two angles.", "dominant_node": "מחשבות קדושות", "nodes": ["מחשבות קדושות", "חיות טהורות"], "node_degrees": {"מחשבות קדושות": 1, "חיות טהורות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 233, "label": "מַחֲשָׁבוֹת רָעוֹת וְחַיּוֹת טְמֵאוֹת", "label_en": "Evil Thoughts and Impure Animals", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Evil thoughts and impure animals are two expressions of the same negative spiritual quality — the living force of the soul when it is captured by impurity and opposition to holiness. The impure animals (chayot teme'ot) are the structural archetype; evil thoughts are the manifestation of the same impure living-force within the human mind. They share the same essential nature: they are both expressions of the unclean animating drive that opposes the sacred. This is the perfect evil mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 is the holy chayot-force expressed as holy thoughts, Package 2 is the same chayot-force expressed as evil thoughts — same structure, opposite orientation.", "dominant_node": "מחשבות רעות", "nodes": ["מחשבות רעות", "חיות טמאות"], "node_degrees": {"מחשבות רעות": 1, "חיות טמאות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 234, "label": "מַעֲשִׂיּוֹת שֶׁל צַדִּיקִים וְהַמָּאוֹר הַגָּדוֹל", "label_en": "Stories of the Righteous and the Great Light", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah 234 opens and closes with a single binary: the stories of the righteous ARE light (אור), and the stories of the wicked are darkness. The Rebbe then deepens this: 'סיפורי מעשיות של צדיקים הם בחינת מחין דגדלות' — the stories ARE expanded mind (the great intellect, the illuminated consciousness). The text also calls this 'המאור הגדול' (the Great Light), directly fusing the light=expanded-mind equation. Therefore: מעשיות של צדיקים = אור = ספורי מעשיות של צדיקים = מחין דגדלות = המאור הגדול. These are not four different things linked by cause-and-effect — they are four different angles on the same spiritual reality: holy consciousness, expanded beyond the constrictions of the ordinary mind, luminous and purifying. The bechina (shared nature) is: 'the quality of transcendent, purifying luminosity that belongs to the domain of holy stories and expanded awareness.' CC1 and CC4 are merged here because the two node pairs express the identical domain from different angles — stories as LIGHT (CC1) and stories as EXPANDED MIND (CC4) — and the Torah text makes explicit that great light and expanded mind are the same thing ('המאור הגדול' = 'מחין דגדלות').", "dominant_node": "מחין דגדלות", "nodes": ["מעשיות של צדיקים", "אור", "ספורי מעשיות של צדיקים", "מחין דגדלות"], "node_degrees": {"מעשיות של צדיקים": 1, "אור": 1, "ספורי מעשיות של צדיקים": 1, "מחין דגדלות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["ספורי מעשיות של צדיקים", "מחין דגדלות"], "reason": "CC4 merged into Package 1 (from CC1). The proof text for edge id=1752 is: 'סיפורי מעשיות של צדיקים הם בחינת מחין דגדלות, כמו שכתוב: ספרה נא לי את הגדלות.' The Torah text then explicitly links this to 'המאור הגדול' — the Great Light — which is the same אור as in CC1. This is a shared-nature merge: stories=light (CC1) and stories=expanded-mind (CC4) are two bechinas of the same domain of holy luminous consciousness. Not eitza: the stories do not produce the light as a causal effect; they ARE the light and they ARE the expanded mind."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 234, "label": "מֹחִין דְּקַטְנוּת וְהַדִּינִין", "label_en": "Constricted Mind and the Forces of Judgment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "This cluster groups all concepts that share the essential nature of spiritual constriction, darkness of the mind, and the dimension of din (harsh judgment). בלבול הדעת (confusion/turbulence of the mind) IS מחין דקטנות (constricted awareness — the opposite of expanded, luminous consciousness); and מחין דקטנות IS דינין (harsh judgments, tzarot). This is not a causal chain — it is one underlying reality seen from three angles: the psychological angle (confusion = constricted mind), the structural angle (constricted mind = din), and together they form the evil counterpart of the Great Light of Package 1. The Torah teaches that this 'small mind' is what draws evil thoughts and causes tzarot: 'בלבול הדעת הוא בחינת מחין דקטנות... מחין דקטנות הם בחינת דינין.' All three nodes share the nature of 'the constricted, darkened dimension of consciousness that generates suffering.' CC2 is the mirror of Package 1's expanded, luminous consciousness.", "dominant_node": "מחין דקטנות", "nodes": ["בלבול הדעת", "מחין דקטנות", "דין"], "node_degrees": {"מחין דקטנות": 2, "בלבול הדעת": 1, "דין": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 234, "label": "מַעֲשֵׂה הָרְשָׁעִים וְהַחֹשֶׁךְ", "label_en": "Deeds of the Wicked and Darkness", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The direct evil mirror of Package 1's core bechina. Package 1 states: 'מעשיות של צדיקים הם בחינת אור.' Package 3 is the explicit evil counterpart stated in the same sentence of the Torah: 'מעשיות של רשעים הם בחינת חשך.' The structural parallelism is exact and deliberate — the Rebbe invokes the Midrash on Bereishit (separating light from darkness) to establish this polarity. עשיות של רשעים IS חשך — the deeds of the wicked are not merely dark, they ARE darkness, just as the deeds of the righteous are not merely illuminating, they ARE light. This is the behavioral/external evil cluster, as opposed to Package 2 which is the psychological/internal evil cluster. Together, Packages 2 and 3 represent the two dimensions of the חשך that Package 1's אור must overcome.", "dominant_node": "חשך", "nodes": ["עשיות של רשעים", "חשך"], "node_degrees": {"עשיות של רשעים": 1, "חשך": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 235, "label": "שִׂמְחַת יוֹם טוֹב — רֶגֶל וּמוֹעֵד", "label_en": "Yom Tov Joy — Festival as Foot and Appointed Time", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The three nodes in this package all share the same essential nature: they are three aspects of the sacred appointed festival as a spiritual vessel of joy. The Torah establishes two explicit 'niqra' bechinas: Yom Tov IS CALLED רגל (foot/pilgrimage-festival) AND Yom Tov IS CALLED מועד (appointed time). These are not cause-and-effect — Yom Tov does not produce רגל; it IS רגל. They are the same sacred reality named from three angles: the vertical time-dimension (מועד = the appointed rendezvous with God), the embodied-movement dimension (רגל = the foot, the pilgrimage, the treading forward), and the holistic festival itself (יום טוב = the good day of divine joy). The linguistic play that the entire Torah hinges on — רגל מועד/רגלו מועדה — depends on the identity of these three as one: the damaged festival becomes a literal slipping foot precisely because the festival IS the foot-concept.", "dominant_node": "יום טוב", "nodes": ["יום טוב", "רגל", "מועד"], "node_degrees": {"יום טוב": 2, "רגל": 1, "מועד": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 235, "label": "שְׂחוֹק הָעוֹלָם — שִׂמְחוֹת נְפוּלוֹת", "label_en": "The World's Laughter — Fallen Joys", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah states explicitly: 'וזה השחוק שׂשׂוחקין הוא בחינת השמחות נפולות מפגם שמחת יום טוב' — 'this laughter that they laugh is a bechina of the fallen joys from the damage of Yom Tov joy.' This is the clearest bechina statement in the entire Torah, using the exact formula 'הוא בחינת'. The laughter of the world IS the fallen joys; they are the same metaphysical substance in two descriptions — the fallen Yom Tov joy-energy does not produce the laughter, it IS the laughter. These are the degraded sparks of joy-energy (שמחות) that fell from their proper vessel (the festival) and now manifest as the world's mockery. The evil polarity is inherent: these are joy-sparks in a fallen state, disconnected from their holy source.", "dominant_node": "שמחות נפולות", "nodes": ["שחוק", "שמחות נפולות"], "node_degrees": {"שחוק": 1, "שמחות נפולות": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 235, "label": "עֲבוֹדַת אֱלִילִים וּבֹשֶׁת — בִּזָּיוֹן הַמּוֹעֲדוֹת", "label_en": "Idol Worship and Shame — Disgracing the Festivals", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Three concepts share the essential nature of the 'sin-as-shame' axis in this Torah. (1) 'עבודת אלילים נקרא בשת' — idol worship IS CALLED shame/בשת (Hosea 9) — explicit niqra bechina. (2) 'המבזה את המועדות כאלו עובד עכו\"ם' — one who disgraces the Moadim is AS IF worshipping idolatry (Pesachim 118) — functional identity (כאלו = as if, expressing the same essential sin-category). Therefore: disgracing the Moadim IS idol worship IS shame — three descriptions of the same essential spiritual reality on the evil side. The dominant node is עבודת אלילים because it connects both to בשת (direct niqra) and to בזיון המועדות (via the halakhic equivalence). This is the evil mirror of Package 1: Package 1 is the holy festival (מועד) as sacred vessel; Package 3 is the desecration of that same festival (בזיון מועדות) as idolatry and shame. The same object — the מועד/Yom Tov — appears in its holy form in Package 1 and its desecrated form (בזיון מועדות) in Package 3.", "dominant_node": "עבודת אלילים", "nodes": ["עבודת אלילים", "בשת", "בזיון מועדות"], "node_degrees": {"עבודת אלילים": 2, "בשת": 1, "בזיון מועדות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["בשת", "בזיון מועדות"], "reason": "These two nodes are linked through עבודת אלילים as a hub. בשת is the bechina of עבודת אלילים ('עבודת אלילים נקרא בשת'), and בזיון מועדות is functionally equivalent to עבודת אלילים ('כאלו עובד עכו\"ם'). Both express the same essential nature of the evil/shame domain — they are not cause-and-effect (the disgrace does not produce the shame; the disgrace IS shame because disgrace IS idolatry IS shame — a transitive bechina chain)."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 237, "label": "נִגּוּן וְלֵוִי — הַנִּגּוּן כִּבְחִינַת הַהִתְחַבְּרוּת", "label_en": "Melody and Levi — Music as the Aspect of Joining/Union", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts share the essential nature of 'joining and attachment between two things.' Levi IS melody (the Zohar states melody comes from the side of Levi). Melody and musical instruments ARE the bechina of union (hitḥabrut). The wedding instruments are this same bechina — a wedding IS the joining of two people, enacted through music. All four concepts are different expressions of the single underlying reality: the power of attachment and union. The linguistic root ל-ו-ה (Levi / yilaveh / musical accompaniment) unifies them explicitly in the text.", "dominant_node": "נגון וכלי שיר", "nodes": ["לוי", "נגון וכלי שיר", "התחברות", "כלי זמר על חתונה", "חתונה"], "node_degrees": {"נגון וכלי שיר": 3, "לוי": 2, "התחברות": 2, "כלי זמר על חתונה": 1, "חתונה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["כלי זמר על חתונה", "חתונה"], "reason": "These are explicitly identified as the same bechina as the above (וְזֶה בְּחִינַת כְּלֵי זֶמֶר שֶׁמְּנַגְּנִין עַל חֲתֻנָּה). The wedding and its music are not a separate domain — they are the concrete instantiation of the joining-through-melody principle. They belong to the same package."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 241, "label": "מִדַּת הַדִּין וַחֲמַת ה' וְהַקִּנְאָה", "label_en": "The Attribute of Judgment, God's Wrath, and Divine Jealousy", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah uses three terms interchangeably for the same heavenly force of punitive justice: (1) מִדַּת הַדִּין (the attribute of judgment) — the force that 'wants to consume' a person under sins; (2) חֲמַת ה' (God's wrath) — which Pinchas 'returned'; (3) קִנְאָה / קִנְאָתִי (divine jealousy/zeal) — explicitly glossed in line 5 as 'my attribute of judgment': 'בְּקִנְאָתִי, בְּמִדַּת הַדִּין שֶׁלִּי.' The fourth expression, דִּינִים (judgments, plural), is the concrete manifestation of this force operating against an individual — it is the plural/actualized form of מידת הדין in action. All four are different expressions of the same single metaphysical reality: the dimension of divine retributive justice that presses against the sinner but cannot fully consume Israel. They are classified as 'evil' (the opposing force) in the framework of this Torah — their polarity is the negative pressure that the tzaddik strategically redirects.", "dominant_node": "מידת הדין", "nodes": ["מידת הדין", "חמת ה'", "קנאה", "דינים"], "node_degrees": {"מידת הדין": 3, "חמת ה'": 1, "קנאה": 1, "דינים": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["חמת ה'", "מידת הדין"], "reason": "Lines 4–5: 'פִּינְחָס הֵשִׁיב אֶת חֲמַת ה' וְסִלֵּק מִדַּת הַדִּין' — these two terms are used in parallel in the same sentence and treated as one reality: returning the wrath IS displacing the judgment. Same essential nature, not cause-effect."}, {"merged_nodes": ["קנאה", "מידת הדין"], "reason": "Line 5: 'בְּקִנְאָתִי, בְּמִדַּת הַדִּין שֶׁלִּי' — explicit 'הינו' (definitional gloss) equating God's jealousy with His attribute of judgment. This is the clearest bechina statement in the Torah."}, {"merged_nodes": ["דינים", "מידת הדין"], "reason": "'דינים' (judgments) is the plural/actualized presence of 'מידת הדין' — the abstract attribute manifesting as concrete pressures against a person. The Torah treats them as the same force (line 2: 'כשיש דינים... מידת הדין מסתלק ממנו'). They share the same essential nature: divine punitive pressure."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 241, "label": "הַצַּדִּיק הַגָּדוֹל וּפִינְחָס", "label_en": "The Great Tzaddik and Pinchas", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah presents two expressions of the same archetype: the 'great tzaddik who watches over the rectification of the world' (line 3) and Pinchas ben Elazar (line 4). The teaching explicitly applies the abstract principle to Pinchas as its proof-text — meaning Pinchas IS the 'tzaddik gadol who oversees tikkun haolam' in this context. They share the same essential nature: the righteous human agent who deliberately dons the appearance of vengeance in order to displace divine judgment and ultimately act with mercy. Note: this is a weaker bechina claim than Package 1 (no explicit bechina language is used), but the structural equivalence of the two figures is clear from the teaching's logic: 'this is what such a tzaddik does — and THIS is the meaning of [Pinchas].' The 'הינו' (line 4) linking the verse to the principle makes Pinchas the concrete instance of the abstract tzaddik.", "dominant_node": "פינחס", "nodes": ["צדיק גדול המשגיח בתיקון העולם", "פינחס"], "node_degrees": {"פינחס": 1, "צדיק גדול המשגיח בתיקון העולם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 242, "label": "הִרְהוּרֵי נִאוּף וְאֲבִי אָבוֹת הַטֻּמְאָה", "label_en": "Lecherous Thoughts and Ultimate Impurity", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes are different names for the same domain of spiritual death through sexual impurity. In Halacha, a corpse (מֵת) is called 'אֲבִי אָבוֹת הַטֻּמְאָה' — the ultimate source of impurity, the first degree from which all other impurity flows. The Rebbe equates lecherous thoughts (הִרְהוּרִים שֶׁל נִאוּף) with this same halakhic category, meaning they carry the same spiritual weight as a corpse. All three concepts are expressions of one and the same domain: the maximum degree of spiritual death and ritual impurity in the domain of sexual sin. They are not cause-and-effect but parallel faces of the same evil reality.", "dominant_node": "אבי אבות הטמאה", "nodes": ["הרהורים של נאוף", "אבי אבות הטמאה", "מת"], "node_degrees": {"הרהורים של נאוף": 1, "אבי אבות הטמאה": 2, "מת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 243, "label": "צַדִּיק גָּדוֹל וְשִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים — קְדֻשָּׁה הַנֶּעֱלֶמֶת", "label_en": "The Great Tzaddik and Song of Songs — Concealed Holiness", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah is built on a single bechina: the Tzaddik Gadol who conceals himself is the same essential phenomenon as Shir HaShirim, which is Kodesh Kadashim yet contains no explicit words of holiness or purity. Both share the identical nature — supreme, transcendent holiness that is precisely because of its intensity invisible to ordinary perception. The world cannot bear the Tzaddik's holiness, so he appears ordinary; readers cannot 'see' holiness in Shir HaShirim, so no holiness-words appear. The concealment (העלם) is not a deficiency but the signature of the highest degree of holiness (קדש קדשים). These three concepts — the Tzaddik, Shir HaShirim, and the state of concealed holiness — form a single thematic family united by the principle: extreme holiness effaces its own outward markers.", "dominant_node": "צדיק גדול", "nodes": ["צדיק גדול", "שיר השירים", "קדש קדשים", "העלם / התעלמות"], "node_degrees": {"צדיק גדול": 2, "שיר השירים": 2, "קדש קדשים": 1, "העלם / התעלמות": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "source_derived": true, "source_quote": "וְזֶה בְּחִינוֹת: כָּל הַשִּׁירִים קֹדֶשׁ, וְשִׁיר הַשִּׁירִים קֹדֶשׁ קָדָשִׁים — וְזֶה מֵחֲמַת גֹּדֶל עֹצֶם קְדֻשָּׁתוֹ אֵינָם רוֹאִים שָׁם שׁוּם קְדֻשָּׁה"}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 245, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְאָדָם", "label_en": "Torah and the Human Being", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Torah and Adam (the human being) share the same essential nature: reaching the true Torah IS reaching the true human form. They are not cause-and-effect — one does not produce the other. Rather, they are two expressions of the same divine reality. The verse 'זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה אָדָם' (Num. 19:14) declares their identity. Just as there are multiple levels of representing a human being (written word, drawing, wooden sculpture, the actual person), so there are levels of Torah knowledge — and only the innermost, true Torah is the true Adam. The Torah's entire argument is built on this bechina identity.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אדם"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "אדם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 246, "label": "גַּדְלוּת וְתַעֲנִית", "label_en": "Controlled Greatness and Fasting", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah's central and only explicit bechina equivalence: gadlut (greatness/deliberate pride) IS like a fast ('הוּא מוֹעִיל כְּמוֹ תַּעֲנִית'). These two concepts share the same essential nature — both cause the forgetting of previous wisdom, thereby clearing the mind for a higher comprehension (hashagah). The Rebbe's proof is the story of Rabbi Zeira who fasted to forget the Babylonian Talmud so he could ascend to Torat Eretz Yisrael. Both gadlut and ta'anit produce this same effect: 'כִּי עַל־יְדֵי הַגַּדְלוּת שׁוֹכֵחַ גַּם כֵּן אֶת חָכְמָתוֹ' — through gadlut one also forgets one's wisdom, just as through fasting. They are two expressions of the same spiritual mechanism: voluntary self-emptying of lower wisdom as a gateway to higher attainment. Polarity is 'good' because this form of gadlut is the controlled, intentional kind ('לִבּוֹ בְּדַרְכֵי ה׳' — his heart lifted in the ways of God), used as a sacred technique by one who is also genuinely humble.", "dominant_node": "גדלות", "nodes": ["גדלות", "תענית"], "node_degrees": {"גדלות": 1, "תענית": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 246, "label": "גַּאֲוָה וְשׁוֹטֶה", "label_en": "Genuine Pride and Foolishness", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The evil mirror of Package 1. This cluster groups the negative-polarity concepts that share the essential nature of uncontrolled pride resulting in the total loss of wisdom. The Rebbe states: 'וְאִם יִתְגָּאֶה בֶּאֱמֶת, בְּוַדַּאי חָכְמָתוֹ תִּסְתַּלֵּק מִמֶּנּוּ לְגַמְרֵי... וְיִהְיֶה נַעֲשֶׂה שׁוֹטֶה מַמָּשׁ' — if one is genuinely proud, one's wisdom departs completely and one becomes an actual fool. This is a bechina-type statement: genuine gaavah IS foolishness — they are two expressions of the same reality (the one who has lost the divine image). The Talmudic citation (Pesachim 66b) reinforces this: 'כָּל הַמִּתְגָּאֶה חָכְמָתוֹ מִסְתַּלֶּקֶת מִמֶּנּוּ' — pride and the departure of wisdom are inseparable. Gaavah and shote share the same essential nature: both represent the person in whom the divine wisdom has been extinguished. This is the evil mirror of the good package: gadlut (controlled) = ta'anit; gaavah (uncontrolled) = shote. The good version uses the same mechanism redemptively; the evil version is the mechanism run amok.", "dominant_node": "גאוה", "nodes": ["גאוה", "שוטה"], "node_degrees": {"גאוה": 1, "שוטה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 247, "label": "תֵּיקוּ וּמְחֻסַּר תִּקּוּן — מַצָּב הַגָּלוּת", "label_en": "Teiku and Lacking Repair — The State of Exile", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "Teiku (the unresolved Talmudic question marker) and mechusar tikkun (lacking repair/completion) share the same essential nature: both are expressions of the unrectified, incomplete, exilic state. They are not cause-and-effect — one does not produce the other. Rather, they are two faces of the same phenomenon: the absence of the nun of tikkun. Teiku IS mechusar tikkun (the Zohar declares this identity directly). The entire Torah is built on this single bechina. The kinot (lamentations) belong to the same domain — same letters as tikkun but with the nun bent, the visual embodiment of the fallen/exile state. The polarity is evil/deficient: this cluster represents what is broken, unrectified, and in exile, in contrast to the aspired-to state of tikkun (repair) which is the implicit good pole of the teaching.", "dominant_node": "תיקו", "nodes": ["תיקו", "מחסר תקון"], "node_degrees": {"תיקו": 1, "מחסר תקון": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "good_evil_mirror": {"this_package_polarity": "evil", "mirror_package": "NOT_IN_DATASET", "mirror_label": "תִּיקּוּן — Complete Repair", "mirror_note": "The positive pole of this Torah is תִּיקּוּן (complete repair) — the state where the nun is upright and present. This is the aspired-to state, stated explicitly at the end of the Torah ('may the kinot be transformed into tikkun'). However, tikkun itself does not appear as a dataset node for Torah #247 — it is the goal, not yet catalogued. A future package around תִּיקּוּן and the upright נ would be its mirror."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 249, "label": "גְּבוּרָה וְלֵב", "label_en": "Strength and the Heart", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah opens with 'עִקַּר הַגְּבוּרָה בְּהַלֵּב' — the essence of gevurah IS the heart. Gevurah, the heart as its seat, conquering the evil inclination (אֵיזֶהוּ גִּבּוֹר הַכּוֹבֵשׁ אֶת יִצְרוֹ), and Samson's feats are all presented as different expressions of the same underlying reality: inner strength rooted in the heart. The Rebbe uses 'וְזֶה בְּחִינַת' twice to equate these concepts, marking them as parallel aspects of one domain. The conquering of the yetzer hara is not a result of gevurah (eitza) — it IS gevurah, i.e. the inner battle and the outer battle are the same thing from different angles.", "dominant_node": "גבורה", "nodes": ["גבורה", "לב", "כובש את יצרו", "גבורות שמשון"], "node_degrees": {"גבורה": 3, "לב": 1, "כובש את יצרו": 1, "גבורות שמשון": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "note": "This package is entirely derived from source text gap analysis — none of these nodes exist in torahData.js for Torah 249. All are proposed additions (see torah_249_additions.json)."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 250, "label": "הַשְׁגָּחָה וּתְפִלָּה", "label_en": "Divine Providence and Prayer", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All four nodes express the same essential reality: transcendence of natural law through Divine attention. Prayer (תפלה) is itself an act of hashgacha — it changes nature, which only hashgacha can do. Greatness (גדול) is Israel's quality of having God respond to their prayers, which is precisely hashgacha. Tears (דמעות) physically carry 'sight' (ראות) into themselves, and ראות is the faculty through which hashgacha operates — hence tears draw down hashgacha. All four are different expressions of the one reality: God's active, personal, above-nature relationship with Israel.", "dominant_node": "השגחה", "nodes": ["השגחה", "תפלה", "גדול", "דמעות"], "node_degrees": {"השגחה": 3, "תפלה": 2, "גדול": 2, "דמעות": 1}, "edge_count": 5, "edges": [{"edge_id": 609, "node1_id": "תפלה", "node2_id": "גדול", "proof": "וּתְפִלָּה הוּא בְּחִינַת הַשְׁגָּחָה לְמַעְלָה מֵהַטֶּבַע, כִּי הַטֶּבַע מְחַיֵּב כָּךְ, וּתְפִלָּה מְשַׁנָּה הַטֶּבַע, וְהוּא בְּחִינוֹת גָּדוֹל, בְּחִינַת (דְּבָרִים ד): \"כִּי מִי גוֹי גָּדוֹל אֲשֶׁר לוֹ אֱלֹקִים קְרוֹבִים אֵלָיו\""}, {"edge_id": 965, "node1_id": "תפלה", "node2_id": "השגחה", "proof": "וּתְפִלָּה הוּא בְּחִינַת הַשְׁגָּחָה לְמַעְלָה מֵהַטֶּבַע, כִּי הַטֶּבַע מְחַיֵּב כָּךְ, וּתְפִלָּה מְשַׁנָּה הַטֶּבַע,"}, {"edge_id": 966, "node1_id": "גדול", "node2_id": "השגחה", "proof": "גָּדוֹל בְּחִינַת הַשְׁגָּחָה"}, {"edge_id": 967, "node1_id": "דמעות", "node2_id": "השגחה", "proof": "דְּמָעוֹת הֵם בְּחִינוֹת הַשְׁגָּחָה, כְּמָה שֶׁאָמְרוּ רַבּוֹתֵינוּ, זִכְרוֹנָם לִבְרָכָה (שַׁבָּת קנא:): עַל פָּסוּק (קֹהֶלֶת י\"ב): \"וְשָׁבוּ הֶעָבִים אַחַר הַגָּשֶׁם\", 'זֶה הָרְאוּת שֶׁהוֹלֵךְ אַחַר הַבֶּכִי'"}, {"edge_id": 3146, "node1_id": "השגחה", "node2_id": "תפלה", "proof": "הַשְׁגָּחָה, בְּחִינוֹת תְּפִלָּה"}], "mirror_note": "The evil counterpart domain — הטבע / מזל / עכו\"ם — is structurally present in the Torah (the nations operate under nature/fate rather than hashgacha) but does not form a bechina subgraph in the dataset. Those nodes appear only as eitza endpoints. No evil-polarity package can be built from the current bechina edges.", "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 251, "label": "אֱמֶת וּצְדָקָה וְשֶׁמֶשׁ", "label_en": "Truth, Charity, and the Sun", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These five nodes all share the same essential nature: they are different faces of the single, unified force of divine truth (אמת). The Torah teaches that צדקה is a bechina of אמת because truth is singular and so is charity — both have no multiplicity or falsehood. שמש is a bechina of אמת because the sun is one undivided power and change occurs only in its receivers, not in the sun itself — exactly as God's truth is unchanging and the variation lies only with those who receive it. יעקב is a bechina of אמת because the verse explicitly states 'תתן אמת ליעקב'. The 'man of complete mitzvot' is a bechina of אמת because his inner and outer behavior are identical — no gap between public and private, which is the very definition of truth. All five nodes express the same quality: wholeness, unity, the absence of internal contradiction or falsehood.", "dominant_node": "אמת", "nodes": ["אמת", "צדקה", "שמש", "יעקב", "שעושה מצוות בשלמות ובכל הדקדוקים בינו לבין קונו כמו שמדקדק לעשות המצוה בפני בני אדם"], "node_degrees": {"אמת": 4, "צדקה": 2, "שמש": 2, "יעקב": 1, "שעושה מצוות בשלמות ובכל הדקדוקים בינו לבין קונו כמו שמדקדק לעשות המצוה בפני בני אדם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 254, "label": "עֵינַיִם וּמַרְאוֹת — רְאִיָּה כְּבְחִינָה עֶלְיוֹנָה", "label_en": "Eyes and Visions — Sight as a Lofty Spiritual Faculty", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah opens with an explicit IS-statement: 'עֵינַיִם הֵם דְּבָרִים עֶלְיוֹנִים וּגְבוֹהִים מְאֹד' — Eyes ARE lofty and exalted things. This IS-statement (not a cause-effect) identifies eyes as belonging to the realm of elevated spiritual realities. The text then uses a parenthetical bechina equivalence to identify the 'דְּבָרִים גְּדוֹלִים' (great things) that eyes always see AS 'מַרְאוֹת וְחֶזְיוֹנוֹת' (visions and revelations): '(הַיְנוּ מַרְאוֹת וְחֶזְיוֹנוֹת)'. Therefore: eyes = lofty realities; great things seen through eyes = visions and revelations. The further concept of 'עֵינַיִם כְּשֵׁרִים' (worthy/proper eyes) is the eyes in their fully-realized state — not a separate entity but the same eyes in their ideal dimension. כֹּחַ הָרְאוּת (the power of sight) is the active force of the eyes — again the same reality named from a different angle. All these nodes share the essential nature of 'vision as a spiritual instrument that directly perceives divine greatness.' This is a single thematic family with no evil mirror present in this short teaching.", "dominant_node": "עינים", "nodes": ["עינים", "דברים עליונים וגבוהים", "מראות וחזיונות", "דברים גדולים ונוראים", "עינים כשרים", "כח הראות"], "node_degrees": {"עינים": 3, "דברים עליונים וגבוהים": 1, "מראות וחזיונות": 1, "דברים גדולים ונוראים": 2, "עינים כשרים": 1, "כח הראות": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עינים", "דברים עליונים וגבוהים"], "reason": "The text opens with 'עֵינַיִם הֵם דְּבָרִים עֶלְיוֹנִים וּגְבוֹהִים מְאֹד' — a direct IS-bechina equating eyes with lofty spiritual realities. These two are the primary bechina pair of the teaching."}, {"merged_nodes": ["דברים גדולים ונוראים", "מראות וחזיונות"], "reason": "The text uses a parenthetical clarification '(הַיְנוּ מַרְאוֹת וְחֶזְיוֹנוֹת)' to identify the great and wondrous things the eyes see AS visions and revelations — a bechina equivalence through the word הַיְנוּ (i.e., that is to say)."}, {"merged_nodes": ["עינים כשרים", "עינים"], "reason": "עינים כשרים (worthy eyes) are not a different entity but the same eyes in their ideal/complete state. The text says 'if a person merited worthy eyes, they would know great things just from seeing' — this is the eyes in their fullness, the same essential spiritual faculty. Bechina: עינים כשרים IS עינים in their realized dimension."}, {"merged_nodes": ["כח הראות", "עינים"], "reason": "כֹּחַ הָרְאוּת (the power of sight/vision) is the active force of the eyes — the text treats it as the operative faculty of the eyes themselves: 'יָבוֹא וְיוֹשִׁיט כֹּחַ הָרְאוּת לְהַדַּעַת'. It is the same essential reality as the eyes, named from the angle of their function rather than their identity."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 257, "label": "אֲכִילָה יְתֵרָה וְהַכְּלִי", "label_en": "Overeating and the Vessel", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The central and only bechina identification in Torah #257 is the equation of eating beyond one's need (אכילה יתרה / אוכל יותר מצרכו) with placing food into a vessel (כלי). These two concepts share the same essential nature: both describe a state in which there is no living recipient to receive the food's vitality. A vessel cannot receive vitality — it is inert, it cannot use what is placed in it. Eating beyond need produces the same structural state: the extra food has no living root from which to receive vitality, since the person does not actually need it. The Rebbe states this equivalence twice: once descriptively ('הוא דומה כאילו הניחו האדם לתוך כלי') and once as the hermeneutic key to the verse ('הינו שלא תאכל יותר מצרכך, שזה דומה כנותן לתוך הכלי'). Polarity is 'neutral' because the keli is a structural/physical object used as a metaphor — it is not aligned with holiness or evil in this Torah; it is a neutral structural concept that describes the inert state of unclaimed food.", "dominant_node": "אכילה יתרה", "nodes": ["אכילה יתרה", "כלי"], "node_degrees": {"אכילה יתרה": 1, "כלי": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 258, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וְרוֹדְפִים", "label_en": "Controversy and Pursuers", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah opens with the principle that machloket (controversy directed against a person) is a force that can sway him from God's path. The Rebbe then invokes David's verse — 'רַבִּים רוֹדְפַי וְצָרָי' (many are my pursuers and adversaries) — as the bechina of this principle. The phrase 'וְזֶה שֶׁהִתְפָּאֵר' (and this is what David boasted) is the Rebbe's standard bechina marker: David's pursuers and adversaries ARE the bechina of machloket. These two concepts share the same essential nature — they are both expressions of the hostile external force that threatens to displace a person from his spiritual standing. Machloket in the abstract and 'rodoفai v'tzarai' in the concrete are two faces of the same reality. Both are evil-polarity: they represent the opposing force directed against the person of integrity.", "dominant_node": "מחלוקת", "nodes": ["מחלוקת", "רודפי וצרי"], "node_degrees": {"מחלוקת": 1, "רודפי וצרי": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "note": "This package is entirely derived from source-text gap analysis — neither node exists in torahData.js for Torah 258. Both are proposed additions (see torah_258_additions.json)."}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 258, "label": "דֶּרֶךְ הַשֵּׁם וְעֵדוּת", "label_en": "The Path of God and His Testimonies", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah presents the positive counterpart to machloket: what one must not be swayed from. The phrase 'דֶּרֶךְ הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ' (the path of God) appears in the teaching's opening principle, and the verse cited — 'מֵעֵדוֹתֶיךָ לֹא נָטִיתִי' (from Your testimonies I did not turn) — is the Rebbe's bechina for the same concept in David's mouth. The teaching closes by restating the principle: 'לֹא נָטָה מִדֶּרֶךְ הַשֵּׁם יִתְבָּרַךְ' — and this is the exact parallel to 'מֵעֵדוֹתֶיךָ לֹא נָטִיתִי'. God's testimonies (עֵדוֹת) ARE the path of God (דֶּרֶךְ הַשֵּׁם) — they are two expressions of the same reality: the spiritual ground on which a person stands and from which controversy seeks to dislodge him. Both concepts share the same essential nature: the divine anchor that preserves a person's level (מַדְרֵגָה) in the face of external hostility.", "dominant_node": "דרך השם", "nodes": ["דרך השם", "עדותיך"], "node_degrees": {"דרך השם": 1, "עדותיך": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "note": "This package is entirely derived from source-text gap analysis — neither node exists in torahData.js for Torah 258. Both are proposed additions (see torah_258_additions.json)."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 259, "label": "הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת וּשְׁכִינָה — גִּלּוּי שִׂיחָה הַדָּדִי", "label_en": "Hitbodedut and the Shekhinah — Mutual Speech/Disclosure", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The entire Torah is built on a single bechina equation: the reciprocal dynamic between the human who articulates his speech and pain before God (hitbodedut, viduy, charata) and the Shekhinah who correspondingly articulates her speech and pain before him (and comforts him) — this IS the bechina of the verse 'אֶת ה' הֶאֱמַרְתָּ וַה' הֶאֱמִירְךָ' (You declared the Lord, and the Lord declared you — Deuteronomy 26:17). All concepts in the Torah are aspects of this one mutual-disclosure reality: hitbodedut, articulating speech and pain, confession and regret on the human side; the Shekhinah's speech and pain, her consolation, and the tachbulot (remedies) on the divine side. These are not cause-and-effect (the human's hitbodedut does not 'produce' the Shekhinah's speech as a separate event — rather, they are structurally parallel, two expressions of the same cosmic reality of mutual declaration). The soul-flaw (פְּגָם בַּנְּשָׁמָה) and the Shekhinah-flaw (פָּגַם אֶצְלָהּ) form an internal bechina pair within the same package: the human's flaw IS the Shekhinah's flaw, as stated explicitly ('כָּל פְּגָם וּפְגָם שֶׁפָּגַם בְּנִשְׁמָתוֹ, פָּגַם אֶצְלָהּ גַּם כֵּן כִּבְיָכוֹל'). This shared flaw-reality and the shared speech-reality are both instances of the same structural equivalence captured in 'אֶת ה' הֶאֱמַרְתָּ וַה' הֶאֱמִירְךָ'. Polarity is 'good': the teaching is entirely about the positive dimension — the holy practice of hitbodedut, the Shekhinah's holy response, and the path of repair.", "dominant_node": "הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת", "nodes": ["הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת", "פְּרִישׁוּת שִׂיחָה וְצַעַר", "וִידּוּי וַחֲרָטָה", "שְׁכִינָה", "שִׂיחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה וְצַעֲרָהּ", "נִחוּם", "פְּגָם בַּנְּשָׁמָה", "אֶת ה' הֶאֱמַרְתָּ וַה' הֶאֱמִירְךָ", "תַּחְבּוּלוֹת לְתַקֵּן"], "node_degrees": {"הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת": 2, "פְּרִישׁוּת שִׂיחָה וְצַעַר": 2, "וִידּוּי וַחֲרָטָה": 1, "שְׁכִינָה": 3, "שִׂיחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה וְצַעֲרָהּ": 2, "נִחוּם": 1, "פְּגָם בַּנְּשָׁמָה": 2, "אֶת ה' הֶאֱמַרְתָּ וַה' הֶאֱמִירְךָ": 2, "תַּחְבּוּלוֹת לְתַקֵּן": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["הִתְבּוֹדְדוּת", "פְּרִישׁוּת שִׂיחָה וְצַעַר"], "reason": "These two nodes are bechina-identical in the Torah's structure: hitbodedut IS the act of articulating speech and pain before God. The Torah opens by defining hitbodedut precisely as 'מְפָרֵשׁ שִׂיחָתוֹ וְצַעֲרוֹ לִפְנֵי הַשֵּׁם'. They are two names for the same practice."}, {"merged_nodes": ["וִידּוּי וַחֲרָטָה", "פְּגָם בַּנְּשָׁמָה"], "reason": "Confession (viduy) and regret (charata) are the response to the flaws (פְּגָמִים) — but critically, they are not cause-and-effect here. In the Torah's structure, the act of confessing and regretting the flaw IS the expression of acknowledging the flaw in the soul. They are different faces of the same act of honest self-disclosure. Both belong to the human side of the mutual-disclosure bechina."}, {"merged_nodes": ["שִׂיחַת הַשְּׁכִינָה וְצַעֲרָהּ", "נִחוּם", "תַּחְבּוּלוֹת לְתַקֵּן"], "reason": "These three are all aspects of the Shekhinah's response — her speech and pain, her comforting of the person, and her seeking of remedies to repair the flaws. In the Torah's bechina structure, all three are dimensions of what 'וַה' הֶאֱמִירְךָ' means: God (through the Shekhinah) declaring/responding to the human. They are three expressions of the same divine-response reality, not a sequence of cause-and-effect."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 260, "label": "שֵׁם וְנֶפֶשׁ וּמְפֻרְסָם", "label_en": "Name, Soul, and Renown", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly equates all three as a single conceptual identity: the Name (שֵׁם) of a person IS their Soul (נֶפֶשׁ) and IS their Renown (מְפֻרְסָם). They are not cause-and-effect; they are three parallel dimensions of the same existential reality — the essential being and presence of a person (specifically the Tzaddik) in the world. Surrendering one's שֵׁם is therefore, by identity, surrendering one's נֶפֶשׁ, which is מְסִירַת נֶפֶשׁ in the fullest sense. The Torah teaches the positive path: the holy leader willingly gives up his reputation (his שֵׁם/מְפֻרְסָם = his נֶפֶשׁ) to achieve divine unity (יִחוּד), thereby saving Israel.", "dominant_node": "שם", "nodes": ["שם", "נפש", "מפרסם"], "node_degrees": {"שם": 2, "נפש": 1, "מפרסם": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 262, "label": "מַעְיָן וְשֵׂכֶל", "label_en": "Spring and Intellect", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe explicitly equates the spring (מַעְיָן) that flows from the House of God with the שֵׂכֶל (divine intellect): 'מַעְיָן מִבֵּית ה' יֵצֵא, שֶׁהוּא הַשֵּׂכֶל'. The proof-verse 'בְּחָכְמָה יִבָּנֶה בָּיִת' further identifies חָכְמָה (wisdom) as the same spring/intellect domain — it is the wisdom-intellect that builds the house of Torah. These three concepts (מַעְיָן, שֵׂכֶל, חָכְמָה) are bechina of each other: they are all expressions of the divine intellectual vitality that begins to flow when one innovates in Torah. This is the source-domain of the Torah's positive teaching.", "dominant_node": "שֵׂכֶל", "nodes": ["מעין", "שכל", "חכמה"], "node_degrees": {"שכל": 2, "מעין": 2, "חכמה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 262, "label": "בְּכִי וּמַסֶּכֶת", "label_en": "Weeping and the Tractate", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Rebbe makes two bechina identifications that cluster around weeping (בְּכִי): (1) 'חִבּוּר הַהֲלָכוֹת וְהַחִדּוּשִׁין נִקְרָא מַסֶּכֶת, זֶה בְּחִינַת: וְשִׁקֻּוַי בִּבְכִי מָסָכְתִּי' — a tractate IS called masekhta because it embodies the pouring/mixing with weeping; (2) 'עַל נַהֲרוֹת בָּבֶל, שָׁם יָשַׁבְנוּ גַּם בָּכִינוּ – זֶה בְּחִינַת תַּלְמוּד בַּבְלִי' — the Babylonian Talmud IS an expression of sitting and weeping by the rivers. Both מַסֶּכֶת and תַּלְמוּד בַּבְלִי are bechina of the same act: Torah-learning accompanied by tears. The weeping (בְּכִי) is the unifying nature that ties these together — it is not merely a technique but the essential quality shared by all three concepts. This package is the means-domain: what the sage must embody when transmitting Torah.", "dominant_node": "בכי", "nodes": ["בכי", "מסכת", "תלמוד בבלי"], "node_degrees": {"בכי": 3, "מסכת": 2, "תלמוד בבלי": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תלמוד בבלי", "מסכת"], "reason": "Both are bechina of the same weeping-while-pouring concept: מַסֶּכֶת via Ps. 102:10 ('וְשִׁקֻּוַי בִּבְכִי מָסָכְתִּי') and תַּלְמוּד בַּבְלִי via Ps. 137:1 ('עַל נַהֲרוֹת בָּבֶל... גַּם בָּכִינוּ'). The Rebbe explicitly links both to the same bechina of weeping. They are two expressions of the same essential nature — Torah produced through and accompanied by tears."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 262, "label": "נְהָרוֹת", "label_en": "Rivers", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "The rivers (נְהָרוֹת) function as a structural conduit in this Torah's argument. They are produced by Torah innovation (eitza from Package 1's domain) and fixed/directed by weeping (eitza from Package 2), yet they are sought by both the holy (כָּל בָּאִים לִשְׁתּוֹת) and by the klipot/sitra achra. A neutral object cannot be a member of the good or evil package — it exists as the shared structural reality that both sides seek to access. The entire Torah's practical concern is about managing this neutral resource: ensuring the rivers flow only to holy recipients and not to the klipot.", "dominant_node": "נהרות", "nodes": ["נהרות"], "node_degrees": {"נהרות": 0}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["נהרות"], "reason": "Single-node component. Cannot merge into Package 1 (Package 1's spring produces the rivers — that is eitza, not bechina; they are distinct entities). Cannot merge into Package 2 (weeping fixes the rivers — again eitza, not shared nature). The rivers are the structural medium of this Torah, genuinely neutral, and must remain their own package."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 262, "label": "קְלִפּוֹת וְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא", "label_en": "Shells and the Other Side", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The klipot and sitra achra are the evil counterforce in this Torah. They are explicitly named as those who come to drink from the rivers when Torah innovations create them: 'יָבוֹאוּ גַּם כֵּן קְלִפּוֹת וְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא לִשְׁתּוֹת'. This is the evil mirror of the good learning that produces rivers. Where Package 1 (intellect/spring) is the source of holy illumination and Package 2 (weeping/tractate) is the method of holy transmission, the klipot represent the parasitic evil that seeks to feed on that same illumination. They form their own package as the opposition domain of this Torah. The good/evil mirror here: Package 1 (spring of intellect) → the klipot want to drink that spring; Package 2 (weeping) → weeping is the antidote that satisfies the klipot before they can corrupt the new rivers.", "dominant_node": "קליפות", "nodes": ["קליפות", "סטרא אחרא"], "node_degrees": {"קליפות": 1, "סטרא אחרא": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קליפות", "סטרא אחרא"], "reason": "The text uses both terms ('קְלִפּוֹת וְסִטְרָא אָחֳרָא') as a compound to refer to the same evil force. They appear together twice in the text and function as a single entity. They are bechina of each other in this context — two names for the same spiritual-evil domain."}]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 263, "label": "חֳלִי הַקַּדַּחַת וּפְגַם הַדַּעַת", "label_en": "Fever Illness and the Blemish of Da'at", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes share the same essential nature: the disordered union of fire and water. The verse 'we went through fire and water' (באנו באש ובמים) names the exact same fire-and-water structure that constitutes the fever (אש ומים, חמימות וקרירות). The fever itself is declared a bechina of the blemish of da'at because da'at's wholeness consists precisely in the correct union of fire and water — so its blemish is that same fire-water duality gone awry. All three are expressions of the same pathological reality: fire and water in destructive opposition rather than harmonious unity.", "dominant_node": "חלי הקדחת", "nodes": ["באנו באש ובמים", "חלי הקדחת", "פגם הדעת"], "node_degrees": {"באנו באש ובמים": 1, "חלי הקדחת": 2, "פגם הדעת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 265, "label": "רָצוֹא וָשׁוֹב וְשְׁבִירַת כְּלִי חֶרֶס", "label_en": "Advance-and-Retreat and the Breaking of the Clay Vessel", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "All the concepts in this Torah are different expressions of a single structural phenomenon: a revelation that is simultaneously concealed — the ratzoh-vashov (advance and retreat) dynamic. The revealed connection between souls at betrothal, the spark produced by breaking a clay vessel, and the flash of lightning in Ezekiel's vision are explicitly equated by the Rebbe as bechinot of each other. None produces the other causally; each is a different face of the same momentary-and-transient illumination. The polarity is neutral because this Torah describes a metaphysical/structural pattern — the oscillation between hiddenness and revelation — with no good-vs-evil opposition. The breaking of the vessel is itself the hint (רֶמֶז) to this pattern, making the physical act and the cosmic principle two expressions of the same reality.", "dominant_node": "רצוא ושוב", "nodes": ["רצוא ושוב", "גלוי ההתקשרות בשידוך", "העלם וגלוי", "שבירת כלי חרס", "ניצוץ / בזק"], "node_degrees": {"רצוא ושוב": 3, "גלוי ההתקשרות בשידוך": 2, "העלם וגלוי": 1, "שבירת כלי חרס": 2, "ניצוץ / בזק": 2}, "merge_notes": [], "dataset_note": "Torah #265 has zero bechina-type edges in torahData.js. This single package is derived entirely from the source text. All nodes listed are new — they do not yet exist in the dataset. The package structure represents what should be built when this Torah is encoded."}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 266, "label": "סֻכָּה וּבִינָה — הָאֵם הַמְסַכֶּכֶת", "label_en": "Sukkah and Binah — The Sheltering Mother", "polarity": "good", "reason": "All six nodes in this component are different expressions of the same essential reality: the divine maternal wisdom (Binah/Imma) that shelters and sustains. The Sukkah IS Imma that covers her children (אִמָּא דִּמְסַכְּכָא עַל בְּנִין), which IS the verse 'Im la-Binah tikra' (Proverbs 2:3), which IS the sekhel/wisdom, which IS what Binah itself is, and which IS what distinguishes the human (who nurses from the breast-place of Binah) from the animal (who nurses from below). These are all one domain: the sheltering, nurturing, intellectual-spiritual covering that is Binah/Sukkah.", "dominant_node": "סכה", "nodes": ["סכה", "אמא דמסככא על בנין", "\"אם לבינה תקרא\"", "הבדל שבין גדר אדם לגדר בהמה", "חכמה", "בינה"], "node_degrees": {"סכה": 3, "אמא דמסככא על בנין": 2, "\"אם לבינה תקרא\"": 2, "הבדל שבין גדר אדם לגדר בהמה": 1, "חכמה": 1, "בינה": 1}, "source_edges": [{"id": 284, "node1": "סכה", "node2": "אמא דמסככא על בנין", "proof": "סֻכָּה בְּחִינוֹת 'אִמָּא דִּמְסַכְּכָא עַל בְּנִין' (תִּקּוּנִים דַּף ג)"}, {"id": 4, "node1": "אמא דמסככא על בנין", "node2": "\"אם לבינה תקרא\"", "proof": "'אִמָּא דִּמְסַכְּכָא עַל בְּנִין' (תִּקּוּנִים דַּף ג), בְּחִינוֹת: \"אִם לַבִּינָה תִּקְרָא\" (מִשְׁלֵי ב)"}, {"id": 794, "node1": "\"אם לבינה תקרא\"", "node2": "הבדל שבין גדר אדם לגדר בהמה", "proof": "\"אִם לַבִּינָה תִּקְרָא\" — וְזֶהוּ בְּחִינוֹת הַהֶבְדֵּל שֶׁבֵּין גֶּדֶר אָדָם לְגֶדֶר בְּהֵמָה"}, {"id": 1143, "node1": "סכה", "node2": "חכמה", "proof": "הַסֻּכָּה שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת שֵׂכֶל, בְּחִינַת אִם לַבִּינָה כַּנַּ\"ל"}, {"id": 2313, "node1": "בינה", "node2": "סכה", "proof": "מִבִּינָה שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת סֻכָּה כְּמוֹ שֶׁכָּתוּב (מִשְׁלֵי א): \"וְאַל תִּטֹּשׁ תּוֹרַת אִמֶּךָ\", בְּחִינַת: \"אִם לַבִּינָה\" שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת סֻכָּה כַּנַּ\"ל"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 266, "label": "פְּגַם הַחָכְמָה וְהַמִּסְכֵּנוּת", "label_en": "Damage to Wisdom and Impoverishment", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These three nodes all belong to the same negative domain: the consequence and expression of abandoning the Sukkah/Binah. 'Pегam ha-chokhmah' (damage to wisdom) is the sin-side counterpart to the good Sukkah-wisdom package. 'Wisdom of the poor man is despised' (Kohelet 9:16) is the scriptural expression of damaged wisdom. 'Becoming impoverished' (mitmasken) is the punishment that IS an expression of damaged wisdom — not merely caused by it, but constituting the same reality: a person stripped of the dignity and abundance that sekhel/Binah bestow. This is the evil mirror of Package 1.", "dominant_node": "פגם החכמה", "nodes": ["פגם החכמה", "\"חכמת המסכן בזויה\"", "מתמסכן"], "node_degrees": {"פגם החכמה": 2, "\"חכמת המסכן בזויה\"": 1, "מתמסכן": 1}, "source_edges": [{"id": 58, "node1": "פגם החכמה", "node2": "\"חכמת המסכן בזויה\"", "proof": "פְּגַם הַחָכְמָה, בְּחִינוֹת \"חָכְמַת הַמִּסְכֵּן בְּזוּיָה\" (קֹהֶלֶת ט)"}, {"id": 2473, "node1": "מתמסכן", "node2": "פגם החכמה", "proof": "שֶׁמִּתְמַסְכֵּן, שֶׁזֶּה בְּחִינוֹת פְּגַם הַחָכְמָה"}], "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 277, "label": "חִלּוּל שַׁבָּת וּפְסִיעוֹת קְצָרוֹת", "label_en": "Shabbat Desecration and Shortened Steps", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of 'departure / withdrawal / diminishment.' Chilul Shabbat is a chalal (hollow, withdrawn space); chalal is histalkut (departure/ascent of the holy); 'ki yimatzeh chalal ba'adamah' is the scriptural expression of that same hollow. Pesi'ot ktzarot (shortened steps) and 'pesi'otav mitkatzarot' are both expressions of the same contraction — the walking diminished, the mitzvah's legs weakened. Shabbat as 'first wife' and its desecration as her death complete the cluster: the death of the first wife IS the shortened step IS chilul Shabbat. All nodes describe the same reality of sacred diminishment / degraded walking.", "dominant_node": "חלול שבת", "nodes": ["חלול שבת", "חלל", "הסתלקות", "\"כי ימצא חלל באדמה\"", "פסיעות קצרות", "פסיעותיו מתקצרות", "שבת", "אשתו ראשונה"], "node_degrees": {"חלול שבת": 2, "חלל": 2, "הסתלקות": 2, "\"כי ימצא חלל באדמה\"": 1, "פסיעות קצרות": 2, "פסיעותיו מתקצרות": 1, "שבת": 1, "אשתו ראשונה": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["שבת", "אשתו ראשונה"], "reason": "CC2 (shabbat — first wife) is a 2-node isolated pair. The text explicitly defines Shabbat as 'first wife' (bechina), and the entire discourse on chilul Shabbat hinges on this equation: desecrating Shabbat = 'killing' the first wife = shortened steps. The shared nature is the Shabbat entity itself — שבת and אשתו ראשונה are the same thing from two angles (the holy day and the intimate partner-soul). They belong in the Shabbat cluster with chilul shabbat, chalal, and pesi'ot ktzarot."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 277, "label": "תָּמָר בִּקְדֻשָּׁה וְהַמְתָּקַת הַדִּינִים", "label_en": "The Date Palm of Holiness and the Sweetening of Judgments", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes share the essential nature of the holy resolution of conflict. 'Tzaddik ka-tamar yifrach' (the righteous one flourishes like a date palm) is the positive outcome of the tzaddik's machloket — it IS the sweetening of the judgments. Tamar bi-kedushah is the abstract concept; 'tzaddik ka-tamar yifrach' is its scriptural expression; ha-matak ha-dinim is its functional identity. The three are different faces of the same reality: holy flourishing that nullifies the evil tamar (sitra achra).", "dominant_node": "\"צדיק כתמר יפרח\"", "nodes": ["תמר בקדשה", "\"צדיק כתמר יפרח\"", "המתקת הדינים"], "node_degrees": {"תמר בקדשה": 1, "\"צדיק כתמר יפרח\"": 2, "המתקת הדינים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 277, "label": "סִּטְרָא אָחֳרָא וְתָּמָר הָרָע", "label_en": "The Sitra Achra and the Evil Tomar", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "These nodes all share the essential nature of the evil force — the realm of din, opposition, and inversion. The sitra achra IS machloket (conflict/dispute is the very substance of the evil side). The tomar of the sitra achra IS the shaor (leaven = concentrated din = Elohim squared and filled = the evil 'tamar' which means temura/inversion). Eretz Canaan IS 'kan ani' (here is poverty) — the condition that arises from machloket, which is the sitra achra. Canaan as poverty is a bechina of the same evil domain: conflict strips prosperity, the sitra achra's expression in the earthly realm. All nodes name the same essential force: the evil power of machloket/din/sitra achra in its various faces.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["סטרא אחרא", "מחלקת", "תמר בסטרא אחרא", "שאור בככותבת", "ארץ כנען", "כנען", "כאן עני"], "node_degrees": {"סטרא אחרא": 1, "מחלקת": 1, "תמר בסטרא אחרא": 1, "שאור בככותבת": 1, "ארץ כנען": 1, "כנען": 2, "כאן עני": 2}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["תמר בסטרא אחרא", "שאור בככותבת"], "reason": "CC7 is a 2-node pair: tomar of the sitra achra = shaor ba-kachovet (leaven in the date). The text defines the tomar of sitra achra as 'temura' (inversion) — it is the anti-tamar, the inversion of wisdom = foolishness, inversion of life = death. Shaor is 'klalut ve-tokef ha-dinim,' the concentrated force of harsh judgment. Both are names for the same evil entity. They share the essential nature of the sitra achra's concentrated din-force. This belongs with the sitra achra / machloket cluster as the same domain of evil."}, {"merged_nodes": ["ארץ כנען", "כנען", "כאן עני"], "reason": "CC5 is a 3-node cluster: eretz Canaan = Canaan = 'here is poverty.' The text says when machloket exists, the land is called Canaan. Canaan IS 'kan ani' (poverty). This is not a cause-effect relationship — the poverty IS the Canaan nature, which IS the expression of machloket in the material world. Canaan as poverty-land is a bechina of the same evil domain as machloket/sitra achra. The sitra achra's grip creates the Canaan condition. The essential shared nature is: the evil force as it manifests in depletion of resources and spiritual poverty."}]}, {"id": 4, "torah_ref": 277, "label": "שָׁלוֹם וְהָרְפוּאוֹת", "label_en": "Peace and Healing", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These three nodes share the essential nature of wholeness, integration, and the healing power that flows when conflict is absent. Shalom IS 'eretz natna yevulah' (the land gives its produce) — when peace reigns, the earth is fully generative. Refu'ot (medicines/healings) ARE shalom — the healing force is a bechina of peace itself, not merely produced by it. The Rebbe explicitly states 'ha-refu'ot she-hem bechina shalom.' All three express the single quality of integrated wholeness that is the opposite of machloket.", "dominant_node": "שלום", "nodes": ["שלום", "ארץ נתנה יבולה", "רפואה"], "node_degrees": {"שלום": 2, "ארץ נתנה יבולה": 1, "רפואה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 278, "label": "קִנְאַת הַצַּדִּיק — הַקְּנָאָה לַה' לְבַדּוֹ", "label_en": "The Tzaddik's Zealousness — Jealousy for God Alone", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The phrase 'בְּקַנְּאוֹ אֶת קִנְאָתִי' (Pinchas's act of zealousness for God) and 'הַצַּדִּיק שֶׁאֵינוֹ מְקַנֵּא שׁוּם צַדִּיק, לֹא בָּעוֹלָם הַזֶּה וְלֹא בָּעוֹלָם הַבָּא, רַק אוֹתוֹ לְבַדּוֹ יִתְבָּרַךְ' share the same essential nature: both express the quality of being zealous exclusively for God and for nothing else. The scriptural phrase IS a bechina of this Tzaddik-quality — they are two faces of the same thing, not cause and effect. Pinchas's act of jealousy for God alone IS the archetypal expression of the Tzaddik who is free from jealousy toward any fellow tzaddik, channeling all his jealousy solely toward the Divine. The polarity is good: this cluster belongs to the domain of holiness and the positive quality of the true Tzaddik.", "dominant_node": "\"בקנאו את קנאתי\"", "nodes": ["\"בקנאו את קנאתי\"", "הצדיק שאינו מקנא שום צדיק, לא בעולם הזה ולא בעולם הבא, רק אותו לבדו יתברך"], "node_degrees": {"\"בקנאו את קנאתי\"": 1, "הצדיק שאינו מקנא שום צדיק, לא בעולם הזה ולא בעולם הבא, רק אותו לבדו יתברך": 1}, "merge_notes": [], "good_evil_mirror": {"this_package_polarity": "good", "mirror_package": "NOT_IN_DATASET", "mirror_label": "קִנְאָה בֵּין צַדִּיקִים — Jealousy Among Tzaddikim", "mirror_note": "The evil counterpart implied by the Torah is the negative phenomenon of one tzaddik being jealous of another tzaddik — the absence of the quality captured in Package 1. This negative pole (jealousy directed at other tzaddikim rather than at God alone) is the implicit deficiency that the Torah warns against, but it is not explicitly named as a bechina in the text and is not in the dataset for Torah #278."}}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 280, "label": "מַשָּׂא וּמַתָּן וְהַנִּיצוֹצוֹת", "label_en": "Commerce and the Fallen Sparks", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The dominant concept is מַשָּׂא וּמַתָּן (commercial trade), which the Torah defines as בְּחִינוֹת שֶׁמְּנַשְּׂאִין וּמְרִימִין הַדָּבָר — literally the act of lifting and restoring things to their place. This is identified as the cosmic function of raising fallen holy sparks (ניצוצות). Commerce = עֲשִׂיָּה (the World of Action), which is the lowest spiritual level and the realm from which sparks must be lifted. The biblical phrase כִּי תֵצֵא (when you go out) maps onto commerce as the mode of 'going out' into the world — צֵאתְךָ — and Zevulun's verse שְׂמַח זְבוּלוּן בְּצֵאתֶךָ encapsulates the spirit of this domain. All nodes in this package share the essential nature of engaged outward activity in the material world whose inner purpose is the elevation of trapped holiness.", "dominant_node": "משא ומתן", "nodes": ["משא ומתן", "כי תצא", "עשיה", "צאתך", "\"שמח זבולון בצאתך\"", "שמנשאין ומרימין הדבר, ונותנין ומשיבין אותו למקומו", "קדשות נפולות", "ניצוצות שנפלו"], "node_degrees": {"משא ומתן": 5, "כי תצא": 1, "עשיה": 1, "צאתך": 1, "\"שמח זבולון בצאתך\"": 1, "שמנשאין ומרימין הדבר, ונותנין ומשיבין אותו למקומו": 1, "קדשות נפולות": 1, "ניצוצות שנפלו": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["קדשות נפולות", "ניצוצות שנפלו"], "reason": "These two nodes are definitional synonyms of each other (the edge connecting them is a pure equivalence: 'fallen holiness, namely the fallen sparks'). Their thematic home is the commerce package because commerce is not merely the cause of their elevation — commerce IS defined as the bechina of lifting/raising (שֶׁמְּנַשְּׂאִין וּמְרִימִין), which means the sparks and the lifting activity share the same essential nature. The fallen sparks are the very substance of what commerce operates on; they belong inside the commerce package as the implicit object of its cosmic definition. Contrast with faith (Package 3), which PRODUCES the elevation (eitza) but does not share the same nature as the sparks themselves."}]}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 280, "label": "תּוֹרָה וִיצִירָה", "label_en": "Torah and the World of Formation", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package clusters around תּוֹרָה as the inner, higher dimension of spiritual life — the counterpart to the outer activity of commerce. The Torah is identified with the letter וָא\"ו (as taught in the Zohar), and with the World of יְצִירָה (Formation), which is the spiritual level that commerce (= עֲשִׂיָּה) aspires to connect with. The phrase וְשָׁבִיתָ שִׁבְיוֹ (and you shall take captive his captivity) from the verse כִּי תֵצֵא is identified as a bechina of Torah — Torah is the 'captivity taken from on high' (עָלִיתָ לַמָּרוֹם שָׁבִיתָ שֶׁבִי). עֲבוֹדַת הַתּוֹרָה (Torah study as service) is explicitly called the bechina of יִשָּׂשׂכָר בְּאֹהֳלֶיךָ — the 'staying in tents' mode of divine service, the inward, higher domain. All nodes share the essential nature of Torah as the interior dimension, the higher world of Formation, the goal toward which material activity strives.", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אות וא\"ו", "יצירה", "ושבית שביו", "עבודת התורה", "יששכר באהליך"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 2, "אות וא\"ו": 2, "יצירה": 1, "ושבית שביו": 1, "עבודת התורה": 1, "יששכר באהליך": 1}, "merge_notes": [{"merged_nodes": ["עבודת התורה", "יששכר באהליך"], "reason": "The proof text reads 'עֲבוֹדַת הַתּוֹרָה שֶׁהִיא בְּחִינַת יִשָֹּשׂכָר בְּאֹהֳלֶיךָ' — Torah study IS the bechina of Issachar in the tents. This pair (Component D) belongs in the Torah package because its essential nature is identical: both nodes are expressions of deep Torah engagement as an inward spiritual mode. The Torah explicitly contrasts this with commerce (Zevulun = going out), placing Torah study in direct opposition as the 'higher' inward domain — the same domain as יצירה and וא\"ו. This is bechina (same nature as Torah), not eitza."}]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 280, "label": "אֱמוּנָה וִירוּשָׁלַיִם", "label_en": "Faith and Jerusalem", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This package clusters around אֱמוּנָה (faith/faithfulness) as the spiritual quality that dwells among the klipot and keeps the fallen sparks from being entirely lost. The key equation is: יְרוּשָׁלַיִם = קִרְיָה נֶאֱמָנָה = בְּחִינוֹת אֱמוּנָה. Jerusalem the 'faithful city' is a bechina of faith — they share the same essential quality of steadfast presence amid hostile surroundings. The Torah draws on the verse from Yechezkel (5:5): 'This is Jerusalem I have placed in the midst of the nations,' equating Jerusalem's location among the nations with faith's dwelling among the klipot. All three nodes (Jerusalem, faithful city, faith) are expressions of the same essential quality: unshakeable divine presence in an unredeemed world. Note: מַלְכוּת דַּעֲשִׂיָּה is explicitly identified in the source as 'the bechina of faith in commerce' (line 11 of the text) but this node is not in the dataset — see additions file.", "dominant_node": "קריה נאמנה", "nodes": ["קריה נאמנה", "אמונה", "ירושלים"], "node_degrees": {"קריה נאמנה": 2, "אמונה": 1, "ירושלים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 282, "label": "צַדִּיקִים, רוֹעֶה, וּמֹשֶׁה", "label_en": "The Tzaddikim, the Shepherd, and Moses", "polarity": "good", "reason": "This is the largest connected component (3 nodes, 2 edges). כל הצדיקים שבדור and משה are both directly connected to רועה via bechina edges. The Rebbe states that every tzaddik in the generation IS a bechina of 'shepherd,' and the shepherd (the tzaddik of the generation, Moses archetype) IS a bechina of Moses — רְעָיָא מְהֵימָנָא, the faithful shepherd. All three nodes share the same essential nature: the tzaddik who collects good points from every Jew and thereby constructs a Mishkan (tabernacle) through which children receive their pure breath of Torah. Moses is the archetype of this function — collecting, gathering, shepherding the flock. The bechina is: what IS a tzaddik? He is a shepherd. What IS a shepherd? He is Moses. These three are three faces of the same spiritual reality.", "dominant_node": "רועה", "nodes": ["כל הצדיקים שבדור", "רועה", "משה"], "node_degrees": {"רועה": 2, "כל הצדיקים שבדור": 1, "משה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 282, "label": "נִגּוּן וּבֵרוּר הַנְּקֻדּוֹת הַטּוֹבוֹת", "label_en": "The Melody and the Extraction of Good Points", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Two nodes (2-node component, 1 edge). The Rebbe states that playing a melody on an instrument IS a bechina of extracting the good spirit from the spirit of sadness and depression. The player of nigguns and the act of sorting/extracting good points from bad are the same spiritual act — both are expressions of the same essential operation: separating and elevating the holy spark from within the mixture of good and evil. This is not cause-and-effect (the niggun does not produce the extraction — they are the same thing described from two angles). The proof text explicitly uses 'הוּא בְּחִינַת': 'מְנַגֵּן בִּכְלֵי זֶמֶר, שֶׁהוּא בְּחִינַת שֶׁמְּלַקֵּט הָרוּחַ טוֹבָה מִן הָרוּחַ נְכֵאָה עַצְבוּת רוּחַ'. Melody IS the extraction. This is the musical/spiritual face of the core practice of Torah #282.", "dominant_node": "מנגן בכלי זמר", "nodes": ["מנגן בכלי זמר", "מלקט הרוח טובה מן הרוח נכאה עצבות רוח"], "node_degrees": {"מנגן בכלי זמר": 1, "מלקט הרוח טובה מן הרוח נכאה עצבות רוח": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component is retained as a separate package rather than merged into Package 3 (the 'עוד מעט' cluster). While the practice of finding good points and the practice of making nigguns are deeply linked in the Torah's teaching (and are connected via eitza: finding good points PRODUCES nigguns), they are not the same essential nature. The niggun IS the extraction process (bechina: identical nature) — but the niggun/extraction is not the same thing as 'the good point found within the sinner' (עוד מעט). The niggun is the RESULT of gathering good points. This is an eitza connection between Package 2 and Package 3, not a bechina merging them. Keeping separate also preserves the teaching's own structure: the Rebbe introduces the music passage as its own conceptual station (citing LM #54)."]}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 282, "label": "עוֹד שֶׁלִּי וְעוֹד מְעַט וְאֵין רָשָׁע", "label_en": "My 'Still' and 'Yet a Little While and There Is No Wicked'", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Two nodes (2-node component, 1 edge). The Rebbe interprets the Psalm phrase 'אֲזַמְּרָה לֵאלֹקַי בְּעוֹדִי' (I will sing to my God while I still exist) and draws a bechina between 'עוֹד שֶׁלִּי שֶׁאֲנִי מוֹצֵא בְּעַצְמִי' (the 'still' that I find within myself — the remaining good point) and 'עוֹד מְעַט וְאֵין רָשָׁע' (yet a little while and there is no wicked, Psalms 37:10). Both refer to the SAME thing: the small residue of goodness that remains in every person, the 'נְקֻדָּה טוֹבָה' (good point). The subjective experience of finding goodness in oneself ('עוד שלי') IS the objective reality that the verse describes ('עוד מעט ואין רשע') — they are two sides of the same coin. The bechina is symmetrical: just as the Psalm says there is always 'a little bit' of non-wickedness, so too the person's 'I still find something good in myself' is that very same reality viewed from within.", "dominant_node": "עוד מעט ואין רשע", "nodes": ["עוד שלי שאני מוצא בעצמי", "עוד מעט ואין רשע"], "node_degrees": {"עוד שלי שאני מוצא בעצמי": 1, "עוד מעט ואין רשע": 1}, "merge_notes": ["This 2-node component could in principle be merged with Package 2 (niggun/extraction), since finding good points IS the pre-condition for making nigguns. However, the connection between them is eitza (cause-effect: finding the good point leads to/produces the niggun), not bechina (they are not the same essential nature). The 'good point found in oneself' and 'the melody created from it' are two different things — the point is the raw material, the melody is the refined output. They belong to different packages. Package 3 is retained as the foundational conceptual cluster: the 'something good' that can always be found — the theological ground of the teaching."]}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 283, "label": "חֶסֶד וְגִילּוּי תּוֹרָה — דָּוִד", "label_en": "Kindness and Torah Revelation — the David Archetype", "polarity": "good", "reason": "Revealing Torah to others and chesed share the same essential nature: both are the outward-flowing quality of goodness ('דְּאִתְפַּשַּׁט לְבַר'). The Zohar explicitly equates chesed with outward expansion ('חֶסֶד — דְּאִתְפַּשַּׁט לְבַר'), and the Torah identifies the action of teaching Torah publicly as bechina of chesed, citing 'וְתוֹרַת חֶסֶד עַל לְשׁוֹנָהּ.' David who revealed his masechet is named as the prototype of this quality. These concepts are not cause-and-effect — revealing Torah is not a consequence of chesed; they are the same thing viewed from different angles: chesed as a divine quality and revealing-Torah as its human expression.", "dominant_node": "חסד", "nodes": ["מגלה תורתו לאחרים", "חסד"], "node_degrees": {"מגלה תורתו לאחרים": 1, "חסד": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 283, "label": "תּוֹרָה וְאֵשׁ", "label_en": "Torah and Fire — the Contained Energy", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Torah and fire share the same essential nature as an explosive, luminous, consuming force. The proof-text is Jeremiah 23: 'Is not My word like fire?' — establishing Torah as fire by explicit bechina language. This is a neutral metaphor: Torah-as-fire can be the holy teaching that illuminates, or the compressed energy that bursts into conflict when not released. In Torah #283, this bechina serves as the pivotal structural concept: the Torah kept inside (by the טוֹב-type tzaddik) is like fire imprisoned in the heart ('עֲצוּרָה בְּלִבּוֹ כְּאֵשׁ בּוֹעֶרֶת'), and it is this fire that eventually erupts as the thunderous dispute. Torah/fire is therefore the neutral causal substrate — the same energy that, when it flows outward, becomes chesed (Package 1), and when it stays hidden, produces discord (Package 3).", "dominant_node": "תורה", "nodes": ["תורה", "אש"], "node_degrees": {"תורה": 1, "אש": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 3, "torah_ref": 283, "label": "מַחֲלֹקֶת וּרְעָמִים", "label_en": "Dispute and Thunder — the Structural Metaphor", "polarity": "neutral", "reason": "Dispute and thunder share the same essential nature: both are the explosive release of compressed energy. The proof is from an earlier teaching (Torah #56): 'the dispute is a bechina of thunder.' The Torah explains the physical mechanism — hot vapors absorbed by clouds until the cloud cannot contain them, splitting open as thunder — and maps it structurally onto the tzaddik-dispute: the Torah-fire (like hot vapors) is absorbed by the inward-keeping tzaddik and, when it can no longer be contained, bursts out as a dispute. מחלקת and רעמים are therefore two names for the same phenomenon — compression followed by explosive release — applied in two domains (social/spiritual and meteorological). They are not in a cause-effect chain with each other; they are co-equal bechinas of the same structural reality. The polarity is neutral: this Torah explicitly discusses both the holy dispute of tzaddikim (which IS bechina of thunder) and the wicked dispute (line 8) which is explicitly contrasted as NOT rooted in Torah. The thunder-metaphor thus describes a structure that can apply to either domain.", "dominant_node": "מחלקת", "nodes": ["מחלקת", "רעמים"], "node_degrees": {"מחלקת": 1, "רעמים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 285, "label": "אוֹר הַתּוֹרָה וְטַעֲמָהּ", "label_en": "The Light and Taste of Torah", "polarity": "good", "reason": "These concepts all express the same essential quality: the enduring, self-sustaining illumination that the Tzaddik's Torah imprints in a person. טַעַם הַתּוֹרָה (tasting the Torah), אוֹר הַתּוֹרָה (the light of Torah), and נֵר (the candle that does not go out) are all bechinas of the same reality — a spiritual contact with holy teaching that, once experienced, permanently illuminates even in absence. The Tzaddik's Torah (תּוֹרַת הַצַּדִּיק) and his 'merchandise' (סְחוֹרָה) are two ways of naming the same object of that contact.", "dominant_node": "טַעַם הַתּוֹרָה", "nodes": ["טַעַם הַתּוֹרָה", "אוֹר הַתּוֹרָה", "נֵר", "תּוֹרַת הַצַּדִּיק", "סְחוֹרָה"], "node_degrees": {"טַעַם הַתּוֹרָה": 3, "אוֹר הַתּוֹרָה": 2, "נֵר": 2, "תּוֹרַת הַצַּדִּיק": 2, "סְחוֹרָה": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 2, "torah_ref": 285, "label": "הִתְרַחֲקוּת וְלַיְלָה", "label_en": "Distancing and Night", "polarity": "evil", "reason": "The Torah explicitly states two bechina equations that bind these nodes together: (1) הִתְרַחֲקוּת (distancing from the Tzaddik) IS a bechina of לַיְלָה (night), and (2) לַיְלָה and חֹשֶׁךְ (darkness) are paired as equivalent states — 'בְּחִינַת לַיְלָה וְחֹשֶׁךְ'. All three concepts express the same negative existential state: the condition of being spiritually cut off from the source of light. They are not cause-and-effect (distancing does not produce night; it IS night). This is the evil mirror of Package 1: where Package 1 is the persistent light, Package 2 is the darkness that light penetrates.", "dominant_node": "לַיְלָה", "nodes": ["לַיְלָה", "חֹשֶׁךְ", "הִתְרַחֲקוּת"], "node_degrees": {"לַיְלָה": 2, "חֹשֶׁךְ": 1, "הִתְרַחֲקוּת": 1}, "merge_notes": []}, {"id": 1, "torah_ref": 286, "label": "מִשְׁפְּטֵי הַתּוֹרָה וּפוֹסְקִים", "label_en": "Torah Law and Legal Decisors", "polarity": "good", "reason": "The Torah makes explicit that 'mishpetei haTorah' (the laws and judgments of Torah) ARE the Poskim — they are two names for the same essential thing. The Poskim are not a means to Torah law; they ARE the clarification and embodiment of Torah law. Both refer to the domain of halachic adjudication, which clarifies divine law. This bechina is confirmed by the proof text: 'מִשְׁפְּטֵי הַתּוֹרָה, בְּחִינַת פּוֹסְקִים'. This is the active force in the Torah's argument: studying the Poskim establishes dominion over the earth (ארץ), which in turn uncovers the sunken gates of Gan Eden. The domain is entirely good/holy — Torah study and legal clarification.", "dominant_node": "משפטי התורה", "nodes": ["משפטי התורה", "פוסקים"], "node_degrees": {"משפטי התורה": 1, "פוסקים": 1}, "merge_notes": []}];
